hadiths[2455]{hadithnumber,text}:
"14288","ʿUrwa ibn al-Zubayr reported that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه, his name was ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿĀmir ibn ʿAmr ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murra ibn Luʾayy. He was present at Badr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The mother of Abū Bakr was Umm al-Khayr Salmā bint Ṣakhr ibn ʿĀmir ibn ʿAmr ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murra ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr ibn Mālik, and the mother of Umm al-Khayr was Dilāf, who is Umayma bint ʿUbayd ibn al-Nāqid al-Khuzāʿī. The grandmother of Abū Bakr, the mother of Abū Quḥāfa, was Amīna bint ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Ḥarthān ibn ʿAwf ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUwayj ibn ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb."
"14289","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ looked at Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه and said that he is Allah's freed one from the Fire, and from that day he was named ʿAtīq, whereas before that his name was ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān. > **""This is Allah's ʿAtīq from the Fire.""**"
"14290","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها said, by Allah, one day she was in her house while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions were in the courtyard with a curtain between her and them, when Abū Bakr came forward, so the Prophet ﷺ said that whoever is pleased to look at one freed from the Fire should look at Abū Bakr. ʿĀʾisha added that his name which his family gave him was ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān, then the name ʿAtīq prevailed over him. > **""Whoever is pleased to look at one freed from the Fire, let him look at Abū Bakr.""**"
"14291","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said the mother of Abū Bakr, the mother of ʿUthmān, the mother of Ṭalḥa, the mother of al-Zubayr, the mother of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and the mother of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir all accepted Islam. He added that he was only named ʿAtīq ibn ʿUthmān on account of the beauty of his face."
"14292","Al-Layth ibn Saʿd said Abū Bakr was only named ʿAtīq on account of the nobility and beauty of his face, and his name was ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān."
"14293","Al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad said he asked ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها about the name of Abū Bakr, so she said ʿAbd Allāh. He said, they say ʿAtīq. She said Abū Quḥāfa had three sons, so he named one ʿAtīq and one Muʿaytiq and one Muʿattaq."
"14294","Abū Ḥafṣ ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī used to say that Abū Bakr had little flesh on his face, and he was only named ʿAtīq on account of the nobility of his face, and his name was ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān. It has also been narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAtīq from the Fire."
"14295","Ḥakīm ibn Saʿd said he heard ʿAlī رضي الله عنه swearing that Allah sent down the name of Abū Bakr from the heaven as al-Ṣiddīq. > **""Allah sent down the name of Abū Bakr from the heaven: al-Ṣiddīq.""**"
"14296","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was taken up to the lowest heaven, and he did not pass by any heaven except that he found therein his name, Muḥammad the Messenger of Allah, with Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq behind him. > **""I was taken up to the lowest heaven, and I did not pass by any heaven except that I found therein my name: Muḥammad the Messenger of Allah, and Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq behind me.""**"
"14297","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when he was taken up to the heaven, he did not pass by any heaven except that he found his name written therein as Muḥammad the Messenger of Allah, with Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq. > **""When I was taken up to the heaven, I did not pass by any heaven except that I found my name written therein: Muḥammad the Messenger of Allah, Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq.""**"
"14298","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that on the night he was taken for the Isrāʾ, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that his people would not confirm him, so Jibrīl عليه السلام said to him that Abū Bakr would confirm him, and he is al-Ṣiddīq. > **""My people will not confirm me.""** > **""Abū Bakr will confirm you, and he is al-Ṣiddīq.""**"
"14299","In another narration from him, the wording is that the Prophet ﷺ said that his people would accuse him of falsehood. > **""My people will accuse me.""**"
"14300","Umm Hāniʾ رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when he was taken for the Isrāʾ that he wished to go out to Quraysh and inform them, so they belied him while Abū Bakr confirmed him, and on that day he was named al-Ṣiddīq. > **""I wish to go out to Quraysh and inform them.""**"
"14301","Muʿāwiya رضي الله عنه said he entered with his father upon Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه, and he saw Asmāʾ رضي الله عنها standing at his head, fair-skinned, and he saw Abū Bakr fair-skinned and slight in body. He carried him and his father upon two horses, then they were presented before him and he granted them provision."
"14302","A man of Banū Asad said he saw Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه at the expedition of Dhāt al-Salāsil, his beard as if like the flame of the ʿarfaj shrub, upon a dark tawny she-camel of his, fair-skinned and slight in body."
"14303","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها saw a man passing while she was in her howdah, so she said she had never seen a man more resembling Abū Bakr than him. It was said to her, describe Abū Bakr for us. She said he was a fair-skinned, slight man with light cheeks, stooped so that his waist-wrapper would not hold but would slip from his hips, thin in the face, deep-set in the eyes, prominent in the forehead, and bare in the finger-joints. This is his description."
"14304","Rāfiʿ ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنه said the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him on a raid or a ḥajj, so he examined them closely and saw none among them finer in appearance than Abū Bakr, who had wrapped himself in a cloak against heat and cold."
"14305","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Abū Bakr is his companion and comforter in the cave, and ordered that every small door into the mosque be closed except the small door of Abū Bakr. > **""Abū Bakr is my companion and comforter in the cave. Close every small door in the mosque except the small door of Abū Bakr.""**"
"14306","Muʿāwiya ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to pour upon him water from seven skins from various wells so that he might go out to the people and enjoin upon them. He went out with his head bound ﷺ until he ascended the minbar, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said a servant of Allah was given choice between this world and what is with Allah, so he chose what is with Allah. None understood it except Abū Bakr, so he wept and said, we would ransom you with our fathers, mothers and sons. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, at ease, the most excellent of people to me in companionship and in wealth is Ibn Abī Quḥāfa. Look at these doors opening into the mosque and close them, except what was the door of Abū Bakr, for I have seen light upon it. > **""Pour upon me from seven skins from various wells, so that I may go out to the people and enjoin upon them.""** > **""A servant of Allah was given choice between this world and what is with Allah, so he chose what is with Allah.""** > **""At ease. The most excellent of people to me in companionship and wealth is Ibn Abī Quḥāfa. Look at these doors opening into the mosque and close them, except what was the door of Abū Bakr, for I have seen light upon it.""**"
"14307","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the closing of the doors that were in the mosque except the door of Abū Bakr."
"14308","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to close from him every door except the door of Abū Bakr, and had he taken a close beloved friend he would have taken Abū Bakr as a close beloved friend. > **""Close from me every door except the door of Abū Bakr, and had I taken a close beloved friend I would have taken Abū Bakr as a close beloved friend.""**"
"14309","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from al-Baqīʿ, then she related the account of his illness until she said they poured water upon him until he began to say enough, enough. Then he went out with his head bound and sat upon the minbar. The first thing he spoke with was to invoke blessings upon the people of Uḥud and he invoked much blessing upon them, then he said a servant of Allah was given choice by Allah between this world and what is with Him, so he chose what is with Allah. Abū Bakr understood it, so he wept and knew that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ meant himself. He said, at ease O Abū Bakr, look into this mosque at these adjoining doors and close them, except what was from the house of Abū Bakr, for I know no one superior to me in companionship than him. > **""Enough, enough.""** > **""A servant of Allah was given choice by Allah between this world and what is with Him, so he chose what is with Allah.""** > **""At ease O Abū Bakr. Look into this mosque at these adjoining doors and close them, except what was from the house of Abū Bakr, for I know no one superior to me in companionship than him.""**"
"14310","Al-Shaʿbī said he asked Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما who first accepted Islam. Ibn ʿAbbās said, have you not heard the saying of Ḥassān ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه: When you recall grief for a trusted brother, then recall your brother Abū Bakr for what he did, the best of creation, most God-fearing and most just of them, except the Prophet, and most faithful to what he bore. And the second, the follower, praiseworthy in his presence, and the first of the people among them to confirm the messengers. > **""When you recall grief for a trusted brother, then recall your brother Abū Bakr for what he did. The best of creation, most God-fearing and most just of them, except the Prophet, and most faithful to what he bore. And the second, the follower, praiseworthy in his presence, and the first of the people among them to confirm the messengers.""**"
"14311","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said the first to accept Islam was Abū Bakr."
"14312","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه said the first to pray with the Prophet ﷺ was Abū Bakr."
"14313","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنهما said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه walking in front of Abū Bakr, so he said, O Abū al-Dardāʾ, do you walk ahead of a man after the Prophets upon whom the sun has not risen over one more excellent than him. After that Abū al-Dardāʾ was never seen walking except behind Abū Bakr. > **""O Abū al-Dardāʾ, do you walk ahead of a man after the Prophets upon whom the sun has not risen over one more excellent than him?""**"
"14314","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him walking ahead of Abū Bakr, so he said, do not walk ahead of one who is better than you. Abū Bakr is better than all upon whom the sun has risen or set. > **""Do not walk ahead of one who is better than you. Abū Bakr is better than all upon whom the sun has risen or set.""**"
"14315","Salama ibn al-Akwaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq is the best of people, unless there be a Prophet. > **""Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq is the best of people, unless there be a Prophet.""**"
"14316","Asʿad ibn Zurāra رضي الله عنه said he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressing the people, then he glanced and did not see Abū Bakr, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Abū Bakr, Abū Bakr, the Holy Spirit Jibrīl عليه السلام has just informed me that the best of your nation after you is Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq. > **""Abū Bakr, Abū Bakr. The Holy Spirit Jibrīl has just informed me that the best of your nation after you is Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq.""**"
"14317","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions were swimming in a pool, so the Prophet ﷺ said let each man of you swim to his companion. So each man among them swam to his companion, and there remained the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr, so the Prophet ﷺ swam to Abū Bakr until he embraced him and said, I am to my companion, I am to my companion. > **""Let each man of you swim to his companion.""** > **""I am to my companion, I am to my companion.""**"
"14318","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said had he taken a close beloved friend he would have taken Abū Bakr as a close beloved friend. > **""Had I taken a close beloved friend, I would have taken Abū Bakr as a close beloved friend.""**"
"14319","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said had he taken a close beloved friend he would have taken Abū Bakr as a close beloved friend, but the brotherhood of Islam is superior. > **""Had I taken a close beloved friend, I would have taken Abū Bakr as a close beloved friend, but the brotherhood of Islam is superior.""**"
"14320","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that Abū Bakr spoke against ʿUmar with something, then said, seek forgiveness for me O my brother, so ʿUmar became angry. He said that several times and ʿUmar remained angry, so he mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ and they came to him and sat. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, your brother asks you to seek forgiveness for him and you do not do it. He said, by Him Who sent you with the truth as a Prophet, there is no time he asks me except that I seek forgiveness for him, and there is no creature of Allah more beloved to me after you than him. Abū Bakr said, and I, by Him Who sent you with the truth, there is none after you more beloved to me than him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, do not harm me regarding my companion, for Allah عز و جل sent me with guidance and the true religion, so you said you lie, while Abū Bakr said you speak the truth. Were it not that Allah عز و جل named him a companion, I would have taken him as a close beloved friend, but it is brotherhood for Allah. Behold, close every small door except the small door of Ibn Abī Quḥāfa. > **""Your brother asks you to seek forgiveness for him, will you not do it?""** > **""Do not harm me regarding my companion, for Allah عز و جل sent me with guidance and the true religion, so you said you lie, while Abū Bakr said you speak the truth. Were it not that Allah عز و جل named him a companion, I would have taken him as a close beloved friend, but it is brotherhood for Allah. Behold, close every small door except the small door of Ibn Abī Quḥāfa.""**"
"14321","Rabīʿa al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه said he used to serve the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he gave him land and gave Abū Bakr land. Worldly interest came and they differed over a cluster of dates. Abū Bakr said it is within my boundary, while he said it is within his boundary. There occurred words between him and Abū Bakr, so Abū Bakr uttered a word he disliked and regretted it, then said to him, O Rabīʿa, return the like of it upon me so that there be retaliation. He said, I will not do it. Abū Bakr said, you shall do it or I shall seek the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against you. He said, I shall not do it. He abandoned the land, and Abū Bakr went to the Prophet ﷺ while he followed behind him. Some people of Aslam came and said, may Allah have mercy upon Abū Bakr, for what does he seek the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against you while he is the one who said to you what he said. He said, do you know who this is. This is Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, the second of the two, the elder of the Muslims. Beware lest he turn and see you aiding me against him and become angry, then come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who becomes angry for his anger, then Allah becomes angry for their anger, and Rabīʿa perishes. They said, what do you command us. He said, go back. Abū Bakr went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he followed him alone until he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he related the account as it was. The Prophet ﷺ raised his head to him and said, O Rabīʿa, what is between you and al-Ṣiddīq. He said, O Messenger of Allah, it was so and so, he said to me a word I disliked, then said to me say as I said so that there be retaliation. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, yes, do not return upon him, but say, may Allah forgive you O Abū Bakr. So he said it, and Abū Bakr wept and went forth. > **""O Rabīʿa, return the like of it upon me so that there be retaliation.""** > **""You shall do it or I shall seek the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against you.""** > **""O Rabīʿa, what is between you and al-Ṣiddīq?""** > **""Yes, do not return upon him, but say: May Allah forgive you, O Abū Bakr.""**"
"14322","Kaʿb ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه said his last contact with your Prophet ﷺ was five nights before his passing, and he heard him say there was no Prophet except that he had a close beloved friend in his nation, and his close beloved friend is Abū Bakr ibn Abī Quḥāfa, while Allah has taken your companion as a close beloved friend. > **""There was no Prophet except that he had a close beloved friend in his nation, and my close beloved friend is Abū Bakr ibn Abī Quḥāfa, while Allah has taken your companion as a close beloved friend.""**"
"14323","Abū Wāqid رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said had he taken a close beloved friend he would have taken Ibn Abī Quḥāfa, but your companion is the close beloved friend of Allah عز و جل. > **""Had I taken a close beloved friend I would have taken Ibn Abī Quḥāfa, but your companion is the close beloved friend of Allah عز و جل.""**"
"14324","Abū Umāma رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah took him as a close beloved friend just as He took Ibrāhīm as a close beloved friend, and his close beloved friend is Abū Bakr. > **""Allah took me as a close beloved friend just as He took Ibrāhīm as a close beloved friend, and my close beloved friend is Abū Bakr.""**"
"14325","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is none more beneficent to him in deed than Abū Bakr. He married him to his daughter and brought him out to the abode of emigration. Had he taken a close beloved friend he would have taken Abū Bakr, but it is brotherhood and affection until the Day of Resurrection. > **""There is none more beneficent to me in deed than Abū Bakr. He married me to his daughter and brought me out to the abode of emigration. Had I taken a close beloved friend I would have taken Abū Bakr, but it is brotherhood and affection until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"14326","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is none greater to him in favour than Abū Bakr. He consoled him with himself and his wealth, and married him to his daughter. > **""There is none greater to me in favour than Abū Bakr. He consoled me with himself and his wealth.""** > **""And he married me to his daughter.""**"
"14327","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wished to dispatch Muʿādh to Yemen, he consulted some of his Companions including Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥa, al-Zubayr and Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنهم. Abū Bakr said, had you not consulted us we would not have spoken. He said, in what has not been revealed to me I am as one of you. The people spoke, each giving his opinion. He said, what do you see O Muʿādh. He said, I see what Abū Bakr said. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah above His heaven dislikes that Abū Bakr err. > **""In what has not been revealed to me I am as one of you.""** > **""What do you see, O Muʿādh?""** > **""Allah above His heaven dislikes that Abū Bakr err.""**"
"14328","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ consulted Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, so they counselled him, and Abū Bakr was correct."
"14329","ʿUqba ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when he was taken up to the heaven he entered the Garden of Eden, and there fell into his hand an apple. When he placed it in his hands it split open revealing a wide-eyed ḥūrī, pleasing, the lashes of her eyes like the foreparts of vultures' wings. He said to her, for whom are you. She said, I am for the Khalīfa after you. > **""When I was taken up to the heaven I entered the Garden of Eden, and there fell into my hand an apple. When I placed it in my hands it split open revealing a wide-eyed ḥūrī, pleasing, the lashes of her eyes like the foreparts of vultures' wings. I said to her: For whom are you? She said: I am for the Khalīfa after you.""**"
"14330","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed behind Abū Bakr."
"14331","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said a man shall enter Paradise and no household nor chamber in Paradise shall remain except that they say welcome, welcome, to us, to us. Abū Bakr said, O Messenger of Allah, what is the reward of this man on that day. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, yes, you are he O Abū Bakr. > **""A man shall enter Paradise and no household nor chamber in Paradise shall remain except that they say: Welcome, welcome, to us, to us.""** > **""Yes, you are he, O Abū Bakr.""**"
"14332","Ṣila ibn Zufar said when Abū Bakr was mentioned before ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, he would say the forerunner, mentioning the forerunner. He said, by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, they never vied toward any good except that Abū Bakr outstripped them to it. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, we never vied toward any good except that Abū Bakr outstripped us to it.""**"
"14333","Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl said ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه addressed them and said, O people, inform me who is the bravest of people. They said, you O Commander of the Believers. He said, as for him, he never faced anyone in single combat except that he took his due from him, but inform him of the bravest of people. They said, we do not know, who is it. He said, Abū Bakr. On the day of Badr they made a shelter for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said who shall be with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lest any of the polytheists fall upon him. By Allah, none drew near him except Abū Bakr, brandishing a sword over the head of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, none falling upon him except that he fell upon him. This is the bravest of people. ʿAlī said, he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when Quraysh seized him, one pushing him away and another shaking him violently, saying, are you the one who made the gods one God. By Allah, none of them drew near except Abū Bakr, striking this one, defending and shaking off that one, saying, woe to you, would you kill a man for saying my Lord is Allah. Then ʿAlī lifted a cloak that was upon him, then wept until his beard became wet, then said, I adjure you by Allah, is the believer of the household of Pharaoh better or Abū Bakr. The people fell silent, so he said, will you not answer me. By Allah, an hour of Abū Bakr is better than the like of the believer of Pharaoh's household. That was a man who concealed his faith, while this is a man who proclaimed his faith. > **""O people, inform me who is the bravest of people?""** > **""Woe to you! Would you kill a man for saying: My Lord is Allah?""** > **""I adjure you by Allah: Is the believer of Pharaoh's household better, or Abū Bakr? By Allah, an hour of Abū Bakr is better than the like of the believer of Pharaoh's household. That was a man who concealed his faith, while this is a man who proclaimed his faith.""**"
"14334","Shaqīq said it was said to ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, will you not appoint a successor. He said, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not appoint a successor so that he should appoint a successor over you. If Allah تبارك وتعالى intends good for the people, He shall gather them upon the best of them, just as He gathered them after their Prophet upon the best of them. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not appoint a successor so that I should appoint a successor over you. If Allah تبارك وتعالى intends good for the people, He shall gather them upon the best of them, just as He gathered them after their Prophet upon the best of them.""**"
"14335","Usayd ibn Ṣafwān, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said when Abū Bakr passed away he was wrapped in a cloth, Madīna shook with weeping and was stunned as on the day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken. ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه came hastening, seeking reward, saying, today the succession of Prophethood has ceased, until he stood at the door of the house in which Abū Bakr lay and said, may Allah have mercy upon you O Abū Bakr. You were the foremost of the people in Islam, sincerest in faith, firmest in certainty, most fearful of Allah, greatest in detachment, most protective of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, most devoted to Islam, most trustworthy toward his Companions, finest in companionship, most excellent in virtues, most abundant in precedence, highest in degree, nearest to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and most resembling him in guidance, character and demeanour, most reliable with him, noblest in rank and most honoured with him. May Allah reward you on behalf of Islam, His Messenger and the Muslims with good. You confirmed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the people belied him, so Allah named you al-Ṣiddīq in His Book when He said, the one who came with the truth is Muḥammad ﷺ and the one who confirmed it is Abū Bakr. You consoled him when they were miserly, stood with him when they sat back, accompanied him in hardship with the noblest companionship, upon whom tranquillity was sent down, his companion in the emigration and in places of distress. You succeeded him over his nation with the finest succession when the people apostatized, so you upheld the religion of Allah as no Khalīfa of a Prophet ever upheld it. You leapt forth when his Companions weakened, rose when they flagged, clung to the way of his Messenger despite the hypocrites and to the rage of the disbelievers. You upheld the affair when they failed, and proceeded with the light of Allah when they halted, until the cause was established and the religion prevailed. > **""Today the succession of Prophethood has ceased.""** > **""May Allah have mercy upon you, O Abū Bakr. You were the foremost of the people in Islam, sincerest in faith, firmest in certainty, most fearful of Allah, and most devoted to Islam. May Allah reward you on behalf of Islam, His Messenger and the Muslims with good.""**"
"14336","ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Sadūsī from his father said it reached ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها that some people disparaged Abū Bakr, so she sent for a group of them, let down her curtains, rebuked and reproached them, and said, my father, and what of my father. You cannot reach him with your hands. Far from it, by Allah. He was a towering mountain and an extended shade. He succeeded, by Allah, when you belied, and outstripped when you lagged, like the outstripping of a racehorse when it masters the distance. A youth of Quraysh in his youth, and a cave in his old age. He freed their captives, enriched their destitute, mended their breach and gathered their disarray, until hearts adorned him with love. Then he advanced firmly in his religion and his firmness for Allah did not depart, until he took a mosque in his courtyard in which he revived what the adherents of falsehood had caused to die. He was, may Allah have mercy upon him, abundant in tears, restrained in the inner self, choked with sobbing. The women and children of Makka gathered to him mocking and ridiculing him, while Allah mocks them and prolongs them in their transgression wandering blindly. The men of Quraysh deemed that grave, so they bent their bows, fitted their arrows and made him a target, yet they neither blunted his edge nor broke his spear. He continued upon his course until religion took its firm hold, cast its weight, its pegs were fixed, and people entered it in crowds, in successive groups from every faction. Then Allah chose for His Prophet what is with Him, so when Allah عز و جل took him, Satan spread his canopy, set his snares, extended his ropes and came with his horse and foot. The rope of Islam shook, its covenant fell into confusion, its people surged, its firmly twisted cord became undone strands, calamities coveted, and men thought their ambitions had succeeded, far from the time of return. Yet al-Ṣiddīq was among them, so he stood bare and girded, raised his side, gathered his strength, restored the ease of Islam after heedlessness, gathered its disarray by his resolve, revived what had been neglected, and established the order until apostasy was repelled and the religion stood upright."
"14337","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها said when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, the Arabs apostatized and hypocrisy raised its head. There befell her father what, had it befallen firmly rooted mountains, would have crushed them. She said, they did not differ in a single point except that her father flew to it with its full share and spearhead. Then she mentioned ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه and said, by Allah, he was vigorous, unique without peer, who had prepared for affairs their equals. Al-Riyāshī explained that a masterful man without like is called unique, solitary and peerless, and cited verses of a poet describing a veiled rider upon a swift she-camel outstripping all, overcoming every hero he meets, and verses of al-Aṣmaʿī on sleep overcoming him."
"14338","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Abū Bakr over the ḥajj, then sent Barāʾa with ʿAlī. Abū Bakr said, O Messenger of Allah, have you found something against me. He said, no, you are my companion in the cave and at the Pond. > **""No. You are my companion in the cave and at the Pond.""**"
"14339","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه said he brought Abū Quḥāfa to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he said, why did you not leave the elder until I come to him. He said, rather he is more deserving to come to you. He said, we preserve him on account of his son's favours with us. > **""Why did you not leave the elder until I come to him?""** > **""We preserve him on account of his son's favours with us.""**"
"14340","ʿUrwa said Abū Bakr freed seven of those who were tortured for Allah. Among them were Bilāl رضي الله عنه and ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayra رضي الله عنه."
"14341","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said there was revealed concerning Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq: And none has with him any favour to be repaid, except seeking the Face of his Lord, the Most High, and he shall surely be pleased, Surah al-Layl 19-21."
"14342","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said no wealth of anyone benefited them as the wealth of Abū Bakr benefited them. > **""No wealth of anyone benefited us as the wealth of Abū Bakr benefited us.""**"
"14343","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها, in the account of the Ifk, reported that Ḥassān ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه declared himself false with verses: Chaste, dignified, never accused of doubt, who rises in the morning hungry for the flesh of the heedless, meaning she backbites none. If I had said what you claimed, my fingertips would not carry my whip to me. How, while my love and support while I live are for the household of the Messenger of Allah, the adornment of gatherings. Would I revile the best of people as husband, father and self. I would surely have alighted in the worst of abodes. > **""Chaste, dignified, never accused of doubt, who rises in the morning hungry for the flesh of the heedless. How, while my love and support while I live are for the household of the Messenger of Allah, the adornment of gatherings. Would I revile the best of people as husband, father and self? I would surely have alighted in the worst of abodes.""**"
"14344","Mūsā ibn ʿUqba said there are not known four who met the Prophet ﷺ with their sons except these four: Abū Quḥāfa, Abū Bakr, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and Abū ʿAtīq ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and the name of Abū ʿAtīq is Muḥammad."
"14345","Umm Salama رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said in the heaven are two angels, one commands severity and the other commands gentleness, and each is correct: Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl. And two Prophets, one commands severity and the other commands gentleness, and each is correct, and he mentioned Ibrāhīm and Nūḥ عليهما السلام. And he has two companions, one commands severity and the other commands gentleness, and each is correct, and he mentioned Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. > **""In the heaven are two angels, one commands severity and the other commands gentleness, and each is correct: Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl. And two Prophets, one commands severity and the other commands gentleness, and each is correct. And I have two companions, one commands severity and the other commands gentleness, and each is correct.""**"
"14346","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah عز و جل strengthened him with four supporting ministers. They said, O Messenger of Allah, who are these four. He said, two from the people of the heaven and two from the people of the earth. He was asked who are the two from the people of the heaven. He said, Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl. They said, who are the two from the people of the earth. He said, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. > **""Allah عز و جل strengthened me with four supporting ministers: two from the people of the heaven and two from the people of the earth: Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar.""**"
"14347","Abū Arwā al-Dawsī رضي الله عنه said he was with the Prophet ﷺ when Abū Bakr and ʿUmar came forward, so he said, praise be to Allah Who strengthened him through them both. > **""Praise be to Allah Who strengthened me through you both.""**"
"14348","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, praise be to Allah Who strengthened him through them both, and were it not that they differ before him he would not have differed from them. > **""Praise be to Allah Who strengthened me through you both. Were it not that you differ before me, I would not have differed from you.""**"
"14349","Ibn ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهم, concerning His saying, and the righteous believers, Surah al-Taḥrīm 4, said it was revealed concerning Abū Bakr and ʿUmar."
"14350","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every Prophet has an inner circle from his nation, and his inner circle from his Companions are Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. > **""Every Prophet has an inner circle from his nation, and my inner circle from my Companions are Abū Bakr and ʿUmar.""**"
"14351","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wished to send a man for a need that concerned him, while Abū Bakr was at his right and ʿUmar at his left. ʿAlī said, what prevents you from these two. He said, how can he send these two when they are to religion as hearing and sight to the head. > **""How can I send these two when they are to religion as hearing and sight to the head.""**"
"14352","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to take the Qurʾān from four: Ibn Umm ʿAbd, Muʿādh, Ubayy and Sālim. He had intended to send them to the nations just as ʿĪsā ibn Maryam sent the disciples among the Children of Isrāʾīl. A man said to him, O Messenger of Allah, where are you regarding Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, there is no sufficiency without them. Their parable in religion is as the parable of hearing and sight. > **""Take the Qurʾān from four: Ibn Umm ʿAbd, Muʿādh, Ubayy and Sālim. I had intended to send them to the nations just as ʿĪsā ibn Maryam sent the disciples among the Children of Isrāʾīl.""** > **""There is no sufficiency without them. Their parable in religion is as the parable of hearing and sight.""**"
"14353","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he had intended to send Muʿādh ibn Jabal, Sālim the freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfa, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb and Ibn Masʿūd to the nations just as ʿĪsā ibn Maryam sent the disciples. A man said, will you not send Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, for they are more effective. He said, he has no sufficiency without them. Their station in religion is the station of hearing and sight. > **""I had intended to send Muʿādh ibn Jabal, Sālim the freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfa, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb and Ibn Masʿūd to the nations just as ʿĪsā ibn Maryam sent the disciples.""** > **""I have no sufficiency without them. Their station in religion is the station of hearing and sight.""**"
"14354","Ḥudhayfa ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he had intended to send teachers among the people just as ʿĪsā ibn Maryam sent the disciples to the Children of Isrāʾīl. It was said, where are you regarding Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, will you not send them both. He said, they are to religion as the head to the body. > **""I had intended to send teachers among the people just as ʿĪsā ibn Maryam sent the disciples to the Children of Isrāʾīl.""** > **""They are to religion as the head to the body.""**"
"14355","Ibn Ghannām رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, had you both agreed in counsel he would not have opposed them. > **""Had you both agreed in counsel, I would not have opposed you.""**"
"14356","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to follow the two after him, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, for they are Allah's extended rope, and whoever holds fast to them has held fast to the firmest handhold that shall never break. > **""Follow the two after me: Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, for they are Allah's extended rope, and whoever holds fast to them has held fast to the firmest handhold that shall never break.""**"
"14357","Abū Juḥayfa رضي الله عنه said he entered upon ʿAlī in his house and said, O best of people after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, gently, woe to you O Abū Juḥayfa, shall he not inform you of the best of people after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They are Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. Woe to you O Abū Juḥayfa, love for him and hatred of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar do not gather in the heart of a believer. > **""Shall I not inform you of the best of people after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. Love for me and hatred of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar do not gather in the heart of a believer.""**"
"14358","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said they would sit with the Prophet ﷺ as if birds were upon their heads. None of them spoke except Abū Bakr and ʿUmar."
"14359","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar that these two are the masters of the elderly of the people of Paradise from the first and the last. > **""These two are the masters of the elderly of the people of Paradise from the first and the last.""**"
"14360","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are the masters of the elderly of the people of Paradise from the first and the last. Do not inform them, O ʿAlī. > **""Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are the masters of the elderly of the people of Paradise from the first and the last. Do not inform them, O ʿAlī.""**"
"14361","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ with the like of a ḥadīth whose text is that the Prophet ﷺ said Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are the masters of the elderly of Paradise from the first and the last, except the Prophets and Messengers. Do not inform them, O ʿAlī. > **""Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are the masters of the elderly of Paradise from the first and the last, except the Prophets and Messengers. Do not inform them, O ʿAlī.""**"
"14362","Abū Hurayra رضي الله عنه said the Prophet ﷺ came out between Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and said, thus shall they be raised on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Thus shall we be raised on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"14363","It is reported from ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه that he said: whoever gives precedence over Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما to anyone among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has thereby belittled the Muhājirīn and the Anṣār and twelve thousand of the Companions of Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"14364","It is reported from Ibn Abī Ḥāzim that a man came to ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn and asked: what was the standing of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar with the Prophet ﷺ. He replied: like their standing at this very hour, meaning as they are now, honoured and buried beside him."
"14365","It is reported from ʿAlī رضي الله عنه that he said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went ahead foremost, and Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه prayed next, and ʿUmar رضي الله عنه came third, then a tribulation struck us violently — or he said, a tribulation befell us — and Allah pardons whom He wills, intending thereby humility on his part."
"14366","In a narration from him it is reported: on the day of Baṣra, when ʿAlī رضي الله عنه had prevailed, a man delivered a khuṭbah, whereupon ʿAlī said: this is the sharp, eloquent orator. Then the narrator mentioned the ḥadīth in a similar manner."
"14367","It is reported from Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed the people of the highest degrees will be seen by those below them just as stars are seen on the horizon of the sky, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are among them, and how excellent they are.""**"
"14368","It is reported from Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: > **""Indeed a man from the people of ʿIlliyyīn shall look down upon the people of Paradise as though he were a brilliant star, and indeed Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are among them, and how excellent they are.""**"
"14369","It is reported from Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ said to a man: > **""When I have died, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar have died, then if you are able to die, then die.""**"
"14370","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that he said: Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه never sat upon the minbar in the sitting-place of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he met Allah, and ʿUmar رضي الله عنه never sat in the sitting-place of Abū Bakr until he met Allah, and ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه never sat in the sitting-place of ʿUmar until he met Allah."
"14371","It is reported from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه addressed the people one day from the minbar of Madīnah and said in his sermon: indeed in the Gardens of Eden there is a palace with five hundred gates, upon every gate five thousand of the wide-eyed maidens, which none shall enter except a prophet. Then he turned toward the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: congratulations to you, O possessor of this grave. Then he said: or a ṣiddīq. Then he turned toward the grave of Abū Bakr and said: congratulations to you, O Abū Bakr. Then he said: or a martyr. Then he turned to himself and said: and how shall martyrdom come to you, O ʿUmar. Then he said: indeed the One who brought me out of Makkah to the emigration to Madīnah is able to drive martyrdom to me. Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: so Allah drove it to him at the hand of the worst of His creation, a slave owned by al-Mughīrah."
"14372","It is reported from Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه that Uḥud trembled while upon it were the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and ʿUthmān رضي الله عنهم, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Be firm, O Uḥud, for there is none upon you except a Prophet, or a Ṣiddīq, or two martyrs.""**"
"14373","It is reported from Buraydah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting upon Ḥirāʾ, and with him were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and ʿUthmān, when the mountain moved, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Be firm, O Ḥirāʾ, for there is none upon you except a Prophet, or a Ṣiddīq, or a martyr.""**"
"14374","It is reported from Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him, saying: go until you come to Abū Bakr and you shall find him in his house sitting with his knees drawn up, and say to him: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveys salām to you and says to you: receive glad tidings of Paradise. Then go until you come to the mountain pass and you shall meet ʿUmar there upon a donkey with his bald forepart gleaming, and say to him: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveys salām to you and says to you: receive glad tidings of Paradise. Then go until you come to the market and you shall meet ʿUthmān there buying and selling, and say to him: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveys salām to you and says to you: receive glad tidings of Paradise after a severe trial. So I went to Abū Bakr and found him in his house sitting with knees drawn as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said, and I conveyed the salām and the tidings, and he asked where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was, and I named such-and-such a place, and he rose toward him. Then I came to the pass and there was ʿUmar upon a donkey with his baldness gleaming as described, and I conveyed the salām and the tidings, and he asked where the Messenger was, and I named the place, and he set off. Then I went to the market and met ʿUthmān buying and selling as described, and I conveyed the salām and the tidings of Paradise after severe trial, and he asked where the Messenger was, and I named the place, and he too set off toward him: > **""Go until you come to Abū Bakr and you shall find him in his house sitting with knees drawn up, and say to him: the Messenger of Allah conveys salām to you and says to you: receive glad tidings of Paradise. Then go until you come to the pass and you shall meet ʿUmar there upon a donkey, and say to him: receive glad tidings of Paradise. Then go until you come to the market and you shall meet ʿUthmān buying and selling, and say to him: receive glad tidings of Paradise after a severe trial.""**"
"14375","It is reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما that he was with the Prophet ﷺ in a walled garden among the gardens of Madīnah when a man came and sought permission to enter, and the Prophet ﷺ said: stand and admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. So I stood and admitted him, and behold it was Abū Bakr, and I gave him the tidings, and he kept praising Allah until he sat. Then another man came and sought permission, and he said: stand and admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. So I admitted him, and behold it was ʿUmar, and I gave him the tidings, and he kept praising Allah until he sat. Then one with a faint voice came and sought permission, and he said: stand and admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise despite a trial that shall afflict him. So I admitted him, and behold it was ʿUthmān, and I gave him the tidings of Paradise upon a trial that would afflict him, and he said: O Allah, patience, until he sat. I asked: O Messenger of Allah, then where am I. He said: > **""Stand and admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. You are with your father.""**"
"14376","It is reported from Nāfiʿ ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥārith رضي الله عنه that he went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he entered a walled garden, and he said: hold the door for me. Then he went and sat upon the raised rim and let his feet dangle into the well, and the door was knocked. I asked: who is this. He said: Abū Bakr. I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Abū Bakr. He said: admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. So I admitted him and gave him the tidings, and he entered and sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon the rim and let his feet dangle into the well. Then the door was knocked, and I asked who it was, and he said: ʿUmar. I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is ʿUmar. He said: admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. So I admitted him and gave him the tidings, and he sat likewise. Then the door was knocked, and I asked who it was, and he said: ʿUthmān. I said: O Messenger of Allah, this is ʿUthmān. He said: > **""Admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise, along with it a trial.""** So I admitted him and gave him the tidings, and he sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon the rim and let his feet dangle into the well."
"14377","It is reported from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at al-Aswāf with Bilāl رضي الله عنه beside him, and he let his feet dangle into the well and uncovered his thighs. Then Abū Bakr came seeking permission, and he said: O Bilāl, admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. So Abū Bakr entered and sat to the right of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and let his feet dangle into the well and uncovered his thighs. Then ʿUmar came seeking permission, and he said: admit him, O Bilāl, and give him glad tidings of Paradise. So he entered and sat to the left of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likewise. Then ʿUthmān came seeking permission, and he said: > **""Admit him, O Bilāl, and give him glad tidings of Paradise despite a trial that shall afflict him.""** So ʿUthmān entered and sat facing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and let his feet dangle into the well and uncovered his thighs."
"14378","It is reported from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out visiting Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Anṣārī, whose dwelling was at al-Aswāf, and his wife spread for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a mat beneath a cluster of palm-trees, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat and they sat with him. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: there shall now appear before you a man from the people of Paradise. Then Abū Bakr appeared. Then he said: there shall appear before you a man from the people of Paradise. Then ʿUmar appeared. Then he said: > **""There shall now appear before you a man from the people of Paradise.""** Then ʿUthmān appeared."
"14379","It is reported from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: there shall emerge upon you from beneath this cluster of trees a man from the people of Paradise. Then Abū Bakr emerged, and they congratulated him with what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said. Then he tarried a little and said: there shall emerge from beneath this cluster a man from the people of Paradise. Then ʿUmar emerged, and they congratulated him likewise. Then he said: there shall emerge upon you from beneath this cluster a man from the people of Paradise; O Allah, if You will, make him ʿAlī, repeating it three times. Then ʿAlī رضي الله عنه emerged, may the prayers of Allah and His peace be upon them: > **""There shall emerge upon you from beneath this cluster a man from the people of Paradise. O Allah, if You will, make him ʿAlī.""**"
"14380","In a narration of it the wording is: > **""O Allah, make him ʿAlī.""**"
"14381","In a narration: I walked with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to a woman of the Anṣār, and she slaughtered a sheep for him — then he mentioned the like of the preceding ḥadīth about the emergence of Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, and ʿAlī as men of Paradise."
"14382","It is reported from Abū Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered a walled garden one day and said: there shall now enter upon you a man from the people of Paradise. Then Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq entered. Then he said: there shall now enter upon you a man from the people of Paradise. Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb entered. Then he said: > **""There shall now enter upon you a man from the people of Paradise. O Allah, make him ʿAlī.""** Then ʿAlī entered."
"14383","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said — with doubt from ʿAlī ibn Jamīl whether he said a tree or whatever tree is in Paradise — that there is no leaf upon it except that inscribed upon it is: > **""There is no deity but Allah, Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, ʿUmar al-Fārūq, ʿUthmān Dhū al-Nūrayn.""**"
"14384","It is reported from ʿAlī رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him and to Abū Bakr on the day of Badr: > **""With one of you is Jibrīl, and with the other is Mīkāʾīl, and Isrāfīl, a mighty angel, witnesses the fighting, or is in the ranks.""**"
"14385","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that he said: while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was alive we used to say: the best of this ummah after its Prophet are Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān, and the Prophet ﷺ would hear that and not disapprove of it. As far as we know, ʿUthmān never committed anything of the major sins, nor killed a soul without right; rather it is only this wealth — if he gives it to you, you are pleased, and if he gives it to Quraysh, you are angry. You wish only to be like Persia and Rome, who leave no amīr over them except that they kill him. In another wording: Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar, and ʿUthmān, then the people are equal, and that reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he did not disapprove of it against us."
"14386","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them one morning after sunrise and said: shortly before dawn I saw as though I were given the keys and the balances. As for the keys, they are these keys, and as for the balances, they are these with which weighing is done. Then I was placed in one pan and my ummah was placed in the other, and I outweighed them. Then Abū Bakr was brought and weighed against them and outweighed. Then ʿUmar was brought and weighed against them and outweighed. Then ʿUthmān was brought and weighed against them, then it was raised: > **""I saw shortly before dawn as though I were given the keys and the balances. Then I was placed in one pan and my ummah in the other, and I outweighed them. Then Abū Bakr was brought and outweighed them. Then ʿUmar was brought and outweighed them. Then ʿUthmān was brought and weighed against them, then it was raised.""**"
"14387","It is reported from Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I entered Paradise and heard before me a movement, and I asked what it was, and they said: Bilāl. Then I went on, and most of the people of Paradise were the poor of the Muhājirīn and the offspring of the Muslims, and I saw none therein fewer than women and the wealthy. It was said to me: as for the wealthy, they are detained here, being reckoned and sifted, and as for women, the two red things — gold and silk — distracted them. Then we exited from one of the eight gates of Paradise, and when I was at the gate I was brought a balance, and I was placed in one pan and my ummah placed in the other, and I outweighed them. Then Abū Bakr was brought and placed in one pan and my whole ummah placed in the other, and Abū Bakr outweighed. Then ʿUmar was brought and placed in one pan and my whole ummah brought and placed, and ʿUmar outweighed. Then my ummah was presented to me man by man, and they kept passing, but I found ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf delayed, and he came after despair. I said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. He said: by my father and mother, O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with the truth, I did not reach you until I thought I would never reach you except after grey hairs, on account of my abundant wealth, for I was reckoned and sifted: > **""I entered Paradise and heard before me a movement, and they said: Bilāl. Then I was placed in one pan and my ummah in the other, and I outweighed them. Then Abū Bakr was placed in one pan and my whole ummah in the other, and Abū Bakr outweighed. Then ʿUmar was placed in one pan and my whole ummah in the other, and ʿUmar outweighed.""**"
"14388","It is reported from Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I was shown that I was placed in one pan and my ummah in the other, and I balanced them. Then Abū Bakr was placed in one pan and my ummah in the other, and he balanced them. Then ʿUmar was placed in one pan and my ummah in the other, and he balanced them. And ʿUthmān was placed in one pan and my ummah in the other, and he balanced them. Then the balance was raised.""**"
"14389","It is reported from ʿArfajah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in Fajr prayer, then said: > **""My Companions were weighed last night; Abū Bakr was weighed and outweighed, then ʿUmar was weighed and outweighed, then ʿUthmān was weighed and outweighed.""**"
"14390","It is reported from Usāmah ibn Sharīk رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said one day: > **""My Companions were weighed last night, and Abū Bakr outweighed, then ʿUmar outweighed, then ʿUthmān outweighed.""**"
"14391","It is reported from Anas رضي الله عنه that he said: the eldest in age of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq and Suhayl ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما."
"14392","It is reported from ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr recalled their births in her presence, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was older than Abū Bakr. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away at sixty-three years, and Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه passed away at sixty-three, after living two and a half years after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"14393","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that he said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away at sixty-five years, and Abū Bakr was of the same age."
"14394","It is reported from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه passed away at sixty-three years, and he was buried at night, and ʿUmar رضي الله عنه prayed over him."
"14395","It is reported from ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها that she said: Abū Bakr passed away on the night of Tuesday and was buried at night."
"14396","It is reported from al-Haytham ibn ʿImrān that he heard his grandfather say: Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه passed away while afflicted with a trace of consumption, and he ruled for two and a half years."
"14397","It is reported from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār that Abū Bakr was made caliph on the very day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, and he passed away in Jumādā al-Ākhirah in the year thirteen. Then he mentioned the ḥadīth."
"14398","It is reported from Ibn Isḥāq that he said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb ibn Nufayl ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Rabāḥ ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Qurṭ ibn Razāḥ ibn ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr ibn Mālik, surnamed Abū Ḥafṣ. His mother was Ḥantamah bint Hishām ibn al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Makhzūm, and the mother of Ḥantamah was al-Shifāʾ bint ʿAbd Qays ibn ʿAdī ibn Saʿīd ibn Sahm ibn ʿAmr ibn Haṣīṣ ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy."
"14399","It is reported from Ibn Shihāb that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz asked Abū Bakr ibn Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah: who was the first to write as from ʿAbdullāh, the Commander of the Believers. He said: al-Shifāʾ bint ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنها, who was among the first emigrant women, informed me that Labīd ibn Rabīʿah and ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim came to Madīnah and entered the mosque and found ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and said: O Ibn al-ʿĀṣ, seek permission for us to see the Commander of the Believers. He said: by Allah, you have hit his very name; he is the commander and we are the believers. So ʿAmr entered upon ʿUmar and said: peace be upon you, O Commander of the Believers. ʿUmar asked: what is this. He said: you are the commander and we are the believers. So writing in that form began from that day."
"14400","It is reported from ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb mounted a horse and spurred it, so his thigh became uncovered, and the people of Najrān saw upon his thigh a black mole and said: this is the one we find in our Book, that he shall expel us from our land."
"14401","It is reported from Zirr that he said: I was in Madīnah when a man appeared, brown-skinned, left-handed and ambidextrous, bulky, such that when he overlooked the people it was as though he were upon a mount — and behold, it was ʿUmar رضي الله عنه."
"14402","It is reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn Hilāl that he said: I saw ʿUmar as a bulky man, as though he were from the men of Sadūs."
"14403","It is reported from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab that he said: ʿUmar was bald, severely bald."
"14404","It is reported from ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, strengthen Islām through ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, or through Abū Jahl ibn Hishām.""** So Allah assigned the supplication of His Messenger ﷺ to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and built Islām upon him and demolished idols through him. In a wording: support Islām."
"14405","It is reported from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""O Allah, strengthen Islām through ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.""**"
"14406","It is reported from Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated on Thursday evening, saying: > **""O Allah, strengthen Islām through ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, or through ʿAmr ibn Hishām.""** And on Friday morning ʿUmar embraced Islām."
"14407","It is reported from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه that he said: I went out seeking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ before I embraced Islām, and found that he had preceded me to the mosque. So I stood behind him, and he opened with Sūrat al-Ḥāqqah, and I marvelled at the composition of the Qurʾān. I said to myself: by Allah, this is a poet, as Quraysh say. Then he recited: Indeed it is the word of a noble Messenger, and it is not the word of a poet, little do you believe. I said to myself: a soothsayer. Then he recited: Nor the word of a soothsayer, little do you remember; a revelation from the Lord of the worlds, to the end of the sūrah. He said: so Islām took every hold in my heart."
"14408","It is reported from Thawbān رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, strengthen Islām through ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.""** He had beaten his sister in the early night while she was reciting: Read in the Name of thy Lord who created, until he thought he had killed her. Then he rose before dawn and heard her voice reciting: Read in the Name of thy Lord who created. He said: by Allah, this is neither poetry nor humming. So he went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and found Bilāl at the door, and pushed the door. Bilāl asked who it was, and he said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. Bilāl said: wait until I seek permission for you. Bilāl said: O Messenger of Allah, ʿUmar is at the door. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: if Allah wills good for ʿUmar, He shall admit him into the religion. He said to Bilāl: open. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seized him by his upper arms and shook him and said: what do you want and for what have you come. ʿUmar said to him: present to me what you call to. He said: bear witness that there is no deity but Allah alone without partner, and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. So ʿUmar embraced Islām on the spot, and he said: go forth."
"14409","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that when ʿUmar embraced Islām, the people, meaning the polytheists, said: the people have now been halved against us."
"14410","It is reported from ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, that he said: the Islām of ʿUmar was indeed an opening, and his emigration was indeed a victory, and his rule was a mercy. By Allah, we could not pray at the House until ʿUmar embraced Islām; when ʿUmar embraced Islām he fought them until they let us be, and we prayed."
"14411","In it there is a narration: we could not pray openly beside the Kaʿbah."
"14412","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that he said: the first to proclaim Islām openly was ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه."
"14413","It is reported from Aslam, the freedman of ʿUmar, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه said: would you like me to inform you of the beginning of my Islām. They said: yes. He said: I was the harshest of people against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. While I was on an intensely hot day upon one of the roads of Makkah, a man of Quraysh saw me and said: where are you going, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. I said: I intend this man. He said: O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, this affair has entered your own house while you speak thus. I said: what is that. He said: your sister has gone over to him. So I returned in anger and knocked upon her door. Now when any destitute person embraced Islām, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would attach one or two men to a man who would spend upon them, and he had attached two of his Companions to my sister's husband. So I knocked, and it was asked: who is this. I said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. They had been reading from a writing in their hands, and when they heard my voice they rose and hid, leaving the writing. When my sister opened the door for me I said: O enemy of herself, have you apostatized. Then I raised something and struck her head with it, and the woman wept and said: O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, do what you were going to do, for I have embraced Islām. Then I went on and sat upon the bed, and there was a leaf in the middle of the doorway. I said: what is this leaf here. She said to me: leave us alone, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, for you do not wash from major impurity nor purify yourself, and none touches this except the purified. I persisted with her until she gave it to me, and in it was: In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. When I read the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, I recalled whence it is derived, then I recollected myself and read: Glorify Allah whatever is in the heavens and the earth, and He is the Mighty, the Wise, until he reached His saying: Believe, and what follows in Sūrat al-Ḥadīd as far as is narrated."
"14414","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that when ʿUmar embraced Islām he asked: who is the most gossiping of people. They said: such-and-such a man. So he went to him and said: I have embraced Islām, but inform no one. He went out dragging his lower garment with its edge upon his shoulder, crying: beware, ʿUmar has apostatized. And I say: you lie; rather I have embraced Islām, while ʿUmar wore a shirt. Then a group of Quraysh rose against him, and they fought him and he fought them until he fell and they crowded over him. Then a man came and said: what is the matter with you and the man; do you think Banū ʿAdī will let you and your companion be, killing a man who has chosen for himself the following of Muḥammad ﷺ. So the people withdrew from him. He said: I asked my father who the man was, and he said: al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil al-Sahmī."
"14415","It is reported from ʿUmar رضي الله عنه that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, I shall not leave a gathering in which I sat in disbelief except that I proclaim Islām therein. So he came to the mosque, in which were the clans of Quraysh sitting in circles, and he proclaimed Islām and bore witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. Then the polytheists rose, beating him while he beat them, and when they outnumbered him a man rescued him. I asked ʿUmar who the man was who rescued him from the polytheists, and he said: that was al-ʿĀṣ ibn Wāʾil al-Sahmī."
"14416","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that when ʿUmar embraced Islām the polytheists said: the people have now been halved against us, and Allah the Mighty and Majestic revealed: O Prophet, Allah suffices thee and those of the believers who follow thee."
"14417","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ struck the chest of ʿUmar with his hand three times when he embraced Islām, saying three times: > **""O Allah, remove what is in the chest of ʿUmar of rancour, and replace it with faith.""**"
"14418","It is reported from ʿUmar ibn Rabīʿah that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb sent to Kaʿb al-Aḥbār and said: O Kaʿb, how do you find my description. He said: I find your description as a horn of iron. He asked: what is a horn of iron. He said: a strong commander whom no blamer's blame seizes concerning Allah. He asked: then what. He said: then after you there shall be a caliph whom an unjust party shall kill. He asked: then what. He said: then there shall be tribulation."
"14419","It is reported from al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ رضي الله عنه that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have praised my Lord, the Blessed and Exalted, with praises and eulogies, and thee as well. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: thy Lord, the Blessed and Exalted, loves praise; bring what thou hast used to extol thy Lord. So I began reciting to him, when a man sought permission, brown-skinned, tall, bald, left-handed and ambidextrous. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured me to silence for him, as Abū Salamah described, like one does to a cat. The man went out and spoke a while then left, then I resumed reciting, then he returned and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ again gestured me to silence. I asked: O Messenger of Allah, who is this for whom thou silencest me. He said: > **""This is a man who does not love falsehood. This is ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.""** In another wording a tall, aquiline-nosed man entered and he said to me: be silent."
"14420","In another narration from him also: until there entered a man broad between the shoulders. And he added: it was said to me: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and I knew by Allah afterwards that it would have been light upon him, had he heard me, not to speak to me until seizing me by the foot and dragging me to al-Baqīʿ."
"14421","It is reported from Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah has placed the truth upon the tongue of ʿUmar and his heart.""**"
"14422","It is reported from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed Allah has set the truth upon the tongue of ʿUmar and his heart; he speaks by it.""**"
"14423","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah has placed the truth upon the tongue of ʿUmar and his heart.""**"
"14424","It is reported from Bilāl رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah has placed the truth upon the tongue of ʿUmar and his heart.""**"
"14425","It is reported from Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah has placed the truth upon the tongue of ʿUmar and his heart.""**"
"14426","It is reported from ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There was no prophet except that in his ummah there was a teacher, or two teachers, and if there is any of them in my ummah, it is ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. Indeed the truth is upon the tongue of ʿUmar and his heart.""**"
"14427","It is reported from ʿAlī رضي الله عنه that he said: when the righteous are mentioned, then hasten to ʿUmar. We, the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ, did not deem it far-fetched that tranquillity speaks upon the tongue of ʿUmar."
"14428","It is reported from Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that he said: we did not deem it far-fetched that tranquillity speaks upon the tongue of ʿUmar."
"14429","It is reported from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb that he said: we used to relate that tranquillity descends upon the tongue of ʿUmar."
"14430","It is reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that he said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb excelled people in four matters. By mentioning the captives on the day of Badr, when he commanded their killing, whereupon Allah the Mighty and Majestic revealed: Had it not been for a decree from Allah that preceded, there would have touched you for what you took a great punishment. And by mentioning the veil, when he commanded the wives of the Prophet ﷺ to veil, and Zaynab رضي الله عنها said to him: O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, thou speakest to us while revelation descends in our houses, whereupon Allah revealed: And when you ask them for anything, ask them from behind a veil. And by the supplication of the Prophet ﷺ: O Allah, support Islām through ʿUmar. And by his opinion concerning Abū Bakr, for he was the first to pledge allegiance to him."
"14431","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy told that his father said to him: O my son, seek for me from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a garment of his garments, and shroud me in it, and command him to pray over me. So ʿAbdullāh said: O Messenger of Allah, thou knowest the standing of ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy, and he has commanded me to seek from thee a garment in which to shroud him and that thou pray over him. So he gave him a garment of his garments and intended to pray over him, whereupon ʿUmar said: O Messenger of Allah, thou knowest ʿAbdullāh and his hypocrisy, and Allah has forbidden thee to pray over him. He asked: and where. He said: If thou seekest forgiveness for them seventy times, Allah will never forgive them. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I shall increase over it. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic revealed: And never pray over any of them who dies, ever, and revealed: Equal it is for them whether thou seekest forgiveness for them or seekest not forgiveness for them, Allah will never forgive them. He said: and a man entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and prolonged sitting, and the Prophet ﷺ went out thrice that he might follow him, but he did not. Then ʿUmar entered and saw the dislike upon the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at his sitting, and said: perhaps thou hast harmed the Prophet ﷺ. So the man took heed and rose, and the Prophet ﷺ said: I rose thrice that thou mightest follow me, but thou didst not. He said: O Messenger of Allah, would that thou takest a veil, for thy wives are not like other women, and it is purer for their hearts. Then Allah revealed: O ye who believe, enter not the houses of the Prophet except when permission is given to you for a meal, not awaiting its preparation, to the end of the verse. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to ʿUmar, conveying to him what was revealed in his regard: > **""I shall increase over seventy.""**"
"14432","It is reported from ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O ʿUmar, Jibrīl came to me just now, and I said: O Jibrīl, inform me of the virtues of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb in heaven. He said: O Muḥammad, had I informed thee of the virtues of ʿUmar for as long as Nūḥ remained among his people — a thousand years less fifty — the virtues of ʿUmar would not be exhausted. And indeed ʿUmar is a good deed from among the good deeds of Abū Bakr.""**"
"14433","It is reported from ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Had there been a prophet after me, it would have been ʿUmar.""**"
"14434","It is reported from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Had Allah been sending a messenger after me, He would have sent ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb.""**"
"14435","It is reported from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to me and said: convey salām to ʿUmar, and say to him: indeed his pleasure is wise judgment, and indeed his anger is might.""**"
"14436","It is reported from Abū Wāʾil that ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, said: had the knowledge of ʿUmar been placed in one pan of the balance, and the knowledge of the people of the earth placed in the other, his knowledge would have outweighed theirs. Wakīʿ said: al-Aʿmash said: so I disapproved of that, and I went to Ibrāhīm and mentioned it to him, and he said: why dost thou disapprove of that; by Allah, ʿAbdullāh said what is even greater than that. He said: I deem that nine-tenths of knowledge departed on the day ʿUmar departed."
"14437","It is reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I saw in sleep that I was given a large cup filled with milk, and I drank until I was full, until I saw it flowing in my veins between the skin and the flesh. Then there remained a remainder, and I gave it to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. So interpret it. They said: O Prophet of Allah, this is knowledge which Allah gave thee and filled thee therewith, and there remained a remainder and thou gavest it to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. Then he said: > **""I saw in sleep that I was given a large cup filled with milk, and I drank until I was full, until I saw it flowing in my veins between the skin and the flesh. Then there remained a remainder and I gave it to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. You have judged rightly.""**"
"14438","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: ʿUmar رضي الله عنه was the most knowledgeable of us about Allah, the foremost reciter among us of the Book of Allah, and the most understanding of us in the religion of Allah."
"14439","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever hates ʿUmar hates him, and whoever loves ʿUmar loves him, and that Allah boasted to the people generally on the evening of ʿArafah, and boasted of ʿUmar specifically, and that Allah never sent a prophet except that there was a muhaddath in his ummah, and if there were one such in his ummah it would be ʿUmar. They asked how a muhaddath is, and he explained that the angels speak upon his tongue. > **""Whoever hates ʿUmar has hated me, and whoever loves ʿUmar has loved me. Indeed Allah boasted of the people generally on the evening of ʿArafah, and boasted of ʿUmar specifically. Allah never sent a prophet except that there was a muhaddath in his ummah, and if there were one among them in my ummah it would be ʿUmar. The angels speak upon his tongue.""**"
"14440","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Allah Almighty boasted to His angels of His servants generally on the evening of ʿArafah, and boasted of ʿUmar specifically. > **""Indeed Allah Almighty boasted to His angels of His servants generally on the evening of ʿArafah, and boasted of ʿUmar specifically.""**"
"14441","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said that one day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb رضي الله عنه and smiled at him, then asked him whether he knew why he had smiled at him. ʿUmar replied that Allah and His Messenger know best, whereupon he informed him that Allah had boasted generally of the people of ʿArafah and boasted of him specifically. > **""O son of al-Khattāb, do you know why I smiled at you? Indeed Allah Almighty boasted generally of the people of ʿArafah, and boasted of you specifically.""**"
"14442","Sudaysah, the freedwoman of Hafsah رضي الله عنها, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that since ʿUmar accepted Islam, Shaytan never met him except that he fell upon his face. > **""Indeed Shaytan has not met ʿUmar since he accepted Islam except that he fell upon his face.""**"
"14443","Sudaysah, the freedwoman of Hafsah رضي الله عنها, reported from Hafsah رضي الله عنها that she had vowed to beat the duff if the Prophet ﷺ returned from Makkah. While she was doing so, ʿUmar sought permission to enter, so she took the duff to the side of the house and covered it with a cloth, and said that the Prophet of Allah was most deserving of being held in awe, whereupon he affirmed that since ʿUmar accepted Islam, Shaytan never meets him except that he falls upon his face. > **""Indeed Shaytan does not meet ʿUmar since he accepted Islam except that he falls upon his face.""**"
"14444","Shaqīq ibn Salamah Abū Wāʾil reported that ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that Shaytan met a man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ and wrestled him. The Muslim threw him down and gripped him with his thumb. Shaytan asked him to release him and he would teach him a verse which no devil among them hears except that he turns away, but then refused to teach him. They wrestled again, and the Muslim threw him and gripped him, and Shaytan repeated his promise and again refused, until after the third bout he informed him that it is the verse in Sūrat al-Baqarah, Allah, there is no deity but He, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer, to its end. When ʿAbdullah was asked who that man was, he said who could it be but ʿUmar رضي الله عنه."
"14445","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported in another narration that a man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ met a man from the jinn and wrestled him, and the human threw him down. The jinnī asked for a rematch, and the human threw him again. The human remarked that he appeared frail and emaciated, as if his forearms were those of a dog, and asked whether all the jinn were like that. He swore that he was stout among them, and asked for a third bout, promising that if thrown he would teach him something beneficial. After being thrown a third time, he asked whether he recited Āyat al-Kursī, and when the man said yes, he said that it is never recited in a house except that Shaytan leaves it with a breaking wind like that of a donkey and does not enter it until morning. When a man asked Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān who that Companion was, ʿAbdullah frowned, turned to him, and said who could he be but ʿUmar رضي الله عنه."
"14446","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that last night he saw himself at a well drawing water. Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه came and drew while he was weak, and may Allah forgive him. Then ʿUmar came and it turned into a large bucket, and the people drank to satisfaction at the watering place. > **""O Abū Bakr, I saw myself last night at a well drawing water. Then you came and drew while you were weak, and may Allah forgive you. Then ʿUmar came and it turned into a large bucket, and the people drank to satisfaction at the watering place.""**"
"14447","Abū al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that last night while he was drawing water, black and dusty-coloured sheep came to him. Abū Bakr came and drew one or two buckets with weakness in his drawing, and may Allah forgive him. Then ʿUmar came and it turned into a large bucket, so he filled the basins and gave drink to those who came. He had never seen a strong man draw better than ʿUmar. He interpreted the black sheep as the Arabs and the dusty ones as the non-Arabs. > **""While I was drawing water last night, black and dusty sheep came to me. Then Abū Bakr came and drew a bucket or two, and in his drawing was weakness, and may Allah forgive him. Then ʿUmar came and it turned into a large bucket, so he filled the basins and gave drink to those who came. I have never seen a strong man draw better than ʿUmar. I interpreted the black as the Arabs and the dusty as the non-Arabs.""**"
"14448","Abū Wāʾil رحمه الله said that he never saw ʿUmar رضي الله عنه except that there was an angel between his eyes guiding him to the right course."
"14449","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه entered upon her and said that he feared his wealth would destroy him, for he was the wealthiest of Quraysh. She told him to spend, for she had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that among his Companions would be one who would not see him after parting from him. ʿAbd al-Rahmān went out and met ʿUmar and informed him of what Umm Salamah had said. ʿUmar entered upon her and adjured her by Allah whether he was among them. She said no, but that she would not declare anyone innocent after him. > **""Indeed among my Companions is one who will not see me after he parts from me.""**"
"14450","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that one day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them that it had been said to him to recite to ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb. He then called him and commanded him to learn the Quran when it was revealed so that he could recite it back to him. > **""Indeed it has been said to me: Recite to ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb.""**"
"14451","Qudāmah ibn Mazʿūn رضي الله عنه reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb رضي الله عنه caught up with ʿUthmān ibn Mazʿūn رضي الله عنه while each was upon his mount at the pass of al-Uthāyah near al-ʿArj, and ʿUmar's mount cut across ʿUthmān's mount while the mount of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had gone ahead of the caravan. ʿUthmān said that he had hurt him, O lock of fitnah. When the mounts reached easy ground, ʿUmar approached him and asked forgiveness and asked what name he had called him. ʿUthmān swore that he had not named him so himself, but that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who was ahead of the caravan leading the people, had named him so. One day he had passed while they were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he had said, pointing with his hand, that this one is the lock of fitnah, and that there would remain between them and fitnah a tightly locked door as long as he lived among them. > **""This is the lock of fitnah. There will remain between you and fitnah a tightly locked door as long as this one lives among you.""**"
"14452","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that he met ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb رضي الله عنه, who took his hand and squeezed it, for ʿUmar was a strong man. He told him to release his hand, O lock of fitnah. ʿUmar asked what lock of fitnah meant. He explained that one day he had come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was seated with people gathered around him, and he sat at the back of them, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no fitnah would afflict them as long as this one remained among them. > **""No fitnah will afflict you as long as this one is among you.""**"
"14453","Al-Hasan al-Basrī رحمه الله reported that ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀs married a woman from among the wives of ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb. He swore that he had not married her out of desire for wealth or children, but that he loved that she inform him about the night of ʿUmar رضي الله عنه. He asked her how ʿUmar's prayer at night was. She said that he would pray ʿIshāʾ, then order them to place a vessel of water covered near his head. He would wake in the night, place his hand in the water, wipe his face and hands, then remember Allah as long as Allah willed, repeating this several times until the hour arrived in which he would stand for his prayer. Ibn Buraydah asked who had narrated to him, and he said the daughter of ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀs had narrated it to him, whereupon he deemed her trustworthy."
"14454","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in a garden when Abū Bakr sought permission to enter. He said to admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. Then ʿUmar sought permission, and he said to admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise and martyrdom. Then ʿUthmān sought permission, and he said to admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise and martyrdom. > **""Admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise. Admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise and martyrdom. Admit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise and martyrdom.""**"
"14455","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ saw ʿUmar wearing a white garment and asked whether his garment was new or washed. The narrator did not recall what he answered. The Prophet ﷺ then said to wear new clothes, live praiseworthily, die a martyr, and may Allah grant him coolness of the eye in this world and the Hereafter. In the narration of Ahmad and al-Tabarānī there is the addition after that supplication that ʿUmar replied, and upon you too, O Messenger of Allah. > **""Wear new clothes, live praiseworthily, and die a martyr, and may Allah grant you coolness of the eye in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"14456","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillah رضي الله عنهما said that they were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb رضي الله عنه approached wearing a white shirt. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him whether his shirt was new or washed. He replied that it was washed. He then said to wear new clothes, live praiseworthily, die a martyr, and may Allah grant him coolness of the eye in this world and the Hereafter. > **""O ʿUmar, is this shirt of yours new or washed? Wear new clothes, live praiseworthily, and die a martyr, and may Allah grant you coolness of the eye in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"14457","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that while he was walking in Paradise, he came upon a palace and asked Jibrīl for whom it was, hoping it would be his, and he said it belonged to ʿUmar. Then after walking a while he came upon a palace better than the first, and again hoped it would be his, but Jibrīl said it belonged to ʿUmar, and that therein were maidens of the wide-eyed houris. He addressed Abū Hafs that nothing had prevented him from entering it except his jealousy. The eyes of ʿUmar filled with tears and he said that he would never feel jealousy toward him. > **""While I was walking in Paradise, I came upon a palace. I said: To whom does this belong, O Jibrīl? And I had hoped it would be mine. He said: It belongs to ʿUmar. Then I walked a while and I came upon a palace better than the first. I said: To whom does this belong, O Jibrīl? And I had hoped it would be mine. He said: It belongs to ʿUmar, and indeed therein are of the wide-eyed houris. O Abū Hafs, nothing prevented me from entering it except your jealousy.""**"
"14458","In another narration of the same account, the Prophet ﷺ said that he came upon a palace of gold. > **""And I came upon a palace of gold.""**"
"14459","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported the like of the narration about the palace, with the addition that ʿUmar is jealous, and he is more jealous than him, and Allah is more jealous than them both. > **""ʿUmar is jealous, and I am more jealous than him, and Allah is more jealous than us.""**"
"14460","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said that ʿUmar was indeed among the people of Paradise, for whatever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw in wakefulness or sleep was truth. He said that while he was in Paradise he saw a house therein, and asked to whom it belonged, and they said it belonged to ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb. > **""While I was in Paradise I saw a house therein. I said: To whom does this belong? They said: It belongs to ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb.""**"
"14461","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that ʿUmar is the lamp of the people of Paradise. > **""ʿUmar is the lamp of the people of Paradise.""**"
"14462","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Jibrīl, upon him be peace, told him that Islam would weep at the death of ʿUmar. > **""Jibrīl, upon him be peace, said to me: Islam will weep at the death of ʿUmar.""**"
"14463","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said that when Abū Luʾluʾah stabbed ʿUmar with two stabs, ʿUmar thought he might have some fault toward the people of which he was unaware. He summoned Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, whom he loved, kept near, and listened to, and said he wished to learn from a gathering of people whether this had arisen from them. Ibn ʿAbbās went out and passed no gathering except that they were weeping, and returned to ʿUmar saying that every gathering he had passed was weeping as if they had that day lost their firstborn children. ʿUmar asked who had killed him, and he replied Abū Luʾluʾah the Magian, the slave of al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah. Ibn ʿAbbās saw gladness on his face, and ʿUmar praised Allah that he had not been afflicted by one who professes there is no deity but Allah, reminding them that he had forbidden them from bringing any of the foreign captives to them but they had disobeyed him. He then asked for his brothers to be called, naming ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Talhah, al-Zubayr, ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn ʿAwf, and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqās رضي الله عنهم. Messengers were sent to them, and he placed his head in the lap of Ibn ʿUmar. When they arrived, Ibn ʿAbbās announced their presence, and ʿUmar said that he had considered the affair of the Muslims and found these six to be the heads and leaders of the people, and that this matter would only be among them, for as long as they remained upright the affairs of the people would remain upright, but if there were dissension it would be among them. When Ibn ʿAbbās heard him mention dissension and discord, he thought it would occur, for seldom did ʿUmar say anything except that he saw it realised. Then blood flowed heavily and they whispered among themselves until he feared they might pledge one of themselves, so he said that the Commander of the Faithful was still alive and there could not be two caliphs with one looking at the other. ʿUmar asked to be lifted, and they lifted him. He instructed them to consult for three days while Suhayb led the people in prayer. They asked whom to consult, and he said to consult the Muhājirūn and the Ansār and the chiefs of the armies present. He then called for a drink of milk, drank, and the whiteness of the milk came out of his two wounds, so he knew it was death. He said that now, if he owned the whole world, he would ransom himself with it from the terror of what lies ahead, though he had seen nothing but good, and praise belongs to Allah. Ibn ʿAbbās consoled him, recalling how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had supplicated that Allah strengthen the religion and the Muslims through him when they were in fear at Makkah, and how his Islam was a triumph through which Islam and the Messenger and his Companions prevailed, and how his emigration to Madinah was a victory, and how he was never absent from any battle witnessed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against the polytheists, and how the Messenger died pleased with him, and how he supported the Caliph after him upon the way of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until people entered Islam willingly and unwillingly, and how that Caliph died pleased with him, and how he then ruled in the best manner, through whom Allah settled cities, collected revenues, repelled the enemy, and brought ease to every household in their religion and provision, and then sealed for him with martyrdom, so glad tidings to him. ʿUmar replied that the deceived is the one whom they deceive, and asked ʿAbdullah whether he would bear witness for him before Allah on the Day of Resurrection. When he said yes, ʿUmar praised Allah and asked ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar to place his cheek upon the earth. He moved him from his thigh to his leg, but ʿUmar insisted, so he left his beard and cheek until they rested upon the ground, and ʿUmar said woe to him and to his mother if Allah did not forgive him. Then he passed away, may Allah have mercy on him. When he passed, they sent to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, who refused to come unless they did what ʿUmar had commanded of consulting the Muhājirūn and the Ansār and the chiefs of the armies present. Al-Hasan said, when ʿUmar's conduct at his death and his fear of his Lord were mentioned to him, that this is the believer who combines excellence and compassion, while the hypocrite combines evil and delusion, and that he had never found, among those gone or remaining, a servant who increased in excellence except that he increased in fear and compassion, nor one who increased in evil except that he increased in delusion."
"14464","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه said that Abū Luʾluʾah was a slave of al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah who made millstones, and al-Mughīrah took four dirhams from him daily. Abū Luʾluʾah met ʿUmar and complained that al-Mughīrah had laid a heavy tax upon him and asked him to speak to him to lighten it. ʿUmar told him to fear Allah and do good to his master, intending to meet al-Mughīrah and speak to him to lighten it. The slave grew angry and said that his justice encompassed all people except him, and resolved to kill him. He fashioned a double-bladed dagger, sharpened it and poisoned it, then brought it to al-Hurmuzān and asked his opinion of it. He replied that he would strike no one with it except that he would kill him. Abū Luʾluʾah waited for his opportunity and came at the dawn prayer until he stood behind ʿUmar. When the prayer was established, ʿUmar would speak as was his custom, saying to straighten the rows. When he uttered the takbīr, Abū Luʾluʾah struck him in the shoulder and in his side, and ʿUmar fell. He then struck thirteen men with his dagger, seven of whom died and six of whom survived. ʿUmar was carried to his house while the people clamoured until the sun was about to rise. ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn ʿAwf called out for prayer, so they hastened to prayer and ʿAbd al-Rahmān led them with the two shortest chapters of the Quran. When the prayer ended they turned to ʿUmar. He called for a drink to assess his wound. Nabīdh was brought and he drank it, but it came out of his wound, and it was not known whether it was nabīdh or blood. Milk was then called for and he drank it, and it came out of his wound. They said there was no harm upon him, O Commander of the Faithful, but he replied that if being killed was his harm then he had been killed. The people began praising him, saying may Allah reward him with good, recalling what he was and what he did, then leaving as others came and praised him. ʿUmar swore that despite what they said, he wished to leave it even, with nothing for him and nothing against him, save that his companionship of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained safe for him. ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbbās, who was at his head as his close companion as if of his household and a reciter of the Quran, spoke and said that he would not leave it even, for he had accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the finest companionship any companion had given him, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed while pleased with him, then accompanied the Caliph of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then ruled himself as Commander of the Faithful in the finest manner any ruler had ruled, doing this and that. ʿUmar found comfort in the speech of Ibn ʿAbbās and asked him to repeat it, and he repeated it. ʿUmar then swore that despite what they said, if he owned the earth full of gold he would ransom himself with it that day from the terror of what lies ahead. He placed the matter in consultation among six: ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Talhah ibn ʿUbaydillah, al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn ʿAwf, and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqās, with ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar among them as an adviser but not of them. He gave them three days and commanded Suhayb to lead the people in prayer."
"14465","Tāriq ibn Shihāb reported that Umm Ayman رضي الله عنها said on the day ʿUmar was killed that Islam had this day become weak."
"14466","Zayd ibn Wahb reported that two men came to ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه while he was with him and asked how he recited a certain verse. ʿAbdullah recited it to them. One man claimed that Abū Hakīm had taught it to him differently, and the other recited, and when asked who had taught him, he said ʿUmar. ʿAbdullah told him to recite as ʿUmar had taught him, then wept until his tears were seen falling upon the pebbles. He then said that ʿUmar had been a strong fortress for Islam into which people entered and from which they did not exit, but that the fortress had now been breached, so people were leaving it and not entering it."
"14467","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه added in another narration that he did not think any Muslim household remained into which grief had not entered on the day ʿUmar was stricken, except a household of evil. He said that ʿUmar had been the most knowledgeable of them about Allah, the foremost reciter of the Book of Allah, and the most understanding in the religion of Allah. He urged him to recite as ʿUmar had taught him, swearing that it was clearer than the road of al-Saylahayn."
"14468","In another narration ʿAbdullah said, meaning ʿUmar, that when he took a path they found it easy, and that when the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to ʿUmar. He was the decisive distinction between excess and deficiency, and he swore that he wished to have served the like of him until death."
"14469","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه also said that when the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to ʿUmar. His Islam had been a triumph and his rule a victory. He swore that he knew of nothing on the face of the earth, even the thorny trees, except that it felt the loss of ʿUmar. He swore that he believed there was an angel between his eyes guiding him, and that Shaytan fled from him lest he innovate something in Islam only for ʿUmar to refute him. He swore that if he knew a dog loved ʿUmar, he would love it."
"14470","In another narration ʿAbdullah said that he had loved ʿUmar so much that he had feared Allah on account of it, and that he wished he had been a servant of ʿUmar until death."
"14471","In another narration ʿAbdullah said that if ʿUmar had loved a dog, it would have been the most beloved of dogs to him."
"14472","In another narration ʿAbdullah said that he had feared Allah in his love for ʿUmar."
"14473","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that Saʿīd ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه asked him where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was after he passed. He said in Paradise. He asked where Abū Bakr was after he died, and he replied that he was the tender-hearted one present at every sought good. He asked where ʿUmar was after he died, and he replied that when the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to ʿUmar."
"14474","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said that when ʿUmar was stabbed they sent for a physician. A man of the Ansār came and gave him milk to drink, but the milk came out of the stab below his navel. The physician told him to make his bequest, for he did not think he would live till evening. ʿUmar told him he had spoken truthfully."
"14475","ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn Yasār said that he witnessed the death of ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb, and the sun was eclipsed that day."
"14476","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr said that when ʿUmar was killed, al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه erased his name from the register."
"14477","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah رضي الله عنه said that ʿUmar ruled for ten years, then passed away."
"14478","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khattāb died at the age of sixty-six."
"14479","Qatādah رحمه الله said that ʿUmar was killed at the age of sixty-one."
"14480","Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī رحمه الله said that ʿUmar died at the head of fifty-five years."
"14481","Sālim ibn ʿAbdillah رحمه الله reported that ʿUmar was taken at the age of fifty-five."
"14482","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said that ʿUmar passed away at the age of fifty-five, and that he himself said grey hair had come early to him from his maternal uncles, the Banū al-Mughīrah."
"14483","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Ansārī رضي الله عنه said that ʿUmar was buried on Wednesday with three nights remaining of Dhū al-Hijjah in the year twenty-three."
"14484","Al-Layth ibn Saʿd رحمه الله said that the Commander of the Faithful ʿUmar was killed at the time of the departure of the pilgrims, in the year twenty-three."
"14485","Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah رحمه الله said that ʿUmar passed away in the year twenty-three, and his caliphate had lasted ten years."
"14486","ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī رحمه الله said that it is said ʿUmar was killed at the age of sixty-three, but the established report is that he was fifty-eight."
"14487","Yahyā ibn Bukayr رحمه الله said that ʿUmar was appointed caliph in Rajab of the year thirteen, and was killed at the end of Dhū al-Hijjah of the year twenty-three. He remained three days after the stabbing and died at the end of Dhū al-Hijjah. Suhayb رضي الله عنه prayed over him, his son ʿAbdullah washed him, he was shrouded in five garments, and was buried with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He was stabbed on Wednesday with nine nights remaining of Dhū al-Hijjah. Some people said he died the same day. Concerning his age at death, as he had heard Mālik ibn Anas mention, he had reached the age of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, sixty-three years. Some say fifty-nine, some say fifty-three and fifty-five, and some say fifty-four. His caliphate lasted ten years, four months, and some days."
"14488","Maʿrūf ibn Abī Maʿrūf said that when ʿUmar passed away he heard a voice saying that whoever weeps should weep for Islam, for they were nearly ruined though the covenant was recent, and the world and its good had turned away, and one who believed in the promise had grown weary of it."
"14489","Musʿab ibn ʿAbdillah al-Zubayrī said that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān ibn Abī al-ʿĀs ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Qusayy ibn Kilāb ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr. His kunyah was Abū ʿAmr, and it is said Abū ʿAbdillah. The mother of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān was Arwā bint Kurayz ibn Rabīʿah ibn Habīb ibn ʿAbd Shams, and the mother of Arwā was Umm Hakīm al-Baydāʾ bint ʿAbd al-Muttalib, the paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The mother of Umm Hakīm was Fātimah bint ʿAmr ibn ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿImrān ibn Makhzūm, the grandmother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from his father's side."
"14490","Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنهما said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to ʿUthmān with a platter containing meat. He entered upon him while Ruqayyah رضي الله عنها was seated, and he had never seen two more beautiful than them. He looked at Ruqayyah once and at ʿUthmān once. When he returned, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had entered upon them. He said yes, and when asked whether he had seen a couple more beautiful than them, he replied no, explaining how he had looked at each in turn. This was before the verse of hijāb was revealed."
"14491","ʿAbdullah ibn Hazm al-Māzinī رضي الله عنه said that he saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, and he had never seen any male or female more beautiful in face than him."
"14492","ʿAbdullah ibn Shaddād ibn al-Hād رحمه الله said that he saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān on Friday upon the minbar wearing a thick ʿAdanī waist-wrapper worth four or five dirhams and a patterned Kūfī shawl. He was of full flesh, long-bearded, and handsome in face."
"14493","Mūsā ibn Talhah رحمه الله said that ʿUthmān would lean upon a staff on Friday. He was the most beautiful of people, wearing two yellow garments, a waist-wrapper and a cloak, until he came to the minbar and sat upon it."
"14494","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAwn al-Qāriʾ رحمه الله said that he saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān with a white beard."
"14495","Ibn Abī Dhiʾb reported from ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn Saʿd that he saw ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān with a yellow beard."
"14496","Umm Mūsā رحمها الله said that ʿUthmān was among the most beautiful of people."
"14497","Al-Hasan ibn Abī al-Hasan رحمه الله said that he entered the mosque and found ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه reclining upon his cloak. Two water-carriers came disputing to him, and he judged between them. He then approached and looked at him, finding a man handsome in face with marks of smallpox upon it, and his hair covering his forearms."
"14498","Anas رضي الله عنه said that ʿUthmān emigrated to the land of Abyssinia with Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. News of them was delayed to the Prophet ﷺ, who would go out awaiting word of them. A woman came and informed him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said that ʿUthmān was the first to emigrate to Allah with his family after Lūt. > **""Indeed ʿUthmān is the first to emigrate to Allah with his family after Lūt.""**"
"14499","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there was no emigrant between ʿUthmān and Ruqayyah and Lūt, meaning that they were the first to emigrate to Abyssinia. > **""There was no emigrant between ʿUthmān and Ruqayyah and Lūt.""**"
"14500","ʿAbd al-Rahmān ibn ʿUthmān al-Qurashī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon his daughter while she was washing the head of ʿUthmān, and said to her to treat Abū ʿAbdillah well, for he was the most like his Companions to him in character. > **""O my daughter, treat Abū ʿAbdillah well, for he is the most like of my Companions to me in character.""**"
"14501","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said that he entered upon Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and wife of ʿUthmān, with a comb in her hand. She said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had just left her after she had combed his head, and he had asked how she found Abū ʿAbdillah. She replied that he was good, whereupon he told her to honour him, for he was among the most like of his Companions to him in character. > **""How do you find Abū ʿAbdillah? Honour him, for he is among the most like of my Companions to me in character.""**"
"14502","Ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه said that Abū Bakr sought permission to enter upon the Prophet ﷺ while his slave-girl was beating the duff, and he entered. Then ʿUmar sought permission and entered. Then ʿUthmān sought permission and she stopped. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that ʿUthmān was a shy man. > **""Indeed ʿUthmān is a shy man.""**"
"14503","Hafsah bint ʿUmar رضي الله عنها said that one day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her and placed his garment between his thighs. Abū Bakr sought permission and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ admitted him in that state. Then ʿUmar sought permission and he admitted him in that state. Then some of his Companions came and he admitted them, and ʿAlī came and he admitted him in that state. Then ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān sought permission, so he wrapped his garment about himself and admitted him. They conversed for a while, then left. She asked why Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿAlī, and others entered while he remained as he was without moving, yet when ʿUthmān entered he wrapped his garment. He asked whether he should not feel shy before one before whom the angels feel shy. > **""Should I not feel shy before one before whom the angels feel shy!""**"
"14504","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated with ʿĀʾishah seated behind him, Abū Bakr sought permission and entered, then ʿUmar entered, then ʿAlī entered, then Saʿd ibn Mālik entered, then ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān entered while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was conversing with his knees uncovered. He extended his garment over his knees and told his wife to move back from him. They conversed a while, then left. ʿĀʾishah asked why his Companions entered without him adjusting his garment or moving her away until ʿUthmān entered. He replied asking whether he should not feel shy before one before whom the angels feel shy, swearing by Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad that the angels feel shy before ʿUthmān as they feel shy before Allah and His Messenger, and that had he entered while she was near him, he would not have raised his head nor spoken until he left. > **""Should I not feel shy before one before whom the angels feel shy! By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, indeed the angels feel shy before ʿUthmān as they feel shy before Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"14505","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated in a house wearing a waist-wrapper which he had placed between his legs with his thighs exposed. Abū Bakr sought permission and entered, then ʿUmar entered, then ʿUthmān entered. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him, he rose hastily into the house. This weighed upon ʿĀʾishah, and when the people left she asked why Abū Bakr and ʿUmar entered without him changing his state, yet when ʿUthmān entered he rose. He replied asking whether he should not feel shy before one before whom the angels feel shy, for indeed the angels feel shy before ʿUthmān. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, should I not feel shy before one before whom the angels feel shy! Indeed the angels feel shy before ʿUthmān.""**"
"14506","Badr ibn Khālid reported that Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه stood over them on the day of the siege of ʿUthmān and asked whether they did not feel shy before one before whom the angels feel shy. When asked what that meant, he said he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that ʿUthmān had passed by him while an angel was with him, who said he was a martyr whom his people would kill, and that they felt shy before him. Badr said that a group of people then turned away from the siege. > **""ʿUthmān passed by me while an angel of the angels was with me, and he said: A martyr whom his people will kill. Indeed we feel shy before him.""**"
"14507","Al-Hasan رحمه الله, mentioning ʿUthmān and his intense shyness, said that even when he was in the house with the door closed, he would not remove his garment to pour water over himself, for shyness prevented him from straightening his back."
"14508","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah Almighty had revealed to him to marry his noble daughter to ʿUthmān. > **""Indeed Allah Almighty revealed to me to marry my noble daughter to ʿUthmān.""**"
"14509","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at the grave of his second daughter who had been with ʿUthmān and said whether there was no father or brother of a widowed woman to marry her to ʿUthmān, for had there been ten he would have married them to ʿUthmān, and he had only married him by revelation from heaven. > **""Is there no father of a widowed woman? Is there no brother of a widowed woman whom ʿUthmān may marry? Had there been ten, I would have married them to ʿUthmān, and I only married him by revelation from heaven.""**"
"14510","ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him when he married him to his other daughter that had he ten daughters he would have married them to him one after another, for he was pleased with him. > **""Had I ten daughters, I would have married them to you one after another, for indeed I am pleased with you.""**"
"14511","ʿIsmah رضي الله عنه said that when the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who was under ʿUthmān died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to marry ʿUthmān, for had he a third daughter he would have married her to him, and he had only married him by revelation from Allah Almighty. > **""Marry ʿUthmān. Had I a third daughter, I would have married her to him, and I only married him by revelation from Allah Almighty.""**"
"14512","Umm ʿAyyāsh رضي الله عنها said that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that he had only married ʿUthmān to Umm Kulthūm by revelation from heaven. > **""I only married ʿUthmān to Umm Kulthūm by revelation from heaven.""**"
"14513","Umm ʿAyyāsh, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that Ruqayyah, may Allah be pleased with her, bore ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, a boy, and the Messenger ﷺ named him ʿAbdullāh and gave ʿUthmān the kunyah Abū ʿAbdillāh."
"14514","Shaqīq reports that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, met al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah, who asked why he had kept aloof from the Commander of the Faithful ʿUthmān. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān told him to convey that he had not fled on the Day of ʿAynayn, meaning Uḥud, nor stayed back from Badr, nor abandoned the Sunnah of ʿUmar. When this reached ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, he replied that flight on that day was a fault Allah had already pardoned, citing the verse about those who turned back when the hosts met being made to slip by Satan yet pardoned by Allah; that his absence from Badr was due to nursing the daughter of the Messenger ﷺ, Ruqayyah, until she died, for which the Messenger ﷺ assigned him a share, and one given a share had witnessed; and that neither he nor ʿAbd al-Raḥmān could bear the way of ʿUmar, so he should be informed of that. > **""How can he reproach me for a fault Allah has already pardoned, when Allah said those who turned back were pardoned, while I nursed Ruqayyah until she died and the Messenger assigned me a share, and neither I nor he can bear the Sunnah of ʿUmar.""**"
"14515","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when the daughter of the Messenger ﷺ fell ill, the Messenger ﷺ told ʿUthmān to remain with her, for she needed either him or ʿUthmān, and ʿUthmān had the greater right, so the Messenger ﷺ left him behind with her. When Allah granted victory, the Messenger ﷺ sent him glad tidings that Allah had completed their number to include him. > **""Remain with her, for she must have either me or you, and you have the greater right.""**"
"14516","ʿUrwah reports that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, stayed behind in Madīnah for his wife, the daughter of the Messenger ﷺ, who was gravely ill. The Messenger ﷺ assigned him a share of Badr, and when ʿUthmān asked about his reward, the Messenger ﷺ confirmed his reward as well. > **""And your reward.""**"
"14517","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the Prophet ﷺ sent ʿUthmān to the people of Makkah, he took the Pledge of Riḍwān from his Companions and pledged on behalf of ʿUthmān by placing one of his own hands upon the other. People said Abū ʿAbdillāh would congratulate himself in circling the House securely, but the Prophet ﷺ said that even if he remained there a long time he would not circumambulate until the Prophet himself did. > **""Were he to remain there so long, he would not circumambulate the House until I circumambulate.""**"
"14518","ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ left him behind from Badr and assigned him a share. Concerning the Pledge of Riḍwān, ʿUthmān said the Messenger ﷺ struck his right hand upon his left on his behalf, and the left hand of the Messenger ﷺ is better than his own right. > **""This is for ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, and the left hand of the Messenger is better than my right.""**"
"14519","Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab reports that ʿUthmān raised his voice toward ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with them, whereupon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān asked why he raised his voice when he himself had witnessed Badr while ʿUthmān had not, had pledged to the Messenger ﷺ while ʿUthmān had not, and had not fled at Uḥud while ʿUthmān had. ʿUthmān replied that the Messenger ﷺ had left him with his daughter and given him both a share and reward; that the Messenger ﷺ had sent him to the polytheists, as ʿAbd al-Raḥmān knew, and upon his delay struck his right hand upon his left for ʿUthmān, whose left hand of the Messenger was better than his own right; and that flight at Uḥud was covered by the verse that Satan caused them to slip yet Allah pardoned them, so he should not be reproached for a pardoned fault. > **""This is for ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, for the left hand of the Messenger is better than my right, so why reproach me for a fault Allah has pardoned.""**"
"14520","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger ﷺ came to her, saw meat, and asked who had sent it. When she said ʿUthmān, she saw the Messenger ﷺ raise his hands supplicating for ʿUthmān."
"14521","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, attests that he witnessed ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān bringing to the Messenger ﷺ the equipment for the Army of Hardship, coming with seven hundred ūqiyyahs of gold."
"14522","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān came with dīnārs and cast them into the lap of the Prophet ﷺ. The Messenger ﷺ kept turning them over and saying that nothing ʿUthmān did after this day would harm him. > **""No harm shall be upon ʿUthmān for whatever he does after this day.""**"
"14523","Abū Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that during an expedition with the Prophet ﷺ severe hardship struck, gloom appearing on Muslim faces and joy on hypocrite faces. The Messenger ﷺ swore that before sunset Allah would bring provision. ʿUthmān, knowing Allah and His Messenger would prove true, bought fourteen mounts with their food and directed nine to the Prophet ﷺ. When the Prophet ﷺ asked what this was, he was told it was a gift from ʿUthmān, so joy appeared on the face of the Messenger ﷺ and gloom on the hypocrites. The narrator saw the Messenger ﷺ raise his hands until the whiteness of his armpits showed, praying for ʿUthmān with a prayer he had never heard before or after. > **""O Allah, grant ʿUthmān. O Allah, deal with ʿUthmān.""**"
"14524","Abū al-Malīḥ reports from his father that the Prophet ﷺ spoke to the Anṣārī owner of the plot added to the Madīnah mosque, offering him a house in Paradise, but he refused. ʿUthmān then offered him ten thousand dirhams, bought it, came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to buy from him the plot he had bought from the Anṣārī, and the Prophet ﷺ bought it for a house in Paradise. ʿUthmān noted its price of ten thousand, then the Prophet ﷺ laid a brick, called Abū Bakr who laid a brick, then ʿUmar who laid a brick, then ʿUthmān laid a brick, then told the people to lay bricks, and they did. > **""Lay bricks.""**"
"14525","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he stored ten deeds with his Lord: that he was the fourth of four in Islam, never sang nor wished vainly, never placed his right hand upon his private part since pledging to the Messenger ﷺ, never let a Friday pass since embracing Islam without freeing a slave, freeing later if none was at hand, and never committed zinā in Jāhiliyyah or Islam."
"14526","ʿUmar ibn Ibrāhīm al-Yashkurī reports hearing his mother relate from her mother, who went to the House when it had two doors, completed ṭawāf, and visited ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, conveying greetings from one of her sons and asking her view when people spoke much against ʿUthmān. ʿĀʾishah invoked the curse of Allah upon whoever cursed him, repeating it thrice, and said she had seen the Messenger ﷺ reclining his thigh upon ʿUthmān while she wiped sweat from the brow of the Messenger ﷺ as revelation descended, that he married him his two daughters one after the other, and that he would say write, O ʿUthaym, for Allah would not place a servant so near His Prophet except one honoured to Him. > **""Write, O ʿUthaym.""**"
"14527","In another narration the Prophet ﷺ reclined his back upon ʿĀʾishah. Umm Kulthūm bint Thumāmah al-Ḥinṭī reports her brother al-Mukhāriq asked her to visit ʿĀʾishah, convey his greeting, and ask about ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān when people spoke much against him after his killing. ʿĀʾishah returned the greeting with mercy and blessings, then testified that ʿUthmān was in that house with the Prophet of Allah ﷺ when Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came on a fiercely hot night, when revelation came heavily upon him, as Allah said He would cast a weighty word, and then she narrated the like."
"14528","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while with the Messenger ﷺ in a house among Muhājirūn including Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥah, al-Zubayr, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with them, the Messenger ﷺ said each man should rise to his equal. The Prophet ﷺ rose to ʿUthmān, embraced him, and said he was his companion in this world and his companion in the Hereafter. > **""Rise, each man to his equal. You are my companion in this world and my companion in the Hereafter.""**"
"14529","ʿUbayd al-Ḥimyarī reports that he was with ʿUthmān at the time of the siege when ʿUthmān asked whether Ṭalḥah was present. Ṭalḥah, may Allah have mercy on him, affirmed, and ʿUthmān asked him to stand, then adjured him by Allah whether they had been with the Prophet ﷺ when he said each man should take the hand of his companion, so each took another's hand until the Prophet ﷺ took ʿUthmān's hand and said this was his companion in this world and his protector in the Hereafter. Ṭalḥah affirmed by Allah that it was so. > **""Let each man among you take the hand of his companion. This is my companion in this world and my protector in the Hereafter.""**"
"14530","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he was with the Messenger ﷺ when a man came and shook the Prophet's hand, and the Prophet ﷺ did not withdraw his hand until the man withdrew his. The man then said ʿUthmān had come, and the Prophet ﷺ said he was a man of Paradise. > **""A man of Paradise.""**"
"14531","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports hearing the Messenger ﷺ say that ʿUthmān is in Paradise. > **""ʿUthmān is in Paradise.""**"
"14532","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that Umm Kulthūm came to the Messenger ﷺ saying the husband of Fāṭimah was better than her own husband. The Messenger ﷺ fell silent, then said her husband was loved by Allah and His Messenger and loved Allah and His Messenger, adding that were she to enter Paradise and see his dwelling, she would see none of his Companions above him in station. > **""Your husband is loved by Allah and His Messenger and loves Allah and His Messenger, and were you to see his dwelling in Paradise you would see none of my Companions above him.""**"
"14533","ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAdī ibn al-Khiyār reports that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān asked whether he had met the Messenger ﷺ. He said no, but the knowledge and certainty from him had reached him purely as to a maiden behind her veil. ʿUthmān then bore witness, praised Allah, and said Allah sent Muḥammad ﷺ with the truth, he was among those who responded to Allah and His Messenger, believed in what Muḥammad ﷺ was sent with, performed the two migrations as stated, attained marriage to the Messenger ﷺ, pledged to the Messenger ﷺ, and by Allah never disobeyed him nor deceived him until Allah took him."
"14534","Al-Nazzāl ibn Sabrah reports that when ʿUthmān was appointed caliph, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd said they had appointed the best of those remaining and had not fallen short."
"14535","In another narration the wording is that they did not fall short concerning the worthiest of high rank. > **""We did not fall short concerning the worthiest of high rank.""**"
"14536","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥawālah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports coming to the Messenger ﷺ while he sat in the shade of a lote tree with a scribe to whom he dictated. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether he should have something written for Ibn Ḥawālah, who replied he did not know and that Allah and His Messenger would choose for him, so the Prophet ﷺ turned away and continued dictating. Looking, he saw ʿUmar in the writing and knew ʿUmar would be written only for good. Asked a second and third time, he first deferred then accepted. The Prophet ﷺ asked what he would do amid tribulations emerging from the earth's edges like the horns of cattle, and then another after it beside which the first would seem like a rabbit's leap, to which he deferred to Allah and His Messenger. The Prophet ﷺ then said to follow that man, as a muffled man passed; Ibn Ḥawālah hastened, seized his shoulder, turned his face to the Messenger ﷺ asking if it was this one, and the Prophet ﷺ said yes, and it was ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him. > **""Shall I have something written for you, O Ibn Ḥawālah. Follow this one. Yes.""**"
"14537","Jubayr ibn Nufayr reports that while encamped with Muʿāwiyah after the killing of ʿUthmān, Murrah ibn Kaʿb al-Bahzī stood saying he would not have stood there but for something heard from the Messenger ﷺ. When Muʿāwiyah heard the mention of the Messenger ﷺ he seated the people. Murrah said they sat with the Messenger ﷺ when ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān passed, combed and gathered in hair, and the Messenger ﷺ said a tribulation would emerge from beneath the feet of this man, and whoever followed him that day would be upon guidance. He rose, took ʿUthmān by the shoulders, showed him to the Messenger ﷺ asking if it was this one, and the Prophet ﷺ affirmed that it was he and his followers upon guidance. ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥawālah al-Azdī then stood from the pulpit confirming he was the narrator's companion in that, present in that gathering, and would have spoken first had he known he would find one to confirm him in the army. > **""A tribulation shall emerge from beneath the feet of this one, and he and whoever follows him that day shall be upon guidance.""**"
"14538","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, you named him rightly; ʿUmar, a horn of iron; ʿUthmān Dhū al-Nūrayn, you named him rightly; he was killed wronged and shall receive two shares of reward."
"14539","Ḥafṣah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with her, reports sitting with ʿĀʾishah and the Messenger ﷺ when the Messenger ﷺ wished some Companions were with him to converse. ʿĀʾishah suggested Abū Bakr, Ḥafṣah suggested ʿUmar, but he declined both and asked for ʿUthmān. When ʿUthmān came and entered, the two women stood and let down the curtain. The Messenger ﷺ told ʿUthmān he would be killed as a martyr, to be patient as Allah granted patience, and not to remove a shirt Allah had clothed him for twelve years and six months until meeting Allah while He was pleased with him. ʿUthmān asked for the Prophet's prayer for patience, so the Prophet ﷺ prayed O Allah grant him patience. When ʿUthmān left, the Messenger ﷺ kept saying Allah grant him patience, for he would be martyred, die fasting, and break his fast with him. > **""You shall be killed as a martyr, so be patient, may Allah grant you patience, and do not remove a shirt Allah has clothed you for twelve years and six months until you meet Allah while He is pleased with you. O Allah, grant him patience, for he shall die fasting and break his fast with me.""**"
"14540","Ibrāhīm reports his father told him, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, that ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated the like of that account."
"14541","Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Jisrī reports visiting ʿĀʾishah while Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, was with her. ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, told him this was Ḥafṣah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, then adjured Ḥafṣah by Allah to confirm or correct her: both had been with the Messenger ﷺ when he fainted, ʿĀʾishah asked whether he had passed, Ḥafṣah said she did not know, then he awoke saying open the door for him. After a second faint and awakening with the same command, ʿĀʾishah asked whether he meant her father or Ḥafṣah's father, Ḥafṣah said she did not know, then they opened the door and it was ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān. When the Prophet ﷺ saw him he said draw near, ʿUthmān leaned over him and he confided something neither woman knew, then raised his head asking whether he understood, and ʿUthmān said yes. This occurred a second and then a third time with intense leaning, each with private counsel, each confirmed by ʿUthmān saying his ears had heard and his heart retained, after which the Prophet ﷺ told him to go out. Ḥafṣah affirmed by Allah that it was true, with the addition that the Prophet ﷺ said Allah might clothe ʿUthmān with a shirt, and if the hypocrites sought its removal he should not remove it, thrice. > **""Draw near. Have you understood what I said to you. Go out. O ʿUthmān, Allah may clothe you with a shirt, and if the hypocrites seek its removal, do not remove it.""**"
"14542","In another narration at al-Ṭabarānī, ʿĀʾishah adds that she was only surprised by ʿUthmān kneeling and saying whether it would be by injustice and aggression, O Messenger of Allah, thinking he had informed him of his killing. > **""By injustice and aggression, O Messenger of Allah.""**"
"14543","Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn reports that a Kūfan man testified ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān was killed as a martyr, so the guards seized him, raised him, and said they would have killed him had they not been forbidden to kill. The man asked ʿAlī whether he too testified he was a martyr, recalling coming to the Messenger ﷺ asking and receiving, then Abū Bakr, then ʿUmar, then ʿUthmān, each giving him. Then coming to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to pray for blessing, the Prophet ﷺ asked how blessing would not be upon one to whom a Prophet, a Ṣiddīq, and two martyrs had given, repeating it thrice. > **""How shall you not be blessed when a Prophet, a Ṣiddīq, and two martyrs gave to you.""**"
"14544","Aslam, freedman of ʿUmar, reports witnessing ʿUthmān on the day of the siege at the funeral ground, so crowded a thrown stone would fall only upon a head. ʿUthmān looked from the small door facing the station of Jibrīl, peace be upon him, calling thrice whether Ṭalḥah was among the people, met with silence until Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh stood. ʿUthmān expressed surprise at his silence after three calls, then adjured him by Allah whether he recalled being alone with the Messenger ﷺ in such-and-such place with none but the two of them, when the Messenger ﷺ told Ṭalḥah that every Prophet has a companion from his companions of his nation with him in Paradise, and that this ʿUthmān, meaning himself, was his companion in Paradise. Ṭalḥah affirmed by Allah that it was so. > **""O Ṭalḥah, there is no Prophet but with him is a companion from his nation with him in Paradise, and this ʿUthmān is my companion in Paradise.""**"
"14545","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ reports from his mother that al-Ṣaʿbah bint al-Ḥaḍramī went out and was heard telling her son Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh that ʿUthmān's siege had tightened and he should speak for relief. While washing one side of his head he did not answer, so she thrust her hands into her garment, bared her breasts, and adjured him by her bearing and nursing not to refuse. He rose, twisted his washed hair and knotted it, went to ʿAlī sitting beside his house, accompanied by his mother and the mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, and asked that the people be eased from ʿUthmān whose siege had tightened. ʿAlī replied by Allah he loved nothing of that matter which they disliked."
"14546","ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām, may Allah be pleased with him, said when people raged over ʿUthmān that they should not kill the old man but seek his correction, for no nation kills its Prophet without seventy thousand of them shedding blood before their affair is set right, and no nation kills its caliph without forty thousand shedding blood. They disregarded his word and killed him. Later he met ʿAlī on the road asking where he headed; ʿAlī said the land of Iraq, so he told him not to go but to keep to the pulpit of the Messenger ﷺ. Some of ʿAlī's companions rushed at him, but ʿAlī said leave him, for he is of the Household. When ʿAlī was killed, ʿAbdullāh told Ibn Miʿqal this was the head of forty, upon which there would be truce, repeating that killing a Prophet costs seventy thousand and killing a caliph forty thousand. > **""O people, do not kill this old man but seek his correction, for no nation kills its Prophet but seventy thousand are killed, and no nation kills its caliph but forty thousand are killed.""**"
"14547","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr reports that Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Salām sought permission of al-Ḥajjāj ibn Yūsuf, entered, greeted, was given room by two men at the couch, and sat. Al-Ḥajjāj asked by his father whether he knew the ḥadīth his father had told ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān from his grandfather ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām, and which one, namely about the Egyptians when they besieged ʿUthmān. He said he knew it: ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām came while ʿUthmān was besieged, entered with room made for him, greeted him as Commander of the Faithful, was asked what brought him, and replied he had come to stand firm until martyred or Allah granted victory, seeing those people would kill him, which would be better for ʿUthmān and worse for them. ʿUthmān adjured him by his right to go out to them for good Allah might bring or evil Allah might repel through him, so he obeyed, went out, the people gathered expecting pleasing news, and he preached praising Allah, declaring Allah sent Muḥammad ﷺ as bearer of glad tidings and warner, made followers prevail over all religion, chose dwellings including Madīnah as abode of Hijrah and faith, with angels ever surrounding it since the Messenger ﷺ arrived, and warning against violating its sanctity and the covenant of Allah. > **""Peace be upon you, O Commander of the Faithful. I have come to stand firm until I am martyred or Allah grants you victory. Allah sent Muḥammad as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner, and made Madīnah the abode of Hijrah and faith.""**"
"14548","Kulthūm al-Khuzāʿī reports ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said it would not please him to have shot an arrow at ʿUthmān, even missing while intending his killing, even for the like of Uḥud in gold."
"14549","Abū al-Aswad al-Dīlī reports hearing Abū Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say that falling from the sky and being torn apart was dearer to him than sharing in the blood of ʿUthmān. > **""For me to fall from the sky and be torn apart is dearer to me than to share in the blood of ʿUthmān.""**"
"14550","Al-Ḥasan reports reaching ʿUthmān near puberty, hearing and witnessing his address asking what the people blamed against him, while every day abundant good was distributed as he called the people to their stipends which they took in full, then to their clothing when cloaks were brought and divided among them. Al-Ḥasan added the enemy was repelled, stipends flowed, mutual relations were good, good was abundant, no believer on earth feared another believer, whoever met any tribesman found brotherhood, love, and support, with tribulation only if a sword were drawn against it. > **""O people, what do you blame against me. Come in the morning for your stipends. Come in the morning for your clothing.""**"
"14551","Al-Ḥasan reports his informant, the swordsman of ʿUthmān, told that an Anṣārī man entered upon ʿUthmān, who told him to return, O nephew, for he was not his killer. Asked how he knew, he said because the man had been brought to the Messenger ﷺ on his seventh day for taḥnīk and blessing. A second Anṣārī entered with the same exchange. Then Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr entered, and ʿUthmān said he was his killer. Asked how he knew, O Naʿthal, he said because on his seventh day he had been brought for the Prophet ﷺ to perform taḥnīk and pray for blessing, but he had defecated upon the Messenger ﷺ. The man then leapt upon his chest, seized his beard, and ʿUthmān said if he acted it would grieve his father, whereupon he struck his throat with small arrowheads in his hand. > **""Return, O nephew, for you are not my killer. You are my killer.""**"
"14552","Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reports that when the man struck the hand of ʿUthmān, ʿUthmān said it was the first hand to write the Mufaṣṣal. > **""It is the first hand to write the Mufaṣṣal.""**"
"14553","Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb reports that most of the riders who marched against ʿUthmān later went mad."
"14554","Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn reports that the wife of ʿUthmān said when they surrounded him whether they intended his killing, for whether they killed or left him, he kept the whole night alive in one rakʿah gathering the Qurʾān therein."
"14555","Al-Shaʿbī reports Masrūq met al-Ashtar asking whether they had killed ʿUthmān, and he said yes, so Masrūq swore they had killed him fasting and praying by night. Al-Ashtar told ʿAmmār, who came to Masrūq objecting that ʿUthmān had flogged ʿAmmār, banished Abū Dharr, and reserved the pasture, yet Masrūq called him fasting and praying. Masrūq replied they had done neither of two things: neither requited as they had been punished, nor showed patience which is better for the patient, as if stuffing his mouth with a stone. Al-Shaʿbī said no Hamdānī woman bore the like of Masrūq. > **""You killed him fasting and praying. Patience is better for the patient.""**"
"14556","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, said there is no Madīnah after ʿUthmān and no prosperity after Muʿāwiyah. The Prophet ﷺ also said Allah had promised him the Islam of Abū al-Dardāʾ, and he embraced Islam. > **""Allah promised me the Islam of Abū al-Dardāʾ, and he embraced Islam.""**"
"14557","ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when ʿUthmān was killed a man said two goats would not butt over it, but he replied indeed many eyes would be gouged over it. > **""Indeed, many eyes shall be gouged over it.""**"
"14558","Mālik, meaning Ibn Anas, reports ʿUthmān was killed and left thrown upon the refuse heap of Banū Fulān for three days. Twelve men came, including his grandfather Mālik ibn Abī ʿĀmir, Ḥuwayṭib ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, and ʿĀʾishah bint ʿUthmān, with a lamp in a bag. They carried him upon a door, his head knocking taq taq, to al-Baqīʿ, differed over his prayer, and Ḥakīm or Ḥuwayṭib led it. When they sought burial, a man of Banū Māzin threatened to inform the people next day if they buried him with Muslims, so they carried him to Ḥashsh Kawkab. As they lowered him his daughter cried out, Ibn al-Zubayr told her silence threatening to strike between her eyes if she repeated, then after burial and leveling told her to cry as she wished. Mālik added ʿUthmān used to pass Ḥashsh Kawkab saying a righteous man would be buried there, and ḥashsh means garden."
"14559","Sahm ibn Ḥubaysh, among those witnessing the killing of ʿUthmān, reports that in the evening he said if they left their companion till morning they would mutilate him. They set out with him to Baqīʿ al-Gharqad, managing it in the night's depth, carrying him while a dark crowd followed behind frightening them nearly to disperse. A caller cried fear not and remain firm, for they had come to witness him with them. Ibn Ḥubaysh would say by Allah they were angels. > **""No fear upon you, remain firm, for we have come to witness him with you.""**"
"14560","Fulfalah al-Juʿfī reports hearing al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them both, say he saw the Prophet ﷺ in a dream clinging to the Throne, Abū Bakr holding the waist of the Prophet ﷺ, ʿUmar holding the waist of Abū Bakr, and ʿUthmān holding the waist of ʿUmar, with blood pouring from the sky to the earth. When al-Ḥasan narrated this among some Shīʿah, they asked why he had not seen ʿAlī. Al-Ḥasan said none would have been dearer to see holding the waist of the Messenger ﷺ than ʿAlī, but it was a vision he saw. Abū Masʿūd then said they narrated a vision of al-Ḥasan while he himself had been with the Prophet ﷺ on an expedition when hunger struck, gloom on Muslim faces and joy on hypocrite faces. The Messenger ﷺ swore Allah would bring provision before sunset. ʿUthmān, knowing Allah and His Messenger would prove true, bought fourteen mounts with their food and sent nine to the Prophet ﷺ. Asked what this was, they said ʿUthmān's gift, so joy appeared on Muslim faces and gloom on hypocrites. He saw the Prophet ﷺ raise his hands till his armpits showed, praying for ʿUthmān as never heard before. > **""By Allah, provision shall come before the sun sets. What is this. O Allah, grant ʿUthmān. O Allah, act for ʿUthmān.""**"
"14561","Al-Ḥasan also said O people he had seen a wonder last night in a dream: the Lord above His Throne, the Messenger ﷺ coming to stand at a leg of the Throne, Abū Bakr placing his hand upon the shoulder of the Messenger ﷺ, ʿUmar upon Abū Bakr, then ʿUthmān standing apart saying Lord ask Your servants why they killed him, whereupon two spouts of blood burst from the sky to the earth. When ʿAlī was asked about what al-Ḥasan narrated, he said he narrated what he saw. > **""Lord, ask Your servants why they killed me.""**"
"14562","In another narration al-Ḥasan said he would not fight after a vision he saw, then mentioned the like except that he saw ʿUthmān placing his hand upon ʿUmar and saw blood beneath them. Asked what it was, it was said it was the blood of ʿUthmān with which he sought Allah. > **""What is this. It is the blood of ʿUthmān with which he seeks Allah.""**"
"14563","Muslim Abū Saʿīd, freedman of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, reports ʿUthmān freed twenty slaves, called for trousers and fastened them though he had never worn them in Jāhiliyyah or Islam, saying he had seen the Messenger ﷺ last night in a dream along with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar telling him to be patient for he would break his fast with them on the morrow. He then called for a muṣḥaf, spread it before him, and was killed while it lay before him. > **""Be patient, for you shall break your fast with us tomorrow.""**"
"14564","Qatādah reports al-Zubayr prayed over ʿUthmān and buried him, having been entrusted by his bequest."
"14565","Zahdam al-Jarmī reports Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, addressed them saying had the people not sought the blood of ʿUthmān they would have been stoned from the sky. > **""Had the people not sought the blood of ʿUthmān, they would have been stoned from the sky.""**"
"14566","ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd reports from his father that they sat with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, with ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir on his right and Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr on his left, when Ghurāb ibn Fulān al-Ṣudāʾī asked what he said about ʿUthmān. The two men hastened saying he asked about a man who disbelieved after faith and played the hypocrite. The man said he had not asked them nor come to them. ʿAlī told him he did not say what they had said, so both asked why then he had been killed. ʿAlī said he had ruled over them and misruled at the end, they panicked and panicked badly, but by Allah he hoped he and ʿUthmān would be as Allah said, with rancour stripped from breasts as brothers facing upon couches. > **""We stripped what was in their breasts of rancour as brothers facing upon couches.""**"
"14567","Abū al-Aswad reports hearing Ṭalq ibn Khashshāf say they came as a delegation to Madīnah to examine why ʿUthmān was killed. Arriving, they passed some of the family of ʿAlī, some of al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, and some Mothers of the Believers, but he went to ʿĀʾishah, greeted her, she returned it and questioned his origin until he traced to Banū Qays ibn Thaʿlabah. Asked about the killing of the Commander ʿUthmān, she said by Allah he was killed wronged, may Allah curse his killer, may Allah requite from Ibn Abī Bakr for him, bring humiliation to Aʿyan ibn Taym in his house, shed the blood of Banū Budayl upon misguidance, and send to al-Ashtar an arrow from His arrows. By Allah, none of those men escaped her supplication. > **""He was killed wronged, by Allah. May Allah curse whoever killed him.""**"
"14568","Al-Ḥasan reports the wicked Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr was seized in an Egyptian defile, inserted into a donkey's belly, and burned."
"14569","ʿAlqamah ibn Waqqāṣ reports gathering in Dār Makhramah after the killing of ʿUthmān to pledge allegiance. Abū Jahm ibn Ḥudhayfah said whoever they pledged must not block qiṣāṣ. ʿAmmār said not in the blood of ʿUthmān. Abū Jahm exclaimed by Allah, O Ibn Sumayyah, shall lashes you were given be requited while the blood of ʿUthmān is not requited. So they parted that day without pledge. > **""Whomever we pledge among you, we shall not block qiṣāṣ. Not in the blood of ʿUthmān.""**"
"14570","ʿUmayr ibn Rūdī reports ʿAlī addressed the people saying by Allah if none entered the Fire but the killer of ʿUthmān he would not enter it, and if none entered Paradise but the killer of ʿUthmān he would not enter it. Descending, he was told he had spoken a word scattering his companions, so he addressed them saying indeed Allah killed ʿUthmān while he was with him. Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn said it was a Qurashī phrase with two faces, and al-Ṭabarānī explained he meant Allah killed him while he himself was slain with him in weakness. > **""O people, indeed Allah killed ʿUthmān while I am with him. I was weakened the day ʿUthmān was killed.""**"
"14571","With the same chain, ʿAlī addressed them but they cut his address, so he said he was weakened only the day ʿUthmān was killed, striking the parable of three bulls, black, red, and white, with a lion gathered in a thicket. When the lion sought one, they united and repelled him. The lion told the black and red that the white disgraced and exposed them in their thicket, to let him eat it since his colour matched theirs, so they let him and he killed it. Then he told the black the red disgraced them, to let him eat it, and he killed it. Then he told the black he would eat him, who asked to cry thrice that he was eaten the day the white was eaten, thrice, adding indeed he was weakened the day ʿUthmān was killed. > **""I was eaten the day the white was eaten. I was weakened the day ʿUthmān was killed.""**"
"14572","Mughīrah reports Jarīr, ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, and Ḥanẓalah the scribe left Kūfah for Qarqīsiyā saying they would not stay in a town where ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, was reviled."
"14573","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports the council occurred and the people agreed upon ʿUthmān with three nights left of Dhū al-Ḥijjah in year twenty-three. ʿUthmān was killed on Friday, eighteen nights past Dhū al-Ḥijjah at the end of year thirty-five, aged eighty-eight, dyeing his beard yellow, his rule lasting twelve years."
"14574","Qatādah reports ʿUthmān was killed aged ninety or eighty-eight."
"14575","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the caliphate of ʿUthmān lasted twelve years."
"14576","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār reports ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān was killed on Friday, eighteen nights past Dhū al-Ḥijjah in year thirty-six after ʿAṣr, aged eighty-two, fasting that day."
"14577","Abū Qilābah reports a Qurashī man called Thumāmah over Ṣanʿāʾ wept intensely upon reaching him the killing of ʿUthmān. Recovering, he said today the caliphate upon Prophethood was stripped from the nation of Muḥammad ﷺ, becoming kingship and tyranny where whoever seized a thing prevailed over it. > **""Today the caliphate upon Prophethood is stripped from the nation of Muḥammad, becoming kingship and tyranny.""**"
"14578","In another narration, from Thumāmah ibn ʿAdī, who had companionship."
"14579","Al-Ṭabarānī said Abū Khalīfah recited to him from Abū Muḥammad al-Tawzī, and asking al-Riyāshī about it, he said it was by Ḥassān ibn Thābit, may Allah be pleased with him: you abandoned raiding the passes and came to fight a people at the grave of Muḥammad, evil was the guidance you were guided and evil the deed of the striving worshipper. > **""You abandoned raiding the passes and came to fight at the grave of Muḥammad. Evil was the guidance you were guided and evil the deed of the striving worshipper.""**"
"14580","Abū Khalīfah recited from al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Riyāshī verses by Laylā al-Akhyaliyyah: after ʿUthmān does his nation hope for good, when he was the best walker upon a leg, the caliphate of Allah who gave them sweet gold and papers, so belie not Allah's promise, fear Him, grieve not over a thing, and say not for a thing I shall do it, for Allah decreed what each man shall meet. > **""After ʿUthmān does his nation hope for good, when he was the best walker upon a leg. Do not belie the promise of Allah and fear Him.""**"
"14581","Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the Prophet ﷺ killed a Qurashī man in custody on the Day of the Conquest, then said no Qurashī would be killed in custody after that day except the killer of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, so kill him, for if you do not you shall be killed like sheep. > **""No Qurashī shall be killed in custody after this day except the killer of ʿUthmān, so kill him, or you shall be killed like sheep.""**"
"14582","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports hearing the Messenger ﷺ say mankind are from diverse trees, while he and ʿAlī are from one tree. > **""Mankind are from diverse trees, while I and ʿAlī are from one tree.""**"
"14583","Al-Ṭabarānī said ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Quṣayy ibn Kilāb ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr ibn Mālik, kunyah Abū al-Ḥasan, witnessed Badr."
"14584","ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal reports his father told him it reached him from Banū Hāshim that Abū Ṭālib was ʿAbd Manāf ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib was Shaybah ibn Hāshim, Hāshim was ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Quṣayy, and Quṣayy was Zayd."
"14585","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār said the mother of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib was Fāṭimah bint Asad ibn Hāshim ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Quṣayy, said to be the first Hāshimī woman to bear a Hāshimī, who embraced Islam, migrated to the Messenger ﷺ in Madīnah, died, and was buried by the Messenger ﷺ. Her mother was Fāṭimah bint Harim ibn Rawāḥah ibn Ḥujr ibn ʿAbd Maʿīṣ ibn ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy."
"14586","Abū Isḥāq reports going with his father to Friday prayer as a boy. When ʿAlī came out and ascended the pulpit, his father told him, O ʿAmr, stand and look at the Commander of the Faithful. He stood seeing him upright upon the pulpit with white beard and head, wearing a waist-wrap and cloak without a shirt. He never saw him sit upon the pulpit until he descended. Asked whether he performed qunūt, Abū Isḥāq said no."
"14587","In another narration, he did not see him dye his beard, large in the head."
"14588","Shuʿbah reports that he asked Abu Isḥaq whether he was older than al-Shaʿbi, and Abu Isḥaq replied that al-Shaʿbi was older than him by a year or two. He added that Abu Isḥaq had seen ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, and used to describe him to them as large-bellied and bald at the front of the head. Shuʿbah further noted that Abu Isḥaq was older than Abu al-Bakhtari, and that Abu al-Bakhtari had neither met ʿAli nor seen him."
"14589","Abu Rajaʾ al-ʿUṭaridi reports that he saw ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, dignified in bearing and bald, with little hair, as though at his side lay a sheepskin."
"14590","Al-Shaʿbi reports that he saw ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, upon the minbar with a white beard that filled what lay between his shoulders. Yaḥya ibn Saʿid added in his narration that upon his head were small tufts of hair."
"14591","Al-Waqidi relates that it is said ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him, was wheat-complexioned, of medium stature, fleshy, broad-shouldered, with a long beard, bald at the fore, large-bellied, thick-eyed, and white in head and beard."
"14592","Abu al-Ṭufayl reports that the saying of ʿAli was mentioned before Abu Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said asking whether they had not seen his head like a basin, with what surrounded it like leather socks, in reference to baldness in the middle with hair around it."
"14593","Abu al-Ṭufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ came while ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, was sleeping in the dust, and told him that the truest of his names was Abu Turab and that he was Abu Turab. > **""The truest of your names is Abu Turab; you are Abu Turab.""**"
"14594","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ gave ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, the kunyah Abu Turab, and it became among the most beloved of his kunyahs to him. > **""You are Abu Turab.""**"
"14595","Maʿqil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he once helped the Prophet ﷺ perform ablution, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he wished to visit Faṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her. He agreed, and the Prophet ﷺ rose leaning upon him and said another than him would carry her burden while its reward would be his. They entered upon Faṭimah, peace be upon her, and the Prophet ﷺ asked how she found herself. She replied that her grief had grown severe, her poverty had grown severe, and her illness had lengthened. In the handwriting of his father, ʿAbdullah found in this narration the addition that the Prophet ﷺ asked whether she was not pleased that he had married her to the earliest of his community in accepting Islam, the most knowledgeable among them, and the greatest among them in forbearance. > **""Would you visit Faṭimah with me, and are you not pleased that I have married you to the earliest of my community in submission, the most knowledgeable, and the most forbearing.""**"
"14596","Abu Isḥaq reports that when ʿAli married Faṭimah, may Allah be pleased with them, she said to the Prophet ﷺ that he had married her to a bleary-eyed man with a large belly. The Prophet ﷺ replied that he had indeed married her to the first of his Companions in accepting Islam, the most knowledgeable among them, and the greatest among them in forbearance. > **""I have married you to the first of my Companions in submission, the most knowledgeable among them, and the greatest in forbearance.""**"
"14597","Abu Dharr and Salman, may Allah be pleased with them, report that the Prophet ﷺ took the hand of ʿAli and said he was the first to believe in him, the first to shake his hand on the Day of Resurrection, the Greatest Truthful One, the Distinguisher of this community between truth and falsehood, the leader of the believers, while wealth was the leader of the wrongdoers. > **""This is the first who believed in me, the first who will shake my hand on the Day of Resurrection, the Greatest Truthful One and the Distinguisher of this community between truth and falsehood.""**"
"14598","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the foremost are three: the foremost to Musa, peace be upon him, was Yushaʿ ibn Nun; the foremost to ʿIsa, peace be upon him, was the Companion of Yasin; and the foremost to Muḥammad ﷺ was ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him. > **""The foremost are three: the foremost to Musa is Yushaʿ ibn Nun, the foremost to ʿIsa is the Companion of Yasin, and the foremost to Muḥammad is ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib.""**"
"14599","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the first of this community to come to its Prophet ﷺ, and the first among them in Islam, was ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"14600","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the first to accept Islam was ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him."
"14601","Ḥabbah al-ʿUrani reports that he saw ʿAli, peace be upon him, laughing upon the minbar more than ever before until his molars showed. He then said he had remembered the saying of Abu Ṭalib, when Abu Ṭalib came upon them while he was with the Messenger ﷺ praying at Baṭn Nakhlah and asked what they were doing. The Messenger ﷺ invited him to Islam, and he answered that there was no harm in what they did or said, but by Allah his backside would never rise for them. ʿAli laughed in wonder at his father's saying, then swore by Allah thrice that he knew no servant of this community to have worshipped Allah before him except His Prophet, and that he had prayed seven years before the people prayed."
"14602","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ was sent on Monday and that he accepted Islam on Tuesday."
"14603","Al-Ḥasan and others report that the first to believe was ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, when he was fifteen or sixteen years old."
"14604","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reports that ʿAli accepted Islam when he was eight years old."
"14605","ʿAfif al-Kindi reports that he was a merchant who came to Makkah to buy goods from al-ʿAbbas ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. While with him at Mina, a man emerged from a nearby tent, looked toward the sky, and when he saw it had declined he stood to pray. Then a woman emerged from the same tent and stood behind him praying, then a boy nearing puberty emerged and stood with him praying. ʿAfif asked al-ʿAbbas who they were, and he said the man was Muḥammad the son of his brother ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, the woman was his wife Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, and the youth was ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib his cousin. When asked what they were doing, he said they were praying, and that he claimed to be a prophet followed by none except his wife and this youth his cousin, and claimed the treasures of Kisra and Qayṣar would be opened to him. ʿAfif, cousin of al-Ashʿath ibn Qays, who later accepted Islam and whose Islam was good, used to say that had Allah granted him Islam on that day he would have been second with ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib."
"14606","Abu Rafiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ prayed on Monday, Khadijah prayed on Monday at the end of the day, and ʿAli prayed on Tuesday. ʿAli then continued to pray in concealment for seven years and months before anyone else prayed."
"14607","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was the first to pray with the Messenger ﷺ."
"14608","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the first to pray with the Messenger ﷺ was ʿAli. ʿAmr relates that he mentioned this to Ibrahim, who denied it and said it was Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him."
"14609","Abu Rafiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ was commissioned as prophet on Monday and ʿAli accepted Islam on Tuesday."
"14610","Rabaḥ ibn al-Ḥarith reports that a company came to ʿAli at al-Raḥbah and said peace be upon you O our master. He asked how he could be their master when they were Arabs. They said they had heard the Messenger ﷺ say on the Day of Ghadir Khumm that whomever he was master of, this one was his master. When they left, Rabaḥ followed them and asked who they were, and was told they were a group of the Anṣar among whom was Abu Ayyub al-Anṣari. In another wording they reported hearing the Messenger ﷺ say whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with the supplication for Allah to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. Abu Ayyub then uncovered his face and testified he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say it. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14611","ʿAmr Dhu Murr and Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with them, report that the Messenger ﷺ addressed them on the Day of Ghadir Khumm, saying whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him, oppose whoever opposed him, help whoever helped him, and aid whoever aided him. A shorter wording contains only the first sentence. In another narration they halted with the Messenger ﷺ at a valley called Khumm, prayer was ordered and prayed in the heat, shade was made for the Messenger ﷺ by a tree, and he asked whether they knew he was closer to every believer than himself, to which they assented. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him, oppose whoever opposes him, help whoever helps him, and aid whoever aids him.""**"
"14612","Abu al-Ṭufayl reports that ʿAli gathered the people at al-Raḥbah and adjured every Muslim who had heard the Messenger ﷺ say what he said on the Day of Ghadir Khumm to stand. Thirty people stood. Abu Nuʿaym's wording adds that many stood and testified that he had taken his hand and asked whether they knew he was closer to the believers than themselves, to which they said yes O Messenger of Allah, whereupon he said whomever he was master of, this one was his master, with the supplication for befriending and opposition. The narrator left with something in his soul, met Zayd ibn Arqam, told him what ʿAli had said, and Zayd replied asking what he denied, for he himself had heard the Messenger ﷺ say it. > **""Do you not know that I am closer to the believers than themselves. Whomever I am his master, this one is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14613","Saʿid ibn Wahb reports that ʿAli, peace be upon him, adjured the people, whereupon five or six Companions of the Prophet ﷺ stood and testified that the Messenger ﷺ said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14614","ʿAmr ibn Dhi Murr, Saʿid ibn Wahb, and Zayd ibn Yuthayʿ report that they heard ʿAli say he adjured by Allah any man who had heard the Messenger ﷺ speak on the Day of Ghadir Khumm to stand, whereupon thirteen men stood and testified that the Messenger ﷺ asked whether he was not closer to the believers than themselves, to which they said yes O Messenger of Allah. He then took the hand of ʿAli and said whomever he was master of, this one was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him, oppose whoever opposed him, love whoever loved him, hate whoever hated him, help whoever helped him, and abandon whoever abandoned him. > **""Am I not closer to the believers than themselves. Whomever I am his master, this one is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him, oppose whoever opposes him, love whoever loves him, hate whoever hates him, help whoever helps him, and abandon whoever abandons him.""**"
"14615","ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn Abi Layla reports that he witnessed ʿAli at al-Raḥbah adjuring the people, asking any who had heard the Messenger ﷺ say concerning him on the Day of Ghadir Khumm that whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, to stand and testify. Twelve Badri veterans stood, one of them as he could still picture wearing trousers, and testified that they had heard the Messenger ﷺ say on the Day of Ghadir Khumm whether he was not closer to the believers than themselves and his wives their mothers, to which they said yes O Messenger of Allah, whereupon he said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. > **""Am I not closer to the believers than themselves, and my wives their mothers. Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14616","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ ordered the trees cleared beneath and sprinkled, then addressed them, swearing by Allah that nothing to occur until the Hour had been left untold that day. He then asked who was closest to them than themselves, and they said Allah and His Messenger were closer to them than themselves. He said whomever he was master of, this one was his master, meaning ʿAli, then took his hand and spread it, supplicating Allah to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. A shorter wording contains only the first sentence. > **""O people, who is closest to you than yourselves. Whomever I am his master, this one is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14617","Dawud ibn Yazid al-Awdi reports from his father that Abu Hurayrah entered the mosque and people gathered to him, whereupon a youth stood before him and adjured him by Allah whether he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. Abu Hurayrah replied that he bore witness he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say it. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14618","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication for Allah to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14619","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿAli adjured the people, asking any man who had heard the Prophet ﷺ say whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him, to stand. Twelve Badri veterans stood and testified to it, while Zayd himself was among those who concealed it, so his sight left him. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14620","In another wording of the same report, ʿAli supplicated against whoever concealed testimony concerning what he had heard on the Day of Ghadir Khumm."
"14621","Malik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14622","Ḥabashi ibn Junadah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say on the Day of Ghadir Khumm asking Allah that whomever He and His Messenger were master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him, oppose whoever opposed him, help whoever helped him, and aid whoever aided him. > **""O Allah, whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him, oppose whoever opposes him, help whoever helps him, and aid whoever aids him.""**"
"14623","Jarir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they witnessed the season of the Farewell Pilgrimage with the Messenger ﷺ until they reached a place called Ghadir Khumm, where prayer in congregation was announced and the Muhajirun and Anṣar gathered. The Messenger ﷺ stood among them and asked what they testified to, and they said they testified none deserved worship but Allah. He asked then what, and they said Muḥammad was His servant and Messenger. He asked who their guardian was, and they said Allah and His Messenger were their masters. He asked again who their guardian was, then struck his hand to the upper arm of ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, raised him, freed his upper arm and took his forearms, and said whomever Allah and His Messenger were master of, this one was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him, oppose whoever opposed him, love among people whoever loved him, and hate whoever hated him. He added that he found none on earth to entrust after the two righteous servants except him, so to decree good for him. When asked who the two righteous servants were, the narrator said he did not know. > **""Whomever Allah and His Messenger are his master, this one is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him, oppose whoever opposes him, love whoever loves him, and hate whoever hates him.""**"
"14624","Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad reports that he heard ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib adjuring the people, asking any Muslim man who had heard the Messenger ﷺ say what he said on the Day of Ghadir Khumm when he stood to come forward, whereupon twelve Badri men stood and testified."
"14625","Nadhir reports that he heard ʿAli say to Ṭalḥah on the Day of the Camel, adjuring him by Allah whether he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. Ṭalḥah said yes, then remembered and left. > **""O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14626","Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqaṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ took the hand of ʿAli and asked whether he was not closer to the believers than themselves, then said whomever he was guardian of, ʿAli was his guardian. > **""Am I not closer to the believers than themselves. Whomever I am his guardian, ʿAli is his guardian.""**"
"14627","Saʿid ibn Wahb reports from Zayd ibn Yuthayʿ that ʿAli, peace be upon him, adjured the people at al-Raḥbah that whoever had heard the Messenger ﷺ speak on the Day of Ghadir Khumm should stand. Six stood from the side of Saʿid and seven from the side of Zayd, and they testified that they had heard the Messenger ﷺ say on the Day of Ghadir Khumm to ʿAli whether he was not closer to the believers, to which they said yes, whereupon he said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. > **""Am I not closer to the believers. O Allah, whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14628","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said on the Day of Ghadir Khumm that whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. Later narrators added the supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14629","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, called the people to witness, adjuring by Allah any man who had heard the Prophet ﷺ say asking Allah that whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. Sixteen men stood and testified. > **""O Allah, whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14630","Zadhan Abu ʿUmar reports that he witnessed ʿAli at al-Raḥbah adjuring the people as to who had witnessed the Messenger ﷺ on the Day of Ghadir Khumm saying what he said. Thirteen men stood and testified that the Messenger ﷺ said on the Day of Ghadir Khumm that whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14631","Ḥumayd ibn ʿUmarah reports that he heard his father say he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say while holding the hand of ʿAli that whomever he was master of, this one was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. > **""Whomever I am his master, this one is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14632","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, in the course of a longer narration. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14633","ʿUmayrah bint Saʿd reports that she witnessed ʿAli upon the minbar adjuring the Companions of the Messenger ﷺ as to who had heard the Messenger ﷺ say what he said on the Day of Ghadir Khumm to testify. Twelve men stood, among them Abu Hurayrah, Abu Saʿid, and Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them, and testified that they had heard the Messenger ﷺ say whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master, with supplication to befriend whoever befriended him and oppose whoever opposed him. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14634","ʿUmayr ibn Saʿd reports that ʿAli gathered the people at al-Raḥbah while he was present and adjured by Allah any man who had heard the Messenger ﷺ say whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. Eighteen men stood and testified that they had heard the Prophet ﷺ say it. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14635","Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14636","Malik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. > **""Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14637","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he saw the Messenger ﷺ holding the hand of ʿAli and saying this one was his guardian and he was his guardian. > **""This is my guardian and I am his guardian.""**"
"14638","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ sent them in an expedition and placed ʿAli over them. When they returned, he asked how they had found their companion. Either he complained of him or another besides him complained, so he raised his head, being a man who often lowered his gaze, and saw the Prophet ﷺ with reddened face saying whomever he was guardian of, ʿAli was his guardian. Buraydah then said he would never displease him concerning him again. > **""How did you find your companion. Whomever I am his guardian, ʿAli is his guardian.""**"
"14639","Ziyad ibn Muṭarrif reports from Zayd ibn Arqam, though sometimes without naming him, that the Messenger ﷺ said whoever wished to live his life, die his death, and dwell in the Garden of Eternity promised by his Lord, whose branches He planted with His hand, should take ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib as guardian, for he would never lead them out of guidance nor enter them into misguidance. > **""Whoever wishes to live my life, die my death, and dwell in the Garden of Eternity promised by my Lord, let him take ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib as guardian, for he will never take you out of guidance nor enter you into misguidance.""**"
"14640","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said he counseled whoever believed in him and affirmed him to loyalty toward ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib: whoever took him as guardian took him as guardian, and whoever took him as guardian took Allah as guardian; whoever loved him loved him, and whoever loved him loved Allah; whoever hated him hated him, and whoever hated him hated Allah. > **""I counsel whoever believed in me and affirmed me to loyalty toward ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib. Whoever takes him as guardian takes me as guardian, and whoever takes me as guardian takes Allah as guardian, and whoever loves him loves me, and whoever loves me loves Allah, and whoever hates him hates me, and whoever hates me hates Allah.""**"
"14641","Wahb ibn Ḥamzah reports that he accompanied ʿAli from Madinah to Makkah and saw in him some things he disliked, so he said that if he returned he would complain of him to the Messenger ﷺ. When he arrived and met the Messenger ﷺ, he said he had seen such and such from ʿAli, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ told him not to say this, for he was the closest of people to them after him. > **""Do not say this, for he is the closest of people to you after me.""**"
"14642","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that he was to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. In another wording the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli at the expedition of Tabuk that he had left him behind among his family. ʿAli replied that he disliked the Arabs saying he had abandoned his cousin and stayed behind from him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he was not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""I have left you behind among my family. Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14643","Asmaʾ bint ʿUmays, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that he was to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""You are to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14644","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to ʿAli asking whether he was not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. One chain comes through ʿAmir ibn Saʿd from his father and from Umm Salamah, another through ʿAmir from his father from Umm Salamah. > **""Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14645","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to ʿAli asking whether he was not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. In another wording it is shortened to being to him like Harun. > **""Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14646","Ḥabashi ibn Junadah al-Saluli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that he was to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""You are to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14647","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to ʿAli asking whether he was not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be neither prophethood nor inheritance after him. > **""Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophethood after me and no inheritance.""**"
"14648","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ intended an expedition and summoned Jaʿfar, ordering him to remain behind over Madinah. Jaʿfar said he would never stay behind after him, so the Messenger ﷺ sent for ʿAli and pressed him to stay behind before he could speak, and ʿAli wept. When asked what made him weep, he said several traits moved him: that Quraysh would say tomorrow how quickly he stayed behind from his cousin and abandoned him; that he had hoped to expose himself to striving in the way of Allah, for Allah said no ground enraging the disbelievers was trodden nor any gain taken from an enemy except a righteous deed was written for them; and that he had hoped to expose himself to the bounty of Allah. The Messenger ﷺ answered that concerning Quraysh mockery he had in him an example, for they had called him sorcerer, soothsayer, and liar; concerning exposing himself to reward, he should be pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa except that there would be no prophet after him; and concerning exposing himself to bounty, these were two loads of pepper come from Yemen, to sell and enjoy with Faṭimah until Allah gave them of His bounty. > **""What makes you weep. Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me, so sell this pepper and enjoy it with Faṭimah until Allah gives you of His bounty.""**"
"14649","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he suffered a pain and came to the Prophet ﷺ, who stood him in his place, stood to pray, cast the edge of his garment over him, then said he had recovered O son of Abu Ṭalib with no harm upon him. He added that he had asked Allah for nothing except he asked the like for him, and Allah had granted him all he asked except that it was said to him there would be no prophet after him. > **""You are cured, O son of Abu Ṭalib, there is no harm upon you. I asked nothing of Allah except I asked the like for you, and He granted me all except that it was said there will be no prophet after you.""**"
"14650","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said he had left him behind to be his successor. When ʿAli asked whether he should stay behind from him O Messenger of Allah, he asked whether he was not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""I have left you behind to be my successor. Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14651","Jabir, meaning Ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that he was to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""You are to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14652","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that he was to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""You are to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14653","Al-Baraʾ ibn ʿAzib and Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with them both, report that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli when intending to go on campaign that one of them must stay, so he left him behind. Some people said he had left him behind only for something disliked in him. When this reached ʿAli, he came to the Messenger ﷺ and informed him, whereupon he laughed and said O ʿAli, was he not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa, except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""One of us must stay. O ʿAli, are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14654","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to Umm Salamah that this ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, his flesh was his flesh and his blood his blood, and he was to him like Harun to Musa except that there would be no prophet after him. > **""This ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, his flesh is my flesh and his blood is my blood, and he is to me like Harun to Musa except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14655","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when the Prophet ﷺ established brotherhood among his Companions of the Muhajirun and Anṣar but did not pair ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him, with any of them, ʿAli left angry until he reached a rivulet, pillowed his forearm, and wind blew dust over him. The Prophet ﷺ sought him until he found him, nudged him with his foot, and told him to rise, for nothing suited him but to be Abu Turab. He asked whether he was angry that he had paired the Muhajirun and Anṣar but not paired him with any, then asked whether he was not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa except that there would be no prophet after him. He added that whoever loved him would be surrounded by security and faith, and whoever hated him Allah would cause to die a pre-Islamic death and reckon him for his deeds in Islam. > **""Rise, for nothing suits you but to be Abu Turab. Are you angry that I paired the Muhajirun and Anṣar and did not pair you. Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that there will be no prophet after me.""**"
"14656","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said it was written upon the gate of Paradise that none deserved worship but Allah, Muḥammad was the Messenger of Allah, and ʿAli was the brother of the Messenger ﷺ, two thousand years before creation. > **""It is written upon the gate of Paradise: none deserves worship but Allah, Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, and ʿAli is the brother of the Messenger, two thousand years before creation.""**"
"14657","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ established brotherhood among the people and established brotherhood between himself and ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him."
"14658","Sharaḥil ibn Murrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say to ʿAli to receive glad tidings, for his life was with him and his death with him. > **""Receive glad tidings, O ʿAli, your life is with me and your death is with me.""**"
"14659","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when the Prophet ﷺ married ʿAli to Faṭimah, Faṭimah said O Messenger of Allah, he had married her to a poor man with nothing. The Messenger ﷺ asked whether she was not pleased that Allah had chosen two men among the people of the earth, one her father and the other her husband. > **""Are you not pleased, O Faṭimah, that Allah chose two men, one your father and the other your husband.""**"
"14660","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that whenever Allah sent down O you who believe, ʿAli was its leader and noblest. Allah reproached the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ in more than one place, but mentioned ʿAli only with good."
"14661","Jumayʿ ibn ʿUmayr reports that his mother and maternal aunt entered upon ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and the narration continues until they said to tell them about ʿAli. She asked concerning what they asked: concerning a man whose hand was placed from the Messenger ﷺ in such a position that his soul flowed into his hand and he wiped his face with it. When they differed over his burial, he said the most beloved spot to Allah was where He took His prophet. They asked why she had gone out against him, and she said it was a decreed matter, and she wished she could ransom him with all upon earth."
"14662","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, swears by the One she swears by that ʿAli was the closest of people in final counsel with the Messenger ﷺ. She said they visited the Messenger ﷺ morning after morning while he kept asking whether ʿAli had come, several times, thinking he had sent him on some need. When he came later, thinking he had some private need with him, they left the house and sat by it, she nearest to the door, whereupon ʿAli bent over him, confiding and speaking privately, then the Messenger ﷺ passed away that very day, and ʿAli was the closest of people in counsel with him."
"14663","Dhuʾayb reports that when the Prophet ﷺ was approached by death, Ṣafiyyah said O Messenger of Allah, every woman among his wives had family to turn to while he had removed her own family, so if something occurred to whom should she turn. He replied to ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib. > **""To ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib.""**"
"14664","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that they used to say the Messenger ﷺ had confided to ʿAli seventy covenants he had confided to none other."
"14665","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the verse warning his nearest kin was revealed, the Messenger ﷺ gathered thirty men of his household, who ate and drank, then asked them who would guarantee his debts and promises, be with him in Paradise, and be his successor among his family. A man unnamed by Sharik replied that he was a sea, so who could undertake this. He repeated it to another, then presented it to his household, whereupon ʿAli said he would. > **""Who will guarantee my debts and promises, be with me in Paradise, and be my successor among my family.""**"
"14666","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ summoned al-ʿAbbas ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and asked him to guarantee his debts and promises. He said he could not bear it, whereupon his son ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbbas rebuked him, saying the Messenger ﷺ had invited him to settle his debts and promises. Al-ʿAbbas told him to leave him, for his nephew outstripped the wind in generosity. The Prophet ﷺ then summoned ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, who agreed that it was upon him and guaranteed it. When wealth later came to Abu Bakr, he declared it the wealth of Allah and what He had granted the Muslims, rightfully used to settle for His Prophet ﷺ, and invited whoever held a debt or promise from the Messenger ﷺ to take it. Among those who came was Jabir, who said the Messenger ﷺ had promised to scoop for him such and such when wealth came. Abu Bakr told him to take as the Messenger ﷺ had told him, so he took three scoops as the Messenger ﷺ had commanded."
"14667","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that ʿAli would settle his debt. > **""ʿAli will settle my debt.""**"
"14668","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he asked O Messenger of Allah, every prophet had a successor, so who was his successor. He remained silent, then later saw him and called O Salman. Salman hastened to him saying at his service. He asked whether he knew the successor of Musa, and Salman said yes, Yushaʿ ibn Nun. When asked why, Salman said because he was the most knowledgeable among them that day, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said his successor, keeper of his secret, best of those he left behind, fulfiller of his promise, and settler of his debt was ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib. > **""Do you know the successor of Musa. My successor, keeper of my secret, best of those I leave behind, fulfiller of my promise and settler of my debt is ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib.""**"
"14669","It was already mentioned in the chapter of his acceptance of Islam that the Prophet ﷺ said to Faṭimah asking whether she was not pleased that he had married her to the earliest of his community in submission, the most knowledgeable among them, and the greatest in forbearance. > **""Are you not pleased that I married you to the earliest of my community in submission, the most knowledgeable among them, and the greatest in forbearance.""**"
"14670","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said he was the city of knowledge and ʿAli its gate, so whoever sought knowledge should come to it through its gate. > **""I am the city of knowledge and ʿAli is its gate, so whoever seeks knowledge let him come through its gate.""**"
"14671","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that some Companions of the Messenger ﷺ had doors opening into the mosque. One day the Prophet ﷺ ordered these doors closed except the door of ʿAli. Some people spoke about it, so the Messenger ﷺ rose, praised Allah, and said he had ordered these doors closed except the door of ʿAli while their speaker had objected, but by Allah he had closed nothing nor opened anything on his own; he was commanded something and followed it. > **""Close these doors except the door of ʿAli, for I closed nothing nor opened anything but followed what I was commanded.""**"
"14672","ʿAbdullah ibn al-Raqim al-Kinani reports that they went to Madinah at the time of the Camel and met Saʿd ibn Malik there, who said the Messenger ﷺ had ordered the doors opening into the mosque closed and left the door of ʿAli. In an addition the people asked why he had closed all their doors except the door of ʿAli, and he replied he had not closed their doors but Allah had closed them. > **""I did not close your doors, but Allah closed them.""**"
"14673","ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ took his hand and said Musa had asked his Lord to strengthen his mosque through Harun, while he had asked his Lord to strengthen his mosque through him and his offspring. He then sent to Abu Bakr to close his door, who answered hearing and obeying and closed it, then sent the like to ʿUmar and to al-ʿAbbas. The Messenger ﷺ then said he had not closed their doors and opened the door of ʿAli, but Allah had opened the door of ʿAli and closed theirs. > **""Musa asked his Lord to strengthen his mosque through Harun, and I asked my Lord to strengthen my mosque through you and your offspring. I did not close your doors and open the door of ʿAli, but Allah opened the door of ʿAli and closed yours.""**"
"14674","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ told him to go order them to close their doors. He went and told them, and they did so except Ḥamzah. When he reported that all had done so except Ḥamzah, the Messenger ﷺ told him to tell Ḥamzah to move his door. He conveyed that the Messenger ﷺ ordered him to move his door, so he moved it. When ʿAli returned to him while he was standing in prayer, he told him to return to his house. > **""Go and order them to close their doors. Tell Ḥamzah to move his door. Return to your house.""**"
"14675","Al-ʿAlaʾ ibn al-ʿArar reports that Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, was asked about ʿAli and ʿUthman. He said as for ʿAli, not to ask about him but to look at his station with the Messenger ﷺ, for he had closed their doors in the mosque and confirmed his. As for ʿUthman, he had committed a great fault on the day the hosts met yet Allah pardoned him, then committed a lesser fault among them and they killed him."
"14676","Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ ordered all doors closed except the door of ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him. Al-ʿAbbas asked for passage enough to enter and exit alone, but the Prophet ﷺ said he had not been commanded any of that. All were closed except the door of ʿAli, through which he sometimes passed while in major ritual impurity."
"14677","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when the people of the mosque were removed and ʿAli left, people spoke about it until it reached the Prophet ﷺ, who said he had not removed them by himself nor left him by himself, but Allah had removed them and left him. He was only a commanded servant doing what he was ordered, following only what was revealed to him. > **""I did not remove you by myself nor leave him by myself, but Allah removed you and left him. I am only a commanded servant, I follow only what is revealed to me.""**"
"14678","Muḥammad ibn ʿAli reports from Ibrahim ibn Saʿd from his father, and also in disconnected form, that some people were with the Prophet ﷺ when ʿAli entered, whereupon they left. After leaving they blamed one another, swearing by Allah they had not been expelled, so to return. The Prophet ﷺ swore by Allah that he had neither admitted him nor expelled them, but Allah had admitted him and expelled them. > **""By Allah, I did not admit him and expel you, but Allah admitted him and expelled you.""**"
"14679","Kharijah ibn Saʿd reports from his father Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that it was not lawful for anyone to be in major impurity in this mosque except him and him. > **""It is not lawful for anyone to be in major impurity in this mosque except me and you.""**"
"14680","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they used to say the best of the people of Madinah was ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib."
"14681","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he recited seventy chapters before the Messenger ﷺ and completed the Qurʾan before the best of people, ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib."
"14682","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ asked who the chief of the Arabs was. They said he was O Messenger of Allah, whereupon he said he was the chief of the children of Adam and ʿAli was the chief of the Arabs. > **""Who is the chief of the Arabs. I am the chief of the children of Adam and ʿAli is the chief of the Arabs.""**"
"14683","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that when the Messenger ﷺ was angry, none dared speak to him except ʿAli."
"14684","Zadhan reports that ʿAli narrated a report which a man denied, whereupon ʿAli said he would supplicate against him if he was lying. The man told him to do so, so he supplicated against him, and he had not left before his sight was gone."
"14685","Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said a man of Paradise would appear to them from beneath this cluster of date palms. Abu Bakr appeared, and they congratulated him with what the Messenger ﷺ had said. After a while he said again that a man of Paradise would appear from beneath this cluster, and ʿUmar appeared, and they congratulated him. He then said a man of Paradise would appear from beneath this cluster, adding O Allah, if You willed You would make him ʿAli, repeating it thrice, whereupon ʿAli appeared. > **""A man of Paradise will appear to you from beneath this cluster. O Allah, if You will, make him ʿAli.""**"
"14686","In another wording the supplication was O Allah, make him ʿAli, when announcing the man of Paradise from beneath the date palms. > **""O Allah, make him ʿAli.""**"
"14687","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while sitting with the Prophet ﷺ, he said a man of Paradise would appear to them, whereupon ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib entered, greeted, and ascended. > **""A man of Paradise will appear to you.""**"
"14688","Abu Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAli reports from his father from his grandfather that Jibril came to the Prophet ﷺ and said O Muḥammad, Allah loved three of his Companions, so to love them: ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, Abu Dharr, and al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad. Then Jibril came again and said O Muḥammad, Paradise longed for three of his Companions. Anas ibn Malik was present and hoped it might be for some of the Anṣar, but feared to ask the Messenger ﷺ. He went out, met Abu Bakr, and told him Jibril had said Paradise longed for three of his Companions, asking him to enter and inquire. Abu Bakr feared to ask lest he not be among them and his people revile him. He then said the like to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭab. Then he met ʿAli, who said yes: if he was among them he would praise Allah, and if not he would still praise Allah. ʿAli entered upon the Messenger ﷺ and said Anas had told him Jibril had said Paradise longed for three of his Companions, asking who they were. The Prophet ﷺ replied that he was among them O ʿAli, along with ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, who would witness scenes of clear virtue and great good, and Salman, who was of the household and sincere, so to take him for himself. > **""You are among them, O ʿAli, along with ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, and Salman of our household, so take him for yourself.""**"
"14689","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Jibril came to the Prophet ﷺ and said O Muḥammad, Allah loved three of his Companions, then came again saying Paradise longed for three of his Companions. Anas wished to ask the Messenger ﷺ but feared him, so he met Abu Bakr and told him Jibril had said Paradise longed for three, hoping he might be among them. He then said the like to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭab, then met ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib and told him as he had told them. ʿAli said he would ask: if among them he would praise Allah, and if not he would still praise Allah. He entered upon the Messenger ﷺ and said Anas had told him Jibril had come saying Paradise longed for three of his Companions. The Messenger ﷺ replied that he was among them, along with ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, who would witness scenes of clear virtue and great reward, and Salman of the household, so to take him as companion. > **""You are among them, you are among them, along with ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, and Salman of our household, so take him as companion.""**"
"14690","ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while the Messenger ﷺ held his hand walking in a lane of Madinah, they passed a garden and ʿAli exclaimed how fine it was. The Prophet ﷺ said he had finer than it in Paradise. They passed another and said the like, and the Prophet ﷺ repeated it, until they had passed seven gardens with the same exchange. When the road cleared for him, the Prophet ﷺ embraced him then wept aloud. When asked what made him weep, he said rancour in the hearts of people they would not reveal to him except after him. ʿAli asked whether that would be with safety in his religion, and he said with safety in his religion. > **""You have finer than it in Paradise. Rancour lies in the hearts of people they will not show you except after me, yet with safety in your religion.""**"
"14691","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he went out with the Prophet ﷺ and ʿAli in the outskirts of Madinah, passing a garden. ʿAli exclaimed how fine it was O Messenger of Allah, and the Prophet ﷺ said his garden in Paradise was finer. He then gestured with his hand toward his head and beard, then wept until his weeping rose. When asked what made him weep, he said rancour in the hearts of a people they would not reveal to him until they lost him. > **""Your garden in Paradise is finer than it. Rancour lies in the hearts of a people they will not show you until they lose me.""**"
"14692","ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥamiq, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he emigrated to the Messenger ﷺ. One day while with him, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he should show him a mount of Paradise eating food, drinking drink, and walking in the markets. When he said yes at the cost of his parents, the Prophet ﷺ pointed to ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib as that mount of Paradise. > **""O ʿAmr, shall I show you a mount of Paradise eating, drinking, and walking in the markets. This is a mount of Paradise.""**"
"14693","Salma, wife of Abu Rafiʿ, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that while with the Messenger ﷺ in the markets, he said a man of Paradise would appear to them. She then heard footsteps, and behold it was ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib. > **""A man of Paradise will surely appear to you.""**"
"14694","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said looking at ʿAli was worship. > **""Looking at ʿAli is worship.""**"
"14695","Ṭulayq ibn Muḥammad reports that he saw ʿImran ibn al-Ḥuṣayn gazing fixedly at ʿAli. When asked, he said he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say looking at ʿAli was worship. > **""Looking at ʿAli is worship.""**"
"14696","ʿAmr ibn Maymun al-Awdi reports that he was sitting with Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, when nine men came and said either to rise with them or to leave them alone with these people. Ibn ʿAbbas, then healthy before his blindness, said he would rise with them. They withdrew and spoke words he did not know, then Ibn ʿAbbas returned brushing his garment, exclaiming in disgust that they had attacked a man of ten virtues: that the Prophet ﷺ had said he would send a man Allah would never disgrace, who loved Allah and His Messenger, and all aspired to it, then asked where ʿAli was. They said he was at the baggage grinding, and he remarked none of them would grind. ʿAli came bleary-eyed hardly seeing, so the Prophet ﷺ spat in his eyes, shook the banner thrice, and gave it to him, whereupon he brought Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy. The Prophet ﷺ had sent someone with Surat al-Tawbah, then sent ʿAli after him to take it, saying none should bear it except a man from him while he was from him. He asked his cousins which of them would ally with him in this world and the next, and they refused, but ʿAli said he would ally with him in this world and the next, so he said he was his ally in this world and the next. He was the first of people to accept Islam after Khadijah. The Messenger ﷺ took his garment, placed it over ʿAli, Faṭimah, Ḥasan, and Ḥusayn, may Allah be pleased with them, and recited that Allah only wished to remove impurity from the household and purify them. ʿAli sold himself, wearing the garment of the Messenger ﷺ and sleeping in his place while the polytheists pelted him thinking him the Messenger ﷺ. Abu Bakr came while ʿAli slept, called O Prophet of Allah, and ʿAli told him the Prophet ﷺ had gone toward the well of Maymunah, so Abu Bakr went and entered the cave with him. ʿAli kept being pelted with stones as the Messenger ﷺ had been, writhing with his head wrapped until morning, when he uncovered it and they called him mean for writhing while his companion had not, which they found strange. The Prophet ﷺ went out with the people to Tabuk, and ʿAli asked to go with him, but the Prophet ﷺ said no. ʿAli wept, so he asked whether he was not pleased to be to him like Harun to Musa except that he was no prophet, for none should go except he was his successor. The Messenger ﷺ also told him he was the guardian of every believer after him. He closed the doors of the mosque except the door of ʿAli, through which he entered the mosque even in impurity as it was his only path. He said whomever he was master of, ʿAli was his master. Allah had informed them He was pleased with the companions of the tree, knowing what was in their hearts, yet never told them He became angry with them after. The Messenger ﷺ also told ʿUmar when he sought leave to strike a neck that he would do it, asking what he knew, for perhaps Allah had looked upon the people of Badr and said to do as they wished. > **""I shall send a man Allah will never disgrace, who loves Allah and His Messenger. Where is ʿAli. None shall bear it except a man from me while I am from him. Which of you will ally with me in this world and the next. You are my ally in this world and the next. Are you not pleased to be to me like Harun to Musa, except that you are no prophet, for none should go except you are my successor. You are the guardian of every believer after me. Whomever I am his master, ʿAli is his master.""**"
"14697","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that ʿAli held eighteen virtues no one else of this community held."
"14698","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that in the time of the Messenger ﷺ they used to say the best of people, then Abu Bakr, then ʿUmar. Yet the son of Abu Ṭalib had been given three traits, any one of which would be dearer to him than red camels: the Messenger ﷺ married him his daughter who bore him children, closed all doors except his in the mosque, and gave him the banner on the Day of Khaybar."
"14699","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭab said ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib had been given three traits, any one of which would be dearer to him than being given red camels. When asked what they were O Commander of the Faithful, he said the marriage to Faṭimah daughter of the Messenger ﷺ, residence in the mosque with the Messenger ﷺ with what was lawful for him therein, and the banner on the Day of Khaybar."
"14700","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUkaym reports that the Messenger ﷺ said Allah had revealed to him concerning ʿAli three things on the night he was taken by night: that he was the chief of the believers, the imam of the godfearing, and the leader of the brightly radiant. > **""Allah revealed to me concerning ʿAli that he is the chief of the believers, the imam of the godfearing, and the leader of the brightly radiant.""**"
"14701","Abu al-Ḥamraʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he saw the Messenger ﷺ come to the door of ʿAli and Faṭimah for six months, reciting that Allah only wished to remove impurity from the household and purify them. > **""Allah only wishes to remove impurity from you, O household, and to purify you thoroughly.""**"
"14702","Abu al-Ḥamraʾ, servant of the Prophet ﷺ, reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say when he was taken to heaven he entered Paradise and saw written on the leg of the Throne that none deserved worship but Allah, Muḥammad was the Messenger of Allah, whom He had strengthened by ʿAli and aided. > **""When I was taken to heaven, I entered Paradise and saw written on the leg of the Throne: none deserves worship but Allah, Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, whom I strengthened by ʿAli and aided.""**"
"14703","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say to ʿAli that Allah had adorned him with an adornment dearer to Him than any adornment of servants, the adornment of the righteous: abstinence in this world, made so he owned nothing of it and it gained nothing of him, and granted him love of the poor. > **""Allah has adorned you with an adornment dearer to Him than any other, the adornment of the righteous: abstinence in this world and love of the poor.""**"
"14704","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that while with the Messenger ﷺ in shade at Madinah seeking ʿAli, they reached a wall and saw ʿAli sleeping dusty upon the ground. The Prophet ﷺ said he did not blame people for nicknaming him Abu Turab. Seeing ʿAli's face change and the remark weigh upon him, he asked whether he should please him O ʿAli. When he said yes O Messenger of Allah, he said he was his brother and minister, settling his debts, fulfilling his pledges, and clearing his liability. Whoever loved him in life from him had fulfilled his vow, whoever loved him in life from him after him Allah would seal for him with security and faith and shelter him on the day of terror, and whoever died hating him O ʿAli died a pre-Islamic death reckoned by Allah for his deeds in Islam. > **""I do not blame people for nicknaming you Abu Turab. Shall I not please you, O ʿAli. You are my brother and minister, settling my debts, fulfilling my pledges, and clearing my liability.""**"
"14705","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ sought him and found him sleeping in a rivulet, telling him to rise and not blaming people for calling him Abu Turab. Seeing as though he found it heavy within himself, the Prophet ﷺ swore by Allah he would please him: he was his brother and father of his children, fighting for his practice and clearing his liability. Whoever died in his covenant was a treasure of Allah, whoever died in his covenant after him had fulfilled his vow, whoever died loving him after his death Allah would seal with security and faith as long as sun rose and set, and whoever died hating him died a pre-Islamic death reckoned for his deeds in Islam. > **""Rise, I do not blame people for calling you Abu Turab. By Allah, I shall please you: you are my brother and father of my children, fighting for my practice and clearing my liability.""**"
"14706","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he had suffered neither ophthalmia nor headache since the Messenger ﷺ wiped his face and spat in his eye on the Day of Khaybar when giving him the banner."
"14707","ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn Abi Layla reports that ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib came out to them in intense heat wearing winter clothes, and came out to them in winter wearing summer clothes. He then called for water from his drink, wiped sweat from his forehead, and returned to his house. The narrator told his father Abu Layla whether he had seen what the Commander of the Faithful did, going out in winter in summer clothes and in summer in winter clothes. Abu Layla said he had not noticed, so he took his son's hand to ʿAli and told him what he had done. ʿAli replied that the Messenger ﷺ had sent him while bleary-eyed, spat in his eye, told him to open his eyes, which he opened and never complained of them since, and supplicated for him asking Allah to remove heat and cold from him, so he had felt neither heat nor cold to that day. In another wording Suwayd ibn Ghafalah said they met ʿAli wearing two garments in winter and warned him not to be deceived by their land, for it was intensely cold unlike his. He said he had been cold-sensitive, but when the Messenger ﷺ sent him to Khaybar he said he was bleary-eyed, so he spat in his eye, and since then he felt neither heat nor cold nor bleary eyes. > **""Open your eyes. O Allah, remove from him heat and cold.""**"
"14708","In another wording with Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, ʿAli said he had been sensitive to cold, but when the Messenger ﷺ sent him to Khaybar and he complained of ophthalmia, he spat in his eye, after which he felt neither heat nor cold and his eyes never suffered ophthalmia."
"14709","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he saw the Messenger ﷺ line the eye of ʿAli with his saliva."
"14710","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to him when he returned from a funeral words he would not exchange for the entire world."
"14711","Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓi reports that ʿAli said he had seen himself with the Messenger ﷺ binding a stone upon his belly from hunger, while his charity from his wealth reached forty thousand dinars."
"14712","In another wording ʿAli said his charity today was forty thousand."
"14713","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that a man of the Anṣar came to the Messenger ﷺ saying the Jews had killed his brother. The Prophet ﷺ promised to hand the banner to a man who loved Allah and His Messenger and was loved by them, through whom Allah would grant victory and empower him over his brother's killer. The Companions of the Messenger ﷺ aspired to it, so he sent for ʿAli and tied the standard for him. ʿAli said he was bleary-eyed as could be seen that day, so the Prophet ﷺ spat in his eyes and they never suffered ophthalmia after, then he went forth. > **""I shall hand the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and is loved by Allah and His Messenger, through whom Allah will grant victory.""**"
"14714","Jumayʿ ibn ʿUmayr reports that he asked ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar to narrate to him about ʿAli. He said he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say on the Day of Khaybar that he would give the banner to a man who loved Allah and His Messenger and was loved by them. It was as though he still saw it with the Messenger ﷺ embracing it, while ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib was bleary from fortress smoke, so he handed it to him, and by Allah the horses had not yet rested before Allah granted him victory. > **""I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and is loved by Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"14715","Abu Layla, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said he would give the banner to a man who loved Allah and His Messenger and was loved by them, then summoned ʿAli and gave it to him. > **""I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and is loved by Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"14716","ʿImran ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said he would give the banner to a man who loved Allah and His Messenger and was loved by them, then gave it to ʿAli. > **""I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and is loved by Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"14717","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ sent to Khaybar, he thought Abu Bakr, who returned defeated with those with him. Next day he sent ʿUmar, who returned defeated, calling his companions cowardly and called cowardly by them. The Messenger ﷺ then said next morning he would give the banner to a man who loved Allah and His Messenger and was loved by them, who would not return until Allah granted him victory. The people surged, and he asked where ʿAli was. He was complaining of his eyes, so he spat in them, handed him the banner, which he shook, and Allah granted him victory. > **""Tomorrow I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and is loved by Allah and His Messenger, who will not return until Allah grants him victory. Where is ʿAli.""**"
"14718","Abu Layla reports that he said to ʿAli, with whom he used to converse at night, that people found it strange he went out in heat in stuffed garments and in winter in two light wraps. ʿAli asked whether he had not been with them, and when he said yes, ʿAli said the Prophet ﷺ had summoned Abu Bakr, tied a standard for him and sent him, but he marched with the people, was defeated, and returned. He then summoned ʿUmar, tied a standard, and he marched and returned defeated with the people. The Messenger ﷺ then said he would give the banner to a man who loved Allah and His Messenger and was loved by them, through whom Allah would grant victory, who was no fleer. He sent for ʿAli, who came seeing nothing, so he spat in his eye and said O Allah, spare him pain of heat and cold, after which neither heat nor cold ever harmed him. > **""I shall give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and is loved by Allah and His Messenger, through whom Allah will grant victory, who is no fleer. O Allah, spare him pain of heat and cold.""**"
"14719","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ took the banner, shook it, and asked who would take it by its right. Al-Zubayr came saying he would, but he told him to withdraw. Another man rose saying he would, but he told him to withdraw. Then another rose saying he would, but he told him to withdraw. The Messenger ﷺ then swore by the One who honoured the face of Muḥammad that he would give it to a man who would not flee, saying here ʿAli. He seized it and went until Allah granted him victory over Fadak and Khaybar, bringing its ʿajwah dates and dried meat. > **""Who will take it by its right. Withdraw. By the One who honoured the face of Muḥammad, I shall give it to a man who will not flee. Here, O ʿAli.""**"
"14720","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ never sent ʿAli on an expedition except he gave him the banner."
"14721","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ handed the banner to ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib when he was twenty years old."
"14722","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the verse that the Most Merciful would place affection for those who believed and did righteous deeds was revealed concerning ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, meaning love in the hearts of the believers."
"14723","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he served the Messenger ﷺ when a roast chick was presented. The Messenger ﷺ supplicated asking Allah to bring him the most beloved of creation to Him and to him to eat this chick with him. ʿAli came and knocked, and Anas asked who it was. When he said ʿAli, Anas said the Prophet ﷺ was occupied, so he left. The Messenger ﷺ moved aside and ate, then repeated the supplication. ʿAli came again knocking hard, and the Messenger ﷺ heard and asked Anas who it was. When he said ʿAli, he told him to admit him. The Messenger ﷺ said he had asked Allah thrice to bring him the most beloved of creation to Him and to him to eat the chick with him. ʿAli said he had indeed come thrice, each time turned back by Anas. The Messenger ﷺ asked Anas what had carried him to do so, and he said he had loved the call to reach a man of his people. The Messenger ﷺ said a man was not blamed for loving his people. > **""O Allah, bring me the most beloved of creation to You and to me to eat this chick with me. Admit him. A man is not blamed for loving his people.""**"
"14724","In another wording Anas was with the Prophet ﷺ in a garden when a bird was brought."
"14725","In another wording Umm Ayman gifted the Prophet ﷺ a bird between two loaves. When the Prophet ﷺ came he asked whether they had anything, so she brought him the bird. A shorter wording mentions Abu Bakr coming and turned back, then ʿUmar coming and turned back, then ʿAli coming and admitted."
"14726","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that birds were gifted to the Messenger ﷺ, which he divided among his wives, each receiving three. In the morning he was with one of his wives, Ṣafiyyah or another, who brought them to him, whereupon he supplicated asking Allah to bring him the most beloved of His creation to eat with him from this. Anas prayed within himself for a man of the Anṣar. ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, came, and the Messenger ﷺ told Anas to see who was at the door. Anas looked, found ʿAli, and said the Messenger ﷺ was occupied. He returned to stand before the Messenger ﷺ, who again told him to see who was at the door. It was ʿAli again, until this happened thrice, then he entered walking with Anas behind him. The Prophet ﷺ asked what had detained him, and he said this was the third time Anas had turned him back claiming occupation. The Messenger ﷺ asked Anas what had carried him to do so, and he said he had heard his supplication and loved it to be from his people. The Messenger ﷺ repeated thrice that a man might indeed love his people. > **""O Allah, bring me the most beloved of Your creation to eat with me from this. Look who is at the door. What detained you, may Allah have mercy on you. A man may indeed love his people.""**"
"14727","Safinah, servant of the Messenger ﷺ, reports that birds were gifted to the Messenger ﷺ, of which he prepared some. In the morning he brought it to him, who asked whence it was. When he said from what had been brought yesterday, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had not told him not to store food for tomorrow, for each day had its provision. He then supplicated asking Allah to admit to him the most beloved of His creation to eat with him from this bird. ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, entered, and the Prophet ﷺ added and to him. > **""Whence is this. Did I not tell you not to store food for tomorrow, for each day has its provision. O Allah, admit to me the most beloved of Your creation to eat with me from this bird. O Allah, and to me.""**"
"14728","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that a bird was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who supplicated asking Allah to bring him the most beloved of His creation. ʿAli came, and the Prophet ﷺ added and to him. > **""O Allah, bring me the most beloved of Your creation. O Allah, and to me.""**"
"14729","Al-Ḍaḥḥak al-Anṣari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the Prophet ﷺ marched to Khaybar he placed ʿAli at the vanguard, who announced that whoever entered the palms was safe. When the Prophet ﷺ spoke it, ʿAli proclaimed it, and the Prophet ﷺ looked at Jibril, peace be upon him, laughing. When the Messenger ﷺ asked what made him laugh, he said he loved him. The Prophet ﷺ told ʿAli that Jibril said he loved him. ʿAli asked whether he had reached that Jibril loved him, and the Prophet ﷺ said yes, and One better than Jibril, Allah the Blessed and Exalted, loved him. > **""Whoever enters the palms is safe. What makes you laugh. Jibril says he loves you, and One better than Jibril loves you.""**"
"14730","Al-Nuʿman ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Abu Bakr sought leave upon the Prophet ﷺ and heard the voice of ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saying she knew ʿAli was dearer to him than her father, twice or thrice. Abu Bakr sought leave, entered, made toward her, and said O daughter of so-and-so, he should not hear her raising her voice over the Messenger ﷺ."
"14731","Buraydah, meaning Ibn al-Ḥuṣayb, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he hated ʿAli with a hatred he had hated none, and loved a man of Quraysh for no reason except his hatred of ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him. That man was sent over an army, which Buraydah accompanied for no reason except hatred of ʿAli. They took captives, and the man wrote to the Messenger ﷺ to send one to take the fifth. The Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, and among the captives was a slave girl, finest of the captives. ʿAli took the fifth, divided, and emerged with his head dripping. When asked O Abu al-Ḥasan what this was, he said had they not seen the slave girl among the captives, for he had divided and taken the fifth, which fell to the household of the Prophet ﷺ then to the family of ʿAli, so he had relations with her. The man wrote to the Prophet ﷺ, and Buraydah asked to be sent to confirm it. While Buraydah read the letter saying it was true, the Prophet ﷺ held his hand and the letter, asking whether he hated ʿAli. When he said yes, he told him not to hate him, and if he loved him to increase in love, for by the One in whose hand was the soul of Muḥammad ﷺ, the share of the family of ʿAli in the fifth was better than a slave girl. After the word of the Messenger ﷺ, none of people became dearer to him than ʿAli. ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Buraydah, added that by the One none deserved worship but Him, none stood between him and the Prophet ﷺ in this report except his father Buraydah. > **""Do you hate ʿAli. Then do not hate him, and if you love him increase in love, for by the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, the share of the family of ʿAli in the fifth is better than a slave girl.""**"
"14732","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ sent two expeditions to Yemen, over one ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him, and over the other Khalid ibn al-Walid, saying when they met ʿAli was over the people, and when separated each was over his troops. They met Banu Zayd of Yemen, fought, Muslims prevailed over the polytheists, killing fighters and taking offspring captive, and ʿAli chose a woman of the captives for himself. Khalid ibn al-Walid wrote with Buraydah to the Messenger ﷺ informing him of it. When Buraydah brought the letter and it was read to the Prophet ﷺ, he saw anger in his face. Buraydah said O Messenger of Allah, this was the place of one seeking refuge: he had sent him with a man ordering obedience, which he had done as sent. The Messenger ﷺ replied not to speak against ʿAli, for he was from him and he from him, and he was their guardian after him. > **""When you meet, ʿAli is over the people, and when you separate, each of you is over his troops. Do not speak against ʿAli, for he is from me and I am from him, and he is your guardian after me.""**"
"14733","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ sent ʿAli as commander over Yemen and Khalid ibn al-Walid over the mountain, saying if they gathered ʿAli was over the people. They met, gained spoils like none before, and ʿAli took a slave girl from the fifth. Khalid ibn al-Walid summoned Buraydah, telling him to seize it and inform the Prophet ﷺ of what he had done. Buraydah arrived at Madinah, entered the mosque while the Messenger ﷺ was in his house with some Companions at his door. When asked the news O Buraydah, he said good: Allah had granted Muslims victory. When asked what had brought him, he said a slave girl ʿAli had taken from the fifth, come to inform the Prophet ﷺ. They told him to inform the Prophet ﷺ, for he would fall from his eye, while the Messenger ﷺ heard the talk and emerged angry, saying what was with people who belittled ʿAli: whoever belittled ʿAli had belittled him, and whoever parted from ʿAli had parted from him. ʿAli was from him and he from him, created from his clay while he was created from the clay of Ibrahim, and he was better than Ibrahim, offspring one from another, and Allah was Hearing, Knowing. O Buraydah, did he not know ʿAli held more than the slave girl he took, and that he was their guardian after him. Buraydah said O Messenger of Allah, by companionship, to extend his hand and pledge him anew upon Islam. He did not part until he had pledged him upon Islam. > **""What is with people who belittle ʿAli. Whoever belittles ʿAli belittles me, and whoever parts from ʿAli parts from me. ʿAli is from me and I am from him. Did you not know ʿAli holds more than the slave girl he took, and that he is your guardian after me.""**"
"14734","ʿAbdullah ibn Buraydah reports from ʿAli that the Messenger ﷺ sent ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib and Khalid ibn al-Walid each alone, then joined them saying when they gathered ʿAli was over them. They took right and left, ʿAli entered far, took captives, and took a slave girl of the captives. Buraydah said he was among the most intense in hatred of ʿAli. A man came to Khalid ibn al-Walid mentioning he had taken a slave girl from the fifth, asking what this was, then another came, then another, reports following upon it. Khalid summoned Buraydah, saying he knew what had been done, to carry his letter to the Messenger ﷺ. He set out with his letter until entering upon the Messenger ﷺ, who took the letter in his left, being as Allah described neither reading nor writing. Buraydah lowered his head when speaking until finishing his need, so he lowered his head, spoke, and attacked ʿAli until finished. He then raised his head and saw the Messenger ﷺ angry with an anger he had never seen except on the days of Qurayẓah and al-Naḍir. He looked at him and said O Buraydah, to love ʿAli, for he did only what he was ordered. Buraydah rose with none of people dearer to him than him. > **""O Buraydah, love ʿAli, for he does only what he is ordered.""**"
"14735","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that people complained of ʿAli, so the Messenger ﷺ stood among them addressing and he heard him say O people, not to complain of ʿAli, for by Allah he was severe in the cause of Allah or in the way of Allah. > **""O people, do not complain of ʿAli, for by Allah he is severe in the cause of Allah.""**"
"14736","ʿAmr ibn Shas al-Aslami, among the companions of al-Ḥudaybiyah, reports that he went out with ʿAli, peace be upon him, to Yemen, who treated him coldly in that journey until he found it heavy within himself. When he arrived at Madinah he openly complained of him in the mosque until the Messenger ﷺ heard of it. One morning he entered the mosque while the Messenger ﷺ sat among some Companions. When he saw him he fixed his eyes upon him with a stare until he sat, then said O ʿAmr, by Allah he had harmed him. ʿAmr said he sought refuge in Allah from harming him O Messenger of Allah, but the Prophet ﷺ said yes: whoever harmed ʿAli had harmed him. > **""O ʿAmr, by Allah you have harmed me. Whoever harms ʿAli has harmed me.""**"
"14737","Abu Rafiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ sent ʿAli as commander over Yemen, with whom went a man of Aslam called ʿAmr ibn Shas al-Aslami. He returned blaming ʿAli and complaining of him, so the Messenger ﷺ sent for him and said away with you O ʿAmr, had he seen injustice from ʿAli in his judgment or favouritism in his division. When he said no by Allah, he asked then upon what he said what had reached him. He said hatred he could not control. The Messenger ﷺ grew angry until known in his face, then said whoever hated him had hated him, and whoever hated him had hated Allah, and whoever loved him had loved him, and whoever loved him had loved Allah. > **""Away with you, O ʿAmr, did you see injustice from ʿAli in his judgment or favouritism in his division. Whoever hates him hates me, and whoever hates me hates Allah, and whoever loves him loves me, and whoever loves me loves Allah.""**"
"14738","Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqaṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he sat in the mosque with two men beside him, and they spoke against ʿAli. The Messenger ﷺ approached angry with anger known in his face, so Saʿd sought refuge in Allah from his anger. The Prophet ﷺ asked what was with them and him: whoever harmed ʿAli had harmed him. > **""What is with you and me. Whoever harms ʿAli has harmed me.""**"
"14739","Abu Bakr ibn Khalid ibn ʿUrfuṭah reports that he came to Saʿd ibn Malik and asked whether it had reached him that they proposed reviling ʿAli at Kufah, and whether he had reviled him. He said refuge in Allah: by the One in whose hand was the soul of Saʿd, he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say concerning ʿAli words such that were a saw placed upon his parting he would never revile him."
"14740","Abu ʿAbdillah al-Jadali reports that he entered upon Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, who asked whether the Messenger ﷺ was reviled among them. He said refuge in Allah, or glory be to Allah, or some such word. She said she had heard the Messenger ﷺ say whoever reviled ʿAli had reviled him. > **""Whoever reviles ʿAli has reviled me.""**"
"14741","Abu ʿAbdillah al-Jadali reports that Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, asked him O Abu ʿAbdillah, was the Messenger ﷺ reviled among them. When he asked how the Messenger ﷺ could be reviled, she asked whether ʿAli and those who loved him were not reviled, while the Messenger ﷺ had loved him."
"14742","Al-Ṭabarani then narrated with a trustworthy chain to Umm Salamah, from the Prophet ﷺ, the like of the report that whoever reviled ʿAli had reviled him. > **""Whoever reviles ʿAli has reviled me.""**"
"14743","Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said not to revile ʿAli, for he was devoted in the cause of Allah. > **""Do not revile ʿAli, for he is devoted in the cause of Allah.""**"
"14744","Abu Kathir reports that he sat with al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAli when a man came and said a man called Muʿawiyah, meaning Ibn Ḥudayj, had reviled ʿAli foully in the presence of Muʿawiyah, whom al-Ḥasan did not know. Al-Ḥasan told him to bring him when he saw him. When he saw him by the house of ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, he showed him to al-Ḥasan, who asked whether he was Muʿawiyah ibn Ḥudayj. He stayed silent and answered nothing thrice, then al-Ḥasan asked whether he was the one reviling ʿAli before the son of the liver-eater: if he came to him at the Pond, though he did not think he would come to him, he would find him with sleeves rolled and forearms bared, driving the disbelievers and hypocrites from the Pond of the Messenger ﷺ as strange camels were driven from their owner's water, by the word of the truthful believed Muḥammad ﷺ."
"14745","In another wording from ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalḥah, freedman of Banu Umayyah, Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan performed Ḥajj accompanied by Muʿawiyah ibn Ḥudayj, among the most reviling of people toward ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib. He passed through Madinah in the mosque of the Messenger ﷺ while al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAli sat there, and the like was mentioned with the addition that ruined was whoever forged lies."
"14746","ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Najih reports that ʿAli was brought gold and silver on the day of al-Baṣir, whereupon he said to become white and yellow and deceive other than him, to deceive the people of Syria tomorrow when they prevailed over it. His saying weighed upon people and was mentioned to him, so he called among the people and they entered upon him. He said his close friend ﷺ had told him O ʿAli, he would come to Allah with his partisans pleased and pleasing, while his enemy would come upon him angry with heads raised. ʿAli then joined his hand to his neck showing them the raising. > **""Become white and yellow and deceive other than me. O ʿAli, you will come to Allah with your partisans pleased and pleasing, while your enemy will come upon you angry with heads raised.""**"
"14747","Abu Rafiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said concerning ʿAli that whoever loved him had loved him, and whoever loved him Allah had loved; whoever hated him had hated him, and whoever hated him had hated Allah. > **""Whoever loves him loves me, and whoever loves me Allah loves; whoever hates him hates me, and whoever hates me hates Allah.""**"
"14748","With the same chain it is reported that the Messenger ﷺ sent ʿAli on an expedition, and when he returned the Messenger ﷺ told him Allah, His Messenger, and Jibril were pleased with him. > **""Allah, His Messenger, and Jibril are pleased with you.""**"
"14749","With the same chain it is reported that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that he and his partisans would come to the Pond quenched and watered with whitened faces, while his enemy would come to the Pond thirsty with heads raised. > **""You and your partisans will come to the Pond quenched and watered with whitened faces, while your enemy will come to the Pond thirsty with heads raised.""**"
"14750","With the same chain it is reported that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli asking whether he was not pleased that he was his brother while he was his brother. > **""Are you not pleased that you are my brother while I am your brother.""**"
"14751","With the same chain it is reported that the Messenger ﷺ said the first four to enter Paradise were he, ʿAli, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn, with their offspring behind them, their wives behind their offspring, and their partisans to their right and left. > **""The first four to enter Paradise are I, you, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn, with our offspring behind us, our wives behind our offspring, and our partisans to our right and left.""**"
"14752","With the same chain it is reported that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli swearing by the One in whose hand was his soul that were it not that groups of his community would say concerning him what the Christians said concerning ʿIsa son of Maryam, he would say concerning him that day words such that no Muslim he passed except would take dust from his footsteps seeking blessing. > **""By the One in whose hand is my soul, were it not that groups of my community will say concerning you what the Christians said concerning ʿIsa son of Maryam, I would say concerning you words such that no Muslim you passed except would take dust from your footsteps seeking blessing.""**"
"14753","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said O Anas, go summon for him the chief of the Arabs, meaning ʿAli. ʿAʾishah asked whether he was not the chief of the Arabs, and he replied he was the chief of the children of Adam while ʿAli was the chief of the Arabs. When ʿAli came, the Messenger ﷺ sent for the Anṣar, who came to him, and told them O company of Anṣar, should he not guide them to what if they held fast they would never stray after. When they said yes O Messenger of Allah, he said this was ʿAli, so to love him for his love and honour him for his honour, for Jibril, peace be upon him, had commanded him with what he told them from Allah. > **""O Anas, go summon for me the chief of the Arabs. I am the chief of the children of Adam and ʿAli is the chief of the Arabs. O company of Anṣar, shall I not guide you to what if you hold fast you will never stray after. This is ʿAli, so love him for my love and honour him for my honour.""**"
"14754","Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to ʿAli that whoever loved him loved him, and whoever hated him hated him. > **""Whoever loves you loves me, and whoever hates you hates me.""**"
"14755","Abu Maryam al-Thaqafi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say to ʿAli O ʿAli, glad tidings for whoever loved him and spoke truth concerning him, and woe for whoever hated him and lied concerning him. > **""O ʿAli, glad tidings for whoever loves you and speaks truth concerning you, and woe for whoever hates you and lies concerning you.""**"
"14756","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger ﷺ say to ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib that Allah had adorned him with an adornment beyond any adornment of servants: Allah had made the poor beloved to him and nearness to them, made him an imam for them pleased with them, and made them followers for him pleased with him. Glad tidings for whoever loved him and spoke truth of him, woe for whoever hated him and lied of him. Those who loved him and spoke truth of him would be his neighbours in his abode and companions in his Paradise, while those who hated him and lied of him Allah would halt at the stations of liars. > **""Allah has adorned you with an adornment beyond any adornment of servants. Glad tidings for whoever loves you and speaks truth of you, and woe for whoever hates you and lies of you.""**"
"14757","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, testifies that she heard the Messenger ﷺ say whoever loved ʿAli had loved him, and whoever loved him had loved Allah; whoever hated ʿAli had hated him, and whoever hated him had hated Allah. > **""Whoever loves ʿAli loves me, and whoever loves me loves Allah; whoever hates ʿAli hates me, and whoever hates me hates Allah.""**"
"14758","Faṭimah daughter of the Messenger ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger ﷺ came out to them on the evening of ʿArafah and said Allah had boasted of them, forgiven them generally and ʿAli specifically. He was the Messenger of Allah to them without favouring his kin: Jibril informed him the truly fortunate was whoever loved ʿAli in his life and after his death, and the utterly wretched was whoever hated ʿAli in his life and after his death. > **""Allah has boasted of you and forgiven you generally and ʿAli specifically. I am the Messenger of Allah to you without favouring my kin. The truly fortunate is whoever loves ʿAli in his life and after his death, and the utterly wretched is whoever hates ʿAli in his life and after his death.""**"
"14759","Jabir ibn ʿAbdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, swears by Allah that on the covenant of the Messenger ﷺ they knew their hypocrites only by their hatred of ʿAli. In another wording they, the Anṣar, knew their hypocrites by it."
"14760","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger ﷺ looked at ʿAli and said none loved him except a believer and none hated him except a hypocrite. Whoever loved him had loved him, whoever hated him had hated him; his beloved was the beloved of Allah, his hated the hated of Allah; woe for whoever hated him after him. > **""None loves you but a believer and none hates you but a hypocrite. Whoever loves you loves me, and whoever hates you hates me; my beloved is the beloved of Allah and my hated is the hated of Allah. Woe for whoever hates you after me.""**"
"14761","ʿImran ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli that none loved him except a believer and none hated him except a hypocrite. > **""None loves you but a believer and none hates you but a hypocrite.""**"
"14762","ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ summoned him and said in him was a likeness of ʿIsa: the Jews hated him until they slandered his mother, and the Christians loved him until they raised him to a station not his. Two would perish concerning him: an extravagant lover praising him with what was not in him, and a hater whose hatred carried him to slander him. He was no prophet nor revelation came to him, but he acted by the Book of Allah and the practice of His Prophet as he could, so whatever he commanded of obedience to Allah, obedience to him in loved and disliked was due. > **""In you is a likeness of ʿIsa: the Jews hated him until they slandered his mother, and the Christians loved him until they raised him to a station not his. I am no prophet nor is revelation sent to me, but I act by the Book of Allah and the practice of His Prophet.""**"
"14763","Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they sat awaiting the Messenger ﷺ, who emerged from one of his wives' houses. They rose with him, his sandal broke, and ʿAli stayed behind to mend it while the Messenger ﷺ went on with them. He then stopped awaiting him and they stopped with him, saying among them was one who would fight for the interpretation of this Qurʾan as he had fought for its revelation. They looked up, among them Abu Bakr and ʿUmar, but he said no: it was the mender of the sandal. They came to give him glad tidings, as though he had already heard it. > **""Among you is one who will fight for the interpretation of this Qurʾan as I fought for its revelation. No, but it is the mender of the sandal.""**"
"14764","Abu Rafiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he entered upon the Messenger ﷺ while he slept or revelation came to him, with a snake at the side of the house. Disliking to kill it lest he wake him, he lay between him and the snake, so if anything came it would be upon him rather than him. The Prophet ﷺ awoke reciting the verse that their guardian was Allah, His Messenger, and those who believed. He praised Allah, saw him beside him, and asked what had laid him there. When he said the place of this snake, he told him to rise and kill it, which he did. He praised Allah, took his hand, and said O Abu Rafiʿ, after him would be a people fighting ʿAli, upon Allah the duty of striving against them: whoever could not strive with his hand then with his tongue, whoever could not with his tongue then with his heart, with nothing beyond that. > **""All praise is due to Allah. Rise and kill it. O Abu Rafiʿ, after me will be a people fighting ʿAli, upon Allah the duty of striving against them: whoever cannot with his hand then with his tongue, whoever cannot with his tongue then with his heart.""**"
"14765","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that ʿAli used to say in the lifetime of the Messenger ﷺ that Allah said if he died or was killed they would turn upon their heels. By Allah they would not turn upon their heels after Allah had guided them: by Allah, if he died or was killed he would fight upon what he had fought until he died. By Allah he was his brother, guardian, cousin, and heir, so who was more entitled to him than him."
"14766","ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the Messenger ﷺ conquered Makkah he turned to al-Ṭaʾif, besieging it seventeen or eighteen nights without conquering it, then went on an evening or morning journey, halted, then rested at midday, saying O people, he was their forerunner, commending his family to them with good, with their appointment at the Pond. By the One in whose hand was his soul, they would establish prayer and give charity, or he would send to them a man from him or of himself to strike the necks of their fighters and take their offspring captive. People thought it was Abu Bakr or ʿUmar, but he took the hand of ʿAli and said this was he. > **""O people, I am your forerunner and I commend my family to you with good, and your appointment is the Pond. I shall send to them a man from me. This is he.""**"
"14767","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that she heard the Messenger ﷺ say ʿAli was with the Qurʾan and the Qurʾan with ʿAli, never parting until they came to him at the Pond. > **""ʿAli is with the Qurʾan and the Qurʾan is with ʿAli, never parting until they come to me at the Pond.""**"
"14768","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to say ʿAli was upon truth: whoever followed him followed truth, and whoever left him left truth, a covenant made before this day of his."
"14769","Jurayy ibn Samurah reports that when what occurred between the people of Baṣrah and ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib occurred, he set out to Madinah and came to Maymunah bint al-Ḥarith of Banu Hilal, greeting her. When asked whence the man was, he said from the people of Iraq, from Kufah, from Banu ʿAmir. She welcomed kinship upon kinship and ease upon ease, asking what had brought him. He said what had occurred between ʿAli and Ṭalḥah had occurred, so he had come and pledged ʿAli. She told him to join him, for by Allah he had neither strayed nor been led astray, repeating it thrice."
"14770","ʿAli ibn Rabiʿah reports that he heard ʿAli when a man came saying O Commander of the Faithful, why did he see him driving people as a man drove his camels: by covenant from the Messenger ﷺ or something he had seen. ʿAli replied by Allah he had neither lied nor been lied to, neither strayed nor been led astray, but by covenant from the Messenger ﷺ, with the verse that ruined was whoever forged."
"14771","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli O ʿAli, whoever parted from him had parted from Allah, and whoever parted from him O ʿAli had parted from him. > **""O ʿAli, whoever parts from me parts from Allah, and whoever parts from you, O ʿAli, parts from me.""**"
"14772","Abu Saʿid, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said O ʿAli, with him on the Day of Resurrection would be a staff of Paradise staves, with which he would drive the hypocrites from his Pond. > **""O ʿAli, with you on the Day of Resurrection will be a staff of Paradise staves, with which you will drive the hypocrites from my Pond.""**"
"14773","ʿAbdullah ibn Ijara ibn Qays reports that he heard the Commander of the Faithful ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib upon the minbar say he would drive from the Pond of the Messenger ﷺ with these two short hands of his the disbelievers and hypocrites as waterers drove strange camels from their troughs."
"14774","ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ told him whether he was not pleased that when Allah gathered the prophets in one plain barefoot unclothed walking with thirst cutting their necks, the first called would be Ibrahim, clothed in two white garments, standing at the right of the Throne, then a channel burst from Paradise to his Pond, whose expanse exceeded between Buṣra and Ṣanʿaʾ with silver cups as the stars of heaven. He would drink, perform ablution, be clothed in two white garments, and stand at the right of the Throne, then ʿAli would be called to drink, perform ablution, be clothed in two white garments, and stand with him, never called to any good except called to it with him. > **""Are you not pleased, O ʿAli, that when Allah gathers the prophets in one plain, I shall drink and be clothed and stand at the right of the Throne, then you shall be called to drink and be clothed and stand with me, never called to any good except you are called to it.""**"
"14775","ʿAmmar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he and ʿAli were two companions in the campaign of Dhāt al-ʿAshirah. When the Messenger ﷺ halted there and stayed, they saw people of Banu Mudlij working at their spring among palms. ʿAli said O Abu al-Yaqẓan, should they go see how they worked. They watched their work a while, then sleep overcame them, so they lay in a palm nursery upon dusty ground and slept. None woke them but the Messenger ﷺ moving them with his foot, dusted from that dust, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ called ʿAli Abu Turab for the dust upon him. He then asked whether he should tell them of the most wretched of people, two men. When they said yes O Messenger of Allah, he said the red man of Thamud who hamstrung the she-camel, and the one who would strike ʿAli here, meaning his forehead, until this, meaning his beard, was wetted from it. > **""O Abu Turab. Shall I not tell you of the most wretched of people, two men: the red man of Thamud who hamstrung the she-camel, and the one who will strike you here until this is wetted from it.""**"
"14776","Ṣuhayb reports from the Prophet ﷺ that one day he asked ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, who the most wretched of the ancients was. When ʿAli said the one who hamstrung the she-camel O Messenger of Allah, he said he had spoken true. He then asked who the most wretched of the later was. When ʿAli said he had no knowledge O Messenger of Allah, he said the one who would strike him here, pointing the Prophet ﷺ to his crown. ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say to the people of Iraq that he wished their most wretched had risen to dye this, meaning his beard, from this, placing his hand upon the front of his head. > **""Who is the most wretched of the ancients. You have spoken true. The most wretched of the later is the one who will strike you here.""**"
"14777","Jabir, meaning Ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ asked ʿAli who the most wretched of Thamud was. When he said the one who hamstrung the she-camel, he asked who the most wretched of this community was. When ʿAli said Allah knew best, he said his killer. > **""Who is the most wretched of Thamud. The one who hamstrung the she-camel. The most wretched of this community is your killer.""**"
"14778","Jabir reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was a man appointed yet to be slain, with this dyed from this, meaning his beard from his head. > **""You are a man appointed, yet to be slain, with this dyed from this.""**"
"14779","Faḍalah ibn Abi Faḍalah al-Anṣari reports that he went out with his father visiting ʿAli when ill. His father asked what kept him in this dwelling: were he to perish in it none but Bedouins of Juhaynah would manage him, so were he to enter Madinah he would be among his companions, and if what he feared befell him his companions would manage him. His father was of the people of Badr. ʿAli told him he would not die of this sickness or pain: the Prophet ﷺ had covenanted to him that he would not die until struck, or this dyed from this, meaning his striker. Abu Faḍalah was later killed with him at Ṣiffin."
"14780","Abu Sinan al-Duʾali reports that he visited ʿAli in a complaint of which he complained, telling him they had feared for him in this complaint. ʿAli replied that by Allah he had not feared for himself from it, for he had heard the truthful believed ﷺ say he would be struck a blow here and a blow here, pointing to his temple, whose blood would flow until dyeing his beard, its striker the most wretched as the hamstringer of the she-camel was the most wretched of Thamud. > **""You will be struck a blow here and a blow here, until its blood dyes your beard, and its striker will be the most wretched as the hamstringer of the she-camel was the most wretched of Thamud.""**"
"14781","Abu Sinan Yazid ibn Murrah al-Dayli reports that ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib fell severely ill near death, and they feared for him, then he recovered and convalesced. When they congratulated him O Abu al-Ḥasan with praise to Allah who had healed him, saying they had feared for him, he said he had not feared for himself: the truthful believed ﷺ had told him he would not die until struck upon this, pointing to the front left of his head, dyeing this from it with blood, taking his beard and saying his killer was the most wretched of this community, as the hamstringer of the she-camel of Allah was the most wretched of a clan of Thamud, whom the Messenger ﷺ traced to its nearer thigh apart from Thamud."
"14782","ʿAbdullah ibn Subayʿ reports that he heard ʿAli, peace be upon him, say this would surely be dyed from this, so what did the most wretched await for him. When asked O Commander of the Faithful to tell them of him to destroy his family, he said then they would kill for him other than his killer. When asked to appoint a successor over them, he said no, but to leave them to what the Messenger ﷺ had left them. When asked what he would say to his Lord when he came to Him, he said he would say O Allah, he had left him among them as long as it pleased Him, then He took him to Him while He was among them: if He willed He would set them right, and if He willed He would corrupt them."
"14783","Thaʿlabah reports that ʿAli said upon the minbar swearing by Allah it was the covenant of the unlettered Prophet ﷺ to him that the community would betray him."
"14784","ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that she saw the Prophet ﷺ embrace ʿAli, kiss him, and say at the cost of his father the lone martyr, at the cost of his father the lone martyr. > **""At the cost of my father, the lone martyr, the lone martyr.""**"
"14785","Abu Rafiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ said to ʿAli before his death that he would clear his liability and be slain upon his practice. > **""You will clear my liability and be slain upon my practice.""**"
"14786","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿAbdullah ibn Salam came to him while he had placed his foot in the stirrup, telling him not to advance to Iraq, fearing the edge of the sword would strike him there. ʿAli replied by Allah the Messenger ﷺ had already told him of it. Abu al-Aswad said he had never seen as that day a warrior informing of this concerning himself."
"14787","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that ʿAli said O Messenger of Allah, he had told him on the Day of Uḥud when kept back from martyrdom that martyrdom lay behind him. The Prophet ﷺ asked how he would fare when this was dyed from this, gesturing with his hand to his beard and head. ʿAli replied that since he had clarified what he clarified, that was no occasion of patience but of glad tidings and honour. > **""Martyrdom lies behind you. How will you fare when this is dyed from this.""**"
"14788","Abu Ṣaliḥ, meaning al-Ḥanafi, reports from ʿAli that he saw the Prophet ﷺ in his dream and complained to him of what he met from his community of disobedience and contention, whereupon he wept. The Prophet ﷺ told him not to weep O ʿAli but to turn, so he turned to find two men ascending with boulders crushing their heads until split, then returning. The narrator went to ʿAli as he used to go each day until among the tanners he met people telling him the Commander of the Faithful had been killed. > **""Do not weep, O ʿAli, but turn.""**"
"14789","Abu al-Ṭufayl reports that ʿAli summoned them to allegiance, among whom came ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn Muljam, whom he had already seen twice before. He then said what detained the most wretched of them: by the One in whose hand was his soul, this would surely be dyed from this. He recited two verses commanding to gird for death, for death would come, and not to panic of death, for death lay in its valley."
"14790","ʿAwanah ibn al-Ḥakam reports that when ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn Muljam struck ʿAli and he was carried to his house, visitors came to him. He praised Allah, extolled Him, invoked blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, then said every man would meet what he fled from, the term drove the soul, and flight from its ills. How often he had driven people seeking the hidden of this affair, but Allah refused except its concealment: far a stored knowledge. His counsel to them was Allah: to associate nothing with Him, and Muḥammad ﷺ: not to neglect his practice; to uphold these two pillars, with blame removed unless they strayed; each man bore his effort, with the ignorant lightened by a merciful Lord, upright religion, and knowing imam. They had been in winds and scattered branches beneath a cloud whose centre had dissolved, when heights cast them down, their days closing in succession, then air, after which they would leave a hollow lifeless body silent after speech, more eloquent to considerers than eloquent speech. Their caller called watched for meeting: tomorrow they would see his days and his secrets uncovered. Allah would not favour him except he draw near Him with piety, forgiving excess of promise. Peace upon them on the day of reckoning: if he lived he was guardian of his blood, if he perished then perishing was his appointment; pardon was his ransom and their good, so to pardon as Allah pardoned them, with the verse whether they would not love Allah to forgive them, for Allah was Forgiving, Merciful. He then recited verses that however long one lived death was his palace with no departure nor escape, while wealth of a house and its joy vanished and the house collapsed, wondering what was intended by them though wishing availed little."
"14791","Ismaʿil ibn Rashid reports the account of Ibn Muljam, may Allah curse him and his companions: that ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn Muljam, al-Burk ibn ʿAbdillah, and ʿUmar ibn Bakr al-Tamimi gathered at Makkah, discussed the affairs of people, criticised the conduct of their rulers, then mentioned the people of al-Nahrawan with mercy, saying by Allah what would they do remaining after them: their brothers who had summoned people to worship their Lord fearing no blame in Allah. Were they to sell themselves, come to the imams of misguidance, seek to kill them, relieve the lands of them, and avenge their brothers. Ibn Muljam of Egypt said he would suffice them against ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib; al-Burk ibn ʿAbdillah said he would suffice them against Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan; ʿAmr ibn Bakr al-Tamimi said he would suffice them against ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ. They pledged and covenanted by Allah none would turn from his target until killing him or dying before him, took poisoned swords, and appointed the seventeenth passed of Ramaḍan for each to assault his target. Each set out to the city of his target. Ibn Muljam al-Muradi came to his companions at Kufah concealing his affair, met his companions of Taym al-Ribab whom ʿAli had killed in number on the Day of the River, and they mourned their slain. That day he met a woman of Taym al-Ribab called Qaṭam bint al-Shiḥnah, whose father and brother ʿAli had killed on the Day of the River, surpassingly beautiful. Seeing her distracted his mind and he forgot his need, proposing to her. She said she would not marry until he healed her: he asked what she desired, and she said three thousand, a slave and singing girl, and the killing of ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib. He said it was her dowry, though doubting she meant the killing of ʿAli. She said yes: to seek his unguarded moment, for if he struck him he would heal himself and her, and life with her would benefit him, while if killed what was with Allah was better than this world and its splendour. He said only the killing of ʿAli had brought him to this city. She told him to inform her to seek helpers to strengthen and aid him, sending for Wardān of her people of Taym al-Ribab, who agreed, while Ibn Muljam approached Shabib ibn Najdah of Ashjaʿ asking whether he desired the honour of this world and the next in killing ʿAli. When Shabib exclaimed at the enormity and how he could kill him, he proposed ambush in the dawn: when ʿAli emerged for morning prayer they would assault and kill him; if they escaped they would heal themselves and attain vengeance, if killed what was with Allah was better. Shabib said had it been other than ʿAli it would be easier, knowing his tribulation in Islam and precedence with the Prophet ﷺ, finding no ease for his killing. Ibn Muljam reminded him he had killed the worshipping praying people of al-Nahrawan, so they agreed to kill him for their brothers. They entered upon Qaṭam in the great mosque in retreat, agreeing upon the killing of ʿAli. On the appointed night she bound them with silk, they took swords and sat opposite the door from which ʿAli emerged. ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, emerged for morning prayer calling prayer, prayer, whereupon Shabib assaulted but his sword struck the doorposts or arch, then Ibn Muljam assaulted striking his crown and fled. Others seized killers in the ensuing tumult, Jaʿdah ibn Hubayrah led morning prayer, and people crowded from every side. Muḥammad ibn Ḥunayf swore he prayed that night near the door among many townspeople in standing, bowing, and prostration unwearied from first to last, until ʿAli emerged calling O people, prayer, prayer. Whether he spoke the words or saw the gleam of swords, he heard judgment belongs to Allah, not to ʿAli nor his companions, saw swords and people, heard ʿAli say not to let the man escape, and people crowded from every side. He did not move until Ibn Muljam was seized and brought to ʿAli. When brought, ʿAli asked O enemy of Allah, had he not done good to him: he said yes. He asked what had carried him to this: he said he had sharpened it forty mornings asking Allah to kill with it the worst of His creation. ʿAli told him he saw none killed by it but him, and none but among the worst of Allah's creation. Ibn Muljam stood bound before al-Ḥasan when Umm Kulthum daughter of ʿAli wept calling O enemy of Allah, no harm upon her father while Allah would disgrace him. He asked why she wept: by Allah he had bought it for a thousand and poisoned it for a thousand, and were this blow upon all townspeople none would survive an hour, yet her father still lived. ʿAli told al-Ḥasan if he lived he would judge concerning him, and if he perished of this blow to strike him one blow without mutilation, for he had heard the Messenger ﷺ forbid mutilation even of a rabid dog. Jundab ibn ʿAbdillah entered asking ʿAli, if they lost him yet not lost him, should they pledge al-Ḥasan. He said he neither commanded nor forbade them; they knew best. When ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, passed, al-Ḥasan sent for Ibn Muljam, who offered a trait: by Allah he never gave Allah a covenant except he fulfilled it, having covenanted to kill ʿAli and Muʿawiyah or die before them, so if al-Ḥasan freed him to Muʿawiyah, by Allah if he did not kill him he would return to place his hand in his. Al-Ḥasan swore by Allah no until he faced the Fire, brought him forward and killed him, whereupon people wrapped him in mats and burned him with fire. ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, had said O sons of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, not to find them plunging into Muslim blood saying the Commander of the Faithful was killed: none should be killed for him except his killer. As for al-Burk ibn ʿAbdillah, he waited for Muʿawiyah emerging for morning prayer, assaulted with his sword while Muʿawiyah fled, the sword striking his buttock. He claimed news to gladden him: if he told him would it benefit him. When asked what, he said his brother had killed ʿAli that night. Muʿawiyah doubted his power, but he said ʿAli emerged with none guarding him, so Muʿawiyah ordered him killed. He summoned al-Saʿidi the physician, who said his wound was poisoned: to choose heated iron placed upon the sword's place, or a draught cutting offspring yet healing him, for the wound was poisoned. Muʿawiyah said no patience for fire, while in Yazid, ʿAbdullah, and their children was0 coolness of his eye, so he drank the draught that night and healed, with none born to him after. Thereafter Muʿawiyah ordered screened enclosures and guards standing over him. ʿAli counselled al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, and Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah with piety, prayer at its time, charity at its place, good ablution, forgiveness of faults, restraint of anger, joining kin, forbearance toward the ignorant, understanding in religion, deliberation in affairs, care of the Qurʾan, good neighbourliness, commanding right, forbidding wrong, and avoiding indecencies, enjoining honour to the brothers and no decision without them. His testament witnessed none deserved worship but Allah alone without partner and Muḥammad was His servant and Messenger sent with guidance and true religion to prevail over all religion though polytheists disliked; his prayer, rites, life, and death belonged to Allah Lord of worlds without partner, as commanded among the Muslims. He counselled al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, all family, children, and whomever his letter reached with piety to their Lord, dying only as Muslims, holding to the rope of Allah together without division, for he had heard the Messenger ﷺ say rectifying relations surpassed general prayer and fasting. To join kin easing reckoning; Allah in orphans not neglected in their presence; Allah in prayer, pillar of their religion; Allah in charity extinguishing the anger of the Lord; Allah in poor and needy shared in livelihood; Allah in the Qurʾan lest others precede in acting by it; Allah in striving with wealth and selves; Allah in the house of their Lord never left while they remained, for if abandoned they would not be respited; Allah in the covenant of their Prophet ﷺ not wronged among them; Allah in neighbours, the counsel of their Prophet ﷺ, for Jibril kept counselling of them until he thought he would bequeath them; Allah in the Companions of their Prophet ﷺ, for he had counselled of them; Allah in the two weak of women and what their right hands possessed, the last word of the Prophet ﷺ commending the two weak. Prayer, prayer, fearing no blame in Allah, Allah sufficing whoever intended and transgressed them, speaking good to people as Allah commanded. Not to abandon commanding right and forbidding wrong lest their worst rule them, then they supplicated unanswered. Upon connection and exchange, beware severance, turning backs, and division, with the verse to cooperate in righteousness and piety not in sin and aggression, fearing Allah severe in punishment. May Allah preserve them of the household and preserve in them their Prophet ﷺ. He entrusted them to Allah and greeted them with peace. Then he uttered only none deserved worship but Allah until taken in Ramaḍan of year forty, washed by al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, and ʿAbdullah ibn Jaʿfar, shrouded in three garments without shirt, with al-Ḥasan pronouncing nine takbirs over him, then al-Ḥasan managed affairs six months. Poems were recited on the dowry of Qaṭam, the killing of the best of people in the sacred month, and the escape of ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ when his police chief Kharijah ibn Ḥabib was killed in his place, with Muʿawiyah's verses to ʿAmr thereupon. The bearer of his death news was Sufyan ibn ʿAbd Shams; al-Ḥasan sent Qays ibn Saʿd ibn ʿUbadah with twelve thousand in his vanguard; Muʿawiyah halted at Iliyaʾ that year; al-Ḥasan halted at the white palaces in al-Madaʾin; Muʿawiyah halted at Maskan. Al-Mukhtar ibn Abi ʿUbayd, then a young lad, proposed to his uncle Saʿd ibn Masʿud governing al-Madaʾin to bind al-Ḥasan and seek authority through him to Muʿawiyah, but Saʿd cursed him for assaulting the son of the daughter of the Messenger ﷺ. When al-Ḥasan saw people disperse from him, he sent to Muʿawiyah seeking truce, who sent ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmir and ʿAbdullah ibn Samurah, who came to al-Ḥasan at al-Madaʾin, granted what he sought, and made peace. Al-Ḥasan then stood among people saying O people of Iraq, only three of himself kept him from them: their killing his father, stabbing him, and plundering his goods. He entered the obedience of Muʿawiyah, entered Kufah, and people pledged him."
"14792","Abu Yaḥya reports that when Ibn Muljam struck ʿAli, peace be upon him, the blow, ʿAli said to do with him as the Messenger ﷺ had intended to do with a man who sought to kill him: to kill him, then burn him. > **""Kill him, then burn him.""**"
"14793","Musa ibn Ṭalḥah reports that ʿAli, al-Zubayr, and Saʿd ibn Abi Waqqaṣ were of one year at weaning, born in the same year."
"14794","Muḥammad ibn ʿAli ibn al-Ḥusayn reports that ʿAli passed away at fifty-eight years."
"14795","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reports that ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib was killed on Friday, the seventeenth day of Ramaḍan, in year forty."
"14796","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah reports that ʿAli was killed in year forty, his caliphate lasting five years and six months."
"14797","ʿAbdullah ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqil reports that ʿAli was killed in year forty."
"14798","Abu al-Ṭufayl reports that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib addressed them, praising Allah and extolling Him, mentioning the Commander of the Faithful ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, as seal of successors, legatee of prophets, and trustee of the truthful and martyrs. He then said O people, a man had parted them unmatched by the first and unreached by the last: the Messenger ﷺ used to give him the banner, with Jibril fighting at his right and Mikaʾil at his left, never returning until Allah granted him victory. Allah took him on the night the legatee of Musa was taken, his spirit ascended on the night the spirit of ʿIsa son of Maryam ascended, on the night Allah sent down the Criterion. By Allah he left neither gold nor silver, with only seven hundred and fifty dirhams remaining of his stipend in his treasury, intended to buy a servant for Umm Kulthum. He then said whoever knew him knew him, and whoever did not: he was al-Ḥasan son of Muḥammad ﷺ. He recited the saying of Yusuf that he had followed the creed of his fathers Ibrahim, Isḥaq, and Yaʿqub, then took to the Book of Allah, saying he was son of the bringer of glad tidings, son of the warner, son of the Prophet, son of the summoner to Allah by His leave, son of the luminous lamp, son of the one sent as mercy to worlds, of the household from whom Allah removed impurity and purified, of the household whose affection and loyalty Allah had obligated, with the verse revealed to Muḥammad ﷺ to ask no reward but affection for kin. In another wording Yushaʿ ibn Nun the lad of Musa was killed therein, on the twenty-seventh night of Ramaḍan, or the twenty-first of Ramaḍan, with Jibril fighting at his right when battle grew fierce."
"14799","In another wording Yushaʿ ibn Nun the lad of Musa was killed therein. Al-Ṭabarani narrated it briefly except that he said the twenty-seventh night of Ramaḍan; Abu Yaʿla briefly; al-Bazzar likewise except that he said he gave him the banner, and when battle grew fierce Jibril fought at his right; it was the twenty-first of Ramaḍan."
"14800","Abu ʿUbaydah Maʿmar ibn al-Muthanna reports that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah was son of ʿUthman ibn ʿAmir ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik. His mother was al-Ṣaʿbah daughter of al-Ḥaḍrami, called al-Ḥaḍrami for dwelling in Ḥaḍramawt, where he killed ʿAmr ibn Nahid al-Ḥimyari then fled to Makkah and allied with Ḥarb ibn Umayyah. The name of al-Ḥaḍrami was ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmir ibn Rabiʿah of Kinda. Al-Ṣaʿbah was sister of al-ʿAlaʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍrami, her mother ʿAtikah daughter of Wahb of Quṣayy."
"14801","Musa ibn Ṭalḥah reports that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah was white inclining to red, of medium stature nearer short, broad-chested, wide-shouldered closing together when met, large-footed."
"14802","Al-Waqidi reports that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah was wheat-complexioned, abundantly haired neither curly nor lank, handsome-faced, fine-nosed, quick when walking, never dyeing his grey hair. He was killed on the Day of the Camel in Jumada in year thirty-six."
"14803","Qabiṣah ibn Jabir reports that he never saw a man give lavish wealth unasked like Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah. Sufyan said his family said the Messenger ﷺ had named him al-Fayyaḍ, the lavish."
"14804","Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger ﷺ named him on the Day of Uḥud Ṭalḥah al-Khayr, in the campaign of Tabuk, of Dhu al-ʿAshirah, Ṭalḥah al-Fayyaḍ, and on the Day of Ḥunayn Ṭalḥah al-Jud. > **""On Uḥud you are Ṭalḥah al-Khayr, on Tabuk Ṭalḥah al-Fayyaḍ, and on Ḥunayn Ṭalḥah al-Jud.""**"
"14805","Musa ibn Ṭalḥah reports that Ṭalḥah slaughtered a camel and dug a well on the Day of Dhu Qarad, feeding and watering them, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said O Ṭalḥah al-Fayyaḍ, so he was named Ṭalḥah al-Fayyaḍ. > **""O Ṭalḥah al-Fayyaḍ.""**"
"14806","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah bought a well by the mountain, slaughtered a camel, and fed people, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said he was O Ṭalḥah al-Fayyaḍ. > **""You are, O Ṭalḥah, al-Fayyaḍ.""**"
"14807","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reports that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah was nicknamed Abu Muḥammad."
"14808","Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥya reports from his grandmother Suʿda that Ṭalḥah entered upon her one day and she saw heaviness in him. When she asked what was with him, whether something from them had disturbed and hidden him, he said no: she was the best wife of a Muslim man, but wealth had gathered with him and he knew not what to do with it. She asked what grieved him of it: to summon his people and divide it among them. He called his servant for his people, and she asked the treasurer how much he had divided: four hundred thousand."
"14809","ʿAmr ibn Dinar reports that the yield of Ṭalḥah each day was a full thousand."
"14810","ʿUrwah reports that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah ibn ʿUthman ibn ʿAmr ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Taym ibn Murrah was in Syria, then came and spoke to the Messenger ﷺ concerning his share, so he struck his share for him. When he asked about his reward O Messenger of Allah, he said and his reward, meaning on the Day of Badr. > **""And your reward.""**"
"14811","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they mentioned the Day of Uḥud while the Prophet ﷺ stood praying. When he finished and turned from his prayer toward them, he said should he not tell them of the Day of Uḥud: none was with him except Jibril at his right and Ṭalḥah at his left. > **""Shall I not tell you of the Day of Uḥud: none was with me except Jibril at my right and Ṭalḥah at my left.""**"
"14812","ʿAʾishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that by Allah while in a house one day with the Messenger ﷺ and his Companions in the courtyard, a curtain between her and them, Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillah approached, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ said whoever was pleased to look at a man walking upon earth who had fulfilled his vow should look at Ṭalḥah. > **""Whoever is pleased to look at a man walking upon earth who has fulfilled his vow, let him look at Ṭalḥah.""**"
"14813","Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that whenever the Prophet ﷺ saw him he announced his rank among the martyrs. > **""Whoever wishes to look at a martyr walking upon the face of the earth, let him look at Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh.""**"
"14814","Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the Day of Uḥud he carried the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon his back until he reached the rock and was shielded from the polytheists, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ gestured behind his back and gave him tidings concerning Jibrīl, peace be upon him. > **""This is Jibrīl. He has informed me that he will not see you on the Day of Resurrection in any terror except that he will rescue you from it.""**"
"14815","Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the Day of Uḥud an arrow struck him and he cried out in pain, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ directed him to the remembrance of Allah at calamity. > **""If you had said Bismillah, the angels would have flown with you while the people looked at you.""**"
"14816","Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that whenever the Prophet ﷺ saw him he honored him with a declaration of closeness in both abodes. > **""My predecessor in this world and my predecessor in the Hereafter.""**"
"14817","Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that saddling the mount of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was entrusted to him. A man repeatedly sought that duty through the Prophet ﷺ, and Ṭalḥa twice refused until he reasoned that the Prophet ﷺ wished to fulfill the man's need, so he yielded it, preferring the pleasure of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ over retaining the service. When the man saddled poorly and the mount threw the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ asked who had saddled it and commanded its return. > **""Return it to Ṭalḥa.""** Ṭalḥa added that by Allah he never deceived anyone in Islam except that man, intending only that the saddle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ return to him."
"14818","Al-Ḥārith al-Hamdānī reported that he was with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, when the son of Ṭalḥa came. ʿAlī welcomed him as his nephew, seated him beside himself, and expressed hope that he and his father were among those of whom Allah says: > **""And We shall remove whatever rancour is in their breasts.""**"
"14819","ʿĪsā ibn Ṭalḥa reported that Ṭalḥa was sixty-two years old on the day he was killed, and al-Wāqidī added that he was killed on the Day of al-Jamal in Jumādā in the year thirty-six."
"14820","Al-Muhājir ibn Qunfudh reported that Ṭalḥa was killed at the age of sixty-four and was buried at al-Baṣra in the quarter of Thaqīf."
"14821","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reported that Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh was killed on the Day of al-Jamal in Jumādā in the year thirty-six at fifty-two or fifty-four years, that al-Zubayr was older than him, and that his kunya was Abū Muḥammad."
"14822","Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim reported that he saw Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam shoot Ṭalḥa on that day with an arrow that struck the eye of his knee, and he continued glorifying Allah until he died."
"14823","Ṭalḥa ibn Muṣarrif reported that ʿAlī came upon Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbaydillāh after he had died, dismounted, raised him, wiped dust from his face and beard, invoked mercy upon him, and lamented the day. > **""Would that I had died twenty years before this day.""**"
"14824","Qays ibn ʿAbbād reported that he witnessed ʿAlī on the Day of al-Jamal saying to his son al-Ḥasan in grief over the strife: > **""O Ḥasan, would that I had died twenty years ago.""**"
"14825","Al-Ṭabarānī recorded that al-Zubayr was the son of al-ʿAwwām ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy, his kunya Abū ʿAbdillāh, and his mother Ṣafiyya, may Allah be pleased with her, the paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"14826","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reported that al-Zubayr's kunya was Abū ʿAbdillāh."
"14827","ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUrwa ibn al-Zubayr reported that al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, was fair-skinned, tall, lean-bodied, and light in the cheeks."
"14828","ʿUrwa reported that among those of Banū Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām ibn Khuwaylid ibn Asad."
"14829","ʿUrwa reported that al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, was tall so that his feet touched the ground when he rode a mount, and he was hairy, with hair at times reaching his shoulders."
"14830","ʿUrwa reported that the first to draw a sword in the path of Allah was al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him. > **""The first to draw a sword in the path of Allah was al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām.""**"
"14831","An elder from al-Mawṣil reported that he accompanied al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, on a journey. In a barren land al-Zubayr became ritually impure and asked him to screen him. Glancing at him, he saw his body marked with sword cuts and exclaimed he had never seen such marks on anyone. Al-Zubayr asked if he had seen them, and when he confirmed, al-Zubayr declared their honor. > **""By Allah, there is not one wound among them except that it was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and in the path of Allah.""**"
"14832","Muṭīʿ ibn al-Aswad reported that he heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, say that if he were to make a covenant or leave a trust, he would most love to entrust it to al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, for he was a pillar of the religion. > **""He is a pillar from the pillars of the religion.""**"
"14833","Abū al-Aswad reported that al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, accepted Islam at eight years and emigrated at eighteen. His uncle wrapped him in a mat and smoked him with fire, commanding him to return to disbelief, but al-Zubayr refused. > **""I will never disbelieve.""**"
"14834","Hishām ibn ʿUrwa reported that al-Zubayr accepted Islam at sixteen, never stayed back from any battle fought by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and was killed in his sixties near al-Baṣra at about a barīd's distance."
"14835","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that every Prophet has a disciple, and al-Zubayr is his disciple and the son of his paternal aunt. > **""Every Prophet has a disciple, and al-Zubayr is my disciple and the son of my paternal aunt.""**"
"14836","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār reported that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with them both, met on the Day of al-Jamal. ʿAlī asked al-Zubayr that if he would not fight alongside them, then not to aid against them. Al-Zubayr asked if ʿAlī wished him to withdraw, and ʿAlī affirmed, honoring him as the son of the Prophet's ﷺ paternal aunt, the son of his maternal uncle, his disciple meaning his closest confidant, his kinsman through ʿĀʾisha and Asmāʾ, daughters of Abū Bakr, and his sword as the first to draw a sword in Allah's path, descended through his mother Ṣafiyya and through the clan of Āmina bint Wahb. > **""If you will not fight with us, then do not aid against us.""**"
"14837","ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every Prophet has a disciple and his disciple is al-Zubayr. > **""Every Prophet has a disciple, and my disciple is al-Zubayr.""**"
"14838","Nāfiʿ reported that Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, heard a man address someone as O son of the disciple of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon he remarked that this applies only if he is of the family of al-Zubayr. > **""If you are of the family of al-Zubayr, then yes; otherwise, no.""**"
"14839","Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him on a cold night or cold morning. When he returned, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with one of his wives under a blanket and threw over him the edge of the garment."
"14840","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that al-Zubayr sought ʿUmar's permission for jihād, and ʿUmar told him to remain, for he had already fought with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Remain, for you have already fought with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"14841","Al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for him, his children, and his grandchildren, and he heard his father telling his elder sister that she was among those reached by that supplication. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for me, for my children, and for the children of my children.""**"
"14842","Ibn ʿAwn reported that the righteous were killed, then wept and recounted how the killer of al-Zubayr repeatedly confronted him. Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, turned upon him, but the man invoked Allah and al-Zubayr restrained himself, until after repeated instances al-Zubayr denounced him for invoking Allah yet forgetting Him."
"14843","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reported that al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed on the Day of al-Jamal in Jumādā in the year thirty-six. Al-Layth conveyed from Abū al-Aswad from ʿUrwa that al-Zubayr accepted Islam at eight with the kunya Abū ʿAbdillāh, so that if the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained in Makkah thirteen years he was fifty-seven when killed, and if ten years then fifty-four."
"14844","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr reported that al-Zubayr was killed at sixty-four in the year thirty-six."
"14845","Hishām ibn ʿUrwa reported that al-Zubayr accepted Islam at sixteen and was killed in his sixties."
"14846","Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that Ḥassān composed verses that al-Zubayr abided by the covenant and guidance of the Prophet ﷺ as his disciple whose word is matched by deed, a famed knight and hero charging on the appointed day, cleaving battle with a swift white blade hastening to death, whose mother is Ṣafiyya and whose house is of Asad, worthy of such hope."
"14847","Saʿd, meaning Ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ asking his lineage, and the Prophet ﷺ affirmed it and warned against altering it. > **""Saʿd ibn Mālik ibn Uhayb ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Zuhra. Whoever says other than that, upon him is the curse of Allah.""**"
"14848","Muṣʿab ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Zubayrī reported that the mother of Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ was Ḥamna bint Sufyān ibn Umayya ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf, and her mother was the daughter of Abū Sarḥ ibn Ḥabīb ibn Judhayma ibn Naṣr ibn Mālik ibn Ḥisl ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib."
"14849","ʿĀʾisha bint Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that her father was short, stout, thick-set with a large head and coarse fingers, and he witnessed Badr."
"14850","Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿd reported that Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, had curly hair, a hairy body, was tall, and flat-nosed."
"14851","ʿĀmir, meaning al-Shaʿbī, reported that Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked when his supplication was granted. He recalled shooting arrows before the Prophet ﷺ at Badr while supplicating against the enemy, and the Prophet ﷺ invoking acceptance for him. > **""O Allah, answer Saʿd.""**"
"14852","Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ heard him supplicating and invoked acceptance for his future supplications. > **""O Allah, answer him when he calls upon You.""**"
"14853","Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab reported that a slave girl of Saʿd called Zīrā went out in a silk shirt which the wind lifted, so ʿUmar struck her with a stick. When Saʿd came to prevent him he too was struck, and intended to supplicate against ʿUmar, whereupon ʿUmar handed him the stick for retaliation and Saʿd pardoned him."
"14854","Qays, meaning Ibn Abī Ḥāzim, reported a dispute over wealth between Ibn Masʿūd and Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with them both, in which harsh lineage words were exchanged. Hāshim ibn ʿUtba reminded them that people looked to them as Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Saʿd cast down his staff and raised his hand in supplication, but at Ibn Masʿūd's plea not to curse he fell silent, declaring that were it not for fear of Allah he would have supplicated against him with an unfailing supplication."
"14855","ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd reported that while Saʿd was walking he passed a man reviling ʿAlī, Ṭalḥa, and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them. Saʿd warned him to desist or face his supplication, but the man mocked him as though a prophet. Saʿd then prayed that if the man reviled those favored by Allah, Allah make him an example that day. A camel scattered the people and trampled him, and onlookers told Saʿd that Allah had answered him. > **""O Allah, if he is reviling peoples for whom there has preceded favor from You, make him an example today.""**"
"14856","Qabīṣa ibn Jābir reported that on the Day of al-Qādisiyya a cousin composed verses claiming Allah sent victory while Saʿd clung to the gate and that many women were widowed except Saʿd's women. When it reached Saʿd he prayed against the man's tongue and hand. An arrow struck his mouth rendering him mute, then his hand was cut in battle. Saʿd then had himself carried to the gate, uncovered his back showing wounds, explained his excuse, and the people excused him, for he was no coward."
"14857","In another narration concerning the same incident, the man fought until Allah sent His victory, and it was added that his hand was cut and he was killed."
"14858","Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to scout enemy news. He hastened out but returned walking calmly lest the enemy think him fleeing. When the Prophet ﷺ questioned the difference, Saʿd explained, and the Prophet ﷺ praised his judgment. > **""Saʿd is indeed experienced.""**"
"14859","Jābir ibn Samura, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the first to shoot an arrow alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was Saʿd."
"14860","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the first to shoot an arrow in the path of Allah was Saʿd."
"14861","Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a polytheist had scorched the Muslims, so the Prophet ﷺ joined to him his own parents in encouragement to shoot. Saʿd loosed a blunt arrow striking the man's side so he fell exposed, and the Prophet ﷺ laughed until his molars were seen. > **""O Saʿd, shoot, may my father and mother be ransom for you.""**"
"14862","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the Day of Badr Saʿd fought both as horseman and foot soldier."
"14863","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet ﷺ announced the first to enter through a door would be of Paradise, and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ entered. > **""The first to enter upon you through this door is a man of Paradise.""**"
"14864","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet ﷺ announced for three days that a man of Paradise would enter, and on each occasion Saʿd entered. > **""A man of Paradise will enter upon you.""**"
"14865","Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that food lay before the Prophet ﷺ who prayed for Allah to send a servant whom He loves and who loves Him to it, whereupon Saʿd himself appeared. > **""O Allah, send to this food a servant whom You love and who loves You.""**"
"14866","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that on the Day of Uḥud the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ to engage the enemy. Saʿd placed his arrow in his bow dedicating it to Allah and asking victory for His Messenger, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed for acceptance of his supplications. > **""O Allah, answer Saʿd when he calls upon You.""**"
"14867","ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that during a journey she felt lonely on a dark night and feared for the Prophet ﷺ. He assured her Allah would send a man loving Allah and His Messenger to guard them, and Saʿd ibn Mālik appeared offering to guard the remainder of the night, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laid his head and slept. > **""No; Allah will send for us a man who loves Allah and His Messenger to guard us the rest of our night. Who is this? I am Saʿd ibn Mālik, come to guard you the rest of this night.""**"
"14868","Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he witnessed Badr with the Prophet ﷺ when he had but one hair, meaning one daughter, then Allah increased his beard, meaning his offspring."
"14869","ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd reported that Saʿd was the last of the Emigrants to die, may Allah be pleased with him."
"14870","Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal reported that Saʿd died at eighty-three, ten miles from Madīna, was carried on men's necks to Madīna when Marwān was its governor, and had accepted Islam at seventeen."
"14871","Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd reported that Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ died in Muʿāwiya's era after his first ḥajj at eighty-three."
"14872","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr reported that Saʿd died when Marwān governed Madīna and led his funeral prayer, in the year fifty-five."
"14873","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār reported that Saʿd died at al-ʿAqīq in his palace ten miles from Madīna and was carried on men's necks to Madīna, and it is said he was in his seventies."
"14874","Shabāb al-ʿUṣfurī reported the lineage of Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Rabāḥ ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Qurṭ ibn Razāḥ ibn ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb, his kunya Abū al-Aʿwar, and his mother Fāṭima bint Naʿja ibn Umayya ibn Khuwaylid of Khuzāʿa."
"14875","ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī reported that Saʿīd ibn Zayd was dark, tall, and fair-reddish."
"14876","ʿUrwa reported that Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl arrived from al-Shām after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Badr, spoke with him, and the Prophet ﷺ struck for him his share. When he asked about his reward, the Prophet ﷺ assured him of it. > **""And your reward.""**"
"14877","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named ten of Quraysh in Paradise: Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥa, al-Zubayr, Saʿd, Saʿīd, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. > **""Ten of Quraysh are in Paradise: Abū Bakr and ʿUmar are in Paradise, ʿUthmān is in Paradise, ʿAlī is in Paradise, Ṭalḥa is in Paradise, al-Zubayr is in Paradise, Saʿd is in Paradise, Saʿīd is in Paradise, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf is in Paradise, and Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ is in Paradise.""**"
"14878","Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Muʿāwiya wrote to Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam in Madīna to secure allegiance for his son Yazīd, and a Syrian said the people would not respond until Saʿīd ibn Zayd pledged, for he was the noblest of the city whose pledge the people would follow."
"14879","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reported that Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl died in the year fifty-one in his seventies, died at al-ʿAqīq, was buried in Madīna, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and Ibn ʿUmar descended into his grave, and his kunya was Abū al-Aʿwar."
"14880","ʿĀʾisha bint Saʿd reported that Saʿd washed Saʿīd ibn Zayd at al-ʿAqīq, they carried him forth, Saʿd walked until opposite his house, entered, bathed, then clarified he bathed from heat not from washing Saʿīd."
"14881","Zayd ibn Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿīd ibn Zayd reported that Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ washed Saʿīd with warm water."
"14882","Abū ʿUbayda Maʿmar ibn al-Muthannā reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf was son of ʿAbd ʿAwf ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥārith ibn Zuhra ibn Kilāb."
"14883","Ibn Sīrīn reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf's name in Jāhiliyya was ʿAbd al-Kaʿba, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān."
"14884","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that his name in Jāhiliyya was ʿAbd ʿAmr and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān."
"14885","Ibn Isḥāq reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf ibn ʿAbd ʿAwf ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥārith ibn Zuhra, kunya Abū Muḥammad, witnessed Badr."
"14886","ʿUrwa ibn al-Zubayr reported that among Banū Zuhra ibn Kilāb ibn Murra who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf ibn ʿAbd ʿAwf."
"14887","Ibn Isḥāq reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf had fallen front teeth, used both hands, and limped, having been struck on Uḥud with twenty or more wounds, some in his leg causing his limp."
"14888","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were born in the same year in kinship of age, and that he was among the first people to accept Islam."
"14889","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him concerning his wealth and commanded charity that frees one's steps. > **""O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, you are among the rich. You will not enter Paradise except crawling, so lend to Allah and He will free your feet.""** ʿAbd al-Raḥmān asked what he should lend, then departed, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent word commanding him to honor the guest, feed the poor, and give the beggar, for that suffices for much of what weighs upon him."
"14890","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the first of the rich of his umma to enter Paradise would be ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, yet only crawling. > **""The first of the rich of my umma to enter Paradise is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, he will not enter it except crawling.""**"
"14891","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he was shown Paradise and saw none entering it but the poor, and he entered with them crawling. When he awoke he resolved that his camels awaited in al-Shām with their loads would be in the path of Allah so he might enter walking with them."
"14892","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while ʿĀʾisha was in her house she heard a noise in Madīna and was told it was a caravan of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf arriving from al-Shām with seven hundred camels bearing every provision, shaking the city with sound. She recalled hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say he had seen ʿAbd al-Raḥmān entering Paradise crawling. When this reached ʿAbd al-Raḥmān he declared that if able he would enter it standing, and devoted the camels with their halters and loads in the path of Allah. > **""I have seen ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf entering Paradise crawling.""**"
"14893","Busra bint Ṣafwān, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked her who had proposed to Umm Kulthūm bint ʿUqba. When she named several men including ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, he commanded her marriage to him, describing him among the best of Muslims and his like among their best. > **""Marry her to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, for he is among the best of Muslims, and among their best is whoever is like him.""**"
"14894","In another narration the Prophet ﷺ asked why they neglected ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, describing him as a master of the Muslims and among their best. > **""Where are you with regard to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, for he is a master of the Muslims and among their best.""**"
"14895","Al-Miswar ibn Makhrama, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf sold a garden to ʿUthmān for forty thousand dīnārs, and ʿUthmān ordered ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ to pay the price. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān divided it among Banū Zuhra, the poor Muslims, and the wives of the Prophet ﷺ. When al-Miswar brought the share to ʿĀʾisha, she recalled the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying only the patient would show kindness to them after him, and she prayed that Allah give Ibn ʿAwf to drink from Salsabīl of Paradise. > **""None will show kindness to you after me except the patient. May Allah give Ibn ʿAwf to drink from Salsabīl of Paradise.""**"
"14896","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the best of them would be the best to his wives after him, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān bequeathed them property sold for forty thousand. > **""The best of you are the best of you to my wives after me.""**"
"14897","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say only the patient and truthful would show affection to his wives, whereupon he sold property to ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ for forty thousand and divided it among the wives of the Prophet ﷺ. > **""None will show affection to you except the patient and truthful.""**"
"14898","Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to his wives that he who shows kindness to them after him is truthful and righteous, and she heard his supplication for ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf. > **""Indeed he who shows kindness to you after me is the truthful, righteous one. O Allah, give ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf to drink from Salsabīl of Paradise.""**"
"14899","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Awfā reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf complained of Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with them, to the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ defended the merit of a man of Badr beyond the weight of Uḥud in gold, and when Khālid replied they criticized him and he answered back, the Prophet ﷺ forbade harming Khālid, describing him as a sword Allah unsheathed upon the disbelievers. > **""O Khālid, why do you harm a man of Badr? Had you spent the like of Uḥud in gold you would not reach his deed. Do not harm Khālid, for he is a sword among the swords of Allah which Allah has poured upon the disbelievers.""**"
"14900","Al-Zuhrī reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf gave half his wealth in the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, four thousand dirhams, then forty thousand, then forty thousand dīnārs, then five hundred horses in the path of Allah, then fifteen hundred camels in the path of Allah, and most of his wealth was in trade."
"14901","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he was with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey when the Prophet ﷺ withdrew for his need and prayer time arrived. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān stepped forward to lead, and the Prophet ﷺ arrived and prayed a rakʿa behind him with the people. When he gave salām he approved their act. In the wording of Aḥmad and al-Bazzār, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān intended to step back but the Prophet ﷺ gestured him to remain in place and prayed with his prayer. > **""You did right, or you did well.""**"
"14902","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said no Prophet was taken until a man of his umma had led him in prayer. > **""No Prophet was taken until a man of his umma has led him in prayer.""**"
"14903","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted him and ʿUmar land. Al-Zubayr, may Allah have mercy on him, bought the share of the family of ʿUmar and came to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān citing ʿAbd al-Raḥmān's claim, whereupon ʿUthmān affirmed the validity of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān's testimony for and against. > **""ʿAbd al-Raḥmān's testimony is valid, for and against him.""**"
"14904","Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf reported that he heard ʿAlī on the day ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf died say that Ibn ʿAwf had departed having attained its purity and avoided its turbidity."
"14905","In another narration ʿAlī said ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf had departed safeguarding his religion without any deficiency."
"14906","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf was born twenty years after the Elephant, died in thirty-one or thirty-two at seventy-five, and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with them both, prayed over him."
"14907","Ibn Isḥāq reported that Abu ʿUbayda was ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Jarrāḥ ibn Hilāl ibn Uhayb ibn Ḍabba ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Fihr, left no offspring, and his mother was Umm Ghānm bint Jābir ibn ʿUdayy ibn al-ʿAdāʾ ibn ʿĀmir ibn ʿUmayra ibn Wadīʿa ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Fihr."
"14908","ʿUrwa reported that among Banū al-Ḥārith ibn Fihr who witnessed Badr was Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ, may Allah be pleased with him."
"14909","Ibn Shawdhab reported that the father of Abū ʿUbayda repeatedly exposed himself to him on the Day of Badr while Abū ʿUbayda turned away, until at his persistence Abū ʿUbayda faced and killed him. Allah then revealed the verse that no believing people befriend those opposing Allah and His Messenger even if fathers, sons, brothers, or kin, those in whose hearts faith is written and strengthened with a spirit from Him, admitted to Gardens beneath which rivers flow, Allah pleased with them and they with Him, the party of Allah, the successful. > **""You will not find a people believing in Allah and the Last Day befriending those opposing Allah and His Messenger, even if they are their fathers, sons, brothers, or kin.""**"
"14910","Abū al-Bakhtarī reported that Abū Bakr said to Abū ʿUbayda to extend his hand for allegiance, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ call him the trustworthy of this umma. Abū ʿUbayda replied he would not step before a man whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had appointed to lead them until his death. > **""You are the trustworthy of this umma.""**"
"14911","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that al-ʿĀqib and al-Sayyid of Najrān came intending to invoke mutual curse with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but one warned the other that if he were a Prophet and they cursed him neither they nor their descendants would prosper. They offered payment and asked for a trustworthy man to be sent with them. The companions of Muḥammad ﷺ looked forward eagerly, and the Prophet ﷺ appointed Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. > **""I shall send a man truly trustworthy, truly trustworthy. Rise, O Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. This is the trustworthy of this umma.""**"
"14912","Shurayḥ ibn ʿUbayd, Rāshid ibn Saʿd, and others reported that when ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb reached Sargh and learned of severe plague in al-Shām, he declared that if his term came while Abū ʿUbayda lived he would appoint him, answering Allah that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say every Prophet has a trustworthy and his trustworthy is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. The people objected concerning the nobility of Quraysh, meaning Banū Fihr. He added that if Abū ʿUbayda had died he would appoint Muʿādh ibn Jabal, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say he would be raised on Resurrection Day ahead of the scholars by a span. > **""Every Prophet has a trustworthy, and my trustworthy is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. He will be raised on the Day of Resurrection ahead of the scholars by a span.""**"
"14913","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said every Prophet has a trustworthy and the trustworthy of this umma is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. > **""Every Prophet has a trustworthy, and the trustworthy of this umma is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ.""**"
"14914","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say every umma has a trustworthy and the trustworthy of this umma is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. > **""Every umma has a trustworthy, and the trustworthy of this umma is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ.""**"
"14915","Khālid ibn al-Walīd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say every umma has a trustworthy and the trustworthy of this umma is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ. > **""Every umma has a trustworthy, and the trustworthy of this umma is Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ.""**"
"14916","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ held a staff and gestured to the flank of Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ, describing it as believing. > **""This is a believing flank.""**"
"14917","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reported that Abū ʿUbayda died in the plague of ʿAmawās in the year eighteen at fifty-eight, witnessed Badr at forty-one, and it is said Muʿādh ibn Jabal prayed over him."
"14918","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the distinctions of his Companions: Abū Bakr most merciful, ʿUmar most gentle, ʿUthmān most truthful in modesty, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib most judicious, Muʿādh ibn Jabal most learned in lawful and unlawful who comes before scholars by a span, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb best reciter, Zayd ibn Thābit best in inheritance, and ʿUwaymir, meaning Abū al-Dardāʾ, given worship. > **""The most merciful of my umma to my umma is Abū Bakr, the gentlest of my umma to my umma is ʿUmar, the most truthful of my umma in modesty is ʿUthmān, the most judicious of my umma is ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, the most learned in lawful and unlawful is Muʿādh ibn Jabal who comes on Resurrection Day ahead of the scholars by a span, the best reciter of my umma is Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, the best in inheritance is Zayd ibn Thābit, and ʿUwaymir has been given worship.""**"
"14919","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the kindest is Abū Bakr, the sternest in Islam is ʿUmar, the truest in modesty is ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, the most judicious is ʿAlī, the best in inheritance is Zayd ibn Thābit, the most learned in lawful and unlawful is Muʿādh ibn Jabal, the best reciter is Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and every umma has a trustworthy whose trustworthy here is Abū ʿUbayda. > **""The kindest of my umma to my umma is Abū Bakr, the sternest of them in Islam is ʿUmar, the truest of them in modesty is ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, the most judicious of them is ʿAlī, the best of them in inheritance is Zayd ibn Thābit, the most learned of them in lawful and unlawful is Muʿādh ibn Jabal, the best reciter of them is Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and every umma has a trustworthy and the trustworthy of this umma is Abū ʿUbayda.""**"
"14920","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on Ḥirāʾ when the mountain shook, and he commanded it to be still for none was upon it but a Prophet, a truthful one, or a martyr. Upon it were the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥa, al-Zubayr, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, Saʿd, and Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with them. > **""Remain firm, O Ḥirāʾ. There is none upon you but a Prophet, a truthful one, or a martyr.""**"
"14921","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that ʿUthmān adjured the people whether they knew the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended Uḥud with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar until it trembled, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded it to be still for none was upon it but a Prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs. > **""Remain firm, O Uḥud. There is none upon you but a Prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs.""**"
"14922","ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among ten companions including Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, and al-Zubayr on Mount Ḥirāʾ and it moved, the Prophet ﷺ commanded it to abide calmly for none was upon it but a Prophet, a truthful one, or a martyr. > **""Abide calmly, O Ḥirāʾ, for there is none upon you but a Prophet, a truthful one, or a martyr.""**"
"14923","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he seemed to see them behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿAlī, ʿUthmān, Ṭalḥa, al-Zubayr, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with them."
"14924","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that when ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb was stabbed and appointed the council, his daughter Ḥafṣa told him people deemed its members displeasing. He asked to be raised and defended each: ʿUthmān, for whom angels of heaven pray at death; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who relieved the Prophet's ﷺ hunger with two loaves and was promised sufficiency in this world and guarantee in the next; Ṭalḥa, who righted the Prophet's ﷺ saddle by night and was promised Paradise; al-Zubayr, who fanned the Prophet's ﷺ face in sleep and was greeted by Jibrīl with protection from Hell's sparks; ʿAlī, whose hand is with the Prophet's ﷺ hand entering wherever he enters; and Saʿd, whose supplication the Prophet ﷺ asked Allah to answer. > **""On the day ʿUthmān dies the angels of heaven will pray over him. Allah suffice you in your worldly affair, and as for the Hereafter I am its guarantor. To you is Paradise from me tomorrow, O Ṭalḥa. This is Jibrīl conveying salām to you and saying he will ward the sparks of Hell from your face on Resurrection Day. O ʿAlī, your hand is with my hand on Resurrection Day, entering wherever I enter. O Allah, answer Saʿd when he calls upon You.""**"
"14925","Zayd ibn Abī Awfā, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque of Madīna while he summoned the men one by one until they gathered. He announced a narration to preserve and convey: Allah selects from creation, reciting the verse that Allah selects messengers from angels and men, and he would select among them and join them in brotherhood as Allah joined angels. He joined Abū Bakr to himself as his shirt to his body, ʿUmar beside him as third of three in Paradise, ʿUthmān drawn knee to knee with glad tidings of the Pond though his veins bleed blood, and continued joining al-Zubayr with Ṭalḥa, ʿAlī with ʿAmmār, Ubayy with Ibn Masʿūd, Abū al-Dardāʾ with Salmān, Saʿd with Ṣuhayb, Abū Dharr with Bilāl, Usāma with Abū Hind the cupper, and Abū Ayyūb with ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām. > **""I shall narrate to you a narration, preserve it, comprehend it, and narrate it to those after you. Rise, O Abū Bakr. Draw near, O ʿUmar. Draw near to me, O ʿUthmān. You are brothers in this world and brothers in Paradise, so let each greet and shake the hand of his fellow.""**"
"14926","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to his companions assembled and said he had seen their homes in Paradise and their nearness. Turning to Abū Bakr he described a man known by name and parentage whom every gate of Paradise welcomes, namely Abū Bakr ibn Abī Quḥāfa. To ʿUmar he described a white pearl palace of Quraysh reserved for ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, withheld from entering only by his protective jealousy. To ʿUthmān he promised companionship in Paradise. Taking ʿAlī's hand he asked his pleasure at a home facing his own. To Ṭalḥa and al-Zubayr he named them his disciples. To ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf he noted his delay from accounting over abundant wealth, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān wept over his standing for reckoning of earning and spending. > **""I saw last night your homes in Paradise and the nearness of your homes. O Abū Bakr, I know a man whose name, father's name, and mother's name I know, who comes to no gate of Paradise but they say welcome, welcome. O ʿUmar, I saw in Paradise a palace of white pearl adorned with ruby and was told it is for ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb. O ʿUthmān, every Prophet has a companion in Paradise and you are my companion in Paradise. O ʿAlī, are you not pleased that your home in Paradise faces mine? O Ṭalḥa and O Zubayr, every Prophet has a disciple and you two are my disciples.""**"
"14927","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the forerunners are four: himself for the Arabs, Salmān for Persia, Bilāl for Abyssinia, and Ṣuhayb for Rome. > **""The forerunners are four: I am the forerunner of the Arabs, Salmān is the forerunner of Persia, Bilāl is the forerunner of Abyssinia, and Ṣuhayb is the forerunner of Rome.""**"
"14928","Abū Umāma al-Bāhilī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say he is the forerunner of the Arabs to Paradise, Ṣuhayb of Rome, Bilāl of Abyssinia, and Salmān of Persia. > **""I am the forerunner of the Arabs to Paradise, Ṣuhayb is the forerunner of Rome to Paradise, Bilāl is the forerunner of Abyssinia to Paradise, and Salmān is the forerunner of Persia to Paradise.""**"
"14929","Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir al-Jumaḥī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and joined them as brothers in this world and Paradise by revealed command, smiling that one precedes and dies before the other. He likewise joined al-Zubayr with Ṭalḥa, ʿAlī with ʿAmmār as brothers for Allah, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb with Ibn Masʿūd, Abū al-Dardāʾ with Salmān, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ with Ṣuhayb, Abū Dharr with Bilāl, Usāma with Abū Hind the cupper who cupped the Prophet ﷺ, and Abū Ayyūb with ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām, commanding each to greet and shake hands. > **""I have been commanded to join you in brotherhood by revelation sent down upon me from heaven, so you are brothers in this world and brothers in Paradise. Let each of you greet his fellow and shake his hand.""**"
"14930","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named Abū Bakr most merciful, ʿUmar most gentle, ʿUthmān most truthful in modesty, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib most judicious, Muʿādh ibn Jabal most learned in lawful and unlawful coming before scholars by an elevation, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb best reciter, Zayd ibn Thābit most understanding, and ʿUwaymir, meaning Abū al-Dardāʾ, given worship. > **""The most merciful of my umma to my umma is Abū Bakr, the gentlest of my umma to my umma is ʿUmar, the most truthful of my umma in modesty is ʿUthmān, the most judicious of my umma is ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, the most learned of them in lawful and unlawful is Muʿādh ibn Jabal who comes on Resurrection Day ahead of the scholars by an elevation, the best reciter of my umma is Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, the most understanding of them is Zayd ibn Thābit, and ʿUwaymir has been given worship.""**"
"14931","ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Paradise longs for four of his companions and his Lord commanded him to love them. When Ṣuhayb al-Rūmī, Bilāl ibn Rabāḥ, Ṭalḥa, al-Zubayr, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, Ḥudhayfa ibn al-Yamān, and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir asked who the four were, he told ʿAmmār that Allah had made him know the hypocrites, and named the four as ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad al-Kindī, Salmān al-Fārisī, and Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī. > **""Paradise longs for four of my companions, and my Lord has commanded me to love them. One of them is ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad al-Kindī, the third is Salmān al-Fārisī, and the fourth is Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī.""**"
"14932","Burayda, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came saying his Lord loves four of his companions and commands him to love them. Asked to name them, he first said ʿAlī is among them, and next morning named them as ʿAlī, Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī, al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad, and Salmān al-Fārisī. > **""Your Lord loves four of your companions and commands you to love them. ʿAlī, Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī, al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad, and Salmān al-Fārisī.""**"
"14933","Nāfiʿ reported from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that when told he praised ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd well, he recalled hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command learning the Qurʾān from four: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfa, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and Muʿādh ibn Jabal. He added his wish to dispatch them as ʿĪsā dispatched disciples, but explained Abū Bakr and ʿUmar were indispensable as hearing and sight to the head. > **""Learn the Qurʾān from four: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfa, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and Muʿādh ibn Jabal. I had intended to send them to the nations as ʿĪsā sent the disciples. I have no sufficiency without them; they are to the religion as hearing and sight to the head.""**"
"14934","ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that three of the Anṣār had none preferred above them after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr, and ʿAbbād ibn Bishr."
"14935","ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Zayd ibn Ḥāritha went to Makkah and brought the daughter of Ḥamza ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib claimed her as his cousin with her maternal aunt in his house, for the maternal aunt is a mother. ʿAlī claimed greater right through kinship and the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in his house, raising his voice so the Prophet ﷺ heard his plea. Zayd claimed right by journeying for her. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out asking their affair, then judged: Zayd is his and her freed ally, Jaʿfar resembles him in creation and character from his tree, ʿAlī is his elite and trustworthy, and the girl goes to Jaʿfar with her maternal aunt. > **""What is your affair? I shall judge between you. As for you, O Zayd, you are my freed ally and hers. As for you, O Jaʿfar, you resemble my creation and character, and you are from my tree from which I was created. As for you, O ʿAlī, you are my elite and my trustworthy. As for the girl, I judge her for Jaʿfar to be with her maternal aunt, for the maternal aunt is a mother.""**"
"14936","ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every Prophet was given seven noble companions and ministers, while he was given fourteen: Ḥamza, Jaʿfar, ʿAlī, Ḥasan, Ḥusayn, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Abū Dharr, al-Miqdād, Ḥudhayfa, ʿAmmār, Salmān, and Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with them. > **""No Prophet existed but was given seven companions, noble and ministers, and I have been given fourteen: Ḥamza, Jaʿfar, ʿAlī, Ḥasan, Ḥusayn, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Abū Dharr, al-Miqdād, Ḥudhayfa, ʿAmmār, Salmān, and Bilāl.""**"
"14937","Sahl ibn Yūsuf ibn Sahl reported from his father from his grandfather that when the Prophet ﷺ returned from the Farewell Pilgrimage he ascended the pulpit, praised Allah, and declared Abū Bakr never displeased him, and that he was pleased with Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥa, al-Zubayr, Saʿd, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and the first Emigrants. He enjoined guarding his right in his companions, in-laws, and kin, withholding tongues from Muslims, and speaking good of the dead. > **""O people, Abū Bakr never displeased me, so recognize that for him. O people, I am pleased with Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥa, al-Zubayr, Saʿd, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and the first Emigrants, so recognize that for them. O people, guard me in my companions, my in-laws, and my kin. Guard your tongues from the Muslims, and when one of them dies speak good of him.""**"
"14938","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that three of Quraysh were brightest in face, finest in character, and firmest in heart: if they spoke they did not lie, and if spoken to they did not accuse of lying: Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, Abū ʿUbayda ibn al-Jarrāḥ, and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with them."
"14939","ʿUbāda ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that alone with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he asked which of his companions was dearest to him so he might love whom he loves. The Prophet ﷺ first enjoined concealment in his lifetime, then named Abū Bakr, then ʿUmar, then ʿAlī, then after silence named al-Zubayr, Ṭalḥa, Saʿd, Abū ʿUbayda, Muʿādh, Abū Ṭalḥa, Abū Ayyūb, ʿUbāda himself, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, Abū al-Dardāʾ, Ibn Masʿūd, Ibn ʿAwf, Ibn ʿAffān, then among freed allies Salmān, Ṣuhayb, Bilāl, and Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfa, all noble and beloved even if an Abyssinian slave. Asked why Ḥamza and Jaʿfar were unmentioned, ʿUbāda explained they had already been martyred when he asked. > **""Conceal this from me in my lifetime. Abū Bakr, then ʿUmar, then ʿAlī. Who else after these but al-Zubayr, Ṭalḥa, Saʿd, Abū ʿUbayda, Muʿādh, Abū Ṭalḥa, Abū Ayyūb, and you, O ʿUbāda, and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, Abū al-Dardāʾ, Ibn Masʿūd, Ibn ʿAwf, Ibn ʿAffān, then this group of freed allies: Salmān, Ṣuhayb, Bilāl, and Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfa. These are my intimates, and every companion of mine is noble to me and beloved, even if an Abyssinian slave.""**"
"14940","Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim reported that ʿAlī was asked about ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd and said he recited the Qurʾān, stood at its ambiguous verses, permitted its lawful and forbade its unlawful. Asked about ʿAmmār he called him a forgetful believer who remembers when reminded, filled with faith from forelock to heel. Asked about Ḥudhayfa he called him most learned of the Prophet's ﷺ companions about hypocrites through asking and being informed. Asked about Salmān he said he attained former and latter knowledge, an inexhaustible sea, of the Household. Asked about Abū Dharr he spoke of knowledge people neglected. Asked about himself he said when silent he was addressed first and when asking he was given, with abundant knowledge between the jaws."
"14941","Abū al-Aswad Wardān al-Kindī reported that one day with ʿAlī in good cheer they asked about Muḥammad's ﷺ companions. ʿAlī asked which, then described Ibn Masʿūd as reciter of Qurʾān and knower of Sunna sufficing thereby; Abū Dharr as frequent asker, eager over religion and knowledge, a sea filling his vessel; Ḥudhayfa ibn al-Yamān as knower of hypocrites' names and difficult matters; Salmān as like Luqmān the Wise, of the Household, attaining former and latter knowledge and scriptures, an unfailing sea; ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir as one whose flesh, blood, hair, and skin faith permeated so fire should not consume him; and of himself that Allah forbade self-praise but by His favor when asking he was given and when silent he was addressed first."
"14942","Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās sought permission to enter upon Muʿāwiya while Quraysh chiefs sat with Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ at his right. Muʿāwiya vowed to pose baffling questions but Saʿīd trusted Ibn ʿAbbās. Asked of Abū Bakr, Ibn ʿAbbās praised him as reciter of Qurʾān, distant from deviation, heedless of indecency, forbidding wrong, knowing religion, fearing Allah, praying by night, fasting by day, sound from worldliness, resolute on justice, commanding good, grateful, ever remembering Allah morning and evening, subduing his soul to righteousness, surpassing companions in piety and chastity, with curses upon his reviler till Resurrection. Asked of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, he praised him as ally of Islam, shelter of orphans, abode of faith, refuge of weak, fortress of upright, enduring for Allah till religion triumphed and Allah remembered in horizons and valleys, dignified amid obscenity, grateful in hardship and ease, ever remembering Allah, with curse upon his hater till the Day of Regret. Asked of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, he praised him as noblest attendant, most joining of kin, most patient warrior, vigil in pre-dawn, tearful at Allah's remembrance, ever reflecting night and day, hastening to every honor and salvation, fleeing every ruin."
"14943","Masrūq reported that he examined the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and found their knowledge ending with six: ʿUmar, ʿAlī, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Muʿādh, Abū al-Dardāʾ, and Zayd ibn Thābit. Then examining the six he found their knowledge ending with ʿAlī and ʿAbdullāh."
"14944","Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz reported that the scholars after Muʿādh ibn Jabal were ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Abū al-Dardāʾ, Salmān, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām. After them was Zayd ibn Thābit, and after Zayd were ʿUmar in one wording and Ibn ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās."
"14945","Hishām ibn ʿUrwa reported that ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, questioned the knowledge of Abū Saʿīd and Anas of the Prophet's ﷺ narrations, since they were but two small boys."
"14946","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that a blind Anṣārī man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to mark a mosque in his house for his prayer. When the Prophet ﷺ came amid his people and one man was absent and disparaged, the Prophet ﷺ asked had he not witnessed Badr, and declared perhaps Allah looked upon Badr's people offering them forgiveness for whatever they do. > **""What happened to so-and-so? Has he not witnessed Badr? Perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do as you wish, for I have forgiven you.""**"
"14947","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expressed hope that none who crossed the pass would enter the Fire. > **""I hope that none who crossed the pass will enter the Fire.""**"
"14948","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that on the Day of al-Ḥudaybiyya the Messenger of Allah ﷺ first forbade kindling fire by night, then commanded kindling and work, for none after them would reach their mudd or ṣāʿ. > **""Do not kindle fire by night. Kindle and work, for none after you will reach your mudd nor your ṣāʿ.""**"
"14949","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said all who pledged beneath the tree would enter Paradise except the owner of the red camel. > **""All who pledged beneath the tree will enter Paradise, except the owner of the red camel.""**"
"14950","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expressed hope that, Allah willing, none who witnessed Badr would enter the Fire. > **""I hope that none who witnessed Badr will enter the Fire, Allah willing.""**"
"14951","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet's ﷺ concubine Umm Ibrāhīm stayed in her chamber while a Copt brought her water and wood, provoking gossip of a man visiting a woman. The Prophet ﷺ sent ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib ordering his killing. ʿAlī found him on a palm tree; seeing the sword he fell, his wrap slipped revealing he was castrated. ʿAlī returned asking whether one commanded who then sees otherwise should refer back, and the Prophet ﷺ affirmed. He reported the Copt's condition. When Umm Ibrāhīm bore Ibrāhīm, the Prophet ﷺ remained in doubt until Jibrīl, peace be upon him, greeted him as father of Ibrāhīm and he was reassured. > **""Yes. Peace upon you, O father of Ibrāhīm.""**"
"14952","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon Umm Ibrāhīm Māriya the Copt, his umm walad pregnant with Ibrāhīm, and found her kinsman who came from Egypt, embraced Islam well, and visited her. The kinsman had castrated himself for his station near the Prophet's ﷺ umm walad. Doubt entered the Prophet's ﷺ heart as with people, and he returned changed in color and told ʿUmar, who hastened with sword to Māriya and raised it to kill the man, who then uncovered himself. ʿUmar returned and informed the Prophet ﷺ, who told him Jibrīl had already declared her and her kinsman innocent of his thought, announced a boy from him most resembling him in creation, commanded naming him Ibrāhīm, and addressed him as Abū Ibrāhīm, a kunya he would have adopted but for dislike of changing his known kunya. > **""Shall I not inform you, O ʿUmar? Jibrīl came to me and informed me that Allah has declared her and her kinsman innocent of what occurred in my heart, and gave me glad tidings that in her womb is a boy from me, the most resembling of creation to me. He commanded me to name him Ibrāhīm and addressed me as Abū Ibrāhīm. Were I not averse to changing my kunya by which I am known, I would have taken Abū Ibrāhīm as Jibrīl addressed me.""**"
"14953","Al-Suddī reported that he asked Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over his son Ibrāhīm. He said he did not know, then invoked mercy upon Ibrāhīm, declaring had he lived he would have been a truthful Prophet."
"14954","Al-Barāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning his son Ibrāhīm that he has a nursing mother in Paradise. > **""He has a nursing mother in Paradise.""**"
"14955","Ibn Abī Awfā was asked whether he saw Ibrāhīm son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He replied yes, he died young, the most resembling of people to him ﷺ."
"14956","Sīrīn reported that she attended the death of Ibrāhīm son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Whenever she and her sister cried the women cried and he did not forbid them, but after death he forbade wailing. He carried him to the grave edge with al-ʿAbbās beside him, while al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās and Usāma ibn Zayd descended into the grave, and she wept at his grave without prohibition. The sun eclipsed and people said it was for Ibrāhīm's death, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared eclipses are for neither death nor life. Seeing a gap in the grave he ordered it filled, explaining it neither benefits nor harms him but comforts the living eye. He died Tuesday, ten nights past Rabīʿ al-Awwal, year ten. > **""It does not eclipse for the death of anyone nor for his life. It neither benefits him nor harms him, but it comforts the eye of the living.""**"
"14957","Zayd ibn Thābit, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he leaves among them two successors: the Book of Allah, a rope extended between heaven and earth, and his kin, his Household, never parting until they return to him at the Pond. > **""I am leaving among you two successors: the Book of Allah, a rope extended between heaven and earth, and my kin, my Household. They will never part until they return to me at the Pond.""**"
"14958","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he has left among them two after which they will never stray: the Book of Allah and his lineage, never parting until they return to him at the Pond. > **""I have left among you two after which you will never stray: the Book of Allah and my lineage. They will never part until they return to me at the Pond.""**"
"14959","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he is to be taken and has left among them the two weighty things, meaning the Book of Allah and his Household, after which they will never stray, and the Hour will not rise until the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ are sought as a stray is sought and not found. > **""I am to be taken, and I have left among you the two weighty things, meaning the Book of Allah and my Household. You will never stray after them, and the Hour will not rise until the companions of the Messenger of Allah are sought as a stray is sought and not found.""**"
"14960","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah and turned to al-Ṭāʾif, besieging it seventeen or nineteen days, he stood to preach, praised Allah, then enjoined his kin with good, named the Pond as appointment, swore they would establish prayer and pay zakāt or he would send a man from him, as himself, to strike their necks. Then he took ʿAlī's hand and declared him. > **""I enjoin you to my kin with good, and your appointment is the Pond. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall establish prayer and pay zakāt, or I shall send to you a man from me, as myself, to strike your necks. This.""**"
"14961","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the last words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were a command to succeed him in his Household. > **""Succeed me in my Household.""**"
"14962","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he leaves among them the two weighty things, one greater than the other: the Book of Allah, a rope extended from heaven to earth, and his kin, his Household, never parting until they return to him at the Pond. > **""I am leaving among you the two weighty things, one greater than the other: the Book of Allah, a rope extended from heaven to earth, and my kin, my Household. They will never part until they return to me at the Pond.""**"
"14963","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at al-Juḥfa, turned to the people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then reminded them that no Prophet lives but half the age of his predecessor and that he expected soon to be called and to answer. He asked what they would say, and they replied that he had counseled sincerely. He had them testify there is no god but Allah, Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger, Paradise is true and Fire is true, and he bore witness with them with his hand upon his chest. He then described himself as preceding them at the Pond, broad between Ṣanʿāʾ and Buṣrā with silver cups as numerous as stars, and enjoined watchfulness over the two weighty things. When asked what they were, he named the Book of Allah with one end in Allah's Hand and one in theirs, and his kin, never parting until they return to him at the Pond, not to be preceded, fallen short of, nor taught over, for they are more learned. Then he took ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, by the hand and declared his guardianship with supplication for ally and adversary. > **""I am leaving among you the two weighty things: the Book of Allah, one end in the Hand of Allah and one end in your hands, hold fast to it and do not stray, and the other is my kin. The Subtle, Aware has informed me they will never part until they return to me at the Pond. Whoever I am closer to than his own self, ʿAlī is his guardian. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him.""**"
"14964","In a shorter narration of the same event, the Pond is described as containing cups of gold and silver as numerous as stars, and the two weighty things are named as the greater and the smaller. > **""In it are cups of gold and silver as numerous as the stars. The greater is the Book of Allah, and the smaller is my kin.""**"
"14965","In another narration, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Farewell Pilgrimage and halted at Ghadīr Khumm, he ordered the trees to be cleared, then stood and spoke as one soon to be called and answered. The narrator asked Zayd whether he himself heard it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he affirmed that everyone beneath the trees saw with his eyes and heard with his ears. > **""It is as though I have been called and I have answered.""**"
"14966","Ḥudhayfa ibn Usayd al-Ghifārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Farewell Pilgrimage he forbade settling beneath scattered lote trees at al-Baṭḥāʾ, had their thorns cleared, prayed there, then stood to address the people. He spoke of Prophets living half the age of predecessors, of his imminent call, and of mutual questioning. The people testified he conveyed, strove, and counseled sincerely. He had them testify to unity, messengership, Paradise, Fire, death, resurrection, the Hour, and raising of graves, and called Allah to witness. He declared Allah his Master and himself master of believers, closer than their selves, and named ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, as master of whom he is master, with supplication for ally and adversary. He then described his precedence at the Pond, wider than Buṣrā to Ṣanʿāʾ with silver cups as numerous as stars, and his questioning over the two weighty things: the Book of Allah as an extended cord and his kin, his Household, never parting until the Pond. > **""O people, I am to be questioned and you are to be questioned, so what will you say? O Allah, bear witness. O people, Allah is my Master and I am master of the believers, closer to them than their selves. Whoever I am his master, this is his master. O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him. I am your predecessor and you are coming to the Pond. Look at how you succeed me in the two weighty things: the Book of Allah and my kin, my Household.""**"
"14967","ʿAlī ibn ʿAlī al-Hilālī reported from his father that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the illness in which he was taken, with Fāṭima, may Allah be pleased with her, at his head weeping aloud. The Prophet ﷺ raised his gaze and asked his beloved Fāṭima why she wept. She feared destitution after him. He reminded her that Allah looked upon earth and chose her father with messengership, then looked again and chose her husband with revelation of her marriage, and that their House was granted seven traits granted none before nor after: himself as Seal, noblest and most beloved of Prophets and creation and her father; his executor best and most beloved of executors and her husband; their martyr best and most beloved of martyrs, her uncle Ḥamza ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib; among them one with two green wings flying with angels in Paradise, her father's cousin and her husband's brother; among them the two grandsons of this umma, her sons al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, masters of Paradise's youth, yet their father better than them; and from them the Mahdī when the world fills with chaos, severed roads, mutual raids, and lost mercy and reverence, who opens fortresses of misguidance and sealed hearts, upholds religion in the last age as he upheld it in the first, and fills earth with justice as filled with tyranny. He forbade her grief, for Allah is more merciful to her than himself by her place in his heart, and described her husband as noblest in lineage, rank, mercy, equity, and judgment among her House, asking his Lord she be first of his Household to join him. > **""O my beloved, did you not know that Allah looked upon the earth and chose your father, then looked again and chose your husband? We are a Household granted seven traits granted none before us nor after us. From them is the Mahdī of this umma when the world becomes chaos and trials appear. Do not grieve nor weep, for Allah is more merciful to you than I. I asked my Lord that you be the first of my Household to join me.""**"
"14968","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Fāṭima, may Allah be pleased with her, that their Prophet is best of Prophets and her father, their martyr best of martyrs and her father's uncle Ḥamza, among them one with two wings flying in Paradise where he wills, her father's cousin Jaʿfar, among them the two grandsons al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, her sons, and among them the Mahdī. > **""Our Prophet is the best of Prophets, and he is your father. Our martyr is the best of martyrs, and he is your father's uncle Ḥamza. Among us is one with two wings flying in Paradise where he wills, your father's cousin Jaʿfar. Among us are the two grandsons of this umma, al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, your sons. And among us is the Mahdī.""**"
"14969","Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in her house, the servant announced ʿAlī and Fāṭima at the entrance. He told her to rise and withdraw from his Household. She withdrew nearby. ʿAlī and Fāṭima entered with their sons al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, two little boys. He took the boys into his lap, kissed them, embraced ʿAlī with one hand and Fāṭima with the other, kissed each, covered them with a black cloak, and prayed for their direction to Allah rather than Fire. When Umm Salama asked for herself, he included her. > **""O Allah, to You, not to the Fire, I and my Household. And you.""**"
"14970","Umm Salama, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told Fāṭima to bring her husband and sons. She brought them, and he cast over them a Khaybarī cloak from beneath her, obtained from Khaybar, then prayed for blessings upon the family of Muḥammad as upon the family of Ibrāhīm. > **""O Allah, these are the family of Muḥammad. Grant Your prayers and blessings upon the family of Muḥammad as You granted them upon the family of Ibrāhīm. You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.""**"
"14971","Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that Fāṭima, daughter of the Prophet ﷺ, came carrying al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn with a pot of hot food in her hand. When she set it before him, he asked where Abū Ḥasan was. She said at home, so he summoned him. The Prophet ﷺ, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn sat eating. Umm Salama noted he did not invite her that day though he always had before. When finished, he wrapped them in his garment and prayed for enmity to their enemy and loyalty to their ally. > **""O Allah, oppose whoever opposes them and befriend whoever befriends them.""**"
"14972","Shaddād Abū ʿAmmār reported that he visited Wāthila ibn al-Asqaʿ while people mentioned ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him. After they left, Wāthila offered what he had seen from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He had gone to Fāṭima, may Allah be pleased with her, asking for ʿAlī, and she said he went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with Ḥasan and Ḥusayn. He waited until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came holding each boy by a hand, entered, drew ʿAlī and Fāṭima near, seated each boy upon a thigh, wrapped them in his garment or cloak, recited the verse of purification, and declared them his Household most worthy. > **""Allah intends only to remove impurity from you, O Household, and to purify you thoroughly. O Allah, these are my Household, and my Household are most worthy.""**"
"14973","Abū ʿAmmār also reported that while sitting with Wāthila ibn al-Asqaʿ people reviled ʿAlī. After they rose, Wāthila told him of the reviled one: one day with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, Ḥasan, and Ḥusayn, may Allah be pleased with them, came, and the Prophet ﷺ cast his cloak over them and prayed for removal of impurity and thorough purification. When Wāthila asked for himself, the Prophet ﷺ included him, which Wāthila deemed his most binding deed. > **""O Allah, my Household, remove impurity from them and purify them thoroughly. And you.""**"
"14974","In another narration Wāthila described that inclusion as his most hopeful expectation. > **""It is the most hopeful of what I hope for.""**"
"14975","Wāthila ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that seeking ʿAlī he was told he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He found them in a reed enclosure: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, Ḥasan, and Ḥusayn gathered beneath a garment, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed for his prayers, mercy, forgiveness, and pleasure upon himself and them. > **""O Allah, You have granted Your prayers, mercy, forgiveness, and pleasure upon me and upon them.""**"
"14976","Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the verse of purification came down concerning five: himself, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, Ḥasan, and Ḥusayn, may Allah be pleased with them. > **""This verse came down concerning five: concerning me, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, Ḥasan, and Ḥusayn. Allah intends only to remove impurity from you, O Household, and to purify you thoroughly.""**"
"14977","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Household from whom Allah removed impurity and purified thoroughly were five counted on his hand: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn. He added that the verse came down in the house of Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her."
"14978","Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened his Household to the ark of Nūḥ, peace be upon him: whoever boards is saved and whoever lags is drowned, and whoever fights them in the last age is as one fighting with the Dajjāl. > **""The likeness of my Household is as the ark of Nūḥ. Whoever boards it is saved and whoever lags from it is drowned, and whoever fights us in the last age is as one fighting with the Dajjāl.""**"
"14979","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened his Household to the ark of Nūḥ: whoever boards is saved and whoever lags is drowned. > **""The likeness of my Household is as the ark of Nūḥ. Whoever boards it is saved, and whoever lags from it is drowned.""**"
"14980","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Prophet ﷺ likened his Household to the ark of Nūḥ: whoever boards is safe and whoever abandons it is drowned. > **""The likeness of my Household is as the ark of Nūḥ. Whoever boards it is safe, and whoever abandons it is drowned.""**"
"14981","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ liken his Household among them to the ark of Nūḥ and to the gate of Ḥiṭṭa among Israel: boarding saves and lagging drowns, and entering it brings forgiveness. > **""The likeness of my Household among you is as the ark of Nūḥ. Whoever boards it is saved, and whoever lags from it is drowned. And the likeness of my Household among you is as the gate of Ḥiṭṭa among Israel. Whoever enters it is forgiven.""**"
"14982","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that when the verse commanding affection for kin was revealed, the people asked who the kin whose affection was obligatory were, and the Prophet ﷺ named ʿAlī, Fāṭima, and their two sons, may Allah be pleased with them. > **""ʿAlī, Fāṭima, and their two sons.""**"
"14983","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of three sanctities of Allah: whoever guards them Allah guards his religion and world, and whoever neglects them Allah guards nothing for him: the sanctity of Islam, his own sanctity, and the sanctity of his kinship. > **""Allah has three sanctities. Whoever guards them, Allah guards for him his religion and world, and whoever does not guard them, Allah guards nothing for him: the sanctity of Islam, my sanctity, and the sanctity of my kinship.""**"
"14984","ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb reported that he visited Zaynab bint Abī Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, who told him the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with Umm Salama, holding Ḥasan on one side, Ḥusayn on the other, and Fāṭima in his lap, then invoked mercy and blessings upon the Household. > **""The mercy of Allah and His blessings upon you, O Household. He is Praiseworthy, Glorious.""**"
"14985","Abū al-Ḥamrāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ come to the door of Fāṭima, may Allah be pleased with her, for six months, reciting the verse of purification. > **""Allah intends only to remove impurity from you, O Household, and to purify you thoroughly.""**"
"14986","Abū Barza, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for seventeen months, and whenever the Prophet ﷺ left his house he came to the door of Fāṭima announcing prayer and reciting the verse of purification. > **""Prayer upon you. Allah intends only to remove impurity from you.""**"
"14987","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the door of ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, for forty mornings after his union with Fāṭima, greeting the Household with peace, mercy, and blessings and reciting the verse of purification. > **""Peace upon you, O Household, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Allah intends only to remove impurity from you, O Household, and to purify you thoroughly.""**"
"14988","ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ when he had spread a wrap. He sat with him alongside Fāṭima, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ gathered its edges over them and prayed for Allah's pleasure with them as his own. > **""O Allah, be pleased with them as I am pleased with them.""**"
"14989","Ṣubayḥ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he was at the Prophet's ﷺ door when ʿAlī, Fāṭima, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn sat aside. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out in a Khaybarī cloak, declared them upon good, covered them with it, and announced war and peace by their measure. > **""You are upon good. I am war to whoever wars you, and peace to whoever makes peace with you.""**"
"14990","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at ʿAlī, al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, and Fāṭima, peace upon them, and announced war to their enemy and peace to their ally. > **""I am war to whoever wars you, and peace to whoever makes peace with you.""**"
"14991","ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon him while he slept. Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn asked for drink, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose to a scanty-milked goat of theirs, milked it, and it flowed. Al-Ḥasan came forward and the Prophet ﷺ set him aside in turn, whereupon Fāṭima asked if he were dearer to him. He replied that the other had asked first, then declared that he, Fāṭima, the two boys, and the sleeper would be in one place on Resurrection Day. In the wording of Aḥmad and al-Bazzār they slept under a cover and he poured from a vessel into a cup while al-Ḥusayn leapt up, and he repeated the same promise. In the wording of al-Ṭabarānī he pressed a waterskin into the cup and declared them of one rank with him. > **""No, but he asked for drink before him. I, you, these two, and this sleeper shall be in one place on Resurrection Day. They are with me of one rank.""**"
"14992","Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī reported that they asked ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar to narrate only what he himself heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ or saw from him, even if from another trustworthy source. He reported hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ define the private region. > **""What is between the navel and the knee is ʿawra.""**"
"14993","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the effect of charity upon divine anger. > **""Charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord.""**"
"14994","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the worst of his umma as those born in luxury, nourished in it, eating many kinds of food, and speaking with affected eloquence. > **""The worst of my umma are those born in luxury and nourished in it. They eat many kinds of food and speak with affected eloquence.""**"
"14995","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ address Banū Hāshim with his supplications for nobility, mercy, guidance of their astray, security of their fearful, and satiety of their hungry. > **""O Banū Hāshim, I have asked Allah for you to make you noble and merciful. I asked Him to guide your astray, secure your fearful, and feed your hungry.""**"
"14996","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that he saw cucumber in the right hand of the Prophet ﷺ and ripe dates in his left, eating alternately from each."
"14997","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that a sheep and loaves were gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ate and they ate with him."
"14998","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commend the meat of the back as among its finest. > **""Upon you is the meat of the back, for it is among its finest.""**"
"14999","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited in the two rakʿas before Fajr and the two after Maghrib the chapters of disbelievers and sincerity. > **""Say, O disbelievers. Say, He is Allah, One.""**"
"15000","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the mahr of Fāṭima, may Allah be pleased with her, was a coat of iron."
"15001","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that al-ʿAbbās came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining that people fell silent at seeing him out of hatred for them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked if they had done so, then swore none believes until he loves them, questioning whether they hope to enter Paradise by his intercession while Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib do not. > **""Have they done so? By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, none of you believes until he loves you. Do they hope to enter Paradise by my intercession while Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib do not hope for it?""**"
"15002","In another narration the condition of faith is expressed as love for them through love of the Prophet ﷺ. > **""None of you believes until he loves you for my love.""**"
"15003","Shahr ibn Ḥawshab reported that orators stood reviling ʿAlī until the last, an Anṣārī called Unays, declared he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise intercession for more than the trees and stones upon earth, then swore none maintains kinship like the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and questioned whether others hope for it while his Household fall short. > **""I shall intercede on Resurrection Day for more than the trees and stones upon the earth.""**"
"15004","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited Fāṭima one day while ʿAlī slept and she reclined with their sons beside them. Al-Ḥasan asked for drink, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose to a milch camel of theirs, milked and brought it. Al-Ḥusayn awoke and struggled to drink first until he wept. The Prophet ﷺ explained his brother asked first. When Fāṭima asked if al-Ḥasan held preference, he denied preference and affirmed equal rank, declaring himself, Fāṭima, the two boys, and the sleeper in one place on Resurrection Day. > **""Your brother asked for drink before you. He is not dearer to me than him. They are with me of one rank. I, you, they two, and this sleeper shall be in one place on Resurrection Day.""**"
"15005","ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb reported that he visited Zaynab bint Abī Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, who told them the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with Umm Salama when al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, and Fāṭima entered. He placed al-Ḥasan on one side, al-Ḥusayn on the other, Fāṭima in his lap, and invoked mercy and blessings upon the Household. When Umm Salama wept that they were singled out apart from her and her daughter, he included them among the Household. > **""The mercy of Allah and His blessings upon you, O Household. He is Praiseworthy, Glorious. What makes you weep? You and your daughter are of the Household.""**"
"15006","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib with his three requests for steadfastness of their upright, knowledge for their ignorant, guidance for their astray, and for courageous mercy, then warned that one standing between the Corner and the Station in prayer and fasting who dies hating the family of Muḥammad ﷺ enters the Fire. > **""O Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, I asked Allah for you three things: to make firm your upright, teach your ignorant, and guide your astray. I asked Him to make you courageous and merciful. Had a man stood between the Corner and the Station in prayer and fasting, then died hating the family of Muḥammad, he would enter the Fire.""**"
"15007","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded holding to love of the Household, for whoever meets Allah loving them enters Paradise by their intercession, and no deed benefits a servant without recognition of their right. > **""Hold to love of us, the Household. Whoever meets Allah loving us enters Paradise by our intercession. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, no deed benefits a servant except by recognition of our right.""**"
"15008","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them both, warned Muʿāwiya ibn Khudayj against hating them, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said none hates or envies them but is driven from the Pond on Resurrection Day with whips of fire. > **""None hates us nor envies us except he is driven from the Pond on Resurrection Day with whips of fire.""**"
"15009","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preach that whoever hates the Household Allah raises on Resurrection Day as a Jew. Asked even if he fasts, prays, and claims Islam, he affirmed, noting it only shields his blood and subjects him to jizya in humiliation. He added that his umma was shown to him in clay, the standard-bearers passed, and he sought forgiveness for ʿAlī and his partisans. > **""O people, whoever hates us, the Household, Allah raises him on Resurrection Day as a Jew. Even if he fasts, prays, and claims he is a Muslim, he is shielded thereby from shedding of his blood, and pays jizya in humiliation. I sought forgiveness for ʿAlī and his partisans.""**"
"15010","Abū Jamīla reported that when ʿAlī was killed and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was appointed successor, while leading the people in prayer a man leapt upon him and stabbed him with a dagger in his hip, sickening him for months. He then rose to preach from the pulpit, enjoining the people of Iraq to fear Allah concerning them, for they are their rulers and guests and the Household of the verse of purification, until none in the mosque was seen but weeping. > **""O people of Iraq, fear Allah concerning us. We are your rulers and your guests, and we are the Household of whom Allah says: Allah intends only to remove impurity from you, O Household, and to purify you thoroughly.""**"
"15011","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said hatred of Banū Hāshim and the Anṣār is disbelief, and hatred of Arabs is hypocrisy. > **""Hatred of Banū Hāshim and the Anṣār is disbelief, and hatred of Arabs is hypocrisy.""**"
"15012","Salmān, may Allah be pleased with him, instructed to place the family of Muḥammad as the head to the body and as the eyes to the head, for the body is guided only by the head and the head only by the eyes."
"15013","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah placed the offspring of every Prophet in his own loins, and placed his own offspring in the loins of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him. > **""Allah placed the offspring of every Prophet in his loins, and Allah placed my offspring in the loins of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib.""**"
"15014","Fāṭima al-Kubrā, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every mother's children are ascribed to their paternal kin except the children of Fāṭima, whose guardian and kin he himself is. > **""Every mother's children are ascribed to their kin except the children of Fāṭima. I am their guardian and I am their kin.""**"
"15015","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that al-ʿAbbās visited the Prophet ﷺ in his illness, lifted and seated him upon his bed, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed for his elevation. Al-ʿAbbās announced ʿAlī seeking entry with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn. Al-ʿAbbās called them the Prophet's ﷺ children, the Prophet ﷺ called them his uncle's children too, and when asked if he loved them, he prayed for reciprocal love. > **""May Allah raise you, O uncle. Let him enter. They are your children too, O uncle. May Allah love you as you love them.""**"
"15016","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who was dearer to him, himself or Fāṭima. He replied Fāṭima was dearer and ʿAlī more honored, then envisioned him warding people from his Pond with pitchers as numerous as heaven's stars, and declared himself, ʿAlī, al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, Fāṭima, ʿAqīl, and Jaʿfar in Paradise as brethren facing one another, ʿAlī with him and his partisans in Paradise, none looking at another's back. > **""Fāṭima is dearer to me than you, and you are more honored to me than her. I see you at my Pond warding people from it, with pitchers upon it as numerous as the stars of heaven. I, you, al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, Fāṭima, ʿAqīl, and Jaʿfar shall be in Paradise as brethren facing one another. You are with me and your partisans are in Paradise. None looks at the back of his fellow.""**"
"15017","ʿUthmān, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever does a favor to one of the children of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib unrepaid in this world, its repayment falls upon him when they meet tomorrow. > **""Whoever does a favor to one of the children of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and is not repaid for it in this world, its repayment is upon me tomorrow when he meets me.""**"
"15018","Thawbān, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for his family, naming ʿAlī, Fāṭima, and others. When Thawbān asked if he was of the Household, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed conditionally upon not lingering at gates of power nor approaching rulers to beg. > **""Yes, so long as you do not stand at the door of a court or come to a ruler begging.""**"
"15019","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb address the people upon marrying the daughter of ʿAlī, requesting congratulations, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say every tie and lineage is cut on Resurrection Day except his own. > **""Congratulate me not? Every tie and lineage is cut on Resurrection Day except my tie and my lineage.""**"
"15020","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every tie and lineage is cut on Resurrection Day except his tie and lineage. > **""Every tie and lineage is cut on Resurrection Day except my tie and my lineage.""**"
"15021","Umm Bakr bint al-Miswar ibn Makhrama, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī proposed to al-Miswar ibn Makhrama for his daughter and he married her to him, citing his hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say every tie and lineage is cut on Resurrection Day except his own. > **""Every tie and lineage is cut on Resurrection Day except my tie and my lineage.""**"
"15022","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn shall be on Resurrection Day in a dome beneath the Throne. > **""I, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn shall be on Resurrection Day in a dome beneath the Throne.""**"
"15023","ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, Ḥasan, and Ḥusayn shall be gathered with their lovers on Resurrection Day eating and drinking until servants are parted. When a man asked about presentation and reckoning, he was answered with the example of the companion of Yā-Sīn admitted to Paradise at once. > **""I, ʿAlī, Fāṭima, Ḥasan, and Ḥusayn shall be gathered, and whoever loves us shall on Resurrection Day eat and drink with us until servants are parted.""**"
"15024","Abū Rāfiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, that the first four to enter Paradise are himself, ʿAlī, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn, their offspring behind them, their wives behind offspring, and their partisans to right and left. > **""The first four to enter Paradise are I, you, al-Ḥasan, and al-Ḥusayn. Our offspring are behind us, our wives behind our offspring, and our partisans to our right and to our left.""**"
"15025","Salama ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that stars are security for heaven's people and his Household are security for his umma. > **""Stars are security for the people of heaven, and my Household are security for my umma.""**"
"15026","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, explained the verse peace upon the family of Yā-Sīn as referring to the family of Muḥammad ﷺ. > **""Peace upon the family of Yā-Sīn. We are the family of Muḥammad.""**"
"15027","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the best of them are best to his family after him, with a narrator noting the wording as to my family rather than his family. > **""The best of you are the best of you to my family after me.""**"
"15028","Sawda bint Musarraḥ, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that she was among the women attending Fāṭima, may Allah be pleased with her, in labor. The Prophet ﷺ came asking her condition and commanded nothing be done for the child before him. When she bore a boy they cut his cord and wrapped him in yellow. The Prophet ﷺ asked what was done, rebuked haste, ordered the child brought, removed the yellow wrap for white, spat in his mouth and fed him his saliva. When ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, came and said he named him Jaʿfar, the Prophet ﷺ declined and named him Ḥasan, then Ḥusayn after him, and named ʿAlī father of Ḥasan. > **""How is she? When she delivers, do nothing for him before me. What did you do? You disobeyed me? Bring him to me. What did you name him, O ʿAlī? No, but Ḥasan, and after him Ḥusayn, and you are Abū Ḥasan.""**"
"15029","In another narration the Prophet's ﷺ address to ʿAlī is preserved with the honorific of goodness. > **""And you are Abū Ḥasan al-Khayr.""**"
"15030","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he proposed to the Prophet ﷺ for his daughter Fāṭima. He sold his armor and some goods for four hundred eighty dirhams. The Prophet ﷺ ordered two-thirds for perfume and one-third for clothes, spat in a water jar commanding them to bathe with it, and ordered her not to nurse her child before him. She preceded him in nursing al-Ḥusayn, while for al-Ḥasan the Prophet ﷺ placed something unknown in his mouth, and he became the more learned of the two."
"15031","Abū Bakra, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, and when he prostrated al-Ḥasan, peace be upon him, leapt upon his back and neck. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose gently lest he fall. Asked why he treated al-Ḥasan uniquely, he called him his basil in this world and a master through whom Allah would reconcile two factions. > **""He is my basil in this world. My son here is a master, and perhaps Allah will reconcile through him between two factions.""**"
"15032","In another narration the boy leapt upon his back repeatedly during prayer."
"15033","Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that Ḥasan came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while prostrating and rode upon his back. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ held him by hand until he rose, then bowed and he again mounted, and when he rose he released him and the boy went."
"15034","Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrating until al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī climbed upon his back. He did not lower him until the boy descended himself, and he would spread his legs so the boy entered from one side and exited from the other."
"15035","Al-Bahī reported that he asked ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who most resembled the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He named al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī as closest in resemblance and dearest to him: he would come while the Prophet ﷺ prostrated and fall upon his back so the Prophet ﷺ would not rise until he moved aside, or enter beneath his belly while he spread his legs until he emerged."
"15036","Ibn Abī Mulayka reported that Fāṭima, may Allah be pleased with her, would pat al-Ḥasan and say her son resembles the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and not ʿAlī, peace be upon him."
"15037","Kulayb ibn Shihāb reported that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was mentioned before Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he affirmed he resembled the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15038","ʿAlī, may Allāh honour his face, stated that al-Ḥasan was the person who most resembled the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ from his head down to his throat."
"15039","Zuhayr ibn al-Ḥārith reported that while al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, was delivering a khuṭbah after the killing of ʿAlī, a tall dark-complexioned man from Azd stood and testified that he had seen the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ holding al-Ḥasan in his lap and enjoining love for him, adding that were it not for the Messenger's insistence he would not have narrated it. > **""Whoever loves me, let him love him, and let the witness convey to the absent""**"
"15040","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, swore that his two ears heard and his two eyes saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ holding Ḥasan — or Ḥusayn — with both hands while the boy's feet stood upon the Prophet's feet, coaxing him upward until the boy climbed and placed his feet upon the Prophet's chest, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ bade him open his mouth, kissed him, and supplicated for him. > **""Ḥuzuqqah, ḥuzuqqah, climb up, O small-eyed one. Open your mouth. O Allāh, whoever loves him, for I love him""**"
"15041","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ would take Ḥasan and press him to himself, supplicating for him and for whoever loves him. > **""O Allāh, this is my son, so love him, and love whoever loves him""**"
"15042","Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn Nufayl, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ embraced Ḥasan and declared his love for him while asking Allāh to love him. > **""O Allāh, I love him, so love him""**"
"15043","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ supplicated for al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, declaring his own love for him and asking Allāh to love him and to love whoever loves him. > **""O Allāh, I love him, so love him, and love whoever loves him""**"
"15044","Rajāʾ ibn Rabīʿah reported that he was sitting in the mosque of the Messenger ﷺ in Madīnah in a circle containing Abū Saʿīd and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, when al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī passed and greeted them. The people returned his greeting while ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr remained silent, then followed him with a greeting and declared him the most beloved of the people of earth to the people of heaven, adding that he had not spoken to him since the nights of Ṣiffīn. Abū Saʿīd urged him to go and apologise to him, so they sought permission and entered upon him. Abū Saʿīd asked ʿAbdullāh to repeat his statement, and al-Ḥasan challenged him as to why he had fought against them or swelled the ranks at Ṣiffīn. ʿAbdullāh swore he neither swelled their numbers nor struck with a sword alongside them but had merely been present with his father, who had complained about his continuous fasting and night prayer to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ had commanded moderation and obedience to his father, and his father had gone out at Ṣiffīn and he with him. > **""Fast and break your fast, pray and sleep, for I pray and sleep, and I fast and break my fast. O ʿAbdullāh, obey your father""**"
"15045","ʿUmayr ibn Isḥāq reported that he saw Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, meet al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī and ask him to uncover his belly at the place where he had seen the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ kiss, whereupon al-Ḥasan uncovered his belly and Abū Hurayrah kissed it."
"15046","In another version of the preceding report, Abū Hurayrah kissed his navel, while in the wording in question al-Ḥasan uncovered his belly and Abū Hurayrah placed his hand upon his navel."
"15047","Muʿāwiyah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sucking the tongue — or, as the narrator doubted, the lip — of al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, and affirmed that no tongue or lips sucked by the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would ever be punished."
"15048","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAwf reported that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ and Abū al-Aʿwar al-Sulamī told Muʿāwiyah that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was tongue-tied, whereupon Muʿāwiyah forbade them such speech, citing that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had spat into his mouth, and whomever the Messenger spat into his mouth could not be tongue-tied. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī then retorted against ʿAmr regarding the dispute over his paternity and against Abū al-Aʿwar by recalling the Prophet's curse upon Riʿl, Dhakwān, and ʿAmr ibn Sufyān."
"15049","Al-Maqburī reported that they were with Abū Hurayrah when al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, came, greeted, and passed on while Abū Hurayrah did not notice. When told that it was Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī who had greeted, Abū Hurayrah caught up with him and greeted him as his master, and when questioned about saying so, he testified to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ having declared him a chief. > **""He is a sayyid""**"
"15050","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said concerning al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī that he was a chief through whom Allāh would reconcile two great parties of Muslims. > **""This son of mine is a sayyid, and Allāh will reconcile through him between two great parties of Muslims""**"
"15051","Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī reported, thinking it was from Anas as a raised narration, that the Prophet ﷺ said of al-Ḥasan that he was a chief, noting that he resembled him, or words to that effect. > **""This son of mine is a sayyid""**"
"15052","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ declared al-Ḥasan to be the chief of the youth of Paradise. > **""Al-Ḥasan is the sayyid of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15053","Raqabah ibn Maṣqalah reported that when death approached al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, he asked to be taken out to the open land so that he might reflect upon the kingdom of the heavens, meaning its signs, and when taken out he declared that he sought reward with Allāh for his own soul, since it was the dearest soul to him, and among what Allāh granted him was that he rendered his account to himself."
"15054","Shuraḥbīl reported that he was with al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī when the bier of al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī was brought forth, intending to bury him alongside the Prophet ﷺ while fearing the Banū Umayyah would prevent it. When they reached the mosque with him, the Banū Umayyah rose in opposition, whereupon ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar stood and testified that he had heard al-Ḥasan instruct that if they were prevented, they should bury him with his mother."
"15055","Maymūn ibn Mihrān reported that when Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, lost his sight, he would tell his guide to direct him to Muʿāwiyah's couch and release his hand lest Muʿāwiyah gloat over him. One day Muʿāwiyah remarked to his companions that he would soon grieve, and when Ibn ʿAbbās sat with him upon his couch, Muʿāwiyah offered condolences for al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī. Upon confirming his death, Ibn ʿAbbās invoked Allāh's mercy and pleasure upon him, prayed that he join his righteous forebears, and reminded Muʿāwiyah that he had neither filled his grave nor consumed his provision nor would remain forever after him, and that they had already been bereaved by one far greater, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, yet Allāh had not forsaken them after him."
"15056","Al-Haytham ibn ʿAdī stated that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, died in the year forty-four, though this dating was disputed."
"15057","Abū Nuʿaym stated that in that year — the year fifty-eight — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ died."
"15058","Abū Bakr ibn Ḥafṣ stated that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī died in the year forty-eight."
"15059","From the same narrator it is reported that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī and Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ died after ten years of Muʿāwiyah's rule had passed."
"15060","Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah stated that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī died in the year forty-eight."
"15061","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr stated that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī died in the year forty-nine, that Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ led his funeral prayer, that he died in Madīnah at the age of forty-six or forty-seven, and that his kunyah was Abū Muḥammad."
"15062","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr stated that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, died at the age of forty-seven and that his kunyah was Abū Muḥammad."
"15063","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came out to them with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, peace be upon them, one upon each shoulder, kissing one then the other until he reached them. A man asked whether he loved them, and the Prophet ﷺ declared that love and hatred for them was love and hatred for himself. > **""Whoever loves them has loved me, and whoever hates them has hated me""**"
"15064","ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār reported from a man who had seen the Prophet ﷺ pressing Ḥasan and Ḥusayn to himself and supplicating for them. > **""O Allāh, I love them, so love them""**"
"15065","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would be praying, and when he prostrated al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn would leap upon his back. When the people wished to restrain them he gestured to leave them, and when he finished the prayer he placed them in his lap and commanded love for them. > **""Whoever loves me, let him love these two""**"
"15066","From the same narrator it is reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn that he loved them and asked Allāh to love them, and that whoever loved them had loved him. > **""O Allāh, I love them, so love them, and whoever loves them has loved me""**"
"15067","Qurrah ibn Iyās, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn — with doubt in the wording whether he invoked Allāh by name — that he loved them and asked that they be loved. > **""I love them, so love them — O Allāh, I love them, so love them""**"
"15068","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said concerning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn that he loved them and asked Allāh to love them. > **""O Allāh, I love them both, so love them both""**"
"15069","From the same narrator it is reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say concerning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn that whoever loved him should love them both. > **""Whoever loves me, let him love them both""**"
"15070","From the same narrator it is reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ stopped at the house of Fāṭimah and greeted, whereupon al-Ḥasan or al-Ḥusayn came out to him. The Prophet ﷺ bade him climb upon his father, calling him little-eyed one, and lifted him by his fingers until he sat upon his shoulder. Then the other came from another corner and he did the same with him upon his other shoulder. He then held them by their necks until he placed their mouths upon his own mouth and supplicated for them and for whoever loves them. > **""Climb up with your father, O eye of the small-eyed one. O Allāh, I love them both, so love them both, and love whoever loves them both""**"
"15071","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, also reported that Marwān visited him in the illness in which he died and said he had held nothing against him since their companionship except his love for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn. Abū Hurayrah sat up and testified that they had set out with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ until on part of the road the Prophet ﷺ heard al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn weeping with their mother. He hastened to them and asked what was wrong with his two sons, and she answered thirst. The Prophet ﷺ turned to a waterskin seeking water, though water was then scarce and the people were seeking it, and called out asking whether any had water. Each man reached for his waterskin yet none found a drop. The Prophet ﷺ then asked for one of the two, and she handed him over from beneath the curtain, so that Abū Hurayrah saw the whiteness of her forearms. He pressed the child to his chest while he wailed without ceasing, then thrust out his tongue which the child sucked until he calmed and his crying ceased. The other still wept likewise, so he asked for the other and did the same until both fell silent. He then ordered the march, and they moved aside from the litters until they met him upon the open road. > **""What is wrong with my two sons? Is there any of you with water? Hand me one of them. Hand me the other. March on""**"
"15072","Salmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn were such that whoever loved them he would love, and whomever he loved Allāh would love, and whomever Allāh loved He would admit to gardens of bliss; and whoever hated them or transgressed against them he would hate, and whomever he hated Allāh would hate, and whomever Allāh hated He would admit to Hell with an abiding punishment. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn — whoever loves them I love him, and whomever I love Allāh loves him, and whomever Allāh loves He admits to gardens of bliss; and whoever hates them or rebels against them I hate him, and whomever I hate Allāh hates him, and whomever Allāh hates He admits to Hell, and for him is an abiding punishment""**"
"15073","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he entered upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ while al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, may Allāh be pleased with them both, were playing before him — or in his lap. When he asked whether he loved them, the Prophet ﷺ answered with wonder at the question and described them as his sweet-scented plants of this world whom he smelled. > **""How should I not love them when they are my two rayḥānatay from this world whom I smell""**"
"15074","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he entered upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ while al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn were playing upon his belly. When he asked whether he loved them, the Prophet ﷺ answered likewise. > **""Why should I not love them when they are my two rayḥānatay""**"
"15075","Yaʿlā ibn Murrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ declared Ḥusayn to be from him and himself from Ḥusayn, invoked Allāh's love for whoever loved him, and described al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn as two sibṭs among the asbāṭ. > **""Ḥusayn is from me and I am from him. May Allāh love whoever loves him. Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are two sibṭs among the asbāṭ""**"
"15076","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that they would pray the last ʿishāʾ with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and when he prostrated al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn would leap upon his back. When he raised his head he would take them gently from behind and set them down, and when he returned they would return, until he finished his prayer and seated them upon his thighs. When Abū Hurayrah offered to take them back, a flash of lightning shone, and the Prophet ﷺ bade them join their mother, and its light remained until they entered upon her. > **""Join your mother""**"
"15077","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would be prostrating when al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn would come and mount his back, so he would prolong the prostration. When asked about lengthening it, he explained that his son had mounted him and he disliked hurrying him. > **""My son mounted me, so I disliked hastening him""**"
"15078","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he saw al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn upon the shoulders of the Prophet ﷺ and remarked what a fine horse was beneath them, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ praised the riders likewise. > **""And what fine riders they are""**"
"15079","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ while he walked upon all fours with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, may Allāh be pleased with them both, upon his back, praising their camel and themselves as riders. > **""What a fine camel yours is, and what fine riders you are""**"
"15080","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was praying when al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn — or one of them — came and mounted upon his back. Whenever he raised his head he would hold him or them with his hand and praise their mount. > **""What a fine mount yours is""**"
"15081","Salmān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that they were around the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when Umm Ayman came and said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn had strayed, in the high morning. The Prophet ﷺ ordered them to rise and seek his two sons, and each man set off in his own direction while Salmān followed the Prophet's direction until he reached the foot of a mountain, where al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, may Allāh be pleased with them both, clung to one another while a serpent stood upon its tail with sparks of fire issuing from its mouth. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ hastened toward it, whereupon it turned addressing him and then slipped away into the rocks. He then parted the two boys, wiped their faces, invoked his parents as ransom for them and marvelled at their honour with Allāh, then carried one upon his right shoulder and the other upon his left. When Salmān congratulated them upon their mount, the Prophet ﷺ praised the riders and their father above them. > **""Rise and seek my two sons. By my father and mother, how honoured you are to Allāh. And what fine riders they are, and their father is better than them""**"
"15082","ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15083","ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, swearing by Allāh that no prophet gave birth to prophets except himself, and that her two sons are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise, excepting the two sons of the maternal aunt, Yaḥyā and ʿĪsā. > **""By Allāh, there is no prophet but prophets were born except through me, and your two sons are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise, except the two sons of the aunt: Yaḥyā and ʿĪsā""**"
"15084","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15085","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that an angel from heaven who had never visited him sought Allāh's permission to visit him and gave him glad tidings that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""An angel from heaven who had never visited me sought Allāh's permission to visit me, and gave me glad tidings that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15086","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he spent the night with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and saw a figure beside him. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had seen, and when he affirmed, he told him it was an angel who had not descended since his mission, come that night to give him glad tidings that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""O Ḥudhayfah, did you see? This is an angel who has not descended since I was sent; he came to me tonight to give me glad tidings that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15087","Ḥudhayfah also reported that they saw joy upon the face of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ one day and remarked upon the signs of delight, whereupon he asked how he should not rejoice when Jibrīl, peace be upon him, had come giving him glad tidings that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise and that their father is better than them. > **""How should I not rejoice when Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to me giving me glad tidings that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise, and their father is better than them""**"
"15088","Qurrah ibn Iyās, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise and that their father is better than them. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise, and their father is better than them""**"
"15089","Mālik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said the same concerning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn and their father. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise, and their father is better than them""**"
"15090","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Ḥasan and Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""Ḥasan and Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15091","Usāmah ibn Zayd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15092","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15093","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15094","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Paradise boasted over the Fire, each claiming superiority, the Fire citing the tyrants, Nimrod and Pharaoh within it, until it was silenced. Then Allāh revealed to Paradise that it should not submit, for He would adorn its two corners with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, whereupon it swayed as a bride sways in her chamber. > **""Paradise boasted over the Fire, saying: I am better than you; so the Fire said: Rather I am better than you. Paradise asked: And in what? It said: For within me are the tyrants, and Nimrod, and Pharaoh — so it was silenced. Then Allāh revealed to it: Do not submit, for I shall adorn your two corners with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn — so it swayed as the bride sways in her chamber""**"
"15095","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the two adornments of the Throne, not suspended. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the shanfā of the Throne, and they are not suspended""**"
"15096","The Prophet ﷺ said that when the people of Paradise settle therein, Paradise will say: O Lord, You promised to adorn me with two pillars from Your pillars, and He will answer asking whether He had not adorned it with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn. > **""When the people of Paradise settle in Paradise, Paradise will say: O Lord, You promised me that You would adorn me with two pillars from Your pillars. He will say: Did I not adorn you with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn""**"
"15097","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed ʿaṣr, and in the fourth rakʿah al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn came until they rode upon the back of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. When he gave salām he placed them before him, then carried al-Ḥasan upon his right shoulder and al-Ḥusayn upon his left, and addressed the people asking whether he should tell them of the best in grandfather and grandmother, paternal uncle and aunt, maternal uncle and aunt, and father and mother — they being al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, whose grandfather was the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, whose grandmother was Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, whose mother was Fāṭimah daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, whose father was ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, whose uncle was Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib, whose aunt was Umm Hāniʾ daughter of Abī Ṭālib, whose maternal uncle was al-Qāsim son of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and whose maternal aunts were Zaynab, Ruqayyah and Umm Kulthūm, daughters of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ — and that grandfather, father, mother, uncle, aunt and maternal aunts are in Paradise, and they two are in Paradise, and whoever loves them is in Paradise. > **""O people, shall I not tell you of the best of people in grandfather and grandmother? Shall I not tell you of the best of people in paternal uncle and aunt? Shall I not tell you of the best of people in maternal uncle and aunt? Shall I not tell you of the best of people in father and mother? They are al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn""**"
"15098","Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, reported that she brought al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ in the illness in which he died and asked him to bequeath them something as his two sons. He apportioned to Ḥasan his dignity and leadership and to Ḥusayn his boldness and generosity. > **""As for Ḥasan, he has my dignity and my leadership, and as for Ḥusayn, he has my boldness and my generosity""**"
"15099","Abū Rāfiʿ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, brought Ḥasan and Ḥusayn to the Messenger of Allāh in the illness in which he was taken, asking likewise that he bequeath them something. He apportioned to Ḥasan his steadfastness and leadership and to Ḥusayn his vigour and generosity. > **""As for Ḥasan, he has my steadfastness and my leadership, and as for Ḥusayn, he has my vigour and my generosity""**"
"15100","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that whenever a man came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saying he owed the freeing of a slave from the children of Ismāʿīl, the Prophet ﷺ would direct him to Ḥasan and Ḥusayn. > **""Upon you is Ḥasan and Ḥusayn""**"
"15101","Abū Shaddād reported that he would play with al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn with small pebbles for pitching, and whenever they outplayed him they would ride upon him, while whenever he outplayed them they would exclaim in wonder whether he would ride a piece of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ."
"15102","Bishr ibn Ghālib reported that he was with Abū Hurayrah when he saw al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī and addressed him as Abū ʿAbdullāh, recalling that he had seen him upon the hands of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ stained with blood when he was brought at his birth, that he was delighted, wrapped him in a cloth, spat into his mouth, spoke words he could not recall, and — Fāṭimah having preceded him with the navel-string of al-Ḥasan — bade her not to precede him with this one. > **""Do not precede me with this""**"
"15103","Muḥammad ibn al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān al-Ḥizāmī stated that the body of al-Ḥusayn resembled the body of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ."
"15104","Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad reported from his father that between al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn there was only one ṭuhr."
"15105","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said concerning al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī that whoever loved him had loved him. > **""Whoever loves this has loved me""**"
"15106","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, was with the Prophet ﷺ, who loved him intensely. When he said he was going to his mother, Abū Hurayrah asked whether to go with him, whereupon a flash came from the sky and he walked in its light until he arrived."
"15107","Abū Saʿīd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that al-Ḥusayn came running while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was praying, embraced his neck, and the Prophet ﷺ stood carrying him and holding his hand, never releasing it until he bowed."
"15108","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that he saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ part what was between the thighs of al-Ḥusayn and kiss his little member."
"15109","Rajāʾ ibn Rabīʿah reported that he was in the mosque of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī passed and greeted, and the people returned his greeting while ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr stayed silent, then raised his voice after they fell silent with a full greeting. Turning to the people, he asked whether to tell them of the most beloved of the people of earth to the people of heaven — that retreating one — swearing he had not exchanged a word with him since the nights of Ṣiffīn, and that his pleasure was dearer to him than the like of Uḥud. Abū Saʿīd suggested visiting him in the morning, they agreed, and the narrator accompanied them. Abū Saʿīd sought permission and they entered except Ibn ʿAmr, for whom they persisted until al-Ḥusayn permitted him. When he saw him he shifted for him while seated beside al-Ḥusayn, and al-Ḥusayn drew him, yet Ibn ʿAmr stood and would not sit until space was made between them and he sat between them. Abū Saʿīd recounted the story, and al-Ḥusayn asked Ibn ʿAmr whether he truly knew him to be the most beloved of earth to heaven, which he affirmed by the Lord of the Kaʿbah. He then asked what had carried him to fight him and his father at Ṣiffīn, though his father was better than himself. Ibn ʿAmr affirmed it but cited that ʿAmr had complained of his fasting by day and standing by night to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, who had commanded prayer and sleep, fasting and breaking fast, and obedience to ʿAmr; then at Ṣiffīn his father had sworn upon him, though he neither swelled their numbers nor drew sword nor thrust spear nor shot arrow. Al-Ḥasan then asked whether he knew there is no obedience to a creature in disobedience to the Creator, and he affirmed it, as if accepting it. > **""Pray and sleep, fast and break your fast, and obey ʿAmr""**"
"15110","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that whoever would delight in looking at a man of Paradise should look at al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, for he had heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say so. > **""Whoever delights in looking at a man of the people of Paradise, let him look at al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī""**"
"15111","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the angel of rain sought his Lord's permission to visit the Prophet ﷺ and it was granted. The Prophet ﷺ told Umm Salamah to guard the door so none would enter upon them. Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī came to enter and she prevented him, but he leapt past and entered, sitting upon the Prophet's back, shoulder and shoulders. The angel asked the Prophet ﷺ whether he loved him, and when he affirmed, told him his community would kill him and offered to show him the place where he would be killed. He struck with his hand and brought red clay, which Umm Salamah took and tied in her veil. Thābit added that it was reported to them to be Karbalāʾ. > **""Guard the door for us; let none enter upon us. Yes. Your community will kill him, and if you wish I shall show you the place where he will be killed""**"
"15112","Nujayy al-Ḥaḍramī reported that he travelled with ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, as keeper of his water for purification, and when opposite Nīnawā while heading to Ṣiffīn, ʿAlī called out bidding patience to Abū ʿAbdullāh by the bank of the Euphrates. When asked why, he recalled entering upon the Prophet ﷺ one day with his eyes flowing, asking whether anyone had angered him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ answered that Jibrīl, peace be upon him, had just risen from him and told him al-Ḥusayn would be killed by the bank of the Euphrates, offering to make him smell his soil. When ʿAlī accepted, he stretched his hand, took a handful of dust and gave it to him, and ʿAlī could not restrain his eyes from overflowing. > **""Be patient, Abū ʿAbdullāh; be patient, Abū ʿAbdullāh, by the bank of the Euphrates. Rather Jibrīl, peace be upon him, rose from me just now and told me that al-Ḥusayn will be killed by the bank of the Euphrates. Would you like me to make you smell his soil""**"
"15113","ʿĀʾishah or Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with them, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to one of them that an angel who had never entered upon him before had entered the house and told him that this son of his, Ḥusayn, would be killed, offering to show him the soil of the land where he would be killed, whereupon he brought forth red soil. > **""An angel visited me in the house who had never visited me before. He said: This son of yours, Ḥusayn, will be killed, and if you wish I shall show you the soil of the land where he will be killed""**"
"15114","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, entered upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ while revelation was descending upon him, and climbed upon the Prophet ﷺ as he leaned forward upon his back. Jibrīl asked the Prophet ﷺ whether he loved him, and when he affirmed his love for his son, Jibrīl told him his community would kill him after him, stretched his hand, brought white soil, and named the land al-Ṭaff as the place of his killing. When Jibrīl left, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came out holding him in his hand weeping, told ʿĀʾishah that Jibrīl had informed him his son Ḥusayn would be killed in the land of al-Ṭaff and that his community would be tried after him, then went out to his Companions — among them ʿAlī, Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, Ḥudhayfah, ʿAmmār and Abū Dharr, may Allāh be pleased with them — weeping. When they asked what made him weep, he told them Jibrīl had informed him his son al-Ḥusayn would be killed after him in the land of al-Ṭaff, had brought him this soil, and had told him it contained his resting place. > **""O Jibrīl, how should I not love my son. O ʿĀʾishah, Jibrīl has told me that my son Ḥusayn will be killed in the land of al-Ṭaff, and that my community will be tried after me. Jibrīl, peace be upon him, told me that my son al-Ḥusayn will be killed after me in the land of al-Ṭaff, and he brought me this soil and told me that in it is his resting place""**"
"15115","Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ was sleeping with her while Ḥusayn crawled in the house. When she was heedless of him he crawled to the Prophet ﷺ, climbed upon his belly and urinated upon his navel. When the Prophet ﷺ awoke she rose to lift him from his belly, but the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ bade her leave his son. When he finished urinating he took a vessel of water, poured it over it, and explained that the urine of a boy is sprinkled over and that of a girl is washed. She added that he then stood to pray holding him, laying him down when bowing and prostrating and lifting him when standing, and when seated he supplicated raising his hands. When he finished prayer she remarked upon his unprecedented conduct, and he told her Jibrīl had come informing him his son would be killed, and upon his request had brought him red soil. > **""Leave my son. It is sprinkled from a boy and washed from a girl. Jibrīl came to me and told me that my son will be killed. I said: Then show me — so he brought me red soil""**"
"15116","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was once seated in her house and ordered that none enter upon him. While she waited, al-Ḥusayn entered, and she heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sobbing. Peering in, she saw Ḥusayn in his lap while the Prophet ﷺ wiped his forehead weeping. When she swore she had not known of his entry, he told her Jibrīl, peace be upon him, had been with them in the house and asked whether he loved him. He answered that in this world, yes. Jibrīl told him his community would kill him in a land called Karbalāʾ and took a handful of its soil, which the Prophet ﷺ showed her. Later, when al-Ḥusayn was surrounded where he was killed, he asked the name of the land, and upon being told Karbalāʾ, he affirmed the truth of Allāh and His Messenger — karb and balāʾ."
"15117","In another version of the preceding report, his words were that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ spoke the truth — a land of karb and balāʾ."
"15118","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn were playing before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ in her house when Jibrīl descended and told him his community would kill this son of his after him, gesturing toward al-Ḥusayn. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ wept and pressed him to his chest, then entrusted the soil to Umm Salamah as a deposit, smelled it, lamented woe and distress and trial, and told her that when this soil turned to blood she would know his son had been killed. Umm Salamah placed it in a flask and would look at it daily, saying the day it turned to blood would be a grave day. > **""O Umm Salamah, this soil is a trust with you. Woe, distress and trial. O Umm Salamah, when this soil turns to blood, know that my son has been killed""**"
"15119","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ told his wives not to make this boy — meaning Ḥusayn — weep. It was the day of Umm Salamah when Jibrīl descended, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ entered within and told Umm Salamah to let none enter upon him. Al-Ḥusayn came, and upon seeing the Prophet ﷺ in the house wished to enter. Umm Salamah seized him, embraced him, coaxed and hushed him, but when his weeping intensified she released him, so he entered and sat in the lap of the Prophet ﷺ. Jibrīl told the Prophet ﷺ his community would kill this son of his. When the Prophet ﷺ asked whether they would kill him while believing in him, Jibrīl affirmed it, took soil, and named such-and-such place. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came out embracing Ḥusayn, downcast and grieved. Umm Salamah supposed he was angry at the boy's entry, and pleaded that he had commanded them not to make the boy weep and had ordered her to admit none, yet the boy came and she released him. He did not answer her but went out to his Companions as they sat and told them his community would kill him. Among them Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, the boldest toward him, asked whether they would be believers, and he affirmed it and showed them his soil. > **""Do not make this boy weep. Let none enter upon me. Will they kill him while they believe in me. Yes, they will kill him. My community will kill this one. Yes, and this is his soil""**"
"15120","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came out to them changed in complexion and declared himself Muḥammad, given the openings and sealings of speech, commanding obedience while among them and adherence to the Book of Allāh after his departure, permitting its lawful and forbidding its unlawful. He foretold death bearing spirit and rest by a prior decree of Allāh, trials like portions of dark night succeeding one another, prophethood transformed into kingship, mercy for whoever took it by right and left it as he entered it, then bade Muʿādh hold and count. When he reached five he named Yazīd and invoked no blessing upon him, then his eyes ﷺ shed tears. He announced that Ḥusayn's killing had been conveyed to him, his soil brought, his killer named, and swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that he would not be killed among a people who failed to defend him but Allāh would set their hearts at variance, empower their worst over them, and divide them into factions. He lamented the chicks of the family of Muḥammad at the hands of a luxurious successor who would kill his descendants, bade Muʿādh hold, and upon reaching ten named al-Walīd by Pharaoh's name as demolisher of the ordinances of Islam, before whom a man of his household would stand while Allāh unsheathed His sword without sheathing, and they would differ thus, interlacing his fingers. After one hundred and twenty there would be swift reckoning and rampant killing wherein their ruin lay, and a man from the children of al-ʿAbbās would rule over them. > **""I am Muḥammad; I have been given the openings of speech and its sealings, so obey me while I am among you, and when I am taken, upon you is the Book of Allāh — permit its lawful and forbid its unlawful. Yazīd — may Allāh not bless Yazīd. Ḥusayn has been announced to me, his soil brought to me, his killer named to me. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, they will not kill him among a people who fail to defend him but Allāh will set variance between their breasts and hearts, empower their worst over them, and clothe them in factions. Woe to the chicks of the family of Muḥammad from a successor made a luxurious ruler, who kills my descendants and the descendants of my descendants. Hold, O Muʿādh. Al-Walīd — Pharaoh's name — demolisher of the ordinances of Islam; before him a man of his household while Allāh unsheathes His sword with no sheathing for it. After one hundred and twenty there will be swift reckoning and rampant killing — therein is their ruin — and a man from the children of al-ʿAbbās will rule over them""**"
"15121","Abū al-Ṭufayl, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the angel of rain sought permission to greet the Prophet ﷺ in the house of Umm Salamah, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ ordered that none enter upon them. Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, came and entered. Umm Salamah said it was al-Ḥusayn, and the Prophet ﷺ bade her leave him. He climbed upon the neck of the Prophet ﷺ and played with him while the angel looked on. The angel asked Muḥammad ﷺ whether he loved him, and when he swore by Allāh that he loved him, the angel told him his community would kill him and offered to show him the place, stretching his hand and taking a handful of dust. Umm Salamah took the dust and tied it in her veil, and they held it to be from Karbalāʾ. > **""Let none enter upon us. Leave him. Yes, by Allāh, I love him. Your community will kill him, and if you wish I shall show you the place""**"
"15122","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī would be killed sixty years after his hijrah. > **""Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī will be killed sixty years after my hijrah""**"
"15123","With the same chain she reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said al-Ḥusayn would be killed when greyness spread over him. > **""Al-Ḥusayn will be killed when greyness covers him""**"
"15124","ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, swore that al-Ḥusayn would surely be killed, and that he knew the soil where he would be killed, near the two rivers."
"15125","Shaybān ibn Mukharrim, who was ʿUthmānī in sympathy, reported that he was with ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, when he came to Karbalāʾ and declared that a martyr would be killed in this spot like no martyrs but the martyrs of Badr. Shaybān deemed it one of his falsehoods, and there lay the leg of a dead donkey, so he told his servant to take the donkey's leg, drive it into his seat and conceal it, striking its back. Later, when al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was killed, he set out with his companions to find the body of al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī lying by that donkey's leg with his companions fallen around him."
"15126","Abū Harthamah reported that he was with ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, at the river of Karbalāʾ when they passed a tree beneath which lay gazelle droppings. He took a handful, smelled it, then declared that seventy thousand would be gathered from this land entering Paradise without reckoning."
"15127","Abū Hubayrah reported that he accompanied ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, until he reached Kūfah, ascended the minbar, praised Allāh and extolled Him, then asked how they would be when the progeny of their Prophet descended among them. They answered they would strive for Allāh concerning them a fine striving, whereupon he swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that they would surely descend among them and they would surely go out against them and kill them, then recited his verse that they brought him by delusion and failed, loving his escape though there was no escape nor excuse."
"15128","Al-Musayyab ibn Najyah reported that ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, asked whether to tell them of his own particular affair and that of his household. When they agreed, he described Ḥasan as a man of bowls and tables, a youth among youths who would avail them nothing in war worth a sparrow's snare when the rings met; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar as a man of diversion, shade and vanity; warned them not to be deceived by the two sons of ʿAbbās; and declared that as for himself and Ḥusayn, he was from them and they from him. He feared these people would be given sway through their righteousness in their land and their corruption in theirs, their fidelity and their treachery, their obedience to their leader and their disobedience, their unity upon falsehood and their division from truth, until their dominion lengthened and they permitted every prohibition of Allāh, their injustice entered every house of clay and wool, until one of them followed them as a slave follows his master — obeying when present and reviling when absent — and the greatest among them in detachment would be the best in expectation of Allāh. If Allāh granted wellness they should accept, and if tried they should endure, for the end belongs to the God-fearing."
"15129","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that al-Ḥusayn sat in the lap of the Prophet ﷺ when Jibrīl, peace be upon him, asked whether he loved him. He answered how he should not love him when he was the fruit of his heart. Jibrīl told him his community would kill him and offered to show him the site of his grave, then seized a handful of red soil. > **""How should I not love him when he is the fruit of my heart""**"
"15130","Al-Shaʿbī reported that when al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī resolved to leave for the land of Iraq, he wished to meet Ibn ʿUmar and ask him, and was told he was at his land. He went to bid him farewell and told him he intended Iraq. Ibn ʿUmar bade him not do so, citing the Prophet's ﷺ choice between being a king-prophet or a servant-prophet, his being told to be humble, and his choosing to be a servant-prophet, and reminding him he was a piece of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. When he refused, he bade him farewell entrusting him to Allāh as one to be killed. > **""I was given choice between being a king-prophet or a servant-prophet, and it was said to me: Be humble — so I chose to be a servant-prophet. I entrust you to Allāh as one slain""**"
"15131","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that Ḥusayn sought his permission for departure, whereupon he answered that were it not disgraceful for him or for Ḥusayn he would have clasped his head with his hands. Ḥusayn replied to him that to be killed in such-and-such place was dearer to him than that the sanctuary of Allāh and His Messenger be violated through him, and with that his soul was consoled concerning him."
"15132","ʿUbaydullāh ibn al-Ḥurr reported that he asked al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, whether the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had entrusted anything to him concerning this march of his, and he answered no."
"15133","Al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥanṭab reported that when al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was surrounded, he asked the name of the land, and upon being told Karbalāʾ, he affirmed that the Prophet ﷺ spoke the truth — it is a land of karb and balāʾ. > **""It is a land of karb and balāʾ""**"
"15134","ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn reported that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī said to him a day before his killing that the Children of Isrāʾīl had a king, then narrated the ḥadīth."
"15135","Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan reported that when ʿUmar ibn Saʿd descended upon al-Ḥusayn and he was certain they would kill him, he stood among his companions as a speaker, praised Allāh and extolled Him, then declared that what they saw had befallen, the world had changed and grown unrecognisable, its good had turned away and withdrawn until naught remained but dregs like the dregs of a vessel and mean livelihood like foul pasture. He asked whether they did not see truth unpractised and falsehood unforbidden, urging the believer to desire meeting Allāh, for he saw death as naught but happiness and life with wrongdoers as naught but disgust. Al-Ḥusayn was killed on ʿĀshūrāʾ in the year sixty-one at al-Ṭaff in Karbalāʾ, wearing a dark khazz cloak, dyed black, at the age of fifty-six."
"15136","Al-Kalbī reported that a man shot al-Ḥusayn while he drank, paralysing his jaw, whereupon he prayed that Allāh never quench him, and the man drank until he burst."
"15137","Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿUthmān reported that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī set out to Kūfah resenting the rule of Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah, whereupon Yazīd wrote to ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād, his governor over Iraq, that Ḥusayn had marched to Kūfah and that his era, land and governorship above all others had been tried through him — thereby he would either be freed or return a slave as slaves are enslaved. ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād killed him and sent his head to him, and when it was placed before him, Yazīd illustrated with the verse of al-Ḥuṣayn ibn Ḥimām al-Murrī that they split the skulls of beloved men though they were most undutiful and unjust."
"15138","Ibn Wāʾil — or Wāʾil ibn ʿAlqamah — who witnessed what occurred there, reported that a man stood and asked whether Ḥusayn was among them. When they affirmed, he told him tidings of the Fire, and Ḥusayn answered with tidings of a Merciful Lord and an obeyed intercessor. When they asked who he was, he named himself Ibn Juwayrah — or Ḥuwayzah. Ḥusayn prayed that Allāh drive him to the Fire, whereupon his mount bolted, his foot caught in the stirrup, and by Allāh naught remained of him upon it but his foot."
"15139","Ibn Abī Laylā reported that when Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī sensed death, he asked for a garment none would desire to place beneath his clothes lest he be stripped. When offered drawers, he refused, saying that is the dress of one humiliated, so he took a garment, tore it, and placed it beneath his clothes; yet when he was killed they stripped him."
"15140","ʿAmmār al-Duhnī reported that ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, passed Kaʿb al-Aḥbār, who declared that a man from the progeny of this man would be killed among a band whose horses' sweat would not dry before they came to Muḥammad ﷺ. When Ḥasan passed they asked whether it was he, O Abū Isḥāq, and he said no; then when Ḥusayn passed and they asked likewise, he said yes."
"15141","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that he saw the Prophet ﷺ in a dream at midday dishevelled and dusty, with a flask wherein was blood he collected or traced. When asked what it was, he answered it was the blood of al-Ḥusayn and his companions, which he had not ceased tracing that day. ʿAmmār added that they remembered the day and found he had been killed that very day. > **""The blood of al-Ḥusayn and his companions""**"
"15142","ʿUmārah ibn Yaḥyā ibn Khālid ibn ʿUrfuṭah reported that they were with Khālid ibn ʿUrfuṭah on the day al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, was killed, when Khālid told them what he had heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say — that they would be tried concerning his household after him. > **""You will be tried concerning my household after me""**"
"15143","Ḥabīb ibn Yasār reported that when al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, was struck, Zayd ibn Arqam stood at the door of the mosque and exclaimed whether they had done it, testifying that he had heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say that he entrusted them both and the righteous believers to Allāh. When ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād was told of Zayd's words, he dismissed him as an old man gone in mind. > **""O Allāh, I entrust them both and the righteous believers to You""**"
"15144","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār stated that al-Ḥusayn was born five nights into Shaʿbān in the year four of the hijrah, and was killed on Friday, the day of ʿĀshūrāʾ in Muḥarram of the year sixty-one, slain by Sinān ibn Abī Anas and finished by Khawlī ibn Yazīd al-Aṣbaḥī of Ḥimyar, who severed his head and brought it to ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād, whereupon Sinān boasted in verse that his mounts be laden with silver and gold for he had killed the veiled king, the best of people in mother and father."
"15145","Shahr ibn Ḥawshab reported that he heard Umm Salamah, when the tidings of al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī came, curse the people of Iraq, declaring they had killed him — may Allāh kill them — deceived and humiliated him, may Allāh curse them."
"15146","Aslam al-Minqarī reported that he visited al-Ḥajjāj when Sinān ibn Abī Anas, the killer of al-Ḥusayn, entered — an aged dark man with henna, long-nosed, freckled — and stood before al-Ḥajjāj, who asked whether he had killed al-Ḥusayn. When he affirmed and described propping him with the spear and hacking him with the sword, al-Ḥajjāj told him the two would never meet in one abode."
"15147","Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī declared that had he been among those who killed al-Ḥusayn, then been forgiven and admitted to Paradise, he would still be ashamed to pass the Prophet ﷺ lest he look upon his face."
"15148","Al-Layth ibn Saʿd stated that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī refused to surrender, so they fought and killed him along with his sons and companions who fought with him at a place called al-Ṭaff. They carried ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, Fāṭimah bint Ḥusayn and Sukaynah bint Ḥusayn to ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād — ʿAlī then a youth come of age — who sent them to Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah. Yazīd ordered Sukaynah placed behind his couch lest she see the head of her father and kin, with ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn in fetters. Placing his head, he struck the front teeth of al-Ḥusayn reciting that they split the skulls of beloved men though most undutiful and unjust. ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn recited that no calamity befalls the earth or yourselves but is in a Book before We bring it about — indeed that is easy for Allāh. Yazīd found it heavy that he illustrated with verse while ʿAlī recited a verse of Allāh's Book, and answered with what their hands earned while He pardons much. ʿAlī swore that had the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ seen them fettered he would have loved to free them, and Yazīd affirmed and freed them; then that had they stood distant before him he would have loved to draw them near, and Yazīd affirmed and drew them near. Fāṭimah and Sukaynah craned to see their father's head while Yazīd craned in his مجلس to veil it, then ordered them prepared, treated them well, and sent them to Madīnah."
"15149","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that when Ibn Ziyād was brought the head of al-Ḥusayn, may Allāh be pleased with him, he began tapping with a rod in his hand upon its eye and nose, whereupon Zayd bade him lift the rod, and when asked why, answered that he had seen the mouth of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ in its place."
"15150","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that when ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād was brought the head of al-Ḥusayn, he began tapping its front teeth with a rod, remarking — as he thought — upon its beauty. Anas swore he would grieve him, testifying that he had seen the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ kiss where his rod fell, whereupon he withdrew."
"15151","Al-Shaʿbī reported that he saw in a dream as though men from heaven descended with spears pursuing the killers of al-Ḥusayn, and soon after al-Mukhtār rose and killed them."
"15152","Al-Shaʿbī stated that he saw al-Ḥusayn as the first head carried in Islam."
"15153","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr reported that he entered upon ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād to find the head of al-Ḥusayn before him upon a shield, then soon after entered upon al-Mukhtār to find the head of ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād upon a shield, then soon after entered upon Muṣʿab ibn al-Zubayr to find the head of al-Mukhtār upon a shield, then soon after entered upon ʿAbdullāh to find the head of Muṣʿab ibn al-Zubayr upon a shield."
"15154","Thuwayd al-Juʿfī reported from his father that when al-Ḥusayn was killed, a camel from his camp was seized, and when cooked it turned to blood, so they overturned it."
"15155","Ḥumayd al-Ṭaḥḥān reported that he was among Khuzāʿah when part of the legacy of al-Ḥusayn was brought and they were asked whether to slaughter or sell and divide. When slaughter was chosen, he sat upon a bowl, and as he sat it burst into fire."
"15156","ʿAmr ibn Baʿjah stated that the first humiliation to enter upon the Arabs was the killing of al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī and the claiming of Ziyād."
"15157","Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī warned against reviling ʿAlī or any of the household, recounting that a neighbour of theirs from Balhujaym jeered at the sinful al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī — may Allāh kill him — whereupon Allāh cast two stars into his eyes and effaced his sight."
"15158","The chamberlain of ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād reported that he entered the palace behind him when al-Ḥusayn was killed to find his face blazing with fire, so he gestured with his sleeve over his face asking whether he had seen, and when he affirmed, he ordered him to conceal it."
"15159","Al-Zuhrī reported that ʿAbd al-Malik asked him, as one uniquely informed, what sign marked the day al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was killed. He answered that no pebble was lifted in Bayt al-Maqdis but fresh blood was found beneath it, whereupon ʿAbd al-Malik declared them two companions in this ḥadīth."
"15160","Al-Zuhrī stated that no stone was lifted in Sham on the day al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was killed but over blood."
"15161","Umm Ḥakīm reported that al-Ḥusayn was killed when she was a young girl, and the sky remained for days like clotted blood."
"15162","Jamīl ibn Zayd reported that when al-Ḥusayn was killed the sky turned red. When challenged, he insisted the denier was a hypocrite liar — the sky indeed reddened at his killing."
"15163","Abū Qabīl reported that when al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was killed the sun eclipsed so that the stars appeared at midday until they thought it was night."
"15164","ʿĪsā ibn al-Ḥārith al-Kindī reported that when al-Ḥusayn was killed, for seven days whenever they prayed ʿaṣr they looked at the sky above the walls as though saffron-dyed cloaks, and saw the stars striking one another."
"15165","Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn stated that there had been no redness in the sky until al-Ḥusayn was killed."
"15166","Sufyān reported from his grandmother, Umm Abīh, that two men of Juʿf witnessed the killing of al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī: one whose organ lengthened until he wound it, and another who would face the waterskin with his mouth until he drained it. Sufyān added that he saw the child of one of them afflicted and as though mad."
"15167","With the same chain he added that he saw the wars plant taken from the camp of al-Ḥusayn turned like ash."
"15168","Al-Aʿmash reported that a man of Banī Asad defecated upon the grave of al-Ḥusayn, whereupon the people of that house were struck with disorder, madness, leprosy, vitiligo and poverty."
"15169","Al-Layth ibn Saʿd stated that Muʿāwiyah died in Rajab with four nights elapsed thereof, Yazīd succeeded in the year sixty, and in the year sixty-one al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī and his companions, may Allāh be pleased with them, were killed ten nights into Muḥarram on ʿĀshūrāʾ, along with al-ʿAbbās son of ʿAlī whose mother was Umm al-Banīn the ʿĀmiriyyah, Jaʿfar and ʿAbdullāh and ʿUthmān sons of ʿAlī, Abū Bakr son of ʿAlī whose mother was Laylā bint Masʿūd the Nahshaliyyah, ʿAlī the elder son of al-Ḥusayn whose mother was Laylā the Thaqafiyyah, ʿAbdullāh son of al-Ḥusayn whose mother was al-Rabāb bint Imriʾ al-Qays the Kalbiyyah, Abū Bakr and al-Qāsim sons of al-Ḥusayn by slave mothers, ʿAwn and Muḥammad sons of ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, Jaʿfar and Muslim sons of ʿAqīl, and Sulaymān the freedman of al-Ḥusayn. Al-Ḥusayn was killed at fifty-eight years of age, may Allāh be pleased with them."
"15170","Mundhir al-Thawrī reported that whenever they mentioned Ḥusayn and those killed with him, Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah would say seventeen youths were killed with him, all of whom had stirred in the womb of Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her and them."
"15171","Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn stated that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was killed at the age of fifty-eight."
"15172","Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī stated that sixteen men of his household were killed with al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, and that by Allāh there was no household upon the earth that day resembling them. Sufyān added: and who doubts this."
"15173","Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah stated that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was killed on ʿĀshūrāʾ in the year sixty-one at the age of fifty-eight, and that he dyed with henna and katam."
"15174","Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad reported from his father that ʿAlī was killed at fifty-eight, al-Ḥusayn likewise, and ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn died likewise."
"15175","ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn reported that al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was killed owing many debts, for which ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn sold such-and-such spring and such-and-such spring."
"15176","Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Makhzūmī reported that when the baggage of al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī was brought to Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah and his head placed before him, Yazīd wept and recited that they split the skulls of beloved men though most undutiful and unjust, swearing that had he been his companion he would never have killed him. ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn corrected him, and when Yazīd asked how, O son of my mother, he recited that no calamity befalls the earth or yourselves but is in a Book before We bring it about. With him was ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Umm al-Ḥakam, who recited of skulls by al-Ṭaff nearer in kin than Ibn Ziyād the slave of base lineage, while Sumayyah's progeny numbered as pebbles and the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh had no progeny left. Yazīd raised his hand, struck the chest of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and bade him be silent."
"15177","Abū Qabīl reported that when al-Ḥusayn was killed and they severed his head, they halted at the first stage drinking wine and toasting the head, when an iron pen emerged from a wall and wrote in a line of blood whether a nation that killed Ḥusayn could hope for his grandfather's intercession on the Day of Reckoning. They fled leaving the head, then returned."
"15178","An imām of Banī Sulaymān reported from his elders that they raided Byzantium and lodged in one of their churches, where they read upon an inscribed stone whether a nation that killed Ḥusayn could hope for his grandfather's intercession on the Day of Reckoning. When asked how long since the church was built, they answered three hundred years before their Prophet was sent."
"15179","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that she heard the jinn lamenting al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī."
"15180","Maymūnah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that she heard the jinn lamenting al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī."
"15181","Umm Salamah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that she had not heard the lament of the jinn since the Prophet ﷺ was taken except that night, and deemed her son — meaning al-Ḥusayn, may Allāh be pleased with him — taken. She told her maid to go and ask, and word came that he had been killed, while a jinn woman lamented bidding the eye exert itself in weeping, for who would weep for martyrs after her — over a band whom death drove to a tyrant in a slave's dominion."
"15182","Abū Janāb al-Kalbī reported from plasterers who said that when they went out to the graveyard by night at the killing of al-Ḥusayn, they heard the jinn lamenting him, chanting that the Messenger wiped his forehead so it shone upon the cheeks, his parents from the noblest Quraysh and his grandfather the best of grandfathers."
"15183","Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayd al-Jahmī, of the progeny of Abū Jahm ibn Ḥudhayfah, would recite concerning the killing of al-Ḥusayn the verse of Zaynab bint ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib asking what they would say if the Prophet asked them, the last of nations, what they had done with his kin, supporters and progeny — captives and slain stained with blood — though his reward in counsel was that they succeed him evilly among his kin. Abū al-Aswad al-Duʾalī answered that they would say: Our Lord, we wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us we shall be among the losers."
"15184","With another, better chain he narrated the same and added Abū al-Aswad's verse declaring in wrath that may Allāh remove the dominion of Banī Ziyād and distance them as they distanced and betrayed, as Thamūd and ʿĀd were distanced, and may their mounts never return to them when they depart toward the Day of Assembly."
"15185","Sulaymān ibn al-Haytham reported that ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī would circumambulate the House, and when he wished to touch the Stone the people made way, while al-Farazdaq ibn Ghālib looked on. When a man asked Abū Firās who he was, al-Farazdaq answered in verse that this is he whose tread the plain knows, whom the House, the lawful and the sacred know — son of the best of Allāh's servants, the pious, pure, guiding banner, whom the corner of al-Ḥaṭīm nearly grasps from knowing his palm when he touches, at whose sight Quraysh declare all nobility ends, who is modest yet awe-inspiring and speaks only smiling, in whose hand is a fragrant cane of a noble high-nosed bearer, sprung from the Messenger of Allāh of blest stock and traits, whose goal no generous one attains nor any tribe approaches however noble, and what clan owes not allegiance to his forebears' primacy."
"15186","Sufyān reported that he asked ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Yazīd whether he had seen al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, and he answered black in head and beard except a few hairs at the front of his beard — uncertain whether he dyed leaving that place in imitation of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ or naught else had greyed. He added that he had seen Ḥasan after prayer was established prostrating between the imām and some people, and when told to sit, answered that prayer had been established."
"15187","Muṣʿab ibn ʿAbdullāh stated that al-Ḥusayn performed twenty-five pilgrimages on foot."
"15188","Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād reported that the Prophet ﷺ came out of the house of ʿĀʾishah, passed by the house of Fāṭimah, and heard Ḥusayn weeping, whereupon he asked whether she did not know that his weeping hurt him. > **""Do you not know that his weeping hurts me""**"
"15189","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise and that Fāṭimah is the chief of their women, except for what belongs to Maryam bint ʿImrān. > **""Al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn are the sayyids of the youth of Paradise, and Fāṭimah is the sayyidah of their women, except for what belongs to Maryam bint ʿImrān""**"
"15190","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the chief women of Paradise after Maryam bint ʿImrān are Fāṭimah and Khadījah, then Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim, wife of Pharaoh. > **""The sayyidahs of the women of Paradise after Maryam bint ʿImrān are Fāṭimah and Khadījah, then Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim, wife of Pharaoh""**"
"15191","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that an angel from heaven who had never visited him sought Allāh's permission to visit him and gave him glad tidings — or informed him — that Fāṭimah is the chief of the women of his community. > **""An angel from heaven who had never visited me sought Allāh's permission to visit me, and gave me glad tidings that Fāṭimah is the sayyidah of the women of my community""**"
"15192","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Fāṭimah asking whether she would not be pleased to be the chief of the women of Paradise while her two sons are the chiefs of the youth of Paradise. > **""Are you not pleased to be the sayyidah of the women of Paradise, and your two sons the sayyids of the youth of Paradise""**"
"15193","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, stated that she never saw one more excellent than Fāṭimah except her father. When something occurred between the two, she said: O Messenger of Allāh, ask her, for she does not lie. In the other wording she said she never saw anyone at all more truthful than Fāṭimah."
"15194","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Abū Bakr sought permission upon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and heard the voice of ʿĀʾishah raised, declaring twice or thrice that she knew ʿAlī and Fāṭimah were more beloved to him than herself and her father. Abū Bakr sought permission, entered, leaned toward her and rebuked her as daughter of so-and-so never to raise her voice above the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ."
"15195","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ entered upon ʿAlī and Fāṭimah while they laughed, and when they saw the Prophet ﷺ they fell silent. When he asked why they had laughed then fallen silent upon seeing him, Fāṭimah hastened and said: By my father, O Messenger of Allāh — he claims he is more beloved to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ than I, while I claim I am more beloved to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ than he. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ smiled and told her she had a child's tenderness while ʿAlī was dearer to him than she. > **""What is wrong with you — you laughed, then when you saw me you fell silent. O my daughter, you have a child's tenderness, and ʿAlī is dearer to me than you""**"
"15196","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib asked: O Messenger of Allāh, who is more beloved to you, I or Fāṭimah. He answered that Fāṭimah was more beloved to him than he, while he was dearer to him than she. > **""Fāṭimah is more beloved to me than you, and you are dearer to me than she""**"
"15197","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that she would see the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ kiss Fāṭimah, and remarked that she saw him do what he had not done before. He answered her as Ḥumayrāʾ that on the night he was taken to heaven he entered Paradise and stood at a tree of Paradise the fairest, whitest-leafed and sweetest-fruited he had seen, took a fruit and ate it, and it became a sperm-drop in his loins. When he descended to earth he was with Khadījah and she conceived Fāṭimah, so whenever he longed for the fragrance of Paradise he smelled Fāṭimah. He added that Fāṭimah was not as human women are, nor suffered ailments as they do. > **""O Ḥumayrāʾ, on the night I was taken to heaven I entered Paradise and stood at a tree of Paradise — I never saw a tree in Paradise fairer, whiter in leaf, or sweeter in fruit. I took a fruit and ate it, and it became a sperm-drop in my loins. When I descended to earth I was with Khadījah and she conceived Fāṭimah, so whenever I long for the fragrance of Paradise I smell the fragrance of Fāṭimah. O Ḥumayrāʾ, Fāṭimah is not as human women are, nor suffers ailments as they suffer""**"
"15198","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, that Allāh would not punish her nor her children. > **""Allāh will not punish you nor your children""**"
"15199","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Fāṭimah guarded her chastity, and that Allāh would admit her and her progeny to Paradise for her guarding it. > **""Fāṭimah guarded her chastity, and Allāh will admit her and her progeny to Paradise for her guarding her chastity""**"
"15200","ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he was with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ when he asked what is best for a woman, and they fell silent. When he returned he asked Fāṭimah what is best for women, and she answered that men should not see them. When he mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, he affirmed that Fāṭimah was a piece of him, may Allāh be pleased with her. > **""What is best for a woman. Men do not see them. Fāṭimah is but a piece of me""**"
"15201","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh honour him, proposed to the daughter of Abū Jahl, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ told him that if he married her he should return their daughter to them. Here ends the ḥadīth of Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ, with an addition that the Prophet ﷺ swore by Allāh that the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and the daughter of the enemy of Allāh would never be gathered under one man. > **""If you marry her, return our daughter to us. By Allāh, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and the daughter of the enemy of Allāh will never be gathered under one man""**"
"15202","Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, proposed to her. When that reached Fāṭimah she came to the Prophet ﷺ and said that Asmāʾ was marrying ʿAlī, whereupon he told her she had no right to hurt Allāh and His Messenger. > **""She has no right to hurt Allāh and His Messenger""**"
"15203","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Ḥasan ibn Ḥasan sent to al-Miswar proposing his daughter. He bade the messenger arrange a meeting, and upon meeting praised Allāh and declared no kinship, lineage or marriage dearer to him than theirs, but cited the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that Fāṭimah is a branch from him — what delights her delights him and what grieves her grieves him — and that on the Day of Resurrection all lineages would be cut except his lineage and cause. Since the daughter of Fāṭimah was under Ḥasan's care, marrying his daughter to him would grieve her, so the suitor left excusing him. > **""Fāṭimah is a branch from me; what delights her delights me, and what grieves her grieves me. On the Day of Resurrection all lineages will be cut except my lineage and my cause""**"
"15204","ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, that Allāh is angry for her anger and pleased for her pleasure. > **""Allāh is angry for your anger and pleased for your pleasure""**"
"15205","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he sat with the Prophet ﷺ when Fāṭimah came and stood facing him. He bade her draw near thrice until she stood before him. ʿImrān saw yellowness upon her face and blood gone. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ spread his fingers, placed his palm between her collar-bones, raised his head and supplicated to the Satisfier of hunger, Fulfiller of need and Raiser of the lowly not to let Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad hunger. The yellowness of hunger left her face and blood returned, and when asked thereafter she swore she never hungered after that, O ʿImrān. > **""Draw near, O Fāṭimah. O Allāh, Satisfier of hunger, Fulfiller of need, Raiser of the lowly — do not let Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad hunger""**"
"15206","Ḥujr ibn ʿAnbas, who had reached the Jāhiliyyah, reported that ʿAlī, may Allāh have mercy upon him, proposed Fāṭimah to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, who answered that she was his, O ʿAlī, for he was no deceiver — meaning he had promised him and would not break the promise. > **""She is yours, O ʿAlī; I am no dajjāl""**"
"15207","Ḥujr ibn ʿAnbas also — who had eaten blood in the Jāhiliyyah and witnessed al-Jamal and Ṣiffīn with ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him — reported that Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them both, proposed to Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ declared she was for ʿAlī. > **""She is yours, O ʿAlī""**"
"15208","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that Allāh had commanded him to marry Fāṭimah to ʿAlī. > **""Allāh commanded me to marry Fāṭimah to ʿAlī""**"
"15209","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allāh be pleased with him, declared he would narrate a ḥadīth heard from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ for which he never found corroboration: he heard him on the expedition of Tabūk while marching with him say that when Allāh commanded him to marry Fāṭimah to ʿAlī and he did so, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, told him Allāh had built a paradise of hollow pearl, between each reed and reed a pearl of ruby inlaid with gold, its roofs green peridot, its lattices of pearl crowned with rubies, its chambers of bricks of silver, gold, pearl, ruby and peridot, its springs welling in its corners, girt by rivers over which were domes of pearl hung with chains of gold, girt by kinds of trees, in every branch a dome, in every dome a couch of white pearl covered with sundus and istabraq, its earth spread with saffron kneaded with musk and ambergris, in every dome a ḥūr, each dome with a hundred doors, upon each door two guards and two trees, in every dome a carpet and a writing, with Āyat al-Kursī inscribed around the domes. When he asked Jibrīl for whom Allāh built this paradise, he answered He built it for his daughter Fāṭimah and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, apart from their other gardens, as a gift delighting his eyes, O Messenger of Allāh. > **""When Allāh commanded me to marry Fāṭimah to ʿAlī and I did so, Jibrīl, peace be upon him, said: Allāh has built a paradise of hollow pearl for Fāṭimah your daughter and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, apart from their gardens — a gift by which He delights your eyes, O Messenger of Allāh""**"
"15210","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Abū Bakr came to the Prophet ﷺ, sat before him, and cited his counsel and precedence in Islam, asking to marry Fāṭimah. The Prophet ﷺ asked what then, and upon his request fell silent — or turned away. Abū Bakr returned to ʿUmar declaring ruin, and upon explaining that he had proposed to Fāṭimah and been turned from, ʿUmar bade him wait while he sought the same. ʿUmar came likewise, sat before the Prophet ﷺ, cited the same, asked likewise, and was turned from. Returning to Abū Bakr, he concluded the Prophet awaited Allāh's command concerning her and proposed they urge ʿAlī to seek the same. ʿAlī reported they came to him upon the road telling him his cousin was sought, alerting him to the matter. He rose dragging his cloak, one end upon his shoulder and the other upon the ground, until he sat before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and cited his precedence and counsel. When asked what then, O ʿAlī, he asked to marry Fāṭimah. Asked what he had, he named his horse and his body-armour — meaning his coat of mail. Told his horse was indispensable but to sell his armour, he sold it for four hundred and eighty dirhams, brought it, placed it in the Prophet's lap, from which he took a handful and bade Bilāl fetch perfume therewith. He ordered her preparation: a bed laced with ribbon, a leather pillow stuffed with fibre, the house filled with sand. He warned ʿAlī to do nothing until he came. She arrived with Umm Ayman, each sitting apart, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether his brother was there. Umm Ayman exclaimed at calling his son-in-law brother, and he bade Fāṭimah bring water. She filled a cup, he spat into it, bade her rise, sprinkled between her breasts and upon her head, and sought refuge for her and her progeny from the accursed Satan. Then, bade turn, he sprinkled between her shoulders with the same supplication. Then, understanding his wish, ʿAlī filled the cup, the Prophet ﷺ spat into it, poured upon his head and before him, sought refuge for him and his progeny likewise, and bade him enter upon his wife in Allāh's name with blessing. > **""What then, O ʿAlī. What have you. As for your horse you need it, but as for your armour sell it. O Bilāl, fetch us perfume therewith. When she comes to you do nothing until I come to you. Is my brother here. Bring me water. Rise. O Allāh, I seek refuge for her with You and her progeny from the accursed Satan. Turn. Bring me water. O Allāh, I seek refuge for him with You and his progeny from the accursed Satan. Enter upon your wife in Allāh's name with blessing""**"
"15211","Anas, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allāh be pleased with him, came to Abū Bakr, may Allāh have mercy upon him, asking what prevented him marrying Fāṭimah daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. Abū Bakr answered he would not marry him, and ʿUmar protested who else he would marry when noblest and earliest in Islam. Abū Bakr went to the house of ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, bidding her mention his thought of Fāṭimah when she saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ in good cheer toward her, hoping Allāh would make her easy for him. When the Messenger came in such mood she mentioned that Abū Bakr had thought of Fāṭimah, and he answered to wait till the decree descended. Abū Bakr returned, and she wished she had never mentioned it. Abū Bakr told ʿUmar, who went to Ḥafṣah bidding her likewise mention him and Fāṭimah when she saw his goodwill, hoping Allāh would make her easy for him. When the Messenger met Ḥafṣah in such mood she mentioned Fāṭimah, and he answered likewise till the decree descended. ʿUmar met Ḥafṣah, who wished she had mentioned nothing. ʿUmar then went to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, asking what prevented him from Fāṭimah. When ʿAlī feared he would not marry him, ʿUmar protested who else he would marry when nearest of Allāh's creation to him. ʿAlī went to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with no intercessor like ʿĀʾishah or Ḥafṣah, declared he wished to marry Fāṭimah, was bidden do so, named only his Ḥuṭamiyyah armour, was told to gather what he could and bring it, brought twelve ounces — four hundred and eighty — and was married to Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her. The Prophet took three handfuls, gave them to Umm Ayman, bidding a handful for perfume and the rest, as he thought, for the woman's furnishing. When the preparation was done and they were admitted to a house, he warned ʿAlī to do nothing toward his wife until he came. The Messenger came to find Fāṭimah veiled, ʿAlī seated, and Umm Ayman in the house. He called for a cup of water, drank, spat into it, gave Fāṭimah to drink, struck her forehead, between her shoulders and her breast therefrom, then gave ʿAlī to drink and struck his forehead and between his shoulders likewise, then supplicated to remove impurity from his household and purify them. He left with Umm Ayman, committing his wife to him. > **""Until the decree descends. Do so. Gather what you can and bring it to me. Make a handful thereof into perfume. O ʿAlī, do nothing toward your wife until I come to you. O Umm Ayman, bring me a cup of water. O ʿAlī, drink. My household — remove impurity from them and purify them thoroughly. O ʿAlī, your wife""**"
"15212","In another version it is reported that ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, proposed to Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, then the narrator mentioned the ḥadīth."
"15213","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that Fāṭimah was mentioned to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ but whomever mentioned her he turned from, until they despaired of her. Saʿd ibn Muʿādh met ʿAlī and swore he deemed the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ withheld her only for him. ʿAlī asked why he thought so, protesting he was neither a man of worldly means sought for what he had — having neither gold nor silver — nor a disbeliever softened toward his religion, but the first to submit. Saʿd adjured him to relieve him therein, for his relief lay in it, instructing him to declare he came suing to Allāh and His Messenger for Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad ﷺ. ʿAlī set out burdened and reticent, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had a need, O ʿAlī. When he declared he came suing to Allāh and His Messenger for Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, the Prophet ﷺ answered welcome — a faint word. Returning to Saʿd complaining he had only been welcomed with a faint word, Saʿd swore by Him Who sent him with truth that he had married him, for there was no breaking or lying with him, and adjured him to return morrow asking when he would house him. ʿAlī deemed this harder than the first, but as commanded asked when he would house him, and was answered that night if Allāh willed. He summoned Bilāl, declaring he had married his daughter to his cousin and loved that food at marriage be his community's practice, bidding him take a sheep and four mudds and make a bowl gathering the Emigrants and Helpers, then inform him. Bilāl did so and brought the bowl before him; the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ pierced its top, bade men enter in troops without any troop returning, and they came successively till all had done. From the remainder he spat, blessed, and bade Bilāl carry it to his mothers bidding them eat and feed visitors. Then he entered upon the women declaring he had married his daughter to his cousin whose station they knew, and committed her to them. The women perfumed, dressed and adorned her from their own. When the Prophet ﷺ entered the women left behind a curtain, save Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, may Allāh be pleased with her, who explained she guarded his daughter, for a bride on her wedding night needs a woman near for any need. He prayed Allāh guard her before, behind, right and left from the accursed Satan. Then he called Fāṭimah, who came weeping upon seeing ʿAlī seated with the Prophet ﷺ. Fearing her tears were for his lack of wealth, he asked what made her weep, assuring he had not wronged her concerning himself but had secured the best of his family — swearing he had married her to one fortunate in this world and among the righteous in the next — and she softened. He called for a wash-basin filled with water, spat therein, wiped his face and feet, called Fāṭimah, dashed a handful upon her head and between her breasts, sprinkled their skins, embraced her and prayed that as Allāh had removed impurity from him and purified him so He purify them. With another basin he did the same with ʿAlī and supplicated likewise, then bade them rise to their house, praying Allāh gather them, bless their secret and set right their minds, closing their door with his hand. Ibn ʿAbbās added that Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, may Allāh be pleased with her, told him she watched the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ never cease supplicating for them alone, associating none with them, till he disappeared into his chamber ﷺ. > **""As though you have a need, O ʿAlī. Welcome. When will you house me. Tonight if Allāh wills. O Bilāl, I have married my daughter to my cousin, and I love that food at marriage be my community's practice — take a sheep and four mudds, make a bowl for me gathering the Emigrants and Helpers, and when finished inform me. Admit the people to me troop by troop, and let no troop depart to another. O Bilāl, carry it to your mothers and tell them: Eat and feed whoever visits you. I have married my daughter to my cousin, and you know her station with me, and I commit her to you — so attend to her. Gently, who are you. I ask my God to guard you before you, behind you, to your right and to your left from the accursed Satan. What makes you weep — I have not wronged you concerning myself, and I have secured for you the best of my family; by Him in Whose Hand is my soul I have married you to one fortunate in this world, and in the next he is among the righteous. O Asmāʾ, bring me the wash-basin filled with water. O Allāh, she is from me and I am from her — O Allāh, as You removed impurity from me and purified me, purify them. Rise to your house — may Allāh gather you, bless your secret and set right your minds""**"
"15214","Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that men of the Anṣār told ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, that Fāṭimah was available. He came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, greeted him, and was asked what the son of Abū Ṭālib needed. When he mentioned Fāṭimah daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, he answered welcome and kin, adding naught. ʿAlī went out to the Anṣārī company awaiting him, and when they asked what followed, he answered he knew not save those two words. They assured him one of the two sufficed — he had been granted kin and welcome. After he married him, he told him a bride must have a feast. Saʿd offered a ram, and the Anṣār gathered ṣāʿs of grain. On the wedding night he warned him to do nothing till he met him. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ called for water, performed ablution therefrom, poured it over him, and prayed for blessing in them and in their union. > **""What does the son of Abū Ṭālib need. Welcome and kin. O ʿAlī, a bride must have a feast. Do nothing till you meet me. O Allāh, bless them both, and bless them both in their union""**"
"15215","Jābir, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that they attended the wedding of ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, and Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, and never saw a finer wedding. They stuffed the mattress — meaning fibre — brought dates and raisins and ate, and her bed on her wedding night was a ram's hide."
"15216","Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that when Fāṭimah was conducted to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, they found in his house naught but spread sand, a pillow stuffed with fibre, a jar and a cup. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sent to ʿAlī warning him to do nothing — or not to approach his wife — until he came. The Prophet ﷺ came asking whether his brother was there. Umm Ayman — mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd, an Abyssinian, a righteous woman — exclaimed whether this brother to whom he had married his daughter. The Prophet ﷺ had made brotherhood among his Companions, and between ʿAlī and himself, and answered that it would indeed be so, O Umm Ayman. He called for a vessel of water, said what Allāh willed, wiped the breast and face of ʿAlī, then called Fāṭimah, who rose stumbling in her wrap from shyness. He sprinkled her therefrom, said what Allāh willed, then assured her he had not stinted her but married her to the most beloved of his family to him. Seeing a shape beyond the curtain — or door — he asked who it was. She answered Asmāʾ, and upon confirming Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays, asked whether she came honouring the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with his daughter. She affirmed that a bride on her wedding night needs a woman near for any need to confide. He supplicated for her — her most trusted deed — then committed his wife to ʿAlī, left, and never ceased supplicating for them till he disappeared into his chamber. > **""Do nothing till I come to you — do not approach your wife till I come to you. Is my brother here. It will indeed be so, O Umm Ayman. Who is this. Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays. Did you come honouring the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with his daughter. I have not stinted you but married you to the most beloved of my family to me. Your wife is yours""**"
"15217","In another version from Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays also, she reported that she attended the wedding of Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. Next morning the Prophet ﷺ came, knocked at the door, and Umm Ayman rose and opened it. He bade her call his brother, and she answered in Yemeni idiom whether this brother to whom he married his daughter. He repeated his call, the women heard his voice and withdrew, and he sat apart. Then ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, came; he supplicated for him, sprinkled water upon him, then called for Fāṭimah. She came sweating — or constrained — from shyness, and he bade her be still, for he had married her to the most beloved of his household to him, then the like was mentioned. > **""O Umm Ayman, call my brother for me. O Umm Ayman, call him for me. Call Fāṭimah for me. Be still — I have married you to the most beloved of my household to me""**"
"15218","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prepared Fāṭimah for ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with them both, he sent with her a khamīl — ʿAṭāʾ asked what a khamīl was and was told a qaṭīfah — a leather pillow stuffed with fibre, idhkhir grass and a waterskin. They would spread the khamīl and cover with half of it."
"15219","Umm Ayman, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that the Prophet ﷺ married his daughter to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with them both, and commanded him not to enter upon his wife until he came. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ then came and the ḥadīth was mentioned."
"15220","Umm Salmā reported that Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, fell ill with the illness in which she was taken, and she nursed her. One morning she appeared better than ever seen in that illness, while ʿAlī had left for some need. She asked her mother — meaning Umm Salmā — to pour her washing water, so she poured it and she washed as finely as ever seen. She then asked for her new clothes, put them on, asked to have her bed laid mid-house, lay down facing the qiblah with her hand beneath her cheek, and declared she was now to be taken, purified, and none should uncover her. She was taken in her place, and when ʿAlī came she informed him."
"15221","ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl reported that when death came to Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, she ordered ʿAlī, may Allāh be pleased with him, who laid her washing water. She washed, purified herself, called for her shrouds, was brought coarse rough garments which she wore, touched ḥanūṭ, then ordered ʿAlī that she not be uncovered when taken but wrapped as she was in her clothes. Asked whether he knew any who did so, he named Kathīr ibn al-ʿAbbās, upon whose shroud edges was written that Kathīr ibn al-ʿAbbās testifies there is no god but Allāh."
"15222","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq stated that Fāṭimah, may Allāh be pleased with her, died at twenty-eight, born when Quraysh built the Kaʿbah seven years and six months before the Prophet's ﷺ mission. The Prophet ﷺ remained ten years in Mecca after his mission, then emigrated and remained ten, then Fāṭimah lived six months after him and died in the year eleven of the hijrah."
"15223","Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah stated that Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, died at twenty-seven."
"15224","Ibn Jurayj reported from more than one that Fāṭimah was the youngest child of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and the most beloved of them to him, though al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār held Ruqayyah younger than Fāṭimah."
"15225","Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, called Fustuqah, stated that Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, was kunyed Umm Abīhā, the youngest child of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ by Khadījah; and it was said she was the twin of ʿAbdullāh, son of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ."
"15226","ʿĀʾishah, may Allāh be pleased with her, reported that Fāṭimah died six months after the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib buried her by night."
"15227","Abū Jaʿfar — meaning Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī — stated that Fāṭimah remained three months after the Prophet ﷺ and was never seen laughing after the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ save that they glimpsed the edge of her eyetooth."
"15228","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that on the Day of Resurrection it will be called: O people of the gathering, lower your gazes until Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad ﷺ passes — and she will pass wearing two green — or red — wraps. > **""O people of the gathering, lower your gazes until Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad passes""**"
"15229","Ibn Jurayj reported from more than one that Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, was the eldest of his daughters."
"15230","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār stated that al-Qāsim was born to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, his eldest child, then Zaynab. Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, was with Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn ʿAbd Shams and bore him ʿAlī and Umāmah. ʿAlī was nursed among Banī Ghāḍir, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ weaned him away while his father was then a polytheist. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ declared that whoever shared with him in aught, he had most right to it, and whatever disbeliever shared with a Muslim in aught, the Muslim had most right to it than he. Al-Zubayr added from ʿUmar ibn Abī Bakr al-Muʾammalī that ʿAlī ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ, son of the Prophet's ﷺ daughter, died near puberty, and that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had mounted him behind him upon his mount on the day of the Conquest. > **""Whoever shares with me in aught, I have most right to it, and whatever disbeliever shares with a Muslim in aught, he has most right to it than he""**"
"15231","ʿĀʾishah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ arrived in Mecca, his daughter Zaynab left Mecca with Kinānah — or Ibn Kinānah — and they went seeking her. Habbār ibn al-Aswad overtook her and kept prodding her camel with his spear till she was thrown and miscarried, then she was carried on. Banū Hāshim and Banū Umayyah disputed concerning her, the latter claiming most right as she was under their cousin Abū al-ʿĀṣ and with Hind bint ʿUtbah, who would say this was on her father's account. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ told Zayd ibn Ḥārithah to set out and fetch Zaynab, giving his ring to deliver to her. Zayd kept gentle until he met a shepherd pasturing for Abū al-ʿĀṣ with sheep belonging to Zaynab bint Muḥammad ﷺ. Walking awhile with him, he asked whether to give him something to deliver to her mentioning none, and upon his assent gave him the ring. She recognised it, asked who gave it, was told a man at such-and-such place, and fell silent till night, when she went out to him. When she reached him he bade her mount before him upon his camel, but she refused save he mount before her — so he mounted and she behind him till she arrived. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say she was the best of his daughters, afflicted for his sake. When that reached ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, he went to ʿUrwah asking what talk reached him wherein he diminished Fāṭimah's right. ʿUrwah swore he would not love all between east and west to diminish a right of Fāṭimah's, and that thereafter he would never narrate it at all. > **""Will you not set out and fetch Zaynab. Take my ring and give it to her. Ride before me upon his camel — rather ride you before me. She is the best of my daughters, afflicted for my sake""**"
"15232","Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, sought her husband Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ's permission when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ emigrated to go to him, and he permitted her. She arrived to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, then Abū al-ʿĀṣ followed her to Madīnah and sent to her to take him aman from her father. She thrust her head from her chamber door while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed Fajr with the people and announced: O people, I am Zaynab, and I have granted Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ protection. When the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ finished prayer he declared he had known naught thereof till he heard it, and that the lowest of Muslims grants protection over them. > **""O people, I am Zaynab, and I have granted Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ protection. O people, I knew naught thereof till I heard it now — and the lowest of Muslims grants protection over the Muslims""**"
"15233","Ibn Isḥāq stated that among the captives on Badr day was Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn ʿAbd Shams, son-in-law of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and husband of his daughter, among the counted men of Mecca in wealth and trust, bound to Hālah bint Khuwaylid, Khadījah being his maternal aunt. Khadījah had asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ to marry him to Zaynab, which he never opposed, before revelation, counting him as a son. When Allāh honoured His Prophet ﷺ with prophethood, Khadījah and his daughters believed, affirmed and testified his truth and followed his religion, while Abū al-ʿĀṣ remained upon polytheism. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had married one of his daughters — Ruqayyah or Umm Kulthūm — to ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab. When the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ openly confronted Quraysh with Allāh's command and they confronted him, they declared they had relieved Muḥammad of his care, so return his daughters and busy him with them. They went to Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ asking him to part his wife, wedding him to any woman he wished. He swore by Allāh he would not part his wife, nor love any Qurashī woman in her stead. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would praise him concerning kinship, as reached him. They went to the sinful ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab bidding him divorce Muḥammad's daughter, wedding him to any Qurashī woman; he agreed upon marriage to the daughter of Abān — or Saʿīd — ibn al-ʿĀṣ, so they wed him to the daughter of Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ and he parted her, the enemy of Allāh never having consummated with her, so Allāh removed her from his hand as honour to her and disgrace to him. ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān succeeded him thereafter. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ could neither permit nor forbid in Mecca, overpowered in his affair, and Islam had separated Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, from Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ, save the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ could not enforce separation. She remained with him upon her Islam while he remained upon polytheism until the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ emigrated to Madīnah with her resident in Mecca. When Quraysh marched to Badr, Abū al-ʿĀṣ marched with them and was struck among the captives on Badr day, held in Madīnah with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ."
"15234","Ibn Isḥāq reported from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, from his father ʿAbbād, from ʿĀʾishah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, that when the Meccans sent concerning ransom of their captives, Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, sent concerning ransom of Abū al-ʿĀṣ, sending therein a necklace of hers which Khadījah had brought her in with to Abū al-ʿĀṣ when he built with her. When the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw it he softened intensely and proposed that if they deemed fit they free her captive and return her wealth. They assented, freed him and returned her property. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had taken upon him his promise to free Zaynab's way to him, stipulated upon his release, undisclosed from him or the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. When Abū al-ʿĀṣ left to Mecca released, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and an Anṣārī man bidding them halt at Baṭn Yaʾjaj till Zaynab passed, then accompany and bring her — setting out a month or so after Badr. When Abū al-ʿĀṣ reached Mecca he ordered her to join her father, and she left openly. > **""If you deem fit to free her captive and return her wealth, do so. Halt at Baṭn Yaʾjaj till Zaynab passes you, then accompany her and bring her to me""**"
"15235","Ibn Isḥāq reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm that he was told Zaynab said that while she prepared in Mecca to join her father, Hind bint ʿUtbah met her offering any baggage easing her journey or reaching her father from her store, urging no restraint, for between women enters not what enters between men. Zaynab deemed her sincere but feared her and denied intending so, prepared, and when done her brother-in-law Kinānah ibn al-Rabīʿ, her husband's brother, brought a camel which she mounted, took his bow and quiver, and led her by day in her howdah. Quraysh talked thereof and went seeking till they overtook her at Dhū Ṭuwā, foremost Habbār ibn al-Aswad and Nāfiʿ ibn ʿAbd al-Qays al-Zuhrī. Habbār frightened her with a spear in her howdah — she being pregnant, as they claimed — till she fell and miscarried. Her brother-in-law alighted, scattered his arrows, and swore none should near him but he would plant an arrow in him. The people withdrew, and Abū Sufyān came with Qurashī elders bidding him withhold his arrows for speech. He withheld, and Abū Sufyān reproached him for leading a woman openly by day amid their calamity and setback from Muḥammad, lest folk deem their letting his daughter leave openly humiliation and weakness, though they had no need to withhold her from her father. He bade him return her till clamour calmed and folk said they had returned her, then slip her secretly to her father. He did so, she stayed nights, then when folk calmed he led her by night and delivered her to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and his companion, who brought her to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. Abū al-ʿĀṣ remained in Mecca, Zaynab with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, Islam having separated them, until shortly before the Conquest, when Abū al-ʿĀṣ left trading to Sham, trusted with wealth of his own and wealth of Qurashī men entrusted with him. Finishing trade and returning, a detachment of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ met him, seized what was with him, and he escaped them fleeing. When the detachment brought his wealth, Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ came by night, entered upon Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, sought her refuge and she granted it, come seeking his wealth. When the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ went out to Fajr — as Yazīd ibn Rūmān narrated — he takbīred and the people takbīred, and Zaynab came from the women's row announcing she had granted Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ refuge. When the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ gave salām he faced the people asking whether they had heard. They affirmed, and he swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul he had known naught till he heard it — the lowest grants protection over Muslims. Then he withdrew to his daughter bidding her honour his stay but never let him be alone with her, for she was not lawful to him. > **""O people, have you heard. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I knew naught thereof till I heard it — the lowest of Muslims grants protection over them. O my daughter, honour his stay, but let him never be alone with you, for you are not lawful to him. O people, I am Zaynab, and I have granted Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ refuge""**"
"15236","Ibn Isḥāq reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sent to the detachment that had seized the wealth of Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ that this man was of them as they knew, and they had seized wealth of his — if they did good and returned his property he would love it, and if they refused it was Allāh's fayʾ granted them and they had most right. They answered they would return it, and returned his wealth wholly, even a waterskin, a flask, even a cord, till naught was missing. He carried it to Mecca, repaid every Qurashī owner entrusted with him, asked whether aught remained with him, was thanked as chaste and noble, then testified there is no god but Allāh and Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger — swearing naught had barred his Islam with him but fear they would deem he meant to devour their wealth; now Allāh had delivered it and he submitted. He left till he arrived to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. > **""This man is of us as you know, and you have seized wealth of his — if you do good and return his property, we love it; and if you refuse, it is Allāh's fayʾ He granted you, and you have most right to it""**"
"15237","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported that a man set out with Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, when two Qurashī men pursued, fought and overcame him, thrust her so she fell upon a rock, miscarried and bled. They took her to Abū Sufyān, the women of Banī Hāshim came, and he delivered her to them. Thereafter she emigrated, remained ailing till she died of that ailment, and they held her a martyr."
"15238","Qatādah ibn Diʿāmah reported that Ruqayyah was with ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab, and when Allāh revealed: Perish the hands of Abū Lahab, the Prophet ﷺ asked ʿUtbah to divorce Ruqayyah, and Ruqayyah asked him likewise, so he divorced her. ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allāh be pleased with him, then married Ruqayyah, and she died with him."
"15239","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār stated that Ruqayyah, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, was with ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab, who parted her. ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān married Ruqayyah in Mecca, emigrated with her to Abyssinia, where she bore him ʿAbdullāh by whom he was kunyed. She arrived with him to Madīnah, and he stayed back from Badr over her by the Messenger's ﷺ permission, for whom the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ struck a share with the shares of Badr's men. Asked whether his reward too, he answered his reward likewise. > **""And your reward""**"
"15240","Al-Zuhrī stated that Ruqayyah died the day Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, freedman of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, arrived with the glad tidings of Badr."
"15241","Al-Zuhrī stated that ʿUthmān married Umm Kulthūm, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and she died with him, bearing him nothing."
"15242","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār stated that Umm Kulthūm, daughter of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, was with ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab whom the lion devoured, and he parted her. When Ruqayyah died with ʿUthmān, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married him to Umm Kulthūm, and she died with him bearing nothing. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ told him that had he ten he would have married them to him. > **""Had I ten I would have married them to you""**"
"15243","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allāh be pleased with them both, reported that Khadījah bore the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ six: ʿAbdullāh, al-Qāsim, Zaynab, Ruqayyah, Umm Kulthūm and Fāṭimah, and Māriyah the Copt bore him Ibrāhīm."
"15244","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār stated that al-Qāsim was born to the Prophet ﷺ, his eldest child, then Zaynab, then ʿAbdullāh called al-Ṭayyib and al-Ṭāhir, born after prophethood and dying small, then Umm Kulthūm, then Fāṭimah, then Ruqayyah — each the first in order. Al-Qāsim died in Mecca, then ʿAbdullāh."
"15245","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the Rock of Bayt al-Maqdis stands upon a palm, the palm upon a river of Paradise's rivers, and beneath the palm Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim, wife of Pharaoh, and Maryam daughter of ʿImrān string the jewels of Paradise's people till the Day of Resurrection. > **""The Rock of Bayt al-Maqdis stands upon a palm, the palm upon a river of Paradise's rivers, and beneath the palm Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim, wife of Pharaoh, and Maryam daughter of ʿImrān string the jewels of Paradise's people till the Day of Resurrection""**"
"15246","Abū Umāmah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say to ʿĀʾishah asking whether she knew that Allāh had married him in Paradise to Maryam bint ʿImrān, Kulthum sister of Mūsā, and the wife of Pharaoh. > **""Did you know that Allāh has married me in Paradise to Maryam bint ʿImrān, Kulthum sister of Mūsā, and the wife of Pharaoh""**"
"15247","Saʿd ibn Junādah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Allāh had married him in Paradise to Maryam bint ʿImrān, the wife of Pharaoh, and the sister of Mūsā. > **""Allāh has married me in Paradise to Maryam bint ʿImrān, the wife of Pharaoh, and the sister of Mūsā""**"
"15248","Abū Rawwād reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ entered upon Khadījah, may Allāh be pleased with her, in the illness wherein she died, and told her how unwelcome was what he saw of her, though Allāh may place much good in the unwelcome, asking whether she knew Allāh had married him with her in Paradise to Maryam bint ʿImrān, the wife of Pharaoh, and Kulthum sister of Mūsā. When she asked whether Allāh had done so, O Messenger of Allāh, and he affirmed, she answered with good cheer and sons. > **""How unwelcome to me is what I see of you, O Khadījah, though Allāh may place much good in the unwelcome. Did you not know that Allāh has married me with you in Paradise to Maryam bint ʿImrān, the wife of Pharaoh, and Kulthum sister of Mūsā. Yes. With good cheer and sons""**"
"15249","Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Pharaoh drove four pegs into his wife's hands and feet, and when they dispersed from her the angels shaded her. She prayed: My Lord, build for me with You a house in Paradise, save me from Pharaoh and his deed, and save me from the wrongdoing folk. Her house in Paradise was unveiled to her. > **""My Lord, build for me with You a house in Paradise, save me from Pharaoh and his deed, and save me from the wrongdoing folk""**"
"15250","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār stated that the mother of the Messenger's ﷺ sons and daughters except Ibrāhīm was Khadījah bint Khuwaylid — called in the Jāhiliyyah al-Ṭāhirah — daughter of Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy, her mother Fāṭimah bint Zāʾidah ibn Jundub al-Aṣamm ibn Ḥujr ibn ʿAbd Maʿīṣ ibn ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy, her mother Hālah bint ʿAbd Manāf ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Munqidh ibn ʿAmr ibn Maʿīṣ ibn ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy, her mother al-ʿAriqah named Qilābah bint Saʿd ibn Sahl ibn ʿAmr ibn Haṣīṣ ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy. Ḥibbān ibn ʿAbd Manāf, brother of Hālah by father and mother, was he who shot Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, may Allāh have mercy upon him, on Trench day, crying: Take it, I am the son of al-ʿAriqah — whereupon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed that Allāh sweat his face in the Fire. He struck Saʿd's medial vein — may Allāh have mercy upon Saʿd — and he died a martyr. Khadījah bint Khuwaylid was before the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ with ʿAtīq ibn ʿĀʾidh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Makhzūm, bearing him Hind ibn ʿAtīq. Then Abū Hālah Mālik ibn Nabbāsh ibn Zurārah ibn Waqdān ibn Ḥabīb ibn Salāmah ibn ʿAdī of Banī Asad ibn ʿAmr ibn Tamīm, ally of Banī ʿAbd al-Dār ibn Quṣayy, succeeded him, bearing him Hind and Hālah — Hind ibn ʿAtīq and Hind and Hālah sons of Abū Hālah — siblings of the Prophet's ﷺ children by Khadījah through their mother. > **""Allāh sweat your face in the Fire""**"
"15251","Ibn Shihāb stated that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married Khadījah in Mecca, his first wife married, previously with Abū Hālah al-Tamīmī. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married her at twenty-one, and she died seven years after his mission."
"15252","ʿUmar ibn Abī Bakr al-Muʾammalī reported that ʿAmr ibn Asad married Khadījah to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, who married her at twenty-five while Quraysh built the Kaʿbah."
"15253","Ibn Jurayj stated that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married at thirty-seven."
"15254","ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUrwah reported from his father that ʿAmr ibn Asad declared that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh proposed to Khadījah bint Khuwaylid — this stallion is not struck upon the nose."
"15255","Ibn Shihāb stated that Khadījah bint Khuwaylid was with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ before the Qurʾān descended upon him, then the Qurʾān descended while she was with him. She was the first to affirm the Prophet ﷺ and believe in him, dying in Mecca three years before the Prophet's ﷺ departure to Madīnah."
"15256","Mālik ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the first to submit among men was ʿAlī, and among women Khadījah."
"15257","Buraydah, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that Khadījah was the first to submit with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, along with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib."
"15258","Abū Rāfiʿ, may Allāh be pleased with him, reported that the first to submit among men was ʿAlī, and the first to submit among women was Khadījah."
"15259","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq stated that Khadījah was the daughter of Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy."
"15260","Al-Ṭabarānī stated that Khadījah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy was the first woman the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ married, mother of his sons and daughters male and female except Ibrāhīm, peace be upon him, who was from his concubine Māriyah the Copt."
"15261","Qatādah ibn Diʿāmah reported that Khadījah bint Khuwaylid died three years before the hijrah, the first to believe in the Prophet ﷺ among women and men. He married none in the Jāhiliyyah besides her, and none of the dowered wives bore him besides her."
"15262","Al-Zuhrī stated that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ never married over Khadījah till she died."
"15263","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that Khadījah, may Allah be pleased with her, died before the prayer was made obligatory."
"15264","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, as Ḥammād understood it, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned Khadījah, whose father was reluctant to marry her to him, so she prepared food and drink and invited her father and a group of Quraysh who ate and drank until intoxicated, when Khadījah asked her father to marry her to Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh, which he did, after which she perfumed him and clothed him with a robe as was customary, and when his intoxication lifted and he found himself perfumed and robed he protested that he would never marry the orphan of Abū Ṭālib, but Khadījah shamed him lest he disgrace himself before Quraysh by admitting drunkenness, until he accepted it. > **""Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh seeks my hand in marriage, so marry me to him.""**"
"15265","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, may Allah be pleased with him, whenever he heard people discuss the marriage of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to Khadījah would say he knew most about it, for he was his close companion in the Jāhiliyyah, and one day he went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they passed Khadījah's sister sitting in her leather tent, who called him while the Prophet ﷺ waited and asked whether his companion desired Khadījah, which he conveyed and the Prophet ﷺ affirmed, and next morning they found a cow slaughtered, Khadījah's father robed and canopied, her brother spoke to her father about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his station and request, he married her to him, they ate, her father slept then awoke asking about robe, canopy and food, his daughter told him his son-in-law Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh had clothed and gifted it at the wedding, he denied it and went to al-Ḥijr where Banū Hāshim came, demanding the man they claimed he had married, then declared it stood or now stood. > **""Yes, by my life.""**"
"15266","Jābir ibn Samurah, or another Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that the Prophet ﷺ grazed sheep then camels with a partner, and they were hired by Khadījah's sister, and when payment remained due his partner would go to claim it while Muḥammad declined from shyness, until the woman remarked she had never seen one more modest and chaste, and it entered Khadījah's mind, so she sent to him to propose to her father, he objected her father was wealthy and would refuse, she told him to meet him and she would manage, and to come in intoxication, which he did and the father married him, and in the morning when congratulated he denied it, went to her, she confirmed it and urged him not to disgrace his judgment praising Muḥammad, until he accepted, then she sent the Prophet ﷺ two ūqiyyahs of silver or gold to buy a robe and ram. > **""Come to my father and propose to me.""**"
"15267","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reported the first thing he knew of the affair of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was when he came to Makkah with his uncles and was directed to al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib sitting at Zamzam, when a man came from Bāb al-Ṣafā, fair tinged with redness, curly hair to his earlobes, high-bridged nose, bright teeth, dark eyes, thick beard, thin chest hair, fleshy hands and feet, in two white garments like the full moon, with a handsome beardless youth near puberty on his right and a veiled woman behind them, who headed to the Stone and touched it all three, circumambulated seven times, stood with takbīr, the youth right and woman behind, prolonged standing, bowed long, rose, prostrated with them following, which they found strange and asked Abū al-Faḍl if it was new, and he swore it was his nephew Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh, the youth ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, and Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, and none on earth worshipped Allah in this religion but those three."
"15268","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew four lines on the ground, asked whether they knew what it was, they said Allah and His Messenger knew best, and he named the best women of Paradise. > **""The best women of the people of Paradise are Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, Maryam daughter of ʿImrān, and Āsiyah daughter of Muzāḥim, wife of Pharaoh.""**"
"15269","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said four women of the worlds suffice. > **""Four of the women of the worlds suffice you: Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, Maryam bint ʿImrān, and Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim.""**"
"15270","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Khadījah was favored over the women of his Ummah as Maryam was favored over the women of the worlds. > **""Khadījah has been favored over the women of my Ummah as Maryam was favored over the women of the worlds.""**"
"15271","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the mistresses of the women of Paradise. > **""The mistresses of the women of Paradise are Maryam daughter of ʿImrān, then Fāṭimah daughter of Muḥammad, then Khadījah, then Āsiyah, wife of Pharaoh.""**"
"15272","ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was commanded to give Khadījah glad tidings of a house of hollow pearl with neither noise nor toil. > **""I was commanded to give Khadījah glad tidings of a house of qaṣab, in which there is neither ṣakhab nor naṣab.""**"
"15273","Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, asked the Prophet ﷺ where their mother Khadījah was, and he told her of a house of qaṣab with neither idle talk nor toil between Maryam and Āsiyah, and when she asked of this reed he clarified it was strung pearl, ruby and jewels. > **""In a house of qaṣab, with neither laghw nor naṣab, between Maryam and Āsiyah.""**"
"15274","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about Khadījah who died before obligations and rulings were revealed, and he said he saw her by a river of Paradise in a house of qaṣab with neither idle talk nor toil, and asked about Abū Ṭālib whether he benefited him, he said he brought him from Hell to its shallowest part. > **""I saw her upon a river of Paradise, in a house of qaṣab with neither laghw nor naṣab.""**"
"15275","Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Khadījah glad tidings of a house in Paradise of qaṣab with neither noise nor toil. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Khadījah glad tidings of a house in Paradise of qaṣab, with neither ṣakhab nor naṣab.""**"
"15276","Jābir ibn Riʾāb, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Prophet ﷺ told Khadījah that Jibrīl, peace be upon him, came to him commanding him to give her tidings. > **""Give Khadījah glad tidings of a house of qaṣab, with neither ṣakhab nor naṣab.""**"
"15277","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat with Khadījah, Jibrīl came addressing Muḥammad and commanding him to convey salām and tidings of a pearl house with neither harm nor toil. > **""O Muḥammad, convey salām to Khadījah, and give her tidings in Paradise of a house of qaṣab, with neither harm nor toil.""**"
"15278","Ibn Abī Awfā, may Allah be pleased with him, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Jibrīl told him to give Khadījah tidings of a pearl house in Paradise with neither noise nor toil. > **""Give Khadījah tidings of a house in Paradise of qaṣab, with neither ṣakhab nor naṣab.""**"
"15279","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would mention Khadījah often, so she objected that Allah had replaced an old red-lipped woman long dead, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angrier than she had ever seen and declared Allah granted her from him what He granted none of them, so she begged pardon and swore never to mention Khadījah with dislike again. > **""Allah granted her from me what He granted none of you.""**"
"15280","In another narration, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whenever he mentioned Khadījah never tired of praising her and seeking forgiveness for her, declaring she was granted children from him while they were deprived. > **""She was granted children from me while you were deprived of them from me.""**"
"15281","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he mentioned Khadījah would praise her beautifully, until one day jealousy seized her and she objected that he mentioned the red-lipped one so much when Allah had given him better, whereupon he asked whether Allah had given him better than her, for she believed when people disbelieved, affirmed him when people denied, supported him with her wealth when people withheld, and Allah granted him her children and withheld the children of others. > **""She believed in me when people disbelieved in me, affirmed me when people denied me, supported me with her wealth when people withheld from me, and Allah granted me her children and withheld the children of others.""**"
"15282","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā reported that Jibrīl was with the Prophet ﷺ when Khadījah came, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said O Jibrīl, this is Khadījah, and Jibrīl عليه السلام told him to convey to her salām from Allah and from himself. > **""Convey to her salām from Allah and from me.""**"
"15283","Saʿīd ibn Kathīr reported that Jibrīl عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ at Ḥirāʾ and told him Khadījah was coming with ḥays in her leather vessel, so tell her Allah conveys salām, and when she came he told her Jibrīl had informed him of her and the ḥays before she arrived and that Allah conveyed salām to her. > **""Allah conveys salām to you.""**"
"15284","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fed Khadījah from the grapes of Paradise."
"15285","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that when Khadījah died, Khawlah bint Ḥakīm ibn al-Awqaṣ, wife of ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn, said at Makkah O Messenger of Allah, will you not marry, and when he asked whom she offered a virgin or a thayyib, naming the virgin as the daughter of the most beloved of Allah's creation to him, ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr, and the thayyib as Sawdah bint Zamʿah who believed in him and followed him, and he told her to go mention him to them, so she entered Abū Bakr's house, found Umm Rūmān, conveyed the glad tidings that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent her to propose ʿĀʾishah, she welcomed it and told her to await Abū Bakr, who objected that she was his brother's daughter, and when this was conveyed the Prophet ﷺ told her to return and say he was his brother in Islam yet his daughter was lawful for him, whereupon Abū Bakr asked her to call the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who came and he married her to him. > **""You are my brother in Islam, and I am your brother, and your daughter is lawful for me.""**"
"15286","Abū Salamah and Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥāṭib reported that when Khadījah died, Khawlah bint Ḥakīm, wife of ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn, asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether he would not marry and offered a virgin, ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr, or a thayyib, Sawdah daughter of Zamʿah who believed and followed his words, and he told her to mention him to them, so she proposed ʿĀʾishah to Umm Rūmān and awaited Abū Bakr who objected she was his brother's daughter, and the Prophet ﷺ sent her back to say he was his brother in Islam yet lawful for her daughter, while Umm Rūmān explained Muṭʿim ibn ʿAdī had mentioned her for his son and Abū Bakr never broke a promise, so Abū Bakr went to Muṭʿim whose wife feared he would turn their son from religion, Muṭʿim agreed with her, Allah removed the engagement from his mind, he told Khawlah to call the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who came and he married her to him when ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها was six, then Khawlah went to Sawdah bint Zamʿah who welcomed it and sent her to her aged father who had missed Ḥajj, greeted him with Jāhiliyyah greeting, told him Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh proposed Sawdah as a noble match, he asked his daughter who agreed and told him to call him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and he married her to him, while her brother ʿAbd ibn Zamʿah returned from Ḥajj throwing dust on his head and later after Islam called himself foolish for it, and ʿĀʾishah added they came to Madīnah at Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj at al-Sunḥ, her mother took her from a swing between two branches with her hair parted and face wiped, led her breathless to the door then to the Prophet ﷺ sitting on a bed with Anṣār men and women, seated her saying these are your family with blessings, they left and the Prophet ﷺ consummated with her in their house with no camel or sheep slaughtered until Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah sent a bowl he sent to the Prophet ﷺ among his wives, when she was nine. > **""I am your brother and you are my brother in Islam, and your daughter is lawful for me.""**"
"15287","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not marry her until Jibrīl brought her image and declared her his wife, and he married her when she was a young girl in light dress, after which Allah cast modesty upon her. > **""This is your wife.""**"
"15288","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr reported that when Khadījah died it weighed so heavily upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that concern was felt for him until he married ʿĀʾishah."
"15289","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emigrated he left her and his daughters behind, and when settled in Madīnah he sent Zayd ibn Ḥārithah with his freedman Abū Rāfiʿ, giving them two camels and five hundred dirhams taken from Abū Bakr to buy mounts, while Abū Bakr sent with them ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Urayqiṭ al-Dīlī with two or three camels and wrote to ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr to bring Umm Rūmān, Abū Bakr's mother, ʿĀʾishah, her brother, and Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr wife of al-Zubayr, so they set out together to Qudayd where Zayd bought three camels, entered Makkah together, met Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh intending emigration, all left together, Zayd and Abū Rāfiʿ bringing Fāṭimah, Umm Kulthūm, and Sawdah bint Zamʿah, Zayd carrying Umm Ayman with her children Ayman and Usāmah, until at al-Bayḍ of Namir her camel bolted while she was in a litter with her mother crying for daughter and bride, until overtaken descending Thaniyyat Habshā, Allah delivering them to Madīnah where she stayed with Abū Bakr's family, the Prophet ﷺ visiting while building the mosque with houses around it lodging his family, Abū Bakr asking what prevented consummation, the Prophet ﷺ citing dower, Abū Bakr giving twelve ūqiyyahs and a nash which was sent, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ consummating with her in the very house she remained in where he died and was buried, lodging Sawdah in another house, noting he had married her while she played with girls and only realized it when her mother confined her in the house and herself informed her. > **""What prevents you from consummating with your family?""**"
"15290","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that they came as emigrants through a difficult pass when her camel bolted violently, and she never forgot her mother's cry of Yā ʿuraybisah as it tossed its head, until she heard a caller say throw its rein, which she did, whereupon it stood turning as though a man stood beneath it. > **""Throw its rein.""**"
"15291","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out and when they were at al-Ḥadd they turned with her upon a camel, her last contact with them hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cry Wa ʿarūsāh, and while so a caller told her to throw the rein, which she did, and Allah held it in His hand through that night journey. > **""Wa ʿarūsāh.""**"
"15292","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ viewed ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها adorned among her family before consummating with her."
"15293","Ibn Shihāb reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr in Shawwāl and consummated with her in Shawwāl in Madīnah six months after his emigration, and ʿĀʾishah died in Madīnah with seventeen nights passed of Ramaḍān after al-Witr in year fifty-eight and was buried that night."
"15294","Nāfiʿ and other people of knowledge reported they prayed over ʿĀʾishah and Umm Salamah, wives of the Prophet ﷺ, in the midst of al-Baqīʿ, the imām when they prayed over ʿĀʾishah being Abū Hurayrah with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar present, and those who entered her grave were ʿAbdullāh and ʿUrwah sons of Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, and she died in year fifty-eight in Ramaḍān with seventeen passed and was buried that night."
"15295","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that Umm Misṭaḥ came to her and she went out for her need to a ḥushsh when Umm Misṭaḥ stepped upon a bone or thorn and said may Misṭaḥ perish, so she rebuked her for reviling a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon Umm Misṭaḥ testified she was among the heedless believing women and asked whether she knew what had spread against her, and when she swore she did not, asked when her last contact with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was, she answered he did as he pleased among his wives, so Umm Misṭaḥ told her such and such had spread, she fell unconscious, word reached her mother Umm Rūmān who carried her to her house, word reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that ʿĀʾishah had learned the news so he came, sat with her and said O ʿĀʾishah Allah has widened repentance, which worsened her state, then Abū Bakr entered saying O Messenger of Allah why regard one who betrayed you and disgraced me, which worsened her further, then he sent for ʿAlī asking his view, who answered Allah and His Messenger knew best, pressed him he said Allah widened women but ask Barīrah her servant who may have seen something, so he sent for Barīrah, made her testify he was Allah's Messenger and charge her not to conceal, she swore to tell all, asked if she ever saw anything blameworthy in ʿĀʾishah, she swore by Prophethood she saw only one trait, that she kneaded dough, asked ʿĀʾishah to guard it while she fetched fire to bake, ʿĀʾishah prayed and neglected it until a sheep ate it, then he sent for Usāmah asking his view, who answered Allah and His Messenger knew best, pressed him he advised silence until Allah revealed, and after a short while revelation descended and signs showed in the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announcing glad tidings that Allah had cleared her, to which she replied without his or his companion's praise, and thereupon she spoke. > **""Rejoice O ʿĀʾishah, rejoice O ʿĀʾishah, Allah has brought you your clearance.""**"
"15296","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when intending travel cast lots among his wives, and the lot fell upon ʿĀʾishah in the campaign of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq, and in the depth of night she went for her need when her necklace loosened and she sought it, while Misṭaḥ an orphan under Abū Bakr's care remained with his household, and when she returned she saw no army, while Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal al-Sulamī who lagged behind to collect fallen cups, bags and water-skins saw her, covered his face from her, brought his camel near, reached the army with her, people spoke about it, and the narrator continued the account till its end, noting the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would come stand at the door asking how she was until one day he announced glad tidings that Allah had revealed her clearance, she replied by praise of Allah not of him, Allah revealed ten verses beginning with those who brought the slander, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ imposed the ḥadd upon Misṭaḥ, Ḥamnah and Ḥassān. > **""Rejoice O ʿĀʾishah, Allah has revealed your clearance.""**"
"15297","Al-Aswad reported he asked ʿĀʾishah, O Mother of the Believers, would she narrate how the affair of the slander was, and she said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her at Makkah while she waded the rain with nothing men desire, a girl of six, and when it reached her that he had married her Allah cast modesty upon her, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emigrated with her and she was taken to him at nine, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ journeyed with her, she light in her curtained litter which they lifted with her inside and bound upon the camel, they halted and she went for her need, returned after the call to depart, entered the litter after they had seen her stir the curtains, sat and struck her chest finding she had forgotten her necklace of onyx, rushed to seek it, returned to find the people gone with only distant dust, the litter placed upon the camel supposing her inside due to her lightness, when a man holding his camel's head asked who it was, he said Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal, asked if she was the Mother of the Believers, she said yes, he said to Allah we belong and return, she told him to turn his face and place his foot upon the camel's foreleg, which he gladly did, she caught the people at their halt, he placed her at the litter, people looked without her perceiving, she noticed coldness from her parents and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without knowing what was said until her servant informed her and a Muhājir man called her heedless, shivering fever seized her, her mother heaped every garment in the house upon her, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ consulted his Companions asking their view, some said there are many women and replacement possible, others affirmed messengership, and the account continued."
"15298","ʿĀʾishah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that the Prophet ﷺ when intending travel cast lots among his wives and whichever lot emerged he took her, and ʿĀʾishah's lot emerged in his campaign against Banū al-Muṣṭaliq of Khuzāʿah, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned back near Madīnah, ʿĀʾishah then a young light girl of little flesh keeping her veil, when people prepared to depart she went to relieve herself, returned and entered her litter which was saddled and lifted upon her camel, and the hypocrites and others who joined in ʿĀʾishah's affair first said she had gone to relieve herself near Madīnah when her necklace of onyx beads slipped from her neck, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and people departed while she sought the necklace unaware of their departure, they bound her litter upon her camel supposing her inside as usual, she returned to the camp finding no one and was overcome by sleep, while Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal al-Sulamī, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, had lagged that night till morning, passed by her, saw her, uttered istirjāʿ and was awed at her solitude, having known her before the veil, asked her affair, she veiled her face with her jilbāb and informed him, he brought his camel, stepped upon its foreleg, turned his back till she mounted and arranged her clothes, then drove it walking with her till they entered Madīnah at midday or near it, whereupon the people of slander spoke about her and him while she knew nothing, being ill those nights, first noticing from the Prophet ﷺ that whereas he used to visit her in sickness he now neither entered nor visited but only said passing how is she and asked her household, until he learned people talked much and grieved, she had complained to her mother of his estrangement and her mother told her to be patient that a beautiful beloved wife with co-wives is envied, then on the night after whose morning the Prophet ﷺ consulted ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and Usāmah ibn Zayd she needed to relieve herself, there being no privies in houses and going outside at night by Arab custom, she asked Umm Misṭaḥ to take a water vessel to al-Manāṣiʿ, that woman and her son Misṭaḥ being kin of Abū Bakr maintained by him and dwelling with him, she stumbled and cursed Misṭaḥ, ʿĀʾishah rebuked her, she stumbled again and repeated it, ʿĀʾishah rebuked her for a Badr Companion, she asked if she was heedless of what circulated that Misṭaḥ and others with hypocrites misled by Satan gathered at ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl of Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj speaking about her and Ṣafwān, her need vanished and she returned home, next morning the Prophet ﷺ summoned ʿAlī and Usāmah, informed them of the talk and consulted them, Usāmah swore they knew only good of his wife, ʿAlī said there are many women but ask the servant meaning Barīrah, the Prophet ﷺ agreed, ʿAlī asked her and she praised her saying only that she was a young girl whose dough a domestic goat ate, when the Prophet ﷺ heard it he addressed the people asking O group of Muslims who would excuse him from the man who harmed him concerning his wife of whom he knew only good and who slandered a Companion of blameless record always with him on journeys, Saʿd ibn Muʿādh al-Anṣārī of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal offered to deal with him if of Aws or to obey command if of Khazraj, Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah al-Khazrajī accused him of falsehood, Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr al-Ashhalī rose and factions disputed till it escalated, the Prophet ﷺ came to her house, summoned her parents, praised Allah as due then told her O ʿĀʾishah she was among daughters of Adam and if she erred to repent and seek forgiveness, she asked her father to answer for her but he said he could not for he was Allah's Prophet receiving revelation, asked her mother likewise who said the same, so she swore if she confessed falsely they would believe and if she declared innocence while Allah knew it they would call her liar, recalling only the words of Yūsuf's father that patience was best and Allah's help sought for what they alleged, her grief making her forget Yaʿqūb's name, whereupon the Prophetic state of revelation seized him, when lifted he wiped his face and announced glad tidings that Allah had revealed her innocence, she had expected no Qurʾān but only a dream vindication, her parents told her to kiss the Prophet's head but she refused praising only Allah, and the narrator continued that Abū Bakr who maintained Misṭaḥ and his mother swore to spend no more, then when the Prophet ﷺ recited that they pardon and overlook and love Allah's forgiveness, Abū Bakr wept, affirmed his Lord, resumed spending, Ṣafwān once ambushed Ḥassān with a sword striking him and versifying about guarding the innocent and chaste against the liar, Ḥassān cried for help, people came, Ṣafwān fled, Ḥassān sought redress from the Prophet ﷺ who asked him to gift the blow, which he did, so the Prophet ﷺ compensated him with a large date garden and a Roman or Coptic slave-girl Sīrīn who bore his poet son ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Abū Uways transmitted via Ḥusayn ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUbaydillāh ibn ʿAbbās from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās that Ḥassān later sold the garden to Muʿāwiyah for great wealth, that the painful torment verse concerned ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl of Banū al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, with verses by Abū Bakr against Misṭaḥ called ʿAwf, by Umm Saʿd ibn Muʿādh testifying to the chastity of Abū Bakr's daughter, and by Ḥassān exonerating and apologizing to ʿĀʾishah as chaste, dignified, guarded from backbiting, beloved of the best of mankind in religion and rank, noble of Luʾayy ibn Ghālib whose glory never fades, refined whom Allah made wholesome and pure, swearing if he ever repeated slander his fingers would not raise his voice, for the charge never clung to her but was the word of an undaunted liar, while his lifelong love and support belonged to the Household of the Messenger of Allah, adornment of gatherings, whose lofty rank outstrips the ambitious, and Abū Uways added his father told him the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered those who cast at ʿĀʾishah flogged the ḥadd eighty each, whereupon Ḥassān versified that ʿAbdullāh, Ḥamnah and Misṭaḥ tasted what they deserved for blind conjecture against their Prophet's wife incurring wrath of the Noble Throne, harming the Messenger and spreading shameful disgraces exposed. > **""Rejoice O ʿĀʾishah, Allah has revealed your innocence.""**"
"15299","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ when traveling took some of his wives casting lots between them, and traveled with ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr رضي الله عنها who had a hawdaj they lifted and set down, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions halted for the night while ʿĀʾishah went far for her need unnoticed, and when the Prophet ﷺ awoke the people had departed and the bearers lifted the hawdaj supposing her inside and marched, so she returned to find them gone and sat in place, when an Anṣārī man named Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal who kept from women approached with his camel, having seen her before the veil, mounted her and led the camel till they reached the people and the Prophet ﷺ, and people spoke much till it reached the Prophet ﷺ and weighed upon him so he withdrew from her and consulted Zayd ibn Thābit and others, who said leave her that Allah may reveal something concerning her, while ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib said there are many women, so the Prophet ﷺ was hard upon her, and one night ʿĀʾishah walked among women when Umm Misṭaḥ stumbled and cursed Misṭaḥ, ʿĀʾishah rebuked her for a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, she replied she knew not what they said and informed her of the news, so ʿĀʾishah fell unconscious, then the Qurʾān descended in Sūrat al-Nūr from those who brought the slander till the one who took most of it and till do not swear the possessors of bounty to Allah is Forgiving Merciful, while Abū Bakr who used to give Misṭaḥ, maintain and join him though he had spoken most against ʿĀʾishah, swore to give him nothing, so the verse do you not love Allah to forgive you descended, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to come give her glad tidings, so Abū Bakr came informing her of her clearance and what Allah revealed, and she replied not by his praise nor his companion's. > **""Leave her, perhaps Allah will reveal something concerning her for you.""**"
"15300","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when intending travel cast lots thrice among his wives and took whomever the lot struck, who would go out giving water and treating the wounded, and when he campaigned against Banū al-Muṣṭaliq he cast lots and it struck ʿĀʾishah and Umm Salamah, so he took them, and on the road Umm Salamah's saddle tilted so they couched her camel to mend it, while ʿĀʾishah needing relief thought to relieve herself till they mended it, so she descended from the hawdaj taking the bucket unnoticed, reached a hollow place, her necklace broke and she busied herself retrieving and stringing it, the people drove their camels supposing her in the hawdaj without descending, she saw no one, followed till exhausted, judged they would miss and return seeking her, slept on the road, when Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal passed, companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whom he had asked to place over the rear guard, who when people departed prayed then followed carrying whatever fell till he brought it to its owners, and when he passed he thought her a man saying O sleeper rise for the people have gone, she replied she was no man but ʿĀʾishah, he said to Allah we belong and return, couched his camel hobbling its forelegs, turned away telling her O mother rise and mount and inform him when mounted, she mounted, he loosed the hobble, drove the camel by its rein, and Ibn ʿUmar said he spoke not a word to her till he brought her to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl swore by the Kaʿbah she had sinned with him, aided by Ḥassān ibn Thābit, Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah and Ḥamnah, it spread through the army and reached the Prophet ﷺ weighing in his heart till they returned to Madīnah where the hypocrite spread it and it weighed heavily upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then one day Umm Misṭaḥ entered seeing ʿĀʾishah about to go out, carried the water bucket which fell from her, she cursed Misṭaḥ, ʿĀʾishah exclaimed glory of Allah at cursing a Badr Companion and her own son, Umm Misṭaḥ replied the flood had carried her unaware and informed her, fever seized her and what was in her contracted and she went no farther, perceiving coldness from the Prophet ﷺ whose cause she had not known till Umm Misṭaḥ told her, so she asked the Prophet ﷺ permission to go to her family, he permitted, she went to her father Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه who asked what was wrong, she said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had expelled her from his house, he asked whether the Messenger expelled her and he should shelter her, swearing he would not till the Messenger commanded, which he did, then Abū Bakr swore such was never said to them in Jāhiliyyah so how after Islam honored them, and ʿĀʾishah, her mother Umm Rūmān, Abū Bakr, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and the household wept, it reached the Prophet ﷺ who ascended the minbar, praised Allah then asked O people who would excuse him from one harming him, Saʿd ibn Muʿādh drew his sword offering to protect him, to bring the head if of Aws or to obey command if of Khazraj, Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah accused him of lying and inability, claiming he sought old Jāhiliyyah blood, each rallying O Aws and O Khazraj till they clashed with sandals and stones and slaps, Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr rose asking why dispute when the Messenger commanded obedience despite noses, Jibrīl عليه السلام descended while he was on the minbar, Abū ʿUbaydah ascended embracing him, when lifted he signaled all the people then recited what Jibrīl brought down to if two parties of believers fight make peace till its end, the people shouted acceptance of what Allah revealed, stood reconciling and embracing, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended awaiting revelation concerning ʿĀʾishah, sent for ʿAlī, Usāmah and Barīrah, consulting none beyond ʿAlī and Usāmah after the death of his father Zayd concerning his household, asking ʿAlī what he said of ʿĀʾishah weighed by people's words, who said divorce was lawful for him, asking Usāmah who declared glory of Allah unlawful to utter, glory to You this is great slander, asking Barīrah O Barīrah what she said, who swore by Allah she knew only good except that she was a sleepy woman who slept till the goat ate her dough, and if anything existed Allah would inform him, then the Prophet ﷺ went to Abū Bakr's house, entered telling her O ʿĀʾishah if she had done the deed to say so that he seek Allah's forgiveness for her, she swore never to seek Allah's forgiveness for it, if she had done it may He not forgive, finding no parable for them but the father of Yūsuf, Yaʿqūb's name gone from grief, I only complain my suffering and grief to Allah and I know from Allah what you know not, and while he spoke Jibrīl عليه السلام descended with revelation, tremor seized the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr told ʿĀʾishah to embrace the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, she swore never to approach, Abū Bakr embraced him, when lifted he smiled announcing O ʿĀʾishah Allah had revealed her clearance, she replied by praise of Allah not his, he recited Sūrat al-Nūr to her story, clearance and innocence, told her to rise to the house, went to the mosque commanding Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ to gather the people, recited what Allah revealed of ʿĀʾishah's innocence, descended, sent for the hypocrite ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl and struck him two ḥadds, sent for Ḥassān ibn Thābit, Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah and Ḥamnah bint Jaḥsh striking them painfully about the necks, Ibn ʿUmar explaining two ḥadds because slandering the Prophet's wives incurred double, then Abū Bakr sent for Misṭaḥ ibn Uthāthah his sister's son asking what carried him to speak of ʿĀʾishah when Ḥassān was an Anṣārī not of his folk, Ḥamnah a weak mindless woman, Ibn Ubayy a hypocrite, while he had been in his care since his father's death at four, spending and clothing him till grown never cutting till that day, swearing never to join him with dirhams nor favor with good, expelling him from his house, so the Qurʾān descended let not the possessors of bounty swear till its verse, and when do you not love Allah to forgive you Abū Bakr wept declaring since the Qurʾān descended concerning his command he would double spending, forgave him as Allah commanded, while the wife of ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy was a hypocrite with him, so the Qurʾān descended the evil women for the evil men meaning his wife for ʿAbdullāh, and the evil men for evil women meaning ʿAbdullāh for his wife, and the good women for the good men meaning ʿĀʾishah and the Prophet's wives, and the good men meaning the Prophet ﷺ for the good women meaning ʿĀʾishah and the Prophet's wives, such are cleared, to the end of the verses. > **""Rejoice O ʿĀʾishah, Allah has revealed your clearance.""**"
"15301","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that when she was cast at with what she was cast at she intended to throw herself into a well."
"15302","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that when her clearance descended Abū Bakr kissed her head, so she asked why he had not cleared her, and he replied what sky would shade him and what earth bear him if he said what he knew not."
"15303","Zaynab bint Jaḥsh رضي الله عنها reported that she, ʿĀʾishah and Zaynab boasted, Zaynab saying she was the one Allah married from heaven, ʿĀʾishah saying she was the one whose clearance descended from heaven when Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal carried her, Zaynab asking what she said when she mounted, ʿĀʾishah answering Allah suffices me and He is the best Disposer, Zaynab affirming she spoke the word of the believers. > **""Allah suffices me and He is the best Disposer.""**"
"15304","Muḥammad ibn Jaḥsh reported that ʿĀʾishah and Zaynab boasted, Zaynab saying she was the one Allah married from heaven, ʿĀʾishah saying she was the one whose clearance descended when Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal carried her, Zaynab asking what she said when she mounted, ʿĀʾishah answering Allah suffices me and He is the best Disposer, Zaynab affirming she spoke the word of the believers. > **""Allah suffices me and He is the best Disposer.""**"
"15305","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when Resurrection Day comes Allah will impose the ḥadd upon those who reviled ʿĀʾishah eighty eighty before all creation, his Lord will grant him the Muhājirūn among them, then he would consult her O ʿĀʾishah, and ʿĀʾishah hearing the words wept in the house swearing by Him who sent him with truth that his joy was dearer to her than her own, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled laughing and declared her her father's daughter. > **""You are your father's daughter.""**"
"15306","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told her he was to her as Abū Zarʿ was to Umm Zarʿ except that Abū Zarʿ divorced and he would never divorce. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, I was to you as Abū Zarʿ was to Umm Zarʿ, except that Abū Zarʿ divorced and I do not divorce.""**"
"15307","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported she was given nine never given a woman except Maryam daughter of ʿImrān: Jibrīl descended with her image in his palm till the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was commanded to marry her, he married her a virgin and married no virgin besides her, he passed with his head in her lap, she buried him in her house, angels surrounded her house, revelation descended while he was among his household and they dispersed from him yet descended upon him while she was with him in his blanket, she was the daughter of his caliph and ṣiddīq, her clearance descended from heaven, she was left pure with one pure, and promised forgiveness and noble provision."
"15308","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported seven traits in her never in any women except what Allah gave Maryam daughter of ʿImrān, swearing she said it not boasting over her companions, and when ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣafwān asked what they were, Mother of the Believers, she said the angel descended with her image, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her at seven, she was conducted to him at nine, he married her a virgin none sharing her among people, revelation came to him while she and he were in one blanket, she was the most beloved people to him and daughter of the most beloved people to him, verses of the Qurʾān descended concerning her, the Ummah almost perished concerning her, she saw Jibrīl whom none of his wives saw besides her, he passed in her house attended by none but her, and the angel strengthened."
"15309","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported from the Prophet ﷺ that she asked O Messenger of Allah who is most beloved people to him, he asked why, she said to love what he loved, and he named ʿĀʾishah. > **""ʿĀʾishah.""**"
"15310","ʿAbdullāh ibn Shaqīq reported he asked ʿĀʾishah which women were most beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, she said ʿĀʾishah, and which men, she said her father."
"15311","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her while she wept, asking what made her weep, she said Fāṭimah reviled her, so he summoned Fāṭimah asking whether she had reviled ʿĀʾishah, she affirmed O Messenger of Allah, he asked whether she loved whom he loved, she affirmed, whether she hated whom he hated, she affirmed, declaring he loved ʿĀʾishah so she should love her, whereupon Fāṭimah vowed never to say to ʿĀʾishah anything harming her ever. > **""I love ʿĀʾishah, so love her.""**"
"15312","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported she was given seven never given the Prophet's wives: she was among the most beloved people to him in person and most beloved in father, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her and married no virgin besides her, Jibrīl عليه السلام descended upon him with revelation while she was with him in a blanket never done with another, and she had two days and two nights while his wives had a day and night."
"15313","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that on the day ʿĀʾishah died she said today the most beloved person in the world to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, then added seeking Allah's forgiveness except her father."
"15314","ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith ibn al-Muṣṭaliq reported that Ziyād sent wealth to the Prophet's wives preferring ʿĀʾishah, the messenger kept apologizing to Umm Salamah, who declared Ziyād apologized to them when one greater in favoring her than Ziyād had favored her, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15315","ʿUrwah reported he told ʿĀʾishah he reflected upon her affair in amazement, finding her among the most learned people and saying what prevented her being the wife of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and daughter of Abū Bakr, finding her learned in Arab days, genealogies and poetry and saying what prevented her when her father was the scholar of Quraysh, but marveling to find her learned in medicine and asking whence, so she took his hand saying O ʿUrayyah the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had many illnesses and Arab and non-Arab physicians were dispatched to him, so she learned that."
"15316","Masrūq reported when asked whether ʿĀʾishah knew the inheritance laws well, he swore by his soul he saw the elders of Muḥammad's Companions ﷺ ask her about them."
"15317","ʿUrwah reported he never saw a woman more learned in medicine, fiqh or poetry than ʿĀʾishah."
"15318","Al-Zuhrī reported that the Prophet ﷺ said if the knowledge of the women of this Ummah including the Prophet's wives were gathered, ʿĀʾishah's knowledge would exceed theirs. > **""If the knowledge of the women of this Ummah were gathered, including the wives of the Prophet, ʿĀʾishah's knowledge would exceed theirs.""**"
"15319","Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه reported swearing by Allah he never saw an orator more eloquent, fluent or sagacious than ʿĀʾishah."
"15320","Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah reported he never saw anyone more eloquent than ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها."
"15321","Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه reported he never feared speech before anyone as he feared it before ʿĀʾishah, and never heard her speech but recalled the speech of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15322","ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī reported a man said all Mothers of the Believers were dearer to him than ʿĀʾishah, so he told him he had opposed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for she was the most beloved of them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15323","Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها reported she entered upon ʿĀʾishah asking where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was, she said in the house revelation was coming to him, she waited as Allah willed, then heard the Prophet ﷺ afterwards tell her O ʿĀʾishah this is Jibrīl عليه السلام conveying salām to her. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, this is Jibrīl conveying salām to you.""**"
"15324","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the excellence of ʿĀʾishah over women was as the excellence of tharīd over all food. > **""The excellence of ʿĀʾishah over women is as the excellence of tharīd over all food.""**"
"15325","Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd from Saʿd, if Allah wills, from the Prophet ﷺ reported that ʿĀʾishah was favored over women as tharīd was favored over all food. > **""ʿĀʾishah is favored over women as tharīd is favored over all food.""**"
"15326","Qurrah ibn Iyās رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the excellence of ʿĀʾishah over women was as the excellence of tharīd over all food. > **""The excellence of ʿĀʾishah over women is as the excellence of tharīd over all food.""**"
"15327","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that when she saw good mood in the Prophet ﷺ she asked O Messenger of Allah supplicate Allah for her, so he prayed O Allah forgive ʿĀʾishah her past and future, secret and open sins, and ʿĀʾishah laughed till her head fell in her lap from laughter, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether his supplication pleased her, she asked why his supplication would not please her, and he swore by Allah it was his supplication for his Ummah in every prayer. > **""O Allah, forgive ʿĀʾishah her past and future sins, her secret and her open.""**"
"15328","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported he told her she was only named Mother of the Believers to be blessed, and it was her name before she was born. > **""You were only named Mother of the Believers to be blessed, and it is your name before you were born.""**"
"15329","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār reported that the children of ʿUmar were ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar and his full sister, and their mother Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar رضي الله عنها wife of the Prophet ﷺ, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān the elder, and their mother was Zaynab bint Maẓʿūn ibn Ḥabīb ibn Wahb ibn Ḥudhāfah ibn Jumaḥ among the emigrant women, before the Prophet ﷺ she was with Khunays ibn Ḥudhāfah al-Sahmī, her father witnessed Badr, her uncle Zayd ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, her maternal uncles ʿUthmān, Qudāmah and ʿAbdullāh, and her cousin al-Sāʾib ibn ʿUthmān."
"15330","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that ʿUmar entered upon Ḥafṣah weeping asking what made her weep, perhaps the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had divorced her, for the Prophet ﷺ had divorced and reviewed her for his sake, swearing if he had divorced her he would never speak a word to her ever."
"15331","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ divorced Ḥafṣah, word reached ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb who placed dust upon his head declaring what Allah cared for him O son of al-Khaṭṭāb after her, so Jibrīl عليه السلام descended upon the Prophet ﷺ commanding him by Allah to review Ḥafṣah as mercy for ʿUmar. > **""Allah commands you to take back Ḥafṣah as mercy for ʿUmar.""**"
"15332","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divorced Ḥafṣah, Jibrīl came telling him to take back Ḥafṣah for she was fasting, praying by night, and his wife in Paradise, while al-Ṭabarānī's wording reported the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to divorce her when Jibrīl عليه السلام came telling him not to divorce her for the same reason. > **""Take back Ḥafṣah, for she is fasting, praying by night, and she is your wife in Paradise.""**"
"15333","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ divorced Ḥafṣah and people grieved, her maternal uncle ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn and her brother Qudāmah entered upon her grieving, while with her the Prophet ﷺ entered telling her O Ḥafṣah Jibrīl عليه السلام had just come reporting Allah conveyed salām to him and commanded him to review her for she was fasting, praying by night, and his wife in Paradise. > **""O Ḥafṣah, review her, for she is fasting, praying by night, and she is your wife in Paradise.""**"
"15334","Qays ibn Yazīd reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divorced Ḥafṣah once, her maternal uncles ʿUthmān and Qudāmah sons of Maẓʿūn came to her, she swore by Allah he had not divorced her from satiety, then the Prophet ﷺ came and entered while she veiled herself, announcing Jibrīl عليه السلام had told him to review Ḥafṣah for she was fasting, praying by night, and his wife in Paradise. > **""Review Ḥafṣah, for she is fasting, praying by night, and she is your wife in Paradise.""**"
"15335","Mālik ibn Anas reported that Ḥafṣah died the year Africa was conquered, while Marwān governed Madīnah."
"15336","Zayd ibn Abī Ḥabīb reported that Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥudayj campaigned to Africa thrice, the first in thirty-four, the second in forty, the third in fifty."
"15337","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Umm Salamah's name was Hind bint Abī Umayyah Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Makhzūm ibn Yaqaẓah ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib, transmitted by ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār, noting before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ she was with Abū Salamah ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Asad bearing him Salamah, ʿUmar and Zaynab, then he died and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ succeeded him with her. ---"
"15338","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he came to her, folded his cloak upon the threshold, leaned upon it and asked whether she would have him, she answered she was intensely jealous and feared displaying to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what he disliked, so he left then returned asking whether she would have him and offering increase in her dower, she repeated her words, whereupon Umm ʿAbd said O Umm Salamah did she know what Quraysh women said, that she only refused Muḥammad seeking a younger wealthier Qurashī youth, so she went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he married her. > **""Would you have me, O Umm Salamah?""**"
"15339","Al-Haytham ibn ʿAdī reported that the first of the Prophet's wives ﷺ to perish was Zaynab bint Jaḥsh in the caliphate of ʿUmar, and the last to perish was Umm Salamah in the time of Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah in year sixty-two."
"15340","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ married Sawdah bint Zamʿah, her brother ʿAbd ibn Zamʿah came from Ḥajj throwing dust upon his head, and after accepting Islam declared himself foolish that day for resenting the Prophet's ﷺ marriage to Sawdah."
"15341","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then married Sawdah bint Zamʿah, who before him was under al-Sakrān ibn ʿAmr, brother of ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy."
"15342","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sābiṭ reported that the Prophet ﷺ intended separation from Sawdah and summoned Abū Bakr and ʿUmar to witness her divorce, whereupon she pleaded O Messenger of Allah she had no desire for the world except to be mustered on Resurrection Day among his wives with their reward. > **""I have no desire for the world except to be mustered on Resurrection Day among your wives.""**"
"15343","Al-Haytham, or Abū al-Haytham, reported that the Prophet ﷺ divorced Sawdah once, she sat in his path, and when he passed she asked for return and to gift her share to whichever wife he wished, hoping to be raised on Resurrection Day as his wife, so he reviewed her and accepted it from her."
"15344","Zaynab bint Jaḥsh رضي الله عنها reported that several Qurashīs proposed to her so she sent her sister Ḥamnah to consult the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked where she was from one who would teach her her Lord's Book and her Prophet's Sunnah, naming Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, whereupon Ḥamnah angrily objected at marrying his cousin to his freedman, came informing her, she grew angrier and spoke harsher, so Allah revealed no believer male or female had choice when Allah and His Messenger decreed, she then sent word seeking forgiveness, obeying Allah and His Messenger to do as he saw, he married her to Zayd, she behaved proudly toward him, he complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who reproached her, she repeated offense with her tongue, he complained again, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to keep his wife and fear Allah, he declared he would divorce her, did so, and when her waiting period ended she found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entering upon her while her hair was uncovered, declared it a heavenly command, asked without proposal or witnesses, and he answered Allah was the Marrier and Jibrīl the witness. > **""Allah is the Marrier, and Jibrīl is the witness.""**"
"15345","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ then married Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, who before him was under Zayd ibn Ḥārithah."
"15346","Al-Zuhrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Zaynab bint Jaḥsh ibn Riʾāb ibn Asad ibn Khuzaymah, her mother Umaymah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim, paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she was the first of the Prophet's ﷺ wives to die."
"15347","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that among Banū Asad women who emigrated were Zaynab bint Jaḥsh and other women whom he named."
"15348","Abū Bakr ibn Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah reported in mursal form, part from Umm Salamah as seen, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah's house seeking permission, Zaynab admitted him with no head-cover, casting her sleeve's cuff upon her head, he asked of Zayd, she said he went nearby O Messenger of Allah, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose murmuring, Umm Salamah followed hearing him repeat blessed is the Turner of hearts till he vanished. > **""Blessed is the Turner of hearts.""**"
"15349","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ consummated with Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, then narrated the walīmah account till saying Zaynab sat at the side of the house, a woman granted beauty while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was intensely modest, then narrated the account."
"15350","Rāshid ibn Saʿd reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered his dwelling with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb finding Zaynab bint Jaḥsh praying and supplicating in her prayer, so the Prophet ﷺ declared her oft-returning to Allah. > **""She is indeed awwāhah.""**"
"15351","Abū Barzah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ had nine wives and said one day the best of them was longest of hand, each stood placing her hand upon the wall, so he clarified he did not mean that but most industrious of hands. > **""The best of you is the longest of you in hand.""**"
"15352","Maymūnah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered while they sat and declared the first of them to reach him at the Ḥawḍ would be longest of hand, so they measured forearms which was longest, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ clarified he did not mean that but most industrious of hand. > **""The first of you to reach me at the Ḥawḍ is the longest of you in hand.""**"
"15353","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā reported that ʿUmar gave four takbīrs over Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, then sent to the Prophet's wives ﷺ asking who should lower her into her grave, they answered whoever used to enter upon her in life, then ʿUmar recalled the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say the quickest of them to join him was longest of hand, so they stretched hands, though it meant she was craftswoman aiding with her craft in Allah's path. > **""The quickest of you to join me is the longest of you in hand.""**"
"15354","Ibn al-Munkadir reported that Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, died in the caliphate of ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما."
"15355","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, died in year twenty."
"15356","Al-Shaʿbī reported he prayed with ʿUmar over Zaynab, she was the first of the Prophet's wives ﷺ to die, ʿUmar wished to lower her into her grave and sent to the Prophet's wives ﷺ asking who should lower her, they answered whoever saw her in life should lower her."
"15357","Al-Zuhrī reported that the Prophet ﷺ married Zaynab bint Khuzaymah, Mother of the Poor, so named for abundantly feeding the poor, of Banū Hilāl ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah, and she died while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lived, remaining with him only briefly."
"15358","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Zaynab bint Khuzaymah al-Hilāliyyah, Mother of the Poor, before him with al-Ḥuṣayn, or with al-Ṭufayl ibn al-Ḥārith, she died in Madīnah, first of his wives to die."
"15359","Al-Zuhrī reported that Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥazn ibn Bujayr ibn al-Huzam ibn Ruʾaybah ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Hilāl ibn ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah was the one who offered herself to the Prophet ﷺ."
"15360","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reported he was once in an expedition when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to go bring Maymūnah, he objected he was in the expedition, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he did not love what he loved, he affirmed, so he commanded him to go bring her, which he did. > **""Do you not love what I love?""**"
"15361","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Maymūnah at Sarif."
"15362","Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm reported that Maymūnah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, grew heavy in Makkah with none of her nephews present, so she asked to be taken out of Makkah for she would not die in it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had told her she would not die in Makkah, they carried her to Sarif to the tree under which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had consummated with her at the dome's site, she died, and when placed in her laḥd he took his cloak placing it under her cheek, but Ibn ʿAbbās took and cast it away."
"15363","Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm reported he saw the Mother of the Believers shaving her head after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when questioned Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm considered she debased her adornment."
"15364","Maymūnah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the sisters believers, meaning Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith, Salmā wife of Ḥamzah, and Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays. > **""The sisters are believers.""**"
"15365","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, died the year of al-Ḥarrah, year sixty-three."
"15366","Al-Zuhrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān ibn Ḥarb ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Quṣayy ibn Kilāb ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghālib ibn Fihr ibn Mālik, her name Ramla, and noted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married Ruqayyah رضي الله عنها to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه because Umm Ḥabībah's mother Ṣafiyyah bint Abī al-ʿĀṣ was ʿUthmān's paternal aunt, full sister of ʿAffān, and Sharḥbīl ibn Ḥasanah brought Umm Ḥabībah to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15367","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took captive Juwayriyah bint al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq of Khuzāʿah in his campaign wherein he demolished Manāt, the campaign of al-Muraysīʿ."
"15368","Al-Zuhrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took captive Juwayriyah bint al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿĀʾidh ibn Mālik ibn al-Muṣṭaliq of Khuzāʿah, al-Muṣṭaliq being Khuzaymah, the day he encountered Banū al-Muṣṭaliq."
"15369","Al-Shaʿbī reported that Juwayriyah was the property of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he freed her making her freedom her dowry, and freed every Banū al-Muṣṭaliq captive."
"15370","Mujāhid reported that Juwayriyah told the Prophet ﷺ his wives boasted over her claiming the Prophet ﷺ had not married her, and he asked had he not magnified her dowry and freed forty of her people. > **""Did I not magnify your dowry? Did I not free forty of your people?""**"
"15371","Shabāb al-ʿUṣfurī reported that Juwayriyah bint al-Ḥārith, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, died in year fifty-six."
"15372","Abū Barzah رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended upon Khaybar with Ṣafiyyah a bride in her adornments, she had dreamed the sun fell upon her breast and told her husband, who swore she desired only the king who had descended upon them, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered it, struck her husband's neck in retribution, youths of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ vied for her, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her, cast dates upon a mat and told them to eat the Messenger's walīmah over Ṣafiyyah. > **""Eat the walīmah of the Messenger of Allah over Ṣafiyyah.""**"
"15373","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that Ṣafiyyah had greenness in her eyes, the Prophet ﷺ asked its cause, she said she told her husband she dreamed as sleepers see a moon falling in her lap, so he slapped her accusing her of desiring the king of Yathrib, and she added none was more hateful to her than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who had killed her father and husband, but he kept excusing himself explaining O Ṣafiyyah her father had incited the Arabs against him and done such and such till it left her soul. > **""O Ṣafiyyah, your father incited the Arabs against me.""**"
"15374","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported that when Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy رضي الله عنها entered the tent of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, people including himself gathered expecting a share in her, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out telling them to rise from their mother, and at evening they gathered, he came out with about a mudd and a half of ʿajwah dates in his cloak's edge telling them to eat their mother's walīmah. > **""Rise from your mother.""**"
"15375","Razīnah رضي الله عنها reported that on the day of Qurayẓah and al-Naḍīr the Messenger of Allah ﷺ brought Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy holding her forearm in his hand, when she saw the captives she testified none had right to worship but Allah and he was Allah's Messenger, so he released her forearm, freed her, proposed, married her, and dowered her a zurbiyyah garment."
"15376","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took captive Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab of Banū al-Naḍīr among what Allah granted him as fayʾ."
"15377","Al-Zuhrī reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took captive Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab of Banū al-Naḍīr on the day of Ḥunayn, a bride with Kinānah ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq."
"15378","Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb رضي الله عنه reported that when Allah granted the Prophet ﷺ Ṣafiyyah he asked his Companions their view of the girl, they said he was worthiest and most entitled, so he declared he had freed and married her making her freedom her dowry, a man mentioned walīmah, and he declared walīmah obligatory, the second customary, the third pride and hardship. > **""I have freed her, married her, and made her freedom her dowry.""**"
"15379","Ṣafiyyah رضي الله عنها reported she reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with none more hateful to her than him, he told her her people had done such and such, and she did not rise from her seat with any more beloved to her than him. > **""Your people did such and such.""**"
"15380","In another narration from her, she never saw better character than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, recalling he mounted her behind his she-camel by night from Khaybar, she dozed and the saddle's back struck her head, he wiped with his hand saying gently O daughter of Ḥuyayy, till at al-Ṣahbāʾ he apologized O Ṣafiyyah for what he did to her people for they had told him such and such. > **""Gently, O daughter of Ḥuyayy.""**"
"15381","Al-Zuhrī listed the wives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, ʿĀʾishah bint Abī Bakr, Umm Salamah bint Abī Umayyah, Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar, Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān, Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, Juwayriyah bint al-Ḥārith, Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, Sawdah bint Zamʿah, and Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy, nine gathered with him after Khadījah, besides the Kindiyyah of Banū al-Jawn, al-ʿĀliyah bint Ẓabyān of Banū ʿĀmir ibn Kilāb, Zaynab bint Khuzaymah, and a Hilāliyyah woman."
"15382","Al-Zuhrī reported ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed him that when the Kindiyyah entered upon the Prophet ﷺ she said she sought refuge in Allah from him, so he told her she sought refuge in the Mighty and to join her family. > **""You have sought refuge in the Mighty, join your family.""**"
"15383","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported there was no woman with the Prophet ﷺ who had offered herself to him."
"15384","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported the Prophet ﷺ married Khadījah bint Khuwaylid previously under ʿAtīq ibn ʿĀʾidh al-Makhzūmī, then ʿĀʾishah at Makkah marrying no virgin besides her, then at Madīnah Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar previously under Khunays ibn Ḥudhāfah al-Sahmī, then Sawdah bint Zamʿah previously under al-Sakrān ibn ʿAmr brother of Banū ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy, then Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān previously under ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaḥsh al-Asadī of Asad Khuzaymah, then Umm Ḥarām, then Umm Salamah bint Abī Umayyah named Hind previously under Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, then Zaynab bint Jaḥsh previously under Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, then Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, took captive Juwayriyah bint al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Ḍirār of Banū al-Muṣṭaliq of Khuzāʿah in the Manāt-demolishing Muraysīʿ campaign, took captive Ṣafiyyah bint Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab of Banū al-Naḍīr among Allah's fayʾ, took Rayḥānah of Banū Qurayẓah as concubine then freed her so she joined her family veiled among them, divorced al-Ghāliyah bint Ẓabyān, separated from the sister of Banū ʿAmr ibn Kilāb and from the Jawniyyah Kindiyyah for whiteness upon her, Zaynab bint Khuzaymah al-Hilāliyyah died while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lived, and it reached them al-Ghāliyah bint Ẓabyān married before Allah forbade his wives, wedding a cousin among her people and bearing among them."
"15385","Qatādah reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married fifteen women, six of Quraysh, one of Qurayẓah women, seven of other Arabs, one of Banū Isrāʾīl, marrying none of them in Jāhiliyyah besides her, first married in Jāhiliyyah was Khadījah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Quṣayy previously under ʿAtīq ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Makhzūm, then after ʿAtīq under Abū Hālah Hind ibn Zurārah ibn Nabbāsh ibn Ḥabīb ibn Ṣurad ibn Salāmah ibn Jarāwah ibn Usayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Taym bearing him Hind ibn Hind, Zuhayr quoting Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd adding Hind passed through al-Baṣrah and perished there when no market nor pasture stood that day, then the Prophet ﷺ married her after them, she bore him ʿAbd Manāf in Jāhiliyyah and two boys and four girls in Islam."
"15386","Ibn Abī Mulaykah and ʿAmr ibn Dīnār reported nine women including Ṣafiyyah gathered with the Prophet ﷺ after Khadījah, all of whom he died leaving, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Sulaymān adding two women besides the nine of Banū ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah both named Jamʿ, one called Mother of the Poor best of his women to the poor, he married a Jawniyyah woman who when she came sought refuge from him so he divorced her, married a Kindiyyah woman without consummating, she married after the Prophet ﷺ so ʿUmar separated them striking her husband, she protested O ʿUmar fear Allah, if she was among Mothers of Believers veil her and grant like them, he denied that, she asked leave to marry, he refused and would entice none to it. > **""Fear Allah, O ʿUmar, if I am among the Mothers of the Believers then cast the veil over me.""**"
"15387","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Shirāf bint Khalīfah ibn Farwah al-Kalbiyyah, sister of Diḥyah ibn Khalīfah, was married by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without consummation."
"15388","Ibn Abī Mulaykah reported that the Prophet ﷺ proposed a Kalbī woman and sent ʿĀʾishah to view her."
"15389","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Qutaylah bint Qays al-Kindiyyah, sister of al-Ashʿath ibn Qays, was married by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without consummation till he parted her."
"15390","Khawlah bint Ḥakīm ibn al-Awqaṣ رضي الله عنها was among those who offered themselves to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15391","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported a necklace of onyx inlaid with gold was gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while his wives gathered all in a house and Umāmah bint Abī al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ played as a girl in the side with dust, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked their view, they declared never seeing finer or more wondrous, he asked it returned to him, took it swearing to place it upon the neck of the most beloved of the Household to him, ʿĀʾishah said the earth darkened between her and him fearing he place it upon another, all fell silent alike, and he placed it upon Umāmah's neck relieving them. > **""By Allah, I shall place it upon the neck of the most beloved of the Household to me.""**"
"15392","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār reported that Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ entrusted his daughter Umāmah and his estate to al-Zubayr, who married her to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib after Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها, ʿAlī was killed while Umāmah was with him bearing him none, whereupon Umm al-Haytham al-Nakhaʿiyyah versified her forelock grayed and pillar humbled the day Umāmah parted her mate whom she circled in need till comforted she raised a cry."
"15393","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported that Umāmah bint Abī al-ʿĀṣ whose mother was Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, when he died he told her not to marry except by counsel of al-Mughīrah ibn Nawfal, Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān proposed, she consulted al-Mughīrah who declared himself better for her and asked her affair to him, she complied, he summoned men and married her, Umāmah perished with al-Mughīrah ibn Nawfal bearing him none, leaving Zaynab no progeny."
"15394","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkār reported that Ṣafiyyah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib never veiled her head from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ nor from ten first Muhājirūn: her brother Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, her nephews Jaʿfar and ʿAlī sons of Abū Ṭālib, her son al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām, her great-nephew ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān whose mother Arwā bint Kurayz daughter of al-Bayḍāʾ Umm Ḥakīm bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, her sister Barrah's sons Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Asad and Abū Sabrah ibn Abī Ruhm, Ṭulayb ibn ʿUmayr ibn Wahb ibn ʿAbd ibn Quṣayy's mother Arwā bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and the blind poet Abū Aḥmad named ʿAbd ibn Jaḥsh ibn Riʾāb ibn Yaʿmar ibn Ṣabrah ibn Murrah ibn Kathīr ibn Ghanm ibn Wardān ibn Asad ibn Khuzaymah whose mother was Umaymah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and Ṣafiyyah died in ʿUmar's caliphate."
"15395","ʿĀtikah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنها reported she saw a rider take a rock from Abū Qubays hurling it toward the Corner, it split, and every Quraysh house received a fragment except Banū Zuhrah houses, then the narrator mentioned the account to its end."
"15396","Muṣʿab ibn ʿAbdillāh and other Qurashīs reported that ʿĀtikah bint ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib affirmed her dream's truth and Quraysh's denial when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ smote them at Badr, versifying was not the dream true with its interpretation brought by a fleeing remnant, he saw and brought the certainty his eyes beheld what cutting swords effect, they accused her of lying though she lied not, only a liar denies the truthful, only fear of death made Ḥakīm flee when paths narrowed, do hearts flee the people's dawn call as air with absent minds, they passed swords through blood face to face as clouds stream, how did his cousins find Muḥammad on encounter day with war's trials, did he not overwhelm them with blows bewildering cowards and revealing stars by day, may he be ransomed on encounter day when war's fiercest bites, as his swords flashed from sovereignty like rosy gales after stiffness, swearing if they returned he would uproot them with a teeming host whose vanguards hasten like sunlight flashing bolts with radiant piercing sides."
"15397","ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, reported that Fāṭimah daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sufficed him inside work and Fāṭimah bint Asad sufficed him outside work, meaning the Prophet ﷺ."
"15398","In another narration from ʿAlī رضي الله عنه he told his mother Fāṭimah bint Asad ibn Hāshim to suffice Fāṭimah daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with drawing water and errands, while she would suffice her with inside service of grinding and kneading."
"15399","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that when Fāṭimah bint Asad ibn Hāshim, mother of ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما, died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered, sat at her head declaring mercy upon his mother after his mother who hungered to feed him, went bare to clothe him, denied herself delicacies to feed him seeking Allah's Face and the Hereafter, then ordered washing thrice, poured the camphor water with his hand, removed his shirt clothing her with it, shrouded her with a cloak above, summoned Usāmah ibn Zayd, Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb and a black youth to dig, dug her grave, at the laḥd dug it with his hand removing its soil with his hand, when finished entered and lay in it supplicating Allah who gives life and death, Living undying, to forgive his mother Fāṭimah bint Asad, dictate her proof, widen her entrance by right of his Prophet and prior Prophets, Most Merciful of merciful, gave four takbīrs over her, and he, al-ʿAbbās and Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنهم lowered her into the laḥd. > **""May Allah have mercy upon you, O my mother, you were my mother after my mother.""**"
"15400","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that when Fāṭimah mother of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib died the Prophet ﷺ removed his shirt clothing her with it and lay in her grave, and when soil was leveled they asked O Messenger of Allah they saw him do what he never did, so he explained he clothed her his shirt for garments of Paradise and lay with her in her grave to lighten grave pressure, for after Abū Ṭālib she was finest of Allah's creation to him in conduct. > **""I clothed her my shirt that she wear of Paradise garments, and I lay with her in her grave that grave pressure be lightened for her.""**"
"15401","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Rāfiʿ reported that Umm Hāniʾ bint Abī Ṭālib went out adorned with visible earrings, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb told her to work for Muḥammad would avail her nothing, she came informing the Prophet ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked what was with folk claiming his intercession would not reach his Household when it would reach Ḥāʾ and Ḥakam, two tribes. > **""What is with folk claiming my intercession will not reach my Household, when my intercession will reach Ḥāʾ and Ḥakam.""**"
"15402","Ibn ʿUmar, Abū Hurayrah and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنهم reported that Durrah bint Abī Lahab came emigrating lodging at Rāfiʿ ibn al-Muʿallā al-Zuraqī's house, Banū Zurayq women sitting with her taunted her as daughter of Abī Lahab of whom Allah said perish the hands, implying what her emigration availed, so Durrah complained to the Prophet ﷺ, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ calmed her bidding her sit, prayed Ẓuhr, sat awhile on the minbar declaring why he was harmed concerning his family when by Allah his intercession would reach Ḥayy Ḥāʾ, Ḥakam, Ṣudāʾ and Salhab on Resurrection Day. > **""O people, why am I harmed concerning my family.""**"
"15403","Ibn Abī al-Ḥusayn, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥusayn in mursal form, reported that Durrah bint Abī Lahab was with al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Nawfal bearing him ʿUqbah, al-Walīd and Abū Muslim, then came to the Prophet ﷺ in Madīnah, people spoke much of her parents, she pleaded O Messenger of Allah was none born of disbelievers but her, he asked what was it, she said Madīnah folk harmed her over her parents, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told her when he prayed Ẓuhr to pray where he saw, he prayed Ẓuhr, turned to her, faced the people asking O people had they lineage and he none, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb leapt declaring Allah angered at whoever angered him, so he declared her his cousin of whom none should speak but good. > **""This is my cousin, let none speak of her but good.""**"
"15404","Durrah daughter of Abī Lahab رضي الله عنها reported she was with ʿĀʾishah when the Prophet ﷺ entered asking for ablution water, she and ʿĀʾishah raced to the jug, she beat her taking it, he performed ablution raising his eye declaring she was from him and he from her, then a man was brought claiming he only repeated what was told him, and he used to ask him on the minbar who were best people, he answered most understanding in Allah's religion and most joining kin, Sharīk mentioning two other things she did not retain. > **""You are from me, and I am from you.""**"
"15405","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Umm Ayman, mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd, freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, belonged to Khadījah's sister who gifted her to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who married her to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and it was said her name was Barakah."
"15406","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that Umm Ayman was mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd."
"15407","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reported from Umm Ayman that she was among those who pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ."
"15408","Ibn Shihāb reported that Umm Ayman, mother of Usāmah ibn Zayd, was Abyssinian, a maid of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib nursing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in childhood, whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ freed then married to Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and she died five months after the Prophet ﷺ, though al-Zuhrī also said and narrated in this account she lived after ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb's death."
"15409","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reported that Umm Ayman said on the day ʿUmar was killed today Islam weakened. > **""Today Islam has weakened.""**"
"15410","Khawlah bint Ḥakīm رضي الله عنها, whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had married, was deferred among those deferred."
"15411","Zaynab bint Abī Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered to wash, her mother told her to go enter, she entered, he sprinkled water in her face telling her to return, and al-ʿAṭṭāf's mother reported seeing Zaynab as a great old woman with nothing diminished of her face. > **""Return.""**"
"15412","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Ḥalīmah bint Abī Dhuʾayb, Abū Dhuʾayb being ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥayyān of Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr ibn Hawāzin, was the mother of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who nursed and weaned him."
"15413","Abū al-Ṭufayl رضي الله عنه reported he was a boy carrying a camel limb when he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributing meat at al-Jiʿrānah, a woman came to him and he spread his cloak for her, and when he asked who she was he was told she was his mother who had nursed him."
"15414","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the mother of Abū Bakr, the mother of ʿUthmān, the mother of Ṭalḥah, the mother of al-Zubayr, the mother of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, and the mother of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir all accepted Islam."
"15415","Al-Haytham ibn ʿAdī reported that the mother of Abū Bakr was called Umm al-Khayr bint Ṣakhr ibn ʿĀmir, Abū Bakr died leaving both parents as heirs after they had accepted Islam, and the mother of Abū Bakr died before his father."
"15416","Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī Fustuqah reported that Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنها died in year seventy-three, nights after her son ʿAbdullāh, she was the paternal sister of ʿĀʾishah, her mother was Qutaylah bint ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn ʿAbd Asʿad of Banū Mālik ibn Ḥisl, she was a hundred years old when she died, born twenty-seven years before the calendar and seventeen years before the mission, and Asmāʾ was born to Abū Bakr when he was twenty-one."
"15417","Yaʿlā ibn Ḥarmalah reported he entered Makkah after Ibn al-Zubayr was killed when his mother Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr came as a very old, tall, blind woman and addressed al-Ḥajjāj about the rider descending. > **""Has it not yet come time for this rider to dismount?""**"
"15418","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr in naming those who emigrated to Abyssinia reported that Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib went with his wife Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays al-Khathʿamiyyah, who bore him in Abyssinia ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, ʿAwn ibn Jaʿfar, and Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar."
"15419","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the believing sisters as his wife Maymūnah, Umm al-Faḍl wife of al-ʿAbbās, Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays wife of Jaʿfar, and the wife of Ḥamzah, maternal sisters to one another. > **""The believing sisters are Maymūnah wife of the Prophet, Umm al-Faḍl wife of al-ʿAbbās, Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays wife of Jaʿfar, and the wife of Ḥamzah, sisters by their mother.""**"
"15420","Muhājir reported that Asmāʾ bint Yazīd ibn al-Sakan, cousin of Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنهما, killed nine Romans on the day of Yarmūk with a tent pole."
"15421","Al-Naḍr ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported that Umm Sulaym came to Abū Anas declaring she brought what he disliked, he complained she always brought dislike from that Bedouin whom Allah had chosen and made a Prophet, she announced wine was prohibited, he declared separation and died a polytheist, then Abū Ṭalḥah came proposing while a polytheist, she refused marriage to a polytheist, he claimed her time was for yellow and white wealth, she called him and the Prophet ﷺ to witness that his Islam would satisfy her, sent Anas with his uncle to the Prophet ﷺ who recognized the glory of Islam between his eyes, he greeted and testified, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married him upon Islam, she bore a boy whom his father loved, Allah took him, Abū Ṭalḥah asked after him and she answered with the best state, served his meal, then likened the son to a borrowed item its owners reclaimed without cause for grief, he agreed, she told him the son had departed this world in the inner chamber, he uncovered him uttering istirjāʿ and informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of her words, who swore Allah had cast a male into her womb for her patience, she gave birth, the Prophet ﷺ ordered Anas to bring the infant untouched after cutting the cord, Anas carried him on his forearm, the Prophet ﷺ asked for three ʿajwah dates, removed stones, chewed them, opened the boy's mouth placing them therein while he smacked, declaring him Anṣārī loving dates, and sent word of blessing and piety for him. > **""This is Abū Ṭalḥah with the glory of Islam between his eyes.""**"
"15422","In another narration of al-Bazzār she said to him whether she would marry him while he worshipped wood dragged by her slave so-and-so, then the narrator mentioned the account in full. > **""Would I marry you while you worship wood dragged by my slave so-and-so?""**"
"15423","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that Abū Ṭalḥah intended to divorce Umm Sulaym, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared divorcing Umm Sulaym a grave sin. > **""Divorcing Umm Sulaym is indeed ḥūb.""**"
"15424","Abū Aḥmad ibn Jaḥsh رضي الله عنه reported he saw with his own eyes Ḥamnah bint Jaḥsh on the day of Uḥud giving drink to the thirsty and treating the wounded."
"15425","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that among Banū Asad women who emigrated was Ḥamnah bint Jaḥsh among women whom he named."
"15426","Umm ʿAyyāsh رضي الله عنها, servant of the Prophet ﷺ, was sent by him with his daughter to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه."
"15427","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that Umm al-Mundhir who narrated from the Prophet ﷺ was Salmā bint Qays, and she prayed toward both qiblas with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15428","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that Abū Ayyūb divorced his wife, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ told him divorcing Umm Ayyūb was sin, and Ibn Sīrīn explained ḥūb as sin. > **""O Abā Ayyūb, divorcing Umm Ayyūb was indeed ḥūb.""**"
"15429","Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn reported that the servant of the Prophet ﷺ was called Khuḍayrah."
"15430","Rawḍah رضي الله عنها reported she was a maid to a Madinan woman, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emigrated from Makkah her mistress ordered her to watch the door for the man and inform her, she stood, the Prophet ﷺ came with companions, she grasped his cloak's edge and he smiled in her face, Shaybah adding he thought he wiped his hand upon her head, she told her mistress the man had come, her mistress and household came out, he offered Islam and they accepted, and ʿAbd al-Jalīl added Shaybah told him Rawḍah lived with him among Banū Sulaym such that neighbors buying a slave, servant, garment or food asked her to place her hand upon it and everything she touched held blessing."
"15431","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that ʿĀtikah bint Zayd was under ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr."
"15432","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Umm Maʿbad al-Khuzāʿiyyah was ʿĀtikah bint Khālid ibn Munqidh ibn Ḍubays al-Kaʿbiyyah al-Khuzāʿiyyah."
"15433","Hishām ibn Ḥarām from his father reported that Umm Maʿbad received clothing, Yemeni produce, and pitch for her camels, when ʿUthmān passed she demanded where her clothing and Yemeni produce due to her were, he affirmed they were hers with them, and she followed him till he gave them to her."
"15434","Hishām ibn al-Ghāz reported that the grave of Umm Ḥarām bint Milḥān is in Cyprus, people saying this is the grave of the righteous woman."
"15435","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Fāṭimah bint al-Khaṭṭāb ibn Nufayl, called Umm Jamīl, sister of ʿUmar, early in Islam accepting before ʿUmar, was the wife of Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl رضي الله عنهما."
"15436","Umm Khālid bint al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth رضي الله عنها entered upon the Prophet ﷺ, he asked who she was, they said the daughter of al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth, and he praised Allah who brings the living from the dead, meaning the believer from the disbeliever. > **""Praise belongs to Allah who brings the living from the dead.""**"
"15437","In another narration the Prophet ﷺ entered asking who she was, they said some maternal aunt of his, he remarked his maternal aunts in this land were strangers and asked again, they said Umm Khālid bint al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth, and he glorified Him who brings the living from the dead. > **""Glory to Him who brings the living from the dead.""**"
"15438","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that Ṣafiyyah bint ʿUmar was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Ḥunayn."
"15439","Sallāmah bint al-Ḥurr رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed her in early Islam while she grazed, asking what she testified, she testified none had right to worship but Allah and Muḥammad was Allah's Messenger, and he smiled laughing. > **""O Sallāmah, in what do you testify?""**"
"15440","Yaḥyā ibn Abī Sulaym reported he saw Samrāʾ bint Nuhayk who had reached the Prophet ﷺ wearing coarse armor and coarse veil, whip in hand disciplining people, commanding good and forbidding evil."
"15441","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Hind bint ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf was the mother of Muʿāwiyah."
"15442","Ḥumayd ibn Muhabb al-Ṭāʾī reported that Hind bint ʿUtbah was with al-Fākih ibn al-Mughīrah al-Makhzūmī, a Qurashī youth with a guest house entered without permission, one day the house was empty while al-Fākih and Hind napped at midday, al-Fākih left for a need, a frequenter entered, seeing the woman fled, al-Fākih saw him leaving, struck Hind with his foot demanding who was with her, she swore none was and she awoke only when he woke her, he ordered her to her father, people talked, her father demanded the truth promising to have the man killed if truthful or to contest before a Yemeni soothsayer if lying, she swore by their oaths he lied, he challenged al-Fākih to the soothsayer, ʿUtbah set out with ʿAbd Manāf folk, al-Fākih with Makhzūm folk, Hind with women, nearing the land her state changed, her father feared disgrace before witnesses, she denied wrongdoing but feared fallible man branding her with lasting shame, he tested the soothsayer first by whistling his horse till it exposed, inserting a wheat grain into its sheath tied with a strap, next morning after honor and slaughter ʿUtbah asked him to divine the hidden test, he said a date in a sheath, pressed for clearer he said a wheat grain in a foal's sheath, confirmed truthful he bade him examine the women, he approached each striking her shoulder declaring her innocent till Hind, striking her shoulder declaring she would rise neither wild nor adulteress and would bear a boy called Muʿāwiyah, al-Fākih took her hand, she withdrew declaring by Allah she would strive it be from another, then Abū Sufyān married her and she bore Muʿāwiyah. > **""Rise, neither wild nor adulteress, and you shall bear a boy called Muʿāwiyah.""**"
"15443","Qaylah bint Makhramah رضي الله عنها reported she came to the Prophet ﷺ praying part of the prayer with him, when he finished she stood, he looked at her, a tall woman, remarking her son would have fought behind the barrier, she swore he was so O Messenger of Allah but had died, asked for a document, having three daughters, so he wrote from Muḥammad Allah's Messenger to Qaylah and the three women that their right not be wronged nor coerced into marriage, every believer and Muslim was helper to him and them, enjoining kindness without harm. > **""From Muḥammad Allah's Messenger to Qaylah and the three women: they shall not be wronged in right nor coerced into marriage.""**"
"15444","Jamrah bint ʿAbdillāh al-Yarbūʿiyyah رضي الله عنها reported her father took her to the Prophet ﷺ after camels came to him, asking Allah's blessing for his daughter, so the Prophet ﷺ seated her in his lap, placed his hand upon her head, and supplicated for blessing for her."
"15445","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that al-Tawʾamah bint Umayyah ibn Khalaf was mentioned though no narration came from her."
"15446","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥakam ibn Abī Ziyād reported that Ṣāliḥ was the freedman of al-Tawʾamah, daughter of Umayyah ibn Khalaf."
"15447","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Tamīmah bint Wahb, divorced by Rifāʿah ibn Samawʾal, was mentioned though no narration came from her."
"15448","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr reported that Shuraḥbīl ibn Ḥasanah was so named for his mother Ḥasanah, among those who emigrated to Abyssinia."
"15449","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Dhaqirah, mother of Udhaynah's child, was said to have companionship."
"15450","He reported that Rāʾiṭah bint Munabbih ibn al-Ḥajjāj al-Sahmī was the mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ."
"15451","He reported that Saffānah bint Ḥātim was the sister of ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim."
"15452","He reported that al-Sawdāʾ was the daughter of Khalaf ibn Ḍirār ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Qurṭ ibn Zarāḥ ibn ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb."
"15453","He reported that Shaymāʾ bint al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā ibn Rifāʿah was the foster sister of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15454","He reported that Laylā bint Abī Ḥathmah ibn Ḥudhayfah ibn Ghānim ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn Huwayj ibn ʿAdī ibn Kaʿb, mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah, was among emigrant women, noting her account concerned emigration to Abyssinia."
"15455","He reported Umm Usayd al-Anṣāriyyah."
"15456","He reported that Umm ʿAbdillāh bint al-Ḥārith ibn Qudayd al-Hudhaliyyah, mother of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, was granted by ʿUmar among women's acquisition from spoils."
"15457","Al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه reported that ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib married Hālah bint Uhayb ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Zuhrah, who bore him Ḥamzah and Ṣafiyyah."
"15458","ʿUrwah in naming those who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ listed Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib."
"15459","Ibn Shihāb in naming those who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ listed Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Manāf."
"15460","Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī reported that Ḥamzah's Islam رضي الله عنه was from zeal, he was an archer leaving the sanctuary to hunt, returning past Quraysh gatherings at al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah boasting of shots and deeds then going home, one day returning from shooting a woman met him telling him O Abā ʿUmārah what his nephew suffered from Abū Jahl ibn Hishām in insult, assault and deeds, he asked if any saw, she swore people saw, he advanced to the gathering where Abū Jahl sat, leaned upon his bow recounting shots and deeds, then gripped the bow with both hands striking between Abū Jahl's ears breaking its tooth, declaring take it by bow and another by sword, testifying he was Allah's Messenger come with truth from Allah, they answered O Abā ʿUmārah he reviled their gods and even his excellence would not excuse that, nor was he ever foul. > **""I testify that he is the Messenger of Allah and that he has come with the truth from Allah.""**"
"15461","Yaʿqūb ibn ʿUtbah ibn al-Mughīrah ibn al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, ally of Banū Zuhrah, reported that Abū Jahl confronted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-Ṣafā harming him while Ḥamzah رضي الله عنه, a hunter, was out hunting, upon return his wife who had witnessed Abū Jahl's deed told him O Abā ʿUmārah had he seen what Abū Jahl did to his nephew, Ḥamzah angered went as he was before entering his house, bow hung upon his neck, till the mosque finding Abū Jahl in a Quraysh gathering, wordlessly split his head with his bow, Quraysh men rose to restrain him, Ḥamzah declared his religion Muḥammad's testifying he was Allah's Messenger never turning from it, daring them to prevent him if truthful, and with Ḥamzah's Islam the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Muslims were strengthened, their affair stabilized, Quraysh feared knowing Ḥamzah would protect him. > **""My religion is the religion of Muḥammad; I testify that he is the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"15462","Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Labībah from his father from his grandfather reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore by Him in whose Hand was his soul that it was written with Allah in the seventh heaven that Ḥamzah was Allah's lion and His Messenger's lion. > **""By Him in whose Hand is my soul, it is written with Allah in the seventh heaven: Ḥamzah is the lion of Allah and the lion of His Messenger.""**"
"15463","ʿUmayr ibn Isḥāq reported that Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib fought before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring himself Allah's lion and His Messenger's lion. > **""I am the lion of Allah and the lion of His Messenger.""**"
"15464","ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the master of martyrs to be Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. > **""The master of martyrs is Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.""**"
"15465","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the best of martyrs with Allah to be Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib. > **""The best of martyrs with Allah is Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.""**"
"15466","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the master of martyrs on Resurrection Day to be Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and a man who stood to an unjust ruler enjoining and forbidding till he killed him. > **""The master of martyrs on Resurrection Day is Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, and a man who stands to an unjust ruler, enjoining him and forbidding him, and he kills him.""**"
"15467","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reported that al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib was called Abū al-Faḍl, his mother Natīlah bint Janāb ibn Kulayb ibn Mālik ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿĀmir ibn Zayd ibn ʿAbd Manāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn al-Khazraj ibn Taym Allāt ibn Namir ibn Qāsiṭ ibn Afṣā ibn Jadīlah ibn Asad ibn Rabīʿah ibn Nizār ibn Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān."
"15468","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb told al-ʿAbbās to accept Islam, swearing his Islam was dearer to him than al-Khaṭṭāb's, solely because he was dearer to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so his precedence would benefit him. > **""Accept Islam, for by Allah your accepting Islam is dearer to me than al-Khaṭṭāb's accepting Islam.""**"
"15469","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reported he gave the Prophet ﷺ glad tidings of al-ʿAbbās's Islam, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ freed him."
"15470","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said of al-ʿAbbās that he was the most generous-handed and most joining of kin among Quraysh. > **""This is al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, the most generous-handed of Quraysh and the most joining of kin.""**"
"15471","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه reported that al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib sought the Prophet's ﷺ permission to emigrate, who told him O uncle remain where he was, for Allah would seal emigration with him as He sealed prophethood with him. > **""O uncle, remain in your place, for Allah will seal emigration with you as He sealed prophethood with me.""**"
"15472","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported that al-ʿAbbās had accepted Islam, remained over his watering duty, and did not emigrate."
"15473","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded guarding him concerning al-ʿAbbās, remnant of his forefathers. > **""Guard me concerning al-ʿAbbās, for he is the remnant of my forefathers.""**"
"15474","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded treating his uncle al-ʿAbbās well as remnant of his forefathers, for a man's uncle was like his father. > **""Treat my uncle al-ʿAbbās well, for he is the remnant of my forefathers; a man's uncle is like his father.""**"
"15475","ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه reported that al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib entered the mosque greeting them but perceived dislike in their faces, returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at home asking why dislike met him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque declaring they would not believe in Allah nor be believers till they loved ʿAbbās. > **""O people, you will not believe in Allah nor be believers until you love ʿAbbās.""**"
"15476","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ returned from a campaign on a hot day, water was placed for cooling, al-ʿAbbās came turning his back shading him with his cloak, he asked who it was, they said his uncle al-ʿAbbās O Messenger of Allah, when finished the Prophet ﷺ raised his hands till they appeared from the cloak praying Allah shield his uncle and progeny from the Fire. > **""May Allah shield you, O uncle, and your progeny from the Fire.""**"
"15477","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told his uncle al-ʿAbbās he was the seal of prophets, then raised his hands praying forgiveness for al-ʿAbbās, his sons, and his sons' sons. > **""O Allah, forgive al-ʿAbbās, the sons of al-ʿAbbās, and the sons of the sons of al-ʿAbbās.""**"
"15478","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ghasīl رضي الله عنه reported he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he passed al-ʿAbbās bidding him O uncle follow with his sons, he brought six sons, al-Faḍl, ʿAbdullāh, ʿUbaydullāh, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Qutham and Maʿbad, the Prophet ﷺ entered them into a house covering them with his black shawl striped with red, praying they were his Household and kin to be shielded from the Fire as the shawl shielded them, whereupon every mud-brick and door in the house said amen. > **""O Allah, my Household and my kin, shield them from the Fire as I have shielded them with this shawl.""**"
"15479","Abū Usayd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib not to leave his house with his sons next day till he came for a need with them, they awaited till after forenoon, he entered greeting, asking their morning, they praised Allah well, he bade them draw near till he enfolded them in his mantle praying O Lord this was his uncle and his father's like and these his Household, shield them from the Fire as his mantle shielded them, and the threshold and walls said amen thrice. > **""O Lord, this is my uncle and my father's like, and these are my Household, so shield them from the Fire as I have shielded them with this mantle.""**"
"15480","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that Abū Bakr had a seat with the Prophet ﷺ he vacated only for al-ʿAbbās, delighting the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, one day al-ʿAbbās approached and Abū Bakr yielded, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked why, he said his uncle approached, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked then smiled at Abū Bakr noting al-ʿAbbās approached in white while his descendants after him would wear black and twelve men among them would rule, and when al-ʿAbbās asked what he told Abū Bakr he affirmed only good repeating it, which al-ʿAbbās affirmed with his parents' ransom that he spoke only good. > **""This is al-ʿAbbās approaching in white garments, and his descendants after him shall wear black, and twelve men among them shall rule.""**"
"15481","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥārithah رضي الله عنه reported that when Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah al-Jumaḥī came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he asked O Abā Wahb with whom he lodged, he said with al-ʿAbbās, and he declared him the most loving of Quraysh to Quraysh. > **""With the most loving of Quraysh to Quraysh.""**"
"15482","Abū Razīn رضي الله عنه reported al-ʿAbbās was asked who was greater, he or the Prophet ﷺ, and answered he was greater than him though he was born before him, al-ʿAbbās being older, born three years before the Elephant. > **""He is greater than me, though I was born before him.""**"
"15483","Al-Haytham ibn ʿAdī reported that al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, Ibn Masʿūd, and Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb perished nine years into ʿUthmān's rule, some saying year thirty-four, and ʿUthmān رضي الله عنهما prayed over him."
"15484","It reached him that ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib lost his sight, al-ʿAbbās lost his sight, and ʿAbdullāh ibn al-ʿAbbās lost his sight."
"15485","It reached him that al-ʿAbbās had ten male children besides females, among them al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās, ʿAbdullāh, Qutham, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Maʿbad, and Umm Ḥabīb, their mother Umm al-Faḍl the younger named Lubābah bint al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥazn ibn Qays Ghaylān, early Muslim in Makkah, about whom the poet said no noble woman bore from a stallion on known hill or plain like six from Umm al-Faḍl's womb, honored gray couple, uncle of the chosen Prophet of merit, seal and best of messengers, while al-Ḥārith ibn al-ʿAbbās's mother was Ḥujaylah bint Jundub ibn Rabīʿah of Tamīm ibn Saʿd ibn Hudhayl ibn Mudrikah, his daughter Ummuhu married al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿUtbah ibn Abī Lahab, Ṣafiyyah full sister of al-Ḥārith though some said otherwise, and Kathīr, ʿAwn, Rawḥ, and Tamām ibn al-ʿAbbās the youngest, Tamām full brother of Kathīr, about whom al-ʿAbbās versified they were completed by Tamām to ten, praying his Lord make them noble dutiful, a remembrance and fruitful growth."
"15486","Al-Haytham ibn ʿAdī reported that al-Faḍl ibn al-ʿAbbās perished four years before his father in year twenty-eight, though they differed saying martyred in Syria at Ajnādayn or Marj al-Ṣuffar both in year thirteen, or at Yarmūk in fifteen, or in ʿAmwās plague in eighteen, dying aged twenty-one."
"15487","Al-Ṭabarānī reported that Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib al-Ṭayyār in Paradise رضي الله عنه was called Abū ʿAbdillāh, his mother Fāṭimah bint Asad ibn Hāshim."
"15488","Abū Juḥayfah (رضي الله عنه) reports that Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib (رضي الله عنه) came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Abyssinia, and the Prophet ﷺ kissed him between the eyes and spoke of his joy: > **""I do not know whether I rejoice more at the arrival of Jaʿfar or at the conquest of Khaybar.""**"
"15489","Al-Shaʿbī reports that when news of the conquest of Khaybar reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he was told Jaʿfar had arrived from the Negus. The Prophet ﷺ expressed which joy was greater, and when Jaʿfar came to him he kissed him between the eyes: > **""I do not know at which of the two I rejoice more, the arrival of Jaʿfar or the conquest of Khaybar.""**"
"15490","Jābir (رضي الله عنه) reports that when Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنه) arrived from Abyssinia, the Prophet ﷺ embraced him."
"15491","Jābir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to meet Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib (رضي الله عنه) on his arrival from Abyssinia. Jaʿfar hastened forward out of reverence, and the Prophet ﷺ kissed him between the eyes and addressed him as his beloved, describing his likeness to him and their common clay: > **""O my beloved, you are the people most resembling me in form and character, and you were created from the same clay from which I was created.""**"
"15492","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aslam, freedman of the Prophet ﷺ, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنه): > **""You resemble my form and my character.""**"
"15493","Usāmah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Prophet ﷺ spoke to Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنه) and to ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) concerning their likeness to him and their closeness: > **""Your form is as my form, and your character most resembles my character, so you are from me; and you, O ʿAlī, are from me and the father of my children.""**"
"15494","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib passing with Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl, granted two wings in place of his hands, greeting him and recounting his encounter with the polytheists, for which he was named al-Ṭayyār in Paradise: > **""Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib passed by with Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl, peace be upon them, and he has two wings with which Allah compensated him for his hands; so he greeted me, then informed me of how it was with him when he met the polytheists, and for that Jaʿfar was named al-Ṭayyār in Paradise.""**"
"15495","By the same chain, it is reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat with Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays (رضي الله عنها) nearby when he returned a greeting and told her Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib had passed with Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl and greeted them. Jaʿfar recounted meeting the polytheists, receiving seventy-three wounds on the front of his body, losing first his right hand then his left while holding the banner, and being granted two wings in their place. Asmāʾ asked the Prophet ﷺ to ascend the minbar and tell the people, which he did, praising Allah and announcing Jaʿfar's station: > **""O Asmāʾ, this is Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib with Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl, peace be upon them; they passed and greeted us, and I returned their greeting.""** > **""O people, Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib is with Jibrīl and Mīkāʾīl, and he has two wings with which Allah compensated him for his hands, with which he flies in Paradise wherever he wishes, and he greeted me.""**"
"15496","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he saw Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib in the form of an angel, flying where he wished with wings stained with blood: > **""I saw Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib as an angel flying in Paradise with two wings, flying with them wherever he wished, his forelocks stained with blood.""**"
"15497","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that when news of Jaʿfar's death arrived, the Prophet ﷺ visited Asmāʾ bint ʿUmays (رضي الله عنها), seated Jaʿfar's sons ʿAbd Allāh and Muḥammad upon his thigh, conveyed what Jibrīl had told him of Jaʿfar's martyrdom and wings, and supplicated for his offspring: > **""Jibrīl has informed me that Allah has granted Jaʿfar martyrdom, and that he has two wings with which he flies with the angels in Paradise.""** > **""O Allah, take Jaʿfar's place in caring for his children.""**"
"15498","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Glad tidings to you, O ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar, your father flies with the angels in the heaven.""**"
"15499","Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd reports that the Prophet ﷺ was shown them in sleep: Jaʿfar as an angel with two wings stained with blood, and Zayd opposite him upon a couch."
"15500","Al-Shaʿbī reports that Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنه) was killed on the day of Muʾtah at al-Balqāʾ."
"15501","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, with doubt from the narrator as to the order: > **""ʿAlī is my root and Jaʿfar is my branch — or: Jaʿfar is my root and ʿAlī is my branch.""**"
"15502","Abū Isḥāq reports in mursal form that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAqīl ibn Abī Ṭālib (رضي الله عنه), addressing him by his kunyah and naming kinship and his uncle's love as the two reasons: > **""O Abū Yazīd, I love you with two loves: a love for your kinship, and a love for what I knew of my uncle's love for you.""**"
"15503","Al-Ṭabarānī states that ʿAqīl (رضي الله عنه) was present at the conquest of Khaybar, and the Prophet ﷺ allotted him a share from it."
"15504","Al-Ṭabarānī states that al-Mughīrah Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim (رضي الله عنه) accepted Islam on the day of the Conquest, met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the road, stood firm with him on the day of Ḥunayn, and died in the year twenty."
"15505","Abū Ḥabbah al-Badrī (رضي الله عنه) reports that on the day of Ḥunayn, wherever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked he saw Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith (رضي الله عنه) fighting, and the Prophet ﷺ praised him, with doubt whether he said the best or among the best: > **""Abū Sufyān is the best of my household — or: among the best of my household.""**"
"15506","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq records the full lineage of Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (رضي الله عنه) back through Quḍāʿah, and notes it is said his mother was Suʿād bint Zayd ibn Ṭayyiʾ."
"15507","Ibn Hishām reports that Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām brought Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (رضي الله عنه) from Syria as a servant; his aunt Khadījah (رضي الله عنها), then wife of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, asked for him and then gifted him to the Prophet ﷺ, who freed him and adopted him before revelation. When his father came, the Prophet ﷺ gave him the choice to stay or go, and he chose to stay, remaining until the mission came, believing and praying with him; after the verse calling them by their fathers was revealed, he declared his true lineage: > **""If you wish, remain with me, and if you wish, depart with your father.""**"
"15508","By the same chain, Ibn Isḥāq states that Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (رضي الله عنه) accepted Islam after ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه), the first to do so after him."
"15509","Ibn Shihāb states that the first to accept Islam was Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (رضي الله عنه)."
"15510","Usāmah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنهما) reports that Jaʿfar, ʿAlī and Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (رضي الله عنهم) each claimed to be most beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and went to ask him. Usāmah announced them without naming his father, they were admitted, and when they asked about men after he answered Fāṭimah, he gave each his station: > **""Fāṭimah.""** > **""As for you, O Jaʿfar, your form resembles my form and your character resembles my character, and you are from me and my tree. As for you, O ʿAlī, you are my son-in-law and the father of my children, and I am from you and you are from me. As for you, O Zayd, you are my freedman and from me, and the most beloved of the people to me.""**"
"15511","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that after Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (رضي الله عنه) was struck down, Usāmah ibn Zayd was brought and stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whose eyes filled with tears, so he was taken back; returning the next day, the Prophet ﷺ spoke of the grief renewed at his sight: > **""Shall I meet from you today what I met from you yesterday.""**"
"15512","Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (رضي الله عنه) said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""O Messenger of Allah, you established brotherhood between me and Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.""**"
"15513","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that while the Prophet ﷺ was in the mountain pass, his father came and said, O Muḥammad, I see Umm al-Faḍl is with child. The Prophet ﷺ expressed hope for a boy who would cool their eyes, and when the child was born he was brought wrapped in cloths and given taḥnīk. Mujāhid added that none else is known to have been given taḥnīk with prophetic saliva: > **""Perhaps Allah will cool our eyes with a boy.""**"
"15514","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports from his mother Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith (رضي الله عنها) that as she passed while the Prophet ﷺ was in the Ḥijr, he called her, told her she carried a boy despite Quraysh's pact against births, and bade her bring him when born. She did so; he named him ʿAbd Allāh, gave him taḥnīk with his saliva, and declared he would prove sagacious. She told al-ʿAbbās, who smiled and visited the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ rose to him, kissed him between the eyes, seated him at his right, and praised the uncle's station: > **""O Umm al-Faḍl, you are carrying a boy.""** > **""It is as I tell you; when you deliver him, bring him to me.""** > **""Take him; you will find him sagacious.""** > **""This is my uncle; let whoever wishes boast of his uncle.""** > **""Why should I not say so, when you are my uncle and the remainder of my forefathers? The uncle is a father.""**"
"15515","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand upon his shoulder — Saʿīd, the narrator, doubted whether he said shoulder or upper arm — and supplicated for him: > **""O Allah, grant him understanding in religion and teach him interpretation.""**"
"15516","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed for him and praised him as an interpreter of the Qurʾān, adding that Jibrīl, peace be upon him, had prayed for him twice: > **""What an excellent interpreter of the Qurʾān you are.""**"
"15517","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand upon his head and prayed for wisdom and interpretation, then placed his hand upon his chest so that ʿAbd Allāh felt its coolness, and prayed that his breast be filled with knowledge and forbearance. Thereafter he never felt shy in asking anyone, and remained the scholar of this nation until Allah took him: > **""O Allah, grant him wisdom and teach him interpretation.""** > **""O Allah, fill his breast with knowledge and forbearance.""**"
"15518","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he was with his father in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a man stood privately conversing with him, seeming to turn away from his father. When they left, his father remarked on his cousin's apparent avoidance; Ibn ʿAbbās explained a man had been in private counsel. They returned and asked, and the Prophet ﷺ asked ʿAbd Allāh if he had seen him, then told him the visitor's identity and cause of preoccupation: > **""Did you see him, O ʿAbd Allāh?""** > **""That was Jibrīl, peace be upon him; he is the one who kept me from you.""**"
"15519","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he passed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in white garments while he conversed privately with Diḥyah ibn Khalīfah al-Kalbī — who was in truth Jibrīl, peace be upon him — unknown to him, so he did not greet. Jibrīl asked who he was, the Prophet ﷺ identified his cousin Ibn ʿAbbās, and Jibrīl remarked on the whiteness of his garments, that his offspring would wear black after him, and that had he greeted they would have answered. When Ibn ʿAbbās returned, the Prophet ﷺ asked why he had not greeted; he excused himself for not wishing to interrupt, confirmed he had seen the man, and received tidings that his sight would depart and be restored at his death. ʿIkrimah added that at his funeral a radiant bird entered his shroud, which he forbade them to disturb as the Prophet's glad tidings, and that those at the grave edge heard the verses of the reassured soul: > **""This is my cousin; this is Ibn ʿAbbās.""** > **""O Ibn ʿAbbās, what kept you from greeting?""** > **""Did you indeed see? Your sight shall depart, and it shall be returned to you at your death.""**"
"15520","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that al-ʿAbbās sent him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a need; finding a man with him, he withdrew without speaking. The Prophet ﷺ later asked if he had seen him, confirmed the visitor, and foretold his future: > **""Did you see him? That was Jibrīl; he shall not die until his sight departs and he is granted knowledge.""**"
"15521","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away, he invited a man to join him in learning from the Prophet's Companions, who were many; the man marvelled that people would ever need Ibn ʿAbbās while so many Companions lived. Ibn ʿAbbās persisted, pursuing the Companions for every ḥadīth he heard they had heard, even resting his cloak at a door while wind blew dust upon his face until the man emerged asking what the Prophet's cousin wanted, offering to come if summoned, while Ibn ʿAbbās insisted he was worthier to come. When the Companions passed on and people needed him, that same man admitted his greater knowledge."
"15522","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Maysarah reports from Ṭāwūs that he sat with seventy — or eighty — elders among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and whenever any of them met Ibn ʿAbbās in disagreement, he would concede the word was as Ibn ʿAbbās said, or say, you spoke the truth."
"15523","Abū Bakr al-Hudhalī reports that he visited al-Ḥasan and remarked on Ibn ʿAbbās's station with the Qurʾān, whereupon al-Ḥasan quoted ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) describing him as the youth of elders, with a questioning tongue and an understanding heart, who would stand upon their minbar on the eve of ʿArafah reciting Sūrat al-Baqarah and Sūrat Āl ʿImrān and expounding them verse by verse, an overflowing vessel of knowledge."
"15524","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that Heraclius wrote to Muʿāwiyah (رضي الله عنه) asking, if any trace of prophethood remained among them, for answers concerning the Milky Way, the bow, and the patch of land the sun struck but for a single hour. Muʿāwiyah confessed he had never expected such questions, folded the letter, and sent it to Ibn ʿAbbās, who wrote back identifying each: > **""The bow is security for the people of the earth against drowning; the Milky Way is the gate of heaven from which it splits open; and as for the patch the sun struck for but an hour of the day, it is the sea that was parted for the Children of Isrāʾīl.""**"
"15525","Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Muzāḥim al-Hilālī reports that Nāfiʿ ibn al-Azraq and Najdah ibn ʿUwaymir set out with leaders of the Khawārij seeking knowledge until they reached Makkah, where they found ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) seated near Zamzam in a red cloak and shirt, with people standing and asking him concerning exegesis. Nāfiʿ challenged his boldness in answering; Ibn ʿAbbās retorted that bolder still was one who spoke without knowledge or concealed what he knew. Nāfiʿ conceded and began his questions, each time asking the meaning of a Qurʾānic word and whether the Arabs knew it before revelation, Ibn ʿAbbās assigning each meaning and citing a pre-Islamic verse as witness — shuwāẓ as smokeless flame, nuḥās as smokeless smoke, amshāj as the mingled male and female fluids, the joining of leg with leg as war, sons and grandsons as those who serve and attend, the bewitched as the created, mulīm as the sinful, al-falaq as the dawn, grief as sorrow, return as coming back, scalding water at utmost heat, the dark night, the darkness of night, the sustainer as the powerful, the night's advancing darkness, the guarantor, wheat, divining arrows, the companions of the left, seas made to overflow, paths, majesty exalted, worn out, heedless diversion, gathering, the One sought in all needs, recompense, the silent, a sound, slaying by permission — and finally whether divorce was known in the Jāhiliyyah, to which Ibn ʿAbbās answered yes, an irrevocable triple divorce, citing the verses of al-Aʿshā divorcing his wife thrice before her brothers."
"15526","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the last part of the night and prayed behind him; the Prophet ﷺ took his hand and drew him to stand alongside him, but when the Prophet ﷺ turned to his prayer, Ibn ʿAbbās withdrew behind. When the prayer ended, the Prophet ﷺ asked his reason; Ibn ʿAbbās answered that none ought to pray alongside him while he was the Messenger Allah had honoured. This pleased him, and he prayed that Allah increase him in knowledge and understanding: > **""What is the matter with you — I place you alongside me and you withdraw?""**"
"15527","Ibn Abī Mulaykah reports that he witnessed Ibn al-Zubayr say to Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما), do you remember when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met us coming from a journey? Ibn ʿAbbās answered yes: he carried me and a boy of Banū Hāshim and left you."
"15528","Ibn Buraydah al-Aslamī reports that when a man reviled Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما), he answered that despite his three traits: whenever he came upon a verse of Allah's Book he wished all people knew what he knew; whenever he heard of a Muslim ruler judging justly he rejoiced though he might never come before him; and whenever he heard of rain falling upon a Muslim land he rejoiced though he owned no grazing livestock there."
"15529","Ḥassān ibn Thābit (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Anṣār needed something from the governor, a difficult matter, so they walked to him with men of Quraysh and others who spoke to him and reminded him of the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ counsel concerning them. He pleaded the difficulty; the people excused him, but Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) persisted until, by Allah, the governor found no escape from fulfilling the need. As they left and entered the mosque with groups gathered, Ḥassān laughed as he heard them, declaring Ibn ʿAbbās foremost in it, a remnant of prophethood and heir of Aḥmad ﷺ, guided by his veins. When the people bade him compose, Ibn ʿAbbās assented, and Ḥassān extolled him in verse: when Ibn ʿAbbās's face appears you see his merit in every assembly; when he speaks he leaves no word for another, with gathered utterances admitting no gap; he suffices and heals what is in breasts, leaving no jest or earnest for any eager speaker; he rose to the heights without hardship and attained their peaks, neither base nor intruding; created an ally of manliness and bounty, eloquent, never dull nor feeble-minded — whereupon the governor protested that by the dull and feeble he meant none but himself, with Allah between them."
"15530","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) died in the year sixty-eight at seventy-two years, dyeing his beard yellow. He said: I was born three years before the Hijrah while we were in the mountain pass, and the Prophet ﷺ passed away when I was thirteen."
"15531","Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit reports that he saw Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) with a full head of flowing hair."
"15532","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq reports that Ibn ʿAbbās ʿAbd Allāh (رضي الله عنهما) was tall, fair with a reddish-yellow tinge, handsome and bright-faced, with flowing locks."
"15533","Abū Isḥāq reports that he saw Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) during the days of Minā with long hair, wearing a waist-wrapper trailing somewhat and a yellow cloak."
"15534","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed away when he was fifteen years old and already circumcised."
"15535","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr reports that Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) died at al-Ṭāʾif and they attended his funeral, when a bird of never-seen form came and entered his bier and was never seen emerging; and when he was buried, this verse was recited at the grave's edge though none knew who recited it: > **""O reassured soul, return to your Lord well-pleased and pleasing, enter among My servants and enter My Paradise.""**"
"15536","It is likewise reported from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yāmīn from his father, except that he said a white bird came, called al-ghurnūq."
"15537","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would line up ʿAbd Allāh, ʿUbayd Allāh and Kathīr, the sons of al-ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهم), and promise a gift to whoever reached him first; they would race to him and fall upon his back and chest, and he would embrace and kiss them: > **""Whoever beats the others to me shall have such-and-such.""**"
"15538","It is reported of ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنهم) that they pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when they were seven years old; when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw them he smiled, extended his hand, and accepted their pledge."
"15539","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he recalled himself with Qutham and ʿUbayd Allāh, the sons of al-ʿAbbās, as playing boys when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by riding; he bade them lift one child to him, so ʿAbd Allāh was carried in front, then bade them lift Qutham, who was carried behind — though ʿUbayd Allāh was dearer to al-ʿAbbās than Qutham, yet he felt no shyness before his uncle in carrying Qutham and leaving him. Then he wiped ʿAbd Allāh's head thrice, each time praying to succeed Jaʿfar in his children. Asked later what became of Qutham, he said he was martyred; and to the remark that Allah and His Messenger knew best the good, he assented: > **""Lift this one to me.""** > **""O Allah, take Jaʿfar's place in caring for his children.""**"
"15540","ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنهما) while he traded as boys trade — or as children trade — and prayed for blessing upon his dealing, or upon his bargain: > **""O Allah, bless him in his trade — or: in his bargain.""**"
"15541","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr states that in that year — meaning the year eighty — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib (رضي الله عنه) died at Madīnah, his kunyah being Abū Jaʿfar."
"15542","Ibn ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Usāmah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنهما), people spoke against him until it reached the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon he declared what had reached him of their words about Usāmah as before about his father, affirmed thrice his fitness for command, and named him most beloved to him, some excepting Fāṭimah and others: > **""What you have said about Usāmah has reached me, and you said the same about his father before him; he is fit for command, he is fit for command, he is fit for command, and he is the most beloved of people to me.""**"
"15543","In another narration the words are that he is the most beloved of all people to him, while Ibn ʿUmar would say, excepting Fāṭimah (رضي الله عنها). > **""He is the most beloved of all people to me.""**"
"15544","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that after hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say it, none ought to hate Usāmah (رضي الله عنه): > **""Whoever loves Allāh and His Messenger, let him love Usāmah.""**"
"15545","Abū Bakr ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Ḥabḥāb reports that he heard their elders say the inscription on the ring of Usāmah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنهما) was: beloved of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15546","Al-Zuhrī reports that Usāmah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنهما) was addressed as commander until he died, people saying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had dispatched him and never removed him until he passed away."
"15547","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq states that he is ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Shamkh ibn Makhzūm ibn Ṣāhilah ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Tamīm ibn al-Hudhayl ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyās ibn Muḍar ibn Nizār ibn Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān, an ally of Banū Zuhrah, who witnessed Badr."
"15548","In another narration his line runs through Makhzūm ibn Kāhil ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Saʿd ibn Hudhayl, allies of Banū Zuhrah."
"15549","Aḥmad ibn Rushdīn al-Miṣrī reports that Mūsā ibn ʿAwn dictated to him the lineage of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd ibn Kāhil ibn Ḥabīb ibn Thābit ibn Makhzūm ibn Ṣāhilah ibn Kāhil ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Tamīm ibn Saʿd ibn Hudhayl ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyās ibn Muḍar ibn Nizār."
"15550","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that he recalls being the sixth of six, with no other Muslim upon the earth besides them."
"15551","Qays ibn Marwān reports that a man came to ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) at ʿArafah saying he had left a man at Kūfah dictating the codices from memory. ʿUmar swelled with anger until near filling the saddle's sides, demanding who he was; told it was ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, his anger subsided until he returned to his former state, declaring none worthier of it and recounting how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would spend the night conversing with Abū Bakr over Muslim affairs, ʿUmar with them, until they came upon a man standing in prayer in the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood listening to his recitation, and when they nearly knew the man, he praised reciting fresh as revealed upon the reading of Ibn Umm ʿAbd. Then the man sat supplicating, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade him ask and be given. ʿUmar swore he would return to give him the tidings, but in the morning found Abū Bakr had already done so, as ever outstripping him to every good: > **""Whoever is pleased to recite the Qurʾān fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the reading of Ibn Umm ʿAbd.""** > **""Ask, you shall be given.""**"
"15552","In another narration ʿUmar went to Ibn ʿAbd Allāh to gladden him but found Abū Bakr coming out, and remarked that in such deeds he was ever foremost in good."
"15553","ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) — reports that Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) gladdened him with the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ words: > **""Whoever is pleased to recite the Qurʾān fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the reading of Ibn Umm ʿAbd.""**"
"15554","ʿAbd Allāh (رضي الله عنه) reports through Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) that they gladdened him with the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ words to him: > **""Ask, you shall be given.""**"
"15555","Abū Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever loves to recite the Qurʾān fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the reading of Ibn Umm ʿAbd.""**"
"15556","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever loves to recite the Qurʾān fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the reading of Ibn Umm ʿAbd.""**"
"15557","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah reports that while Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) supplicated in the mosque, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed with Abū Bakr (رضي الله عنه); drawing near, he heard the supplication without recognising him and asked who he was, bidding him ask and be given. Abū Bakr returned to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd and asked what supplication he had made; he said he had praised and glorified Allah, then declared His promise, meeting, Book, prophets, Muḥammad ﷺ, Paradise, Fire and messengers all true: > **""Who is this? Ask, you shall be given.""** > **""O Allah, there is no god but You; Your promise is true, meeting You is true, Your Book is true, the prophets are true, Muḥammad ﷺ is true, Paradise is true, the Fire is true, and Your messengers are true.""**"
"15558","Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that while he supplicated in the mosque, the Prophet ﷺ passed with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما); drawing near, he heard the supplication without recognising him and bade him ask and be given. Abū Bakr returned asking what supplication he had made; he said he had praised and glorified Allah, then affirmed His promise, meeting, Paradise, Fire, messengers, prophets and Muḥammad ﷺ all true: > **""Ask, you shall be given.""** > **""O Allah, there is no god but You; Your promise is true, meeting You is true, Paradise is true, the Fire is true, Your messengers are true, the prophets are true, and Muḥammad ﷺ is true.""**"
"15559","Mujāhid reports that Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) asked which of the two readings was later, that of ʿAbd Allāh or that of Zayd. Told Zayd's, he corrected them: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would review the Qurʾān with Jibrīl, peace be upon him, once each year, but in the year he passed away he reviewed it with him twice, and the final reading was the reading of ʿAbd Allāh."
"15560","ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) — reports that he recited seventy chapters before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and completed the Qurʾān before the best of people, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (رضي الله عنه)."
"15561","ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ ordered Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) up a tree to fetch something; when his companions saw ʿAbd Allāh's legs as he climbed and laughed at their thinness, the Prophet ﷺ rebuked their laughter and declared the weight of his leg on the Day of Judgement: > **""What are you laughing at? The leg of ʿAbd Allāh shall be heavier in the Balance on the Day of Judgement than Uḥud.""**"
"15562","Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that he would gather siwāk from an arāk tree, his legs being thin, when the wind overturned his garment and the people laughed at him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked what they laughed at; told at the thinness of his legs, he swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that in the Balance they would outweigh Uḥud — in some wordings, that the two legs of Ibn Masʿūd on the Day of Judgement shall be firmer and greater than Uḥud: > **""What are you laughing at? By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, they are heavier in the Balance than Uḥud.""**"
"15563","Qurrah ibn Iyās reports that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) climbed a tree to gather siwāk from it and placed his feet upon it, whereupon the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed at the thinness of his legs, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared their weight in the Balance: > **""They are heavier in the Balance than Uḥud.""**"
"15564","Abū al-Ṭufayl reports that Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) went with some people to the kabāth fruit, climbed a tree to gather from it, and they looked at his legs and laughed at their thinness. The Prophet ﷺ asked what they laughed at; told at the thinness of Ibn Masʿūd's legs, he swore they outweighed Uḥud. Then each man went and gathered ripe fruit to eat, while Ibn Masʿūd brought his gathering in his lap, placed it before the Prophet ﷺ, and recited his verses declaring it his harvest with its best within, every gatherer's hand to his mouth, and the Prophet ﷺ ate from it: > **""What are you laughing at? By Allah, they are heavier in the Balance than Uḥud.""**"
"15565","Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out for his need and he met him bringing water. Asked who had ordered him to do so, he answered that no one had, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ praised him and gave him glad tidings of Paradise; then ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) came and he gave him the same tidings: > **""Who ordered you to do this? You have done well; receive glad tidings of Paradise.""**"
"15566","Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that he never lied after accepting Islam except for a single lie: he used to saddle for the Prophet ﷺ, and a man from al-Ṭāʾif asked him which manner of saddling the Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved most. He answered the Ṭāʾifī inverted style, though the Prophet disliked it. When it was brought thus, the Prophet ﷺ asked who had saddled it; told it was his saddler, he ordered that Ibn Umm ʿAbd do the saddling, and the task was returned to him: > **""Who saddled this? Tell Ibn Umm ʿAbd to saddle.""**"
"15567","Abū al-Dardāʾ (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a light sermon, then bade Abū Bakr rise and address, who fell short of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; then ʿUmar, who fell short of both; then a certain man, who spoke with affectation, so the Prophet ﷺ bade him be silent — or sit — for affectation is from Satan and eloquence is magic. Then he bade Ibn Umm ʿAbd rise; he praised Allah, declared their Lord, religion, scripture, qiblah and Prophet, pointing to the Prophet ﷺ, and professed pleasure with what Allah and His Messenger pleased and hatred of what they hated, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ affirmed him twice and declared his own pleasure and hatred in accord for himself, his nation and Ibn Umm ʿAbd: > **""Be silent — or sit — for affectation is from Satan, and eloquence is magic.""** > **""O Ibn Umm ʿAbd, rise and address.""** > **""Ibn Umm ʿAbd is right, Ibn Umm ʿAbd is right and truthful; I am pleased with what Allah, Most High, is pleased with for me, my nation and Ibn Umm ʿAbd, and I hate what Allah, Most High, hates for me, my nation and Ibn Umm ʿAbd.""**"
"15568","ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) — reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he was pleased for his nation with what Ibn Umm ʿAbd was pleased with for them, and hated for them what he hated: > **""I am pleased for my nation with what Ibn Umm ʿAbd is pleased with for them, and I hate for my nation what Ibn Umm ʿAbd hates for them.""**"
"15569","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that only four remained with the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Uḥud, one of them ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه)."
"15570","Al-Ḥasan reports that a man asked ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (رضي الله عنه) whether one whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died loving was not righteous; he agreed. The man noted the Prophet ﷺ died loving ʿAmr and had employed him. ʿAmr answered that his employment proved nothing of love or mere use, but he would name two men the Prophet ﷺ died loving: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنهما); in al-Ṭabarānī's wording, that he died well-pleased with them both."
"15571","Zayd ibn Wahb reports that while they sat with ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه), ʿAbd Allāh (رضي الله عنه) arrived, so short that sitting people nearly equalled him. ʿUmar laughed upon seeing him, conversed and joked with him as he stood over him, then followed him with his gaze until he vanished, and described him as a vessel brimful of jurisprudence."
"15572","Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib reports that ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) wrote to the people of Kūfah that he had sent ʿAmmār as commander and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd as minister, both eminent among the companions of Muḥammad ﷺ and veterans of Badr, bidding them follow and heed them, and declaring he had preferred them with ʿAbd Allāh over himself."
"15573","Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim reports that he saw Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) clean and tidy."
"15574","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه), kunyah Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, died at Madīnah aged past sixty, in the year thirty-two; he bequeathed to al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām (رضي الله عنه), who prayed over him, and he was buried at al-Baqīʿ."
"15575","Al-Zuhrī states that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) did not emigrate earlier than his brother ʿUtbah (رضي الله عنه), though he died before him."
"15576","Al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān states that ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) died in the time of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (رضي الله عنهما)."
"15577","Al-Layth ibn Saʿd states that ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) died in the year forty-four."
"15578","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq states that ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه), his father and his mother were all a household of Islam."
"15579","Ibn Hishām records that he is ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir ibn ʿAbs ibn Zayd ibn Madhḥij, who witnessed Badr and all the scenes after it; his mother's name is said to be Sumayyah bint Salam ibn Lakhm; his kunyah Abū al-Yaqẓān; and he was killed alongside ʿAlī (رضي الله عنهما) on the day of Ṣiffīn in the year thirty-seven."
"15580","ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ reports that Abū Salamah and Umm Salamah (رضي الله عنهما) migrated, and ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) went out with them, being their ally."
"15581","Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam reports that he asked ʿAṭṭāf ibn Khālid whether ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) had been their ally, and he answered: rather, our mawlā."
"15582","Abū Kaʿb al-Ḥārithī reports that he entered upon ʿUthmān (رضي الله عنه) when a dark, bald man arrived with a few hairs at the front of his head; asked who he was, they said ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه)."
"15583","Kulayb ibn Manfaʿah reports from his father that he saw ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه) at al-Kunāsah, dark-skinned and curly-haired, reciting the verse that among His signs is creating them from dust and then spreading as human beings."
"15584","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salamah reports that he saw ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) on the day of Ṣiffīn, dark-complexioned and tall, with a spear in his hand."
"15585","Muṭarrif reports that he entered upon ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) while a tailor with him cut a cloak upon a fox-fur rug."
"15586","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reports that a man addressed ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) as O mutilated one, his ear having been cut off alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon ʿAmmār answered him: > **""The best of my two ears you have reviled.""**"
"15587","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salamah reports that ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) met two men emerging from the bath anointed, asked who they were; they claimed to be Emigrants, and he answered that they lied — the true Emigrant was ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه)."
"15588","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he never saw the like of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr (رضي الله عنهم): neither loved that Allah be disobeyed for the blink of an eye, nor that truth be opposed by a hair's breadth."
"15589","Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd reports that ʿUthmān (رضي الله عنه) summoned some companions of the Prophet ﷺ, among them ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه), adjured them whether the Prophet ﷺ preferred Quraysh over others and Banū Hāshim over Quraysh; they fell silent, and he declared that had he the keys of Paradise he would have given them to Banū Umayyah till the last entered. Then he sent for Ṭalḥah and al-Zubayr (رضي الله عنهما) and recounted walking hand in hand with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-Baṭḥāʾ until they passed ʿAmmār's father and mother under torture; Abū ʿAmmār asked, O Messenger of Allah, shall time remain thus? The Prophet ﷺ bade patience and prayed for the family of Yāsir, whose forgiveness was already effected: > **""Be patient.""** > **""O Allah, forgive the family of Yāsir — and indeed You have done so.""**"
"15590","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say to Abū ʿAmmār, Umm ʿAmmār and ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنهم): > **""Be patient, family of Yāsir; your appointment is Paradise.""**"
"15591","ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Be patient, family of Yāsir; your appointment is Paradise.""**"
"15592","Jābir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed by ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and his family while they were tortured for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and said: > **""Rejoice, family of Yāsir; your appointment is Paradise.""**"
"15593","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ lay reclined in ʿAmmār's lap when a man entered and asked what the polytheists had just said about him — meaning ʿAmmār. The Prophet ﷺ, his head still in ʿAmmār's lap, passed his hand behind his back until he embraced him and declared their safekeeping of good skin: > **""They guard a good hide.""**"
"15594","Al-Ḥasan reports that ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه) would say he fought alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against jinn and men: sent to the well of Badr, he met Satan in human form, wrestled and threw him, and crushed him with a stone he carried. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced that ʿAmmār had met Satan at the well and fought him; when ʿAmmār returned and reported, he confirmed the adversary: > **""ʿAmmār met Satan at the well and fought him. That was Satan.""**"
"15595","Khālid ibn al-Walīd (رضي الله عنه) reports that harsh words passed between him and ʿAmmār, so ʿAmmār went complaining to the Prophet ﷺ, and Khālid followed to complain likewise. Khālid persisted harshly while the Prophet ﷺ remained silent; ʿAmmār wept asking did the Messenger not see, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ raised his head and declared Allah's enmity and hatred toward his enemies, and Khālid thereafter prized nothing above ʿAmmār's pleasure until they met and he was pleased: > **""Whoever opposes ʿAmmār, Allāh opposes him; and whoever hates ʿAmmār, Allāh hates him.""**"
"15596","Khālid ibn al-Walīd (رضي الله عنه) reports that no deed weighed on him with greater fear of Hell than the affair of ʿAmmār: dispatched by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with companions to an Arab clan, he seized them though they included a Muslim household; ʿAmmār interceded for some, demanding their release, while Khālid refused until he brought them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to release or judge as he willed. Entering upon the Prophet ﷺ, ʿAmmār complained of Khālid's deed, and Khālid retorted that were it not for his gathering, Ibn Sumayyah would not have reviled him. The Prophet ﷺ ordered ʿAmmār out, and he left weeping that the Messenger had not aided him against Khālid; asked why he had not answered the man, Khālid pleaded only contempt had restrained him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ warned that contempt, reviling and disparagement of ʿAmmār return upon their doer from Allah. Khālid followed him until ʿAmmār sought forgiveness for him; in one narration, whoever opposes ʿAmmār is opposed by Allah: > **""Go out, O ʿAmmār. Did you not answer the man? Whoever belittles ʿAmmār, Allāh belittles him; whoever reviles ʿAmmār, Allāh reviles him; and whoever disparages ʿAmmār, Allāh disparages him.""**"
"15597","The batch provides no Arabic matn or Urdu rendering for this number, recording only its chapter on the virtues of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) and his household with a citation to al-Ṭabarānī's al-Muʿjam al-Kabīr."
"15598","In al-Awsaṭ the wording is confined to the threat and hatred returned for reviling and hating ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه): > **""Whoever reviles ʿAmmār, Allāh reviles him; and whoever hates ʿAmmār, Allāh hates him.""**"
"15599","Al-Ḥasan reports that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (رضي الله عنه) said they never deemed that one whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died loving would enter the Fire. Told the Prophet ﷺ had employed him, he answered Allah knew best, but he had loved a man; asked who, he named ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه). Al-Ṭabarānī adds the rejoinder that he was their slain one at Ṣiffīn, answered with an oath that they had indeed killed him."
"15600","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak of the dust-covered, garmentless ones whose oath Allah fulfils if they swear by Him, naming ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) among them: > **""How many a dust-covered one with two worn cloths and no garment, whose oath Allāh would fulfil if he swore by Him — among them is ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir.""**"
"15601","Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz reports that ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) swore on the day of Uḥud and the polytheists were routed, swore on the day of al-Jamal and the people of Baṣrah were overcome; told on the day of Ṣiffīn to swear, he answered that though driven by swords to the fronds of Hajar they knew themselves upon truth and their foes upon falsehood, so he did not swear and was killed that day. On Uḥud his oath had invoked Jibrīl and Mīkāl that no misguided host would overcome them while they stood upon truth against the ignorant, until he pierced the polytheists' rank."
"15602","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said of Ibn Sumayyah (رضي الله عنه) that whenever two courses were offered him, he always chose the more guided of the two: > **""Ibn Sumayyah — never were two courses offered him but he chose the more guided of the two.""**"
"15603","ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say of ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه): > **""The blood of ʿAmmār and his flesh are forbidden for the Fire to taste.""**"
"15604","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that she could speak of any companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ if she wished, except ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه), for she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say of him: > **""He is filled with faith to his very bones.""**"
"15605","Bilāl ibn Yaḥyā reports that when ʿUthmān (رضي الله عنه) was killed, Ḥudhayfah (رضي الله عنه) was approached amid people's disagreement; asked to be propped against a man's chest, he declared he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that Abū al-Yaqẓān remained upon the primordial nature, never abandoning it until death or dotage: > **""Abū al-Yaqẓān is upon the fitrah; he shall not abandon it until he dies or dotage touches him.""**"
"15606","Ḥudhayfah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded following the two after him, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما), guidance by ʿAmmār's guidance, and holding fast to the covenant of Ibn Umm ʿAbd (رضي الله عنه): > **""Follow the two after me: Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allāh be pleased with them both; be guided by the guidance of ʿAmmār; and hold fast to the covenant of Ibn Umm ʿAbd.""**"
"15607","A freedwoman of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) reports that he fell gravely ill and fainted; waking to find them weeping around him, he asked why they wept — did they suppose he would die upon his bed? His beloved ﷺ had told him the rebellious party would kill him, and that his final provision would be a draught of milk."
"15608","Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf reports that he heard ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) at Ṣiffīn on the day he was struck, calling out that he had met the Compeller, wed the wide-eyed maidens, and would today meet the beloved Muḥammad ﷺ and his party, as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had covenanted that his final provision of this world would be thin milk: > **""Today we meet the beloved, Muḥammad, and his party; my final provision of this world is thin milk.""**"
"15609","In Aḥmad's narration, when the milk was brought to him, he laughed."
"15610","Abū Rāfiʿ (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه): > **""The rebellious party will kill you.""**"
"15611","Abū Ayyūb (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The rebellious party shall kill ʿAmmār.""**"
"15612","Abū al-Yusr ibn ʿAmr and Ziyād ibn al-ʿArd (رضي الله عنهما) both heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه): > **""The rebellious party shall kill you.""**"
"15613","The daughter of Hishām ibn al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, who nursed ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه), reports that Muʿāwiyah (رضي الله عنه) visited him in illness, and upon leaving said, O Allah, make not his death by our hands, for he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The rebellious party shall kill ʿAmmār.""**"
"15614","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (رضي الله عنه) reports that they carried bricks for the mosque one by one while ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه) carried two by two, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dusted the earth from his shoulder and said: > **""Woe to you, O Ibn Sumayyah! The rebellious party shall kill you.""**"
"15615","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (رضي الله عنه) also reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered them to build the mosque, and they carried brick by brick while ʿAmmār carried two by two; his companions — not he himself directly from the Prophet ﷺ — reported that he said: > **""O Ibn Sumayyah, the rebellious party shall kill you.""**"
"15616","Abū Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ built the mosque, men carried one stone while ʿAmmār carried two, and one brick while he carried two, and then he mentioned the same warning."
"15617","Ḥabbah reports that Ḥudhayfah and Abū Masʿūd (رضي الله عنهما) gathered, one reminding the other that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said the rebellious party would kill ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه), and the other affirming him: > **""The rebellious party shall kill ʿAmmār.""**"
"15618","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal reports that he heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ and Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān (رضي الله عنهم) say the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said to ʿAmmār (رضي الله عنه) that the rebellious party would kill him; Muʿāwiyah retorted, mocking childishness, did we kill him — only he who brought him killed him: > **""The rebellious party shall kill you.""**"
"15619","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (رضي الله عنهما) reports that two men came to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (رضي الله عنه) disputing over ʿAmmār's blood and spoils, and he told them to leave it, for he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The killer of ʿAmmār and his despoiler are in the Fire.""**"
"15620","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The killer of ʿAmmār and his despoiler are in the Fire.""**"
"15621","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith reports that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (رضي الله عنه) asked Muʿāwiyah (رضي الله عنه) whether he had heard, when the mosque was built, the Prophet's ﷺ words to ʿAmmār that he was keen upon jihād, among the people of Paradise, yet the rebellious party would kill him; he answered yes, and to the question why then they killed him, returned the same retort that they had not killed him but he who brought him had: > **""You are keen upon jihād, and you are among the people of Paradise, yet the rebellious party shall surely kill you.""**"
"15622","Hāniʾ, freedman of ʿAmr, reports that he accompanied Muʿāwiyah and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (رضي الله عنهما) at Ṣiffīn, surveyed the slain that day and found ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) killed. Going to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, he asked what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning ʿAmmār; he answered that he had heard him tell ʿAmmār the rebellious party would kill him. Told that they had killed him, he denied it and demanded to be shown; standing over him and asked his judgement, he answered that only his companions had killed him: > **""The rebellious party shall kill you.""**"
"15623","Abū al-Bakhtarī and Maysarah report that ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) fought at Ṣiffīn without falling, repeatedly coming to ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) asking whether this was the appointed day, and told to go away, until milk was brought and he drank, declaring the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said it was the last draught he would drink of this world; then he rose, fought and was killed."
"15624","Ḥudhayfah (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, striking ʿAmmār's side, declare he would not die until the rebellious party, turned aside from truth, killed him, his final provision a draught of milk: > **""You shall not die until the rebellious party, turned aside from truth, kills you; your final provision of this world shall be a draught of milk.""**"
"15625","Abū Sinān al-Duʾalī, a companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that he saw ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) summon his boy for drink; brought a cup of milk, he drank and declared Allah and His Messenger truthful — today he would meet the beloved Muḥammad ﷺ and his party, as the Messenger ﷺ had said his final provision would be thin milk — then swore that though driven back to the fronds of Hajar, they knew themselves upon truth and their foes upon falsehood."
"15626","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ struck his flank and said it was a believing flank, that the rebellious party would kill him, his final provision thin milk: > **""A believing flank; the rebellious party shall kill you, and your final provision is thin milk.""**"
"15627","Kulthūm ibn Jabr reports that at Wāsiṭ al-Qaṣab, in the house of ʿAnbasah ibn Saʿīd with ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Kurayz al-Qurashī, a man announced ʿAmmār's killer at the door seeking entry; some disliked it, some bade admit him, and there entered a man in patched garments declaring he had reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while benefiting his family herding sheep. Asked by one of the company, Abū al-Ghādiyah, how it was with ʿAmmār, he said they counted ʿAmmār among their best until hearing him revile ʿUthmān (رضي الله عنه) in the mosque of Qubāʾ, wishing to trample him, praying after every prayer to be brought face to face with him, until at Ṣiffīn a man leading a battalion met him, they exchanged two blows, he hastened and struck him down upon his face, then killed him."
"15628","In another narration ʿAbd al-Aʿlā bade admit him, and there entered a tall, striking man as though not of this nation. The like of the account follows, until at Ṣiffīn he advanced on foot at the head of the battalion between the two ranks, speared a man in the knee and felled him, his helmet fell back — strike, and behold the head of ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (رضي الله عنه). A mawlā remarked which hand would suffice him, and the narrator never saw a man of clearer misguidance."
"15629","Kurdūs reports that Khabbāb (رضي الله عنه) accepted Islam as the sixth of six — a sixth of Islam."
"15630","Al-Zuhrī reports that Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt (رضي الله عنه), freedman-ally of Zuhrah, kunyah Abū ʿAbd Allāh, died in the year thirty-seven as ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) returned from Ṣiffīn to Kūfah; he was the first of the Prophet's ﷺ companions buried at Kūfah, and his Islam had been at Makkah."
"15631","ʿUrwah, naming those who witnessed Badr, lists Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt ibn Khuwaylid ibn Saʿd ibn Juzaymah ibn Kaʿb ibn Saʿd (رضي الله عنه)."
"15632","Zayd ibn Wahb reports that they travelled with ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) returning from Ṣiffīn until at Kūfah's gate they saw seven graves to their right. Asked whose they were, they were told Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt (رضي الله عنه) had died after his departure for Ṣiffīn and willed burial outside Kūfah, whereas people had buried their dead in courtyards and doorways; seeing his will, others buried there too. ʿAlī (رضي الله عنه) invoked mercy upon Khabbāb — accepting Islam eagerly, migrating obediently, living as a striver, tried in his body, whose reward Allah never wastes — then approached the graves, greeted the believing dwellings as predecessors soon to be followed, prayed forgiveness and forbearance for all, and blessed whoever intends the return, works for the best, contents himself with sufficiency and is pleased with Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"15633","Sahl ibn Saʿd (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he entered Paradise and heard a movement, looked, and behold it was Bilāl (رضي الله عنه): > **""I entered Paradise and heard a movement; I looked, and behold it was Bilāl.""**"
"15634","Abū Umāmah (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he entered Paradise and heard a footfall before him; asking Jibrīl what it was, he was told Bilāl walked before him: > **""I entered Paradise and heard a footfall before me; I said, O Jibrīl, whose footfall is this? He said, Bilāl walks before you.""**"
"15635","Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when he was taken by night, he heard a rustling in Paradise and asked Jibrīl, who told him it was Bilāl (رضي الله عنه); Abū Bakr (رضي الله عنه) exclaimed, would that Bilāl's mother and father had borne him, and he were like Bilāl: > **""When I was taken by night in Paradise, I heard a rustling; I said, O Jibrīl, what is this rustling? He said, this is Bilāl.""**"
"15636","Zayd ibn Arqam (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Bilāl (رضي الله عنه) was an excellent man and chief of the witnesses, and that muezzins shall be longest-necked of people on the Day of Judgement: > **""What an excellent man Bilāl is; he is the chief of the witnesses, and the muezzins shall be the longest-necked of people on the Day of Judgement.""**"
"15637","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that on the night the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was taken by night, he entered Paradise and heard a faint sound; asking Jibrīl who it was, he was told it was Bilāl the muezzin (رضي الله عنه). When he returned to the people he announced that Bilāl had succeeded, having seen such-and-such for him, and mentioned the ḥadīth: > **""O Jibrīl, who is this? Bilāl has succeeded; I saw such-and-such for him.""**"
"15638","Ibn ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that he gave Bilāl (رضي الله عنه) glad tidings, who asked what tidings he bore. He answered that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe Bilāl coming on the Day of Judgement upon a camel with feet of gold and a bridle of pearl and ruby, bearing a banner followed by the muezzins, admitting them to Paradise, even all who called adhān for forty mornings seeking Allah's Face, Blessed and Exalted: > **""Bilāl shall come on the Day of Judgement upon a camel whose feet are of gold and whose bridle is of pearl and ruby, with a banner, followed by the muezzins, and he shall admit them to Paradise, even whoever called adhān for forty mornings seeking thereby the Face of Allāh, Blessed and Exalted.""**"
"15639","Abū Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Bilāl's parable is that of the bee, which goes out eating of sweet and bitter, yet all becomes sweet: > **""The parable of Bilāl is as the bee: it goes out eating of the sweet and the bitter, yet it is all sweet.""**"
"15640","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports that Bilāl (رضي الله عنه), freedman-ally of Abū Bakr (رضي الله عنه) — and it is said his coeval — died of plague at Damascus and was buried at Bāb al-Ṣaghīr; his kunyah was Abū ʿAbd Allāh, also said Abū ʿAmr, in the year seventeen, and he was of those born in servitude."
"15641","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, naming those of Quraysh of Banū ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manāf who witnessed Badr, lists Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfah (رضي الله عنهم)."
"15642","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (رضي الله عنه) reports that when alarm struck Madīnah, he came upon Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfah (رضي الله عنهما) wrapped in his sword-belt, took his own sword and wrapped himself likewise, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked why their resort had not been to Allah and His Messenger, and bade them act as these two believing men had acted: > **""O people, why was your resort not to Allāh and His Messenger? Did you not act as these two believing men acted?""**"
"15643","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Prophet ﷺ heard Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfah (رضي الله عنهما) reciting by night and said: > **""Praise be to Allāh, who has placed in my nation one like him.""**"
"15644","ʿUrwah, naming those martyred on the day of Yamāmah, includes him — thus al-Ṭabarānī narrates in the biography of Sālim (رضي الله عنه)."
"15645","Al-Ṭabarānī states that ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah (رضي الله عنه), freedman of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (رضي الله عنه), was among the first Emigrants, migrated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr from Makkah to Madīnah, was a veteran of Badr, and was martyred on the day of Biʾr Maʿūnah."
"15646","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (رضي الله عنه) reports that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh (رضي الله عنه) spoke harshly to ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah (رضي الله عنه), so the Prophet ﷺ restrained him, reminding him that ʿĀmir had witnessed Badr as he had, and that the best are best to their freedmen: > **""Gently, O Ṭalḥah; he witnessed Badr as you witnessed it, and the best of you are the best of you to their freedmen.""**"
"15647","Al-Zuhrī reports that Ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah related to him, being among the elders of Banū ʿAdī, and that his father had witnessed Badr (رضي الله عنه)."
"15648","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq records that those who trace him to ʿItr ibn Wāʾil name him ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah ibn Kaʿb ibn ʿUmayrah ibn Mālik ibn Kinānah ibn ʿĀmir ibn Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Rufaydah ibn ʿAdnān, also said Ṭāhir ibn Rabīʿah of Yemen."
"15649","ʿUrwah states that Ibn Rabīʿah (رضي الله عنه) was of Yemen, and those tracing him to Yemen name him ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah ibn Kaʿb ibn ʿUmayrah ibn Mālik ibn Kinānah ibn Khuzaymah ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAbs ibn Zayd ibn ʿAkkah ibn Madhḥij."
"15650","Muṣʿab ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Zubayrī states that ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah (رضي الله عنه) died in the year thirty-two."
"15651","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah reports that when people embroiled themselves in tribulation, Rabīʿah (رضي الله عنه) would pray by night, was shown in a dream and told to rise and ask Allah to shield him from the tribulation from which He shielded His righteous servants; so he rose, prayed, then fell ill and never left except as a bier."
"15652","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (رضي الله عنه) reports that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaḥsh (رضي الله عنه) said to him on the day of Uḥud, will you not supplicate Allah? Withdrawing aside, Saʿd prayed: O Lord, when I meet the enemy, bring me a man of fierce strength and fury whom I fight for You and who fights me, then grant me victory to slay him and take his spoil — and ʿAbd Allāh said āmīn. Then ʿAbd Allāh prayed: O Allah, grant me a man of fierce fury and strength whom I fight for You and who fights me, then seizes me and cuts off my nose and ears, so that when I meet You tomorrow and You ask who cut my nose and ears, I answer, for You and Your Messenger ﷺ, and You say, you spoke truly. Saʿd said, O my son, ʿAbd Allāh's supplication proved better than mine; at day's end I saw him with his nose and ears hanging by a thread."
"15653","Zayd ibn Thābit (رضي الله عنه) reports that when ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (رضي الله عنه) was buried, Umm al-ʿAlāʾ said, be glad, Abū al-Sāʾib, you are in Paradise. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard her and asked who she was; she answered, and to his question what made her know, she named ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn. He affirmed they had seen only good, yet declared himself Allah's Messenger who knew not what would be done with him: > **""Who is this? What makes you know? I am the Messenger of Allāh, and by Allāh I do not know what shall be done with me.""**"
"15654","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that when ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (رضي الله عنه) died, his wife said, Paradise be yours. The Prophet ﷺ looked at her with anger and asked what made her know; she answered, your horseman and companion. He swore he knew not what would be done with him, which weighed heavily upon the Companions for one so excellent; then when Ruqayyah daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, he bade her join their predecessor: > **""What makes you know? By Allāh, I do not know what shall be done with me. Join our predecessor ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn.""**"
"15655","Al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ (رضي الله عنه) reports that when ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (رضي الله عنه) died the Muslims grieved for him; then when Ibrāhīm son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died, he said: > **""Join our righteous predecessor ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn.""**"
"15656","Ibn ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that when anyone died, the Prophet ﷺ would say, send him forth to our vanguard; and the best vanguard for his nation was ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (رضي الله عنه): > **""Send him forth to our vanguard; what an excellent vanguard for my nation is ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn.""**"
"15657","Anas ibn Mālik (رضي الله عنه) reports that when Ruqayyah daughter of the Prophet ﷺ died, he said: > **""Join our righteous predecessor ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn.""**"
"15658","ʿĀʾishah bint Maẓʿūn (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Prophet ﷺ kissed ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (رضي الله عنه) upon the cheek after he died, and they knew of none else he kissed."
"15659","Ibn ʿAbbās (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Prophet ﷺ visited ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn (رضي الله عنه) the day he died, bent over him as though confiding, then raised his head with traces of weeping in his eyes; bent a second time and was seen weeping; bent a third time and raised his head sobbing, and they knew he had died, so the people wept. The Prophet ﷺ bade them hush, for that was from Satan, and to seek Allah's forgiveness, then addressed the departed as having left this world unentangled: > **""Hush; this is from Satan, so seek Allāh's forgiveness. Depart, O Abū al-Sāʾib, for you have left and never entangled yourself with anything of it.""**"
"15660","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (رضي الله عنهما) reports that Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah (رضي الله عنه) wrote to the people of Makkah informing them the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to raid them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was guided to the woman bearing the letter, sent to her, and took the letter from her head. Asked if he had done it, Ḥāṭib admitted it, denying treachery or hypocrisy, affirming Allah would manifest His Messenger, explaining only that as a sojourner among them with his mother there, he sought favour with them. ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) asked to strike his neck, and the Prophet ﷺ answered, would you kill a man of Badr — who knows, perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and bade them do as they wished: > **""O Ḥāṭib, did you do it? Would you kill a man of Badr? Who knows — perhaps Allāh looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do whatever you wish.""**"
"15661","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah (رضي الله عنه) was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked if he had written the letter. He answered yes, swearing faith had never departed his heart, but every Qurashī had kin at Makkah guarding his family, so he wrote hoping Allah would thereby guard his own. ʿUmar, may Allah have mercy on him, sought leave concerning him; the Prophet ﷺ asked if he would have killed him, and upon his assent, reminded him of Badr: > **""Did you write this letter? Would you have killed him? Who knows — perhaps Allāh looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do whatever you wish.""**"
"15662","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (رضي الله عنه) reports that Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah (رضي الله عنه) wrote to Makkah, Allah informed His Prophet ﷺ, who sent ʿAlī and al-Zubayr (رضي الله عنهما) after the letter. They overtook the woman on her camel, extracted it from her braids, brought it, and it was read to the Prophet ﷺ. Ḥāṭib was summoned and confessed; asked his motive, he swore sincere counsel to Allah and His Messenger, pleading only strangerhood at Makkah with his family among them, fearing for them, writing what could harm Allah and His Messenger nothing while perchance benefiting his folk. ʿUmar drew his sword and sought leave to strike his neck for disbelief, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered, O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who knows — perhaps Allah looked upon this band of Badr and declared forgiveness: > **""O Ḥāṭib, did you write this letter? What moved you to it? O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, who knows? Perhaps Allāh looked upon this band of the people of Badr and said: Do whatever you wish, for I have forgiven you.""**"
"15663","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah reports from his father that while with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a veteran of Badr, he wrote to the disbelieving Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned ʿAlī and al-Zubayr (رضي الله عنهما), bade them overtake a woman bearing a letter and bring it. They met her, demanded the letter, warning they would not turn back till every garment upon her was searched; she asked were they not two Muslims, they affirmed yet insisted the Messenger ﷺ had told them of the letter, so despairing escape she loosed it from her head and handed it over. Ḥāṭib was summoned, the letter read to him, he acknowledged it, and pleaded children and kin there and strangerhood among Quraysh. ʿUmar sought leave to kill Ḥāṭib, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reminded him of Badr and divine forgiveness: > **""Go until you overtake a woman bearing a letter, and bring it to me. Do you know this letter? What moved you to it? He witnessed Badr, and you know not — perhaps Allāh looked upon the people of Badr and said: Do whatever you wish, for I forgive you.""**"
"15664","Umm Mubashshir (رضي الله عنها) reports that Ḥāṭib's boy came saying, by Allah, Ḥāṭib shall never enter Paradise, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared him lying, for Ḥāṭib had witnessed Badr and Ḥudaybiyah: > **""You lie; he witnessed Badr and al-Ḥudaybiyah.""**"
"15665","Ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the nations were shown to him at the season of pilgrimage, then his own nation passed and their multitude filling plain and mountain pleased him. Asked was he pleased, O Muḥammad, he answered yes; told that with them were seventy thousand entering Paradise without reckoning — those who seek no ruqyah, draw no omens, use no cauterisation, and trust in their Lord — ʿUkkāshah (رضي الله عنه) rose asking the Prophet ﷺ to pray Allah place him among them, and he prayed for him; then another rose asking likewise, and he answered that ʿUkkāshah had outstripped him: > **""Are you pleased, O Muḥammad? With them are seventy thousand entering Paradise without reckoning: those who seek no ruqyah, draw no omens, use no cauterisation, and trust in their Lord. ʿUkkāshah has outstripped you to it.""**"
"15666","Abū Maysarah reports that Ayman (رضي الله عنه) was in charge of the Prophet's ﷺ purification vessel, while Thaʿlabah assisted him in his needs."
"15667","By the same chain from Abū Maysarah, Saʿd (رضي الله عنه) reports that he once saw Ayman (رضي الله عنه) fleeing combat, and perceived displeasure on the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ face. Later, when battle was joined, Saʿd saw Ayman restrain the enemy, which pleased the Prophet ﷺ. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (رضي الله عنه) remarked he had been told Ayman would not stand between the ranks from cowardice, and Ayman answered that he hoped to take a stand loved by Allah and His Messenger; ʿUmar affirmed he was worthy to do so."
"15668","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the foremost are four: he the foremost of the Arabs, Ṣuhayb foremost of Rome, Salmān foremost of Persia, and Bilāl foremost of Abyssinia: > **""The foremost are four: I am the foremost of the Arabs, Ṣuhayb is the foremost of Rome, Salmān is the foremost of Persia, and Bilāl is the foremost of Abyssinia.""**"
"15669","Abū Umāmah (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say he was the foremost of the Arabs to Paradise, Ṣuhayb of Rome, Bilāl of Abyssinia, and Salmān of Persia: > **""I am the foremost of the Arabs to Paradise, Ṣuhayb is the foremost of Rome to Paradise, Bilāl is the foremost of Abyssinia to Paradise, and Salmān is the foremost of Persia to Paradise.""**"
"15670","Umm Hāniʾ (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the foremost are four: he of the Arabs, Salmān of Persia, Ṣuhayb of Rome, and Bilāl of Abyssinia: > **""The foremost are four: I am the foremost of the Arabs, Salmān is the foremost of Persia, Ṣuhayb is the foremost of Rome, and Bilāl is the foremost of Abyssinia.""**"
"15671","Ṣuhayb (رضي الله عنه) reports that he accompanied the Prophet ﷺ before revelation came to him."
"15672","ʿIkrimah, freedman of Ibn ʿAbbās, reports that Ṣuhayb (رضي الله عنه) ransomed himself from his folk with half his wealth, then set out migrating; overtaken on the road, he surrendered the remainder of his wealth and went on."
"15673","Ṣuhayb (رضي الله عنه) reports that Abū Bakr (رضي الله عنه) passed with a captive for whom he sought amnesty from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while Ṣuhayb sat in the mosque. Asked who accompanied him, Abū Bakr answered a polytheist captive for whom he sought the Prophet's ﷺ protection; Ṣuhayb remarked there was room at his neck for the sword. The Prophet ﷺ perceived anger and asked its cause; told what Ṣuhayb had said, he asked had Abū Bakr harmed him, and upon his oath of denial, warned that harming him would harm Allah and His Messenger: > **""Perhaps you harmed him? Had you harmed him, you would have harmed Allāh and His Messenger.""**"
"15674","Ṣuhayb (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never attended a scene but he was present, never raided early or late but he stood at his right or left, never took a pledge but he attended it, never dispatched a detachment but he joined it; if they feared ahead he was ahead, if behind he was behind, and he never placed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ between himself and the enemy until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away."
"15675","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (رضي الله عنهما) reports that when ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) was stabbed, he ordered Ṣuhayb (رضي الله عنه), freedman of Banū Judʿān, to lead the people in prayer."
"15676","Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah states that al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad (رضي الله عنه) is Abū ʿAmr."
"15677","Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq records that he is al-Miqdād ibn ʿAmr ibn Thaʿlabah ibn Rabīʿah ibn Thumāmah ibn Maṭrūd ibn ʿAmr ibn Saʿd ibn Zuhayr ibn Thawr ibn Thaʿlabah ibn Mālik ibn Hazl ibn Qābis ibn Ruwaym ibn al-Qayn ibn al-Hūn ibn Bahz ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥāf ibn Quḍāʿah, attributed to al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth ibn Wahb ibn ʿAbd Manāf ibn Zuhrah because he adopted and allied him. He was pot-bellied, dark, dyeing his beard yellow, aquiline and long-nosed; died at Madīnah aged seventy, and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (رضي الله عنه) prayed over him."
"15678","ʿUthmān — as al-Ṭabarānī states — records that al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad ibn ʿAmr (رضي الله عنه) was a Badr veteran, kunyah Abū Maʿbad, also said Abū ʿAmr, ally of Banū Zuhrah, his lineage disputed, an early Emigrant, may Allah have mercy on him."
"15679","Hammām ibn al-Ḥārith reports that he saw al-Miqdād (رضي الله عنه), and he was stout."
"15680","Al-Zuhrī states that al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad (رضي الله عنه) was of Kindah."
"15681","Sufyān ibn Ṣahbānah al-Mahrī reports that he accompanied al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad (رضي الله عنه) in the Jāhiliyyah: a man of Bahz, he shed blood among them and fled to Kindah, allying with them; then shed blood among them and fled to Makkah, allying with al-Aswad ibn ʿAbd Yaghūth."
"15682","Anas ibn Mālik (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Paradise longs for four: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, Salmān al-Fārisī and al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad (رضي الله عنهم): > **""Paradise longs for four: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, Salmān al-Fārisī and al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad.""**"
"15683","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports that on the day of Ḥudaybiyah the Prophet ﷺ said, leave me, then went with the sacrificial animals and slaughtered them — or as narrated — whereupon al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad (رضي الله عنه) swore they would not be as the chiefs of Israel who told Mūsā, go you and your Lord and fight, we sit here; rather go you and your Lord and fight, and we shall fight with you both. Qatādah added that seventy camels accompanied them that day."
"15684","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports that al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad (رضي الله عنه) died at al-Jurf, men bearing him upon their necks to Madīnah in the year thirty-three; ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (رضي الله عنه) prayed over him; his kunyah was Abū Maʿbad, aged about seventy."
"15685","Abū ʿUbaydah Maʿmar ibn al-Muthannā states that ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān (رضي الله عنه), kunyah Abū ʿAbd Allāh, also said Abū Ghazwān, was tall and handsome; died in the year seventeen while bound for Baṣrah the second time, buried by some waters, aged fifty-five, an ally of Banū Nawfal ibn ʿAbd Manāf."
"15686","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports that ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān (رضي الله عنه) died in the year seventeen on the Baṣrah road as ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb's governor (رضي الله عنه), aged fifty-seven — also said twenty — he who founded Baṣrah, parcelled its dwellings, built its mosque, conquered al-Ubullah, governing Baṣrah six months under ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (رضي الله عنه)."
"15687","Al-Ṭabarānī states that Saʿd ibn Muʿādh al-Anṣārī, then al-Ashhalī (رضي الله عنه), veteran of Badr and Uḥud, kunyah Abū ʿAmr, was martyred on the day of the Trench, as already given with chains concerning Badr."
"15688","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (رضي الله عنه) reports that while they sat with the Prophet ﷺ, Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه) arrived, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""This is your chief.""**"
"15689","Al-Mājishūn reports that Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه) said: in three things I am not weak as in others: whatever I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ I knew to be truth; never did I pray a prayer diverting myself to other thoughts until finishing; nor did I follow a bier without reflecting upon what its bearer says and what is said to him."
"15690","In another narration the third is: nor did I attend a dead man without reflecting upon what he says and what is said to him."
"15691","Ibn ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said seventy thousand angels descended for the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه), never having trodden earth before; and when he was buried he exclaimed in glorification that had any escaped the grave's pressure, Saʿd would have escaped: > **""Seventy thousand angels descended for the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, who never trod the earth before. Glory be to Allāh — had any escaped the pressure of the grave, Saʿd would have escaped it.""**"
"15692","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""The Throne shook for the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh.""**"
"15693","Rumaythah (رضي الله عنه) reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — so near him he could have kissed the Seal between his shoulders — say to Saʿd ibn Muʿādh the day he died: > **""The Throne of the Merciful shook for him.""**"
"15694","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that returning from pilgrimage or ʿumrah, they were met at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah where Anṣārī youths met their families, and Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr (رضي الله عنه) was informed of his wife's death, covered himself and wept. She reproached him — a companion of the Messenger ﷺ of precedence weeping for a woman — and he uncovered his head, affirming she spoke truly, yet by his life he ought to weep for none after Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, concerning whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had spoken; asked what he had said, he quoted the shaking of the Throne for Saʿd's passing, as he walked between her and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""The Throne shook for the passing of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh.""**"
"15695","Al-Ṭabarānī narrates from ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from a journey he would halt at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, children coming out to inform them of their families; Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr (رضي الله عنه) was told of his wife's death and wept. Asked did he weep, he answered why should he not, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the supports of the Throne shook for Saʿd's death: > **""The supports of the Throne shook for the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh.""**"
"15696","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd ibn al-Sakan (رضي الله عنها) reports that when Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه) died — in Aḥmad's wording, when his bier was brought out — his mother cried aloud, and the Prophet ﷺ comforted her, bidding her tears cease and grief depart, for her son was the first whom Allah met smiling and for whom the Throne shook: > **""Let your tears cease and your grief depart; your son is the first whom Allāh met smiling, and for whom the Throne shook.""**"
"15697","Muʿayqīb (رضي الله عنه) reports from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""The Throne shook for the death of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh.""**"
"15698","Saʿd — meaning Ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (رضي الله عنه) — reports that as the bier of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه) passed, the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The Throne shook for him.""**"
"15699","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that when Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه) died, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (رضي الله عنهما) wept till each one's weeping was known from the other's; asked whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wept, she answered no, but he grasped his beard ﷺ."
"15700","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the bier of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه) with tears flowing upon his beard."
"15701","ʿUṭārid reports that a brocade garment gifted by Kisrā was presented to the Prophet ﷺ; his Companions entered asking had it descended from heaven, and he marvelled at their wonder, declaring a handkerchief of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's handkerchiefs in Paradise better, then bade a boy carry it to Abū Jahm ibn Ḥudhayfah demanding the khamīṣah in return: > **""What do you marvel at? A handkerchief of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's handkerchiefs in Paradise is better than this. O boy, take it to Abū Jahm ibn Ḥudhayfah and tell him to send me the khamīṣah.""**"
"15702","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reports that Ukaydir of Dūmah sent the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a sundus cloak; he wore it, the people marvelled, and he swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that Saʿd ibn Muʿādh's handkerchiefs in Paradise were better. Then he gifted it to ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه), who protested that the Prophet disliked it yet he should wear it; he answered he sent it only that ʿUmar send it onward and gain wealth thereby — this before silk was forbidden: > **""Do you marvel at this? By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, the handkerchiefs of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh in Paradise are better than it. O ʿUmar, I sent it to you only that you send it onward and gain wealth thereby.""**"
"15703","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that three Anṣār, all of Banū ʿAbd al-Ashhal, had none counted above them in merit after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr and ʿAbbād ibn Bishr (رضي الله عنهم)."
"15704","Umm Saʿd bint Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ (رضي الله عنها) reports that she entered upon Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (رضي الله عنه), who spread a garment for her to sit upon; then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (رضي الله عنه) entered asking, O Successor of the Messenger of Allah, who is she? He answered, she is the daughter of one better than me and you, save the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — a man taken in the Prophet's ﷺ lifetime, occupying his seat in Paradise, while we remained."
"15705","Al-Ṭabarānī narrates that Usayd (رضي الله عنه) witnessed al-ʿAqabah as a chief and Badr, as already given."
"15706","ʿĀʾishah (رضي الله عنها) reports that Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr (رضي الله عنه) was among the most excellent of men, saying that were he to remain in any of three states he would be of Paradise without doubt: when reciting the Qurʾān or hearing it recited, when hearing the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ sermon, and when attending a bier — never attending one without reflecting upon what is done with it and whither it goes."
"15707","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports that Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr (رضي الله عنه), kunyah Abū Yaḥyā, died in the year twenty; ʿUmar (رضي الله عنه) bore him between the bier's poles until laid at al-Baqīʿ, and prayed over him (رضي الله عنه)."
"15708","It is reported of Muʿādh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) that while ill he spat to his right — or intended to spit — and remarked that he had never spat to his right since accepting Islam."
"15709","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that Muʿādh (رضي الله عنه) was a nation, devout to Allah, upright, submitted, never of the polytheists. When a companion cited Ibrāhīm, he said he had not forgotten, then asked did they know what a nation was — they did not — and answered, one who teaches people good; asked did they know the devout, they did not, and answered, obedient to Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"15710","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (رضي الله عنه) reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Take the Qurʾān from four: from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Sālim freedman of Abū Ḥudhayfah.""**"
"15711","Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Muʿādh ibn Jabal is the foremost of the scholars by a rank.""**"
"15712","Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reports that he heard Anas ibn Mālik (رضي الله عنه) say Muʿādh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) died aged twenty-eight — another saying thirty-two — and that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Muʿādh was the foremost of scholars by a rank; Ibn Bukayr adds that al-ratwah means a station: > **""Muʿādh is the foremost of the scholars by a rank.""**"
"15713","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Muʿadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه died in the plague of ʿAmawas in the year seventeen or eighteen."
"15714","Saʿid ibn al-Musayyab reported that Muʿadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه passed away when he was thirty-three or thirty-four years old."
"15715","Yaḥya ibn Saʿid reported that Muʿadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه died when he was twenty-eight years old, while the one who traces his lineage upward says he was thirty-two."
"15716","It is noted that al-Ṭabarani narrated that Muʿadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه witnessed the battle of Badr."
"15717","Abu Ḥabbah al-Badri رضي الله عنه reported that when the verses, Those who disbelieved among the People of the Book, up to its end, were revealed, Jibril عليه السلام said, O Messenger of Allah, your Lord commands you to have Ubayy recite it. The Prophet ﷺ then said to Ubayy, Jibril عليه السلام has commanded me to have you recite this surah. Ubayy asked, Have I been mentioned there, O Messenger of Allah. He replied, Yes. Thereupon Ubayy wept. > **""Jibril عليه السلام has commanded me to have you recite this surah""**"
"15718","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O Abu al-Mundhir, I have been commanded to present the Qurʾan to you. He replied, Through Allah I have believed, at your hands I submitted, and from you I learned. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeated his statement, and Ubayy asked, O Messenger of Allah, and was I mentioned there. He said, Yes, by your name and lineage in the highest assembly. Ubayy then said, Then recite, O Messenger of Allah. > **""O Abu al-Mundhir, I have been commanded to present the Qurʾan to you""**"
"15719","In another narration Ubayy reported that he presented the Qurʾan to the Prophet ﷺ, who said, Jibril عليه السلام commanded me to have you present the Qurʾan to me. > **""Jibril عليه السلام commanded me to have you present the Qurʾan to me""**"
"15720","In another narration Ubayy رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, I have been commanded to have you recite the Qurʾan. > **""I have been commanded to have you recite the Qurʾan""**"
"15721","It was previously mentioned concerning the virtue of Muʿadh ibn Jabal that the Prophet ﷺ said, Take the Qurʾan from four, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb. > **""Take the Qurʾan from four, from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb""**"
"15722","ʿAmir al-Shaʿbi reported that six men among the Anṣar collected the Qurʾan during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Zayd ibn Thabit, Abu Zayd, Muʿadh ibn Jabal, Abu al-Dardaʾ, Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, and Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, رضي الله عنهم, while Jāriyah ibn Majmaʿ had recited all of it except a surah or two."
"15723","Masruq reported that the authorities in judicial matters among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were six: ʿUmar, ʿAli, ʿAbdullah, Ubayy, Zayd, and Abu Musa, رضي الله عنهم."
"15724","ʿUtayy al-Saʿdi reported that he saw Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه white in his head and beard, and he had not dyed them."
"15725","Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh reported that there was cheerfulness in the hospitality of Ubayy رضي الله عنه."
"15726","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه died during the caliphate of ʿUmar in the year twenty-two, while some say during the caliphate of ʿUthman, and one saying states in the year thirty during the caliphate of ʿUthman."
"15727","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه whose kunyah was Abu al-Mundhir, died at Madinah in the year twenty-two."
"15728","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the voice of Abu Ṭalḥah is harder upon the polytheists than a troop. > **""The voice of Abu Ṭalḥah is harder upon the polytheists than a troop""**"
"15729","In another narration the Prophet ﷺ said that the voice of Abu Ṭalḥah in the army is better than a troop. > **""The voice of Abu Ṭalḥah in the army is better than a troop""**"
"15730","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that Abu Ṭalḥah recited Surat Baraʾah, and when he came to the verse, March forth, light and heavy, he said, I do not see my Lord but mobilizing me, young and old, so equip me. His sons said to him, You fought with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he passed away, and you fought with Abu Bakr until he died, and you fought with ʿUmar, so we shall fight on your behalf. He said, Equip me. He then boarded the sea and died, and they found no island in which to bury him except after seven days, yet he had not changed."
"15731","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that Abu Ṭalḥah went out raiding in the sea and died in the ship. They found no place to bury him, so they waited with him six days until after seven they found a place to bury him, and he had not changed as he was."
"15732","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Abu Ṭalḥah, Zayd ibn Sahl رضي الله عنه died in the year thirty-four, and ʿUthman ibn ʿAffan, رضي الله عنهما, prayed over him, while his age was seventy years."
"15733","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that Abu Ṭalḥah, Zayd ibn Sahl, died in the year thirty-four, and ʿUthman ibn ʿAffan prayed over him, and he died at seventy years of age, while it is said that Abu Ṭalḥah died in the year thirty-two."
"15734","Ibn Shihab, in naming those among the Anṣar who witnessed Badr, then from Banu al-Najjar, listed Ḥarithah ibn al-Nuʿman, and he is the one who passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was with Jibril عليه السلام at the seats."
"15735","Muḥammad ibn Isḥaq, in naming those who witnessed Badr, listed Ḥarithah ibn Nuʿman ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmir ibn Sawad ibn Kaʿb, and Kaʿb is Ẓafar, ibn al-Khazraj ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Aws."
"15736","ʿAʾishah رضي الله عنها reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said, I entered Paradise and heard recitation therein. I asked, Who is this. They said, Ḥarithah ibn al-Nuʿman. Thus is devotion, thus is devotion. And he was dutiful to his mother. > **""I entered Paradise and heard recitation therein; thus is devotion, thus is devotion""**"
"15737","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmir ibn Rabiʿah reported that Ḥarithah ibn al-Nuʿman said, I passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while Jibril عليه السلام was with him, sitting in the seats. I greeted him and then passed on. When I returned and the Prophet ﷺ departed, he said, Did you see who was with me. I said, Yes. He said, He is Jibril عليه السلام, and he returned your greeting. > **""He is Jibril عليه السلام, and he returned your greeting""**"
"15738","Musa ibn ʿUqbah reported that Abu Salamah narrated from the man who passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was conversing privately with Jibril عليه السلام. Abu Salamah claimed that he kept away from approaching the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fearing to hear his conversation. In the morning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, What prevented you from greeting when you passed by me last night. He said, I saw you conversing privately with a man, so I feared you would dislike my approaching you both. He said, Do you know who the man was. He said, No. He said, Jibril عليه السلام, and had you greeted he would have returned the greeting. And I heard from other than Abu Salamah that he was Ḥarithah ibn al-Nuʿman. > **""What prevented you from greeting when you passed by me last night""**"
"15739","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmir ibn Rabiʿah reported that Ḥarithah ibn al-Nuʿman said, I passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while Jibril عليه السلام was with him, seated in the seats. I greeted him and then passed on. When I returned and the Prophet ﷺ departed, he said, Did you see who was with me. I said, Yes. He said, He is Jibril عليه السلام, and he returned your greeting. > **""He is Jibril عليه السلام, and he returned your greeting""**"
"15740","Musa ibn ʿUqbah reported that Abu Salamah narrated from the man who passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was conversing privately with Jibril عليه السلام. Abu Salamah claimed that he kept away from approaching the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fearing to hear his conversation. In the morning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, What prevented you from greeting when you passed by me last night. He replied, I saw you conversing privately with a man, so I feared you would dislike my approaching you both. He said, Do you know who the man was. He said, No. He said, Jibril عليه السلام, and had you greeted he would have returned the greeting. And I heard from other than Abu Salamah that he was Ḥarithah ibn al-Nuʿman. > **""Jibril عليه السلام, and had you greeted he would have returned the greeting""**"
"15741","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, reported that Ḥarithah ibn al-Nuʿman passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while Jibril عليه السلام was conversing privately with him, and he passed without greeting. Jibril عليه السلام said, What prevented him from greeting. Had he greeted I would have returned it. Then he said, Yet he is among the eighty. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, What are the eighty. He said, The people will flee from you except eighty who will stand firm with you; their provision and their children's provision is upon Allah in Paradise. When Ḥarithah returned he greeted, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, Did you not greet when you passed. He said, I saw a person with you and disliked interrupting your conversation. He asked, And did you see him. He said, That is Jibril عليه السلام. And he informed him of what Jibril عليه السلام had said. > **""Had he greeted I would have returned it""**"
"15742","Ibn ʿAbbas, رضي الله عنهما, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O company of Anṣar, who is your chief. They said, Al-Jadd ibn Qays, though we deem him miserly. He said, He is not your chief, but your chief is ʿAmr ibn al-Jamuḥ, and he was generous. > **""O company of Anṣar, who is your chief""**"
"15743","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Who is your chief, O Banu Salamah. They said, Al-Jadd ibn Qays, though we deem him miserly. He said, Rather your chief is the fair white one, ʿAmr ibn al-Jamuḥ. And Ibn al-Jamuḥ used to host a feast for the Messenger of Allah whenever he married. > **""Who is your chief, O Banu Salamah""**"
"15744","Kaʿb ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said, Who is your chief, O Banu Salamah. They said, Al-Jadd ibn Qays, though we account him miserly. He said, And what disease is worse than miserliness. Rather your chief is the curly-haired fair one, ʿAmr ibn al-Jamuḥ. > **""And what disease is worse than miserliness""**"
"15745","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O Banu Salamah, who is your chief today. They said, Al-Jadd ibn Qays, though we deem him miserly. He said, And what disease is worse than miserliness. But your chief is ʿAmr ibn al-Jamuḥ. > **""And what disease is worse than miserliness""**"
"15746","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه who was present at that, reported that ʿAmr ibn al-Jamuḥ came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, tell me, if I fight in the path of Allah until I am killed, shall I walk with this foot of mine sound in Paradise, and his leg was lame. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Yes. He was killed on the day of Uḥud, he and his nephew and a freedman of theirs. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him and said, As if I see him walking with this leg of his sound in Paradise. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then ordered concerning them and their freedman, and they were placed in a single grave. > **""Yes""**"
"15747","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Who is your chief, O Banu ʿUbayd. They said, Al-Jadd ibn al-Qays, though there is miserliness in him. He said, And what disease is worse than miserliness. Rather your chief is Bishr ibn al-Baraʾ ibn Maʿrur. > **""And what disease is worse than miserliness""**"
"15748","Kaʿb ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said, Who is your chief, O Banu Salamah. They said, Jadd ibn Qays, though we account him miserly. He said, And what disease is worse than miserliness. They said, Then who is our chief, O Messenger of Allah. He said, Bishr ibn al-Baraʾ ibn Maʿrur. > **""And what disease is worse than miserliness""**"
"15749","Ibn Shihab reported, concerning those who witnessed al-ʿAqabah among the Anṣar, then from Banu Salamah: Bishr ibn al-Baraʾ ibn Maʿrur, and he is the one who ate with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from the sheep in which poison was placed on the day of Khaybar."
"15750","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, رضي الله عنهما, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, May Allah have mercy upon my brother ʿAbdullah ibn Rawahah; wherever prayer overtook him, he made his camel kneel. > **""May Allah have mercy upon my brother ʿAbdullah ibn Rawahah""**"
"15751","ʿAʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ sat on the minbar on Friday, and when he sat he said, Sit. ʿAbdullah ibn Rawahah heard the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Sit, so he sat among Banu Ghunm. It was said, O Messenger of Allah, that is Ibn Rawahah sitting among Banu Ghunm; he heard you saying to the people, Sit, so he sat in his place. > **""Sit""**"
"15752","Muḥammad ibn Isḥaq, in naming those among the Anṣar who witnessed Badr, then from Banu al-Khazraj, listed Abu al-Yusr, Kaʿb ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAbbad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ghunm ibn Sawad ibn Ghunm ibn Kaʿb ibn Salamah ibn ʿAli."
"15753","Abu al-Yusr, Kaʿb ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنه reported: By Allah, I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Khaybar one evening when sheep belonging to a Jewish man approached, heading for their fortress while we were besieging them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Which man will feed us from these sheep. I said, I will, O Messenger of Allah. He said, Then do so. He said: I went out running like an ostrich, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me turning away he said, O Allah, grant us enjoyment through him. He said: I caught up with the sheep after the first of them had entered the fortress, so I took two sheep from the last of them and clasped them under my arms, then came back running as though I carried nothing, until I cast them before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they slaughtered them and ate them. And Abu al-Yusr was among the last of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to perish; when he narrated this ḥadith he wept, then said, Grant me enjoyment, by my life, until I was the last of them. > **""O Allah, grant us enjoyment through him""**"
"15754","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Abu al-Yusr, Kaʿb ibn ʿAmr, died in the year fifty-five at Madinah, and he was the last of the people of Badr to die."
"15755","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that Abu al-Yusr, Kaʿb ibn ʿAmr, died in the year fifty-five at Madinah."
"15756","Jabir ibn ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥaram, رضي الله عنهما, reported that his father ordered a ḥarirah to be made, so he made it, then his father ordered him and he carried it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said to me, What is this, O Jabir, is it meat. I said, No, O Messenger of Allah, but my father ordered me to make a ḥarirah, so I made it, then he ordered me and I carried it. He said, Put it down. I came to my father, who asked, What did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to you. I said, He said to me, What is this, O Jabir, is it meat. My father said, I think the Messenger of Allah ﷺ desires meat. He stood to a domestic animal and slaughtered it, then ordered it roasted, then ordered me and I brought it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, May Allah reward you, O company of Anṣar, with good, and especially the family of ʿAmr ibn Ḥaram and Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah. > **""May Allah reward you, O company of Anṣar, with good""**"
"15757","ʿAʾishah, Mother of the Believers رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Jabir, Shall I not give you glad tidings, O Jabir. He said, Yes, O Messenger of Allah, with good. He said, Allah gave life to your father and seated him before Him, and said, Wish of Me whatever you will and I shall give it to you. He said, O Lord, we did not worship You as You deserve to be worshipped; I wish of You to return me to the world so I may fight with Your Prophet and be killed once more. He said to him, It has already preceded from Me that you shall not return to it. > **""Shall I not give you glad tidings, O Jabir""**"
"15758","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that his father and his uncle were martyred while his father had a debt. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to me and said, O Jabir, shall I not give you tidings of a glad tiding from Allah and His Messenger. Allah, Blessed and Exalted, gave life to your father and your uncle and presented to them, and they asked their Lord to return them to the world. He said, Is it after what I have decreed in the Book that they shall not be returned to it. > **""O Jabir, shall I not give you tidings of a glad tiding from Allah and His Messenger""**"
"15759","Usamah ibn Zayd, رضي الله عنهما, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Banu al-Muṣṭaliq, the son of ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy stood and drew his sword against his father, saying, It is binding upon me before Allah not to sheathe it until you say, Muḥammad is the mightiest and I am the lowest. He said, Woe to you, Muḥammad is the mightiest and I am the lowest. It reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and it pleased him and he thanked him for it."
"15760","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy, رضي الله عنهما, reported that he sought permission from the Prophet ﷺ to kill his father. He said, Do not kill your father. > **""Do not kill your father""**"
"15761","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by ʿAbdullah ibn Ubayy while he was in the shade of a fortress, and he said, Ibn Abi Kabshah has raised dust upon us. His son ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbdullah said, O Messenger of Allah, by the One Who honored you, if you wish I shall bring you his head. He said, No, but honor your father and keep good company with him. > **""No, but honor your father and keep good company with him""**"
"15762","Shabab reported that ʿUmarah ibn Ḥazm ibn Lawdhan ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd ʿAwf ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar, and his mother, the mother of his brothers ʿAmr and Maʿmar, the sons of Ḥazm, was Khalidah bint Anas ibn Shayban ibn Wahb ibn Lawdhan ibn ʿAmr ibn Thaʿlabah ibn al-Khazraj ibn Saʿidah."
"15763","Shabab also reported that ʿUmarah ibn Ḥazm witnessed al-ʿAqabah, Badr, Uḥud, and all the scenes."
"15764","ʿUrwah, in naming those martyred on the day of al-Yamamah among the Anṣar, then from Banu Malik ibn al-Najjar, listed ʿImarah ibn Ḥazm."
"15765","Muḥammad ibn Isḥaq, in naming those martyred on the day of al-Yamamah among the Anṣar, then from Banu al-Khazraj, then from Banu al-Najjar, listed ʿUmarah ibn Ḥazm ibn Lawdhan ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd ʿAwf ibn Ghunm ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar."
"15766","Ibn Isḥaq, in naming those who witnessed Badr among the Anṣar, then from al-Aws, then from Banu Ẓafar, listed Qatadah ibn al-Nuʿman ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmir ibn Sawad ibn Kaʿb, and Kaʿb is Ẓafar, ibn al-Khazraj ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Aws."
"15767","Qatadah ibn al-Nuʿman رضي الله عنه reported: I went out one dark night, and I said, If only I would come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and witness prayer with him and keep him company. I did so, and when I entered the mosque the sky flashed lightning, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me and said, O Qatadah, what has brought you out. I said, With my father and mother as ransom, I wished to keep you company. He said, Take this palm stalk and hold it at your side, for when you go out it will illuminate ten cubits before you and ten behind you. Then he said to me, When you enter your house you will see something like a rough stone in the curtains of your house, for that is a devil. He said: I went out and it illuminated for me, then I struck the thing like a rough stone until it left my house. > **""Take this palm stalk and hold it at your side""**"
"15768","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Qatadah ibn al-Nuʿman, whose kunyah was Abu ʿUthman, died in the year twenty-three, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭab prayed over him, while his age was sixty-five years. Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, Muḥammad ibn Maslamah, and al-Ḥarith ibn Ḥazmah descended into his grave, and it is said Khazmah."
"15769","Abu Qatadah al-Ḥarith ibn Ribʿi رضي الله عنه reported that he guarded the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the night of Badr, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O Allah, preserve Abu Qatadah as he preserved Your Prophet this night. > **""O Allah, preserve Abu Qatadah as he preserved Your Prophet this night""**"
"15770","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه reported that the polytheists raided the milch camels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he rode out and overtook them, prevailed over them, and killed Masʿadah. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him he said, The face has prospered; O Allah, forgive him, three times, and he granted him the spoils of Masʿadah. > **""The face has prospered; O Allah, forgive him""**"
"15771","Abu al-ʿAlaʾ ibn ʿUmayr reported that he was with Qatadah ibn Milḥan when death came to him, and a man passed at the far end of the house, and he said, I saw him in the face of Qatadah. He said: Whenever I saw him it was as though oil was upon his face, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had wiped his face."
"15772","Muḥammad ibn Isḥaq, in naming those who witnessed Badr among the Anṣar, then from Banu Ḥarithah, listed Muḥammad ibn Maslamah ibn Khalid ibn ʿAdi ibn Majdaʿah ibn Ḥarithah ibn al-Ḥarith ibn al-Khazraj ibn ʿAmr ibn Malik ibn al-Aws, and he was an ally among Banu ʿAbd al-Ashhal."
"15773","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Muḥammad ibn Maslamah died at Madinah in the year forty-three, at seventy-seven years of age."
"15774","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that Muḥammad ibn Maslamah died in Ṣafar in the year forty-three."
"15775","ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣamit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to him, O Abu al-Walid. And he was a Badri, ʿAqabi, Uḥudi, and a chief who pledged beneath the tree. > **""O Abu al-Walid""**"
"15776","ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣamit رضي الله عنه reported that Muʿawiyah said to them, O company of Anṣar, why do you not meet me with your brothers among Quraysh. ʿUbadah said, Need. He said, Then why not the irrigation camels. They said, We wore them out on the day of Badr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15777","Muḥammad ibn Isḥaq reported that ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣamit is ibn Qays ibn Aṣram ibn Fihr ibn Thaʿlabah ibn Ghunm ibn Salim ibn ʿAwf ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf ibn al-Khazraj."
"15778","Makḥul reported that ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣamit and Shaddad ibn Aws used to reside at Bayt al-Maqdis."
"15779","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣamit died in al-Sham, in the land of Palestine at al-Ramlah, in the year thirty-four, when he was seventy-two years old."
"15780","Khuzaymah ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ bought a horse from Suwaʾ ibn al-Ḥarith, who then denied it, so Khuzaymah ibn Thabit testified for him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, What prompted you to testify when you were not present with us. He said, Your truthfulness in what you brought, and my knowledge that you speak only truth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, Whomever Khuzaymah testifies for or against, it suffices him. > **""Whomever Khuzaymah testifies for or against, it suffices him""**"
"15781","Ibn Shihab reported from ʿUmarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit al-Anṣari, and Khuzaymah is the one whose testimony the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made equal to the testimony of two men. Ibn Shihab said: ʿUmarah ibn Khuzaymah informed me, from his uncle, who was among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, that Khuzaymah ibn Thabit saw in a dream that he was prostrating upon the forehead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned that, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lay down for him and he prostrated upon his forehead."
"15782","Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas al-Anṣari رضي الله عنه reported: I said, O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I have feared that I am ruined. He said, Why. I said, Allah forbade a man to be praised for what he did not do, yet I find I love praise. Allah forbade pride, yet I find I love beauty. And He forbade us to raise our voices above your voice, while I am a man loud of voice. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Are you not pleased to live praised, be killed a martyr, and enter Paradise. He said, Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So he lived praised and was killed a martyr on the day of Musaylimah. > **""Are you not pleased to live praised, be killed a martyr, and enter Paradise""**"
"15783","Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas رضي الله عنه reported that when this verse was revealed, Do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet, Thabit sat in the road weeping. ʿAṣim ibn ʿAdi passed by him and asked, What makes you weep, O Thabit. He said, I am loud of voice, and I fear this verse was revealed concerning me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O my son, are you not pleased to live praised, be killed a martyr, and enter Paradise. He said, I am pleased with the glad tidings of Allah and His Messenger; I shall never raise my voice above the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then was revealed, Indeed those who lower their voices. > **""O my son, are you not pleased to live praised, be killed a martyr, and enter Paradise""**"
"15784","ʿAṭaʾ al-Khurasani reported that he came to Madinah and asked for someone to narrate to him the ḥadith of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas, so they directed him to his daughter. He asked her and she said: I heard my father say, When there was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Indeed Allah does not love every arrogant boaster, it weighed heavily upon Thabit. He closed his door and wept, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was informed, so he sent to him and asked him. He told him what weighed upon him from it, saying, I am a man who loves beauty and to be chief of his people. He said, You are not among them; rather you shall live well, die well, and Allah shall admit you to Paradise. She said: When Allah revealed upon His Messenger, O you who believe, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet nor speak loudly to him, he did likewise. The Prophet ﷺ was informed, so he sent to him. He told him what weighed upon him, that he was loud of voice and feared being among those whose deeds are nullified. The Prophet ﷺ said, Rather you shall live praised, be killed a martyr, and Allah shall admit you to Paradise. When Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه mobilized the Muslims to fight the apostates at al-Yamamah against Musaylimah the liar, Thabit ibn Qays marched among those who marched. When they met Musaylimah and Banu Ḥanifah, the Muslims were defeated three times, so Thabit and Salim, the freedman of Abu Ḥudhayfah, said, This is not how we fought with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They dug a pit for themselves, entered it, and fought until they were killed. She said: A Muslim man saw Thabit ibn Qays in his dream, and he said, When I was killed yesterday a Muslim man passed by me and took from me a precious coat of mail. His dwelling is at the far end of the camp, and near his dwelling is a horse prancing in its tether. He has covered the coat with a cooking pot and placed a saddle upon the pot. Go to Khalid ibn al-Walid and let him send for my coat and take it. When you come to the caliph of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, inform him that I owe such-and-such debt, and that so-and-so among my slaves is freed. Beware of saying this is a dream and neglecting it. She said: He came to Khalid ibn al-Walid, who directed to the coat and found it as described. He came to Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه and informed him, and Abu Bakr executed his bequest after his death. We know of no one whose bequest was executed after his death except Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas. > **""Rather you shall live praised, be killed a martyr, and Allah shall admit you to Paradise""**"
"15785","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas came on the day of al-Yamamah with his shrouds spread and perfumed, saying, O Allah, I disavow to You what these have brought, and I apologize for what these have done. He was killed, and he had a coat of mail which was stolen. A man saw him in a dream, and he said, My coat is in a pot beneath the hearth in such-and-such place, and he enjoined bequests. They sought the coat and found it, and they executed the bequests."
"15786","ʿUrwah, in naming those killed on the day of al-Yamamah among the Anṣar, then from Banu al-Ḥarith ibn al-Khazraj, listed Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas in the year twelve."
"15787","Abu Ayyub al-Anṣari رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was performing ṭawaf between al-Ṣafa and al-Marwah when a feather fell upon his beard. Abu Ayyub hastened to him and took it from his beard, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him, May Allah remove from you what you dislike. > **""May Allah remove from you what you dislike""**"
"15788","Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed with him, and he was the first among whom he stayed."
"15789","Ibn ʿAbbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, with whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed when he migrated, raided the land of Rome and passed by Muʿawiyah رضي الله عنهما who treated him coldly. He went on then returned from his raid and Muʿawiyah again treated him coldly and paid him no regard. He said, The Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me that after him we shall see preference shown to others. Muʿawiyah asked, What did he command you. He said, He commanded us to be patient. He said, Then be patient. He then came to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAbbas at al-Baṣrah, over which ʿAli رضي الله عنهما had appointed him, and Ibn ʿAbbas said, O Abu Ayyub, I wish to vacate my dwelling for you as you vacated yours for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He ordered his household to leave and gave him everything the house contained. When his departure came he asked, Your need. He said, My need is my stipend and eight slaves to work my land, and his stipend was four thousand, so he multiplied it for him five times and gave him twenty thousand and forty slaves."
"15790","In another narration Abu Ayyub came to Muʿawiyah and complained to him that he owed a debt, and he mentioned the ḥadith with two chains."
"15791","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that a man said, O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so has a palm tree and I straighten my wall with it, so order him to give it to me that I may straighten my wall with it. The Prophet ﷺ said, Give it to him for a palm tree in Paradise. He refused, so Abu al-Daḥdaḥ came to him and said, Sell me your palm tree for my walled garden. He did so, then he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, I have bought the palm tree with my garden, so assign it to him for I have given it to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, How many a heavy laden bunch of dates for Abu al-Daḥdaḥ in Paradise. He said that repeatedly. He said: He then came to his wife and said, O Umm al-Daḥdaḥ, go out of the garden, for I have sold it for a palm tree in Paradise. She said, Profitable is the bargain, or a word resembling it. > **""Give it to him for a palm tree in Paradise""**"
"15792","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud رضي الله عنه reported that when there was revealed, Who is it that will loan Allah a goodly loan, Abu al-Daḥdaḥ said, O Messenger of Allah, does Allah seek a loan from us. He said, Yes, O Abu al-Daḥdaḥ. He said, Show me your hand. He extended his hand to him and he said, I have loaned my Lord my garden, and in his garden were six hundred palm trees. He walked until he came to the garden while Umm al-Daḥdaḥ and his children were in it, and he called, O Umm al-Daḥdaḥ. She said, At your service. He said, Go out, for I have loaned it to my Lord. > **""Yes, O Abu al-Daḥdaḥ""**"
"15793","ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn Abza رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ sent to Abu al-Daḥdaḥ seeking a loan. When the messenger came to him the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, He sent to me seeking a loan. He said, Yes. He said, I call Allah to witness that my wealth in such-and-such place is in the path of Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, How many a bunch of dates for Abu al-Daḥdaḥ in Paradise. > **""How many a bunch of dates for Abu al-Daḥdaḥ in Paradise""**"
"15794","Muḥammad ibn Sirin reported that Anas ibn Malik entered upon al-Baraʾ ibn Malik while he was composing poetry, so he said to him, My brother, has Allah not taught you what is better than this. Al-Baraʾ said to him, Do you fear I shall die upon my bed. By Allah that shall never be, by Allah's trial of me, for I have killed a hundred among the polytheists, some whom I killed alone and some in whose killing I shared."
"15795","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that al-Baraʾ ibn Malik lay upon his back then hummed, so Anas said to him, Remember Allah, O my brother. He sat upright and said, O Anas, do you think I shall die upon my bed when I have killed a hundred among the polytheists in single combat apart from those in whose killing I shared."
"15796","Isḥaq ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Ṭalḥah reported that while Anas ibn Malik and his brother al-Baraʾ ibn Malik were at a fortress of the enemy, the enemy casting grappling hooks in heated chains that caught a man and lifted him to them, one of those hooks caught Anas ibn Malik and they lifted him till he left the ground. His brother al-Baraʾ came, and it was said to him, Reach your brother, while he was fighting the people. He came running until he leapt at the wall, then seized the chain with his hand while it was turning, and he kept pulling them with his hands smoking until he cut the rope. He then looked at his hands and behold the bones were visible with the flesh upon them gone. Allah Almighty thereby delivered Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه."
"15797","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him the kunyah Abu Ḥamzah."
"15798","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that he had a forelock, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stretch it and hold it."
"15799","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that he hopes to meet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and say, O Messenger of Allah, your little servant. > **""O Messenger of Allah, your little servant""**"
"15800","Thabit reported that when he came to Anas his presence would be announced, so he would enter upon him and take his hands and kiss them, saying, With my father be ransomed these two hands that touched the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would kiss his eyes and say, With my father be ransomed these two eyes that saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15801","Qatadah reported that when Anas ibn Malik died, Muwarriq al-ʿIjli said, Today half of knowledge has gone. It was said, How is that, O Abu al-Mughirah. He said, When a man of the people of desires opposed us in ḥadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ we would say to him, Come to one who heard it from him."
"15802","Jarir ibn Ḥazim reported that he asked Shuʿayb ibn al-Ḥabḥab, When did Anas ibn Malik die. He said, In the year ninety."
"15803","Al-Sari ibn Yaḥya reported that Anas ibn Malik died in the year ninety-three."
"15804","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him choice between emigration and support, so he chose emigration. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me choice between emigration and support, so I chose emigration""**"
"15805","Muḥammad ibn Ḥamzah ibn Yusuf ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Salam reported from his father that ʿAbdullah ibn Salam said to the rabbis of the Jews, I shall renew a covenant at the mosque of our father Ibrahim and Ismaʿil. He set out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Makkah, and he met them when they had turned from the ḥajj, finding the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Mina with the people around him. He stood with the people, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at him he said, ʿAbdullah ibn Salam. He said, Yes. He said, Come near. So I drew near to him. He said, I adjure you by Allah, O ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, do you not find me in the Tawrah as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said to him, Describe our Lord to us. Then Jibril عليه السلام came until he stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, Say, He is Allah, the One, Allah the Eternal, He neither begets nor is born, nor is there to Him any equivalent. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited it to us, so ʿAbdullah ibn Salam said, I bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah. Then Ibn Salam departed to Madinah and concealed his Islam. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ migrated and came to Madinah while I was atop a palm cutting it, I heard a stir in Madinah and asked, What is this. They said, This is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who has come. He said: I cast myself down from the top of the palm, then went out hastening until I came to him and greeted him, then returned, and my mother said, By Allah, had it been Musa ibn ʿImran عليه السلام you would not thus cast yourself from the top of the palm. I said, By Allah, my joy at the coming of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is greater than at Musa when he was sent. > **""I adjure you by Allah, O ʿAbdullah ibn Salam, do you not find me in the Tawrah as the Messenger of Allah""**"
"15806","Saʿd, meaning Ibn Abi Waqqaṣ رضي الله عنه, reported that the Prophet ﷺ was brought a bowl and ate from it, and a remainder remained. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, A man shall come from this mountain pass among the people of Paradise to eat this remainder. Saʿd said: I had left my brother ʿUmayr performing ablution, so I said, It is ʿUmayr. Then ʿAbdullah ibn Salam came and ate it. > **""A man shall come from this mountain pass among the people of Paradise to eat this remainder""**"
"15807","Yusuf ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Salam رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated him in his lap, wiped over his head, named him Yusuf, and supplicated for blessing for him. > **""Yusuf""**"
"15808","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that he is the nearest of them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Resurrection, for he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, The nearest of you to me on the Day of Resurrection is he who leaves the world in the state he was upon the day I left him, and by Allah there is none among you but has clung to something of it except me. > **""The nearest of you to me on the Day of Resurrection is he who leaves the world in the state he was upon the day I left him""**"
"15809","Ibn ʿAbbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Abu Dharr said, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, The most beloved of you to me and the nearest of you to me is he who succeeds me upon the covenant I left him upon. > **""The most beloved of you to me and the nearest of you to me is he who succeeds me upon the covenant I left him upon""**"
"15810","Al-Ṭabarani said concerning Abu Dharr: He is Jundub ibn Junadah ibn Sufyan ibn ʿUbayd ibn Ḥaram ibn Ghifar ibn Mulayl ibn Ḍamrah ibn Bakr ibn ʿAbd Manaf ibn Kinanah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyas ibn Muḍar ibn Nizar ibn Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnan."
"15811","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that the name of Abu Dharr is Jundub ibn Junadah, and it is said the name of Abu Dharr is Burayr."
"15812","Zayd ibn Aslam reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Abu Dharr, O Burayr. > **""O Burayr""**"
"15813","Jubayr ibn Nufayr reported that Abu Dharr used to say, I saw myself as a quarter of Islam, for none had accepted Islam before me except the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Bilal رضي الله عنهما."
"15814","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that he had a brother called Unays, a poet, who contended with another poet. Unays said, I am more poetic than you, and the other said, I am more poetic. Unays said, Whom will you accept as judge between us. He said, I accept the soothsayer of Makkah to be between us. He said, Yes. They went out to Makkah and gathered before the soothsayer, and each recited his words to him. He said to Unays, I have judged for yourself, as though he favored the poetry of Unays. My brother said, In Makkah there is a man who claims he is a prophet, and he is upon your religion. Ibn ʿAbbas said: I said to Abu Dharr, What was your religion. He said, I had turned away from the deities of my people which they worshipped. I said, What did you worship. He said, Nothing; I would pray in the night until I fell as though a covering, until the heat of the sun awakened me. He was asked, Where would you turn your face. He said, Wherever my Lord directed me. Unays said to me, And they had hated him, meaning disliked him. Abu Dharr said: I came until I entered Makkah, and I remained between the Kaʿbah and its curtains fifteen nights and days. I would go out each night and drink a draught from Zamzam water, and I found no pangs of hunger upon my liver though my belly had wrinkled. Two women began supplicating their deities one night, one saying, O Isaf, grant me a boy, and the other saying, O Naʾilah, grant me such-and-such. I said, They are through them, and they turned away saying, The Sabian between the Kaʿbah and its curtains. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed with Abu Bakr walking behind him, and they said, The Sabian between the Kaʿbah and its curtains. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke words that condemned what they said. Abu Dharr said: I thought he was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I went out to him and said, Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah. He said, And upon you peace and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, three times. Then he said to me, How long have you been here. I said, Fifteen days and nights. He said, From where did you eat. I said, I would come to Zamzam each night at midnight and drink a draught from it, and I found no pangs of hunger upon my liver though my belly wrinkled. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, It is nourishing food and drink, and it is blessed, saying it three times. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked me, From whom are you. I said, From Ghifar. And Ghifar used to cut off the pilgrims, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seemed to withdraw from me, and he said to Abu Bakr, Go, O Abu Bakr, go, O Abu Bakr. He took us to the house of Abu Bakr, and he brought us raisins and we ate from them. I stayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he taught me Islam, and I recited something of the Qurʾan. I said, O Messenger of Allah, I wish to proclaim my religion. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, I fear for you that you will be killed. I said, It must be. He said, I fear for you that you will be killed. I said, It must be, O Messenger of Allah, even if I am killed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell silent toward me while Quraysh were in circles conversing in the mosque, and I said, I bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. The circles dispersed and they rose against me and beat me until they left me as though a red idol, and they thought they had killed me. I stood and came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he saw my condition and said, Did I not forbid you. I said, O Messenger of Allah, it was a need in myself that I fulfilled. I stayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said to me, Join your people, and when my triumph reaches you then come to me. I came when I had tarried from them, and I met Unays who wept and said, O my brother, I thought you had been killed when you tarried from us. What did you do. Did you meet the companion you sought. I said, I bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. He accepted Islam on the spot, then I came to my mother, and when she saw me she wept and said, O my son, you tarried from us until I feared you had been killed. What did you do. Did you meet the companion you sought. I said, Yes, I bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. She said, What did Unays do. I said, He accepted Islam. She said, I have no aversion to you both; I bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. I stayed among my people and many of them accepted Islam, until the triumph of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached us, so I came to him. > **""It is nourishing food and drink, and it is blessed""**"
"15815","In another narration with him also: I carried my mother and my sister until we settled near Makkah. My brother said, I am contending a man over water with poetry, and he was a poet. I said, Do not do so. He was overcome by obstinacy until he contended Durayd ibn al-Ṣimmah, his flock against his flock. By Allah, on that day Durayd was more poetic than my brother, so they sought judgment from al-Khansaʾ, who judged for my brother against Durayd, for Durayd had proposed to her father, and she said, An old shaykh, I have no need of him, so she bore that against him. We joined his flock to ours, and it became a multitude for us. Then I came to Makkah and began at al-Ṣafa, and upon it were men of Quraysh. It had reached me that among them was a Sabian, or madman, or poet, or sorcerer. I said, Where is the one they allege. They said, He is where you see. I turned to him, and by Allah I had not passed beyond them a stone's throw before they fell upon me with every stone, bone, and clod, and they bloodied me. I came to the House and entered between the curtains and the structure, and I fasted therein thirty days, neither eating nor drinking except Zamzam water, until on a bright moonlit night two women of Khuzaʿah came and circumambulated the House. He said: Then he mentioned the ḥadith like what is in al-Ṣaḥiḥ."
"15816","Abu al-Dardaʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Neither the green sky has shaded nor the dusty earth carried one of more truthful tongue than Abu Dharr. > **""Neither the green sky has shaded nor the dusty earth carried one of more truthful tongue than Abu Dharr""**"
"15817","ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn Ghunm reported that he visited Abu al-Dardaʾ at Ḥimṣ and stayed with him nights. He ordered his donkey saddled for him, and Abu al-Dardaʾ said, I see myself but following you, so he ordered his donkey saddled, and they rode upon their donkeys. They met a man who had attended Friday the day before with Muʿawiyah at al-Jabiyah; the man recognized them though they did not recognize him, and he informed them of the people's news. Then the man said, And another news I disliked informing you of, for I see you dislike it. Abu al-Dardaʾ said, Perhaps Abu Dharr has been exiled. He said, Yes, by Allah. Abu al-Dardaʾ and his companion said, To Allah we belong, nearly ten times. Then Abu al-Dardaʾ said, Await them and be patient as was said to the companions of the she-camel. O Allah, if they belie Abu Dharr I shall not belie him. O Allah, if they accuse him I shall not accuse him. O Allah, if they distrust him I shall not distrust him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would trust him when he trusted no one, and confide to him when he confided to no one. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Abu al-Dardaʾ, had Abu Dharr cut off my right hand I would not hate him after what I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, Neither the green sky has shaded nor the dusty earth carried one of more truthful tongue than Abu Dharr. > **""Neither the green sky has shaded nor the dusty earth carried one of more truthful tongue than Abu Dharr""**"
"15818","Abu al-Dardaʾ رضي الله عنه reported: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would draw Abu Dharr near when he was present and miss him when he was absent."
"15819","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Abu Dharr rivals ʿIsa son of Maryam عليه السلام in his worship. > **""Abu Dharr rivals ʿIsa son of Maryam in his worship""**"
"15820","With his chain from ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud رضي الله عنه, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Whoever is pleased to look at the likeness of ʿIsa son of Maryam عليه السلام in creation and character, let him look at Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه. > **""Whoever is pleased to look at the likeness of ʿIsa son of Maryam in creation and character, let him look at Abu Dharr""**"
"15821","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O Abu Dharr, I saw as though I was weighed against forty with you among them, and I outweighed them. > **""O Abu Dharr, I saw as though I was weighed against forty with you among them, and I outweighed them""**"
"15822","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAli رضي الله عنهما reported that Jibril عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Muḥammad, Allah loves three among your Companions, so love them: ʿAli ibn Abi Ṭalib, Abu Dharr, and al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad. > **""O Muḥammad, Allah loves three among your Companions, so love them""**"
"15823","Anas رضي الله عنه reported, raised to the Prophet, that Paradise longs for three: ʿAli, ʿAmmar, and, I think he said, Abu Dharr. > **""Paradise longs for three: ʿAli, ʿAmmar, and Abu Dharr""**"
"15824","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left nothing of what Jibril and Mikaʾil عليهما السلام poured into his chest except that he poured it into his chest. And I left nothing of what he poured into my chest except that I poured it into the chest of Malik ibn Ḍamrah."
"15825","ʿAbdullah ibn Khirash reported that he saw Abu Dharr at al-Rabadhah in a black canopy with a stout woman with him, sitting upon a piece of sack. It was said to him, O Abu Dharr, you are a man whose children do not remain. He said, Praise be to Allah Who takes them in the abode of passing and stores them in the abode of remaining. They said, O Abu Dharr, if only you took a wife other than this one. He said, For me to marry a woman who lowers me is more beloved to me than a woman who raises me. They said to him, If only you took a mat softer than this. He said, O Allah, forgiveness; take from what You have granted as seems fit to You."
"15826","Muḥammad ibn Sirin reported that it reached al-Ḥarith, a man in al-Sham among Quraysh, that Abu Dharr was in need, so he sent to him three hundred dinars. He said, Allah's servant found none more insignificant to him than me. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, Whoever asks while having forty has importuned. And Abu Dharr had forty dirhams, forty sheep, and two servants. > **""Whoever asks while having forty has importuned""**"
"15827","Abu Shuʿbah reported that a man came to Abu Dharr and offered him maintenance, so Abu Dharr said, We have goats we milk, donkeys that carry us, a freedwoman who serves us, and a spare cloak beyond our clothing. I fear I shall be reckoned for surplus."
"15828","Abu al-Aswad al-Diyali reported that he saw the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, and he saw none resembling Abu Dharr."
"15829","Ibrahim, meaning Ibn al-Ashtar, reported that death came to Abu Dharr while he was at al-Rabadhah, so his wife wept. He asked, What makes you weep. She said, I weep that I have no means for yourself, nor have I a garment large enough for your shroud. He said, Do not weep, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say one day while I was with him among a group, A man among you shall die in a desert land witnessed by a company of believers. He said: All who were in that gathering died in a company and a village, none of them remaining but me, and I have come to die in the desert, so watch the road, for you shall see what I say, for by Allah I neither lied nor was lied to. She said, How is that when the pilgrims have ceased. He said, Watch the road. He said: While she was thus, behold people with their mounts hastening as though vultures. The people came until they stood over her and asked, What is with you. She said, A man among the Muslims whom you may shroud and be rewarded for. They asked, Who is he. She said, Abu Dharr. They ransomed him with their fathers and mothers, and put their whips in the girths hastening to him. He said, Rejoice, for you are the group concerning whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said what he said. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, There are no two Muslim parents between whom two or three children die and they anticipate reward and are patient but they shall never see the Fire. Then I have come today where you see, and had I a garment of mine large enough to be shrouded in, I adjure you by Allah that no man among you who was a headman, or commander, or courier shall shroud me, for all the people have attained something of that except a youth among the Anṣar who was with them. He said, I am your companion, with two garments in my bag from my mother's spinning and one of these two garments upon me. He said, You are my companion, so shroud me. > **""A man among you shall die in a desert land witnessed by a company of believers""**"
"15830","Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb reported that Ibn Masʿud came in the company of ʿAmmar and passed by the funeral of Abu Dharr upon the roadside, so he and his companions alighted and buried him. And Abu Dharr had entered Egypt and marked out a house therein."
"15831","Yazid ibn Abi Ḥabib reported that Abu Dharr was among those who witnessed the conquest with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ."
"15832","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Abu Dharr died at al-Rabadhah in the year thirty-two, and his name was Jundub ibn Junadah."
"15833","Salman al-Farisi رضي الله عنه reported: I was a Persian man from the people of Iṣbahan, from a village thereof called Jayy, and my father was the chief of his village. I was the most beloved of Allah's creation to him, and his love for me kept me confined in a house as a slave girl is confined. I strove in Magianism until I became the tender of the fire who kindled it, never letting it die for an hour. He said: My father had a great estate. He said: He was occupied one day with a building of his and said to me, O my son, I have been occupied with this building of mine today from my estate, so go and inspect it, and he charged me with some of what he wished therein, then said to me, Do not tarry from me, for if you tarry from me you will be more pressing upon me than my estate and will divert me from all my affairs. I went out intending his estate and passed by a church of the Christians, hearing their voices therein while they prayed, though I knew nothing of people's affairs due to my father's confining me in his house. When I passed them and heard their voices I entered upon them to see what they did. When I saw them their prayer pleased me and I desired their affair, saying, By Allah, this is better than the religion we are upon. By Allah, I did not leave them until the sun set, and I neglected my father's estate and did not go to it. I said to them, Where is the origin of this religion. They said, In al-Sham. He said: Then I returned to my father who had sent seeking me and whom I had diverted from all his work. He said: When I came to him he said, O my son, where were you. Did I not enjoin upon you what I enjoined. I said, O my father, I passed by people praying in a church of theirs, and what I saw of their religion pleased me. By Allah, I remained with them until the sun set. He said, O my son, there is no good in that religion; your religion and your fathers' religion is better than it. He said: I said, Nay, by Allah, it is better than our religion. He said: He feared me, so he put a fetter upon my leg, then confined me in his house. He said: I sent to the Christians saying, When traders from al-Sham among the Christians come to you, inform me of them. A company from al-Sham, Christian traders, came to them, and they informed me. He said: I said, When they finish their needs and wish to return to their lands, let me know of them. He said: When they wished to return to their lands, I cast the iron from my leg, then went out with them until I came to al-Sham. When I came to it I asked, Who is the most excellent of the people of this religion. They said, The bishop in the church. He said: I came to him and said, I have desired this religion, and I wish to be with you in your church, serving you in your church, learning from you, and praying with you. He said, Enter. So I entered with him. He said: He was an evil man; he commanded them to give charity and urged them therein, yet when they gathered something of it he hoarded it for himself and gave none to the poor, until he amassed seven jars of gold and silver. He said: I hated him intensely for what I saw him do. Then he died, and the Christians gathered to bury him. I said to them, He was an evil man; he commanded you to give charity and urged you therein, yet when you gathered things for him and brought them to him he hoarded them for himself and gave nothing of them to the poor. They said, How do you know that. I said, I shall show you his treasure. They said, Then show us. He said: I showed them its place, and they extracted seven jars filled with gold and silver. When they saw them they said, By Allah, we shall never bury him. He said: They crucified him, then stoned him, then brought another man and put him in his place. He said: Salman says, I scarcely saw a man who prayed the five prayers whom I deemed more excellent than him, nor more ascetic in the world, nor more desirous of the Hereafter, nor more constant night and day than him. He said: I loved him with a love I had loved none before him, and I stayed with him a time. Then death came to him, and I said to him, O so-and-so, I was with you and loved you with a love I loved none before you, and there has come to you what you see of Allah's decree, so to whom do you commend me, and what do you command me. He said, O my son, by Allah, I know no one today upon what I was upon; the people have perished, altered, and abandoned most of what they were upon, except a man at al-Mawṣil, and he is so-and-so, and he is upon what I was upon, so join him. He said: When he died and was buried I joined the companion of al-Mawṣil. I said to him, O so-and-so, so-and-so commended me at his death to join you, and informed me that you are upon the like of his affair. He said, Stay with me. I stayed with him and found him the best of men upon the affair of his companion. He soon died, and when death came to him I said to him, O so-and-so, so-and-so commended me to you, and you have commanded me to join you, and there has come to you of Allah's decree what you see, so to whom do you commend me, and what do you command me. He said, O my son, by Allah, I know no man upon the like of what we were upon except a man at Naṣibin, and he is so-and-so, so join him. He said: When he died and was buried I joined the companion of Naṣibin. I came to him and informed him of my story and what my companion commanded me. He said, Stay with me. I stayed with him and found him upon the affair of his two companions; I stayed with the best of men. By Allah, he soon met death, and when it came I said, O so-and-so, so-and-so had commended me to so-and-so, then so-and-so commended me to you, so to whom do you commend me, and what do you command me. He said, O my son, by Allah, I know none remaining upon our affair to whom I may command you to come except a man at ʿAmmuriyah, for he is upon the like of what we are upon; if you wish then come to him, for he is upon the like of our affair. He said: When he died and was buried I joined the companion of ʿAmmuriyah. I informed him of my story, and he said, Stay with me. I stayed with a man upon the affair of his companions and their guidance, and I earned until I came to own calves and a ewe. He said: Then Allah's decree befell him. He said: When it came I said to him, O so-and-so, I was with so-and-so, and he commended me to so-and-so, and so-and-so commended to so-and-so, and so-and-so commended to so-and-so, and so-and-so commended me to you, so to whom do you commend me, and what do you command me. He said: By Allah, I know none of the people upon what we were upon to whom I may command you to come, but the time of a prophet has drawn near who is sent with the religion of Ibrahim. He shall come out in the land of the Arabs, his migration to a land between two lava tracts with palms between them. He has signs not hidden: he eats gifts and does not eat charity, and between his shoulders is the seal of prophethood. If you can join that land, then do so. He said: Then he died and was buried, and I stayed at ʿAmmuriyah as long as Allah willed me to stay. Then a group of Kalb traders passed by me, and I said to them, Will you carry me to the land of the Arabs and I shall give you these calves and this ewe. They said, Yes. I gave them to them and they carried me, until when they brought me to Wadi al-Qura they wronged me and sold me to a Jewish man. I stayed with him, and I saw the palms, hoping it was the town my companion described to me, though it did not settle in myself. While I was with him, a cousin of his came to him from Madinah, from Banu Qurayẓah, and bought me from him and carried me to Madinah. By Allah, no sooner did I see it than I recognized it by my companion's description. I stayed therein, and Allah sent His Prophet ﷺ, who stayed at Makkah with no mention of him reaching me due to my bondage. Then he migrated to Madinah. By Allah, I was atop a palm trunk of my master working therein with some work, my master sitting, when a cousin of his came until he stood over him and said, So-and-so, may Allah smite Banu Qaylah; by Allah, they are now gathered upon a man who came today from Makkah claiming he is a prophet. He said: When I heard it a tremor seized me until I thought I would fall upon my master. He said: I descended from the palm and kept saying to that cousin of his, What do you say, what do you say. My master grew angry and struck me a severe blow, then said, What is with you and this. Attend to your work. He said: I said, Nothing; I only wished to question him about what he said. I had something I had gathered, and in the evening I took it and went with it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Qubaʾ. I entered upon him and said to him, It has reached me that you are a righteous man with companions of yours, strangers in need, and this is something I had for charity, and I deemed you more deserving of it than others. I brought it near to him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions, Eat. He withheld his hand and did not eat. He said: I said to myself, This is one. Then I turned from him and gathered something, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ moved to Madinah. Then I came to him and said, I saw you do not eat charity, and this is a gift with which I honor you. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate from it and ordered his Companions to eat with him. He said: I said to myself, These are two. He said: Then I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Baqiʿ al-Gharqad, having followed a funeral of his Companions, with two garments upon him, sitting among his Companions. I greeted him, then turned to look at his back whether I saw the seal my companion described to me. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw me turn behind him, he knew I was verifying something described to me. He said: He cast his cloak from his back, and I looked at the seal and recognized it. I fell upon him kissing him and weeping, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me, Turn around. So I turned, and I recounted my story to him as I narrated to you, O Ibn ʿAbbas. It pleased the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that his Companions hear that. Bondage kept Salman occupied until Badr and Uḥud with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed him by. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me, Write a contract, O Salman. I contracted with my master for three hundred palm saplings to plant for him with digging, and forty ounces. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions, Aid your brother. They aided me with palms, a man with thirty saplings, a man with twenty saplings, a man with fifteen saplings, and a man with ten, each aiding according to what he had, until three hundred saplings gathered to me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Go, O Salman, and dig for them; when you finish then come to me and I shall place them with my hand. He said: I dug for them with my companions' aid until I finished them, then came to him and informed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with me to them, and we brought the saplings near to him while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed them with his hand. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Salman, not a single sapling among them died. I delivered the palms, and wealth remained upon me. There came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ something like a hen's egg of gold from some mine, and he said, What has the Persian contractee done. He said: I was called for him, and he said, Take this and pay with it what is upon you, O Salman. He said: I said, Where shall this fall of what is upon me, O Messenger of Allah. He said, Take it, for Allah shall pay it off for you. He said: I took it and weighed out to them from it, by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Salman, forty ounces. I fulfilled their right and was freed. I witnessed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the Trench, then no scene with him passed me by. > **""Write a contract, O Salman""**"
"15834","In another narration from Salman: When I said, Where shall this fall of what is upon me, O Messenger of Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it and turned it upon his tongue, then said, Take it and fulfill to them from it all their right, forty ounces. > **""Take it and fulfill to them from it all their right, forty ounces""**"
"15835","Salman رضي الله عنه reported: I was among the sons of the nobles of Persia. He said: Then he mentioned the ḥadith. He said: I set out, land raising me and land lowering me, until I passed a people of Bedouins who enslaved me and sold me, until a woman bought me. I heard them mention the Prophet ﷺ, and living was dear, so I said to her, Grant me a day. She said, Yes. He said: I went out and gathered firewood and sold it, then made food and brought it to the Prophet ﷺ, placing it before him. He said, What is this. I said, Charity. He said to his Companions, Eat. He did not eat, so I said, This is among his signs. Then I stayed as long as Allah willed me to stay, and I said to my mistress, Grant me a day. She said, Yes. I went out and gathered firewood and sold it for more than that, then made food and brought it to him while he sat among his Companions, placing it before him. He said, What is this. I said, A gift. He placed his hand and said to his Companions, Take in the name of Allah. I stood and he laid aside his cloak, and behold the seal of prophethood. I said, I bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. He said, What is that. I informed him about the man, saying to him, Shall he enter Paradise, O Messenger of Allah, for he informed me that you are a prophet. He said, None shall enter Paradise except a Muslim soul. > **""None shall enter Paradise except a Muslim soul""**"
"15836","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported that Salman came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he arrived at Madinah with a tray bearing fresh dates, placing it before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, What is this, O Salman. He said, Charity upon you and your Companions. He said, Lift it, for we do not eat charity. He lifted it, and came the next day with the like, carrying it and placing it before him. He said, What is this, O Salman. He said, This is a gift to you. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions, Be eager. He said: He looked at the seal upon the back of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and believed in him. He belonged to the Jews, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bought him for such-and-such dirhams, on condition of planting palms in which Salman would work until they bore fruit. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ planted the palms except one palm which ʿUmar planted. The palms bore fruit that year except that palm, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, Who planted this. ʿUmar said, I planted it, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ uprooted it then planted it, and it bore fruit that year. > **""Lift it, for we do not eat charity""**"
"15837","Salman al-Farisi رضي الله عنه reported: I was a man from the people of Jayy, and the people of my village worshipped piebald horses, though I knew they were upon nothing. It was said to me, The religion you seek is in the west. I went out until I came to al-Mawṣil and asked for the most excellent man therein, and I was directed to a man in a hermitage. I came to him and said, I am a man from the people of Jayy, and I have come seeking knowledge, learning from you; join me to you that I may serve you, accompany you, and you teach me something of what Allah taught you. He said, Yes. I accompanied him, and he provided for me the like of what he provided for himself of vinegar, oil, and grains. I remained with him until death befell him, and I sat at his head weeping for him. He asked, What makes you weep. I said, By Allah, I weep that I left my land seeking knowledge, and Allah granted me your company, and you taught me and kept good company with me, and death has befallen you and I know not where to go. He said, I have a brother in al-Jazirah in such-and-such place, upon the truth, so come to him and convey my greeting to him, and inform him that I commended you to him, and I commend you to his company. He said: When the man was taken I went out until I came to the man he described. I informed him of the news and conveyed the greeting of his companion, informing him that he perished and commanded me to his company. He joined me to him and provided for me as he provided, and I accompanied him as Allah willed, and death befell him. When death befell him I sat at his head weeping and said, I left my land seeking good, and Allah granted me the company of so-and-so who kept good company with me and taught me. When death befell him he commended me to you, and you joined me and kept good company with me and taught me, and death has befallen you and I know not where to turn. He said, My maternal uncle is near the Romans, upon the truth, so come to him and convey my greeting to him and accompany him, for he is upon the truth. When the man was taken I went out until I came to him. I informed him of my story and the bequest of the one before him. He said: He joined me to him and provided for me as he provided. When death befell him I sat weeping at his head, and he asked, What makes you weep. I recounted my story, saying to him, Allah granted me your company and you kept good company with me, and death has befallen you and I know not where to turn. He said, None remains whom I know upon the religion of ʿIsa عليه السلام on earth, but this is a time in which a prophet shall come out, or has come out, at Tihamah. Keep to the road; none shall pass by you but you ask him of him. When it reaches you that he has come out, come to him, for he is the Prophet whom ʿIsa عليه السلام gave tidings of. His sign is that between his shoulders is the seal of prophethood, he eats gifts, and does not eat charity. He said: None would pass by me but I asked him of him. Men from the people of Makkah passed by me and I asked them. They said, Yes, there has appeared among us a man claiming he is a prophet. I said to some of them, Will you take me as a servant of some of you on condition you carry me behind and feed me morsels of bread. When you reach your lands, if he wishes he may sell, and if he wishes he may enslave. A man among them said, I will. I became a servant to him until we came to Makkah. He placed me in a garden of his with Abyssinians therein. I went out and asked, meeting a woman from my land. I asked her, and behold her household had accepted Islam. She said, The Prophet ﷺ sits in al-Ḥijr with his Companions, when a sparrow cries at Makkah, until when dawn lights for them they disperse. I went to the garden and would visit in my night, and the Abyssinians said to me, What is with you. I said, I complain of my belly. He said: I only did that lest they miss me when I went to the Prophet ﷺ. He said: When the hour came which the woman informed me wherein he and his Companions sit, I went out walking until I saw the Prophet ﷺ, and behold he was sitting with his knees drawn up with his Companions around him. I came to him from behind, and the Prophet ﷺ knew what I wanted, so he loosened his garment and I looked at the seal of prophethood between his shoulders. I said, Allah is Greatest, this is one. Then I departed, and the next night I gathered good dates, then went with them to the Prophet ﷺ and placed them before him. He said, What is this. I said, A gift. He ate from them and said to the people, Eat. He said: I said, I bear witness that there is no deity but Allah and that you are the Messenger of Allah. He asked me of my affair and I informed him. He said, Go and buy yourself. I went to my master and said, Meaning myself. He said, Yes, on condition you plant for me a hundred palms, and when they grow you bring me the weight of a date pit of gold. I came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, and the Prophet ﷺ said, Buy yourself with what he asked of you, and bring me a bucket of water from the well from which you watered those palms. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for me, then I watered them. By Allah, I planted a hundred palms and not a palm among them but grew. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him that the palms had grown, and he gave me a piece of gold. I went with it and placed it in the scale's pan, placing in the other side a date pit. He said: By Allah, the piece of gold did not lift from the earth. He said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, and he freed me. > **""Go and buy yourself""**"
"15838","Salman رضي الله عنه reported: I was a man from a quarter, the city of Iṣbahan. While I was, Allah Almighty cast into my heart who created the heavens and the earth, so I went to a man who would not speak to people, being scrupulous. I asked him, Which religion is most excellent. He said, What is with you and this talk. Do you desire a religion other than yours. I said, No, but I wish to know who created the heavens and the earth, and which religion is most excellent. He said, I know none upon this but a monk at al-Mawṣil. He said: I went to him and dwelt with him, and behold he was straitened in the world. He would fast the day and stand the night, and I would worship as he worshipped. I stayed with him three years, then he died, and I said, To whom do you commend me. He said, I know none of the people of the east upon what I am upon, so go to a monk beyond the island, and convey my greeting to him. He said: I came to him and conveyed the greeting, informing him that he had died. I stayed with him also three years, then he died, and I said, To whom do you command me to go. He said, I know none of the people of the earth upon what I am upon except a monk at ʿAmmuriyah, an old shaykh, and I know not whether you shall reach him. I went to him and stayed with him, and behold a man with means. When death came to him I said to him, Where do you command me to go. He said, I know none of the people of the earth upon what I am upon, but if you live to a time when you hear of a man emerging from the house of Ibrahim عليه السلام, though I deem you shall not reach him, yet I had hoped to reach him; if you can be with him then do so, for it is the religion. Its sign is that his people say, Sorcerer, madman, soothsayer, he eats gifts and does not eat charity, and at the cartilage of his shoulder is the seal of prophethood. While I was thus a company came from toward Madinah, and it was said, Who are you. They said, We are from the people of Madinah, traders living by our trade, but a man from the house of Ibrahim عليه السلام has come out and come to us while his people fight him. We feared he would come between us and our trade, yet he has taken Madinah. I said, What do they say of him. He said: They say, Sorcerer, madman, soothsayer. I said, This is the sign; direct me to your companion. I came to him and said, Will you carry me to Madinah. He said, What will you give me. I said, I find nothing to give you but that I am a servant to you. He carried me, and when we came he placed me in his palms. I would draw water as a camel draws, until my back and chest were galled from that, finding none who understood my speech, until an old Persian woman came drawing water. I spoke to her and she understood my speech. I said to her, Where is this man who has come out. Direct me to him. She said, He shall pass by you early when he prays dawn at the first of the day. I went out and gathered dates, and in the morning I came, then brought the dates near to him. He said, What is this, charity or gift. I indicated it was charity, so he said, Go to those. His Companions were with him, and they ate while he did not eat. I said, This is the sign. The next day I came with dates, and he said, What is this. I said, This is a gift. He ate and called his Companions and they ate. Then he saw me seeking to see the seal, so he knew and cast his cloak. I took to kissing it and embracing him, and he said, What is your affair. He asked me, and I informed him of my story. He said, You stipulated to them that you are a servant, so buy yourself from them. The Prophet ﷺ bought him on condition he plant for them three hundred palms and forty ounces of gold, then he is free. The Prophet ﷺ said, Plant. So he planted, then go and cast the bucket upon the well, then do not lift it until it rises, for when it fills it rises, then sprinkle at their roots. He did so, and the palms grew the fastest of growth. He said, Glory be to Allah, we never saw such a servant; this servant has an affair. The people gathered to him, and the Prophet ﷺ gave him unminted gold, and therein were forty ounces. > **""You stipulated to them that you are a servant, so buy yourself from them""**"
"15839","Salamah al-ʿIjli reported that a nephew of his from the desert, called Qudamah, said to him, I wish to meet Salman and greet him. We went out to him and found him at al-Madaʾin, when he was over twenty thousand, finding him upon a couch weaving a basin. We greeted him, and I said, O Abu ʿAbdullah, this is a nephew of mine who came to me from the desert and wished to greet you. He said, And upon him peace and Allah's mercy. I said: He claims he loves you. He said, May Allah love him. He said: We conversed and said to him, O Abu ʿAbdullah, will you not narrate to us of your origin and from whom you are. He said, As for my origin and from whom I am, I am from Ramahurmuz. We were a Magian people, and a Christian man from the people of al-Jazirah came, passing by us and lodging among us, taking a monastery among us. I was in the Persian school, and a boy with me in the school would ever come beaten, weeping, for his parents had beaten him. I said to him one day, What makes you weep. He said, My parents beat me. He said: Why do they beat you. He said, I come to the master of this monastery, and when they know that they beat me. Had you come to him you would hear wondrous talk. I said, Take me with you. We came to him and he narrated to us of the beginning of creation, the creation, the beginning of the creation of the heavens and the earth, and of Paradise and Hell. He said: He narrated to us wondrous talk. He said: I would visit him with him. He said: Boys of the school noticed us and began coming with us. When the villagers saw that they came to him and said to him, O man, you have neighbored us and we saw of your neighboring only good, yet we see our boys visiting you, and we fear you may turn them from us; leave us. He said, Yes. He said to that boy who came to him, Go with me. He said, I cannot do that, for you know my parents' severity upon me. I said, But I shall go out with you. I was an orphan with no father. I went out with him, taking the mountain of Ramahurmuz, walking and trusting, eating from the fruit of trees until we came to al-Jazirah, coming to Naṣibin. My companion said to me, O Salman, a people here are the worshippers of the earth's people, and I wish to meet them. He said: We came to them one Sunday when they had gathered, and my companion greeted them. They greeted him and welcomed him, asking, Where was your absence. He said, I was among brothers of mine toward Persia. We conversed what we conversed, then my companion said to me, Rise, O Salman, let us go. I said, No, leave me with these. He said: I said you cannot bear what these bear; they fast from Sunday to Sunday and do not sleep this night. Among them was a man of kings' sons who left kingship and entered worship. I stayed among them until evening when they began going one by one to the cave wherein he stayed. He said: In the evening the man of kings' sons said, What is this boy. Let a man among you take him lest he be wasted. They said, Take him yourself. He said to me, Come, O Salman. He took me with him until he came to the cave wherein he stayed, saying, O Salman, this is bread and this is condiment; eat when hungry, fast when eager, pray as seems fit to you, and sleep when weary. Then he stood in his prayer and spoke to me only that and did not look at me. Grief seized me those seven days with none speaking to me until Sunday. He turned to me, and we went to their place wherein they gathered. He said: They gather each Sunday, breaking fast therein, meeting one another, greeting one another, then paying no heed to the like. He said: We returned to our dwelling and he said to me the like of what he said the first time: This is bread and condiment; eat of it when hungry, fast when eager, pray as seems fit to you, and sleep when weary. Then he entered his prayer and paid no heed to me nor spoke to me until the next Sunday. Grief seized me, and I resolved to flee. Then he entered his prayer and I said, I shall endure one or two Sundays. When Sunday came we returned to them, and they broke fast and gathered. He said to them, I wish to go to Bayt al-Maqdis. They said to him, What do you want with that. He said, I have not visited it. They said, We fear an event may occur there and another than us may assume you, though we wish to assume you. He said, I have not visited it. When I heard him mention that I rejoiced, saying, We shall travel and meet people. The grief I felt left me. We went out, I and he. He would fast from Sunday to Sunday, pray all night, and walk by day. When we alighted he would stand praying, and that remained his wont until we reached Bayt al-Maqdis, with upon the gate a crippled man begging people, saying, Give me. He said, I have nothing. We entered Bayt al-Maqdis, and when its people saw him they welcomed him and rejoiced at him. He said to them, This boy of mine, treat him well. They took me and fed me bread and meat, while he entered his prayer and did not turn to me until the next Sunday. Then he turned and said to me, O Salman, I wish to lay my head; when the shade reaches such-and-such place then awaken me. He laid his head, and the shade reached what he said, yet I did not awaken him out of pity for him from what I saw of his striving and toil. He awoke startled and said, O Salman, did I not say to you, when the shade reaches such-and-such place then awaken me. I said, Yes, but only pity for you from what I saw of your wont prevented me. He said, Woe to you, O Salman, I dislike that anything of time pass me wherein I have not wrought good for Allah. Then he said to me, O Salman, know that the most excellent of our religion today is Christianity. I said, Shall there be after today a religion more excellent than Christianity, a word cast upon my tongue. He said, Yes, a prophet is about to be sent who eats gifts and does not eat charity, with between his shoulders the seal of prophethood. If you reach him then follow him and confirm him. I said, Though he command me to leave Christianity. He said, Yes, for he is Allah's prophet who commands only truth and speaks only truth. By Allah, had I reached him and he commanded me to fall into the Fire I would fall into it. Then we left Bayt al-Maqdis and passed that crippled man. He said to him, You entered and gave me nothing, and now you leave, so give me. He turned and saw none around him. He said: Give me your hand. He said: He extended his hand to him, saying, Rise by Allah's permission. He stood sound and upright, heading toward his house. I followed him with my gaze in wonder at what I saw, while my companion left hastening, and I followed him. A company of Kalb Bedouins met me and took me captive, carrying me upon a camel and binding me. Traders passed me along until I fell to Madinah, where a man among the Anṣar bought me and placed me in a walled garden of palms. I stayed therein. He said: Thence I learned palm-leaf work; I would buy palm leaves for a dirham, work them, and sell them for two dirhams, returning a dirham to leaves and spending a dirham, wishing to eat from my hand's work, while he was then commander over twenty thousand. It reached us at Madinah that a man had come out at Makkah claiming Allah Almighty sent him. We stayed as long as Allah willed us to stay, then he migrated to us and came to us. I said, By Allah, I shall test him. I went to the market and bought camel meat for a dirham, then cooked it, making a bowl of tharid, and carried it upon my shoulder until I brought it to him and placed it before him. He said, What is this, charity or gift. I said, Rather charity. He said to his Companions, Eat in the name of Allah. He withheld and did not eat. I stayed days, then bought also for a dirham camel meat, making the like and carrying it until I brought it to him and placed it before him. He said, What is this, gift or charity. I said, No, rather a gift. He said to his Companions, Eat in the name of Allah. He ate with them, and I said, This, by Allah, eats gifts and does not eat charity. I looked and saw between his shoulders the seal of prophethood like a pigeon's egg, so I accepted Islam. Then I said to him one day, O Messenger of Allah, what people are the Christians. He said, There is no good in them. I loved them intensely for what I saw of their striving. Then I asked him after days, O Messenger of Allah, what people are the Christians. He said, There is no good in them nor in whoever loves them. I said to myself, By Allah, I love them. He said: That, by Allah, was when he sent expeditions and unsheathed the sword, expedition entering and expedition leaving, the sword dripping. I said, Now he shall learn that I love them and send to me and strike my neck. I sat in the house, and the messenger came to me one day saying, O Salman, answer. I asked, Whom. He said, The Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said, By Allah, this is what I feared. I said, Yes, go until I join you. He said, No, by Allah, until you come. I resolved that had he gone I would flee. He took me, and I came to him. When he saw me he smiled and said to me, O Salman, rejoice, for Allah has relieved you. Then he recited to me these verses: Those to whom We gave the Book before it believe in it, and when it is recited to them they say, We believe in it; it is the truth from our Lord; we were Muslims before it. Those shall be given their reward twice for their patience, repel evil with good, spend of what We provided them, and when they hear idle talk they turn from it and say, To us our deeds and to you your deeds; peace be upon you; we seek not the ignorant. I said, O Messenger of Allah, by the One Who sent you with truth as a prophet, I heard him say, Had I reached him and he commanded me to fall into the Fire I would fall into it; he is a prophet who speaks only truth and commands only truth. > **""O Salman, rejoice, for Allah has relieved you""**"
"15840","In a brief narration: Allah Almighty revealed, You shall find the strongest among men in enmity to the believers the Jews and the polytheists, until reaching, overflowing with tears. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to me and said, O Salman, your companions are these whom Allah mentioned. > **""O Salman, your companions are these whom Allah mentioned""**"
"15841","Umm al-Dardaʾ رضي الله عنها reported that Salman al-Farisi came to greet her wearing a Qaṭawani cloak wrapped in it. I offered a cushion and he refused it, folding his cloak and sitting upon it. He said, What reaches the resting place suffices you. Then he praised Allah a while, magnified, and invoked blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, then asked, Where is your companion, meaning Abu al-Dardaʾ. I said, He is in the mosque. He went to him, then both came while Abu al-Dardaʾ had bought meat for a dirham held hanging in his hand. He said, O Umm al-Dardaʾ, bake and cook. We did so, then we brought Salman the food. Abu al-Dardaʾ said, Eat with Umm al-Dardaʾ, for I am fasting. Salman said, I shall not eat until you eat. Abu al-Dardaʾ broke fast and ate with him. When the hour wherein Abu al-Dardaʾ stood came, he went to stand, but Salman seated him. Abu al-Dardaʾ said, Do you forbid me from my Lord's worship. Salman said, Your eye has a share upon you, and your family has a share upon you. He restrained him until near dawn when both stood and bowed units, then performed witr, then went out to dawn prayer. They mentioned their affair to the Prophet ﷺ, who said, What is with Salman, may his mother be bereaved of him; he has been filled with knowledge. > **""Your eye has a share upon you, and your family has a share upon you""**"
"15842","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gaze with his sight toward heaven. We said, O Messenger of Allah, what is this. He said, I saw an angel ascending with the deed of Salman. > **""I saw an angel ascending with the deed of Salman""**"
"15843","Anas رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that three the dark-eyed maidens long for: ʿAli, ʿAmmar, and Salman. > **""Three the dark-eyed maidens long for: ʿAli, ʿAmmar, and Salman""**"
"15844","Buqayrah, wife of Salman, reported that when death came to Salman he called her while he was in an upper room having four doors. He said, Open, O Buqayrah, these doors, for today I see visitors, not knowing from which of these doors they shall enter upon me. Then he called for his musk, saying, Mix it in a vessel. I did so, then he said, Sprinkle around my bed, then go down and stay, for you shall soon darken my bier upon my bed. She looked and behold his soul had been taken in his place upon his bed, or the like of this."
"15845","Ibn Shihab, in naming those among the Anṣar who witnessed al-ʿAqabah, listed Abu al-Haytham, and he was a chief."
"15846","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan died in the year twenty, and his name was Malik."
"15847","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ arrived at Madinah while he was eleven years old."
"15848","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted him on the day of the Trench and clothed him in a Qibṭi garment."
"15849","Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd reported that ʿUthman, meaning Ibn ʿAffan رضي الله عنه, said, Call to me Zayd ibn Thabit, the scribe of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15850","Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd reported that ʿUthman said, Which people are most skilled in writing. They said, Zayd ibn Thabit."
"15851","Al-Shaʿbi reported that Zayd ibn Thabit magnified Allah four times over his mother, then his mount was brought and Ibn ʿAbbas held the stirrup for him. Zayd said to him, Leave it. Ibn ʿAbbas said, Thus we do with great scholars."
"15852","Yaḥya ibn Saʿid reported that Abu Hurayrah said when Zayd ibn Thabit died, Today the learned man of this nation has died, and may Allah place in Ibn ʿAbbas a successor from him."
"15853","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Zayd ibn Thabit died in the year forty-five at fifty-six years of age. Some people say he died in the year forty-eight and the year fifty-nine, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted him on the day of the Trench while he was fifteen years old, and the Trench was in Shawwal in the year four, and his death is disputed."
"15854","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the station of Qays ibn Saʿd with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the station of the chief of police with the commander."
"15855","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that when the Prophet ﷺ came to Makkah, Qays ibn Saʿd was in his vanguard before him in the station of the chief of police. The Prophet ﷺ was spoken to concerning Qays, to remove him from the position he placed him in, fearing he might advance upon something, so he removed him from that."
"15856","The wife of Rafiʿ ibn Khadij reported that Rafiʿ was struck with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Uḥud or the day of Khaybar, ʿAmr doubted, by an arrow in his breast. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, remove the arrow. He said, O Rafiʿ, if you wish I shall remove the arrow and its head together, and if you wish I shall remove the arrow and leave the head, and I shall testify for you on the Day of Resurrection that you are a martyr. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ removed the arrow and left the head. He lived with it until during the caliphate of Muʿawiyah the wound erupted and he died after ʿaṣr. Ibn ʿUmar was approached and told, O Abu ʿAbd al-Raḥman, Rafiʿ has died. He invoked mercy upon him and said, The like of Rafiʿ is not taken out until those around Madinah among the villagers are notified. When we went out with his funeral to pray over him, Ibn ʿUmar came until he sat at the head of the grave. Then he mentioned the ḥadith. > **""O Rafiʿ, if you wish I shall remove the arrow and its head together, and if you wish I shall remove the arrow and leave the head, and I shall testify for you on the Day of Resurrection that you are a martyr""**"
"15857","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that Rafiʿ ibn Khadij died in the year seventy-three at Madinah."
"15858","Al-Waqidi reported that therein Rafiʿ ibn Khadij died at the beginning of the year, and Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah have mercy on him, attended his funeral, meaning the year seventy-three. Rafiʿ was eighty-six years old the day he died."
"15859","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that Rafiʿ ibn Khadij died in the year seventy-four at its beginning."
"15860","Maymun ibn Mihran reported that he heard Ibn ʿUmar say, I saw us at Fajj al-Rawḥaʾ in a raid the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raided; he saw me and supplicated for me with supplications for which the world and what is therein would not delight me in exchange."
"15861","Mujahid reported that Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah have mercy on him, witnessed the Conquest at twenty years of age, with an unruly horse and a heavy spear. Ibn ʿUmar went to cut fodder for his horse, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ʿAbdullah is a righteous man. > **""ʿAbdullah is a righteous man""**"
"15862","Isḥaq ibn ʿAbdullah al-Ṭafawi reported that Ibn ʿUmar would not mention the Messenger of Allah ﷺ but wept."
"15863","Nafiʿ reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that he would enliven the night with prayer, then say, O Nafiʿ, has dawn come. He would say, No, so he would resume prayer, then say, O Nafiʿ, has dawn come. I would say, Yes, so he would sit seeking forgiveness and supplicating until morning."
"15864","Nafiʿ reported that Ibn ʿUmar would distribute thirty thousand in a gathering, then a month would pass over him wherein he ate no morsel of meat. Burd said: I said to Nafiʿ, Did he eat meat. He said, When he fasted or traveled, for that was his most frequent food."
"15865","Nafiʿ reported that Ibn ʿUmar fell ill, and a bunch of grapes was bought for him for a dirham. A beggar came, and he said, Give it to him. Then a man went and bought it for a dirham, then brought it to him. The beggar came asking, and he said, Give it to him. Then a man went and bought it from him for a dirham. He wished to return until he was prevented, and had he known of that bunch he would not have tasted it."
"15866","Zayd ibn Aslam reported that Ibn ʿUmar passed by a shepherd of sheep and said, O shepherd of sheep, is there a slaughter animal. The shepherd said, Its master is not here. He said, Say the wolf ate it. The shepherd raised his head to heaven then said, Then where is Allah. Ibn ʿUmar said, By Allah, I am more deserving to say, Then where is Allah. Ibn ʿUmar bought the shepherd and bought the sheep, then freed him and gave him the sheep."
"15867","Al-Muṭʿam ibn Miqdam al-Ṣanʿani reported that al-Ḥajjaj ibn Yusuf wrote to ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar that it reached him that he seeks, and rule suits neither the incapable, nor the miserly, nor the jealous. Ibn ʿUmar wrote to him: As for your mention of rule that I seek it, I neither sought it nor is it in my mind. As for your mention of incapability, miserliness, and jealousy, whoever has gathered Allah's Book is not incapable, and whoever pays his wealth's zakah is not miserly. As for your mention of jealousy, the most deserving thing I am jealous over is my child lest another share him with me."
"15868","Malik reported that Ibn ʿUmar lived sixty years after the Prophet ﷺ with delegations of people coming to him."
"15869","Nafiʿ reported that a man came to Ibn ʿUmar and said, What prevents you from this affair when you are the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the son of the Commander of the Believers. He said, What prevents me from it is that Allah Almighty forbade to me the blood of my Muslim brother."
"15870","Makḥul reported that while he was with Ibn ʿUmar walking, a black man with a spear passed by him and placed the spear's butt between the forefinger and thumb of Ibn ʿUmar's foot. The old man was carried in and his leg swelled. Al-Ḥajjaj came visiting him and said, O Abu ʿAbd al-Raḥman, who struck you with this that I may take for you from him. He said, Allah; Allah shall take from him, Allah shall take from him. He said, How is it that arms are carried in Allah's sanctuary and security."
"15871","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, whose kunyah was Abu ʿAbd al-Raḥman, died at Makkah after the ḥajj and was buried at al-Muḥaṣṣab. Some people say at Fakhkh. His age when the Prophet ﷺ permitted him on the day of the Trench for combat was fifteen, and the Trench was in Shawwal in the year four, so his age the day he died was eighty-four years."
"15872","Malik ibn Anas reported that the age of Ibn ʿUmar the day he died was eighty-four years."
"15873","Al-Waqidi reported that Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما died in the year seventy-four and was buried at Fakhkh at eighty-four years of age."
"15874","Al-Ṭabarani said: Khalid ibn al-Walid, his kunyah Abu Sulayman, and he is Khalid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah ibn ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar ibn Makhzum ibn Yaqaẓah ibn Murrah ibn Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik. His mother was Lubabah bint al-Ḥarith ibn Ḥazan ibn Bujayr ibn al-Huzam ibn Ruwaybah ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Hilal ibn ʿAmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him a sword among Allah's swords."
"15875","Waḥshi ibn Ḥarb رضي الله عنه reported that Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه appointed Khalid ibn al-Walid over fighting the apostates, saying, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, An excellent servant of Allah and brother of the clan is Khalid ibn al-Walid, a sword among Allah's swords which Allah unsheathed against the disbelievers and hypocrites. > **""An excellent servant of Allah and brother of the clan is Khalid ibn al-Walid, a sword among Allah's swords which Allah unsheathed against the disbelievers and hypocrites""**"
"15876","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr reported that ʿUmar employed Abu ʿUbaydah over al-Sham and removed Khalid ibn al-Walid. He said: Khalid ibn al-Walid said, There has been sent over you the trustee of this nation; I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, The trustee of this nation is Abu ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarraḥ. Abu ʿUbaydah said, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, Khalid is a sword among Allah's swords, and an excellent youth of the clan. > **""The trustee of this nation is Abu ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarraḥ""**"
"15877","Nashirah ibn Sumayy al-Yazani reported that he heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭab رضي الله عنه on the day of al-Jabiyah addressing, and he said, I apologize to you for removing Khalid ibn al-Walid, for I ordered him to withhold this wealth for the weak among the Emigrants, yet he gave it to the mighty, the noble, and the eloquent. So I removed him and appointed Abu ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarraḥ. Abu ʿAmr ibn Ḥafṣ ibn al-Mughirah said, By Allah, you have not excused yourself, O ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭab; you removed a governor the Messenger of Allah ﷺ employed, sheathed a sword the Messenger of Allah ﷺ unsheathed, lowered a banner the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised, severed kinship, and envied your cousin. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭab said, You are near of kin, young in age, biased toward your cousin."
"15878","ʿAbdullah ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥman ibn ʿAwf complained of Khalid ibn al-Walid to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Prophet ﷺ said, O Khalid, do not harm a man among the people of Badr, for had you spent the like of Uḥud in gold you would not reach his deed. He said, They attack me so I answer them. He said, Do not harm Khalid, for he is a sword among Allah's swords which Allah poured upon the disbelievers. > **""O Khalid, do not harm a man among the people of Badr""**"
"15879","Qays, meaning Ibn Abi Ḥazim, reported that he was informed that the Prophet ﷺ said, Do not revile Khalid, for he is a sword among Allah's swords which Allah unsheathed against the disbelievers. > **""Do not revile Khalid, for he is a sword among Allah's swords which Allah unsheathed against the disbelievers""**"
"15880","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced the people of Muʾtah upon the minbar, saying, Then the banner was taken by a sword among Allah's swords. > **""Then the banner was taken by a sword among Allah's swords""**"
"15881","ʿAbdullah ibn Jaʿfar رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced the people of Muʾtah he said, Then the banner was taken by a sword among Allah's swords, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Allah granted victory upon him. > **""Then the banner was taken by a sword among Allah's swords, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Allah granted victory upon him""**"
"15882","Jaʿfar ibn ʿAbdullah ibn al-Ḥakam reported that Khalid ibn al-Walid lost a cap of his on the day of al-Yarmuk and said, Seek it. They did not find it, so he said, Seek it. They found it, and behold a worn cap. Khalid said, The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ʿumrah and shaved his head, and the people hastened to the sides of his hair, so I preceded them to his forelock and placed it in this cap. I never witnessed a battle with it with me but I was granted victory."
"15883","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed none equal to him and Khalid ibn al-Walid since they accepted Islam in his war."
"15884","Abu al-Safar reported that Khalid ibn al-Walid alighted at al-Ḥirah upon the chief of Banu al-Marazibah, and they said to him, Beware poison; the non-Arabs may give you to drink. He said, Bring it to me. It was brought and he took it in his hand, then swallowed it saying, In the name of Allah. It did not harm him at all."
"15885","Qays, meaning Ibn Abi Ḥazim, reported that Khalid ibn al-Walid said, No night wherein a bride is led to my house, whom I love and wherein I am given tidings of a boy, is more beloved to me than a severely frosty night in a detachment among the Emigrants with which I attack the enemy at dawn."
"15886","Qays, meaning Ibn Abi Ḥazim, reported that Khalid ibn al-Walid said, Jihad in the path of Allah kept me much from recitation."
"15887","Abu Waʾil reported that when death came to Khalid ibn al-Walid he said, I sought killing yet it was not decreed for me but to die upon my bed. No deed of mine is more hopeful than there is no deity but Allah, and I shield myself with it. Then he said, When I die, look to my arms and my horse and make them equipment in the path of Allah."
"15888","Yunus ibn Abi Isḥaq reported that they entered upon Khalid ibn al-Walid visiting him, and one said, He is in the race. He said, Yes, by Allah, I seek aid upon that."
"15889","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that Khalid ibn al-Walid died about the year twenty-one."
"15890","Rashid, freedman of Ḥabib ibn Aws al-Thaqafi, reported that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ narrated to him from his mouth to his mouth: When we turned from the Confederates at the Trench, I gathered men among Quraysh who regarded my place and heard from me, saying to them, By Allah, you know I see Muḥammad's affair rising with a great remarkable ascendancy, and I have seen a matter, so what do you deem concerning it. They said, What have you seen. I said, I deem we join the Negus and stay with him; if Muḥammad triumphs over our people we shall be with the Negus, for to be under his hand is more beloved to us than to be under Muḥammad's hand, and if our people triumph we are known, and only good shall come to us from them. They said, This is the opinion. He said: I said to them, Gather for me what is gifted, and the most beloved gift to him from our land was hides. We gathered many hides for him, then went out until we came to him. By Allah, while we were with him, ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamri came, whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sent to him concerning Jaʿfar and his companions. When he entered to him and left from him, he said: I said to my companions, This is ʿAmr ibn Umayyah; if I enter upon the Negus and ask him for him and he gives him to me, I shall strike his neck. If I do that, Quraysh shall see I have sufficed them by killing Muḥammad's messenger. He said: I entered upon him and prostrated to him as I used to do, and he said, Welcome to my friend; have you gifted me something from your land. He said: I said, Yes, O king. Then I said, O king, I have gifted you many hides. Then I presented them to him, and they pleased him and he desired them. Then I said, O king, I saw a man leave from you, the messenger of an enemy man of ours, so give him to me that I may kill him, for he has struck among our nobles and best. He said: He grew angry, stretched his hand, and struck his nose with a blow I thought he broke it. Had the earth split for me I would have entered it in fear of him. Then I said, O king, by Allah, had I thought you disliked this I would not have asked it. He said, Do you ask me to give you the messenger of a man to whom comes the Greatest Law that came to Musa, that you may kill him. He said: I said, O king, is it thus. He said, Woe to you, O ʿAmr, obey me and follow him, for by Allah he is upon truth, and he shall triumph over whoever opposes him as Musa triumphed over Pharaoh and his troops. He said: Will you pledge to him for me upon Islam. He said, Yes. He stretched his hand, and he pledged to him upon Islam. Then I went out to my companions with my opinion changed from what I was upon, concealing my Islam from my companions. Then I went out heading to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and met Khalid ibn al-Walid before the Conquest, coming from Makkah. I said, O Abu Sulayman. He said, By Allah, the course is clear; the man is a prophet; go and accept Islam, until when. He said: I said, By Allah, I came only to accept Islam. He said: We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Khalid ibn al-Walid advanced, accepted Islam, and pledged. Then I drew near and said, O Messenger of Allah, I pledge to you on condition You forgive me what preceded of my sin, not mentioning what comes after. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O ʿAmr, pledge, for Islam effaces what was before it, and emigration effaces what was before it. He said: I pledged to him, then departed. Ibn Isḥaq said: One I do not suspect narrated to me that ʿUthman ibn Ṭalḥah ibn Abi Ṭalḥah was with them both, accepting Islam when they accepted Islam. > **""O ʿAmr, pledge, for Islam effaces what was before it, and emigration effaces what was before it""**"
"15891","ʿAlqamah ibn Ramthah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ to al-Baḥrayn. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out in an expedition and we went out with him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dozed and said, May Allah have mercy upon ʿAmr. We mentioned every one named ʿAmr. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dozed and said, May Allah have mercy upon ʿAmr. He said: Then he dozed a third time and awoke saying, May Allah have mercy upon ʿAmr. We said, O Messenger of Allah, which ʿAmr is this. He said, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ. We said, What is his affair. He said, When I called people to charity he would come and give abundantly thereof. I would say, O ʿAmr, whence is this for you. He said, From Allah. ʿAmr spoke truth; he has with Allah much good. Zuhayr ibn Qays said: When the Prophet ﷺ passed away I said, I shall cleave to this one of whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, He has with Allah much good. > **""May Allah have mercy upon ʿAmr""**"
"15892","Muḥammad ibn Isḥaq reported that the Islam of ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and ʿUthman ibn Ṭalḥah was at the Negus, and they came to Madinah in Ṣafar in the year eight of the emigration. I say: Their Islam on one day is known, but the Islam of Khalid and ʿUthman ibn Ṭalḥah at the Negus I found only from Ibn Isḥaq from his statement."
"15893","Rafiʿ ibn Abi Rafiʿ al-Ṭaʾi reported that during the raid of Dhat al-Salasil the Messenger of Allah ﷺ employed ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ over an army among whom were Abu Bakr. He mentioned the ḥadith."
"15894","Ṭalḥah, meaning Ibn ʿUbaydullah رضي الله عنه, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, O ʿAmr, you are of sound judgment in Islam. > **""O ʿAmr, you are of sound judgment in Islam""**"
"15895","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, The two sons of al-ʿAṣ are believers, and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ is in Paradise. > **""The two sons of al-ʿAṣ are believers, and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ is in Paradise""**"
"15896","Ibn Buraydah reported that ʿUmar said to Abu Bakr when he escorted ʿAmr, Will you increase people in fire; do you not see what this one does with people. He said, Leave him, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ only placed him over us for his knowledge of war."
"15897","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to him saying, Take your garments and your arms then come to me. He said: I came to him while he performed ablution, and he raised his gaze upon me then lowered it, saying, I wish to send you upon an army, and Allah shall keep you safe and enrich you, and I desire for you a righteous share of wealth. I said, O Messenger of Allah, I did not accept Islam for wealth, but I accepted Islam desiring Islam and to be with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, O ʿAmr, how excellent lawful wealth is for the righteous man. > **""O ʿAmr, how excellent lawful wealth is for the righteous man""**"
"15898","Muḥammad ibn al-Aswad ibn Khalaf reported that they sat in al-Ḥijr with people among Quraysh when it was said, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ arrived tonight. He said: No sooner did he enter upon us than we extended our gazes to him. He circumambulated, then prayed two units in al-Ḥijr, saying, Did you backbite me. We said, We mentioned you only with good; we mentioned you and Hisham ibn al-ʿAṣ, asking which is more excellent. Some said, This one, and some of us said, Hisham. He said, I shall inform you of that; we accepted Islam, loved the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and advised him. Then he mentioned the day of al-Yarmuk, saying, I took the tent pole, then washed, perfumed, then shrouded myself. We offered ourselves to Allah Almighty, and He accepted him, so he is better than me, saying it three times."
"15899","Abu Nawfal ibn Abi ʿAqrab reported that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ grieved intensely at death. When his son ʿAbdullah saw that he said, O Abu ʿAbdullah, what is this grief, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would draw you near and employ you. He said, O my son, that was, and I shall inform you of that: By Allah, I know not whether that was love or conciliating me, but I testify concerning two men that he left the world loving them both: the son of Sumayyah, and the son of Umm ʿAbd. When the matter pressed him he placed his hand where the fetters are of his chin, saying, O Allah, You commanded us and we neglected, You forbade us and we rode on, and only Your forgiveness encompasses us. That was his constant utterance until he died."
"15900","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that ʿAmr died in Egypt on the day of Fiṭr in the year forty-two."
"15901","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ, whose kunyah was Abu ʿAbdullah, died in Egypt on the night of Fiṭr in the year forty-three, and was buried on the day of Fiṭr. His son ʿAbdullah prayed over him, and his age was about a hundred years."
"15902","Ṭalḥah, meaning Ibn ʿUbaydullah رضي الله عنه, reported: Shall I not inform you of something from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I heard him say, ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ is among the righteous of Quraysh, and what an excellent household: Abu ʿAbdullah, Umm ʿAbdullah, and ʿAbdullah. > **""ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ is among the righteous of Quraysh, and what an excellent household: Abu ʿAbdullah, Umm ʿAbdullah, and ʿAbdullah""**"
"15903","ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, What an excellent household: Abu ʿAbdullah, Umm ʿAbdullah, and ʿAbdullah. > **""What an excellent household: Abu ʿAbdullah, Umm ʿAbdullah, and ʿAbdullah""**"
"15904","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: I was one day with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in his house, and he said, Do you know who is with us in the house. I said, Who, O Messenger of Allah. He said, Jibril عليه السلام. I said, Peace be upon you, O Jibril, and Allah's mercy. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, He has returned your greeting. > **""He has returned your greeting""**"
"15905","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that a goodness he knows today is more beloved to him than its double with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerned with the Hereafter, caring not for the world, while today the world has inclined with them."
"15906","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullah ibn Numayr reported that ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr died in the year sixty-five."
"15907","Yaḥya ibn Bukayr reported that ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAṣ, whose kunyah was Abu Muḥammad, died in Egypt and was buried in his house in the year sixty-five. One saying states the year sixty-eight, and his age was seventy-two years, or ninety-two years, Yaḥya ibn Bukayr doubting between seventy and ninety."
"15908","Al-Ṭabarani said: Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan ibn Ḥarb ibn Umayyah ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manaf, his kunyah Abu ʿAbd al-Raḥman رضي الله عنه. His mother was Hind bint ʿUtbah ibn Rabiʿah ibn ʿAbd Shams ibn ʿAbd Manaf. Her mother was Ṣafiyyah bint Umayyah ibn Ḥarithah ibn al-Awqaṣ among Banu Sulaym. Her mother was the daughter of Nawfal ibn ʿAbd Manaf. Her mother was the daughter of Jabir ibn Naṣr ibn Malik ibn Ḥisl ibn ʿAmir ibn Luʾayy. Her mother was the daughter of al-Ḥarith ibn Ḥabib ibn Khuzaymah ibn Naṣr ibn Malik ibn Ḥisl ibn ʿAmir. Her mother was the daughter of Saʿid ibn Sahm."
"15909","Isḥaq ibn Yasar reported that he saw Muʿawiyah at al-Abṭaḥ white of head and beard as though snow."
"15910","Khalid ibn Maʿdan reported that Muʿawiyah was tall, fair, and bald."
"15911","Aslam, freedman of ʿUmar, reported that Muʿawiyah came to them, the fairest of people and most beautiful."
"15912","Bakkar ibn Muḥammad ibn Rafiʿ reported that Muʿawiyah said, No Qurashi woman bore to a Qurashi man better for her in her world than me. Maʿd ibn Yazid said, No Qurashi woman bore to a Qurashi man better for her in her religion than Muḥammad ﷺ. And no Qurashi woman bore to a Qurashi man worse for her in her world than you. He said, Why. He said, For you accustomed her to a custom, and as though I see them seeking it from another than you, and as though I see them fallen in the road. He said, Woe to you, by Allah, I conceal from her myself such-and-such."
"15913","Al-Shaʿbi reported that Muʿawiyah left al-Sham intending Makkah and alighted at a station between Makkah and Madinah called al-Abwaʾ. He looked into an old well and was struck with facial palsy, so he hastened until he entered Makkah. The chamberlain came to him saying, O Commander of the Believers, the people are at the door, not a face missing. He said, Then spread for me. He said: Then he called for a turban and wrapped his head and one side of his face with it, then went out, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said, To proceed, if I am healed then the righteous before me were healed, and I hope to be among them. If a limb of mine has sickened, my sound parts are countless. If some of your elite find fault with me, I was compassionate toward your common folk. I have no right to wish of Allah more than He gave me, so may Allah have mercy upon a man who supplicates for my health. Voices rose with supplication and he wept. Marwan said to him, What makes you weep, O Commander of the Believers. He said, I revisited what you turned from. He said, My age has grown, my bone thinned, tears multiplied in my eye, I was struck in my best and what appears of me, and but for my desire concerning Yazid I would see my course."
"15914","Saʿid ibn ʿAmr ibn Saʿid ibn al-ʿAṣ reported that Muʿawiyah took the water vessel after Abu Hurayrah, following the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while Abu Hurayrah complained. While he performed ablution for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he raised his head to him once or twice as he performed ablution, saying, O Muʿawiyah, if you are entrusted with rule then fear Allah and be just. He said: I ever thought I would be tried with office for the Messenger of Allah's saying until I was tried. > **""O Muʿawiyah, if you are entrusted with rule then fear Allah and be just""**"
"15915","ʿAʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that on the day of Umm Ḥabibah with the Prophet ﷺ, one knocked at the door. The Prophet ﷺ said, See who this is. They said, Muʿawiyah. He said, Permit him. He entered with a pen upon his ear writing with it, and he said, What is this pen upon your ear, O Muʿawiyah. He said, A pen I prepared for Allah and His Messenger. He said, May Allah reward you well on behalf of your Prophet; by Allah, I employed you as a scribe only by revelation from Allah Almighty, and I do nothing small or great but by revelation from Allah Almighty. How shall you be when Allah clothes you a garment, meaning rule. Umm Ḥabibah stood and sat before him, saying, O Messenger of Allah, shall Allah clothe my brother a garment. He said, Yes, but therein are faults, faults, faults. She said, O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah for him. He said, O Allah, guide him with guidance, keep him from ruin, and forgive him in the Hereafter and the first. > **""O Allah, guide him with guidance, keep him from ruin, and forgive him in the Hereafter and the first""**"
"15916","ʿAbdullah ibn Busr رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought permission of Abu Bakr and ʿUmar in a matter, saying, Advise me. They said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, Advise me. They said, Allah and His Messenger know best. He said, Call Muʿawiyah to me. Abu Bakr and ʿUmar said, Was there not in the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and two men among Quraysh what executes their affair, until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to a youth among Quraysh's youths. When he stood before him he said, Attend to your affair, or witness your affair, for he is strong and trustworthy. > **""Attend to your affair, for he is strong and trustworthy""**"
"15917","Al-ʿIrbad ibn Sariyah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, O Allah, teach Muʿawiyah writing and arithmetic, and shield him from punishment. > **""O Allah, teach Muʿawiyah writing and arithmetic, and shield him from punishment""**"
"15918","Maslamah ibn Mukhallad رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to Muʿawiyah, O Allah, teach him writing and arithmetic, and establish him in the lands. > **""O Allah, teach him writing and arithmetic, and establish him in the lands""**"
"15919","In another narration also: And shield him from evil punishment. > **""And shield him from evil punishment""**"
"15920","Abu al-Dardaʾ رضي الله عنه reported that he saw none after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ more resembling the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in prayer than their commander here, meaning Muʿawiyah."
"15921","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he saw none among people after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ more majestic than Muʿawiyah."
"15922","Ibn ʿAbbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Jibril عليه السلام came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, O Muḥammad, commend Muʿawiyah, for he is trustworthy over Allah's Book, and an excellent trustee he is. > **""O Muḥammad, commend Muʿawiyah, for he is trustworthy over Allah's Book, and an excellent trustee he is""**"
"15923","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon Umm Ḥabibah with Muʿawiyah's head in her lap while she kissed him, saying to her, Do you love him. She said, Why should I not love my brother. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, Allah and His Messenger love him. > **""Allah and His Messenger love him""**"
"15924","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that Muʿawiyah would write before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"15925","ʿAwf ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that he napped in a church at Ariḥa, then a mosque prayed in, and ʿAwf ibn Malik awoke from his sleep with a lion in the house walking to him. He rose startled to his arms, and the lion said to him, Hush; I was only sent to you with a message to convey. I said, Who sent you. He said, Allah sent me to you to inform Muʿawiyah the traveler that he is among the people of Paradise. I said, Which Muʿawiyah. He said, Son of Abu Sufyan."
"15926","Al-Aʿmash reported that had you seen Muʿawiyah you would say, This is the Mahdi."
"15927","Yazid ibn al-Aṣamm reported that ʿAli رضي الله عنه said, My slain and Muʿawiyah's slain are in Paradise."
"15928","Thabit, freedman of Abu Sufyan, reported that he raided with Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan the land of Rome, and a leprous sore appeared in his saddle. He called, O servants of Allah the Muslims. The first to answer was Muʿawiyah, who alighted and the people alighted saying, We suffice the commander. He said, It reached me that the first to aid is Jibril, so I wished to be second."
"15929","Mujalid ibn Saʿid reported: May Allah have mercy upon Muʿawiyah; how intense was his love for the Arabs."
"15930","Qays, meaning Ibn Abi Ḥazim, reported that Muʿawiyah said to his brother, Ride behind, but he refused. He said, Evil is your training. Abu Sufyan said, Leave your brother."
"15931","Abu Nuʿaym reported that Muʿawiyah died in the year sixty."
"15932","Al-Layth, meaning Ibn Saʿd, reported that Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan died four nights past of Rajab in the year sixty, aged between seventy and eighty."
"15933","Al-Ṭabarani said: ʿAbdullah ibn Qays, Abu Musa al-Ashʿari, ally of the family of ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd Shams; his Islam was at Makkah, and he migrated to Abyssinia until he came at the time of Khaybar. It is said Abu Musa died in the year fifty and was buried at al-Tutah two miles from al-Kufah."
"15934","Shabab al-ʿUṣfuri reported that Abu Musa governed al-Kufah, with family and a house there facing the mosque. Abu Musa died in the year fifty-one. His lineage, he said: Abu Musa ʿAbdullah ibn Qays al-Ashʿari, he is ʿAbdullah ibn Qays ibn Ḥiṣn ibn Ḥarb ibn ʿAmir ibn Tamim ibn Bakr ibn ʿAmir ibn ʿAdi ibn Waʾil ibn Najiyah ibn Jamahir ibn al-Ashʿar ibn Udud ibn ʿUrayb ibn Yashjub ibn Zayd ibn Kahlan ibn Sinan ibn Yashjub ibn Yaghuth ibn Qaḥṭan."
"15935","Qays ibn al-Rabiʿ ibn Abi Musa reported that Abu Musa died in the year fifty-two."
"15936","Saʿid ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAziz reported that Abu Musa al-Ashʿari came to the Prophet ﷺ at Khaybar, and the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for the eldest and youngest of the ship's people. Abu ʿAmir used to say, I am the eldest of the ship's people and my son the youngest. Saʿid said: Therein were Abu ʿAmir, Abu Malik, Abu Musa, and Kaʿb ibn ʿAṣim; they went out at al-Abwaʾ."
"15937","Ibn Isḥaq reported that Abu Musa al-Ashʿari was among those who migrated to Abyssinia, staying therein until the Prophet ﷺ sent to the Negus ʿAmr ibn Umayyah, who placed them in two ships and brought them to Khaybar after al-Ḥudaybiyah."
"15938","Ibn Buraydah reports from his father Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet ﷺ took him by the hand into the mosque at night where a man was reciting, then heard the man supplicating with the testimony of the Unique, the Eternal, who begets not nor is begotten. The Prophet ﷺ swore that the man had asked by the Greatest Name, and on the next night identified the reciter as al-Ash'ari, a believing, repentant man granted a melodious voice. > **""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, he has asked Allah by His Greatest Name, which when asked by He gives, and when supplicated by He answers.""**"
"15939","Mihjan ibn al-Adra' reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took his hand up Uhud, looked over Madinah, foretold that its people would abandon it at its most flourishing and that the Dajjal would find an angel with drawn sword at every pass, then descended to the mosque where a man stood praying and reciting, whom he praised as devoted and forbearing, and at home repeated that the best of the religion is its easiest. > **""The best of your religion is its easiest, the best of your religion is its easiest.""**"
"15940","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that Abu Musa was granted a share of the melodious voice of Dawud, peace be upon him. > **""Abu Musa has indeed been given a flute from the flutes of Dawud.""**"
"15941","Salamah ibn Qays reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed by Abu Musa while he was reciting and remarked upon the gift he had been given from the family of Dawud. > **""This one has been granted a share from the flutes of the family of Dawud.""**"
"15942","Abu Musa reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed by him one night while he recited in his house, accompanied by 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and the two stood listening; in the morning the Prophet ﷺ told him so, and Abu Musa replied that had he known of his presence he would have beautified his recitation for him. > **""O Abu Musa, I passed by you last night with 'A'ishah while you were reciting in your house, and we stood listening to your recitation.""**"
"15943","Anas reports that Abu Musa sat in his house reciting to a gathered people, and a man informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked to be seated where none could see him, listened to the recitation, and likened it to the melody of the family of Dawud. > **""He recites upon a flute from the flutes of the family of Dawud.""**"
"15944","Al-Bara' reports that the Prophet ﷺ heard Abu Musa reciting and likened his voice to the melodies of the family of Dawud. > **""It is as though the voice of this man is from the flutes of the family of Dawud.""**"
"15945","'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, hearing Abu Musa al-Ash'ari recite, addressed his companions concerning the gift granted to their brother. > **""Your brother has been granted a share from the flutes of the family of Dawud.""**"
"15946","Al-Sha'bi reports that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote in his bequest that none of his appointed governors should remain more than a year, except that al-Ash'ari should be retained for four years. > **""Let none of my governors be retained more than a year, but retain al-Ash'ari for four years.""**"
"15947","Abu 'Ubaydah details the lineage of al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah ibn Abi 'Amir, his kunyah Abu 'Abdullah, his mother from Banu Nasr ibn Mu'awiyah, his governorship of Basrah for about two years then Kufah where he died in the year fifty, and that his first campaign with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was al-Hudaybiyah. > **""His first campaign with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was al-Hudaybiyah.""**"
"15948","Yahya ibn Bukayr reports that al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah died in the year fifty. > **""Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah died in the year fifty.""**"
"15949","Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was among those who dug the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that they made a lahd, and that when the Prophet ﷺ was lowered in he dropped his axe, descended, and placed his hand upon the lahd. > **""I was among those who dug the grave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"15950","Ibn Marhab reports that four men descended into the grave of the Prophet ﷺ, among them 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, and that al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah was called the last of the people to be with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying he dropped his ring deliberately so that he might touch the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and be the last to be with him. > **""My ring fell from my hand, so that I might touch the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and be the last of the people to be with him.""**"
"15951","Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah reports that he was with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, when a horse was presented and a man rudely claimed to be a better horseman than Abu Bakr and his father; al-Mughirah seized the man by the head and struck his nose, and Abu Bakr refused retaliation against al-Mughirah, defender of Allah's servants. > **""I would sooner expel them from their homes than grant them retaliation against al-Mughirah.""**"
"15952","Qays al-Madani reports that Zayd ibn Thabit sent a questioner to Abu Hurayrah, recalling how he, Abu Hurayrah, and another sat supplicating in the mosque when the Prophet ﷺ joined them and said Amen to their supplications; Abu Hurayrah asked for the like of what his companions asked plus knowledge never forgotten, the Prophet ﷺ said Amen, and when the others asked the same he told them the Dawsi youth had preceded them in it. > **""The Dawsi youth has preceded you in it.""**"
"15953","Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Abu Hurayrah was bold in asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about matters that none besides him would ask about. > **""Abu Hurayrah was bold in asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about matters none besides him would ask.""**"
"15954","Abu al-Sha'tha' Sulaym reports that he came to Madinah and found Abu Ayyub narrating from Abu Hurayrah; when challenged that he had himself seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Ayyub replied that Abu Hurayrah had indeed heard. > **""He indeed heard.""**"
"15955","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that when he sees him his eyes are comforted and his soul delighted, and when he does not see him his soul is not at ease. > **""O Messenger of Allah, when I see you my eye is comforted and my soul is delighted.""**"
"15956","Abu Hurayrah reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him to spread his garment, narrated to him for most of the day, then spat into it, and after he gathered it to his belly he never forgot anything thereafter. > **""Spread your garment.""**"
"15957","Abu Hurayrah reports that the Qur'an was reviewed with the Prophet ﷺ once every year, and in the year in which he passed away it was reviewed with him twice. > **""He would review the Qur'an with the Prophet ﷺ once every year, and in the year he died he reviewed it with him twice.""**"
"15958","Abu Malik 'Ubayd reports, in what reached him, that the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for him that Allah bless 'Ubayd Abu Malik and raise him above many of the people. > **""O Allah, bless 'Ubayd Abu Malik and raise him above many of the people.""**"
"15959","Abu Hurayrah would ask about a man who entered Paradise though he never prayed, then identify him as Usayrim of Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal, 'Amr ibn Thabit ibn Waqsh; Mahmud ibn Labid explained that he refused Islam until the day of Uhud, then accepted it, seized his sword, fought among the people until wounds pinned him down, told his tribesmen he came out of desire for Islam and belief in Allah and His Messenger, died among them, and the Prophet ﷺ said he was of the people of Paradise. > **""He is of the people of Paradise.""**"
"15960","Salamah ibn al-Akwa', may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted him behind him many times, wiped his head many times, and sought forgiveness for him and his progeny as many times as the fingers on his hands. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted me behind him many times and sought forgiveness for me and my progeny.""**"
"15961","Abu Qatadah al-Harith ibn Rib'i, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the best of their horsemen is Abu Qatadah and the best of their foot soldiers is Salamah ibn al-Akwa'. > **""The best of our horsemen is Abu Qatadah, and the best of our foot soldiers is Salamah ibn al-Akwa'.""**"
"15962","'Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd reports that he heard Abu Usayd say he campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in twenty campaigns, one after another. > **""I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in twenty campaigns, one after another.""**"
"15963","Sulayman ibn Yasar reports that Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi lost his sight before the killing of 'Uthman and said praise belongs to Allah who let him enjoy his sight during the life of the Prophet ﷺ and withheld it when He decreed tribulation among His servants. > **""Praise belongs to Allah who let me enjoy my sight during the life of the Prophet ﷺ.""**"
"15964","Yahya ibn Bukayr reports that Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi, whose name was Malik ibn Rabi'ah, died in the year thirty at the age of ninety. > **""Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi died in the year thirty at the age of ninety.""**"
"15965","Zirr ibn Hubaysh reports that during the caliphate of 'Uthman ibn 'Affan he came in a delegation to meet Ubayy ibn Ka'b and the Companions, met Safwan ibn 'Assal al-Muradi, asked whether he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Safwan replied yes and that he had campaigned with him in twelve campaigns. > **""Yes, and I campaigned with him in twelve campaigns.""**"
"15966","Sa'd, the freedman of Abu Bakr, reports that a man complained to the Prophet ﷺ that Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal had lampooned him in verse, and the Prophet ﷺ told them to leave Safwan alone, for his tongue is sharp but his heart is pure. > **""Leave Safwan alone, for Safwan is foul of tongue but pure of heart.""**"
"15967","'Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan ibn Qudamah reports that his father Safwan migrated to the Prophet ﷺ in Madinah, pledged Islam, and the Prophet ﷺ extended his hand and wiped it; Safwan declared his love for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who replied that a man is with whom he loves. Safwan urged his people and nephews to emigrate with him, they refused, and he departed with his two sons 'Abd al-Rahman and 'Abdullah, whose pre-Islamic names the Prophet ﷺ changed. His nephew Nasr composed verses lamenting his departure, Safwan answered him in verse, and 'Abd al-Rahman elegized his father upon his death in Madinah, where he remained through the caliphate of 'Umar, who dispatched Jarir ibn 'Abdullah and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan as reinforcements to al-Muthanna ibn Harithah. > **""A man is with whom he loves.""**"
"15968","Abu Miskin reports through Talhah ibn Miskin from Talhah ibn al-Bara' that he came to the Prophet ﷺ asking to pledge allegiance, was tested three times over obedience even if commanded to sever his parents, and the Prophet ﷺ clarified that there is no severing of kinship in their religion but he wished no doubt to remain in his faith; Talhah embraced Islam excellently, fell ill, the Prophet ﷺ visited him unconscious and expected his passing that night, Talhah awoke at midnight, forbade summoning the Prophet ﷺ at that hour lest a mount sting him, sent his greetings and asked his forgiveness, died before dawn, and the Prophet ﷺ raised his hand in supplication for him. > **""O Allah, meet him while he laughs to You and You laugh to him.""**"
"15969","Husayn ibn Wahwah reports that when Talhah ibn al-Bara' met the Prophet ﷺ and offered unreserved obedience, the Prophet ﷺ, marveling at the youth, told him to go and slay his father; when he turned to do it the Prophet ﷺ called him back, declaring he was not sent to sever kinship. Talhah later fell ill, the Prophet ﷺ visited him on a cloudy cold evening, told his family he saw death in him and asked to attend his burial and prayer, but Talhah died before the Prophet ﷺ reached Banu Salim ibn 'Awf, having instructed that he be buried without summoning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for fear the Jews might harm him on his account; in the morning the Prophet ﷺ stood at his grave with the people in rows and supplicated for him. > **""O Allah, meet Talhah while You laugh to him and he laughs to You.""**"
"15970","Sa'id ibn Jumhan reports that he met Safinah at Batn Nakhlah in the days of al-Hajjaj, stayed eight nights asking him about the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, asked his name, and Safinah refused to give any but the name the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him; the Prophet ﷺ had told him to spread his cloak, the Companions loaded their baggage into it and onto him, and named him Safinah, after which he felt he could have carried the loads of many camels without heaviness. > **""Carry, for you are but a Safinah.""**"
"15971","'Imran al-Bajali reports from a freedman of Umm Salamah that he accompanied the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, reached a valley, and began helping the people across or carrying them, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ told him he was naught that day but a Safinah. > **""You were naught today but a Safinah.""**"
"15972","Safinah reports that he was at sea when their ship broke and they lost the way, and a lion confronted them while his companions hung back; he approached it, declared himself Safinah the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and that they had lost the way, and the lion walked before him until it set them upon the road, then nudged him as in farewell. In another wording his plank cast him into a thicket with a lion, he addressed it as Abu al-Harith and freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and it lowered its head and pushed him with its shoulder likewise. > **""I am Safinah, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we have lost the way.""**"
"15973","In some of its paths the report is given from Safinah from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to similar effect, though what is meant by attributing it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is unclear. > **""From Safinah, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, to similar effect.""**"
"15974","Abu al-Darda' reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he would surely be rivaled over some of his Companions at the Hawd, saying they are of his Companions only to be told he does not know what they innovated after him; Abu al-Darda' asked the Prophet ﷺ to supplicate that Allah not make him of them, and he replied that he is not of them. > **""You are not of them.""**"
"15975","Abu al-Darda' reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether he truly said that some of his nation would disbelieve after their faith, and the Prophet ﷺ confirmed it, adding that Abu al-Darda' is not of them. > **""Yes, O Abu al-Darda', and you are not of them.""**"
"15976","Khaythamah reports that Abu al-Darda' said he was a merchant before the Prophet ﷺ was sent, and after his sending he wished to combine trade and worship but it would not hold together, so he abandoned trade and devoted himself to worship. > **""I abandoned trade and devoted myself to worship.""**"
"15977","Abu Barzah al-Aslami reports that Julaybib would visit women in jest, that the Ansar would not marry off a widow until they knew whether the Prophet ﷺ had any need of her, and that the Prophet ﷺ asked an Ansari for his daughter not for himself but for Julaybib; the father consulted her mother, who refused Julaybib outright, but the maiden asked who had proposed, declared they must not reject the order of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and trusted he would never let her be lost. Her father consented, the Prophet ﷺ married her to Julaybib, then went out on campaign, and after Allah granted spoils he asked whom they missed; they said no one, but he said he missed Julaybib. They found him beside seven polytheists he had slain before they slew him; the Prophet ﷺ declared twice or thrice that he is of him and he is of Julaybib, laid him upon his forearms with no bed but the arms of the Prophet ﷺ until his grave was dug, placed him in it without recorded washing, and Thabit added that no widow among the Ansar was more richly provided for, after the Prophet ﷺ supplicated that goodness be poured upon her abundantly and her life never be one of toil. > **""He slew seven and then they slew him; this one is of me and I am of him.""**"
"15978","Anas reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ proposed to an Ansari girl on behalf of Julaybib, her father sought her mother's counsel, the mother refused Julaybib though she had already refused others for the girl, and the maiden listening in her chamber asked whether they would reject the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and bade them marry her to him if he was pleased with him for them. Her parents acknowledged her truthfulness, the father consented, the Prophet ﷺ declared his pleasure, married her to him, and soon the people of Madinah rushed out; Julaybib rode forth and was found slain with polytheists he had killed around him, and Anas attested that he saw his widow become among the most richly provided widows of Madinah. > **""If he is pleased with him for you, then marry him to him.""**"
"15979","Anas reports that a desert man named Zahir would bring gifts to the Prophet ﷺ, who would equip him on departing, saying Zahir is their countryside and they are his town; the Prophet ﷺ loved him though he was unsightly, once embraced him from behind while he sold his goods, and joked over who would buy the slave; Zahir said he would be found a worthless bargain, and the Prophet ﷺ replied that with Allah he is not worthless but precious. > **""But with Allah you are not worthless.""**"
"15980","Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd reports from Azhar ibn Haram al-Ashja'i, a desert man who regularly brought rarities and gifts to the Prophet ﷺ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him selling his goods in the market of Madinah, embraced him from behind his shoulder, and when he turned and kissed the Prophet's hand the Prophet ﷺ joked over who would buy the slave; the man said he would be found a poor bargain, and the Prophet ﷺ replied that with Allah he is profitable, adding that every town has its countryside and the countryside of the family of Muhammad is Zahir ibn Haram. > **""But with Allah you are profitable.""**"
"15981","'Uqbah ibn 'Amir reports that the Prophet ﷺ said of a man called Dhu al-Bijadayn that he is oft-returning to Allah, for he remembers Allah abundantly in the Qur'an and raises his voice in supplication. > **""He is oft-returning to Allah.""**"
"15982","Ibn al-Adra' reports that he guarded the Prophet ﷺ, who came out one night for some need, took him by the hand, passed a man praying aloud with the Qur'an, and suggested he might be showing off; on another night the narrator himself voiced the suspicion, and the Prophet ﷺ rejected it, declaring the man oft-returning, and behold it was 'Abdullah Dhu al-Bijadayn. > **""Nay, he is oft-returning to Allah.""**"
"15983","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he all but hears the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the expedition of Tabuk at the grave of 'Abdullah Dhu al-Bijadayn, with Abu Bakr and 'Umar, may Allah have mercy upon them, saying hand me your companion until he settled him in his lahd, then facing the qiblah and declaring he entered the evening pleased with him and asking Allah to be pleased with him. > **""O Allah, I have entered the evening pleased with him, so be pleased with him.""**"
"15984","Ibn 'Abbas reports that Dimam ibn Tha'labah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offering to charm him with incantations, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied with the praise of Allah, seeking His aid and refuge, the testimony of guidance and the two testimonies; Dimam, who had read the scriptures, the Torah, the Gospel, and the Psalms, declared he had never heard the like, asked him to repeat it, heard it again, and then embraced Islam. > **""Praise belongs to Allah; we seek His aid and seek refuge with Allah from the evils of our souls and the misdeeds of our works.""**"
"15985","Al-Tabarani identifies the man as Nu'aym ibn 'Abdullah ibn Asad of 'Adi ibn Ka'b, called al-Nahham because the Prophet ﷺ said he heard a soft voice in Paradise, and nahm means a soft sound. > **""I heard a soft voice in Paradise.""**"
"15986","Abu 'Ubaydah Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna reports that his Islam preceded the migration to Abyssinia and that he was slain at Ajnadayn in the land of Syria. > **""His Islam preceded the migration to Abyssinia, and he was slain at Ajnadayn.""**"
"15987","Al-Tabarani identifies him as 'Abdullah ibn al-Arqam ibn 'Abd Yaghuth of Zuhrah, his mother 'Amrah bint al-Arqam, who lost his sight before his death and served as scribe to the Prophet ﷺ and to Abu Bakr, 'Umar, 'Uthman, and 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them. > **""He was scribe to the Prophet ﷺ and to Abu Bakr, 'Umar, 'Uthman, and 'Ali.""**"
"15988","The source batch contains no transmitted Arabic text for this number, so no translation of matn or chain can be rendered from it here."
"15989","'Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi 'Awn reports that a man's letter came to the Prophet ﷺ, who told 'Abdullah ibn al-Arqam to answer on his behalf; he wrote the reply, read it to him, the Prophet ﷺ declared he had hit the mark and done well and supplicated for his success, and 'Umar would consult him when he ruled. > **""You have hit the mark and done well; O Allah, grant him success.""**"
"15990","'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As reports that he came in the delegation of Thaqif to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, donned his finery at the Prophet's door, and being the youngest volunteered to hold their mounts on their covenant to hold his turn for him; they entered, asked all they wished, and urged him to return to his family without entering, but he insisted upon his covenant, entered hastily, and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to supplicate that Allah grant him understanding in religion and knowledge. The Prophet ﷺ asked him to repeat his words, marveled that none of his companions had asked him such a thing, and appointed him leader over them and over those of his people he would come to. > **""Go, for you are leader over them and over those of your people you come to.""**"
"15991","In another brief wording he reports that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, asked him for a copy of the mushaf that was with him, and he gave it to him. > **""I asked him for a copy of the mushaf that was with him, and he gave it to me.""**"
"15992","Abu Hurayrah reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood upon the pulpit with a book, declaring he would give it to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger and whom Allah and His Messenger love, then called 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As to rise, and handed it to him. > **""Rise, O 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As.""**"
"15993","Abu Nadrah reports that he visited 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As during the ten days when he had set aside a house for hadith, watched a ram priced at twelve dirhams pass by, wished aloud he had twelve dirhams to sacrifice and feed his family, and upon leaving found the messenger of 'Uthman following him with a purse of fifty dirhams, the most blessed coins he ever saw, given in devotion by one who could spare and received in need by one who needed. > **""I never saw coins of greater blessing.""**"
"15994","Nawfal ibn Musahiq reports that while 'Uthman ibn Hunayf, a governor, spoke with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he angered him, so 'Umar seized a handful of gravel and pelted him, a stone cutting his forehead and drawing blood over his beard; 'Umar seemed to regret it and told him to wipe the blood, but 'Uthman replied he should not be alarmed, for what he had suffered from those entrusted to him was harsher, and 'Umar was pleased with him and increased him in good. > **""By Allah, what I have suffered from those you entrusted to me is harsher than what you have inflicted upon me.""**"
"15995","Jarir reports that as he neared Madinah he couched his camel, opened his baggage, donned his fine clothes, and entered to find the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preaching while the people stared at him; his neighbor told him the Prophet ﷺ had just mentioned him in the finest terms, announcing that there would enter upon them through that door a man of the best of Dhu Yaman with the touch of an angel upon his face, and Jarir praised Allah for His favor. > **""There shall enter upon you through this door a man of the best of Dhu Yaman; upon his face is the touch of an angel.""**"
"15996","Ibn 'Abbas reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the best of Dhu Yaman, bearing the touch of an angel, would appear before them, and Jarir ibn 'Abdullah appeared. > **""The best of Dhu Yaman shall appear before you, bearing the touch of an angel.""**"
"15997","'Abdullah ibn Damrah reports that while he sat with the Prophet ﷺ among many Companions, mostly of Yaman, the Prophet ﷺ announced that the best of Dhu Yaman would appear through that pass; each man hoped he would be of his household, then Jarir ibn 'Abdullah appeared, greeted the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions, was greeted by all, was bidden to sit upon the breadth of the Prophet's cloak, sat a while then departed, and when the Companions marveled at the honor shown him the Prophet ﷺ explained that he is the noble of his people and the noble of a people is to be honored when he comes. > **""When the noble of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"15998","Al-Bara' ibn 'Azib reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announce that the best of Dhu Yaman would come through that pass with the touch of an angel upon his face; every man wished it were of his kin, then a rider appeared, dismounted, took the Prophet ﷺ by the hand, greeted him, pledged allegiance, and emigrated. Asked who he was, he said Jarir ibn 'Abdullah al-Bajali, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated him beside him, wiped his head, face, chest, and belly until Jarir bent in modesty, supplicated blessings for him and his progeny, then wiped his head and back likewise in supplication. > **""Who are you?""**"
"15999","Jarir reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ offering allegiance upon emigration, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pledged him stipulating sincerity to every Muslim, which Jarir accepted. > **""And sincerity to every Muslim.""**"
"16000","'Ali ibn Abi Talib reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Jarir is of us, of the household, through and through, repeating it three times. > **""Jarir is of us, Ahl al-Bayt, through and through.""**"
"16001","Jarir reports that whenever delegations came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he would summon him and boast of him before them. > **""Whenever delegations came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he would summon me and boast of me before them.""**"
"16002","A son of Jarir ibn 'Abdullah reports that the sandal of Jarir ibn 'Abdullah was a cubit in length. > **""The sandal of Jarir ibn 'Abdullah was a cubit in length.""**"
"16003","Sulaym Abu al-Hudhayl reports that he was a groom at the door of Jarir ibn 'Abdullah, who would come out, mount his mule, and carry his servant behind him. > **""He would mount his mule and carry his servant behind him.""**"
"16004","Wa'il ibn Hujr reports that when the emergence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached them while they lived in great dominion and obedience, he renounced it and came desiring Allah and His Messenger; the Prophet ﷺ had already announced his coming, greeted him, spread his cloak for him, seated him with himself upon the pulpit, praised Allah and blessed the Prophets, and announced to the people that Wa'il ibn Hujr had come from distant Hadramawt obedient and uncoerced, desiring Allah, His Messenger, and his religion, a remnant of the sons of kings, and Wa'il affirmed his account as truth. > **""O people, this is Wa'il ibn Hujr who has come to you from a distant land, obedient and uncoerced, a remnant of the sons of kings.""**"
"16005","Wa'il ibn Hujr reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who announced that Wa'il had come not from desire nor fear but from love of Allah and His Messenger, spread his cloak for him, seated him beside himself, embraced him, raised him to the pulpit, and bade the people be gentle with him as one newly removed from kingship; when Wa'il complained his family had overpowered him in his property, the Prophet ﷺ promised to grant it and its double, and instructed him that in prayer a man places his hands level with his ears and a woman level with her breasts. > **""Be gentle with him, for he is newly removed from kingship.""**"
"16006","Wa'il ibn Hujr reports that he went as his people's delegate to Madinah, the Companions told him the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had announced his coming three days prior, the Prophet ﷺ welcomed him, drew his seat near, spread his cloak, gathered the people, ascended the pulpit with Wa'il below him, praised Allah, and announced him as the obedient remnant of kings from Hadramawt with blessings upon him and his father. He lodged him far from Madinah under Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan's charge, and on the way Mu'awiyah asked to ride behind him and to borrow his sandals against the scorching ground, but Wa'il refused both lest he be reproached for demeaning royal dignity. Before returning, the Prophet ﷺ granted him three letters: one distinguishing him above his people, one securing his household's properties, and one for him and his people enjoining prayer, zakah with its detailed dues, and forbidding false claims and treachery in Islam, with stated shares and the prohibition of intoxicants. Years later Mu'awiyah's commander Busr ibn Abi Artah seized him alive and brought him to Mu'awiyah, who honored him, debated him over 'Uthman, 'Ali, and withdrawal from the strife citing the Prophet's command to withdraw when two swords differ in Islam, and Wa'il refused governorship of Kufah after the Prophet ﷺ, as he had refused Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman, asking only to join the united body and withdraw from their differences. > **""O Wa'il, when two swords differ in Islam, withdraw from them both.""**"
"16007","Abu Hurayrah reports that he followed al-'Ala' ibn al-Hadrami to Bahrain and witnessed three traits he cannot say which is most wondrous: at the seashore al-'Ala' bade them name Allah and plunge, and they crossed with the water not wetting the camels' lower trappings; on the return in a waterless waste he bade them pray two rak'ahs, supplicated, and a shield-like cloud rained upon them so they drank and drew water; and when he died and they buried him in the sand, they returned fearing a beast would devour him and found no trace of him. > **""Name Allah and plunge in.""**"
"16008","Jubayr ibn Mut'im reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that had al-Mut'im ibn 'Adi, among those foul ones, come to him he would have interceded for him, whereupon Jubayr embraced Islam. > **""Had he come to me among those ones, I would have interceded for him.""**"
"16009","Al-Tabarani identifies Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, by his kunyah Abu 'Abdullah, as of Yaman of Himyar, a freedman of the family of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said to have been taken captive and purchased and manumitted by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, resident in Hims, who died in the year fifty-five. > **""Thawban was a freedman of the family of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and died in the year fifty-five.""**"
"16010","Halah reports that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he slept, the Prophet ﷺ awoke and drew Halah to his chest, uttering his name in joy, as though delighted by him for his kinship to Khadijah, may Allah be pleased with her. > **""Halah! Halah!""**"
"16011","Al-Bara' ibn 'Azib reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told Hassan ibn Thabit to lampoon the polytheists, for Allah the Exalted aids him with the Holy Spirit. > **""Lampoon the polytheists, for Allah the Exalted aids you with the Holy Spirit.""**"
"16012","Sa'id ibn Jubayr reports that a man came to Ibn 'Abbas announcing the arrival of the accursed Hassan, and Ibn 'Abbas replied he is no accursed one, for he strove with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with his tongue and his self. > **""He is no accursed one; he strove with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with his tongue and his self.""**"
"16013","'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that Abu Hind, the freedman of Banu Bayadah, was a cupper who cupped the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever wishes to look at a man in whose heart Allah has formed faith should look at Abu Hind, and he commanded that Abu Hind be married and married to. > **""Whoever wishes to look at a man in whose heart Allah has formed faith, let him look at Abu Hind.""**"
"16014","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Tabuk the sun rose with a radiance never seen before, Jibril came, and when the Prophet ﷺ asked him why, he replied that Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Laythi had died in Madinah that day and Allah had dispatched a thousand angels to pray over him, for he recited Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad abundantly by night and day, standing, sitting, and walking; Jibril offered to fold the earth so the Prophet ﷺ might pray over him, he consented, and prayed over him. > **""He would abundantly recite Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad by night and day, in his walking, standing, and sitting.""**"
"16015","Anas reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Jibril would come to him in the form of Dihyah al-Kalbi, and Anas added that Dihyah was a large, fair man. > **""Jibril would come to me in the form of Dihyah al-Kalbi.""**"
"16016","Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd reports that 'Utbah would say 'Irbad is better than him, and 'Irbad would say 'Utbah is better than him, for 'Utbah preceded him to the Prophet ﷺ by a year. > **""'Irbad is better than me, and 'Utbah is better than me.""**"
"16017","Abu Zayd reports that he campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in seven campaigns. > **""I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in seven campaigns.""**"
"16018","Abu Zayd reports that he fought with the Prophet ﷺ thirteen times, and Shu'bah added that he is the grandfather of 'Azrah. > **""I fought with the Prophet ﷺ thirteen times.""**"
"16019","Abu Zayd 'Amr ibn Akhtab al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked for water, he brought a cup containing water with a hair in it, he removed it, and the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for his beauty; thereafter he was seen at ninety-four with not a white hair in his beard. > **""O Allah, beautify him.""**"
"16020","Abu Zayd ibn Akhtab reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him may Allah beautify you, and he was a handsome man with fair streaks of grey. > **""May Allah beautify you.""**"
"16021","Damrah ibn Tha'labah reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ wearing two Yemeni garments, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he deemed those two garments would admit him to Paradise; he replied that if the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought forgiveness for him he would not sit until he removed them, the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for him, and he hastened away and removed them. > **""O Allah, forgive Damrah ibn Tha'labah.""**"
"16022","He also reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to supplicate for martyrdom, so the Prophet ﷺ supplicated that Allah forbid the blood of Ibn Tha'labah to the polytheists and disbelievers; thereafter he would charge into the midst of the enemy fancying the Prophet ﷺ before them until he reached him, his people warning him of his daring, and he lived long thereafter. > **""O Allah, forbid the blood of Ibn Tha'labah to the polytheists and disbelievers.""**"
"16023","Ma'qil ibn Yasar reports that he accompanied the Prophet ﷺ such-and-such a time. > **""I accompanied the Prophet ﷺ such-and-such a time.""**"
"16024","Al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar identifies Abu al-'As ibn al-Rabi', husband of the Prophet's daughter ﷺ and son of her maternal aunt, his mother Halah bint Khuwaylid, sister of Khadijah by her father, his lineage through 'Abd ibn Ma'is of Lu'ayy, his title Jarw al-Batha', the disputed reports over his given name as Muhashsham, Luqayt, or al-Qasim, the last deemed firmest, and his death in Dhu al-Hijjah of the year twelve. > **""Abu al-'As ibn al-Rabi' died in Dhu al-Hijjah of the year twelve.""**"
"16025","Ibn 'Abbas reports that Farwah ibn 'Amir al-Judhami sent to the Prophet ﷺ declaring his Islam and gifted him a white mule, though he was Caesar's governor over the neighboring Arabs at 'Umman; when Rome learned of it they imprisoned him, and in prison he composed verses of longing and resolve. When they gathered against him and crucified him at the waters of 'Afra' in Palestine, raised upon the wood he spoke verses asking that Salma be told of his state and conveying to the Muslims that his very bones submit to his Lord. > **""Convey to the leaders of the Muslims that I submit, my bones and my fingertips, to my Lord.""**"
"16026","Farwah ibn Musayk al-Muradi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him whether he grieved over his people's day and the day of Hamdan; he replied yes, for it meant the perishing of kith and kin, and the Prophet ﷺ replied that it is yet good for those of them who fear Allah. > **""Yet it is good for those of you who fear Allah.""**"
"16027","One of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reports that the Prophet ﷺ told his Companions that among them are men to whom he gives nothing, entrusting them to their faith, among them Furat ibn Hayyan. > **""Among you are men to whom I give nothing, entrusting them to their faith, among them Furat ibn Hayyan.""**"
"16028","'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said he gives to a people to conciliate them and entrusts others to what they hold or what Allah has placed in their hearts, among them Furat ibn Hayyan. > **""I give to a people to conciliate them, and I entrust a people to what Allah has placed in their hearts.""**"
"16029","Abu 'Ubayd reports that 'Imran ibn Husayn is of Banu Ghadirah, of Khuza'ah. > **""Imran ibn Husayn is of Banu Ghadirah of Khuza'ah.""**"
"16030","Al-Waqidi gives the lineage of 'Imran ibn Husayn ibn 'Ubayd ibn Khalaf through Ghadirah of Khuza'ah. > **""'Imran ibn Husayn ibn 'Ubayd of Khuza'ah.""**"
"16031","Al-Tabarani, through 'Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad, reports that 'Imran's kunyah is Abu Nujayd, that he and his father embraced Islam early, that he campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, remained among his people visiting Madinah often until the Prophet's passing ﷺ, then moved to Basrah where he lived till death leaving descendants, that Khalid ibn Tulayq of his line held its judgeship, and that the sound view is his father Husayn died a Muslim. > **""'Imran embraced Islam early with his father and campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"16032","Hilal ibn Yasaf reports that he came to Basrah, entered the mosque, and found a white-haired, white-bearded elder leaning against a pillar with a circle learning from him; asked who he was, they said 'Imran ibn Husayn. > **""That is 'Imran ibn Husayn.""**"
"16033","Muhammad ibn Sirin reports that none of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ who came to them was deemed superior to 'Imran ibn Husayn. > **""We deemed none of the Companions who came to us superior to 'Imran ibn Husayn.""**"
"16034","Sufyan reports that none like 'Imran ibn Husayn ever came to Basrah. > **""None like 'Imran ibn Husayn ever came to Basrah.""**"
"16035","Abu al-Aswad al-Du'ali reports that he came to Basrah where Abu Nujayd 'Imran ibn Husayn was, whom 'Umar ibn al-Khattab had sent to teach the people of Basrah their religion. > **""'Umar sent 'Imran ibn Husayn to teach the people of Basrah.""**"
"16036","Al-Hakam ibn al-A'raj reports that 'Imran ibn Husayn said he never touched his private part with his right hand since pledging with it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""I have never touched my private part with my right hand since I pledged with it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"16037","'Ata' ibn Abi Maymunah, freedman of 'Imran ibn al-Husayn, reports that 'Imran's brother was killed in the pre-Islamic age and he slew seventy in recompense. > **""'Imran's brother was slain in the pre-Islamic age, and he slew seventy for him.""**"
"16038","Harun ibn 'Abdullah al-Hammal reports that 'Imran ibn Husayn died in the year fifty-two. > **""'Imran ibn Husayn died in the year fifty-two.""**"
"16039","Abu Ishaq reports from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib that he campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in fifteen campaigns. > **""I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in fifteen campaigns.""**"
"16040","He also reports that he heard Zayd ibn Arqam say he campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in some ten campaigns. > **""I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in some ten campaigns.""**"
"16041","'Umayr ibn Sa'd reports that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, appointed him governor of Hims, and after a year without word 'Umar wrote suspecting betrayal and ordering him to come with whatever public revenue he held. 'Umayr packed his bag with his provisions and bowl, hung his water-skin, seized his staff, and walked from Hims to Madinah, arriving pale, dusty, and long-haired. He greeted 'Umar, declared himself sound in body and pure of blood dragging the world by its horns, and when 'Umar expected wealth he displayed only his bag, bowl, water-skin, and staff, declaring the world but follows his gear. He explained he gathered the righteous of the town, entrusted collection of their revenue to them, and set it in its places, bringing nothing to 'Umar. 'Umar ordered his commission renewed, but 'Umayr refused ever to work for him or any after him, reproached the suspicion cast upon him, and returned home miles from Madinah. 'Umar still suspected him and sent al-Harith as a guest-spy with a hundred dinars for dire need; al-Harith found 'Umayr delousing his shirt by a wall, was hosted with meager fare, saw his household's poverty, offered the gold, and 'Umayr refused it, bidding him return it; al-Harith reported his utter destitution, and 'Umar vindicated him and summoned him back, but 'Umayr declined office altogether. > **""I am sound in body, pure of blood, with the world dragging behind me.""**"
"16042","Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Qari reports from his father that Hakim ibn Hizam lived one hundred twenty years, sixty in Islam and sixty in pre-Islamic times, and when he swore emphatically he would say by the One who favored Hakim that he not be slain a disbeliever at Badr, he would not do such-and-such, and he would refrain. > **""Hakim ibn Hizam lived one hundred twenty years, sixty in Islam and sixty before it.""**"
"16043","Mus'ab ibn Thabit reports that it reached him that Hakim ibn Hizam attended the day of 'Arafah with a hundred slaves, a hundred camels, a hundred cattle, and a hundred sheep, declaring all for Allah, manumitting the slaves and ordering the rest sacrificed. > **""All of this is for Allah.""**"
"16044","Hakim ibn Hizam sold a house of his to Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with them, for sixty thousand, and when they said Mu'awiyah had been cheated he replied he had taken it in pre-Islamic times for a wineskin, called them to witness it is now for the cause of Allah, the poor, and slaves, and asked which of them is cheated. > **""I call you to witness that it is for the cause of Allah, the poor, and slaves.""**"
"16045","In another wording the price was a hundred thousand. > **""He sold it for a hundred thousand and gave it in charity.""**"
"16046","Abu Hazim reports that none in Madinah was heard to carry more in the cause of Allah than Hakim ibn Hizam; two Bedouins seeking a mount for the cause were directed to him in his home, found him in cheap Egyptian garb gathering usable rags with his staff for camel gear with his two servants, despaired of him as a mere rag-picker, but watched him buy two fine, fat, pregnant she-camels with their gear, stuff them with food, grain, and fat, add expenses, and grant them all to the two men, who marveled at the rag-picker's bounty. > **""By Allah, I never saw a rag-picker better than today.""**"
"16047","Al-Tabarani identifies 'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl ibn Hisham of Makhzum, his mother Umm Mujalid of Banu Hilal, his Islam in the year of the Conquest, and his martyrdom at Yarmuk in the caliphate of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, or by another account at Ajnadayn. > **""'Ikrimah embraced Islam in the year of the Conquest and was martyred at Yarmuk.""**"
"16048","Mus'ab ibn 'Abdullah al-Zubayri reports that 'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl left no descendants, fled on the day of the Conquest until his wife Umm Hakim bint Hisham secured safety for him from the Prophet ﷺ, overtook him in Yaman, and brought him back; when the Prophet ﷺ saw him he rose, embraced him, and welcomed the emigrant rider. > **""Welcome to the emigrant rider.""**"
"16049","Abu Mulaykah reports that when 'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl swore emphatically he would say by the One who saved him on the day of Badr, and he would take the mushaf, place it upon his face, and say my Lord's speech, my Lord's speech. > **""My Lord's speech, my Lord's speech.""**"
"16050","'Ikrimah ibn Abi Jahl reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ welcomed him as the emigrant rider when he came to him, and he pledged never to withhold any spending against the Prophet ﷺ but to spend it in the cause of Allah. > **""Welcome to the emigrant rider, welcome to the emigrant rider.""**"
"16051","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he saw a neck of Abu Jahl in Paradise, and when 'Ikrimah embraced Islam he declared this is he. > **""I saw a neck of Abu Jahl in Paradise; this is he.""**"
"16052","'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr reports that when the people began Hajj in the year nine, 'Urwah ibn Mas'ud came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a Muslim, sought leave to return to his people, was warned they might kill him, insisted they would wake him were he asleep, was permitted, returned by night, was greeted by Thaqif, invited them to Islam, was accused, angered, and answered with what he never expected, then at dawn stood upon his rooftop, called the adhan and testified, and a man of Thaqif shot him dead; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his likeness is that of the man of Yasin, who called his people to Allah and they killed him. > **""The likeness of 'Urwah is that of the man of Yasin; he called his people to Allah and they killed him.""**"
"16053","Ibn 'Abbas reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent 'Urwah ibn Mas'ud to al-Ta'if, a man shot him with an arrow and killed him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said how like he is to the man of Yasin. > **""How like he is to the man of Yasin.""**"
"16054","'Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an reports that 'Urwah ibn Mas'ud told his people at Hudaybiyah that he had seen and addressed kings, asked to be sent to Muhammad, came to him there, spoke while touching the beard of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah stood armed at his head warning him to withhold his hand; 'Urwah recognized him and recalled his treachery still unforgiven. He returned telling his people he had never seen the like of Muhammad ﷺ, no king, had seen the sacrificial animals held back eating their hair, and warned a calamity would strike them. He withdrew with his company, ascended the wall of al-Ta'if, testified there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is His Messenger, was shot dead by one of his people, and the Prophet ﷺ praised Allah for placing in his nation the like of the man of Yasin. > **""Praise belongs to Allah who has placed in my nation the like of the man of Yasin.""**"
"16055","Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to his people to call them to Allah and present the laws of Islam; he found them after watering and milking their camels, they welcomed him as Sudayy ibn 'Ajlan, accused him of apostasy, and he declared his faith and mission. As he invited them they brought a bowl of blood, gathered about it, ate from it, and invited him; he warned them it was forbidden save what is duly slaughtered, reciting the verse of al-Ma'idah, but they refused. Thirsty and turbaned, denied even water and left to die of thirst, he wrapped his head, slept in the scorching heat, and a visitor in dream gave him a glass cup of unsurpassed drink; he drank, awoke, and never knew thirst again after that draught. > **""By Allah, I never thirsted nor knew thirst after that draught.""**"
"16056","Abu Umamah reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Bahilah; he found them at food, they welcomed and honored him and invited him to eat, but he declared he came to forbid that food as the envoy of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ calling them to faith. They denied him and reviled him, so he departed hungry and thirsty in severe exhaustion, slept, and was given milk in dream which he drank till satiated and great of belly. His people then said a noble of theirs had been rejected, sent him desired food and drink, but he refused, declaring Allah had fed and watered him, bade them look at his state, they looked, and believed in him and in what he brought from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Allah has fed me and given me drink; look at the state I am in.""**"
"16057","In another wording he showed them his belly and they embraced Islam to the last of them. > **""He showed them his belly, and they embraced Islam to the last of them.""**"
"16058","'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah reports that al-Ashajj ibn 'Asar said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him he bears two traits Allah loves; asked what they are, he named forbearance and deliberation; asked whether they are old or new in him, he said old, and al-Ashajj praised Allah for fashioning him upon two traits Allah and His Messenger love. > **""In you are two traits Allah loves: forbearance and deliberation.""**"
"16059","Ibn 'Umar reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told al-Ashajj of 'Abd al-Qays that in him are two qualities Allah and His Messenger love: forbearance and deliberation. > **""In you are two qualities Allah and His Messenger love: forbearance and deliberation.""**"
"16060","Mazbadah, grandfather of Hud al-'Abdi, reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed his Companions he announced riders of the best people of the East would appear through that pass; 'Umar ibn al-Khattab went forth, met thirteen riders, welcomed them, learned they are of 'Abd al-Qays come seeking the Prophet ﷺ not for trade nor to sell their swords, and walked with them to him. They flung themselves from their mounts, some running, some hastening, some walking, seized the Prophet's hand ﷺ kissing it, while al-Ashajj, the youngest, first couched and hobbled the camels and gathered the baggage, then came composedly and kissed his hand. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him he bears two traits Allah and His Messenger love, deliberation and composure, fashioned in him from birth, and al-Ashajj praised Allah for it. The company turned to their dates, the Prophet ﷺ named each kind for them better than they knew, and declared their burni dates among their best, a remedy with no ailment in it. > **""In you are two traits Allah and His Messenger love.""**"
"16061","Al-Zari' reports that he went as delegate to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with his maternal brother Matar ibn Hilal of 'Anazah, an afflicted nephew, and al-Ashajj, whose name was al-Mundhir ibn 'A'idh; al-Mundhir objected to bringing an afflicted man and a youth not of the delegation, but al-Zari' hoped the Prophet's supplication would heal the one and bless the other. Upon reaching Madinah they leapt from their mounts, seized the Prophet's hands and feet ﷺ kissing them, while al-Mundhir composedly hobbled every mount, changed his travel clothes, and came walking; the Prophet ﷺ praised in him the forbearance and deliberation Allah fashioned in him. Al-Zari' then brought the afflicted youth, changed his clothes likewise, and presented him; the Prophet ﷺ had him turned with his back facing him, seized his cloak, raised his hand till the whiteness of his armpits showed, struck his back with his garment, and commanded the enemy of Allah to come out; the youth turned with the gaze of the sound, was seated, supplicated for, his face wiped, and the trace of that wiping remained in youthfulness into his old age, none of the company excelling him after the Prophet's supplication. Then he supplicated for 'Abd al-Qays as the best people of the East who embraced Islam unashamed. > **""Come out, O enemy of Allah.""**"
"16062","Nafi' al-'Abdi reports that al-Mundhir ibn Sawa came from Bahrain to the city of the Messenger ﷺ with men while Nafi' was an uncomprehending lad minding their camels; they laid down their arms, greeted the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, and al-Mundhir changed into fresh clothes and oiled his beard before greeting him. The Prophet ﷺ remarked upon his comely conduct in laying down arms, dressing, and anointing, and when asked whether it was innate or newly adopted replied it was innate; then he declared 'Abd al-Qays embraced Islam willingly while others embraced under compulsion, and blessed them. Nafi' looked upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as one looks today, yet without comprehension, and died at one hundred twenty. > **""'Abd al-Qays embraced Islam willingly, so may Allah bless 'Abd al-Qays.""**"
"16063","Dirar ibn al-Azwar reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ asking to pledge upon Islam, declaring in verse he had abandoned divining arrows, singing girls, wine in revelry, charging the mailed warrior in the fray, and bearing arms against the Muslims, praying his bargain never be a loss for he sold his family and wealth in exchange; and the Prophet ﷺ replied his bargain was no loss, O Dirar. > **""Your bargain is no loss, O Dirar.""**"
"16064","Al-Tabarani identifies him as Nubayshah ibn 'Abdullah al-Hudhali, called Nubayshah al-Khayr, son of 'Abdullah ibn Shayban of al-Harith of 'Abd al-'Uzza of Wathilah. > **""He is Nubayshah ibn 'Abdullah al-Hudhali, called Nubayshah al-Khayr.""**"
"16065","Umm 'Asim, mother of the child of Sufyan ibn Salamah of Hudhayl, reports that Nubayshah entered upon them, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ having named him Nubayshah al-Khayr; he entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ where captives were held and urged either favoring them or ransoming them, and the Prophet ﷺ approved his counsel and confirmed his title. > **""You have counseled good; you are Nubayshah al-Khayr.""**"
"16066","Isma'il ibn Ayyub of Makhzum reports that al-Walid ibn al-Walid was detained in Makkah, and when he resolved to emigrate he sold property called al-Mana in al-Ta'if for a she-camel, versifying his resolve to emigrate, buy adornment and a mount, and fling his longing self at the enemy. Seizing the people's heedlessness he set out on foot with 'Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah and Salamah ibn Hisham fearing pursuit, tired, al-Walid lagging and urging his feet in verse never to promise sloth again; at al-Ajras his finger was injured and he versified it bled but in Allah's cause. He entered Madinah upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring he came though dead, asking to be shrouded in his shirt next his skin; he died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shrouded him in his shirt, and visiting Umm Salamah found her mourning him in verse as the rain and succor of lean years, whereupon he remarked they had all but taken al-Walid as an object of tender devotion, and named his son 'Abdullah. > **""O my feet, catch up with the company; never again promise me sloth.""**"
"16067","Al-Tabarani identifies Tamim ibn Aws al-Dari, also called Ibn Qays, kunyah Abu Ruqayyah, uncle of Tamim ibn Aws ibn Kharijah of al-Dar of Lakhm. > **""Tamim al-Dari, kunyah Abu Ruqayyah, of al-Dar of Lakhm.""**"
"16068","Abu Hurayrah reports that the first to light a lamp in the mosque was Tamim al-Dari. > **""The first to light a lamp in the mosque was Tamim al-Dari.""**"
"16069","Muhammad ibn Salam al-Baykandi gives the name of Abu Salma as Rabi'ah ibn Riyah of Qurth of al-Harith of Mazin of Tha'labah of Thawr of Hudmah of Lati of 'Uthman of Muzaynah. > **""Abu Salma is Rabi'ah ibn Riyah of Muzaynah.""**"
"16070","Muhammad ibn Ishaq reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned to Madinah from al-Ta'if, Bujayr ibn Zuhayr wrote to his brother Ka'b warning him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had slain at Makkah a satirist who harmed him, that Ibn al-Zib'ra and Hubayrah ibn Abi Wahb had fled every way, and that if he valued himself he should flee to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who kills none who comes repentant, else save himself though there is no safety. Ka'b, who had versified against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, found the earth straitened, feared for himself amid his enemies' rumors of his doom, composed his ode praising the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with mention of his fear and the slanderers, journeyed to Madinah, lodged with a Juhani acquaintance, prayed dawn with the people, was shown the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, rose, placed his hand in his, unknown to him, and asked whether Ka'b ibn Zuhayr seeking safety repentant and Muslim would be accepted if brought; the Prophet ﷺ said yes, and he declared himself Ka'b. An Ansari leapt up asking leave to strike the enemy of Allah's neck, but the Prophet ﷺ bade him leave Ka'b, for he came repentant and turning; Ka'b then resented that quarter of the Ansar while none of the Emigrants spoke but good of him, and recited his ode wherein the slanderers proclaim him slain, friends forsake him, all decreed befalls, every son of woman is one day borne upon a curved bier, word reached him the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threatened him yet pardon is hoped from him. > **""Word reached me the Messenger of Allah threatened me, yet pardon is hoped from the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"16071","Al-Tabarani discusses Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, Lashumah ibn Jurthum, over whose name there is disagreement: Lasr ibn Himyar, Lashar ibn Jahim, Jurhum, Ghurnuf ibn Nasim, Nashir, Nashib ibn 'Amr, or Khuraym ibn Nashib. > **""Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani; disagreement surrounds his name.""**"
"16072","Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to tell him what is lawful and unlawful for him; the Prophet ﷺ looked him up and down and called him a little offshoot; asked whether an offshoot of good or evil, he replied an offshoot of good. > **""Rather, an offshoot of good.""**"
"16073","Harun ibn 'Abdullah al-Hammal reports that Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani died in the year seventy-five. > **""Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani died in the year seventy-five.""**"
"16074","Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm reports that Rabi'ah ibn Rawa' al-'Ansi came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, found him at supper, was invited and ate, was asked to testify there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is His servant and Messenger, testified, was asked whether from desire or fear, replied desire is not in the Prophet's hand while fear is groundless in lands no armies reach, but he was warned and feared and bidden to believe so he believed; the Prophet ﷺ remarked how many an eloquent preacher is of 'Ans. He frequented the Prophet ﷺ, then bade farewell, was told if he sensed danger to turn to the nearest village, sensed it, turned aside, and died there. > **""Many an eloquent preacher is of 'Ans.""**"
"16075","Al-Tabarani identifies Haydarah ibn Khayshanah as Abu Qirsafah al-Laythi, freedman of Banu Layth of Bakr of 'Abd Manat of Kinanah. > **""Abu Qirsafah al-Laythi, freedman of Banu Layth.""**"
"16076","Abu Qirsafah, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that his Islam began as an orphan between his mother and maternal aunt, leaning to his aunt, pasturing his little ewes; his aunt oft warned him against approaching the Prophet ﷺ lest he beguile him, yet he left his flock daily to listen to him, returning with shrunken, dry-uddered sheep. Questioned over their state, he knew not what to say, returned a second day hearing him call mankind to emigrate and hold to Islam while striving endures, a third day embraced Islam, pledged, and clasped his hand, then complained of his aunt and flock; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade him bring the ewes, wiped their backs and udders, supplicated blessing over them, and they filled with fat and milk. His mother and aunt, told the tale and his words and conduct, went with him, embraced Islam, pledged the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, though he did not clasp their hands; such was the affair of Abu Qirsafah's Islam. > **""O people, emigrate and hold fast to Islam, for emigration does not cease while striving endures.""**"
"16077","'Azzah bint 'Iyad ibn Abi Qirsafah reports that Rome captured a son of Abu Qirsafah, and thereafter at every prayer time Abu Qirsafah would ascend the wall of Ascalon calling his son by name to prayer, and his son heard him in the land of Rome. > **""At every prayer he would ascend the wall of Ascalon and call: O so-and-so, prayer.""**"
"16078","Harun ibn 'Abdullah al-Hammal identifies Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i as Ka'b ibn 'Amr, also called Khuwaylid ibn 'Amr or 'Amr ibn Khuwaylid. > **""Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i is Ka'b ibn 'Amr.""**"
"16079","Sa'id al-Maqburi reports that Abu Shurayh declared whoever sees him contending with a son-in-law, having housed each other's daughters, should deem him mad and cauterize his head; whoever sees a kid or milk of his sold, it is plunder; whoever sees him disputing a neighbor over a brick, he is mad and should be cauterized. A neighbor called 'Arfajah tested him by seizing ten cubits of his house; told of it, Abu Shurayh said he knows best, and the neighbor restored his right thereafter. > **""He knows best.""**"
"16080","Yahya ibn Bukayr reports that Abu Shurayh, named Khuwaylid, died in Madinah in the year sixty-eight, though his death date is disputed. > **""Abu Shurayh died in Madinah in the year sixty-eight.""**"
"16081","Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr reports that Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i, Ka'b ibn 'Amr, died in the year fifty-eight. > **""Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i died in the year fifty-eight.""**"
"16082","Al-Tabarani identifies Hani' ibn Niyar of Quda'ah as Abu Burdah al-Balawi, ally of Banu Harithah of Khazraj, of 'Aqabah and Badr, maternal uncle of al-Bara' ibn 'Azib. > **""Hani' ibn Niyar is Abu Burdah al-Balawi, of 'Aqabah and Badr.""**"
"16083","Muhammad ibn Ishaq identifies 'Asim ibn 'Adi of Baliyy, ally of Banu 'Ubayd of Aws, who set out with the Prophet ﷺ to Badr but was sent back with a share allotted among the men of Badr, was said to be left over al-'Aliyah, and said to have lived one hundred fifteen years. > **""'Asim ibn 'Adi set out to Badr and was allotted a share among its men.""**"
"16084","Muhammad ibn Ishaq, naming the Ansar who witnessed Badr of Khazraj of Banu Mazin of al-Najjar, lists Qays ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah, whose father's name was 'Amr ibn Zayd of Mabdhul of Ghanm of Mazin of al-Najjar. > **""Qays ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah of Banu Mazin of al-Najjar witnessed Badr.""**"
"16085","Al-Tabarani notes Qays ibn Sa'sa'ah al-Ansari was of 'Aqabah and Badr. > **""Qays ibn Sa'sa'ah was of 'Aqabah and Badr.""**"
"16086","Hani' Abu Malik reports that he came from Yaman to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who invited him to Islam, he embraced it, the Prophet ﷺ wiped his head, supplicated blessing for him, lodged him with Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan, and when Abu Bakr dispatched the army to Syria he went with them and never returned. > **""He embraced Islam, the Prophet ﷺ wiped his head and supplicated blessing for him.""**"
"16087","Abu 'Aqil al-Budayli reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, believed and affirmed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him sawiq to drink, drinking first himself and giving the last to him; ever after he felt its moisture upon his heart when thirsty and its coolness in the forenoon heat. > **""I still feel its moisture upon my heart when thirsty.""**"
"16088","Abu Maryam reports that he campaigned with the Prophet ﷺ, who entrusted the banner to him, and that he shot with stones before him in a manner that pleased him, so he supplicated for him. > **""He entrusted the banner to me and supplicated for me.""**"
"16089","Abu Khayrah reports that he owned camels for carriage, came to Madinah, attended Khaybar or Hunayn with the Prophet ﷺ carrying water for him upon their camels, kept a trade in Madinah, and the Prophet ﷺ supplicated blessing for him and his children. > **""The Prophet ﷺ supplicated blessing for me and my children.""**"
"16090","Abu Nuhaylah, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, reports that he was struck by an arrow and when told to pull it out he prayed Allah to lessen the pain without lessening the reward, and when told to supplicate he prayed to be made of the near ones and his mother of the houris. > **""O Allah, lessen the pain but lessen not the reward; O Allah, make me of the near ones and make my mother of the houris.""**"
"16091","Bashir reports that he walked with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ holding his hand, and the Prophet ﷺ asked him, O son of al-Khasasiyah, what complaint he had against Allah that morning, walking with His Messenger holding his hand; he replied he held no complaint, for Allah had granted him every good. > **""O son of al-Khasasiyah, what complaint have you against Allah this morning, walking with His Messenger holding his hand?""**"
"16092","Abu 'Atiyyah al-Bakri Bakr ibn Wa'il reports that his family took him to the Prophet ﷺ while he was a young lad, and he wiped his head; thereafter Abu 'Atiyyah was seen black of head and beard though a hundred years had passed over him. > **""The Prophet ﷺ wiped my head while I was a young lad, and it never greyed.""**"
"16093","'Ali reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever wishes to look at a man whose limb precedes him to Paradise should look at Zayd ibn Suhan. > **""Whoever wishes to look at a man whose limb precedes him to Paradise, let him look at Zayd ibn Suhan.""**"
"16094","Abu Jum'ah Junbadh ibn Sab' reports that he fought the Prophet ﷺ at the start of the day a disbeliever and fought with him at its end a Muslim, that they were three men and seven women, and concerning them was revealed the verse that were it not for believing men and believing women. > **""Were it not for believing men and believing women.""**"
"16095","Buraydah reports that he was with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, and whenever anything remained over he would load it upon him, naming him al-Zamilah, the ready mount. > **""Whenever anything remained over, he loaded it upon me and named me al-Zamilah.""**"
"16096","Abu al-Fil reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said do not revile Ma'iz. > **""Do not revile Ma'iz.""**"
"16097","Hamzah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah reports that he asked his father 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah ibn Mas'ud what he remembered of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he recalled that he took him while a boy of five or six, seated him in his lap, washed his head with his hand, in another wording wiped it, and supplicated blessing for him and his progeny after him. > **""He seated me in his lap, wiped my head, and supplicated blessing for me and my progeny.""**"
"16098","'Abdullah ibn Hilal al-Ansari reports that his father took him to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to supplicate for him, and he never forgot the Prophet ﷺ placing his hand upon his head till he felt its coolness; he supplicated for him and blessed him, and he lived white of head and beard, unable from age to part his hair, fasting by day and standing by night. > **""I never forgot the coolness of the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon my head.""**"
"16099","'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr reports that a Madinan lad kunya'd Abu Mus'ab came to the Prophet ﷺ with ears of grain before him; he rubbed an ear, blew upon it, and handed it to him to eat, and though the Ansar mocked whoever ate the rubbed grain, the lad would not return what the Prophet ﷺ handed him. He stepped away, returned, and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to supplicate that Allah place him with him in Paradise; asked who taught him this, he said no one, the Prophet ﷺ said he shall, and when he turned bade him help him with much prostration. He told his mother he had eaten from his blessed hands and blessed saliva and would not refuse it, and she praised him, and he returned and he supplicated for him. > **""Help me for yourself with much prostration.""**"
"16100","Abu Bakrah reports that on the day of al-Ta'if he let himself down to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ by a pulley, and he declared him Abu Bakrah. > **""You are Abu Bakrah.""**"
"16101","Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari reports that Humamah, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, went out raiding to Isfahan in the caliphate of 'Umar and prayed that if his claim to love meeting Allah is true He decree it for his truthfulness, and if reluctant decree it though he dislike, and that Humamah never return from that journey; seizure took him, 'Affan once said the belly, and he died in Isfahan. Abu Musa rose and declared to the people that by Allah, of all they heard from their Prophet ﷺ, Humamah is naught but a martyr. > **""O Allah, Humamah claims to love meeting You; if Humamah is truthful then decree it for his truthfulness, and if reluctant then decree it though he dislike.""**"
"16102","'Awf ibn al-Qa'qa' reports that his father delegated to the Prophet ﷺ with him a lad, and the Prophet ﷺ ordered every man two cloaks and him one; on the return one of them sold a cloak of his, which he bought, and when he came to the Prophet ﷺ in two cloaks he looked at him and asked whence they came. He said he bought it of so-and-so, and the Prophet ﷺ replied he was worthier of it than the one who wasted what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him. > **""You were worthier of it than he, for he wasted what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him.""**"
"16103","'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'A'idh reports that Laqit ibn Artah al-Sakuni came to the Prophet ﷺ with crooked legs that touched not the ground, and he supplicated for him so that he walked upon the earth. > **""He supplicated for him, and he walked upon the earth.""**"
"16104","A man of Banu Qushayr called Qurrah ibn Hubayrah reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ telling him they had lords and ladies they worshipped besides Allah, who answered not nor gave when asked, so they came and Allah guided them through him to worship Allah alone; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared he prospers who is granted understanding. He asked to be clothed in two garments the Prophet ﷺ had worn, was clothed, and at the standing of 'Arafat the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade him repeat his account, he repeated it, and he declared again his prosperity. > **""He prospers who is granted understanding.""**"
"16105","Khawwat ibn Jubayr reports that they halted with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Marr al-Zahran, he came out of his tent to find women conversing, was taken with them, returned for his baggage, donned a fine suit, and sat among them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out calling him Aba 'Abdullah; seized with awe and confusion he excused himself that a camel of his had strayed and he sought a shackle for it. The Prophet ﷺ moved on with him following, handed him his cloak, entered the arak thicket with the whiteness of his back showing through its green, relieved himself, performed ablution, and returned with water dripping from his beard upon his chest, asking after the stray camel. On the march none overtook him but greeted him and asked after that camel, till he hastened to Madinah avoiding the mosque and the Prophet's company. When that lengthened he watched for an empty hour, came to pray, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emerged from a chamber, prayed two light rak'ahs, while he lengthened hoping he would leave him; he bade him prolong as he wished, for he would not rise till he finished. He resolved to clear the Prophet's heart, and when he finished and was asked again after his camel he swore by the One who sent him with truth that no camel of his had strayed since he embraced Islam, and the Prophet ﷺ thrice invoked Allah's mercy upon him and never returned to the matter. > **""May Allah have mercy on you.""**"
"16106","Al-Harith ibn 'Amr al-Sahmi reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ in the Farewell Hajj while he rode his she-camel al-'Adba', and being a large man he dismounted, drew near till his face stood level with the Prophet's knee ﷺ, and the Prophet of Allah bowed and wiped the face of al-Harith; a radiance never left the face of al-Harith till he perished. > **""He wiped my face, and its radiance never left me till I perished.""**"
"16107","Al-Talib reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ asking his forgiveness, and he replied when permission is given, or till it be permitted; after what Allah willed he called him, wiped his hand over his face, and prayed thrice for his forgiveness and mercy. > **""O Allah, forgive al-Talib and have mercy upon him.""**"
"16108","Abu al-Darda' reports that a man called Harmalah came to the Prophet ﷺ confessing that faith with him is here, pointing to his tongue, hypocrisy here, pointing to his heart, and he remembers Allah but little; the Prophet ﷺ supplicated that Allah grant him a remembering tongue, a grateful heart, his own love and the love of those who love him, and turn his affair to good. > **""O Allah, grant him a remembering tongue, a grateful heart, my love and the love of those who love me, and turn his affair to good.""**"
"16109","'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla reports that Sa'd ibn 'Ubayd was called al-Qari' in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Sa'd ibn 'Ubayd was called al-Qari' in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"16110","'Amir ibn Laqit al-'Amiri reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ announcing his people's Islam and obedience as their delegate, and when he told the news the Prophet ﷺ declared him the blessed delegate and invoked Allah's blessing upon him, wiped his forelock, shook his hand, and his people greeted him in the morning. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared Allah refuses but good for Banu 'Amir, and by Allah were it not that the ancestor of Quraysh contends for it the caliphate would belong to Banu 'Amir ibn Sa'sa'ah, yet Quraysh's ancestor crowds them for it. Entering the house he asked whether their guest had been fed; 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said they set dates before him having naught else. The flocks returned at evening, the Prophet ﷺ ordered a ewe slaughtered, and when dislike showed he explained they slaughtered for themselves, for when their sheep exceed a hundred they slaughter for themselves. > **""You are the blessed delegate; may Allah bless you.""**"
"16111","Abu 'Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam gives Hatim of Tayy as son of 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn al-Hashraj ibn Imri' al-Qays ibn 'Adi ibn Akhzam. > **""Hatim of Tayy is son of 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd.""**"
"16112","Al-Sha'bi reports that he visited 'Adi ibn Hatim al-Ta'i at Kufah with Kufans asking for a hadith heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and 'Adi told them none of the Arabs bore the Prophet ﷺ fiercer hatred than he, till he fled to Rome and Christianized there; when word reached him of the fine character he summoned and the multitudes gathering to him, he journeyed to him, halted beside him where Suhayb, Bilal, and Salman sat, and the Prophet ﷺ bade him embrace Islam and be safe. He cried brother, brother, couched his camel, sat knee to knee, and asked what Islam is; he answered belief in Allah, His angels, His books, His messengers, and the decree, its good and evil, its sweet and bitter, then foretold that the Hour shall not rise till the lone woman journeys from al-Hirah, no Kufah then, circling this Ka'bah with no guard, and till a man carries a bag of wealth about finding none to accept it, striking the earth with it and wishing it had never been, wishing it were dust. > **""O 'Adi ibn Hatim, embrace Islam and be safe.""**"
"16113","Harun ibn 'Abdullah al-Hammal reports that 'Adi ibn Hatim al-Ta'i, kunyah Abu Tarif, died at Kufah in the days of al-Mukhtar in the year sixty-eight. > **""'Adi ibn Hatim died at Kufah in the year sixty-eight.""**"
"16114","Hassan, freedman of Malik ibn 'Abdullah al-Khath'ami, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, reports that he saw Malik performing ablution with a vein in his leg inscribed to Allah, and as he gazed Malik asked what he looked at, for no scribe had written it. > **""What are you looking at? No scribe wrote it.""**"
"16115","Al-Hasan reports that Qays ibn 'Asim al-Minqari told him he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he saw him he heard him say this is the chief of the people of the tents. > **""This is the chief of the people of the tents.""**"
"16116","Qays ibn 'Asim reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who ordered him to wash with water and sidr; he washed, prayer was established, he stood between Abu Bakr and 'Umar, and when prayer ended the Prophet ﷺ declared Qays had asked him three words none asked him but Abu Bakr. > **""Qays ibn 'Asim asked me three words none asked me but Abu Bakr.""**"
"16117","Al-Waqidi gives 'Iyad ibn Tamim ibn Zuhayr of Dabbah of Fihr, who embraced Islam early before Hudaybiyah, witnessed it, served in Syria with Abu 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, was appointed by him over his office at his death, was confirmed by 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, lived righteous and generous, and died in Syria in the year twenty at sixty, leaving no wealth and owing no debt. > **""'Iyad embraced Islam before Hudaybiyah and died in Syria in the year twenty.""**"
"16118","Al-Zuhri reports that Abu 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah died and appointed his cousin 'Iyad ibn Tamim al-Fihri after him. > **""Abu 'Ubaydah died and appointed 'Iyad ibn Tamim after him.""**"
"16119","'Abdullah ibn Busr reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand upon his head and declared the boy shall live a century, and he lived a hundred years; a wart stood upon his face, and he declared he shall not die till the wart leaves his face, and he died not till it left. > **""This boy shall live a century.""**"
"16120","Al-Hasan ibn Ayyub al-Hadrami reports that 'Abdullah ibn Busr showed him a mole upon his head where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had placed his hand, declaring he shall surely reach a century, and 'Abdullah would comb his head. > **""He shall surely reach a century.""**"
"16121","'Abdullah ibn Busr reports that when his mother sent him with a cluster he nibbled from it before delivering it to the Prophet ﷺ, and when he brought it he wiped his head and cried O treacherous one. > **""O treacherous one.""**"
"16122","'Amr ibn Hurayth reports that his mother took him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who wiped his head and supplicated provision for him. > **""He wiped my head and supplicated provision for me.""**"
"16123","In another wording with him, his mother or father took him, and al-Tabarani transmits both wordings with sound chains. > **""His mother or his father took him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"16124","'Amr ibn Hurayth reports that he was in his mother's womb on the day of Badr. > **""I was in my mother's womb on the day of Badr.""**"
"16125","Abu Nu'aym reports that 'Amr ibn Hurayth died in the year eighty-five. > **""'Amr ibn Hurayth died in the year eighty-five.""**"
"16126","Abu Musa reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed away when 'Amr ibn Hurayth was twelve years old, and that his kunyah is Abu Sa'id. > **""The Prophet ﷺ passed away when 'Amr ibn Hurayth was twelve years old.""**"
"16127","'Amr ibn Tha'labah al-Juhani reports that he met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-Sallah, embraced Islam, and he wiped his head; a hundred years passed over 'Amr with the spot of the Prophet's hand never greying. > **""The spot of the hand of the Prophet ﷺ never greyed.""**"
"16128","'Amr ibn al-Hamiq reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched his horsemen, who complained they had neither provisions nor food nor knowledge of the road; he told them they would pass a fair-faced man who would feed them, water them, guide them, and is of the people of Paradise. When the company descended upon him they gestured to one another gazing at him, announced glad tidings of Allah and His Messenger for he bore the Prophet's description, and told him his words; he fed them, watered them, provisioned them, guided them, returned to his family enjoining his camels, then went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking his call. He answered the testimony that none is worshipped but Allah and he is His Messenger, prayer, zakah, pilgrimage, and the fast of Ramadan; asked whether answering secures their families, blood, and wealth, he said yes, so he embraced Islam, told his family, and many embraced through him, then emigrated to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. One day with him the Prophet ﷺ offered to show him a sign of Paradise eating, drinking, walking the markets, and pointing to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, declared him and his people it; offered to show a sign of the Fire likewise, he pointed to a man as it and his people. When strife befell he remembered the Prophet's words, fled from the sign of the Fire to the sign of Paradise, and foretold that Banu Umayyah are his killers after this, who would extract him though hidden in stone within stone, for his beloved the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him his head is the first head severed in Islam and carried from land to land. > **""You shall pass a fair-faced man who will feed you, water you, guide you, and he is of the people of Paradise.""**"
"16129","'Amr ibn al-Hamiq al-Khuza'i reports that he gave the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drink, and he supplicated that Allah let him enjoy his youth; eighty years passed over him without a white hair seen. > **""O Allah, let him enjoy his youth.""**"
"16130","Fayruz reports that they embraced Islam among its embracers, dispatched their delegation to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with their pledge and Islam, which he accepted; they declared themselves known comers from a known land in submission, asked who is their guardian, he answered Allah and His Messenger, and they said sufficient, they are pleased. > **""Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"16131","Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah al-Muzani reports from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiped his head. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiped my head.""**"
"16132","In another wording he heard his father, who had reached the Prophet ﷺ, say he wiped his head and sought forgiveness for him. > **""He wiped his head and sought forgiveness for him.""**"
"16133","In another wording they asked whether he accompanied him; he said no, but he lived in his time milking and hobbling livestock. > **""I lived in his time, milking and hobbling.""**"
"16134","Ziyadah reports from his grandfather Mas'ud that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Muta', declared him obeyed among his people, mounted him upon a piebald horse, granted him the banner, and bade him go to his companions, for whoever enters beneath that banner of his is safe from punishment. > **""O Muta', go to your companions, for whoever enters beneath this banner of mine is safe from punishment.""**"
"16135","Abu al-Sawwar reports from his maternal uncle that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with people following him, followed among them, then the crowd rushed and outstripped him; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon him and struck him lightly with a palm stalk, stick, miswak, or something in his hand, by Allah without hurting him. He spent the night fearing the blow held some meaning Allah knew in him, resolving to come to him at dawn, while Jibril, peace be upon him, descended declaring him a shepherd who must not break the horn of his flock. At dawn prayer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated that since men follow him and it pleases him not, whomever he strikes or reviles Allah make it expiation and reward, or forgiveness and mercy. > **""O Allah, whomever I strike or revile, make it for him expiation and reward.""**"
"16136","Tariq ibn Shihab reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and campaigned in the caliphates of Abu Bakr and 'Umar in some forty or some thirty campaigns and expeditions. > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and campaigned in some forty expeditions.""**"
"16137","In another wording thirty-three or forty-three, from campaign to expedition. > **""Thirty-three or forty-three, from campaign to expedition.""**"
"16138","Mahmud ibn Labid reports that he remembered the Prophet ﷺ and a mouthful he spewed into a bucket in their house. > **""I remember the Prophet ﷺ and a mouthful he spewed into a bucket in our house.""**"
"16139","'Ali ibn Shayban reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for him, blessing 'Ali ibn Shayban and blessing 'Ali. > **""O Allah, bless 'Ali ibn Shayban and bless 'Ali.""**"
"16140","Hanzalah ibn Hidhaym reports that he delegated with his grandfather Hidhaym to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said he had bearded sons and others and this the youngest; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew him near, wiped his head, and blessed him. Al-Dhayyal reports he saw Hanzalah have the swollen-faced man or swollen-uddered ewe brought, say in Allah's name upon the spot of the Prophet's hand ﷺ, wipe it, and the swelling depart. > **""May Allah bless you.""**"
"16141","Al-Hirmas ibn Ziyad reports that his father delegated with him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking him to supplicate for him and his son; he wiped his head and pledged him upon Islam. > **""He wiped my head and pledged me upon Islam.""**"
"16142","Khuraym reports that the Prophet ﷺ declared him an excellent youth. > **""What an excellent youth is Khuraym.""**"
"16143","'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib reports that he was a partner of the Prophet ﷺ, and when he came to Madinah he asked whether he knew him; he answered he was his partner, and what an excellent partner, who neither disputed nor evaded. > **""You were my partner; what an excellent partner you were, neither disputing nor evading.""**"
"16144","'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ to pledge, asking whether he knew him; he answered yes, was he not his partner, and he found him the best of partners, neither evading nor disputing. > **""Were you not my partner? I found you the best of partners, neither evading nor disputing.""**"
"16145","'Ata', freedman of al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, reports that he saw his master white of beard and black of head, and asked why his head whitened not; he answered it never shall, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed while he played with the boys, he alone among them returned his greeting, he called him, asked his name, he said al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid nephew of al-Namr, he placed his hand upon his head and blessed him, so the spot of the Prophet's hand ﷺ shall never whiten. In al-Kabir the middle of al-Sa'ib's head stayed black and the rest white; asked of the marvel he told how he accosted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at play, greeted him, was asked who he is, said al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid brother of al-Namr ibn Qasit, was wiped and blessed, and shall never whiten nor ever change. > **""May Allah bless you.""**"
"16146","Umayyah bint Abi al-Sha'tha' and Qutbah her freedwoman report that they saw Madluk Abu Sufyan, hearing him say he came to the Prophet ﷺ with his master and embraced Islam; Umayyah added that what the Prophet ﷺ had wiped stayed black while all besides it whitened. > **""What the Prophet ﷺ wiped stayed black, and all besides it whitened.""**"
"16147","Ibn 'Umar reports that he was with the Prophet ﷺ when Harmalah ibn Zayd came, sat before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and confessed that faith with him is here, pointing to his tongue, hypocrisy here, pointing to his breast, and he remembers Allah but little. The Prophet ﷺ stayed silent till he repeated it, then seized the tip of his tongue and prayed Allah grant him a truthful tongue, a grateful heart, his own love and the love of those who love him, and turn his affair to good. Harmalah said his brethren are hypocrites among whom he was a chief and offered to name them, but the Prophet ﷺ answered that whoever comes as he came they seek forgiveness for as for him, who persists is left to Allah, and they tear no veil from any. > **""O Allah, grant him a truthful tongue, a grateful heart, my love and the love of those who love me, and turn his affair to good.""**"
"16148","'Abdullah ibn al-Samit reports that al-Hakam ibn 'Amr al-Ghifari led the people in prayer on a journey before a sutrah, donkeys passed before his companions, and he repeated the prayer with them; they said he meant to do as al-Walid ibn 'Uqbah did when he prayed four with his companions. He offered them more, and when he caught up with al-Hakam and told him, he halted till the company gathered and explained he repeated the prayer for the donkeys that passed, yet they struck him the likeness of Ibn Abi Mu'ayt; he prayed Allah better their conduct, their conveyance, their victory over their enemy, and part him from them. They marched seeing in their course naught but what pleased them, and when they finished, he died. > **""I ask Allah to better your conduct, your conveyance, your victory, and to part me from you.""**"
"16149","Nawfal al-Ashja'i reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted a slave to him for Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, telling him he is but his foster-kin; he stayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as Allah willed, then was asked how the girl fares, and answered she is well with her mother. > **""You are but my foster-kin.""**"
"16150","Shaddad reports that he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as his soul departed, and he asked what ailed him; he said the world had straitened upon him. He answered Syria is no strait upon him, for it shall be opened and Bayt al-Maqdis opened, and he and his son shall be leaders therein. > **""Syria shall be opened and Bayt al-Maqdis opened, and you and your son shall be leaders therein.""**"
"16151","Abu Rashid al-Hubrani reports that Mu'awiyah said to 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl that he is of the earliest Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and their jurists, so when he prays and enters his pavilion he should stand among the people and narrate what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Stand among the people and narrate what you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"16152","Anas ibn Malik reports that when the people of Bahrain came with al-Jarud as delegate to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he rejoiced at him, welcomed him, and drew him near. > **""He rejoiced at him, welcomed him, and drew him near.""**"
"16153","Hamzah ibn 'Amr reports that they journeyed by night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in darkness, and his fingers gave light till they gathered their mounts upon them and whatever gear had fallen, his fingers indeed glowing. > **""My fingers gave light till they gathered their mounts and fallen gear upon them.""**"
"16154","Silah ibn Ashyam reports that Abu Rifa'ah was stricken while he campaigned, and he dreamt Abu Rifa'ah upon a swift she-camel with himself upon a slow camel behind him, alternately nearing to shout and outstripped as she sped; he interpreted his dream that it is the path of Abu Rifa'ah he takes, toiling hard in works after him. > **""It is the path of Abu Rifa'ah I take, toiling hard after him.""**"
"16155","Abyad ibn Hammal reports that a facial mange, the ringworm, covered his face and consumed his nose, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned him, wiped his face, and before that day passed no trace remained upon his nose. > **""He wiped my face, and before the day passed no trace remained.""**"
"16156","'A'idh ibn 'Amr reports that a shot struck him fighting before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Khaybar in his face, and as blood flowed over his face and chest the Prophet ﷺ took with his hand and wiped the blood from his face and chest down to his collarbones, then supplicated for him. Hashraj reports 'A'idh would tell them this in life, and when he perished and they washed him they looked upon the trace he described of the Prophet's hand ﷺ upon his chest, a flowing blaze like a horse's blaze. > **""He wiped the blood from my face and chest with his hand and supplicated for me.""**"
"16157","'A'idh ibn Sa'id al-Jisri reports that they delegated to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he asked by his father and mother to wipe his face and supplicate blessing for him; he wiped his face and supplicated blessing for him. Umm al-Banin his wife said he was never seen waking from sleep but his face anointed, and he sufficed himself with dates. > **""He wiped my face and supplicated blessing for me.""**"
"16158","Rabah ibn al-Rabi' ibn Muraqqa' ibn Sayfi reports that they campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with every three granted a camel, two riding and one driving in the wastes; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed him walking and remarked upon it. He explained he had just dismounted while his two companions rode, and his two companions couched their camel, dismounted, and when he reached them bade him ride its fore and remain till return while they took turns, and when asked why they said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them they have a righteous companion whose company they should keep well. > **""You have a righteous companion, so keep his company well.""**"
"16159","Al-Walid ibn Qays reports that a leprosy afflicted him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for him, and he was healed of it. > **""He supplicated for me, and I was healed.""**"
"16160","Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari reports that when Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, dispatched Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan to Syria he walked out with him, and Yazid said either he ride or Yazid dismount; he answered he shall neither ride nor shall Yazid dismount, for he reckons his footsteps. > **""I shall neither ride nor shall you dismount, for I reckon my footsteps.""**"
"16161","Yahya ibn Bukayr reports that Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan died in Syria in the year eighteen, having appointed Mu'awiyah, whom 'Umar confirmed. > **""Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan died in Syria in the year eighteen, and 'Umar confirmed Mu'awiyah.""**"
"16162","Yasir ibn Suwayd al-Juhani reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched him with cavalry while his wife was with child, she bore him a son, and his mother carried him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking him to name the newborn whose father stood in the cavalry. The Prophet ﷺ took him, passed his hand over him, prayed Allah multiply their men, lessen their widows, leave them needless, and let their youth see no poverty, then bade them name him Musri', for he hastened into Islam, Musri' ibn Yasir. > **""O Allah, multiply their men, lessen their widows, leave them needless, and let their youth see no poverty.""**"
"16163","Hassan ibn Shaddad reported that his mother came as part of a delegation to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him to supplicate for blessing and abundant goodness for her young son. The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution from the remainder of his ablution water, wiped the boy's face, and supplicated for him. > **""O Allah, bless her in him, and make him abundant and good.""**"
"16164","Ishaq Abu al-Harith said he saw Hashraj, a man whom the Prophet ﷺ had taken and placed in his lap, wiped his head, and supplicated for him."
"16165","Sa'id, meaning Ibn Tamim, said the Prophet ﷺ asked him where his sons were, told him to bring them, and he dressed them in white shirts and brought them. The Prophet ﷺ then sought refuge for them with Allah. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge with You for them from disbelief, misguidance, and the poverty that afflicts the children of Adam.""**"
"16166","Mus'ab ibn Sa'd said Uthman رضي الله عنه asked who among the people was most eloquent, and those present said it was Sa'id ibn al-As رضي الله عنه."
"16167","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reported that Thumama ibn Athal رضي الله عنه accepted Islam, so the Prophet ﷺ ordered him to go to the garden of Abu Talha رضي الله عنه and bathe there. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then praised the goodness of his Islam. > **""Your companion's Islam has become good.""**"
"16168","Muslim ibn al-Harith رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote for him a letter containing counsel to the rulers who would come after him, and sealed it."
"16169","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه said whoever is pleased to look at the conduct of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ should look at the conduct of Amr ibn al-Aswad رضي الله عنه."
"16170","Muhammad ibn Hatib رضي الله عنه said he was born in the land of Abyssinia."
"16171","Muhammad ibn Hatib رضي الله عنه said his mother brought him from Abyssinia after his father Hatib had died, and one of his hands had been burned by fire. His mother presented him to the Prophet ﷺ as the son of his brother. Muhammad said, without ascribing falsehood to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he did not recall whether the Prophet ﷺ blew upon him or wiped his head, but he supplicated for blessing for him and his progeny."
"16172","Muhammad ibn Salam, meaning al-Baykandi, said nobility was only counted as what existed shortly before the Prophet ﷺ, continued into his time, and remained in Islam. He mentioned the household of Yemen, Abd al-Uzza among Kinda namely al-Ash'ath ibn Qays, their horseman of Zubayd namely Amr ibn Ma'di Karib, and their poet Imru al-Qays of Kinda, about which there is no disagreement."
"16173","Abu Ishaq said he had a debt upon a man of Kinda which he used to pursue in the early mornings, until Fajr prayer overtook him in the mosque of al-Ash'ath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه. After the imam gave salutation, a robe, a pair of sandals, and five hundred dirhams were placed before every man. When he said he was not of the mosque's people, he was still told to accept, and he was informed that al-Ash'ath had arrived from Makka."
"16174","Qays ibn Abi Hazim said when al-Ash'ath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه was brought, counsel was given to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه to release him from bonds and marry him to his sister. Al-Ash'ath then unsheathed his sword, entered the camel market, and hamstrung every camel he saw. People cried that al-Ash'ath had disbelieved, but when he finished he cast down his sword and swore he had not disbelieved; rather, the man had married him to his sister, and had they been in their own land the wedding feast would have been greater. He invited the people of Madina to slaughter and eat, and invited the camel owners to come and take their price."
"16175","A'isha رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade reviling Waraqa, for he had seen a garden or two gardens for him. > **""Do not revile Waraqa, for I have seen a garden or two gardens for him.""**"
"16176","Asma bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about Waraqa ibn Nawfal and said he would be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation by himself. > **""He will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone.""**"
"16177","Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ was asked whether his prophethood would benefit his uncle Abu Talib, and said yes, he had brought him out from the depths of Hell to its shallow part. Asked about Khadija رضي الله عنها, since she died before the obligations and rulings of the Qur'an, he said he had seen her upon a river of Paradise in a house of pearl with neither clamour nor fatigue therein. Asked about Waraqa, he said he had seen him in the midst of Paradise wearing sundus. Asked about Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, he said he would be raised as a nation alone between him and Isa عليه السلام. > **""Yes, I brought him out from the depths of Hell to its shallow part.""** > **""He will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone, between me and Isa.""**"
"16178","Jabir رضي الله عنه said they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, who used to face the qibla, profess the religion of Ibrahim, pray and prostrate. The Prophet ﷺ said he was a nation alone to be gathered between him and Isa ibn Maryam. Asked about Waraqa ibn Nawfal, who likewise faced the qibla and spoke verses in praise of right guidance and abandonment of idols, the Prophet ﷺ said he had seen him walking in the midst of Paradise wearing a robe of sundus. Asked about Khadija رضي الله عنها, he said he had seen her upon a river of Paradise in a house of pearl with neither toil nor fatigue. > **""That one is a nation alone; he will be gathered between me and Isa ibn Maryam.""** > **""I saw him walking in the midst of Paradise wearing a robe of sundus.""**"
"16179","Sa'id ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه said Waraqa ibn Nawfal and Zayd ibn Amr went out seeking the true religion until they passed through Sham. Waraqa became Christian, while Zayd was told that what he sought lay ahead of him. He continued to al-Mawsil, where a monk questioned him, offered him Christianity which he refused, and told him that what he sought would appear in his own land. Zayd then proclaimed his devotion, declared refuge in what Ibrahim had sought refuge in, bowed, and prostrated toward the Ka'ba. Later Zayd ibn Amr passed by the Prophet ﷺ and Zayd ibn Haritha while they were eating from a tray, and they invited him, but he refused to eat what had been slaughtered upon idols. After that the Prophet ﷺ was never seen eating what had been slaughtered upon idols until he was commissioned. Sa'id then asked the Prophet ﷺ to seek forgiveness for Zayd, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded them to seek forgiveness for him, for he would be raised as a nation alone. > **""Yes; seek forgiveness for him, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone.""**"
"16180","Sa'id ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in Makka with Zayd ibn Haritha when Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl passed by them, and they invited him to their tray. He refused to eat what had been slaughtered upon idols. Thereafter the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was never seen eating anything slaughtered upon idols. Sa'id then pleaded that his father would have believed and followed the Prophet ﷺ had he lived to meet him, and asked him to seek forgiveness for him. The Prophet ﷺ assented and commanded seeking forgiveness for him, for he would be raised as a nation alone. > **""Yes; seek forgiveness for him, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone.""**"
"16181","Sa'id ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه said he and Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Zayd ibn Amr, and he said he would come on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone. > **""He will come on the Day of Resurrection as a nation alone.""**"
"16182","Zayd ibn Haritha رضي الله عنه said he went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a hot Makkan day, riding behind him toward one of the idols, after they had slaughtered a ewe and cooked it. They met Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, and each greeted the other with the greeting of Jahiliyya. The Prophet ﷺ asked why his people had turned against him. Zayd swore it was for no wrong of his, but because he had gone out seeking the true religion to the rabbis of Fadak and then Sham, found them associating partners with Allah, and was directed to a shaykh at al-Hira, who told him that the religion he sought had appeared in his own land with a prophet whose star had risen, while all others were in misguidance. When the tray was offered, Zayd asked what it was, was told it was a ewe slaughtered for an idol, and refused to eat what Allah's name had not been mentioned over. Zayd ibn Haritha added that the Prophet ﷺ then went to the House and circumambulated it with him, and forbade wiping the two brass idols Isaf and Na'ila between al-Safa and al-Marwa as filth. After Zayd ibn Amr died and revelation came to the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ said he would be raised as a nation alone. > **""O Zayd, why do I see your people turned against you?""** > **""Do not wipe them, for they are filth.""** > **""He will be raised as a nation alone.""**"
"16183","Asma bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنها said Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl in Jahiliyya used to stand at the Ka'ba, lean his back against its wall, and declare that none upon the earth followed the religion of Ibrahim but himself. He had abandoned idol worship and eating what was slaughtered upon idols, and used to ransom infant girls lest they be killed by burial."
"16184","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said a delegation of Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked which of them knew Quss ibn Sa'ida al-Iyadi. They all knew him, and said he had perished. The Prophet ﷺ said he remembered him at Ukaz in the sacred month upon a red camel, addressing the people and calling them to gather, hear, and heed: that whoever lives dies, whoever dies passes, and all that is coming will come; that in the heaven is tidings and in the earth are lessons, a bed laid out, a roof raised, stars moving, and seas that do not recede. Quss swore a true oath that if there is contentment on earth there will be displeasure after it, and that Allah has a religion more beloved to Him than theirs. He wondered at people who depart and do not return, whether they were content to remain or left to sleep. The Prophet ﷺ then asked if any of them could recite his poetry, and one of them recited verses on the lessons of past generations, the watering-places of death with no return, and certainty of following where the people had gone. > **""O people, gather, hear and heed: whoever lives dies, whoever dies passes, and all that is coming will come.""**"
"16185","Jarir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said their brother the Negus had died, and commanded seeking forgiveness for him. > **""Your brother the Negus has died, so seek forgiveness for him.""**"
"16186","Ja'far ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه said when they prepared to leave the Negus, he provided them mounts and provisions and gifts, and asked them to inform their master of his treatment, sending his envoys with them while testifying that there is no god but Allah and that he is the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and asking that he seek forgiveness for him. Ja'far said they left until they reached Madina, where the Prophet ﷺ met him, embraced him, and said he did not know whether he rejoiced more at the conquest of Khaybar or the arrival of Ja'far. The Negus's envoy then asked Ja'far to describe his master's conduct, and Ja'far affirmed his provision and his testimony. The Prophet ﷺ supplicated three times for Allah to forgive the Negus, and the Muslims said Amin. Ja'far then told the envoy to convey to his master what he had seen of the Prophet ﷺ. > **""I do not know whether I rejoice more at the conquest of Khaybar or at the arrival of Ja'far.""** > **""O Allah, forgive the Negus.""**"
"16187","Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said the verse concerning eyes overflowing with tears upon hearing what was sent down to the Messenger was revealed about the Negus and his companions. > **""When they hear what has been sent down to the Messenger, you see their eyes overflowing with tears.""**"
"16188","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه said when the Negus died the Prophet ﷺ commanded seeking forgiveness for their brother, whereupon some people objected to seeking forgiveness for one who had died in Abyssinia. Then the verse concerning those among the People of the Book who believe in Allah and what was sent down was revealed. > **""Seek forgiveness for your brother.""**"
"16189","Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal رضي الله عنه said while they were on pilgrimage at al-Arj they saw a serpent writhing which soon died. A man took a cloth from his bag, wrapped it, dug for it in the earth, and buried it. When they reached Makka in the Sacred Mosque, a person asked which of them was Amr ibn Jabir, then which of them was the companion of the serpent. When identified, they thanked him and said he had been among the last to die of the nine who had come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to listen to the Qur'an."
"16190","Al-Tabarani stated that al-Ahnaf ibn Qays was a mukhadram, and his name was Sakhr ibn Qays ibn Mu'awiya ibn Husayn ibn Ubada ibn Nazzal ibn Murra ibn Ubayd ibn al-Harith ibn Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Zayd Manat ibn Tamim ibn Murra."
"16191","Al-Ahnaf ibn Qays said while he was circumambulating the House a man of Banu Sulaym met him and offered glad tidings, recalling when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sent him to his people of Banu Sa'd to call them to Islam. Al-Ahnaf affirmed that he had spoken only good, and when the messenger returned and informed the Prophet ﷺ of his words, the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for Allah to forgive al-Ahnaf. Al-Ahnaf said he had no greater hope than in that supplication. > **""O Allah, forgive al-Ahnaf.""**"
"16192","Junada ibn Salm said Jabir was Jabir ibn Samura ibn Junada ibn Jundub ibn Hajar ibn Ri'ab ibn Habib ibn Suwawa ibn Amir, his kunya was Abu Abd Allah, and the mother of Jabir ibn Samura was Khalda bint Abi Waqqas, sister of Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه."
"16193","Al-Zuhri stated that Hudhayfa رضي الله عنه was of Banu Abs, though his number was among the Ansar."
"16194","Al-Tabarani stated that Huwaytib was Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Abi Qays ibn Abd Wudd ibn Nasr ibn Malik ibn Hisl ibn Amir ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik."
"16195","Al-Tabarani stated that al-Hakam ibn Amr al-Ghifari رضي الله عنه settled in Basra, and he was al-Hakam ibn Amr ibn Mujadda' ibn Hudhaym ibn Hilwan ibn al-Harith ibn Tha'laba ibn Mulayl ibn Damra ibn Bakr ibn Manat ibn Kinana."
"16196","Al-Tabarani stated that al-Harith ibn Hisham ibn al-Mughira ibn Abd Allah ibn Makhzum رضي الله عنه had the kunya Abu Abd al-Rahman, his mother was Asma bint Makhrama, he accepted Islam on the day of the Conquest, was of the Mu'allafa, and died in Sham in the year eighteen."
"16197","Al-Tabarani stated that Habib was Habib ibn Maslama ibn Malik ibn Wuhayb ibn Amr ibn Shayban ibn Muharib ibn Fihr ibn Malik, his mother was Fihriyya, his kunya was Abu Abd al-Rahman, and he was called Habib of the Romans for his striving against the Romans."
"16198","Al-Tabarani stated that Khuzayma was Khuzayma ibn Thabit ibn al-Fakih ibn Amr ibn Adi ibn Wa'il ibn Munabbih ibn Imri al-Qays ibn Salma ibn Habib ibn Adi ibn Tha'laba ibn Imri al-Qays ibn Alqama ibn Mu'awiya ibn Jusham ibn Malik ibn al-Aws ibn Haritha ibn Tha'laba ibn Amr ibn Amir ibn Haritha ibn Tha'laba ibn Ghassan ibn al-Azd ibn al-Ghawth ibn Malik ibn Zayd ibn Kahlan ibn Saba ibn Yashjub ibn Ya'rub ibn Qahtan ibn Hud عليه السلام."
"16199","Abu Ubayda Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna said Zayd ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه was the paternal brother of Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه, and was older than Umar."
"16200","With the same chain he also said the mother of Zayd ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه was Asma bint Habib ibn Wahb ibn Amr ibn Umayr ibn Nasr ibn Asad ibn Khuzayma."
"16201","Abu Ishaq said Abu Talha رضي الله عنه was Zayd ibn Sahl ibn al-Aswad ibn Haram ibn Amr ibn Zayd Manat ibn Adi ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Najjar."
"16202","Ibn Numayr said Abu Talha Zayd ibn Sahl رضي الله عنه reported that he heard Ibn Idris say that on the authority of one of his descendants."
"16203","Shabab al-Usfuri said Sa'd ibn al-Atwal was Sa'd ibn al-Atwal ibn Abd Allah ibn Khalid ibn Wahib ibn Attab ibn Malik ibn Sa'd ibn Saghir ibn Adi ibn Awf ibn Ghatfan ibn Qays ibn Juhayna ibn Zayd, of the residents of Basra."
"16204","Al-Tabarani stated that Suhayl was Suhayl ibn Wahb ibn Rabi'a ibn Hilal ibn Uhayb ibn Dabba ibn al-Harith ibn Fihr, his mother was al-Bayda, whose name was Da'd bint Asad ibn Jahdam ibn Umayya ibn al-Harith ibn Fihr."
"16205","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Shurahbil ibn Hasana رضي الله عنه was Shurahbil ibn Hasana ibn Abd Allah ibn Amr, a man of al-Ghawth."
"16206","Ibn al-Kalbi said Shurahbil was Shurahbil ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Muta' ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ghitrif ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Juthama ibn Malik ibn Muladim ibn Malik Ruhm ibn Sa'd ibn Yashkur ibn Mubashshir ibn al-Ghawth ibn Murakhi of Tamim ibn Murr, and it is said he was of Kinda."
"16207","Al-Tabarani stated that al-Ahnaf ibn Qays was a mukhadram, and his name was Sakhr ibn Qays ibn Mu'awiya ibn Husayn ibn Ubada ibn Nazzal ibn Murra ibn Ubayd ibn al-Harith ibn Amr ibn Sa'd ibn Zayd Manat ibn Tamim ibn Murra ibn Amr."
"16208","Shurahbil ibn Muslim said he heard Abu Umama al-Bahili رضي الله عنه, who was Sudayy ibn Ajlan ibn Amr ibn Wahb."
"16209","Al-Asma'i said Abu Umama al-Bahili رضي الله عنه was Sudayy ibn Ajlan, of a clan called Banu Sahm ibn Amr, a branch of Banu Qabila."
"16210","Al-Tabarani stated that Dirar ibn al-Azwar al-Asadi رضي الله عنه bore that name while al-Azwar's name was Malik ibn Aws ibn Khuzayma ibn Sa'd ibn Malik ibn Tha'laba ibn Dudan ibn Asad ibn Khuzayma ibn Mudrika ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar."
"16211","Al-Tabarani stated that al-Dahhak ibn Qays al-Fihri al-Qurashi رضي الله عنه was the brother of Fatima bint Qays رضي الله عنها, his kunya was Abu Sa'id, and he was al-Dahhak ibn Qays ibn Khalid ibn Wahb ibn Tha'laba ibn Wa'il ibn Amr ibn Shayban ibn Muharib ibn Fihr ibn Malik ibn al-Nadr ibn Kinana, his mother being Umayma bint Rabi'a ibn Kinana. Al-Dahhak was killed on the day of Marj Rahit after the death of Yazid ibn Mu'awiya, when allegiance was given to Marwan ibn al-Hakam in the year sixty-four."
"16212","Al-Haytham ibn Adi said Uthman ibn Abi al-As رضي الله عنه bore that name while Abu al-As was Abu al-As ibn Bishr ibn Abd Allah ibn Hammam ibn Aban ibn Bashshar ibn Malik ibn Hutayt ibn Jusham ibn Qisiyy ibn Munabbih ibn Bakr ibn Hawazin ibn Mansur ibn Ikrima ibn Hafsah ibn Qays ibn Ghaylan ibn Mudar."
"16213","Al-Tabarani stated that Uthman ibn Talha ibn Abi Talha ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Uthman ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Dar ibn Qusayy al-Hajabi رضي الله عنه accepted Islam before the Conquest, and his mother was Umm Sa'id bint Shahida of Banu Amr ibn Awf of the Ansar of Quba."
"16214","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said Abd Allah ibn Jahsh رضي الله عنه was of Asad Khuzayma, an ally of Banu Umayya ibn Abd Shams."
"16215","Al-Tabarani stated that the attribution of Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz' رضي الله عنه was Zubaydi, an ally of Banu Amr ibn Hadid ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib, and he was Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz' ibn Ma'di Karib ibn Amr ibn Ism ibn Amr ibn Uwayj ibn Amr ibn Zubayd."
"16216","Al-Tabarani stated that Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabr al-Ansari then al-Awsi رضي الله عنه was a Badri, it is said his name was Abd Allah, his name in Jahiliyya had been Abd al-Uzza, and he was Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Badr, also said to be Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabr ibn Haritha ibn al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj ibn Amr ibn Malik ibn al-Aws, a Badri."
"16217","Al-Tabarani stated that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه was the full brother of A'isha رضي الله عنها through their father and mother."
"16218","Al-Tabarani stated that Abd al-Rahman was Abd al-Rahman ibn Azhar ibn Abd Manaf ibn Abd ibn Ishaq ibn Zuhra ibn Kilab ibn Murra ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr, and his mother was the daughter of Abd Yazid ibn Hashim ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf."
"16219","Ibn Ishaq said Umayr رضي الله عنه was Umayr ibn Salama ibn Muntab ibn Talha ibn Judayy ibn Damra ibn Bakr ibn Abd Manaf ibn Kinana ibn Khuzayma ibn Mudrika ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar ibn Ma'add ibn Adnan."
"16220","Al-Tabarani stated that Umayr al-Muzani رضي الله عنه had nothing transmitted from him."
"16221","He stated that Qurra was Qurra ibn Da'mus ibn Rabi'a ibn Awf ibn Mu'awiya ibn Qartha' ibn al-Harith ibn Numayr ibn Amir رضي الله عنه."
"16222","Muhammad ibn Sallam al-Jumahi said Abu Layla, the Nabigha of Banu Ja'da رضي الله عنه, was Qays ibn Abd Allah ibn Ghadir ibn Rabi'a ibn Udus ibn Rabi'a ibn Ja'da ibn Amir ibn Sa'sa'a."
"16223","Al-Tabarani stated that al-Nu'man ibn Qawqal al-Ansari al-Khazraji رضي الله عنه was a Badri, and the Qawaqil were the kin of Ubada ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه."
"16224","He stated that Hind ibn Abi Hala رضي الله عنه bore that name while Abu Hala's name was al-Nabbash ibn Zurara ibn Waqdan ibn Habib ibn Salama ibn Awf ibn Jarwa ibn Usayyid ibn Amr ibn Tamim, an ally of Banu Abd al-Dar. He was the son of Khadija رضي الله عنها, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, for before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ she had been with Abu Hala, bearing him Hind, then Hala, after which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ married her. His son was Hind ibn Hind."
"16225","He stated Hilal al-Sulami رضي الله عنه."
"16226","Ibn Ishaq said Habbar رضي الله عنه was Habbar ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy."
"16227","Al-Tabarani stated Hawdha al-Ansari رضي الله عنه."
"16228","He also stated Hawdha, without attribution."
"16229","He stated that Hubayb رضي الله عنه was Hubayb ibn Muhammad ibn Mughaffal Amr ibn Mughaffal ibn al-Waqi'a ibn Haram ibn Ghifar ibn Mulayl ibn Damra ibn Bakr ibn Abd Manat ibn Kinana ibn Khuzayma ibn Mudrika ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar ibn Ma'add ibn Adnan. It has reached him that he was only named Mughaffal because he neglected branding his camels and did not brand them."
"16230","He also stated that Wathila ibn al-Asqa' al-Laythi رضي الله عنه was also called Abu Qirsafa, and it is said Abu Shaddad, and he settled in Sham at Damascus. He was Wathila ibn al-Asqa' ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Abd Yalil ibn Nashib ibn Ghayra ibn Sa'd ibn Layth ibn Bakr ibn Abd Manat ibn Kinana."
"16231","He stated that al-Walid was al-Walid ibn Uqba ibn Abi Mu'ayt ibn Abi Amr ibn Umayya ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, his kunya was Abu Wahb, and he was the maternal brother of Uthman رضي الله عنه, their mother being Arwa bint Kurayz ibn Rabi'a ibn Habib ibn Abd Shams, whose mother was Umm Hakim al-Bayda bint Abd al-Muttalib, paternal aunt of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ had Uqba ibn Abi Mu'ayt killed on his return from Badr, while al-Walid in the time of the Prophet ﷺ was a grown man."
"16232","Ali ibn Rabah said he heard Ibn Makhlad say he was born when the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Madina, and the Prophet ﷺ died when he was ten years old."
"16233","Maslama ibn Makhlad رضي الله عنه said the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Madina when he was four years old, and passed away when he was fourteen years old."
"16234","Al-Tabarani stated that Maslama was Maslama ibn Makhlad ibn Samit ibn Bayr ibn Kawdhan ibn Abdud ibn Zayd ibn Tha'laba ibn al-Khazraj ibn Sa'ida ibn Ka'b ibn al-Khazraj."
"16235","He also stated that Mukhayyisa was Mukhayyisa ibn Nawfal ibn Uhayb ibn Abd Manaf ibn Zuhra ibn Kilab ibn Murra ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy, and his mother was Raqiqa bint Abi Sayfi ibn Hashim ibn Abd Manaf."
"16236","He also stated that Miswar was Miswar ibn Makhrama ibn Nawfal ibn Uhayb al-Zuhri رضي الله عنه, his mother was the sister of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه, said to be named Ramla, and Miswar had married Juwayriyya bint Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, who was the mother of his son Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Miswar."
"16237","Bakr ibn Habib al-Hanafi stated that nothing was transmitted from him."
"16238","He stated that Tamim ibn Hajar رضي الله عنه, Abu Aws al-Aslami, was the grandfather of Burayda ibn Sufyan, had companionship, and nothing of his hadith was transmitted."
"16239","He stated Tamim ibn Abd Amr رضي الله عنه, Abu al-Hasan al-Mazini."
"16240","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said Abu al-Hasan al-Mazini رضي الله عنه was the grandfather of Amr ibn Yahya, his name was Tamim ibn Amr, and Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه employed him over Madina when he left for Iraq at the time Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه came out toward him."
"16241","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami said in the hadith of Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi' naming those who witnessed with Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه was Jubayr ibn Hubab ibn al-Mundhir رضي الله عنه."
"16242","Al-Tabarani stated Jarrah al-Ashja'i رضي الله عنه."
"16243","He stated that Hatib was Hatib ibn al-Harith ibn Ma'mar ibn Habib ibn Wahb ibn Hudhafa ibn Jumah رضي الله عنه, and he, his wife Fatima bint al-Mujallil رضي الله عنها, and with them their two sons al-Harith and Muhammad, sons of Hatib, migrated."
"16244","He stated Husayn ibn Yazid al-Kalbi رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16245","He stated Huwaysa ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16246","He stated that Kharija was Kharija ibn Hudhafa ibn Ghanim ibn Abd Allah ibn Awf ibn Ubayd ibn Uwayj ibn Adi ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه, and he was among those present at the conquest of Egypt, where he died."
"16247","He stated Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiya al-Jushami رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16248","He stated Sa'd ibn Hilal رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16249","Sa'id ibn Iyas Abu Amr al-Shaybani said he recalled hearing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was herding camels for his family at Kazima."
"16250","Al-Tabarani stated Salama ibn Nufay' رضي الله عنه, Salama ibn Jariya رضي الله عنه, Salama al-Khuza'i رضي الله عنه, and Sabiq رضي الله عنه, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"16251","Al-Tabarani stated Shurayk ibn Hanbal رضي الله عنه, Shabib ibn An'um without attribution, and Shu'ayb ibn Amr without attribution."
"16252","Al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman said he met one hundred of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"16253","Al-Tabarani stated Ubayda ibn Sayfi al-Ju'fi رضي الله عنه."
"16254","Abida al-Salmani said he accepted Islam two years before the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, prayed, but never met him."
"16255","Yahya ibn Ma'in said Abd Khayr ibn Yazid al-Hamdani lived in Jahiliyya and Islam, recalling that while they were in Yemen the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached them."
"16256","Al-Tabarani stated Umara ibn Ubayd al-Khath'ami رضي الله عنه."
"16257","Mus'ab ibn Abd Allah al-Zubayri reported that Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه said the Prophet ﷺ passed away when he was sixty-six years old. He added that Abd al-Rahman was among the tallest and most complete of men, and his son Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab governed Kufa for Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, with Abu al-Zinad as his scribe."
"16258","With his chain he stated that Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith رضي الله عنه had the kunya Abu Muhammad, and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham was ten years old when the Prophet ﷺ passed away."
"16259","Al-Tabarani stated that Ali ibn Abi al-As رضي الله عنه, son of Zaynab bint Rasul Allah رضي الله عنها, has mention but no chain."
"16260","He stated Amir ibn Shahr رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16261","He stated Attab ibn Bashir رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16262","Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Bukhari said Ujayr ibn Yazid ibn Abd al-Uzza رضي الله عنه resided in Makka and narrated a hadith from the Prophet ﷺ, though Muhammad ibn Isma'il did not mention the hadith."
"16263","Al-Tabarani stated that Azib was Azib ibn al-Harith ibn al-Harith رضي الله عنه, father of al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه."
"16264","Al-Bukhari said Alqama ibn Hawshab al-Ghifari رضي الله عنه resided in Madina and narrated a hadith from the Prophet ﷺ, though he did not mention the hadith he narrated."
"16265","Al-Tabarani stated that Imran ibn Taym, Abu Raja al-Utaridi, was a mukhadram."
"16266","Ahmad ibn Hanbal stated that Abu Raja al-Utaridi died in the year one hundred and five."
"16267","Yahya ibn Ma'in said Abu Raja al-Utaridi died in the year one hundred and five."
"16268","Abu Raja al-Utaridi said the Prophet ﷺ was commissioned while he was a five-year-old, calling to Paradise."
"16269","Al-Tabarani stated that Abu Ruhm al-Ghifari رضي الله عنه was Kulthum ibn al-Husayn ibn Ubayd ibn Khalaf ibn Qays ibn Ahmus ibn Ghifar ibn Muqbil ibn Bakr ibn Damra ibn Abd Manat ibn Kinana ibn Khuzayma ibn Mudrika ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar ibn Ma'add ibn Adnan, and he was among those who pledged beneath the tree."
"16270","Kurz al-Tamimi stated without attribution."
"16271","Labid stated Abu Abd Allah رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16272","He stated Malik ibn Ahmar al-Judhami رضي الله عنه."
"16273","He stated Muslim ibn Safiyya رضي الله عنه."
"16274","He stated that Ma'qil ibn Yasar رضي الله عنه had the kunya Abu Ali, and he was Ma'qil ibn Yasar ibn Abd Allah ibn Mu'mar ibn Khiraq ibn Lami ibn Ka'b ibn Abd ibn Thawr ibn Hudma ibn Latim ibn Uthman ibn Amr ibn Udd ibn Tabikha, while Amr ibn Udd is Muzayna, attributed to his mother Muzayna bint Kalb ibn Wabra."
"16275","Nafi' stated without attribution."
"16276","He stated Namir ibn Kharasha رضي الله عنه."
"16277","Al-Tabarani stated Yazid ibn Nu'aym رضي الله عنه, Yazid ibn Khalid al-Kharashi رضي الله عنه, Yazid ibn Jariya al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, Yazid ibn Sinan رضي الله عنه, and Yasir, Abu Ammar رضي الله عنه."
"16278","Yusayr ibn Amr رضي الله عنه said the Prophet ﷺ passed away when he was ten years old."
"16279","With his chain he stated that Yusayr ibn Amr رضي الله عنه was an arif in the time of al-Hajjaj."
"16280","Al-Tabarani stated that Yusayr ibn Amr al-Sakuni رضي الله عنه was a mukhadram, resided in Kufa, and died there."
"16281","He stated Abu Iyas رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted, and Abu Sa'sa'a al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, from whom nothing was transmitted."
"16282","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Abu al-Malih ibn Usama رضي الله عنه, his name was Amir ibn Usama."
"16283","Al-Tabarani stated that Abu Rafi' رضي الله عنه, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his name was Ibrahim, and it is said his name was Aslam."
"16284","A man of Madina reported that the name of Abu Rafi' رضي الله عنه, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, was Aslam."
"16285","Al-Tabarani stated that Bashir ibn al-Khasasiya al-Sadusi رضي الله عنه was Bashir ibn Ma'bad ibn Sharahil ibn Sabu' ibn Dubara ibn Sadus, whose name in Jahiliyya was Zahm, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Bashir."
"16286","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Hakim ibn Hizam رضي الله عنه had the kunya Abu Khalid."
"16287","Harun ibn Abd Allah al-Hammal said Abu Waqid al-Laythi رضي الله عنه died in the year sixty-eight, and the name of Abu Waqid was al-Harith ibn Malik, also said to be Awf ibn Malik."
"16288","Yahya ibn Ma'in said Abu Waqid al-Laythi رضي الله عنه, Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, was Awf ibn al-Harith."
"16289","Al-Waqidi said Abu Waqid al-Laythi رضي الله عنه, his name was al-Harith ibn Malik."
"16290","Hisham al-Kalbi said his name was al-Harith ibn Awf."
"16291","Others besides al-Waqidi and Hisham said he was Awf ibn al-Harith Usayd ibn Jabir ibn Awra ibn Anana ibn Kinana ibn Shaj' ibn Amir ibn Layth."
"16292","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Abu Waqid رضي الله عنه, his name was al-Harith ibn Malik."
"16293","Al-Tabarani stated that al-Harith ibn Malik ibn al-Barsa al-Laythi رضي الله عنه was al-Harith ibn Malik ibn Qays ibn Uwaydh ibn Abd Allah ibn Jabir ibn Abd Manat ibn Shaj' ibn Amir ibn Layth ibn Bakr ibn Abd Manat ibn Kinana."
"16294","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Sa'd ibn Ubayd رضي الله عنه was Abu Zayd who gathered the Qur'an, and his son Umayr ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه was Umar's governor, namely Sa'd ibn Umayr ibn al-Nu'man."
"16295","Abu Ma'shar said Sa'd ibn Khawli رضي الله عنه, freedman of Muhammad ibn Hatib ibn Abi Balta'a, was a man of Madhhij."
"16296","Qatada said the last of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to die in Kufa was Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه, and in Basra Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه."
"16297","Ibn Ishaq said in the year one Abu Umama As'ad ibn Zurara رضي الله عنه perished, seized by dhubha while the mosque was being built."
"16298","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Abu Umama ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه died in the year one hundred."
"16299","Harun al-Hammal said Aslam رضي الله عنه, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, died after the killing of Uthman رضي الله عنه in the year thirty-five."
"16300","With his chain he stated that Burayda ibn al-Hasib al-Aslami رضي الله عنه died in Khurasan during the caliphate of Yazid ibn Mu'awiya in the year sixty-two, and Burayda had the kunya Abu Abd Allah."
"16301","Al-Tabarani stated that Jubayr was Jubayr ibn Mut'im ibn Adi ibn Nawfal ibn Abd Manaf رضي الله عنه, his kunya was Abu Muhammad, also said Abu Adi, his mother was Umm Habib bint Shu'ba ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Qays ibn Abdud ibn Nadir ibn Malik ibn Hisl ibn Amir ibn Lu'ayy, whose mother was the daughter of al-As ibn Umayya ibn Shams ibn Abd Manaf, and he died in the year fifty-nine."
"16302","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما died in the year seventy-eight at the age of eighty-five, and his kunya was Abu Abd al-Rahman."
"16303","Muhammad ibn Amr al-Waqidi said Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما died in the year seventy-eight."
"16304","He said Kharija ibn al-Harith related to him that he saw a cloak upon his bier, that Aban ibn Uthman, governor of Madina, prayed over him, and that Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما died at ninety-four years of age with the kunya Abu Abd Allah, his sight having gone."
"16305","Al-Haytham ibn Adi said Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما perished in the year seventy-four."
"16306","Abu Nu'aym said Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما died in the year seventy-nine."
"16307","Ma'n ibn Isa said Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما died in the year sixty."
"16308","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما died in the year seventy-eight with his sight gone."
"16309","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Jabir ibn Atik رضي الله عنه died in the year sixty-one at the age of seventy-one."
"16310","With his chain he stated that Jabbar ibn Sakhr رضي الله عنه died in Madina in the year thirty at the age of sixty-two."
"16311","Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn said Ali رضي الله عنه was killed at fifty-eight years of age, at the same age Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنه was killed, at the same age Ali ibn al-Husayn died, and at the same age Muhammad ibn Ali died. Ja'far ibn Muhammad reported the same from his father."
"16312","Sufyan ibn Uyayna said he heard al-Mahdi ask Ja'far how old Ali رضي الله عنه was when killed, and he replied fifty-eight, at which age Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنه was also killed."
"16313","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman رضي الله عنه died in the year thirty-six."
"16314","The like of it was narrated from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr."
"16315","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Hakim ibn Hizam رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu Khalid, died in the year fifty-four, though one reporter says the year fifty-eight, at the age of one hundred and twenty, living sixty years in Jahiliyya and sixty in Islam."
"16316","With his chain he stated that Abu Qatada رضي الله عنه, al-Harith ibn Rib'i, died in the year fifty-four at the age of seventy."
"16317","With his chain he stated that Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu Muhammad, died in the year fifty-four at the age of one hundred and twenty."
"16318","The like of it was narrated from Ibn Numayr by another chain."
"16319","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Abu Waqid al-Laythi رضي الله عنه died in the year sixty-eight at the age of seventy."
"16320","The like of it was narrated from Ibn Numayr."
"16321","Yahya ibn Bukayr said al-Harith ibn Hisham رضي الله عنه died in Sham in the year eighteen."
"16322","With his chain he stated that Habib ibn Maslama رضي الله عنه died in the year forty-two at the age of fifty."
"16323","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said Hassan ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه died in the year fifty-four."
"16324","Al-Haytham ibn Adi said Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه died in the year fifty in the land of the Romans while campaigning with Yazid."
"16325","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Khawwat ibn Jubayr رضي الله عنه died in the year forty at the age of seventy-four."
"16326","The like of it was narrated from Ibn Numayr."
"16327","Ahmad ibn Hanbal said it reached him that Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه died in the year fifty-one."
"16328","Al-Haytham ibn Adi said Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه died in the year fifty-five."
"16329","Ibn Numayr said Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه died in the year forty-five, and Kharija ibn Zayd died in the year ninety-nine."
"16330","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Khalid ibn Zayd al-Juhani رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu Abd al-Rahman, died in the year seventy-eight at the age of eighty-five."
"16331","The like of it was narrated from Ibn Numayr."
"16332","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Salama ibn al-Akwa' رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu Iyas, and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه both died in the year seventy-four."
"16333","The like of it was narrated concerning Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه alone."
"16334","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Suhayl ibn Amr رضي الله عنه died in Sham in the year eighteen."
"16335","Al-Tabarani stated that Uthman ibn Maz'un al-Jumahi رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu al-Sa'ib, was a Badri and died in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year two of the Hijra."
"16336","Al-Tabarani stated that Uthman ibn Amir ibn Ka'b ibn Sa'd ibn Taym ibn Murra ibn Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib ibn Fihr ibn Malik, Abu Quhafa رضي الله عنه, accepted Islam on the day of the Conquest and died in the year fourteen, a year after Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه, at ninety-four years of age, and he and his mother Salma bint Sakhr ibn Amir ibn Amr ibn Ka'b ibn Sa'd ibn Taym ibn Murra inherited from Abu Bakr."
"16337","Abu Mushir said al-Irbad ibn Sariya رضي الله عنه died in Sham during the caliphate of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan in the year seventy-five."
"16338","With his chain he stated that Abu Ubayda ibn Qays al-Sulami رضي الله عنه, of Murad, died in the year seventy-two, having accepted Islam two years before the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"16339","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Abu Abs ibn Jabr رضي الله عنه died in Madina in the year thirty-four at the age of seventy. Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه prayed over him, and Abu Burda ibn Niyar, Muhammad ibn Maslama, and Salama ibn Waqsh descended into his grave. The name of Abu Abs was Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabr."
"16340","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr رضي الله عنهما died and was buried at al-Habashi, a barid from Makka, at the end of the year fifty-five or fifty-six."
"16341","Muhammad ibn Ishaq said Amr ibn Hazm al-Ansari رضي الله عنه died in the year fifty-four."
"16342","Al-Sha'bi said Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه first sent to Kufa Abu Ubayda al-Thaqafi, father of al-Mukhtar, who was killed, then sent Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه who remained five years, then removed him, then sent Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه who remained a year, then removed him, then sent al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba رضي الله عنه who remained a year, then Umar was killed. When Uthman رضي الله عنه assumed rule he sent Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas to Kufa who remained a year, then removed him, then sent al-Walid ibn Uqba who remained five years, then removed him, then sent Sa'id ibn al-As who remained five years, then removed him, then sent Abu Musa al-Ash'ari رضي الله عنه who remained a year, then removed him, then Uthman was killed and fitna ensued. Then the first whom Mu'awiya رضي الله عنه appointed over Kufa was al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba, who remained four years then died, then he sent Ziyad ibn Abih who remained four years then died, then sent al-Dahhak ibn Qays who remained three years then removed him, then sent al-Nu'man ibn Bashir who remained four months, then Mu'awiya died and fitna ensued, then came the affair of Muslim ibn Aqil and his companions."
"16343","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Sa'd ibn Ubayd رضي الله عنه was killed at Qadisiyya in the year sixteen."
"16344","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu al-Abbas, died in Madina in the year ninety-one at the age of ninety-nine."
"16345","The like of it was narrated from Ibn Numayr."
"16346","Al-Zuhri said Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه, who had seen the Prophet ﷺ and heard from him, mentioned that he was fifteen years old on the day the Prophet ﷺ passed away."
"16347","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Shurahbil ibn Hasana رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu Abd Allah, died in the year eighteen or seventeen at the age of sixty-seven, and he had been a governor appointed by Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه."
"16348","Al-Harith ibn Umayra said Abu Ubayda, Shurahbil ibn Hasana, and Abu Malik رضي الله عنهم were all struck by plague on a single day."
"16349","Al-Haytham ibn Adi said Abu Sufyan ibn Harb رضي الله عنه died nine years into the rule of Uthman رضي الله عنه, and the sight of Abu Sufyan had been taken away."
"16350","Al-Waqidi said in that year Abu Sufyan Sakhr ibn Harb رضي الله عنه died at eighty-eight years of age, meaning the year thirty-one."
"16351","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Suhayb ibn Sinan رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu Yahya, died in Madina in Shawwal of the year thirty-eight, and he had been among the captives of al-Mawsil taken by the Romans."
"16352","Al-Tabarani stated that Safwan was Safwan ibn Umayya ibn Khalaf ibn Wahb ibn Jumah رضي الله عنه, his mother was Unaysa bint Ma'mar ibn Habib ibn Wahb ibn Hudhafa ibn Jumah, his kunya was Abu Wahb. He came to the Prophet ﷺ on the day of the Conquest of Makka, who granted him four months' respite, and he witnessed Hunayn while still a polytheist, then accepted Islam thereafter, and died around the killing of Uthman رضي الله عنه."
"16353","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Abu Umama al-Bahili رضي الله عنه, whose name was Sudayy ibn Ajlan, died in the year eighty-six at the age of ninety-one."
"16354","Abu Nu'aym said Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما died in the year sixty-eight."
"16355","Dawud ibn Rashid said Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما died in the year sixty-eight."
"16356","Al-Tabarani stated that Abd Allah was Abd Allah ibn Zam'a ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy رضي الله عنه, his mother was Qariba bint Abi Umayya ibn al-Mughira ibn Amr ibn Makhzum, and her mother was Atika bint Abd al-Muttalib."
"16357","Nafi' ibn Amr ibn Jumah said Abd Allah ibn al-Sa'ib رضي الله عنه died in the time of Ibn al-Zubayr."
"16358","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz' رضي الله عنه died in the year eighty-six."
"16359","With his chain he stated that Abd Allah ibn Busr رضي الله عنه died in the year eighty-eight, the last of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to die in Sham, at ninety-four years of age."
"16360","Al-Haytham ibn Adi said Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'a رضي الله عنه died in the time of al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik in the year eighty-seven, while he had been born in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ passing away when he was four or five years old."
"16361","Al-Tabarani stated that Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنهما settled in Kufa and died there."
"16362","It was narrated from Yahya ibn Bukayr that Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنهما died in the year eighty-six."
"16363","It was narrated from al-Waqidi that Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه died in the year eighty-four."
"16364","Abu Abd Allah al-Ash'ari said Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه died in Sham in the year thirty-three."
"16365","Yahya ibn Bukayr said Ka'b ibn Ujra رضي الله عنه died in the year fifty-two at the age of seventy-seven."
"16366","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr said Ka'b ibn Ujra رضي الله عنه died in the year fifty-two at seventy-five years of age."
"16367","He narrated from Yahya ibn Bukayr that Makhrama ibn Nawfal رضي الله عنه, whose kunya was Abu al-Miswar, died in the year fifty-four at seventy years of age, though it has been said at one hundred and fifteen, having accepted Islam on the day of the Conquest and being of the Mu'allafa."
"16368","With his chain he stated that al-Miswar ibn Makhrama رضي الله عنه died on the day news of the death of Yazid ibn Mu'awiya reached Ibn al-Zubayr in the year sixty-four. Ibn al-Zubayr prayed over him at al-Hajun. A catapult stone had struck him while he was praying in the Hijr; he survived five days and died in Rabi' al-Awwal of the year sixty-four. He had been brought to Madina at the end of Dhu al-Hijja in the year eight, witnessed the year of the Conquest at six years of age, and the Prophet ﷺ passed away when he, meaning al-Miswar, was eight years old."
"16369","With his chain he stated that Wathila ibn al-Asqa' رضي الله عنه died in the year eighty-five at the age of ninety-eight."
"16370","Sa'id ibn Khalid said Wathila ibn al-Asqa' رضي الله عنه died in the year eighty-three at one hundred and five years of age."
"16371","Al-Waqidi said in that year Abu Amra al-Mazini رضي الله عنه died, meaning the year thirty-seven."
"16372","Ka'b ibn Ujra رضي الله عنه said one day they sat before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque, a group of them of the Ansar, a group of the Muhajirun, and a group of Banu Hashim, and they disputed over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as to which of them was closest and most beloved to him. The Ansar claimed precedence through belief, following, fighting alongside him, and being his squadron against the enemy. The Muhajirun claimed precedence through emigration with Allah and His Messenger, forsaking kin, families, and wealth, while sharing in all that the others had witnessed. Banu Hashim claimed precedence as his kin, while sharing in all that the others had witnessed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them, heard their claims, affirmed each group's truth, and asked who could refute them. He then judged between them, telling the Ansar he was their brother, telling the Muhajirun he was of them, and telling Banu Hashim they were of him and toward him. They all arose pleased and rejoicing with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Shall I not judge between you?""** > **""As for you, O company of the Ansar, I am but your brother.""** > **""As for you, O company of the Muhajirun, I am but of you.""** > **""As for you, O Banu Hashim, you are of me and toward me.""**"
"16373","Maslama ibn Makhlad رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Muhajirun would precede mankind by forty autumns, enjoying bliss therein while mankind would be detained for reckoning, then the second company would be of a hundred autumns. > **""The Muhajirun will precede mankind by forty autumns, enjoying bliss therein while mankind is detained for reckoning.""**"
"16374","Jarir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Muhajirun and the Ansar are allies of one another, while the Tulaqa of Quraysh and the Utaqa of Thaqif are allies of one another. > **""The Muhajirun and the Ansar are allies of one another, and the Tulaqa of Quraysh and the Utaqa of Thaqif are allies of one another.""**"
"16375","In another narration the wording is that they are allies of one another in this world and the Hereafter. > **""They are allies of one another in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"16376","Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه, said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the Muhajirun, the Ansar, the Tulaqa of Quraysh, and the Utaqa of Thaqif are allies of one another in this world and the Hereafter. > **""The Muhajirun, the Ansar, the Tulaqa of Quraysh, and the Utaqa of Thaqif are allies of one another in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"16377","Anas رضي الله عنه said words passed between Khalid ibn al-Walid and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنهما, whereupon Khalid said to Abd al-Rahman, do you assert superiority over us by days through which you preceded us. When that was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, he commanded leaving his Companions to him, swearing that even spending the like of Uhud or the mountains in gold would not reach their deeds. > **""Leave my Companions for me, for by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, were you to spend the like of Uhud in gold, you would not reach their deeds.""**"
"16378","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه said something of what occurs between people occurred between Khalid ibn al-Walid and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنهما, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded leaving his Companions to him, for were one of them to spend the like of Uhud in gold, he would not reach a mudd of one of them nor even half of it. > **""Leave my Companions for me, for were one of you to spend the like of Uhud in gold, he would not reach a mudd of one of them nor even half of it.""**"
"16379","Abd Allah ibn Salam رضي الله عنه said they asked whether they were better or those after them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said were one of the latter to possess the like of Uhud in gold and spend it in the path of Allah, it would not reach a mudd of one of them nor even half of it. > **""Were one of them to have the like of Uhud in gold and spend it in the path of Allah, it would not reach a mudd of one of you nor even half of it.""**"
"16380","Yusuf ibn Abd Allah ibn Salam رضي الله عنهما said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked whether they were better or those after them, and said were one of the latter to spend Uhud in gold, it would not reach a mudd of one of them nor even half of it. > **""Were one of them to spend Uhud in gold, it would not reach a mudd of one of you nor even half of it.""**"
"16381","Yazid ibn Umayra said Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه informed him in his bequest that some men of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ once said their sons were better than them, born in Islam without ever associating partners with Allah while they had done so. When that reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said they were better than their sons, their sons better than their sons' sons, and their sons' sons better than the sons of their sons' sons. > **""We are better than our sons, our sons are better than their sons, and our sons' sons are better than the sons of their sons.""**"
"16382","Anas رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said to his Companions they were better than their sons, and their sons better than their sons' sons. > **""You are better than your sons, and your sons are better than their sons.""**"
"16383","Jabir ibn Abd Allah رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah chose his Companions above the worlds except the prophets and messengers, chose for him four of his Companions, Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali رضي الله عنهم, making them his Companions, declared good in all his Companions, chose his nation above the nations, and chose four generations of his nation, the first, the second, the third, and the fourth. > **""Allah chose my Companions above the worlds except the prophets and messengers.""**"
"16384","Iyad al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, who had companionship, reported that the Prophet ﷺ said to guard him concerning his Companions and in-laws, for whoever guarded him in them Allah would guard in this world and the Hereafter, whoever did not guard him in them Allah would forsake, and whoever Allah forsook He would soon seize. > **""Guard me concerning my Companions and in-laws, for whoever guards me in them, Allah will guard him in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"16385","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to guard him concerning his Companions, for whoever guarded him in them Allah would guard in this world and the Hereafter, whoever did not guard him in them Allah would forsake, and whoever Allah forsook He would soon seize. > **""Guard me concerning my Companions, for whoever guards me in them, Allah will guard him in this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"16386","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever guarded him concerning his Companions would come to his Basin, and whoever did not guard him concerning his Companions would only see him from afar. > **""Whoever guards me concerning my Companions will come to my Basin, and whoever does not guard me concerning my Companions will only see me from afar.""**"
"16387","Abd Allah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he had asked his Lord that no one he married into nor anyone who married into him would be but with him in Paradise, and He granted him that. > **""I asked my Lord that no one I marry into nor anyone who marries into me would be but with me in Paradise, and He granted me that.""**"
"16388","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of asking his Lord about marriage bonds formed with him and through him. > **""I asked my Lord that whomever I marry among, or give in marriage to, should be with me in Paradise, and He granted me that.""**"
"16389","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of lineage and marriage ties on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Every lineage and marriage tie shall be cut off on the Day of Resurrection except my marriage tie and my lineage.""**"
"16390","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reports that when death approached the Prophet ﷺ, the people asked him for a bequest, and he enjoined care for the foremost Muhājirūn and their sons after them, warning of rejection of deeds for neglect; in another wording he enjoined the foremost early ones and their sons after them repeatedly. > **""I enjoin upon you the foremost early ones among the Muhājirīn, and their sons after them; if you do not do so, neither ṣarf nor ʿadl shall be accepted from you.""**"
"16391","ʿUwaym ibn Sāʿidah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of Allah choosing him and choosing companions for him, making among them ministers, helpers, and in-laws, with a curse upon whoever reviles them and rejection of his deeds. > **""Indeed Allah chose me and chose companions for me, and made among them ministers for me, helpers, and in-laws; so whoever reviles them, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all mankind; neither ṣarf nor ʿadl shall be accepted from him.""**"
"16392","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Saʿdī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the best of his Ummah at its beginning and an evil element at its end disowned by him. > **""The best of my Ummah are its first, and its last shall be evil; they are not of me, nor am I of them.""**"
"16393","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of mankind being one party while he and his companions are another, which Zayd ibn Thābit and Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj confirmed in the presence of Marwān. > **""Mankind are one party, and I and my companions are a party.""**"
"16394","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the stars as security for the people of heaven and his companions as security for his Ummah. > **""The stars are a security for the people of heaven, and my companions are a security for my Ummah.""**"
"16395","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened his companions to salt in food, without which food is not right. > **""The likeness of my companions is like that of salt in food; food is not made right except with salt.""**"
"16396","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to tell them they would soon be among mankind like salt in food, without which food is not right. > **""You are soon to be among mankind like salt in food, and food is not made right except with salt.""**"
"16397","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to tell them that one of them would soon wish for a single glance at him more beloved than all his wealth. > **""One of you shall soon wish to look at me with a single glance, more beloved to him than all his wealth.""**"
"16398","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reports that while with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two riders approached, whom he ﷺ named as Kindī Madhḥijīs, and two men of Madhḥij came; one pledged allegiance and asked about seeing him, believing, following, and affirming him, and the other asked about believing, following, and affirming him without mention of sight, receiving a doubled glad tiding. > **""Ṭūbā for him, then ṭūbā for him.""**"
"16399","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Hour shall not come until a man of his companions is sought as a lost stray is sought and is not found. > **""The Hour shall not come until a man of my companions is sought as a lost stray is sought, and he is not found.""**"
"16400","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold a time when armies would ask whether any companion of Muḥammad ﷺ is among them and seek victory through him, then later ask for one who accompanied his companions, so prized that men would cross the sea to reach him. > **""There shall come upon the people a time when an army goes forth and it is said: is there among you one who accompanied Muḥammad ﷺ? So victory is sought through him and they are aided.""**"
"16401","A narration reports that there shall remain a people who recite the Qurʾān without knowing what it is. > **""Then there shall remain a people who recite the Qurʾān and do not know what it is.""**"
"16402","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mawalah reports travelling at al-Ahwāz behind a man on a mule supplicating to join his departed generation, whom he asked to include him; the man turned out to be Buraydah al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه, who reported the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the best generation, those after them, doubt over a third, then a people marked by fatness offering testimony unasked. > **""The best of my Ummah are my generation among them, then those who follow them; then there shall succeed a people in whom fatness appears, pouring forth testimony though it is not sought from them.""**"
"16403","A narration from one who heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reports the best generation in which he was sent, then those after them three times over, then a people whose testimony outruns their oaths and whose oaths outrun their testimony. > **""The best of this Ummah is the generation among whom I was sent, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then those who follow them; then there shall be a people whose testimony outruns their oaths and whose oaths outrun their testimony.""**"
"16404","A narration reports the generation among whom he ﷺ was sent, then those after them repeatedly through five generations. > **""The generation among whom I was sent, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then those who follow them.""**"
"16405","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the best people as his generation then the next three, followed by a people whose oaths outrun their testimony and whose testimony outruns their oaths. > **""The best of people are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then those who follow them; then there shall come a people whose oaths outrun their testimony and whose testimony outruns their oaths.""**"
"16406","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ name the best people as his generation among whom he is, then those after them, then peoples marked by spreading fatness, unsought testimony, and clamour in markets. > **""The best of people are my generation among whom I am, then those who follow them; then there shall arise peoples among whom fatness spreads, testifying though not called to testify, with clamour in their markets.""**"
"16407","A narration from the same Companion reports the best generation in which he ﷺ is, then the second, third, and fourth, of whom Allah cares nothing. > **""The best generation is the generation in which I am, then the second, then the third, then the fourth; Allah cares nothing for them.""**"
"16408","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ named the best people as his generation, then those after them, then those after them. > **""The best of people are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them.""**"
"16409","Saʿīd ibn Tamīm رضي الله عنه reports asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who of his Ummah is best, receiving answers of himself and his peers, then the second, then the third generations, then a people who swear unasked, testify unasked, and betray trusts. > **""I and my peers; then the second generation; then the third generation; then there shall be a people who swear though not asked to swear, and testify though not asked to testify, and are trusted but do not discharge.""**"
"16410","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the best of his Ummah as the generation among whom he was sent, then those after them twice. > **""The best of my Ummah is the generation among whom I was sent, then those who follow them, then those who follow them.""**"
"16411","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ name the best people as his generation and the two after, then a fourth most vile until the Hour. > **""The best of people are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them; then the fourth shall be most vile until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16412","Abū Barzah al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ named the best people as his generation, then those after them twice. > **""The best of people are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them.""**"
"16413","Jaʿdah ibn Hubayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the best people as his generation and the three after, then a vile remainder. > **""The best of people are my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then those who follow them; then the rest shall be most vile.""**"
"16414","The daughter of Abū Lahab reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by them and asked for drink, so she gave him water from a jug, and a man in two green garments asked him, whereupon he ﷺ enjoined worship, prayer, and zakāh, then spoke of the best generation and the next. > **""Worship Allah, associating nothing with Him, establish prayer, and give zakāh; the best of my Ummah is my generation, then those who follow them.""**"
"16415","The daughter of Abū Jahl, named Jamīlah, reports that the Prophet ﷺ spoke of the best people as his generation. > **""The best of people is my generation.""**"
"16416","Yazīd ibn Khumayr reports asking ʿAbd Allāh ibn Busr رضي الله عنه about their state compared to those before them, and he exclaimed in wonder that if the former were raised they would know them only by finding them standing in prayer. > **""Subḥān Allāh! If they were raised from the graves, they would not know you except by finding you standing, praying.""**"
"16417","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Busr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave glad tidings to whoever saw him and whoever saw one who saw him, with a good return. > **""Ṭūbā for whoever saw me, and ṭūbā for whoever saw one who saw me; ṭūbā for them and a good return.""**"
"16418","Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave glad tidings to whoever saw him and whoever saw one who saw him. > **""Ṭūbā for whoever saw me, and whoever saw one who saw me.""**"
"16419","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied continued goodness to the presence among them of one who saw him and accompanied him. > **""You shall remain upon good so long as there remains among you one who saw me and accompanied me.""**"
"16420","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for the Companions and for those who saw, and those who saw, explained as one who saw those who saw him ﷺ. > **""O Allah, forgive the Companions, and those who saw, and those who saw.""**"
"16421","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave glad tidings to whoever saw him and believed in him, and to the next two degrees of seeing. > **""Ṭūbā for whoever saw me and believed in me, and whoever saw one who saw me, and whoever saw one who saw one who saw me.""**"
"16422","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUqbah reports from his father, who was struck by an arrow alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say thrice that no Muslim who saw him, or saw one who saw him, or saw one who saw one who saw him, shall enter the Fire. > **""No Muslim who saw me, or saw one who saw me, or saw one who saw one who saw me, shall enter the Fire.""**"
"16423","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn that people shall increase while his companions decrease, forbidding reviling them with a curse upon the reviler. > **""Indeed people shall increase and my companions shall decrease, so do not revile them; may Allah curse whoever reviles them.""**"
"16424","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ invoked the curse of Allah upon whoever reviles his companions. > **""Whoever reviles my companions, upon him is the curse of Allah.""**"
"16425","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that Mālik ibn al-Dukhshun was mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ, and people attacked him as chief of the hypocrites, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ forbade reviling his companions. > **""Leave my companions; do not revile my companions.""**"
"16426","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked against the reviler of his companions the curse of Allah, the angels, and all mankind. > **""Whoever reviles my companions, Allah, the angels, and all mankind curse him.""**"
"16427","Saʿīd ibn Zayd ibn ʿAmr ibn Nufayl رضي الله عنه reports being urged to revile his companions and refusing, invoking blessing and forgiveness upon them. > **""You order me to revile my companions? Rather, may Allah bless them and forgive them.""**"
"16428","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports reproaching revilers of the predecessors though commanded to seek forgiveness for them, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell the last of the Ummah cursing its first before its end. > **""This Ummah shall not perish until its last curses its first.""**"
"16429","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade reviling his companions, invoking Allah's curse upon the reviler. > **""Do not revile my companions; may Allah curse whoever reviles my companions.""**"
"16430","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked the curse of Allah upon whoever reviles any one of his companions. > **""Whoever reviles any one of my companions, upon him is the curse of Allah.""**"
"16431","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that on her night, while the Prophet ﷺ was with her, Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها came with ʿAlī رضي الله عنه preceding her, and the Prophet ﷺ told ʿAlī that he and his companions are in Paradise, warning of claimants of his love who cast off Islam, reciting without passing throats, called al-Rāfiḍah, to be fought as polytheists, marked by absence from Friday and congregation and attack upon the early predecessors. > **""O ʿAlī, you and your companions are in Paradise, except that among those who claim to love you are peoples who reject Islam then cast it off, reciting the Qurʾān without passing their throats, nicknamed al-Rāfiḍah; if you meet them, fight them, for they are polytheists.""**"
"16432","Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ looked at ʿAlī رضي الله عنه and placed him in Paradise, warning that among his partisans shall be those who cast off and reject Islam, nicknamed al-Rāfiḍah, to be killed as polytheists. > **""This one is in Paradise, and among his partisans shall be those who cast off Islam, rejecting it, nicknamed al-Rāfiḍah; whoever meets them, let him kill them, for they are polytheists.""**"
"16433","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ the coming in the last age of a people nicknamed al-Rāfiḍah who reject and cast off Islam, to be fought as polytheists. > **""There shall be in the last age a people nicknamed al-Rāfiḍah, rejecting Islam and casting it off; fight them, for they are polytheists.""**"
"16434","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports being with the Prophet ﷺ alongside ʿAlī رضي الله عنه when the Prophet ﷺ warned of a people in his Ummah claiming love of the Household, nicknamed al-Rāfiḍah, to be fought as polytheists. > **""O ʿAlī, there shall be in my Ummah a people professing love of the Household, nicknamed al-Rāfiḍah; fight them, for they are polytheists.""**"
"16435","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold the emergence in the last age of a people named al-Rāfiḍah who reject Islam. > **""There shall appear in the last age a people named al-Rāfiḍah, rejecting Islam.""**"
"16436","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr reports that a man asked Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما for counsel, and he enjoined taqwā and caution in mentioning the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for their precedence is unknown. > **""I enjoin upon you taqwā of Allah, and beware of mentioning the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for you do not know what preceded for them.""**"
"16437","Kurayb reports that Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما told him to beware of reviling the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a blameworthy thing. > **""O boy, beware of reviling the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, for it is blameworthy.""**"
"16438","ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah reports asking al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنه about the Shīʿah claim that ʿAlī will return, and al-Ḥasan dismissed the claimants as liars, citing the marriage of his wives and division of his inheritance as proof. > **""Those liars have lied; had we known that, his wives would not have married, nor would we have divided his inheritance.""**"
"16439","Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn reports that Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنه came to him in the kuttāb, asked him to uncover his belly and kissed it, then conveyed a greeting entrusted by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded me to convey salām to you.""**"
"16440","Ibn Abī Laylā reports that on the day of Ṣiffīn a man of Syria called out asking whether Uways al-Qaranī was among them, and upon affirmation recalled hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praise him. > **""Indeed among the best of the Tābiʿīn is Uways.""**"
"16441","Abū ʿUbaydah ibn ʿAbd Allāh reports that when al-Rabīʿ ibn Khuthaym entered upon ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, none was admitted until the two finished with one another, and ʿAbd Allāh addressed him as Abū Yazīd with love and remembrance of the humble. > **""O Abū Yazīd, had the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seen you, he would have loved you; and I see you only and remember the humble.""**"
"16442","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr reports that al-Shaʿbī would narrate the Maghāzī when Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما passed, heard him, and acknowledged his superior retention despite his own presence with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""He is more retentive of them than I, though I witnessed them with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"16443","Abū Burdah رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell a man emerging from al-Kāhinayn with unmatched study of the Qurʾān. > **""There shall emerge from al-Kāhinayn a man who studies the Qurʾān with a study none after him shall study.""**"
"16444","Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī reports that Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Ẓafarī رضي الله عنه spoke against Quraysh, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade him, foretelling men among them whose deeds would dwarf his and incite admiration, withholding their rank with Allah lest they transgress. > **""O Qatādah, do not revile Quraysh, for you may see men among them before whose deeds you deem your deed small, and before whose acts your act small, envying them when you see them; were it not that Quraysh would transgress, I would inform them of what is theirs with Allah.""**"
"16445","ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim رضي الله عنه reports sitting with the Prophet ﷺ upon his return from Badr when an Anṣārī belittled the slain as hobbled old camels slaughtered, changing the Prophet's ﷺ face as though pomegranate seeds burst therein; he ﷺ corrected him that they were the great chiefs of Quraysh, awe-inspiring in their Makkan assemblies, which ʿAdī later witnessed himself, then enjoined love of Quraysh as love of himself, prayed for their latter part after their former punishment, cited remembrance and honour for his people in revelation, the Ṣiddīq, martyr, and Imāms from them, the blessed tree and Sūrah Quraysh, rejoicing whenever Quraysh was praised. > **""O company of men, love Quraysh, for whoever loves Quraysh has loved me, and whoever hates Quraysh has hated me; O Allah, as Thou hast made the first of Quraysh taste punishment, make their last taste favour.""**"
"16446","Umm Hāniʾ رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named seven distinctions of Quraysh granted to none before or after: his being from them, prophethood, veiling, watering, victory over the Elephant, sole worship for ten years, and a Sūrah revealed concerning them alone. > **""Allah favoured Quraysh with seven traits given to none before them nor after them: I am from them, prophethood is among them, the veiling is among them, the watering is among them, He aided them against the Elephant, they worshipped Allah ten years with none but them worshipping, and a Sūrah of the Qurʾān was revealed concerning them revealed concerning none but them.""**"
"16447","Al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the sevenfold favour of Quraysh: sole worship for ten years, victory over the Elephant while polytheists, Sūrah Li-īlāfi Quraysh concerning them alone, and prophethood, caliphate, veiling, and watering among them. > **""Allah favoured Quraysh with seven traits: they worshipped Allah ten years with none but a Qurashī worshipping, He aided them against the Elephant while polytheists, a Sūrah was revealed concerning them with none but them included, and among them are prophethood, the caliphate, the veiling, and the watering.""**"
"16448","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reports drought driving Nābighah of Banū Jaʿdah to ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه in Madīnah with verses likening him to al-Ṣiddīq, ʿUthmān, and al-Fārūq and seeking aid, answered with honour for Quraysh, a share for seeing the Messenger ﷺ and sharing Islam, camels, mounts, wheat and dates, and Nābighah testifying he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tie his and the Prophets' precedence to the justice, mercy, covenant, and promise of Quraysh. > **""Never did Quraysh rule justly, show mercy when asked, covenant faithfully, and fulfil promises, but I and the Prophets stood ahead of the throng.""**"
"16449","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon her declaring he would inform Quraysh of their rank with Allah were it not that they would exult. > **""Were it not that Quraysh would exult, I would inform them of what is theirs with Allah.""**"
"16450","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reports, to his knowledge, that the Prophet ﷺ commanded advancing Quraysh without preceding them, withholding their rank with Allah lest they exult. > **""Advance Quraysh and do not precede them; were it not that Quraysh would exult, I would inform them of what is theirs with Allah.""**"
"16451","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Sāʾib رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger ﷺ commanding precedence for Quraysh and learning from them without teaching them, withholding the reward of their best lest they exult; and ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Jazʾ al-Zubaydī رضي الله عنه reports knowledge in Quraysh and trust in al-Azd. > **""Advance Quraysh and do not precede them, learn from Quraysh and do not teach them; were it not that Quraysh would exult, I would inform them of what is for their best with Allah, Exalted is He.""**"
"16452","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded seeking trust in Quraysh, whose trustworthy and strong excel others twofold. > **""Seek trust in Quraysh, for the trustworthy of Quraysh excels the trustworthy of others, and the strong of Quraysh has twofold excellence over the strong of others.""**"
"16453","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the Qurashī double the strength of others, explained by al-Zuhrī as nobility of judgment. > **""Indeed the Qurashī has double the strength of a man other than Quraysh.""**"
"16454","Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger ﷺ commanding ʿUmar رضي الله عنه to gather his people at his house, emerging to them, declaring allies, nephews, and clients among them, warning against worldly burdens on the Day of Resurrection despite kinship save through piety, then raising his hands thrice that Quraysh are trustworthy and whoever plots pitfalls for them Allah shall cast upon his nostrils. > **""O men, Quraysh are trustworthy; whoever seeks pitfalls for them, Allah shall cast him upon his nostrils.""**"
"16455","Al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه reports praising the fidelity of Quraysh converts at the conquest of Makkah after Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for their understanding and perpetual favour after former punishment. > **""O Allah, grant Quraysh understanding in religion and make them taste favour from this day of mine until the end of time, as Thou hast made them taste punishment.""**"
"16456","Abū Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAbd al-Lāt of Yamanī Azd reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ place trust in al-Azd and modesty in Quraysh. > **""Trust is in al-Azd, and modesty is in Quraysh.""**"
"16457","Al-Mustawrid al-Fihrī رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention Quraysh with four traits: soundest at tribulation, quickest recovery after calamity, swiftest return after retreat, and firmest against royal oppression. > **""Indeed among them are four traits: they are the soundest of men at tribulation, quickest to recover after calamity, swiftest to return after retreat, and most preventive of the oppression of kings.""**"
"16458","ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Mūsā reports Sulaymān ibn ʿAlī seating a Qurashī elder for the right of Quraysh, then hearing from Rabīʿah via Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab via ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān via his father ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that whoever degrades Quraysh Allah shall degrade, enjoining their honour in rule. > **""Whoever degrades Quraysh, Allah shall degrade him.""**"
"16459","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that whoever degrades Quraysh Allah shall degrade before his death. > **""Whoever degrades Quraysh, Allah shall degrade him before his death.""**"
"16460","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reports that when a Thaqafī convert was slain, the Prophet ﷺ cast him away for hating Quraysh. > **""May Allah cast him away, for he hated Quraysh.""**"
"16461","Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stand on the day of Ḥunayn over a slain man of Thaqīf, casting him away for hating Quraysh. > **""May Allah cast you away, for you hated Quraysh.""**"
"16462","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made love of Quraysh faith and hatred disbelief, and love of the Arabs love of himself and hatred hatred of himself. > **""Love of Quraysh is faith and hatred of them disbelief; whoever loves the Arabs has loved me, and whoever hates them has hated me.""**"
"16463","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared hatred of Banū Hāshim and the Anṣār disbelief, and hatred of the Arabs hypocrisy. > **""Hatred of Banū Hāshim and the Anṣār is disbelief, and hatred of the Arabs is hypocrisy.""**"
"16464","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded love of Quraysh as a cause for the love of Allah Almighty. > **""Love Quraysh, for whoever loves them, Allah Almighty loves him.""**"
"16465","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her saying her people would be quickest of his Ummah to join him; when she asked in alarm, he ﷺ explained deaths would claim them and their Ummah would envy them, and after them men would be like wingless locusts whose strong devour the weak until the Hour. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, your people shall be the quickest of my Ummah to join me.""**"
"16466","In another wording ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports the Prophet ﷺ saying her people, this clan of Quraysh, shall perish first as deaths claim them and men turn on them; asked about mankind after them, he ﷺ called them the backbone of men whose ruin ruins mankind. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, the first of men to perish shall be your people; they are the backbone of men, and when they perish, men perish.""**"
"16467","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named Quraysh the quickest Arab tribes to perish, until a woman passing a sandal would call it a Qurashī sandal. > **""The quickest Arab tribes to perish is Quraysh; a woman shall soon pass by a sandal and say: this is a Qurashī sandal.""**"
"16468","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied the firmness of religion to the survival of twenty men of Quraysh. > **""Religion shall remain firm so long as twenty men of Quraysh remain.""**"
"16469","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every people has a strength, and the strength of Quraysh is their clients. > **""Indeed every people has a strength, and the strength of Quraysh is their clients.""**"
"16470","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the angels, the Anṣār, and ʿAbd al-Qays all submitted willingly. > **""The angels submitted willingly, the Anṣār submitted willingly, and ʿAbd al-Qays submitted willingly.""**"
"16471","Saʿīd ibn ʿUbādah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called this clan of Anṣār a trial whose love is faith and whose hatred is hypocrisy. > **""Indeed this clan of the Anṣār is a trial; loving them is faith and hating them is hypocrisy.""**"
"16472","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied love and hatred of himself to love and hatred of the Anṣār, denied hypocrites love and believers hatred of them, tied Allah's love and hatred likewise, called men the outer cloak and Anṣār the inner garment, and preferred their mountain path. > **""Whoever loves me loves the Anṣār, and whoever hates me hates the Anṣār; the Anṣār are the inner garment and men the outer cloak, and had men taken a path and the Anṣār a path, I would take the path of the Anṣār.""**"
"16473","Abū Saʿīd reports from the Prophet ﷺ that no man believing in Allah and the Last Day hates the Anṣār. > **""No man who believes in Allah and the Last Day hates the Anṣār.""**"
"16474","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made love of the Anṣār faith and hatred hypocrisy. > **""Love of the Anṣār is faith, and hatred of them is hypocrisy.""**"
"16475","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave gifts to Quraysh and Arab tribes with nothing for the Anṣār, resentment spread until one said he had met his people; Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah رضي الله عنه conveyed it, declared himself one of his people, gathered them in an enclosure excluding others, and the Prophet ﷺ came, praised Allah, reminded them of guidance after error, riches after poverty, and unity after enmity, drew from them that favour belongs to Allah and His Messenger, rehearsed their truthful claim of belief, aid, shelter, and consolation, reproached worldly rivalry, offered return with the Messenger ﷺ over sheep and camels, declared himself of the Anṣār but for Hijrah, promised their path, and prayed for them and their descendants until they wept and chose the Messenger ﷺ as share. > **""Are you not pleased, O company of Anṣār, that men go with sheep and camels while you return with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in your dwellings? O Allah, have mercy on the Anṣār, the sons of the Anṣār, and the sons of the sons of the Anṣār.""**"
"16476","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports in another wording that an Anṣārī warned his fellows that favouritism would follow stability and was harshly rebuffed; when it reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he ﷺ reminded them of blessings including riding horses, met only with assent, then named the Anṣār his inner circle, household, and refuge, enjoining pardon for their wrongdoers and acceptance from their righteous; Abū Saʿīd later told Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه of the foretold favouritism and the command to patience, which Muʿāwiyah affirmed. > **""The Anṣār are my inner circle, my household, and my refuge to which I resorted; so pardon their wrongdoers and accept from their righteous.""**"
"16477","In another wording the command is to endure until meeting him ﷺ at the Ḥawḍ, transmitted fully by Aḥmad and partly by Abū Yaʿlā. > **""So be patient until you meet me at the Ḥawḍ.""**"
"16478","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه and Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه transmit in abridged form what preceded concerning the Anṣār and patience. > **""Be patient until you meet me at the Ḥawḍ.""**"
"16479","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that after Ḥunayn detachments brought camels and sheep which were divided among Quraysh, the Anṣār felt resentment, and the Prophet ﷺ gathered and addressed them, offering the Messenger of Allah ﷺ himself as their share and vowing to follow their path were men and they to take separate valleys. > **""Are you not pleased that you have been given the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? By Allah, had men taken a valley and you taken a mountain path, I would follow your mountain path.""**"
"16480","Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ divided the fayʾ of Ḥunayn from Hawāzin spoils generously among Makkans of Quraysh and others, angering the Anṣār; he ﷺ came to their dwellings, cleared others, praised Allah, explained conciliating new Muslims whose hearts Allah had entered into Islam, reminded them of faith, honour, and the names Anṣār of Allah and His Messenger, declared himself of the Anṣār but for Hijrah, promised their valley, and offered return with the Messenger ﷺ over livestock; they professed satisfaction, recalled darkness to light and error to guidance, professed pleasure with Allah, Islam, and Muḥammad ﷺ, and he ﷺ affirmed their truth had they claimed shelter, belief, and aid, until they wept and he ﷺ wept with them, bound more by his words than any wealth. > **""Are you not pleased that men go with sheep, livestock, and camels while you go with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?""**"
"16481","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that at Ḥunayn the Prophet ﷺ gained spoils and divided them, the Anṣār saying they fought while others took spoils; he ﷺ summoned them, asked whether any outsider was among them, was told only a nephew and a client, ruled both as of the people, then offered taking Muḥammad ﷺ to their homes over sheep and camels, which they accepted. > **""O company of Anṣār, are you not pleased that men go with sheep and camels while you go with Muḥammad to your homes?""**"
"16482","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jubayr reports that the Prophet ﷺ told the Anṣār that men in general are the outer cloak while they are the inner garment, promising to follow their valley over others and declaring he would be of the Anṣār were Allah not to have named him of the Muhājirīn, which they accepted. > **""Are you not pleased that other men are the outer cloak while you are the inner garment? Had men taken a valley and you taken another, I would follow your valley and leave men.""**"
"16483","ʿAbbād ibn Bashīr al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the Anṣār the inner garment and men the outer cloak, warning against being reached through them. > **""O company of Anṣār, you are the inner garment and men are the outer cloak; I shall not be reached from your side.""**"
"16484","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl reports that Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه came to Madīnah and Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه met him, recalling the Messenger's ﷺ warning of favouritism after him and the command to patience, which Muʿāwiyah affirmed. > **""You shall meet favouritism after me.""**"
"16485","Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ call this clan of Anṣār his trust, declaring himself of the Anṣār but for Hijrah, promising their mountain path, naming Anṣār the inner garment and men the outer, and enjoining kindness to their righteous and pardon for their wrongdoers. > **""Indeed every Prophet has a trust, and my trust is this clan of the Anṣār; the Anṣār are the inner garment and men the outer cloak, so whoever governs men, let him do good to their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16486","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out declaring every Prophet has a legacy and handiwork, and his legacy and handiwork are the Anṣār, enjoining his trust in them. > **""Indeed every Prophet has a legacy and handiwork, and my legacy and handiwork are the Anṣār, so keep my trust concerning them.""**"
"16487","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ heard something and addressed the Anṣār, reminding them of might after abasement, guidance after error, and safety after fear, prompting them to answer with shelter, belief, and possessions on their knees, whereupon love of kin was revealed. > **""Were you not abased, and Allah honoured you through me? Were you not astray, and Allah guided you through me? Were you not fearful, and Allah secured you through me?""**"
"16488","Maṭar Abū Mūsā, client of Ṭalḥah, reports sitting as a boy with Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما when Abū Hurayrah defended his transmission, recalled the Messenger ﷺ preferring the valley of the Anṣār and declaring himself of them but for Hijrah, each confirmed by Ibn ʿUmar. > **""Had men taken a valley and the Anṣār taken a valley, I would walk with the Anṣār in that valley.""**"
"16489","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ knew the mountain path better than the valley, in reference to the Anṣār. > **""The mountain path is better than the valley.""**"
"16490","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the minbar call men the outer cloak and Anṣār the inner, promise their mountain path, declare himself of them but for Hijrah, enjoin kindness and pardon, and warn that frightening them frightens what lies between his sides, pointing to his breast, his heart. > **""Indeed men are the outer cloak and the Anṣār are the inner garment; whoever frightens them has frightened this which lies between these two.""**"
"16491","Ibn Shafīʿ the physician reports that Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنه summoned him to cut a sciatic vein and narrated two reports: two Anṣārī households of Ẓafar and Banū Muʿāwiyah sought division through the Prophet ﷺ, who promised each half and future return, exchanged thanks praising them as chaste and patient, and warned of favouritism after him; later ʿUmar رضي الله عنه distributed robes, Usayd deemed his small and gave it to his daughter, then seeing a Qurashī youth trailing such a robe recalled the warning, reported it to ʿUmar, who explained it had gone to a Badrī Uḥudī ʿAqabī from whom the youth bought it, asking whether he thought it would occur in his time, which Usayd affirmed. > **""You shall meet favouritism after me.""**"
"16492","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنه reports his father ordering khazīrah brought to the Prophet ﷺ at home, first without meat then with a roasted household sheep after Jābir relayed a question about meat, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ prayed for the Anṣār, especially ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām and Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah رضي الله عنهما. > **""May Allah reward the Anṣār well on our behalf, especially ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥarām and Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah.""**"
"16493","Abū Muḥammad ibn Bashīr al-Anṣārī reports from his father and grandfather that Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam wrote to al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه seeking his daughter Umm Abān for his son ʿAbd al-Malik with lavish dower and praise of Anṣārī aid; al-Nuʿmān replied beginning with his own name as Sunnah from the Messenger ﷺ who enjoined beginning with oneself, affirmed their love as faith and hatred as hypocrisy sufficed by Allah's praise, returned the boast of preference, and answered the dower boast with verse claiming suffices and noble marriages among Banū al-ʿAnqāʾ, Ibnay Muḥarriq, Āl ʿImrān, and ʿAmr ibn ʿĀmir. > **""When one of you writes to another, let him begin with himself.""**"
"16494","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared Allah aided him through the strongest-tongued and strongest-armed Arabs, the two sons of Qaylah, al-Aws and al-Khazraj. > **""Indeed Allah aided me through the strongest of Arabs in tongues and arms, through the two sons of Qaylah: al-Aws and al-Khazraj.""**"
"16495","Abū Wāqid al-Laythī رضي الله عنه reports sitting knee to knee with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a comer whispered, changing his ﷺ face with blood rising, complaining that ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl threatened him and those beside him, and Allah sufficed him through sons of Ismāʿīl, the two sons of Qaylah, meaning the Anṣār. > **""Allah sufficed me against him through the sons of Ismāʿīl, through the two sons of Qaylah.""**"
"16496","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the minbar for the Anṣār call men his outer cloak and Anṣār his inner, promise their mountain path, declare himself of them but for Hijrah, enjoin kindness and pardon, and warn that frightening them frightens what lies between his sides, pointing to himself. > **""Indeed men are my outer cloak and the Anṣār are my inner garment; whoever frightens them has frightened this which lies between these two.""**"
"16497","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik reports from a Companion that the Prophet ﷺ stood preaching, praised Allah, sought forgiveness for the martyrs of Uḥud, then said the Muhājirīn increase while the Anṣār do not, naming them his trust and refuge, enjoining honour for their noble and pardon for their wrongdoers, for they discharged their duty while their reward remains. > **""O company of Muhājirīn, you increase while the Anṣār do not increase; the Anṣār are my trust to which I resorted, so honour their noble and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16498","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik al-Anṣārī, meaning his father among the three forgiven, reports through a Companion that the Prophet ﷺ emerged head-bound declaring the Muhājirīn increasing while the Anṣār remain as today, naming them his trust and refuge, enjoining honour and pardon. > **""The Anṣār are my trust to which I resorted, so honour their noble and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16499","Qudāmah ibn Ibrāhīm reports seeing al-Ḥajjāj beat ʿAbbās ibn Sahl over Ibn al-Zubayr, when Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه, an aged braided elder in waist-wrapper and cloak, stood between the rows invoking the Messenger's ﷺ bequest to do good to the righteous of the Anṣār and pardon their wrongdoers, and al-Ḥajjāj released him. > **""He enjoined doing good to the righteous of the Anṣār and pardoning their wrongdoers.""**"
"16500","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded accepting from the righteous of the Anṣār and overlooking their wrongdoers. > **""Accept from the righteous of the Anṣār and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16501","Zayd ibn Saʿd reports from his father that when his own death was announced to the Prophet ﷺ, he ﷺ emerged wrapped in worn clothes to the minbar before townsfolk and marketfolk, praised Allah, and enjoined remembering him well through this clan of Anṣār, his stomach and trust, accepting from their righteous and overlooking their wrongdoers. > **""O men, remember me well through this clan of the Anṣār, for they are my stomach in which I eat and my trust; accept from their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16502","Muhājir ibn Dīnār reports that Abū Saʿīd al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه passed Marwān fallen on the day of the House, vowing to finish him had he known life remained, which ʿAbd al-Malik resented; when caliph he summoned him, and Abū Saʿīd invoked the Messenger's ﷺ bequest to preserve their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers. > **""Preserve their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16503","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reports that Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه wrote to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه recalling the Messenger's ﷺ bequest concerning the Anṣār at his death to accept from their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers. > **""Accept from their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16504","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was told the Anṣār, men and women, wept in the mosque fearing his ﷺ death, so he ﷺ emerged wrapped with ends on his shoulders and head bound, praised Allah, and said men increase while the Anṣār decrease until like salt in food, enjoining acceptance and pardon for whoever governs them. > **""Men shall increase and the Anṣār shall decrease until they are like salt in food; whoever governs any of their affairs, let him accept from their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16505","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emerged, prayed, enjoined good for men, then said the Muhājirīn increase while the Anṣār remain as today, naming them his trust and refuge, enjoining honour and pardon. > **""The Anṣār are my trust to which I resorted, so honour their noble and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16506","ʿAbbās ibn Sahl ibn Saʿd reports that Sahl رضي الله عنه entered upon al-Ḥajjāj leaning on his hand and cited the Prophet ﷺ on doing good to the righteous of the Anṣār and pardoning their wrongdoers, witnessed by ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar and Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥāṭib at his sides. > **""Do good to their righteous and pardon their wrongdoers.""**"
"16507","Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the Anṣār his stomach and trust, noting men increase while they decrease, enjoining acceptance and pardon. > **""The Anṣār are my stomach and my trust; men shall increase while they decrease, so accept from their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16508","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik reports from his father among the three forgiven that the Prophet ﷺ stood preaching, praised Allah, sought forgiveness for the martyrs of Uḥud, and said the Muhājirīn increase while the Anṣār do not, naming them his trust and refuge, enjoining honour and pardon, for they discharged their duty while their reward remains. > **""The Anṣār are my trust to which I resorted, so honour their noble and overlook their wrongdoers, for they have discharged what was upon them and what is theirs remains.""**"
"16509","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik reports from his father that the last sermon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preached to them covered the same concerning the Anṣār in abridged form. > **""Honour their noble and overlook their wrongdoers.""**"
"16510","Muḥammad and Maḥmūd sons of Jābir report going out when Ḥubaysh ibn Duljah entered Madīnah a year after al-Ḥarrah until the minbar showed, frightening men; they brought blind Jābir at al-Ḥarrah, a stone struck him aside, and twice or thrice unasked he invoked Allah's fright upon whoever frightened the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then testified he heard the Messenger ﷺ warn that frightening the Anṣār frightens what lies between his sides. > **""Whoever frightens the Anṣār has frightened what lies between these two.""**"
"16511","In another wording he ﷺ placed his hands upon his sides while uttering the warning concerning frightening the Anṣār, and in other wordings frightening the people of Madīnah. > **""Whoever frightens the Anṣār has frightened what lies between these two.""**"
"16512","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reports that Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه asked the Anṣār why they did not meet him with Quraysh brethren, ʿUbādah citing need and worn camels at Badr without answer, then recalling the Messenger's ﷺ foretelling of favouritism after him and the command to patience until meeting him, which Muʿāwiyah affirmed. > **""There shall be favouritism against you after me.""**"
"16513","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when Muʿāwiyah arrived and the Anṣār delayed meeting him without reprisal, Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه affirmed the truthful promise of favouritism after him ﷺ with patience until meeting him, which Muʿāwiyah affirmed and Abū Ayyūb upheld. > **""Favouritism shall befall you after me, so be patient until you meet me.""**"
"16514","A man reports that Dhū al-Yadayn addressed the Anṣār asking whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not commanded patience until meeting him. > **""Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ not command you to be patient until you meet him?""**"
"16515","Al-Ḥārith ibn Ziyād al-Sāʿidī al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Prophet ﷺ on the day of al-Khandaq while he pledged men to Hijrah, presenting his cousin Ḥawṭ, and the Prophet ﷺ refused their pledge since men emigrate to them, not from them, swearing that love or hatred of the Anṣār until meeting Allah returns like for like. > **""No man loves the Anṣār until he meets Allah but Allah meets him loving him, and no man hates the Anṣār until he meets Allah but Allah meets him hating him.""**"
"16516","Abū Usayd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه reports that when men came to dig al-Khandaq pledging Hijrah, the Prophet ﷺ when finished told the Anṣār not to pledge to Hijrah since men emigrate to them, and meeting Allah with love or hatred of the Anṣār returns like for like. > **""Men emigrate to you; whoever meets Allah loving the Anṣār, Allah meets him loving him, and whoever meets Allah hating the Anṣār, Allah meets him hating him.""**"
"16517","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reports sitting among Anṣār when Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه emerged, asked their talk, heard it concerned the Anṣār, and added what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tying Allah's love and hatred to love and hatred of the Anṣār. > **""Whoever loves the Anṣār, Allah loves him, and whoever hates the Anṣār, Allah hates him.""**"
"16518","Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied love and hatred of the Anṣār to love and hatred of himself. > **""Whoever loves the Anṣār, through love of me he loved them, and whoever hates the Anṣār, through hatred of me he hated them.""**"
"16519","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tied Allah's love and hatred to love and hatred of the Anṣār. > **""Whoever loves the Anṣār, Allah loves him, and whoever hates the Anṣār, Allah hates him.""**"
"16520","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ again tied love and hatred of the Anṣār to love and hatred of himself. > **""Whoever loves the Anṣār, through love of me he loved them, and whoever hates the Anṣār, through hatred of me he hated them.""**"
"16521","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the Anṣār his trust and refuge, enjoining acceptance and pardon, for they discharged their duty while their reward remains. > **""The Anṣār are my trust to which I resorted, so accept from their righteous and overlook their wrongdoers, for they have discharged what was upon them and what is theirs remains.""**"
"16522","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made love of the Anṣār the sign of every believer and hypocrite, through love and hatred of himself. > **""Love of the Anṣār is the sign of every believer and hypocrite; whoever loves the Anṣār, through love of me he loved them, and whoever hates the Anṣār, through hatred of me he hated them.""**"
"16523","Rabāḥ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥuwayṭib reports from his grandmother hearing her father Saʿīd ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه say he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ deny prayer without ablution, ablution without Allah's name, faith in Allah without faith in him, and faith in him without love of the Anṣār. > **""None believes in Allah who does not believe in me, and none believes in me who does not love the Anṣār.""**"
"16524","Rabāḥ reports from his grandmother hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ tie belief in Allah to belief in him and belief in him to love of the Anṣār. > **""None believes in Allah who does not believe in me, and none believes in me who does not love the Anṣār.""**"
"16525","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded renewing Islam through love of the Anṣār, loved only by believers and hated only by hypocrites. > **""Renew Islam through love of the Anṣār, for none loves them but a believer, and none hates them but a hypocrite.""**"
"16526","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Anṣār when harvesting palms would split dates into unequal shares, add fronds to the lesser, let Muslims choose the greater until Khaybar opened, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered release from Khaybar shares for ripened fruits; they recalled his conditions upon them and their condition upon him of Paradise, which he ﷺ confirmed as theirs. > **""You have fulfilled for us what was upon you; that is yours.""**"
"16527","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said no harm befalls a woman dwelling between two Anṣārī houses or between her parents. > **""No harm befalls a woman who dwells between two houses of the Anṣār, or dwells between her parents.""**"
"16528","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that watering hardship drove the Anṣār to ask the Prophet ﷺ to hire them the flowing Sayḥ river; he ﷺ welcomed the Anṣār, promised every request that day and every supplication for them, and at their seeking forgiveness supplicated for the Anṣār, their sons, and their sons' sons, in other wordings adding wives and daughters-in-law. > **""O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the sons of the Anṣār, and the sons of the sons of the Anṣār.""**"
"16529","In another wording the supplication extends to the wives of the Anṣār, greeted thrice with welcome. > **""O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the sons of the Anṣār, the sons of the sons of the Anṣār, and the wives of the Anṣār.""**"
"16530","Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for the Anṣār, their offspring, their offsprings' offspring, and their neighbours. > **""O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the offspring of the Anṣār, the offspring of their offspring, and their neighbours.""**"
"16531","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for the Anṣār, their sons, their sons' sons, their wives, and their offspring. > **""O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the sons of the Anṣār, the sons of the sons of the Anṣār, their wives, and their offspring.""**"
"16532","Khuzaymah ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for the Anṣār, their sons, and their sons' sons. > **""O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the sons of the Anṣār, and the sons of the sons of the Anṣār.""**"
"16533","ʿAwf al-Anṣārī al-Ashhalī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for the Anṣār, their sons, their sons' sons, and their clients. > **""O Allah, forgive the Anṣār, the sons of the Anṣār, the sons of the sons of the Anṣār, and the clients of the Anṣār.""**"
"16534","ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ place faith in Yemen, its support in Qaḥṭān, hardness in ʿAdnān's sons, Ḥimyar the head and tooth, Madhḥij the crown and protection, Azd the shoulder and skull, Hamdān the withers and peak, then praying might for the Anṣār through whom Allah established religion, who sheltered, aided, and protected him, his companions in this world, partisans in the next, and first of his Ummah into Paradise. > **""O Allah, honour the Anṣār through whom Allah established the religion, who sheltered, aided, and protected me; they are my companions in this world, my partisans in the Hereafter, and the first of my Ummah to enter Paradise.""**"
"16535","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told Abū Ṭalḥah رضي الله عنه to convey salām to his people and tell them they are, as he knew them, chaste and patient. > **""Convey salām to your people, and tell them they are, as I knew them, chaste and patient.""**"
"16536","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a fevered Anṣārī, heard him speaking within yet found none, was told a man of unmatched مجلس and speech visited him grieving over men's talk, and identified him as Jibrīl, noting men among them whose oaths Allah fulfils. > **""That was Jibrīl, and among you are men whom, if one swears by Allah, He fulfils him.""**"
"16537","Anas رضي الله عنه reports Aws and Khazraj boasting: Aws naming Ḥanẓalah washed by angels, Saʿd ibn Muʿādh at whom the Throne shook, ʿĀṣim protected by hornets, and Khuzaymah whose testimony equalled two; Khazraj naming four who gathered the Qurʾān under the Messenger ﷺ: Zayd ibn Thābit, Abū Zayd, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنهم. > **""Four gathered the Qurʾān under the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, none but they: Zayd ibn Thābit, Abū Zayd, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and Muʿādh ibn Jabal.""**"
"16538","Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, and Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah report that six companions gathered the Qurʾān under the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, all of the Anṣār, naming Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, Muʿādh ibn Jabal, Zayd ibn Thābit, Abū Zayd, and Saʿd ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنهم, though only five of the six are counted. > **""Six of the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gathered the Qurʾān under the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, all of them of the Anṣār.""**"
"16539","Saʿd ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه reports the like concerning gathering the Qurʾān. > **""Six of the companions gathered the Qurʾān under the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, all of them of the Anṣār.""**"
"16540","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ returned from Tabūk at the pass he ﷺ said Allāhu Akbar, then looking at Uḥud declared its mutual love, then named the best Anṣārī homes as ʿAbd al-Ashhal, Dār al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, Banū al-Najjār, then Banū Sāʿidah; when Saʿd رضي الله عنه complained of being last, he ﷺ answered sufficiency in being among the best. > **""The best homes of the Anṣār are ʿAbd al-Ashhal, then Dār al-Ḥārith ibn al-Khazraj, then Dār Banī al-Najjār, then Dār Banī Sāʿidah.""**"
"16541","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed slave-girls of Banū al-Najjār beating the duff and singing of themselves and the excellence of Muḥammad ﷺ as neighbour, whereupon the Prophet of Allah ﷺ prayed for blessing upon them. > **""O Allah, bless them.""**"
"16542","Zayd ibn Khālid رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named Quraysh, the Anṣār, Aslam, Ghifār, and those of Ashjaʿ and Juhaynah as allied clients with no protector but Allah and His Messenger ﷺ. > **""Quraysh, the Anṣār, Aslam, Ghifār, and whoever is of Ashjaʿ and Juhaynah are allied clients; they have no protector besides Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"16543","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named Quraysh, the Anṣār, Juhaynah, Muzaynah, Aslam, Ghifār, Ashjaʿ, and Sulaym as his allies with no protector but Allah and His Messenger ﷺ. > **""Quraysh, the Anṣār, Juhaynah, Muzaynah, Aslam, Ghifār, Ashjaʿ, and Sulaym are my allies; they have no protector besides Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"16544","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Prophet ﷺ amid boasting Arabs and being told to boast through Quraysh, match numbers through Tamīm, and fight through Qays, whose faces are Kinānah, tongue Asd, and horsemen Qays; Allah's horsemen in heaven are angels and on earth Qays, and the last to fight for Islam when only its mention remains and for the Qurʾān when only its script remains shall be a man of Qays, of Sulaym. > **""O Abā al-Dardāʾ, when you boast before men, boast through Quraysh; when you match numbers, match through Tamīm; and when you fight, fight through Qays.""**"
"16545","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports tribes mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who likened Banū ʿĀmir to a bright camel eating tree edges, Hawāzin to a blossom gushing water, and Banū Tamīm to the firm-footed, weighty-minded, great-headed, hardest against the Dajjāl at the end, a red plateau unharmed by opponents. > **""Firm of foot, weighty of mind, great of head, the hardest of men against the Dajjāl at the end of time.""**"
"16546","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah رضي الله عنه reports horses displayed before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ beside ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn al-Fazārī, who claimed men of Najd bearing swords on shoulders, spears on horses' manes, and cloaks are best; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rejected it, naming men of Yemen best, faith Yemeni toward Lakhm, Judhām, and ʿĀmilah, the eaten of Ḥimyar better than its eaters, Ḥaḍramawt better than Banū al-Ḥārith, tribes better and worse, indifference to the ruin of both Ḥāriths, cursing the four kings Jamadāʾ, Mikhwasāʾ, Mishraḥāʾ, Abḍaʿah and their sister al-ʿAmarradah, then telling of commanded cursing then blessing of Quraysh twice each, disobedience of ʿUṣayyah save Qays, Jaʿdah, and ʿUṣayyah, superiority of Aslam, Ghifār, and Juhaynī allies over Asad, Tamīm, Ghaṭafān, and Hawāzin on the Day of Resurrection, worst Arab tribes Najrān and Banū Taghlib, and most numerous in Paradise Madhḥij and Maʾkūl. > **""You have lied; rather the best of men are the men of Yemen, and faith is Yemeni.""**"
"16547","In another wording the eaten of Ḥimyar is better than its eaters, explained that those who passed are better than those who remain. > **""The eaten of Ḥimyar is better than its eaters.""**"
"16548","In another wording he ﷺ declares himself Yemeni, Ḥaḍramawt better than Banū al-Ḥārith, indifference to both clans perishing, with no ruler or king but Allah. > **""I am Yemeni, and Ḥaḍramawt is better than Banū al-Ḥārith; there is no ruler nor king but Allah.""**"
"16549","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah رضي الله عنه reports in fuller wording horses displayed beside ʿUyaynah, rival claims over horsemen and men of Najd in cloaks with swords and spears, corrected toward Yemen, faith Yemeni toward Lakhm, Judhām, and ʿĀmilah, eaten Ḥimyar better, Ḥaḍramawt better, no ruler, conqueror but Allah; al-Simṭ verified the Ḥaḍramawt wording through him and believed; the Prophet ﷺ cursed the four kings and their sister who mounted prostrating Muslims, cursed Quraysh twice then blessed them twice by command, named most numerous in Paradise Madhḥij, Aslam, Ghifār, Muzaynah with Juhaynī allies better than Banū Asad, Tamīm, Hawāzin, and Ghaṭafān, indifference to both clans, and commanded cursing of Tamīm ibn Murr seven times, Bakr ibn Wāʾil five times, and others. > **""You have lied; rather they are the people of Yemen; faith is Yemeni.""**"
"16550","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ displaying horses in their abode when ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn claimed superior knowledge of men of Najd with swords, spears, and cloaks; the Prophet ﷺ rejected it, naming best men those of Yemeni faith, most numerous in Paradise Madhḥij, eaten Ḥimyar better, Ḥaḍramawt better than Kindah, cursing the four kings and their sister. > **""You have lied; rather the best of men are the men of Yemeni faith, and the most numerous tribe in Paradise is Madhḥij.""**"
"16551","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoked blessing upon al-Sakūn, al-Sakāsik, Khawlān al-ʿĀliyah, and al-Amlūk Amlūk Ridmān. > **""May Allah bless al-Sakūn, al-Sakāsik, Khawlān al-ʿĀliyah, and al-Amlūk Amlūk Ridmān.""**"
"16552","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ count among the best of men al-Amlūk of Ḥimyar, Sufyān, al-Sakūn, and the Ashʿarīs. > **""Among the best of men are al-Amlūk of Ḥimyar, Sufyān, al-Sakūn, and the Ashʿarīs.""**"
"16553","Abū al-Ṭufayl al-Kinānī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked for one to inform of Muḍar, and a man answered that its face of hearing and sight is Quraysh, its tongue in assemblies Banū Asad ibn Khuzaymah, its shoulder Banū Tamīm ibn Murrah, and its horsemen Qays ʿAylān, which the Prophet ﷺ appeared to confirm. > **""Its face is this clan of Quraysh, its tongue this clan of Banū Asad, its shoulder this clan of Banū Tamīm, and its horsemen this clan of Qays.""**"
"16554","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred Aslam, Ghifār, men of Muzaynah, and Juhaynah over the two allies Ghaṭafān and Banū ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah, whereupon ʿUyaynah ibn Badr swore he would rather be with the latter in Fire than the former in Paradise. > **""Aslam, Ghifār, men of Muzaynah, and Juhaynah are better than the two allies: Ghaṭafān and Banū ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah.""**"
"16555","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether Aslam and Ghifār being better than Asad and Ghaṭafān, and Muzaynah and Juhaynah better than Asad, Ghaṭafān, Hawāzin, Tamīm, and ʿĀmir ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah, meant loss, affirmed by them, whereupon ʿUyaynah ibn Badr preferred the latter in Fire, and the Prophet ﷺ named him a gladly-seen obeyed fool. > **""Whoever is pleased to look at an obeyed fool, let him look at this one.""**"
"16556","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Aslam, Ghifār, Muzaynah, Ashjaʿ, Juhaynah, and Banū Kaʿb are his clients apart from men, with Allah and His Messenger as protector. > **""Aslam, Ghifār, Muzaynah, Ashjaʿ, Juhaynah, and whoever is of Banū Kaʿb are my clients apart from men, and Allah and His Messenger are their protector.""**"
"16557","Maʿqil ibn Sinān رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named Ghifār, Aslam, Juhaynah, and Muzaynah clients of Allah Almighty and His Messenger. > **""Ghifār, Aslam, Juhaynah, and Muzaynah are clients of Allah Almighty and His Messenger.""**"
"16558","Abū Barzah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed peace for Aslam and forgiveness for Ghifār, disclaiming authorship and attributing the words to Allah Almighty. > **""Aslam, may Allah grant it peace, and Ghifār, may Allah forgive it; I did not say it, but Allah Almighty said it.""**"
"16559","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke the same words of peace for Aslam and forgiveness for Ghifār as from Allah. > **""Aslam, may Allah grant it peace, and Ghifār, may Allah forgive it; I did not say it, but Allah said it.""**"
"16560","Ibn Sandar reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ added Tujīb answering Allah and His Messenger alongside Aslam and Ghifār, confirmed to Abū al-Khayr by Abū al-Aswad as heard. > **""Aslam, may Allah grant it peace, Ghifār, may Allah forgive it, and Tujīb answered Allah and His Messenger.""**"
"16561","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke briefly the peace for Aslam and forgiveness for Ghifār. > **""Aslam, may Allah grant it peace, and Ghifār, may Allah forgive it.""**"
"16562","Abū Qirṣāfah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke briefly the peace for Aslam and forgiveness for Ghifār. > **""Aslam, may Allah grant it peace, and Ghifār, may Allah forgive it.""**"
"16563","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say Ghifār and Aslam were a trial for many who claimed had it been good Allah would not make it first, while forgiveness and peace were decreed for them. > **""Ghifār, may Allah forgive it, and Aslam, may Allah grant it peace.""**"
"16564","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh reports from his father and grandfather that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, seeing an approaching company, welcomed Azd as best-faced, sweetest-mouthed, and truest in meeting, then seeing a dense host of Bakr ibn Wāʾil prayed for mending their broken, sheltering their expelled, pleasing their righteous, and never showing him a beggar among them. > **""Azd has come to you, best of men in faces, sweetest in mouths, and truest in meeting.""**"
"16565","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports owing a neck of Ismāʿīl's children, intending to free from Khawlān captives but forbidden by the Prophet ﷺ, then commanded to free from Muḍarī Banū al-ʿAnbar captives when they came. > **""Free from them.""**"
"16566","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports that ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها owed a freedman of Ismāʿīl's children, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded freeing from al-ʿAnbar captives, warning none bound by such a vow should free any of Ḥimyar, explained as older than Ismāʿīl. > **""Whoever owes a freedman of Ismāʿīl's children, let him free none of Ḥimyar.""**"
"16567","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها owed a freedman of Ismāʿīl's children, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded her to free from Banū al-ʿAnbar captives when they arrived. > **""Free from them.""**"
"16568","Zubayb ibn Thaʿlabah رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command whoever owes a neck of Ismāʿīl's children to free from Banū al-ʿAnbar. > **""Whoever owes a neck of Ismāʿīl's children, let him free from Banū al-ʿAnbar.""**"
"16569","Dhuʾayb reports that ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها sought a captive of pure Ismāʿīlī stock, and the Prophet ﷺ told her to await the morrow's ʿAnbar fayʾ, then to take four fair handsome boys without hiding heads; ʿAṭāʾ names them Rudayḥ, Samurah, Zukhayy, and Zubayb, whom the Prophet ﷺ stroked, blessed, and declared purely of Ismāʿīl. > **""Take four fair boys; these, O ʿĀʾishah, are of Ismāʿīl's children purely.""**"
"16570","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, mentioning Banū Tamīm, called them large-headed, firm-footed, helpers of truth at the end, hardest against the Dajjāl. > **""They are large of head, firm of foot, helpers of truth at the end of time, hardest against the Dajjāl.""**"
"16571","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would sometimes strike his shoulder commanding love of Banū Tamīm. > **""Love Banū Tamīm.""**"
"16572","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reports riding with the Prophet ﷺ past a herd asked about and attributed to Banū al-ʿAnbar, whom the Prophet ﷺ claimed as his people. > **""Those are our people.""**"
"16573","ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid reports a man disparaging Banū Tamīm before him, prompting him to seize pebbles, then citing a Companion that Tamīm mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ drew charges of lateness answered by pointing to Muzaynah's equal pace, then their red and black camels of charity hailed as his people's camels, and finally forbidding aught but good for them as longest-speared against the Dajjāl. > **""Say nothing of Banū Tamīm but good, for they are the longest-speared of men against the Dajjāl.""**"
"16574","Sabrah ibn Maʿbad رضي الله عنه, Companion of the Messenger, reports notables gathered with Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه praising their peoples until a Juhanī youth faltered, when ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه urged full speech, testifying he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare Juhaynah of him and he of them, angered and pleased together, anger at them anger at Allah; Muʿāwiyah claimed the report concerned Quraysh, answered in verse affirming hearing against ʿUyaynah's dispute, unlike Dhakwān, Riʿl, and two former clans. > **""Juhaynah is of me and I am of them; they anger at my anger and are pleased at my pleasure.""**"
"16575","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reports that when the Bajīlah delegation came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered recording them beginning with al-Aḥmusīs, while a Qaysī lingered to watch, then supplicated for them five times for blessing or prayer, doubted by Mukhāriq. > **""Record the Bajalīs and begin with the Aḥmusīs; O Allah, bless them.""**"
"16576","In another wording the delegations of Aḥmus and Qays came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who ordered beginning with the Aḥmusīs before the Qaysīs, then supplicated seven times for blessing upon Aḥmus, its horses, and its men. > **""Begin with the Aḥmusīs before the Qaysīs; O Allah, bless Aḥmus, its horses, and its men.""**"
"16577","Khālid ibn ʿUrfuṭah رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raising his hands in supplication for the horses of Aḥmus and its men. > **""O Allah, bless the horses of Aḥmus and its men.""**"
"16578","Ghālib ibn Abjar reports Qays mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who invoked mercy upon Qays despite surprise, noting its adherence to the religion of their father Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm Khalīl Allāh, mutual greeting between Qays and Yemen, Qays as Allah's horsemen on earth, its sole support of religion at a coming time, marked horsemen of heaven and distinguished horsemen of earth, an egg split from the Household, and Allah's lion on earth. > **""May Allah have mercy on Qays; Qays are Allah's horsemen on earth.""**"
"16579","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the best of Easterners as ʿAbd al-Qays. > **""The best of Easterners is ʿAbd al-Qays.""**"
"16580","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ the same words naming the best of Easterners as ʿAbd al-Qays. > **""The best of Easterners is ʿAbd al-Qays.""**"
"16581","Nūḥ ibn Mukhallad reports coming to the Prophet ﷺ in Makkah and being asked his origin, answering Ḍubayʿah of Rabīʿah, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the best of Rabīʿah as ʿAbd al-Qays, then the clan he was from, and traded with him in goods to Yemen. > **""The best of Rabīʿah is ʿAbd al-Qays, then the clan you are from.""**"
"16582","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared himself the disputant against whoever wrongs ʿAbd al-Qays. > **""I am the disputant against whoever wrongs ʿAbd al-Qays.""**"
"16583","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised Azd as excellent people with sweet mouths, righteous oaths, and pure hearts. > **""Excellent people are Azd, sweet in mouths, righteous in oaths, pure in hearts.""**"
"16584","Ṭalḥah ibn Dāwūd reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praised the excellent nurslings, the people of Oman, meaning Azd. > **""Excellent nurslings are the people of Oman.""**"
"16585","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to an Arab clan inviting them to Islam, who rejected the letter and reported back; he ﷺ said had it been sent to Azd Shanūʾah on the Oman coast and Aslam they would have accepted; he then sent to al-Julandā inviting him, who accepted Islam and sent a gift arriving after the Prophet ﷺ had passed, which Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه treated as inheritance dividing between Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها and al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه. > **""Had I sent it to a people on the Oman coast of Azd Shanūʾah and Aslam, they would have accepted it.""**"
"16586","Bishr ibn ʿIṣmah, Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring Azd of him and he of them, angered and pleased together; when Muʿāwiyah claimed the words concerned Quraysh, Bishr denied lying upon the Messenger ﷺ, saying had he lied he would have assigned it to his own people. > **""Azd is of me and I am of them; I anger when they anger and am pleased when they are pleased.""**"
"16587","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told Banū Nājiyah that he is of them and they of him. > **""I am of them and they are of me.""**"
"16588","Shuʿbah reports asking Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm about Banū Nājiyah, who called them of them, citing a report through Saʿīd ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه from the Prophet ﷺ that they are a clan of him and he of them. > **""They are a clan of me, and I am of them.""**"
"16589","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports four hundred of Daws coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who welcomed them as best-faced, sweetest-mouthed, and most trustworthy. > **""Welcome, best of men in faces, sweetest in mouths, and greatest in trust.""**"
"16590","Salamah ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reports arriving as a delegation with household and sons, seeking permission and entering, identified as the delegation of ʿAnazah, praised as an excellent clan wronged yet aided, welcomed as the people of Shuʿayb and the two sisters linked with Mūsā; asked his need, he asked the obligations on camels, sheep, and goats, was informed, sat awhile, sought leave, and was bidden farewell with supplication for sufficient provision without lack or excess. > **""Excellent clan is ʿAnazah, wronged yet aided; O Allah, provide ʿAnazah sufficiency without lack or excess.""**"
"16591","Ḥanẓalah ibn Nuʿaym reports ʿUmar ibn ʿIṣām asking what the Commander of the Faithful ʿUmar رضي الله عنه told him concerning ʿAnazah; he answered that ʿUmar, upon hearing his lineage, recalled the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioning his people and pointing eastward as a clan wronged yet aided. > **""A clan from here, wronged yet aided.""**"
"16592","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Prophet ﷺ at al-Abṭaḥ in his red tent, asked their identity and answering Banū ʿĀmir, welcomed as of him ﷺ. > **""Welcome, you are of me.""**"
"16593","In another wording the welcome is addressed to them directly. > **""Welcome to you.""**"
"16594","In another wording he ﷺ adds his belonging to them. > **""And I am of you.""**"
"16595","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports witnessing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicate for this clan of Nakhaʿ, or praise them, until he wished himself one of them. > **""O Allah, bless this clan of Nakhaʿ.""**"
"16596","Yazīd ibn Maʿbad reports delegating to the Prophet ﷺ, asked in whose hands lies justice among Yamāmah's people; intending Banū ʿAbd al-Duʾal but fearing to lie, he named Banū ʿUbayd, confirmed truthful as a land firm on hardship never perishing, explained by working with hands and eating with slaves. > **""You spoke truly; they work with their hands and eat with their slaves.""**"
"16597","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed justice in Muḍar when men differ. > **""When men differ, justice lies in Muḍar.""**"
"16598","Abū Labīd reports a Ṭāḥiyah migrant Bayraḥ ibn Asad reaching Madīnah days after the Messenger's ﷺ passing, recognised by ʿUmar رضي الله عنه as a stranger of Oman, taken to Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه as of the land the Messenger ﷺ knew as Oman washed by the sea, whose Arabs would not shoot his messenger with arrow or stone. > **""I know a land called Oman, washed at its side by the sea, with an Arab clan whom, had my messenger come to them, they would not shoot with arrow or stone.""**"
"16599","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoining him twice to goodness toward the Arabs. > **""O ʿAlī, I enjoin goodness toward the Arabs; I enjoin goodness toward the Arabs.""**"
"16600","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded love of the Arabs for three: his Arabness, the Arabic Qurʾān, and the Arabic speech of Paradise's people. > **""Love the Arabs for three: I am Arab, the Qurʾān is Arabic, and the speech of Paradise's people is Arabic.""**"
"16601","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for the Arabs that whoever meets Allah affirming Him be forgiven life's days, the supplication of Ibrāhīm and Ismāʿīl عليهما السلام, with the banner of praise in his hand on the Day of Resurrection and the Arabs nearest to his banner. > **""O Allah, whoever of them meets Thee affirming Thee, forgive him the days of his life.""**"
"16602","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared himself Arab, the Qurʾān Arabic, and the tongue of Paradise's people Arabic. > **""I am Arab, the Qurʾān is Arabic, and the tongue of Paradise's people is Arabic.""**"
"16603","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore that Allah never sent down direct revelation to a prophet but in Arabic, after which he conveys it to his people in his tongue. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, Allah never sent down revelation to a prophet between Him and him but in Arabic, then he conveys it to his people in his tongue.""**"
"16604","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made love of Quraysh faith and hatred disbelief, and love of the Arabs faith and hatred disbelief, tying love and hatred of the Arabs to love and hatred of himself. > **""Love of Quraysh is faith and hatred of them disbelief, and love of the Arabs is faith and hatred of them disbelief.""**"
"16605","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared none hates the Arabs but a hypocrite. > **""None hates the Arabs but a hypocrite.""**"
"16606","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare no believer hates the Arabs, nor does a believer love Thaqīf. > **""No believer hates the Arabs, and no believer loves Thaqīf.""**"
"16607","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ tied the humiliation of Islam to the humiliation of the Arabs. > **""When the Arabs are humiliated, Islam is humiliated.""**"
"16608","Jarīr reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Iblīs despaired of being worshipped in the land of the Arabs. > **""Iblīs has despaired of being worshipped in the land of the Arabs.""**"
"16609","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه and ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه report the Messenger of Allah ﷺ telling them Satan despaired of being worshipped in the Arabian Peninsula. > **""Satan has despaired of being worshipped in the Arabian Peninsula.""**"
"16610","Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred ten houses in al-Ḥijāz in endurance over twenty houses in al-Shām. > **""Ten houses in al-Ḥijāz outlast twenty houses in al-Shām.""**"
"16611","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed hardness of hearts and harshness in Easterners, faith as Yemeni, and tranquillity in the people of al-Ḥijāz. > **""Hardness of hearts and harshness lie in Easterners, faith is Yemeni, and tranquillity lies in the people of al-Ḥijāz.""**"
"16612","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAbbād ibn Jaʿfar reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ name the first of his Ummah he shall intercede for on the Day of Resurrection as the people of Madīnah, Makkah, and al-Ṭāʾif. > **""The first of my Ummah I shall intercede for on the Day of Resurrection are the people of Madīnah, the people of Makkah, and the people of al-Ṭāʾif.""**"
"16613","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Satan has despaired of ever being worshipped in the Arabian Peninsula, yet he remains content with lesser, belittled sins. > **""Satan has despaired of being worshipped in the Arabian Peninsula, but he has become content with belittled deeds.""**"
"16614","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Satan has despaired of being worshipped in this land of yours, but he is content with belittled deeds among you. > **""Satan has despaired of being worshipped in this land of yours, but he has become content with belittled deeds among you.""**"
"16615","Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah has cleared this peninsula of shirk, unless the stars lead them astray. > **""Allah has indeed cleared this peninsula of shirk, unless the stars lead them astray.""**"
"16616","Khawlah bint Ḥakīm رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out embracing his daughter's two sons, saying that by Allah, children make one cowardly and miserly, yet they are among the fragrance of Allah, and that the last treading Allah will tread is at Wajj. Sufyān noted the Prophet's ﷺ last campaign was Ṭāʾif, citing the poet's verse about treading down like one staggering. > **""By Allah, they make one cowardly and miserly, yet they are among the fragrance of Allah, and the last treading Allah will tread is at Wajj.""**"
"16617","Yaʿlā ibn Murrah رضي الله عنه reported that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn رضي الله عنهما raced to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who embraced them and said that children cause miserliness and cowardice, and that the last treading Allah will tread is at Wajj. > **""Children cause miserliness and cowardice, and the last treading Allah will tread is at Wajj.""**"
"16618","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head to the sky and said the people of Yemen have come to you like fragments of cloud, the best of the people of the earth. When a man present asked, ""And us, O Messenger of Allah?"" he said in a low voice that they were excepted. > **""The people of Yemen have come to you like fragments of cloud, the best of the people of the earth.""** > **""Except you.""**"
"16619","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه reported by way of another narration that while with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the road to Makkah, he ﷺ said the people of Yemen would come upon them like clouds, the choicest of the people of the earth. An Anṣārī man asked three times, ""And not us, O Messenger of Allah?"" and he ﷺ remained silent, then said in a faint voice that they were excepted. > **""The people of Yemen will come upon you as though they are clouds; they are the choicest of the people of the earth.""** > **""Except you.""**"
"16620","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said twelve thousand will come forth from ʿAdan aiding Allah and His Messenger ﷺ, the best of those between him and them. Al-Muʿtamir added his thought that it was said ""in the depths,"" while another wording says ""from ʿAdan Abyan."" > **""Twelve thousand will come forth from ʿAdan aiding Allah and His Messenger; they are the best of those between me and them.""**"
"16621","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Yemen, saying perhaps he would pass by his grave and mosque, that he was sent to a tender-hearted people who fight for truth twice, so he should fight alongside whoever obeys him against whoever disobeys, until they return to Islam such that wife outpaces husband, child father, and brother brother in it, and that he should settle between the two clans of al-Sakūn and al-Sakāsik. > **""Perhaps you will pass by my grave and my mosque. I have sent you to a tender-hearted people who fight for truth twice; so fight with whoever obeys you among them against whoever disobeys you, then they will return to Islam until wife outpaces husband, child father, and brother brother. So settle between the two clans of al-Sakūn and al-Sakāsik.""**"
"16622","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in Madīnah he said Allāhu Akbar when Allah's help and victory came and the people of Yemen came, a people pure of heart and excellent in obedience; faith is Yemeni, understanding is Yemeni, and wisdom is Yemeni. > **""Allāhu Akbar! When the help of Allah and victory came, and the people of Yemen came — a people pure of heart, excellent in obedience. Faith is Yemeni, understanding is Yemeni, and wisdom is Yemeni.""**"
"16623","Ḥayyān ibn Bisṭām al-Hudhalī reported that while with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, some mentioned the Yemeni pilgrims and their conduct and one man reviled them, so Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said not to revile the people of Yemen, for he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the adornment of pilgrims is the people of Yemen. > **""The adornment of pilgrims is the people of Yemen.""**"
"16624","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said faith is Yemeni, that they are from him and toward him though their abode is distant, that they will soon come as helpers and supporters, so he commands kindness toward them. > **""Faith is Yemeni; they are from me and toward me, though their abode is distant. They will soon come to you as helpers and supporters, so I command you to be good to them.""**"
"16625","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the people of Yemen are the softest of heart and the most beneficial in obedience, in another wording the most obedient. > **""The people of Yemen are the softest of heart and the most excellent in obedience.""**"
"16626","ʿUrwah ibn Ruwaym reported that Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه came to Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه in Damascus, who asked him for a ḥadīth heard directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with none between, so Anas رضي الله عنه said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say faith is Yemeni, thus, toward Lakhm and Judhām. > **""Faith is Yemeni, thus, toward Lakhm and Judhām.""**"
"16627","Shabīb Abū Rawḥ reported that a Bedouin asked Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه for a ḥadīth from the Prophet ﷺ, so he mentioned the Prophet ﷺ said faith is Yemeni, wisdom is Yemeni, and he finds the breath of your Lord from the direction of Yemen. > **""Faith is Yemeni, wisdom is Yemeni, and I find the breath of your Lord from the direction of Yemen.""**"
"16628","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said faith is Yemeni, while Muḍar are with the tails of camels. > **""Faith is Yemeni, while Muḍar are with the tails of camels.""**"
"16629","Abū Kabshah al-Anmārī رضي الله عنه reported that on one of the campaigns with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ they halted, came to him, and he ﷺ raised his hands and said faith is Yemeni and wisdom is here toward Lakhm and Judhām. > **""Faith is Yemeni, and wisdom is here toward Lakhm and Judhām.""**"
"16630","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said faith is Yemeni, while Muḍar are with the tails of camels. > **""Faith is Yemeni, while Muḍar are with the tails of camels.""**"
"16631","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said faith is Yemeni, among Ḥindis and Judhām. > **""Faith is Yemeni, among Ḥindis and Judhām.""**"
"16632","A man of Khathʿam رضي الله عنه reported that during the Tabūk campaign the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted one night, his Companions gathered, and he ﷺ said Allah had granted him that night the two treasures, of Persia and Rome, and aided him through kings, the red kings of Ḥimyar; there is no king but Allah; they will come taking from Allah's wealth and fighting in Allah's path. He said it three times. > **""Allah has granted me tonight the two treasures: the treasure of Persia and of Rome, and He has aided me through kings, the red kings of Ḥimyar. There is no king but Allah. They will come taking from the wealth of Allah and fighting in the path of Allah.""**"
"16633","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd رضي الله عنه reported that a man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, curse the people of Yemen, for their might is severe, their numbers many, their fortresses strong,"" but he ﷺ said no. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the non-Arabs, Persia and Rome, and said when the Yemenis pass by you driving their women and carrying their sons on their shoulders, then they are from him and he is from them. > **""No.""** > **""When they pass by you driving their women and carrying their sons upon their shoulders, then they are from me and I am from them.""**"
"16634","Abū Thawr al-Fahmī رضي الله عنه reported that while with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a garment of Maʿāfir cloth was brought, Abū Sufyān said may Allah curse the garment and whoever makes it, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said not to curse them, for they are from him and he is from them. > **""Do not curse them, for they are from me and I am from them.""**"
"16635","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out while they sat, they made room, he sat and asked where are his Companions of whom he is and who are of him, with whom he will enter Paradise. They asked him to inform them, and he said yes: the Yemenis, cast aside at the ends of the earth, turned away from the doors of rulers, each dying with his need unfulfilled in his breast. > **""Where are my Companions of whom I am and who are of me, and I shall enter Paradise and they shall enter it with me?""** > **""Yes: the Yemenis, cast aside at the ends of the earth, turned away from the doors of rulers; each of them dies with his need unfulfilled in his breast.""**"
"16636","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ looked toward Iraq, al-Shām, and Yemen and prayed O Allah, turn their hearts toward Your obedience and encompass those behind them. > **""O Allah, turn their hearts toward Your obedience, and encompass those behind them.""**"
"16637","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed O Allah bless for us our Shām and our Yemen. A man said, ""And our east, O Messenger of Allah?"" He repeated the supplication, and when asked again about the east he said from there rises the horn of Satan, with it nine-tenths of evil and the incurable disease. > **""O Allah, bless for us our Shām and our Yemen.""** > **""From there rises the horn of Satan, and with it nine-tenths of evil and the incurable disease.""**"
"16638","Jubayr ibn Nufayr reported that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that Shām will be opened for you, so when given choice of dwellings therein, take the city called Damascus, for it is the stronghold of the Muslims in the great battles, and their encampment therein is at land called al-Ghūṭah. > **""Shām will be opened for you; when you are given choice of dwellings therein, then take the city called Damascus, for it is the stronghold of the Muslims in the great battles, and their encampment therein is at a land called al-Ghūṭah.""**"
"16639","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that while sleeping angels came, carried the pillar of the Book from beneath his pillow toward Shām, and that faith when tribulations fall will be in Shām. > **""While I slept, angels came and carried the pillar of the Book from beneath my pillow and took it to Shām. Verily, faith when tribulations fall will be in Shām.""**"
"16640","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that while sleeping he saw the pillar of the Book taken from beneath his head, he thought it taken away, followed it with his gaze, and it was taken to Shām; verily faith when tribulations fall is in Shām. > **""While I slept I saw the pillar of the Book taken from beneath my head; I thought it taken away, so I followed it with my gaze, and it was taken to Shām. Verily, faith when tribulations fall is in Shām.""**"
"16641","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that while sleeping he saw the pillar of the Book taken from beneath his head, followed it with his gaze, and it was directed to Shām; verily when tribulations come faith is in Shām. He said it three times. > **""While I slept I saw the pillar of the Book taken from beneath my head, so I followed it with my gaze, and it was directed to Shām. Verily, when tribulations come, faith is in Shām.""**"
"16642","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported in another narration that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when tribulations fall, safety is in Shām. > **""When tribulations fall, safety is in Shām.""**"
"16643","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he saw the pillar of the Book removed from beneath his pillow, followed it with his gaze, and it was a radiant light until he thought it taken away, directed to Shām; and he interpreted that when tribulations fall, faith is in Shām. > **""I saw the pillar of the Book removed from beneath my pillow, so I followed it with my gaze, and it was a radiant light until I thought it taken away, and it was directed to Shām. And I interpreted that when tribulations fall, faith is in Shām.""**"
"16644","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥawālah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that on the night of his ascension he saw a white pillar like a pearl carried by angels; he asked what they carried and they said the pillar of the Book, ordered to place it in Shām. And while sleeping he saw the pillar taken from beneath his pillow, thought Allah had forsaken the people of earth, followed it with his gaze as a radiant light before him until placed in Shām. Ibn Ḥawālah asked him to choose for him, and he said upon you is Shām. > **""I saw on the night I was taken a white pillar as though pearl, carried by angels. I said: What are you carrying? They said: The pillar of the Book; we were commanded to place it in Shām.""** > **""Upon you is Shām.""**"
"16645","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that you will be formed into armies: an army in Shām, in Egypt, in Iraq, and in Yemen. They asked him to choose for them, and he said upon you is Shām. They said they were herders unable for Shām, so he said whoever cannot take Shām should join his Yemen and drink from its pools, for Allah has guaranteed him Shām. > **""You will be formed into armies: an army in Shām, in Egypt, in Iraq, and in Yemen.""** > **""Upon you is Shām; and whoever cannot take Shām should join his Yemen, for Allah has guaranteed me Shām.""**"
"16646","ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that there will be an army in Shām and an army in Yemen. A man stood asking him to choose for him, and he said upon you is Shām, for Allah has guaranteed him Shām and its people. > **""There will be an army in Shām and an army in Yemen.""** > **""Upon you is Shām, for Allah has guaranteed me Shām and its people.""**"
"16647","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥawālah al-Azdī رضي الله عنه reported that he said, ""O Messenger of Allah, choose for me a land to dwell in; were I to know you would remain I would choose nothing over nearness to you."" He ﷺ said upon you is Shām. When he saw his dislike for Shām, he said do you know what Allah says of Shām? Allah says: O Shām, you are My choicest land, in you I settle My choicest servants; Allah has guaranteed him Shām and its people. > **""Upon you is Shām. Do you know what Allah says of Shām? Allah says: O Shām, you are My choicest land; in you I settle My choicest servants. Allah has guaranteed me Shām and its people.""**"
"16648","Al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he stood among the people one day and said O people, you will soon be formed armies: an army in Shām, in Iraq, in Yemen. Ibn Ḥawālah said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if I reach that time then choose for me,"" and he said he chooses Shām for him, for it is the choicest of Muslims and Allah's choicest land, to which He gathers His choicest creation; whoever refuses should join his Yemen and drink from its pools, for Allah has guaranteed him Shām and its people. > **""O people, you will soon be formed armies: an army in Shām, an army in Iraq, and an army in Yemen.""** > **""I choose Shām for you, for it is the choicest of the Muslims and Allah's choicest land; He gathers to it His choicest creation. Whoever refuses should join his Yemen and drink from its pools, for Allah has guaranteed me Shām and its people.""**"
"16649","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said people will be formed into armies: in Yemen, Shām, the East, the West. A man said, ""Welcome, O Messenger of Allah, choose for me, for I am a young man; perhaps I shall reach that — which do you command?"" He said upon you is Shām. > **""People will be formed into armies: an army in Yemen, an army in Shām, an army in the East, and an army in the West.""** > **""Upon you is Shām.""**"
"16650","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speaking to Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنه and Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه while they consulted him about residence, and he pointed to Shām. They asked again and he pointed to Shām a second and third time, saying upon you two is Shām, for it is Allah's choicest land wherein dwell His choicest creation; whoever refuses should join his Yemen and drink from its pools, for Allah has guaranteed him Shām and its people. > **""Upon you two is Shām, for it is Allah's choicest land; His choicest creation dwell therein. Whoever refuses should join his Yemen and drink from its pools, for Allah has guaranteed me Shām and its people.""**"
"16651","Abū Ṭalḥah, named Dharʿ, رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there will be four armies, so upon you is Shām, for Allah has guaranteed him Shām and its people. > **""There will be four armies, so upon you is Shām, for Allah has guaranteed me Shām and its people.""**"
"16652","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah's choicest earth is Shām, wherein are His choicest creation and servants, and a company of his Ummah will surely enter Paradise with neither reckoning nor punishment. > **""Allah's choicest earth is Shām, and therein are His choicest creation and servants. And a company of my Ummah will surely enter Paradise with neither reckoning nor punishment.""**"
"16653","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Shām is Allah's choicest land, to which He gathers His choicest servants; whoever leaves Shām for elsewhere does so by His displeasure, and whoever enters it from elsewhere does so by mercy. > **""Shām is Allah's choicest land; to it He gathers His choicest servants. Whoever leaves Shām for elsewhere does so by displeasure, and whoever enters it from elsewhere does so by mercy.""**"
"16654","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said you will be formed into armies. A man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, choose for me,"" and he said upon you is Shām, for it is Allah's choicest land wherein are His choicest servants; whoever turns away should join his Yemen and drink from its pools, in another wording join his Najd; for Allah has guaranteed him Shām and its people. > **""You will be formed into armies. Upon you is Shām, for it is Allah's choicest land; therein are His choicest servants. Whoever turns away should join his Yemen, for Allah has guaranteed me Shām and its people.""**"
"16655","Khuraym ibn Fātik al-Asadī رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the people of Shām are Allah's whip on earth, through whom He exacts retribution from whomever He wills of His servants; it is forbidden for their hypocrites to prevail over their believers, and they die only in grief and sorrow. > **""The people of Shām are Allah's whip on earth; He exacts retribution through them from whomever He wills of His servants. It is forbidden for their hypocrites to prevail over their believers, and they die only in grief and sorrow.""**"
"16656","Salamah ibn Nufayl رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the core of the abode of Islam is in Shām. > **""The core of the abode of Islam is in Shām.""**"
"16657","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said while they were with him, ""Ṭūbā for Shām."" They asked what for, O Messenger of Allah, and he said the Most Merciful spreads His mercy over it, in another wording the angels of the Most Merciful spread their wings over Shām. > **""Ṭūbā for Shām.""** > **""The Most Merciful spreads His mercy over it.""**"
"16658","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah faced him toward Shām and placed Yemen behind his back, saying O Muḥammad, what is before you I have made spoils and provision, and what is behind your back support; Islam will ever increase and shirk decrease until two women travel fearing no injustice. Then he swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul that days and nights will not pass until this religion reaches as far as this star. > **""Allah faced me toward Shām and placed Yemen behind my back, and said to me: O Muḥammad, I have made what is before you spoils and provision, and what is behind your back support. Islam will ever increase and shirk decrease until two women travel fearing no injustice. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, days and nights will not pass until this religion reaches as far as this star.""**"
"16659","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said Iblīs entered Iraq and fulfilled his need therein, then entered Shām and they drove him out, in another wording drove him until he reached Baysān, then entered Egypt where he laid eggs and hatched and spread his brood. > **""Iblīs entered Iraq and fulfilled his need therein, then entered Shām and they drove him out, then entered Egypt where he laid eggs and hatched and spread his brood.""**"
"16660","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḍirār ibn ʿAmr al-Asadī reported from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, that Allah divided good into ten parts, placing nine in Shām and the rest in the rest of the earth; and He divided evil into ten parts, placing one part in Shām and the rest in the rest of the earth. > **""Allah divided good into ten parts: He placed nine parts in Shām and the remainder in the rest of the earth. And He divided evil into ten parts: He placed one part thereof in Shām and the remainder in the rest of the earth.""**"
"16661","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the people of Shām, their spouses, offspring, slaves and maidservants to the edge of the peninsula are frontier guards; whoever settles in a city of Shām is in ribāṭ, and whoever stands at a frontier post thereof is a mujāhid. > **""The people of Shām, their spouses, their offspring, their slaves and their maidservants, to the edge of the peninsula, are frontier guards. Whoever settles in a city of Shām is in ribāṭ, and whoever stands at a frontier post thereof is a mujāhid.""**"
"16662","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that a party of his Ummah will ever fight at the gates of Damascus and around it, and at the gates of Bayt al-Maqdis and around it; the forsaking of whoever forsakes them will not harm them, manifest upon truth until the Hour comes. > **""A party of my Ummah will ever fight at the gates of Damascus and around it, and at the gates of Bayt al-Maqdis and around it. The forsaking of whoever forsakes them will not harm them, manifest upon truth until the Hour comes.""**"
"16663","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say a fire will emerge from the sea of Ḥaḍramawt — or from Ḥaḍramawt — driving people. They asked what he commanded them, and he said upon you is Shām. > **""A fire will emerge from the sea of Ḥaḍramawt — or from Ḥaḍramawt — driving people.""** > **""Upon you is Shām.""**"
"16664","Ḥamrah ibn ʿAbd Kalāl reported that ʿUmar رضي الله عنه marched to Shām after his first march there until near it, when word reached him and those with him that plague raged therein, so his Companions said return and do not plunge into it; had he descended while it raged they would not see him leave it. So he turned back to Madīnah, halted that night, and Ḥamrah, nearest to him, rose with him and heard him say: they turned me back from Shām after I neared it because of plague — yet my turning from it will not delay my term, nor would my going forward hasten it. Were he to finish necessary affairs in Madīnah he would march until settling in Shām, then enter Ḥimṣ, for he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say Allah will raise from it on Resurrection Day seventy thousand with neither reckoning nor punishment, their rising between the olives and its wall in the red soil. > **""Allah will surely raise from it on the Day of Resurrection seventy thousand with neither reckoning nor punishment upon them; their rising is between the olives and its wall in the red soil.""**"
"16665","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said ʿAsqalān is one of the two brides; seventy thousand with no reckoning will be raised from it on Resurrection Day, and fifty thousand martyrs will be raised from it as delegations to Allah, with rows of martyrs holding their severed heads in their hands, their jugulars gushing blood, saying Our Lord, grant us what You promised us through Your messengers; indeed You never break the promise. So He says My servants have spoken truth; wash them in the river of whiteness, and they emerge pure and white, roaming Paradise wherever they will. > **""ʿAsqalān is one of the two brides. Seventy thousand with no reckoning upon them will be raised from it on the Day of Resurrection, and fifty thousand martyrs will be raised from it as delegations to Allah. Therein are rows of martyrs, their severed heads in their hands, their jugulars gushing blood, saying: Our Lord, grant us what You promised us through Your messengers; indeed You never break the promise.""**"
"16666","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention the people of a graveyard one day, pray over them abundantly, and when asked about them he said they are the martyred people of the graveyard of ʿAsqalān, escorted to Paradise as a bride is escorted to her husband. > **""They are the martyred people of the graveyard of ʿAsqalān; they are escorted to Paradise as a bride is escorted to her husband.""**"
"16667","ʿAbdullāh ibn Mālik ibn Buḥaynah رضي الله عنه reported that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat among his Companions he said thrice, may Allah bless that graveyard, without naming it. A Companion then visited one of the Prophet's ﷺ wives — ʿAṭṭāf was told it was ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها — saying the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned a graveyard and prayed over them without telling which. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited her she asked him, and he said they are the people of a graveyard at ʿAsqalān; in another wording he sought forgiveness and prayed over the people of a graveyard at ʿAsqalān. > **""May Allah bless that graveyard.""** > **""They are the people of a graveyard at ʿAsqalān.""**"
"16668","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ saying he desired to fight in Allah's path, and he ﷺ said upon you is Shām, for Allah has guaranteed him Shām and its people; and keep in Shām to ʿAsqalān, for when the mill turns upon his Ummah — in another wording when the mill of his Ummah turns — its people will be in ease and well-being. > **""Upon you is Shām, for Allah has guaranteed me Shām and its people. And keep in Shām to ʿAsqalān, for when the mill turns upon my Ummah its people will be in ease and well-being.""**"
"16669","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه reported that while sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ they mentioned Shām and the Romans therein, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said you will overcome Shām and seize upon its sea a fortress called Anafah, from which seventy thousand martyrs will be raised on Resurrection Day. > **""You will overcome Shām and seize upon its sea a fortress called Anafah, from which seventy thousand martyrs will be raised on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16670","Shuraḥbīl al-Juʿfī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever finds livelihood difficult should take to ʿUmān. > **""Whoever finds livelihood difficult should take to ʿUmān.""**"
"16671","Shurayḥ ibn ʿUbayd reported that the people of Shām were mentioned before ʿAlī رضي الله عنه while he was in Iraq, and they said curse them, O Commander of the Faithful. He refused, saying he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the Substitutes are in Shām, forty men, each time one dies Allah replaces him with another; through them rain is sought, through them victory over enemies is granted, and through them punishment is turned away from the people of Shām. > **""The Substitutes are in Shām, forty men; each time a man dies Allah replaces him with another. Through them rain is sought, through them victory over enemies is granted, and through them punishment is turned away from the people of Shām.""**"
"16672","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the Substitutes in this Ummah are thirty, like Khalīl al-Raḥmān; each time a man dies Allah replaces him with another. > **""The Substitutes in this Ummah are thirty, like Khalīl al-Raḥmān; each time a man dies Allah replaces him with another.""**"
"16673","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said thirty in his Ummah will ever remain, through whom the earth stands, through whom you are rained upon, through whom you are aided. Qatādah said he hopes al-Ḥasan is among them. > **""There will ever remain thirty in my Ummah; through them the earth stands, through them you are rained upon, and through them you are aided.""**"
"16674","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the earth will never be empty of forty men like Khalīl al-Raḥmān; through them you are given drink, through them you are aided; whenever one dies Allah replaces him with another. Saʿīd said he heard Qatādah say they do not doubt al-Ḥasan is among them. > **""The earth will never be empty of forty men like Khalīl al-Raḥmān; through them you are given drink and through them you are aided. Whenever one of them dies Allah replaces him with another.""**"
"16675","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said forty men of his Ummah will ever remain, their hearts upon the heart of Ibrāhīm; Allah repels harm from the people of earth through them; they are called the Substitutes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said they did not attain it by prayer, fasting, or charity. They asked how then, and he said by generosity and sincere counsel to the Muslims. > **""There will ever remain forty men of my Ummah, their hearts upon the heart of Ibrāhīm; Allah repels harm from the people of earth through them; they are called the Substitutes. They did not attain it by prayer, nor fasting, nor charity.""** > **""By generosity and sincere counsel to the Muslims.""**"
"16676","Shahr ibn Ḥawshab reported that when Egypt was conquered and the people reviled the people of Shām, ʿAwf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه thrust his head from his hooded cloak and said O people of Egypt, do not revile the people of Shām, for he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say among them are the Substitutes; through them you are aided and through them you are provided. > **""Among them are the Substitutes; through them you are aided and through them you are provided.""**"
"16677","Suwayd al-Alhānī رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — or one who heard him narrated — say Allah made this clan of Lakhm and Judhām a support in Shām in mounts and udders, as He made Yūsuf a support for the people of Egypt. > **""Allah has made this clan of Lakhm and Judhām a support in Shām in mounts and udders, as He made Yūsuf a support for its people in Egypt.""**"
"16678","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counseled at his death, saying fear Allah, fear Allah regarding the Copts of Egypt, for you will prevail over them and they will be reinforcement and helpers for you in Allah's path. > **""Fear Allah, fear Allah regarding the Copts of Egypt, for you will prevail over them and they will be reinforcement and helpers for you in the path of Allah.""**"
"16679","Kaʿb ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say when Egypt is opened, treat the Copts well, for they have blood-ties and kinship. > **""When Egypt is opened, treat the Copts well, for they have blood-ties and kinship.""**"
"16680","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported in another narration that the Prophet ﷺ said they have a covenant of protection, meaning that the mother of Ismāʿīl was from them. > **""They have a covenant of protection.""**"
"16681","Abū Hāniʾ Ḥumayd ibn Hāniʾ reported that he heard Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥubulī — ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd — and ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth and others say that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said you will come upon a curly-haired people, so treat them well, for they are strength for you and conveyance to your enemy by Allah's permission — meaning the Copts of Egypt. > **""You will come upon a curly-haired people, so treat them well, for they are strength for you and conveyance to your enemy by Allah's permission.""**"
"16682","Rabāḥ al-Lakhmī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said Egypt will be opened, so seek its good but do not take it as a home, for the shortest-lived people will be driven to it. > **""Egypt will be opened, so seek its good but do not take it as a home, for the shortest-lived people will be driven to it.""**"
"16683","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that after him there will be many expeditions, so be in the expedition of Khurāsān, then settle in the city of Marw, for Dhū al-Qarnayn built it and supplicated blessing for its people, and no evil will harm its people. > **""After me there will be many expeditions, so be in the expedition of Khurāsān, then settle in the city of Marw, for Dhū al-Qarnayn built it and supplicated blessing for its people, and no evil will harm its people.""**"
"16684","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said there are no tents after the tents that were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Badr, through which defense was made, that repel from their people what they repel; none intends them with evil except Allah brings upon him what distracts him from them."
"16685","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said in another narration: O company of Arabs, today you commit matters that in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were outright hypocrisy — meaning the people of these tents, meaning Kūfah. > **""O company of Arabs, today you commit matters that in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were outright hypocrisy.""**"
"16686","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said were knowledge at al-Thurayyā, men of the sons of Persia would reach it. > **""Were knowledge at al-Thurayyā, men of the sons of Persia would reach it.""**"
"16687","Qays ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said were faith suspended at al-Thurayyā, men of the sons of Persia would attain it. > **""Were faith suspended at al-Thurayyā, men of the sons of Persia would attain it.""**"
"16688","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that were religion suspended at al-Thurayyā, men of the sons of Persia would reach it. > **""Were religion suspended at al-Thurayyā, men of the sons of Persia would reach it.""**"
"16689","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported that he sat with the Prophet ﷺ, who asked them to inform him of the best of believers in faith. They said the angels; he ﷺ said they are so, and rightly, with the station Allah granted them — rather others. They said the prophets honoured with mission and prophethood; he ﷺ said they are so and deserve it. They said the martyrs martyred alongside the prophets; he ﷺ said they are so and deserve it, honoured with martyrdom — rather others. They asked who then, and he ﷺ said peoples in the loins of men coming after him, believing in him without seeing him, affirming him without seeing him, finding suspended scripture and acting upon it; they are the best of believers in faith. > **""Inform me of the best of believers in faith?""** > **""Peoples in the loins of men, coming after me, believing in me without seeing me, affirming me without seeing me, finding suspended scripture and acting upon what is in it; they are the best of believers in faith.""**"
"16690","ʿUmar رضي الله عنه reported through al-Bazzār that the Prophet ﷺ said to inform him of the greatest of creation with Allah in rank on Resurrection Day. They said the angels; he said what prevents them with their nearness to their Lord — rather others. They said the prophets; he said what prevents them while revelation descends upon them — rather others. They said then inform us, O Messenger of Allah, and he ﷺ said peoples coming after them, believing in him without seeing him, finding suspended scripture and believing in it; they are the greatest of creation with Allah in rank — or the greatest in faith — on Resurrection Day. > **""Inform me of the greatest of creation with Allah in rank on the Day of Resurrection?""** > **""Peoples coming after you, believing in me without seeing me, finding suspended scripture and believing in it; they are the greatest of creation with Allah in rank on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"16691","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked which creation is most wondrous in faith. They said the angels; he said how should angels not believe. They said the prophets; he said revelation is sent to them, how should they not believe. They said the Companions; he said the Companions are with the prophets, how should they not believe. Rather the most wondrous of people in faith are peoples coming after them, finding a Book of revelation, believing in it and following it; they are the most wondrous of people — or of creation — in faith. > **""Which creation is most wondrous in faith?""** > **""Rather the most wondrous of people in faith are peoples coming after you, finding a Book of revelation, believing in it and following it; they are the most wondrous of people in faith.""**"
"16692","Ṣāliḥ ibn Jubayr reported that Abū Jumʿah al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, came to Bayt al-Maqdis to pray therein, with Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah present that day. When he departed they accompanied him to farewell him, and as they turned back he said they had a due upon him to narrate a ḥadīth heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said relate it, may Allah have mercy on you. He said they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه as tenth of ten, and they asked whether any people were greater in reward than them, having believed and followed him. He ﷺ asked what prevented them while the Messenger stood among them and revelation came from heaven; rather peoples coming after them, to whom a Book between two boards comes, believing in it and acting upon it — they are greater in reward, thrice repeated. > **""What prevents you from that while the Messenger of Allah stands among you and revelation comes to you from heaven? Rather peoples coming after you, to whom a Book between two boards comes, believing in it and acting upon what is in it; they are greater than you in reward, they are greater than you in reward, they are greater than you in reward.""**"
"16693","Abū Jumʿah رضي الله عنه reported that they breakfasted with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ رضي الله عنه present, who asked whether any were better than them, having accepted Islam and fought alongside him. He ﷺ said yes: peoples coming after him, believing in him without seeing him. > **""Yes: peoples coming after me, believing in me without seeing me.""**"
"16694","A man of Banū Asad reported that Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the most intense of his Ummah in love for him are peoples coming — or emerging — after him, each wishing he had given his family and wealth only to see him. > **""The most intense of my Ummah in love for me are peoples coming after me; each of them wishes he had given his family and his wealth only to see me.""**"
"16695","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said peoples coming after him, each wishing to ransom seeing him with his family and wealth. > **""Peoples coming after me, each of them wishing to ransom seeing me with his family and his wealth.""**"
"16696","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه said: By Allah, you love the Messenger of Allah ﷺ more intensely than those who saw him — or than most of those who saw him."
"16697","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he wished he had met his brothers. The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ asked whether they were not his brothers, and he said they are his Companions, but his brothers are those believing in him without seeing him. In Abū Yaʿlā's wording he asked when he shall meet his brothers, they asked whether they were not his brothers, and he said rather they are his Companions, and his brothers are those believing in him without seeing him. > **""I wished I had met my brothers.""** > **""You are my Companions, but my brothers are those who believed in me without seeing me.""**"
"16698","Anas رضي الله عنه reported by Abū Yaʿlā's chain that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said ṭūbā for whoever saw him and believed, and ṭūbā seven times for whoever believed without seeing him. > **""Ṭūbā for whoever saw me and believed in me, and ṭūbā seven times for whoever believed in me without seeing me.""**"
"16699","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that a man said to him, ṭūbā for whoever saw him and believed. He ﷺ said ṭūbā for whoever saw him and believed, then ṭūbā, then ṭūbā, then ṭūbā for whoever believed without seeing him. The man asked what ṭūbā is, and he said a tree in Paradise of a hundred years' journey, the garments of Paradise's people emerging from its calyxes. > **""Ṭūbā for whoever saw me and believed in me, then ṭūbā, then ṭūbā, then ṭūbā for whoever believed in me without seeing me.""** > **""A tree in Paradise of a hundred years' journey; the garments of the people of Paradise emerge from its calyxes.""**"
"16700","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said ṭūbā for whoever saw him and believed, and ṭūbā seven times for whoever believed without seeing him. > **""Ṭūbā for whoever saw me and believed in me, and ṭūbā seven times for whoever believed in me without seeing me.""**"
"16701","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reported that while with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two riders appeared; seeing them he said two Kindīs, two Madhḥijīs, until they came and proved men of Madhḥij. One approached to pledge, took his hand, and asked what is for whoever saw him, believed, affirmed and followed him; he said ṭūbā for him, and he wiped his hand and departed. Then the other came to pledge, asking what is for whoever believed, affirmed and followed him without seeing him; he said ṭūbā for him, then ṭūbā for him, then ṭūbā for him, and he wiped his hand and departed. > **""Two Kindīs, two Madhḥijīs.""** > **""Ṭūbā for him, then ṭūbā for him, then ṭūbā for him.""**"
"16702","Abū ʿAmrah رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about whoever believed without seeing him and affirmed him without seeing him, and he ﷺ said ṭūbā for them, then ṭūbā for them; they are from us, they are with us. > **""Ṭūbā for them, then ṭūbā for them; they are from us, they are with us.""**"
"16703","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said ṭūbā for whoever reached him, believed and affirmed him, and ṭūbā for whoever did not reach him yet believed and affirmed him, and ṭūbā for whoever did not reach him yet believed and affirmed him. > **""Ṭūbā for whoever reached me and believed in me and affirmed me, and ṭūbā for whoever did not reach me yet believed in me and affirmed me.""**"
"16704","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that he heard Abū al-Qāsim, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, say that Allah says: O ʿĪsā, I shall send after you an Ummah; if what they love befalls them they praise and thank, and if what they hate befalls them they hope reward and persevere, without forbearance nor knowledge. He said: O Lord, how is this for them without forbearance nor knowledge? He said: I grant them of My forbearance and My knowledge. > **""O ʿĪsā, I shall send after you an Ummah; if what they love befalls them they praise and thank, and if what they hate befalls them they hope reward and persevere, without forbearance nor knowledge. I grant them of My forbearance and My knowledge.""**"
"16705","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he is their portion of the prophets and they are his portion of the nations. > **""I am your portion of the prophets, and you are my portion of the nations.""**"
"16706","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the parable of his Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better. > **""The parable of my Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better.""**"
"16707","ʿAmmār رضي الله عنه also reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the parable of his Ummah is like rain; Allah places good in its first and good in its last. > **""The parable of my Ummah is like rain; Allah places good in its first and good in its last.""**"
"16708","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the parable of his Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better. > **""The parable of my Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better.""**"
"16709","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the parable of his Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better. > **""The parable of my Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better.""**"
"16710","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the parable of his Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better. > **""The parable of my Ummah is like rain; unknown whether its first or its last is better.""**"
"16711","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was absent and did not emerge until they thought he would not emerge; when he emerged he prostrated so long they thought his soul had been taken therein. Raising his head he ﷺ said his Lord had consulted him about his Ummah — what He should do with them. He said: whatever You will, my Lord; they are Your creation and servants. He consulted him a second time and he answered likewise, so He said: We shall not disgrace you regarding your Ummah, O Muḥammad. And He informed him that the first of his Ummah entering Paradise are seventy thousand, with each thousand seventy thousand, with no reckoning upon them. Then He sent to him saying supplicate and you shall be answered, ask and you shall be given. He asked His messenger whether his Lord would grant his request, and he said He sent him only to grant him. His Lord granted him, without boast: forgave his past and future sins while he walks alive and sound; granted that his Ummah shall neither hunger nor be overcome; granted al-Kawthar, a river of Paradise flowing into his Pond; granted might, victory, and terror travelling a month before his Ummah; granted that he is the first of the prophets to enter Paradise; made spoils wholesome for him and his Ummah; permitted much of what was strict upon those before; and placed no hardship upon them. > **""My Lord consulted me about my Ummah: what should I do with them? I said: whatever You will, my Lord; they are Your creation and Your servants. He said: We shall not disgrace you regarding your Ummah, O Muḥammad.""** > **""Supplicate and you shall be answered, ask and you shall be given.""**"
"16712","Ḥudhayfah ibn Asīd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said his Ummah was shown to him last night beside this chamber, until he knew each man among them better than any of you knows his companion. A man asked: this shown you is those created among them — what of those not yet created? He said their forms were depicted for him, and by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, he knows each person among them better than a man knows his companion. > **""My Ummah was shown to me last night beside this chamber, until I knew each man among them better than any of you knows his companion.""** > **""Their forms were depicted for me; by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I know each person among them better than a man knows his companion.""**"
"16713","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that his Ummah was shown to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he stood behind him. When he finished he turned to him and said: you were here — did you hear? He said yes. > **""You were here — did you hear?""**"
"16714","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said his Ummah on earth are more numerous than pebbles — or the number of rain. > **""My Ummah on earth are more numerous than pebbles — or the number of rain.""**"
"16715","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that every Ummah has part in the Fire and part in Paradise, except his Ummah — all of it in Paradise. > **""Every Ummah has part in the Fire and part in Paradise, except my Ummah — all of it in Paradise.""**"
"16716","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say his Ummah is a merciful Ummah, forgiven, entering graves with sins and emerging from graves sinless, purged through the believers' seeking forgiveness for them. > **""My Ummah is a merciful Ummah, forgiven; entering their graves with their sins and emerging from their graves sinless, purged through the believers' seeking forgiveness for them.""**"
"16717","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Paradise was forbidden the prophets until he enters it, and forbidden the nations until his Ummah enters it. > **""Paradise was forbidden the prophets until I enter it, and forbidden the nations until my Ummah enters it.""**"
"16718","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Paradise is forbidden all nations until he and his Ummah enter it, first in order. > **""Paradise is forbidden all nations until I and my Ummah enter it, first in order.""**"
"16719","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Yamānī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said were he to swear he would prove true: none shall enter Paradise before the forerunners of his Ummah. > **""Were I to swear I would prove true: none shall enter Paradise before the forerunners of my Ummah.""**"
"16720","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for his Ummah, saying O Allah turn their hearts toward Your obedience and encompass those behind them with Your mercy. > **""O Allah, turn their hearts toward Your obedience, and encompass those behind them with Your mercy.""**"
"16721","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that among the nations a parable was struck for an Ummah: like hired workers whom a man hires to work for him the whole day for a qīrāṭ each. They work till midday, tire, and ask for settlement; he settles and they receive half a qīrāṭ. He asks who will work for him till night for a qīrāṭ each, so others pledge and work till near ʿAṣr, tire, ask settlement, and receive half a qīrāṭ each. The man, wishing his work completed before night, hires a people to complete the remainder till night for two qīrāṭs each. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: I hope, Allah willing, that you are the ones of the two qīrāṭs. > **""I hope, Allah willing, that you are the ones of the two qīrāṭs.""**"
"16722","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said you are a merciful, pardoned Ummah; so be upright and take the capacity of the matter. > **""You are a merciful, pardoned Ummah; so be upright and take the capacity of the matter.""**"
"16723","Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Khawlānī reported that he heard ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz say to Abū Burdah: narrate a ḥadīth with none between you and your father therein. He said he heard his father — Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه — say he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say his Ummah is sanctified, blessed, merciful; no punishment upon it on Resurrection Day; its punishment is among them in this world through tribulations. > **""My Ummah is a sanctified, blessed, merciful Ummah; no punishment upon it on the Day of Resurrection. Its punishment is among them in this world through tribulations.""**"
"16724","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that they are the most resembling of nations to the Children of Israel in conduct, marks, and guidance. > **""You are the most resembling of nations to the Children of Israel in conduct, marks, and guidance.""**"
"16725","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that when Allah gathers creation on Resurrection Day, the Ummah of Muḥammad ﷺ will be permitted prostration; they shall prostrate long, then be told to raise their heads — We have made your number a ransom for you from the Fire. > **""Raise your heads; We have made your number a ransom for you from the Fire.""**"
"16726","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows, his Ummah among them eighty rows. > **""The people of Paradise are a hundred and twenty rows; my Ummah among them are eighty rows.""**"
"16727","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall all enter Paradise except whoever refuses or strays against Allah like a camel strays. Asked who refuses to enter Paradise, he said whoever obeys him enters Paradise and whoever disobeys him enters the Fire. > **""By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall all enter Paradise except whoever refuses or strays against Allah like a camel strays.""** > **""Whoever obeys me enters Paradise, and whoever disobeys me enters the Fire.""**"
"16728","ʿAlī ibn Khālid reported that Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه passed Khālid ibn Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah, who asked him for the gentlest word heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: verily all of you enter Paradise except whoever strays against Allah like a camel strays against its people. > **""Verily all of you enter Paradise except whoever strays against Allah like a camel strays against its people.""**"
"16729","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say all of you are in Paradise except whoever strays against Allah like a camel strays against its people. In al-Kabīr as mawqūf upon Abū Umāmah: none of this Ummah remains but enters Paradise except whoever strays against Allah like an evil camel strays against its people; whoever disbelieves him, then Allah says none burns but the most wretched who denied and turned away — denying what Muḥammad ﷺ brought and turning from it. > **""All of you are in Paradise except whoever strays against Allah like a camel strays against its people.""**"
"16730","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said people are but like a hundred camels among which no mount is found. > **""People are but like a hundred camels among which no mount is found.""**"
"16731","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said four mountains are of Paradise's mountains and four rivers of Paradise's rivers. As for mountains: al-Ṭūr, Lubnān, Ṭūr Sīnā, and Ṭūr Zaytā. And the rivers of Paradise: the Nile, the Euphrates, Sayḥān, and Jayḥān. > **""Four mountains are of the mountains of Paradise, and four rivers are of the rivers of Paradise. As for the mountains: al-Ṭūr, Lubnān, Ṭūr Sīnā, and Ṭūr Zaytā; and the rivers of Paradise: the Nile, the Euphrates, Sayḥān, and Jayḥān.""**"
"16732","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the worst two tribes among the Arabs are Najrān and Banū Taghlib. > **""The worst two tribes among the Arabs are Najrān and Banū Taghlib.""**"
"16733","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه reported that delegations of the Arabs came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and no delegation came to them harsher of heart, nor more likely that Islam had not settled in their hearts, than Banū Ḥanīfah."
"16734","Abū Barzah رضي الله عنه reported that the most hated of people — or of tribes — to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were Thaqīf and Banū Ḥanīfah. In Abū Yaʿlā's addition, and likewise al-Ṭabarānī: Banū Umayyah, Thaqīf, and Banū Ḥanīfah."
"16735","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the Hour shall not come until thirty liars emerge, among them Musaylimah, al-ʿAnsī, and al-Mukhtār; and the worst of Arab tribes are Banū Umayyah, Banū Ḥanīfah, and Thaqīf. > **""The Hour shall not come until thirty liars emerge, among them Musaylimah, al-ʿAnsī, and al-Mukhtār; and the worst of Arab tribes are Banū Umayyah, Banū Ḥanīfah, and Thaqīf.""**"
"16736","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما stood at a gate leading into the mosque — the mosque of Minā — praised Allah and extolled Him, then said these enslaved disbelieving sinners aimed at him, then mentioned the ḥadīth in full. > **""These enslaved disbelieving sinners have aimed at me.""**"
"16737","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said no man believing in Allah and the Last Day hates the Anṣār, nor loves Thaqīf. > **""No man believing in Allah and the Last Day hates the Anṣār, nor loves Thaqīf.""**"
"16738","Abū al-Qayn reported that he passed the Prophet ﷺ with some dates, and the Prophet ﷺ reached to take a handful to spread before his Companions, but he clutched the edge of his cloak to his belly and chest, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him: may Allah increase you in miserliness. > **""May Allah increase you in miserliness.""**"
"16739","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said if it pleases you to look at a feeble man obeyed among his people, then look at this — meaning ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn. > **""If it pleases you to look at a feeble man obeyed among his people, then look at this.""**"
"16740","ʿAmr ibn al-Aṣamm reported that he entered upon al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما while he was in the house of ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, and they said people claim ʿAlī will return before Resurrection Day. He laughed and denied it, saying had they known that, they would neither have married his wives nor divided his inheritance. > **""Glory be to Allah! Had we known that, we would not have married his wives nor divided his inheritance.""**"
"16741","Abū Yaḥyā reported that he stood between al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn رضي الله عنهما while he and Marwān exchanged insults, and al-Ḥasan kept silencing al-Ḥusayn. Marwān said they were a cursed household, so al-Ḥasan grew angry and answered that Allah had cursed Marwān while he was in his father's loins. > **""You say a cursed household! By Allah, Allah had cursed you while you were in your father's loins.""**"
"16742","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the devil of the mountain pass cast him down — meaning a man of Bajīlah. > **""The devil of the mountain pass cast him down.""**"
